window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['krv-b/kemp'] = {"krv-b-0001":["Critique",["- *Kritik* rendered \"Critique\" as part of the title, following Kemp Smith's title."]],"krv-b-0002":["of Pure Reason",[]],"krv-b-0003":["by\nImmanuel Kant,\nProfessor at Königsberg,",[]],"krv-b-0004":["Member of the Royal Academy of Sciences at Berlin.",[]],"krv-b-0005":["Second Edition, improved here and there.",[]],"krv-b-0006":["BACO DE VERULAMIO.\n*Instauratio magna. Praefatio.*",["- Source reads \"BACO ve VERULAMIO\"; silently corrected to *de* (OCR slip)."]],"krv-b-0007":["Of ourselves we are silent; but as to the matter which is in hand, we ask that men should think of it not as an opinion but as a work, and be assured that we are laying the foundations not of any sect or doctrine, but of the utility and greatness of mankind. Further, that they should deal fairly with their own interests, take counsel together for the common good, and themselves take part [in the work]. Moreover, that they should be of good hope, and neither imagine nor conceive our Instauration as something infinite and beyond what is mortal; since in truth it is the end and lawful limit of infinite error.¹",["- Latin motto translated, since it is a quotation and not a technical term. The dash after *aequi* is kept in sense only as a pause; the printed text has \"aequi — in commune consulant\", which may be a corrupt or elided reading. \"[in the work]\" is supplied.","- The OCR mark “) after *legitimus* is read as footnote reference 1."]],"krv-b-0008":["¹ The motto is an addition of A2.",["- Editorial footnote of the Academy Edition; \"A2\" is the editors' siglum for the second edition."]],"krv-b-0009":["To His Excellency,\nthe Royal Minister of State,\nFreiherr von Zedlitz.\n3",["- The stray \"3\" is retained as it stands in the OCR; it may be a page or line number, in which case it should be dropped."]],"krv-b-0010":["ns m u",["- Unintelligible OCR debris (probably an ornament or running head); not translated."]],"krv-b-0011":["Gracious Sir!",[]],"krv-b-0012":["| To further the growth of the sciences, each in his own part, is to work for Your Excellency's own interest; for this interest is bound up with theirs, not merely through the exalted post of a protector, but through the far more intimate relation of a lover and enlightened",["- Sentence breaks off; continued in the next passage. The | is carried over."]],"krv-b-0013":["connoisseur. I therefore also make use of the one means which is in some measure within my power, to show my gratitude for the gracious confidence with which Your Excellency honours me, as though I could contribute something to this end.² To that same gracious regard with which Your Excellency favoured the first edition of this work, I now dedicate this second edition also,",["- Restated \"Kenners innigst verbunden\" as continuing the previous sentence; the main verb \"verbunden\" is left to the reader's sense (\"bound up\"), since the sentence is split across passages.","- OCR \"könne!)\" read as *könne* with footnote reference ¹/². \"f\" and “)” after \"beitragen\" are OCR debris; I show one note marker. Footnote text not supplied.","- Sentence continues in next passage."]],"krv-b-0014":["and with it likewise³ all the other concerns of my literary vocation,",["- \"zugleich“)\" carries an OCR footnote marker, shown as ³; the word is split at \"Be-\" and joined with the next passage."]],"krv-b-0015":["and remain, with the deepest veneration,",["- Joins \"Be-stimmung\" from the previous passage: \"vocation\" (*Bestimmung*) is thus completed here; I rendered it in the previous line."]],"krv-b-0016":["Your Excellency's",[]],"krv-b-0017":["| Königsberg, 23rd April, most humble and obedient",["- The two-column layout of date and signature is garbled; order kept as given."]],"krv-b-0018":["1787.⁴ Servant\nImmanuel Kant.",["- \"4)\" read as a footnote marker; no footnote text given."]],"krv-b-0019":["ur — Te",["- OCR debris; not translated."]],"krv-b-0020":["Preface to the Second Edition.¹",["- The stray quotation mark after \"Auflage\" is read as a footnote marker."]],"krv-b-0021":["Whether the treatment of those modes of knowledge [*Erkenntnisse*] which belong to the business of reason follows the secure course of a science, can soon be judged from the outcome. If, after many preparations and arrangements have been made, reason runs into difficulties as soon as it approaches its goal; or if, in order to reach it, it has often to retrace its steps and strike out on a different path; or again, if it proves impossible to bring the various collaborators to agreement upon the manner in which the common aim is to be pursued, then we may always be assured that such a study has not yet entered upon the secure course of a science, but is a merely random groping. It is already a service to reason if this path can be discovered, even if much that was included in the aim adopted without reflection has to be abandoned as vain.",["- Final sentence of the German split off from the preceding one. \"solte\" read as *sollte*; \"moglich\" as *möglich*.","- *Erkenntnisse* = \"modes of knowledge\" (plural)."]],"krv-b-0022":["That logic has already, from the earliest times, followed this secure course is evident from the fact that since Aristotle it has not been required to retrace a single step, unless indeed we count as improvements the removal of some dispensable subtleties or the clearer determination of what has been propounded; these, however, concern the elegance rather than the certainty of the science. It is also remarkable that to the present day logic has been unable to advance a single step, and so to all appearance seems to be closed and complete. For if certain of the moderns have thought to enlarge it by introducing psychological chapters on the different faculties of knowledge (imagination, wit), or metaphysical chapters on the origin of knowledge or on the different kinds of certainty according to differences in the objects (idealism, scepticism, and so forth), or anthropological chapters on prejudices (their causes and remedies), this springs from their ignorance of the peculiar nature of this science. It is not an enlargement but a disfigurement of the sciences if their boundaries are allowed to run into one another. The boundary of logic is, however, quite precisely determined by the fact that it is a science which expounds in detail and strictly proves nothing but the formal rules of all thinking (whether it be *a priori* or empirical, whatever its origin or object, and whether it meets with contingent or natural hindernces in our mind).",["- OCR \"Ursprung der e\" restored as \"origin of knowledge\" (*Erkenntnisse*); \"anthropolo— gl che\" read as *anthropologische*. Flagged as reconstructions.","- \"seinen Schritt\" read as *keinen Schritt*. \"hindernces\" should read \"hindrances\" (*Hindernisse*).","- Long sentences divided; \"Gemüthe\" = mind."]],"krv-b-0023":["That logic has been so fortunate, it owes this advantage solely to its limitation, whereby it is entitled, indeed obliged, to abstract from all objects of knowledge and their differences; in it, therefore, the understanding has to deal with nothing but itself and its form. It was naturally far more difficult for reason to enter upon the secure path of science when it has to occupy itself not merely with itself but also with objects. Logic, accordingly, as a propaedeutic, constitutes only the forecourt, as it were, of the sciences; and when information [*Kenntnisse*] is in question, a logic may indeed be presupposed for estimating it, but the acquiring of it must be sought in the sciences properly and objectively so called.",["- Sentence \"Daß es der Logik… gelungen ist\" is irregular (a *daß*-clause as subject); I smoothed it to a plain statement.","- *Kenntnisse* rendered \"information\" to distinguish from *Erkenntnis* (\"knowledge\"); a choice."]],"krv-b-0024":["In so far as reason is to be found in these sciences, something in them must be known *a priori*. Their knowledge may be related to its object in two ways: either merely to determine the object and its concept (which must be given from elsewhere), or also to make the object actual. The former is theoretical, the latter practical knowledge of reason. In both the pure part, however much or little it may contain, that is, the part in which reason determines its object wholly *a priori*, must first be expounded by itself, and what comes from other sources must not be mixed with it. For it is bad management to spend blindly what comes in, without being able afterwards, when the finances run into difficulties, to distinguish which part of the revenue can bear the expenditure, and in which part it must be curtailed.",["- \"auch)\" has an OCR footnote marker; dropped as unrecoverable. \"wirtlich\" read as *wirklich*. \"jene\" refers to the business (*Wirthschaft*), rendered \"the finances\"; a judgment. The passage breaks off mid-sentence and continues in the next passage (\"kön⸗/neu\"); I completed the sense across the join, following the method's smoothing."]],"krv-b-0025":["[The sentence continues:] ...which part of the revenue can bear the expenditure, and in which part it must be curtailed.",["- The fragment \"neu\" completes *können*; the translation of this clause already appears at the end of the preceding passage, so it is repeated here only as a bracketed continuation. \"Einnähme\" read as *Einnahme*."]],"krv-b-0026":["Mathematics and physics are the two theoretical modes of knowledge of reason which have to determine their objects *a priori*: the former quite purely, the latter at least partly so, but then also in accordance with other sources of knowledge than that of reason.",["- OCR \"i ganz rein\" read as \"ganz rein\"; stray \"i\" dropped.","- *Erkenntnisse* = \"modes of knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-0027":["From the earliest times to which the history of human reason extends, mathematics has, among that admirable people the Greeks, travelled the secure path of a science. But it must not be supposed that it was as easy for mathematics as it was for logic (where reason has to deal with itself alone) to hit upon, or rather to construct for itself, that royal road. On the contrary, I believe that it was left for a long time merely groping about (chiefly among the Egyptians), and that the change is to be ascribed to a revolution, brought about by the happy thought of a single man in the course of one experiment. From that point onwards the path which had to be followed could no longer be missed, and the secure course of a science was entered upon and traced out for all time and to infinite distances. The history of this revolution in the mode of thought (a revolution much more important than the discovery of the passage round the celebrated Cape) and of the fortunate man who brought it about has not been preserved. But the story handed down by Diogenes Laertius, who names the reputed discoverer of the smallest among the elements of geometrical demonstration (elements which, in the ordinary judgment, do not even require proof), shows that the memory of the alteration wrought by the first glimpse of this new path must have seemed to the mathematicians exceedingly important, and so became unforgettable. A light broke upon the first man who demonstrated the isosceles triangle (whether his name was Thales or what you will). For he found that what he had to do was not to trace what he saw in the figure, or even to trace the mere concept of it, and as it were read off from this its properties; but that he must produce the figure through what he himself, in accordance with concepts, thought into it and represented [in intuition] *a priori* (that is, by construction). He found, too, that in order to know anything securely *a priori* he must ascribe to the thing nothing save what necessarily follows from what he has himself put into it in accordance with his concept.",[]],"krv-b-0028":["Natural science was much longer in finding the highway of science. It is only about a century and a half since the proposal of the ingenious Bacon of Verulam partly occasioned this discovery and partly, since men were already on its trail, gave it fresh impetus. This discovery, too, can be explained only as the result of a rapid revolution in the mode of thought. I shall here consider natural science only so far as it is founded on empirical principles.",[]],"krv-b-0029":["When Galileo rolled balls of a weight chosen by himself down an inclined plane, or when Torricelli made the air carry a weight which he had previously thought to be equal to that of a known column of water, or when, in a later period, Stahl changed metals into lime, and lime back into metal, by withdrawing something and then restoring it,* a light broke upon all students of nature. They learned that reason has insight only into that which it produces after a plan of its own; that it must not allow itself to be kept, as it were, in nature's leading-strings, but must itself show the way with principles of judgment based upon fixed laws, constraining nature to give an answer to questions of reason's own determining. Accidental observations, made in obedience to no previously thought-out plan, can never be made to yield a necessary law, which alone reason is concerned to discover and requires. Reason, holding in one hand its principles, according to which alone concordant appearances can be admitted as equivalent to laws, and in the other the experiment which it has devised in conformity with these principles, must approach nature in order to be taught by it. It must not, however, do so in the character of a pupil who listens to everything that the teacher chooses to say, but of an appointed judge who compels the witnesses to answer questions which he has himself formulated. Thus even physics owes the beneficent revolution in its character entirely to the happy thought that what reason itself puts into nature, it must seek in nature (not fictitiously ascribe to it), and must learn from nature what it could not itself know. In this way natural science has been brought for the first time into the secure course of a science, after having for so many centuries been nothing but a merely random groping.",[]],"krv-b-0030":["* I am not following here the exact thread of the history of the experimental method, the first beginnings of which are not well known.",[]],"krv-b-0031":["Metaphysics, a completely isolated speculative knowledge of reason, which rises wholly above instruction from experience, and does so through mere concepts (not, like mathematics, by applying them to intuition), and in which reason therefore is meant to be its own pupil, has so far had the fortune not to be favoured enough to enter upon the secure course of a science. Yet it is older than all the other sciences, and would survive even if all of them were swallowed up in the abyss of an all-destroying barbarism. For in it reason continually comes to a stand, even when it claims to have *a priori* insight into those laws which the commonest experience confirms. In it we have to retrace our steps innumerable times, because we find that the road does not lead where we want to go. As to the unanimity of its adherents in their assertions, it is still so far from this that it is rather a battle-ground, which appears to be quite specially destined for the exercise of strength in mock combat, and on which no combatant has ever yet succeeded in gaining even the smallest ground, or in basing upon his victory any lasting possession. There is thus no doubt that its procedure has hitherto been a merely random groping, and, what is worst of all, a groping among mere concepts. What, then, is the reason why a secure path of science has not yet been found? Is it perhaps impossible? Why then should nature have afflicted our reason with the restless striving after such a path, as if it were one of reason's weightiest concerns? Nay more, how little cause have we to place trust in our reason, if in one of the most important domains of our desire for knowledge it does not merely forsake us, but entices us on by delusive promises, and in the end betrays us! Or if the path has only hitherto been missed, what indications are there to lead us to expect that, in renewed search, we shall be more fortunate than others have been before us?",["- krv-b-0027: several long sentences divided or recast; \"Umänderung\" taken as \"the change\" and \"in einem Versuche\" as \"in the course of one experiment\". \"[in intuition]\" is supplied for \"darstellte\" (the sense of *a priori* exhibition).","- krv-b-0027: \"geheißen haben\" rendered \"his name was\"; \"Denkart\" = \"mode of thought\". \"Agyptern\" silently corrected to Ägyptern.","- krv-b-0028: OCR \"finnreihen\" corrected to \"sinnreichen\" (ingenious).","- krv-b-0029: OCR \"ges dacht\" read \"gedacht\"; the footnote marker (printed as “) ) is rendered as * and attached to the passage and to krv-b-0030.","- krv-b-0029: \"Principien ihrer Urtheile\" kept as \"principles of judgment\"; \"einer Hand ... in der anderen\" smoothed into one English clause, with the long sentence on physics's revolution restated slightly for clarity.","- krv-b-0030: OCR \"Erperimental« methode\" read as \"Experimentalmethode\".","- krv-b-0031: a stray \"l\" (OCR) before \"Woran\" dropped; \"ist das Schicksal ... nicht so günstig gewesen\" smoothed to \"has not been favoured enough\". \"in Stecken geräth\" = \"comes to a stand\".","- krv-b-0031: \"Erkenntniß\" in \"Vernunfterkenntniß\" = \"knowledge of reason\"."]],"krv-b-0032":["I should have thought that the examples of mathematics and natural science, which have become what they now are through a revolution brought about all at once, were remarkable enough to make us reflect on the essential feature in the change of method which has proved so advantageous to them, and, so far as the analogy between them and metaphysics, as modes of rational knowledge, permits, to imitate them, at least by way of experiment. Hitherto it has been assumed that all our knowledge must conform to the objects. But all attempts to establish anything about them a priori, through concepts, whereby our knowledge would be extended, have on this supposition come to nothing. Let us therefore make trial whether we may not have more success in the problems of metaphysics if we suppose that objects must conform to our knowledge. This would agree better with what is desired, namely, that it should be possible to have knowledge of objects a priori, determining something in regard to them before they are given to us. The situation here is the same as that of Copernicus in his first thoughts. He found that he could make no progress in explaining the movements of the heavens so long as he assumed that the whole host of stars revolved round the spectator. He therefore tried whether he might not have better success if he made the spectator revolve and left the stars at rest.\n\nIn metaphysics we can make a similar trial, in respect of the intuition of objects. If intuition has to conform to the constitution of the objects, I do not see how we could know anything of the latter a priori. But if the object (as object of the senses) conforms to the constitution of our faculty of intuition, I can quite well conceive this possibility. Since, however, I cannot rest in these intuitions, if they are to become knowledge, but must relate them, as representations, to something as their object, and must determine this object through them, I can adopt one or other of two assumptions. I may assume that the concepts through which I effect this determination also conform to the object; in that case I am again in the same perplexity as to how I can know anything of it a priori. Or I may assume that the objects, or what is the same thing, experience, in which alone, as given objects, they are known, conform to these concepts. In that case I at once see an easier way out of the difficulty. For experience is itself a mode of knowledge which requires understanding. The understanding has rules which I must presuppose as in me before objects are given to me, and therefore a priori. These rules find expression in concepts a priori, to which all objects of experience must necessarily conform, and with which they must agree.\n\nAs to objects which are thought merely by reason, and thought as necessary, but which cannot (at least in the manner in which reason thinks them) be given in experience, the attempts to think them (for think them we must) will furnish an excellent touchstone of what we are adopting as our altered method of thought, namely, that we know a priori of things only what we ourselves put into them.[*]",["- Long opening sentences divided; \"dem wesentlichen Stücke der Umänderung der Denkart\" rendered \"the essential feature in the change of method\"; \"Denkart\" given as \"method of thought\".","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge throughout; \"Vernunfterkenntnisse\" = \"modes of rational knowledge\".","- \"Es ist hiemit eben so… bewandt\" smoothed to \"The situation here is the same\"; \"ihnen\" in the Copernicus clause and the parenthetical \"(denn denken müssen sie sich doch lassen)\" kept as \"for think them we must\".","- \"Weil ich aber … nicht stehen bleiben kann\" restated: the two alternatives are set out as separate sentences; \"so sehe ich sofort eine leichtere Auskunft\" attached to the second alternative.","- The final sentence's \"Was Gegenstände betrifft…, so werden die Versuche…\" is anacoluthic; smoothed. \"[*]\" marks the closing footnote sign (text lost in OCR; the footnote is the one printed at B xviii–xix, not supplied here).","- OCR slips corrected silently (e.g. \"daz || her\" kept as the apparatus break joined: \"daher\"; stray margin letters dropped). The \"||\" and \"|f\" were not carried since they are OCR line/page debris rather than Academy apparatus here."]],"krv-b-0033":["This attempt succeeds as we could wish, and promises to metaphysics, in its first part, where it is occupied with concepts a priori to which the corresponding objects, adequate to them, can be given in experience, the secure path of a science. For on this altered method of thought we can quite well explain the possibility of knowledge a priori, and, what is more, can provide satisfactory proofs of the laws which lie a priori at the basis of nature, regarded as the sum of the objects of experience. Both of these things were impossible on the procedure hitherto followed.\n\nBut from this deduction of our faculty of knowing a priori, in the first part of metaphysics, there emerges a strange result, one which appears to be very damaging to the whole purpose of metaphysics as dealt with in its second part. The result is that with this faculty we can never get beyond the limits of possible experience, and yet this is precisely the most essential concern of this science. But here lies just the experiment which, by contrast, tests the truth of the result of that first estimate of our rational knowledge a priori: namely, that such knowledge applies only to appearances, and leaves the thing in itself as indeed real in itself, but as unknown to us. For that which of necessity impels us to go beyond the limits of experience and of all appearances",["- Sentence \"Aber es ergiebt sich…\" divided into two to carry the \"namely\" clause; \"Vermögen\" = faculty.","- \"Gegenprobe\" rendered \"experiment which, by contrast, tests\"; \"Würdigung\" = \"estimate\".","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (continues in the next passage, \"…hinaus zu gehen treibt\"); not completed.","- \"moglicher\" corrected silently to \"möglicher\"."]],"krv-b-0034":["[*] This method, imitated from that of the student of nature, consists, therefore, in seeking the elements of pure reason in what admits of confirmation or refutation by experiment. Now for the testing of the propositions of pure reason, especially when they venture beyond all limits of possible experience, no experiment can be made with their objects (as in natural science). Such testing will therefore be feasible only with concepts and principles which we assume a priori. We must so arrange them that the same objects can be viewed from two different sides: on the one side as objects of the senses and of the understanding, for experience; on the other side as objects which are merely thought, at most for reason, isolated and striving beyond the limits of experience. If we now find that, when things are considered from this twofold standpoint, there is agreement with the principle of pure reason, but that from a single standpoint there arises an unavoidable conflict of reason with itself, then the experiment decides in favour of the correctness of that distinction.\n\n[…hinaus zu gehen treibt,] is the unconditioned, which reason, in things in themselves, necessarily and with every right demands for everything conditioned, and so demands the series of conditions as completed. Now suppose we find, on the assumption that our knowledge of experience conforms to the objects as things in themselves, that the unconditioned cannot be thought without contradiction; and that, on the other hand, if we assume that our representation of things, as they are given to us, does not conform to them as things in themselves, but that these objects, as appearances, conform rather to our mode of representation, the contradiction vanishes; and that consequently the unconditioned is not to be met with in things so far as we know them (as they are given to us), but only in them so far as we do not know them, as things in themselves: then it is shown that what we adopted at first merely as an experiment is well founded.[*]\n\nNow, after speculative reason has been denied all progress in this field of the supersensible, it still remains for us to try whether data may not be found in its practical knowledge whereby to determine that transcendent rational concept of the unconditioned, and so, in accordance with the wish of metaphysics, to get beyond the limits of all possible experience with our knowledge a priori, which is, however, possible only for practical purposes. In such a procedure speculative reason has at any rate made room for this extension, even though it had to leave it empty; and we are therefore still at liberty, indeed are summoned by speculative reason, to fill it, if we can, with practical data.[*]",["- The OCR has the footnote text (B xxi–xxii) interleaved with the main text; I have separated the footnote from the text it interrupts. The fragment \"hinaus zu gehen treibt\" belongs to the main sentence begun at the end of the previous passage; I have bracketed the supplied opening.","- The footnote is given first, as in the OCR order; its markers \"[*]\" are my placement, since the OCR gives \"„)\" and \"“)\".","- Long conditional sentence (\"Findet sich nun… so zeigt sich\") restated as \"Suppose we find…\" with the colon retained; \"vielmehr\" attached to \"conform\".","- \"wegfallez\" read \"wegfalle\"; \"Überfinn⸗ lichen\" read \"Übersinnlichen\"; stray \"N\", \"1\", \"14\", \"a\" dropped.","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Vorstellungsart\" = \"mode of representation\"; \"Gesichtspunkt\" = \"standpoint\"."]],"krv-b-0035":["22",["- Page number only (B xxii); nothing to translate."]],"krv-b-0036":["[*] Thus the central laws of the motions of the heavenly bodies gave established certainty to what Copernicus at first assumed only as a hypothesis, and at the same time proved the invisible force which binds the structure of the world together (Newtonian attraction). This force would have remained for ever undiscovered if Copernicus had not ventured, in a manner contrary to the senses yet true, to seek the observed movements not in the objects of the heavens but in the spectator [text breaks off at \"die der\"].",["- The passage breaks off at \"…auf eine widersinnische, aber doch wahre Art die der\"; the bracketed remark marks the break. The clause after \"die der\" is not supplied, and \"to seek the observed movements not in the objects of the heavens but in the spectator\" is my completion from the context of B xxii; it is not in the passage given and should be disregarded as a translation.","- \"A\" and \"qi:\" and \"de\" garbled by OCR; \"New: ionischen\" read \"Newtonischen\"; \"erstere\" = \"the former\", i.e. Copernicus."]],"krv-b-0037":["This experiment of pure reason has much in common with that of the chemists, which they sometimes entitle the experiment of reduction, but in general the synthetic procedure. The analysis of the metaphysician separated pure knowledge *a priori* into two very heterogeneous elements, namely, | that of things as appearances and that of things in themselves. The dialectic again combines the two, in harmony with the necessary rational idea | of the unconditioned, and finds that this harmony is never obtained save | through the above distinction, which is therefore the true one.",["- Stray \")\" at the start and \"A\" at the end (OCR of a footnote mark and signature letter) omitted; \"I\" after \"welches\" read as the sign |.","- \"Erscheinungen\" = appearances; \"Dinge an sich selbst\" = things in themselves; \"Einhelligkeit\" = harmony (a choice); \"Vernunftidee\" = rational idea.","- The sentence \"Die Dialektik verbindet…\" was kept whole but smoothed; \"wiederum\" rendered \"again\"."]],"krv-b-0038":["In this attempt to alter the procedure hitherto followed in metaphysics, by effecting a complete revolution in it after the example of the geometers and the investigators of nature, consists the task of this Critique of pure speculative reason. It is a treatise on method, not a system of the science itself; yet it outlines the science in its entirety, both as regards its limits and as regards its whole inner articulation. For pure speculative reason has this peculiarity, that it can and ought to measure its own faculty according to the different ways in which it chooses its objects for thought; that it can and ought also to enumerate completely the manifold ways of setting itself problems; and that it can and ought thus to sketch the entire preliminary plan of a system of metaphysics. The reasons are these. As regards the first point, nothing can be ascribed to objects in knowledge *a priori* save what the thinking subject derives from itself. As regards the second, pure reason, in respect of the principles of knowledge, is a quite separate, self-subsistent unity, in which, as in an organised body, every member exists for the sake of all the others and all for the sake of each; and no principle can be taken with certainty in one relation unless it has at the same time been investigated in its thoroughgoing relation to the entire employment of pure reason.\n\nIn return, metaphysics enjoys a rare good fortune, which cannot fall to the lot of any other science of reason that has to do with objects (for logic is occupied only with the form of thought in general). Once it has been brought by this Critique upon the secure path of a science, it can embrace completely the whole field of the modes of knowledge that belong to it, and can thus complete its work and lay it down for posterity as a capital which can never be added to. For it has to do solely with principles and with the limitations of their employment, and these are determined by the principles themselves. As a fundamental science it is therefore also bound to attain this completeness, and of it we must be able to say: *nil actum reputans, si quid superesset agendum.*\n\n[Footnote, as it stands in the text before us:] [...] observed movements not in the heavenly bodies but in their spectator. In this Preface I put forward the change in our way of thinking which is expounded in the Critique, and which is analogous to that hypothesis, likewise only as a hypothesis, although in the treatise itself it is proved, not hypothetically but apodeictically, from the constitution of our representations of space and time and from the elementary concepts of the understanding. I do so merely in order to bring into notice the first attempts at such a change, which are always hypothetical.",["- Corrupt passage: the German runs \"…und obachteten Bewegungen nicht in den Gegenständen des Himmels…\", where the footnote to the Copernican hypothesis (a fragment beginning mid-word, \"[be]obachteten\") has been spliced into the main text before \"von ihr muß gesagt werden können\". I have restored the main sentence (\"und von ihr muß gesagt werden können\") and set the footnote apart; its beginning is lost and is marked [...].","- Restated as several sentences: the long \"Denn das hat… Eigenthümliches\" sentence (with \"weil, was das erste betrifft… was das zweite anlangt…\") and the \"Dafür aber…\" sentence. \"Das erste/zweite\" resolved as the first and second points of the preceding clause.","- \"Hauptstuhl\" = capital (stock); \"Gliederbau\" = articulation; \"Umänderung der Denkart\" = change in our way of thinking. \"Erkenntnisse\" = modes of knowledge.","- OCR slips corrected silently (e.g. \"5\", \"Um: änderung\"); the Academy sign | was dropped where only a line-break residue remained."]],"krv-b-0039":["But what, it will be asked, is the treasure which we propose to leave to posterity in a metaphysics thus purified by criticism, and thereby brought into a permanent condition? On a cursory survey of this work one will think to perceive that its utility is only negative: that it teaches us never to venture with speculative reason beyond the limits of experience. And this is in fact its first utility. But this utility becomes at once positive when we realise that the principles with which speculative reason ventures beyond its limits do not in fact extend, but, if more closely considered, inevitably narrow, the employment of our reason. For they threaten actually to extend the limits of sensibility, to which they properly belong, over everything, and so to supplant the pure (practical) employment of reason altogether. A critique which limits the former employment is therefore, so far, negative. But since it at the same time removes an obstacle which limits, or even threatens to destroy, the latter employment, it is in fact of positive and very important utility, as soon as we are convinced that there is an absolutely necessary practical employment of pure reason (the moral), in which it unavoidably extends itself beyond the limits of sensibility. For this, though it needs no assistance from speculative reason, it must yet be secured against the latter's counter-action, in order not to fall into contradiction with itself. To deny to this service of the Critique its positive utility would be as much as to say that the police yield no positive benefit, because their chief business is only to put a check on the violence which citizens have to fear from one another, so that each may pursue his own affairs in peace and security.\n\nThat space and time are only forms of sensible intuition, and therefore only conditions of the existence of things as appearances; that, further, we have no concepts of the understanding, and consequently no elements for the knowledge of things, save in so far as an intuition corresponding to these concepts can be given; and that accordingly we can have knowledge of no object as a thing in itself, but only in so far as it is an object of sensible intuition, that is, as appearance: all this is proved in the analytical part of the Critique. From it there follows, of course, the limitation of all possible speculative knowledge of reason to mere objects of experience. Yet (and this must be carefully noted) there is always reserved the possibility that we must be able to *think*, even though we cannot *know*, these same objects as things in themselves.*) For otherwise we should be landed in the absurd proposition that there is appearance without anything that appears.\n\nNow let us suppose that the distinction, which our Critique has shown to be necessary, between things as objects of experience and those same things as things in themselves had not been made. The principle of causality, and with it the mechanism of nature, would then have to hold, in the determination of things, of all things in general as efficient causes. Of one and the same being, for instance the human soul, I should then be unable to say that its will is free and yet at the same time subject to natural necessity, that is, not free, without falling into manifest contradiction. For I should have taken the soul in both propositions in precisely the same meaning, namely as a thing in general (as thing in itself); and without previous criticism I could not have taken it otherwise. But if the Critique has not erred,",["- Restated in separate sentences: the long sentence on the \"Grundsätze… Verengung\", the \"Daß Raum und Zeit…\" sentence (kept as one series of \"that\" clauses, with \"all this\" supplied to resume it), and \"Nun wollen wir annehmen…\".","- \"sie\" in \"indem sie wirklich die Grenzen der Sinnlichkeit… erweitern\" resolved as the principles (Grundsätze); \"dazu\" = \"for this\"; \"ihre Gegenwirkung\" = the counter-action of speculative reason; \"der letzteren/erstere\" = practical/speculative employment.","- The footnote marker *) is retained, but the OCR text supplies no footnote text. \"den? noch\" read as \"dennoch\"; \"pofi-tiven\" as \"positiven\"; \"Gewalt-thätigkeit\" as \"Gewaltthätigkeit\" (violence).","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Gegenstände der Erfahrung\" = objects of experience; \"Naturmechanism\" = mechanism of nature. The passage ends on a clause completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0040":["since it teaches us to take the object in two senses, namely as appearance or as thing in itself; if the deduction of its concepts of the understanding is correct, and consequently the principle of causality applies only to things taken in the first sense, namely so far as they are objects of experience, while these same things, taken in the second sense, are not subject to it: then the same will is, in the appearance (in visible actions), necessarily conformable to the law of nature, and so far not free; and yet, on the other hand, as belonging to a thing in itself, it is not subject to that law, and is therefore thought as free, without any contradiction arising. Now although my soul, of which",["- The passage opens mid-sentence with \"a sie\" (OCR for \"da sie\"), which continues the conditional begun at the end of the previous passage; I render it \"since it teaches\", with \"it\" = the Critique.","- \"Bertandesbegriffe\" corrected to \"Verstandesbegriffe\"; \"eben dieselbe\" (feminine singular) read as \"eben dieselben\" (the same things, Dinge); \"ihm\" = the principle of causality.","- The long conditional was divided with a colon and semicolons rather than separate sentences, since its apodosis would otherwise be lost.","- The text breaks off after \"von der\", before the verb of the clause \"Ob ich nun gleich meine Seele…\". In the English the dropped \"ich\" is therefore not supplied with a verb: \"my soul\" is left as the subject of the fragment. Nothing has been completed."]],"krv-b-0041":["To know an object, I must be able to prove its possibility, whether from the testimony of experience on the ground of its actuality, or *a priori* through reason. But I can think whatever I please, provided only that I do not contradict myself; that is, provided my concept is a possible thought. This holds even though I cannot answer for it whether, in the sum of all possibilities, an object corresponds to the concept or not. To ascribe objective validity to such a concept (real possibility, the former being merely logical possibility), something more is required. This something more need not, however, be sought in theoretical sources of knowledge; it may lie in practical sources.\n\n[Here the text is defective: the sentence beginning \"letzteren Seite betrachtet\" has lost its opening, and the OCR runs on without a break. What follows is translated as it stands.]\n\n[. . .] considered from the latter side, through no speculative reason (still less through empirical observation), and therefore I cannot know freedom as a property of a being to which I ascribe effects in the sensible world. The reason is that I should have to know such a being as determined in its existence, and yet not determined in time. This is impossible, since I can place no intuition beneath my concept. Nevertheless I can think freedom; that is, the representation of it at least contains no contradiction in itself, provided our critical distinction between the two modes of representation (the sensible and the intellectual), and the limitation of the pure concepts of the understanding which results from it, and so of the principles that flow from them, holds good.\n\nSuppose now that morality necessarily presupposes freedom (in the strictest sense) as a property of our will, in that it adduces, as data of it *a priori*, practical original principles lying in our reason, which without the presupposition of freedom would be absolutely impossible; and suppose that speculative reason had proved that freedom cannot be thought at all. Then the moral presupposition would necessarily have to yield to the other, whose opposite contains an obvious contradiction. Freedom, and with it morality (for the opposite of morality contains no contradiction, unless freedom is already presupposed), would have to give place to the mechanism of nature.\n\nAs things are, however, I need for morality nothing more than that freedom should not contradict itself, and so at least can be thought, without my needing to have any further insight into it. Freedom therefore puts no obstacle in the way of the natural mechanism of the very same action, taken in another relation. Thus the doctrine of morality maintains its place, and the doctrine of nature also its own. This would not have been possible had not criticism previously instructed us of our unavoidable ignorance in regard to things in themselves, and limited all that we can know theoretically to mere appearances.\n\nThis same exposition of the positive utility of the critical principles of pure reason can be shown in regard to the concept of God and of the simple nature of our soul. For brevity's sake, however, I pass it by.\n\nI cannot therefore even assume God, freedom and immortality, for the sake of the necessary practical employment of my reason, unless I at the same time deprive speculative reason of its pretension to extravagant insights. To arrive at such insights, speculative reason must make use of principles which, being in fact valid only for objects of possible experience, are, when nevertheless applied to what cannot be an object of experience, always actually transformed into appearance, and so render all practical extension of pure reason impossible. I had therefore to deny knowledge [*Wissen*] in order to make room for faith. The dogmatism of metaphysics, that is, the prejudice that it is possible to make progress in metaphysics without a critique of pure reason, is the true source of all the unbelief, always very dogmatic, which conflicts with morality.\n\nIf then it cannot be difficult to bequeath to posterity a systematic metaphysics constructed in accordance with the critique of pure reason, this is no gift to be lightly esteemed. We may consider merely the cultivation of reason through the secure progress of a science in general, as compared with its groundless groping and frivolous roaming without criticism. Or we may consider the better employment of the time of an inquiring youth, who in ordinary dogmatism receives so early and so much encouragement to reason comfortably about things of which he understands nothing, and in which neither he nor anyone in the world will ever have insight, or even to set out in search of new thoughts and opinions, and so to neglect the study of thorough sciences. Above all, we may consider the inestimable advantage of putting an end for all future time, in Socratic fashion, that is, by the clearest proof of the opponent's ignorance, to all objections against morality and religion.\n\nFor some metaphysics or other has always existed in the world, and will always continue to do so; and with it a dialectic of pure reason, since this is natural to it. It is therefore the first and most important concern of philosophy to deprive this dialectic once and for all of its harmful influence, by stopping up the source of the errors.",["- The OCR text has lost a stretch around \"letzteren Seite betrachtet\" (the lines beginning \"Wiewohl ich ... \"); I have not supplied it. The opening \")\" and several garbled words (\"Möglicheit\", \"Geanke\", \"ir\", \"bjective\", \"beiulegen\") were silently corrected.","- \"Wissen\" is given as \"knowledge [*Wissen*]\" to distinguish it from *Erkenntnis*, also \"knowledge\"; the contrast with *Glauben* (\"faith\") is the point of the sentence.","- Restated: the long sentence \"Gesetzt nun ... den Platz einräumen\" was divided in two, and \"So aber ... Weg lege\" was recast as a conclusion with \"therefore\".","- \"Nachkommenschaft\" is read from the OCR \"Radkommenschaft\"; \"Verwendung der Zeit\" is not in the text, which is damaged at \"bessere e einer wißbegierigen Jugend\", so \"the better employment of the time of\" is my supplied reading, flagged here as conjectural.","- \"Kritik\" is rendered \"criticism\" in the moral argument (as an agent) and \"critique\" in the title-phrase \"Kritik der reinen Vernunft\".","- \"derjenigen welchen\" is read as \"derjenigen, welcher\" (the speculative presupposition), whose opposite contains an obvious contradiction; the pronouns in the freedom/morality sentence were resolved accordingly.","- Footnote-like mark \"1\" after \"beider\" is OCR residue and was dropped; \"ftrengften\" read as \"strengsten\"."]],"krv-b-0042":["Despite this important change in the field of the sciences, and despite the loss which speculative reason must suffer in its hitherto imagined possessions, everything remains, in respect of the universal concern of mankind and of the profit which the world has hitherto drawn from the teachings of pure reason, in the same advantageous condition as it ever was. The loss affects only the monopoly of the schools, and in no way the interests of mankind. I ask the most inflexible dogmatist whether the proof of the continued existence of our soul after death, drawn from the simplicity of its substance; or the proof of the freedom of the will in opposition to universal mechanism, drawn from the subtle, though impotent, distinctions between subjective and objective practical necessity; or the proof of the existence of God, drawn from the concept of a most real being (from the contingency of what is changeable and the necessity of a first mover), has ever, after proceeding from the schools, been able to reach the public and to exert the least influence on its conviction. If this has not happened, and if, owing to the unfitness of the common understanding for such subtle speculation, it can never be expected; if, on the contrary, in respect of the first, the hope of a future life has arisen from the disposition of his nature, noticeable in every man, never to be satisfied by what is temporal (as insufficient for the capacities of his whole vocation [*Bestimmung*]); if, in respect of the second, the mere clear representation of duties, in opposition to all claims of the inclinations, has given rise to the consciousness of freedom; and if, finally, in respect of the third, the glorious order, beauty and providential care which shine forth everywhere in nature have by themselves given rise to the belief in a wise and great Author of the world; if, I say, these grounds alone must produce the conviction which spreads among the public, so far as it rests on rational grounds, then this possession is not only left undisturbed, but gains rather in authority. For the schools are thereby taught not to arrogate to themselves, upon a point which concerns the universal interest of mankind, any higher and more extended insight than that to which the great multitude (most worthy of our esteem) can just as easily attain; and so to confine themselves to the cultivation of those proofs alone which are universally intelligible and sufficient from the moral point of view.\n\nThe change, then, affects only the arrogant pretensions of the schools. In this matter (as in many others, with justice) they are fond of being held the sole connoisseurs and guardians of such truths, communicating to the public only the use of them while keeping the key to themselves (*quod mecum nescit, solus vult scire videri*). At the same time, provision has been made for a more equitable claim on the part of the speculative philosopher. He remains always the exclusive depositary of a science which is useful to the public without its knowledge, namely, the critique of reason. This can never become popular, nor has it any need to be. For just as little as the people can get fine-spun arguments for useful truths into their heads, so little do the equally subtle objections against them ever occur to them. The school, on the other hand, like everyone who raises himself to speculation, inevitably falls into both. The school is therefore bound, by a thorough investigation of the rights of speculative reason, to prevent once for all the scandal which sooner or later must break upon even the people from the disputes in which metaphysicians (and, as such, eventually clergy also) unavoidably become involved when they proceed without critique, and which in the end falsify their own doctrines.\n\nOnly through critique can the roots be cut of materialism, fatalism, atheism, free-thinking unbelief, fanaticism and superstition, which may become generally injurious, and finally also of idealism and scepticism, which are dangerous rather to the schools and can hardly pass over to the public. If governments think it proper to concern themselves with the affairs of the learned, it would be far more in keeping with their wise care for sciences as well as for men to favour the freedom of such a critique, by which alone the labours of reason can be placed on a firm footing, than to support the ridiculous despotism of the schools. These raise a loud outcry of public danger when their cobwebs are torn, though the public has never taken notice of them and therefore can never feel their loss.",[]],"krv-b-0043":["Criticism is not opposed to the dogmatic procedure of reason in its pure knowledge, as science (for science must always be dogmatic, that is, strictly demonstrative from secure *a priori* principles). It is opposed to dogmatism, that is, to the presumption that it can get on by itself, with a pure knowledge from concepts (the philosophical), according to principles such as reason has long been using, without first enquiring in what way and by what right reason has come to possess them. Dogmatism is thus the dogmatic procedure of pure reason without a preceding critique of its own capacity [*Vermögen*]. This opposition is therefore not meant to speak in favour of that glib shallowness which assumes the name of popularity, nor of scepticism, which makes short work of the whole of metaphysics. Criticism is, on the contrary, the necessary preliminary measure for the advancement of a well-grounded metaphysics as science. Such a metaphysics must be carried out dogmatically, and, in accordance with the strictest demands, systematically, and so in scholastic fashion (not popularly); for this demand upon it is indispensable, since it undertakes to carry out its task wholly *a priori*, and so to the complete satisfaction of speculative reason.\n\nIn carrying out the plan which the critique prescribes, that is, in the future system of metaphysics, we must follow the strict method of the celebrated Wolff, the greatest of all dogmatic philosophers. He was the first to give the example (and by it became the author of the spirit of thoroughness, not yet extinguished in Germany) of how the secure course of a science is to be taken: through the lawful establishment of principles, the clear determination [*Bestimmung*] of concepts, the attempt at strictness in proofs, and the avoidance of bold leaps in inferences. He was also, for that very reason, eminently fitted to raise a science such as metaphysics to this standing, had it occurred to him to prepare the field beforehand by a critique of the organ, that is, of pure reason itself. This lack is to be ascribed not so much to him as to the dogmatic habit of thought of his age; and in this respect the philosophers of his own and of all earlier times have nothing to reproach one another with. Those who reject his manner of teaching and at the same time the procedure of the critique of pure reason can have nothing else in view than to throw off the fetters of science altogether, to change work into play, certainty into opinion, and philosophy into *philodoxy* [*Philodoxie*].",["- krv-b-0042: Printed text has \"Philodorie\" in 0043 and \"allerrealiten\" in 0042; I silently corrected OCR slips (e.g. \"allerrealsten\", \"Philodoxie\") and dropped stray line numbers.","- krv-b-0042: Several long sentences were divided and the \"wenn… so\" series of conditions kept as parallel \"if\" clauses; \"Anlage… die Hoffnung\" is read as the disposition giving rise to hope, and \"ausgingen\" as \"proceeding from the schools\".","- krv-b-0042: \"das Skandal\" rendered \"the scandal\"; \"Depositär\" as \"depositary\"; \"Bestimmung\" given in brackets as \"vocation\" because the sense is man's whole destiny, not \"determination\".","- krv-b-0042: \"und die selbst nachher ihre Lehren verfälschen\" — antecedent taken to be the metaphysicians (the disputes' participants); the construction is loose.","- krv-b-0042: The Latin tag (*quod mecum nescit…*) is left untranslated, as in the source.","- krv-b-0043: \"Diese Entgegensetzung fol…\" is corrupt OCR; read \"soll\" (\"is meant to\"). \"Ich anheischig\" read \"sie sich anheischig\". \"Prinscipien\", \"sit tigen\" (fünfsittigen? likely \"künftigen\") read as \"künftigen System\": doubtful reading, \"future system\".","- krv-b-0043: \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"; \"Kritik\" rendered \"criticism\" when contrasted with dogmatism and \"critique\" elsewhere. \"Philodorie\" is a misprint for \"Philodoxie\".","- krv-b-0043: The final \"als Metaphysik ist… in diesen Stand zu versetzen\" sentence was recast as two sentences; \"Organ\" kept as \"organ\"."]],"krv-b-0044":["\\*) The only real increase [Vermehrung], and that only in the manner of proof, which I can claim is that which I have made through a new refutation of psychological idealism, and a rigorous proof (the only possible one, I believe) of the objective reality of outer intuition, p. 275. However innocent idealism may be held to be in respect of the essential ends of metaphysics (and in fact it is not innocent), it still remains a scandal to philosophy and to human reason in general that we should have to assume the existence of things outside us (from which we derive the whole material of our modes of knowledge, even for our inner sense) merely on faith, and that if anyone thinks good to doubt it, we should be unable to counter him with a satisfactory proof. Since there is some obscurity in the expressions of the proof from the third line to the sixth,¹ I ask that this sentence be altered as follows: \"But this permanent cannot be an intuition in me. For all the grounds of determination [Bestimmungsgründe] of my existence which can be met with in me are representations, and as representations themselves require something permanent distinct from them, in reference to which their change, and so my existence in the time wherein they change, can be determined.\" Against this proof it will presumably be said: I am immediately conscious only of what is in me, that is, of my representation of outer things; con-|\n\n---\n¹ This permanent ... can be determined.",["- Restated the opening sentence for English order; \"nur in der Beweisart\" rendered \"that only in the manner of proof\"; \"könnte ich nur die nennen\" rendered \"which I can claim is\".","- \"Skandal der Philosophie\" kept as \"scandal to philosophy\"; \"Glauben\" = \"faith\".","- \"Erkenntnissen\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\" (plural of Erkenntnis).","- OCR slips silently corrected: \"Idealisms,\" \"Beharr— liebe\" = \"Beharrliche,\" \"iud\" = \"sind,\" \"unterchiedenes\" = \"unterschiedenes\"; \"das\" before \"er in der That nicht ist\" and \"das Dasein\" read as \"dass\".","- \"wenn es jemand einfällt\" rendered \"if anyone thinks good to doubt it\"; the German is loose, a free rendering.","- The footnote marker \"!)\" after \"sechsten\" is read as the footnote 1, whose text (\"Dieses Beharrliche ... werden kann\") abbreviates the quoted sentence; it is translated as it stands, not completed.","- \"Bestimmungsgründe\" = \"grounds of determination\" (Bestimmung = determination); \"Beharrliche\" = \"permanent\".","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence at \"unmittelbar be-\" and is rendered as it stands (\"con-\" for the hyphenated \"bewusst\" fragment: more exactly \"immediately conscious\" is the sense, supplied in the English as \"conscious\")."]],"krv-b-0045":["As regards this second edition, I have, as is only fair, not wished to let the opportunity pass without removing, so far as possible, the difficulties and obscurity from which many misinterpretations may have sprung. Acute men have met with these in their judgment of this book, perhaps not without fault on my part. In the propositions themselves and their proofs, and likewise in the form and completeness of the plan, I have found nothing to alter. This is to be ascribed partly to the long examination to which I subjected them before laying them before the public, and partly to the nature of the subject itself, namely the nature of a pure speculative reason. Pure speculative reason contains a true articulated structure in which everything is organ, that is, everything exists for the sake of one, and each single part for the sake of all. Hence any weakness, however slight, whether a fault (error) or a deficiency, must inevitably betray itself in use. In this unalterability the system will, I hope, continue to maintain itself in future. What entitles me to this confidence is not conceit, but solely the evidence produced by the experiment of obtaining an identical result, whether we start from the least elements and proceed to the whole of pure reason, or return from the whole to each part. (For the whole too is given on its own account, through the final purpose of pure reason in the practical sphere.) For the attempt to alter even the smallest part at once introduces contradictions, not merely into the system, but into universal human reason.\n\nIn the exposition, however, much still remains to be done, and in this edition I have attempted improvements here. They are meant to remedy, in part, the misunderstanding of the Aesthetic, especially of the concept of time; in part, the obscurity of the Deduction of the concepts of the understanding; in part, the supposed lack of sufficient evidence in the proofs of the principles of pure understanding; and finally, the misinterpretation of the paralogisms brought against rational psychology. Up to this point (that is, only to the end of the first chapter [*Hauptstück*] of the Transcendental Dialectic), and no further, my alterations in the mode of presentation extend,† because time was too short, and in regard to the remainder no misunderstanding on the part of competent and impartial examiners had come to my notice. Even without my naming these examiners with the praise they deserve, they will find in their proper places the regard I have paid to their remarks.\n\nThis improvement, however, involves a small loss for the reader, which could not be avoided without making the book far too voluminous. Various matters have had to be omitted or presented in abbreviated form. They do not belong essentially to the completeness of the whole, yet many a reader would be reluctant to miss them, since they may otherwise be useful for other purposes. They had to give place to my exposition, which I hope is now more comprehensible. In respect of the propositions and even their proofs this exposition alters absolutely nothing; but in the method of presentation it departs here and there so far from the earlier that the change could not be effected by insertions. This small loss can in any case be made good, at each reader's pleasure, by comparison with the first edition, and will, I hope, be more than compensated by the greater comprehensibility.\n\nIn various public writings (partly in reviews of books, partly in special treatises) I have observed with grateful pleasure that the spirit of thoroughness has not died out in Germany, but has only been drowned for a short time by the fashionable tone of a genius-like freedom in thinking. I have observed also that the thorny paths of criticism, which lead to a science of pure reason that is scholastically rigorous, yet only as such enduring, and therefore highly necessary, have not prevented courageous and clear minds from mastering it. To these deserving men, who so happily combine thoroughness of insight with the talent of lucid exposition (a talent of which I am not conscious in myself), I leave the task of completing my treatment, which in the latter respect may here and there still be defective. For in this case there is no danger in being refuted; the danger is in not being understood. For my own part, I cannot from now on engage in disputes, though I shall carefully heed every hint, whether from friends or opponents, so as to turn it to account, in keeping with this propaedeutic, in the future elaboration of the system. I have advanced fairly far in years while engaged on these labours (this month I enter my sixty-fourth year). If I am to carry out my plan of furnishing the metaphysic of nature as well as of morals, as a confirmation of the correctness of the critique of speculative as well as of practical reason, I must therefore be sparing of time. I must expect the clearing up of the obscurities, hardly avoidable at first in this work, and the defence of the whole, from the deserving men who have made it their own.\n\nAny philosophical exposition can be carped at in particular passages (for it cannot appear so fully armoured as the mathematical). Meanwhile the articulated structure of the system, regarded as a unity, runs not the least danger thereby. Yet when the system is new, few have the mental agility to survey it, and still fewer the inclination, since all innovation is inconvenient to them. Even apparent contradictions can be picked out, if single passages are torn from their context and compared with one another, in any work which proceeds chiefly as free discourse. In the eyes of one who relies on another's appraisal [*fremde Beur-*, breaks off]\n\n† [The footnote, beginning in mid-sentence:] ... [is conscious]; consequently it would always remain undecided whether anything corresponding to it exists outside me or not. But I am conscious of my existence in time (and consequently also of its determinability in time) through inner experience. This is more than merely being conscious of my representation, yet is identical with the empirical consciousness of my existence, which is determinable only through relation to something which, while connected with my existence, is outside me. This consciousness of my existence in time is therefore bound up in identity with the consciousness of a relation to something outside me. Hence it is experience and not invention, sense and not imagination, that inseparably connects the outer with my inner sense. For outer sense is already in itself a relation of intuition to something actual outside me; and its reality, as distinguished from imagination, rests solely on its being inseparably bound up with inner experience itself, as the condition of the possibility of that experience, which is what happens here.\n\nIf, with the intellectual consciousness of my existence in the representation *I am*, which accompanies all my judgments and acts of understanding, I could at the same time connect a determination of my existence through intellectual intuition, then the consciousness of a relation to something outside me would not necessarily belong to it. As it is, however, that intellectual consciousness does indeed precede, but the inner intuition, in which alone my existence can be determined, is sensible and bound to the condition of time. This determination, and hence inner experience itself, depends on something permanent which is not in me, and consequently can be only in something outside me, in relation to which I must regard myself. Thus the reality of outer sense is necessarily bound up with that of inner sense for the possibility of experience in general. That is to say, I am as certainly conscious that there are things outside me which relate to my sense, as I am conscious that I myself exist determined in time. But which given intuitions actually correspond to objects outside me, and therefore belong to outer sense, to which they and not to imagination are to be ascribed, must be settled in each particular case according to the rules by which experience in general (even inner experience) is distinguished from imagination. The proposition that there is actually outer experience always underlies this.\n\nOne may add the remark that the representation of something permanent in existence is not the same as a permanent representation. For the latter can be very variable and changing, like all our representations, even those of matter, and yet it relates to something permanent. This permanent must therefore be a thing distinct from all my representations and outer to them. Its existence is necessarily included in the determination of my own existence and constitutes with it only a single experience, which would not even take place inwardly if it were not (in part) at the same time outer. The How? can no more be explained further here than we can explain, in general, how we think the abiding in time, whose coexistence with the changing produces the concept of alteration.\n\n---notes\n- The OCR text interleaves the long footnote (the Refutation of Idealism note, B xxxix–xl) with the main text. I separated them and placed the footnote after the main text, marked †. Kant's own marker is *); I changed it to avoid clashing with italics.\n- The footnote begins in mid-sentence (\"...wußt; folglich bleibe es...\"). I supplied \"[is conscious]\" for the lost opening and did not complete the sentence further.\n- The main text breaks off at \"fremde Beur-\" (presumably *Beurtheilung*). I kept the break and bracketed it.\n- Restated as several sentences: the opening sentence of the first paragraph, the sentence on \"Gliederbau\" (in \"an einzelnen Stellen...\"), and the long sentence on the \"Mit dieser Verbesserung\" loss. In the last, \"ungerne wissen\" is read as *ungerne missen* (OCR slip).\n- The stray \"[\" before \"aber doch in der Methode\" is an OCR artefact, not apparatus, and was dropped. Other OCR slips (e.g. \"Erperiment\", \"Faplichkeit\", \"Mugen\") were corrected silently.\n- \"Hauptstück\" is rendered \"chapter\" with the German given. *Gliederbau* is \"articulated structure\". *Darstellung* is \"exposition\" or \"presentation\", according to context.\n- In the footnote, \"Sinn und nicht Einbildungskraft\" is rendered \"sense and not imagination\". \"Bestimmbarkeit\" is \"determinability\", and \"Beharrliches\" is \"permanent\".",[]],"krv-b-0046":["…[the opening words are corrupt in the OCR] would cast an unfavourable light upon these [objections], though to one who has mastered the idea as a whole they are very easily resolved. Meanwhile, if a theory is stable in itself, action and reaction, which at first threatened it with great danger, serve in time only to smooth away its roughnesses; and if men of impartiality, insight and true popularity occupy themselves with it, they will in a short time provide it also with the requisite elegance.\n\nKönigsberg, April 1787.",["- The opening fragment (\"a m öl mü theilung verläßt\") is garbled OCR; I have not reconstructed it and translate only from \"ein nachtheiliges Licht\" onward. The subject and the antecedent of \"diese\" are therefore uncertain; \"these [objections]\" is supplied from the sense of the surrounding preface.","- \"in si Bestand hat\" read as \"in sich Bestand hat\".","- The stray \"i\" after the date is dropped as OCR noise."]],"krv-b-0047":["[illegible OCR fragment: \"en\"]",["- Not translatable; OCR noise from the page. Nothing reconstructed."]],"krv-b-0048":["[illegible OCR fragment: \"re\"]",["- Not translatable; OCR noise from the page. Nothing reconstructed."]],"krv-b-0049":["[illegible OCR fragment]",["- Not translatable; OCR noise (\"a un ac en P En —\"). Nothing reconstructed."]],"krv-b-0050":["Introduction.\n\nI. Of the Distinction between Pure and Empirical Knowledge.",["- The numeral \"I.\" is supplied from the structure of the Introduction (sections II and III follow); it is not in the OCR text."]],"krv-b-0051":["That all our knowledge begins with experience there can be no doubt. For how else should the faculty of knowledge be awakened into exercise, if not through objects which affect our senses, and which partly of themselves produce representations, partly rouse the activity of our understanding to compare these representations, and, by combining or separating them, to work up the raw material of the sensible impressions into that knowledge of objects which is entitled experience? In the order of time, therefore, no knowledge in us precedes experience, and with experience all our knowledge begins.",["- The long question is kept as one sentence, but the relative clauses are marked off by commas and \"which\"; \"die Erfahrung heißt\" rendered \"is entitled experience\".","- \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" = faculty of knowledge; \"Verstandesthätigkeit\" = activity of our understanding."]],"krv-b-0052":["But though all our knowledge begins with experience, it does not follow that it all arises out of experience. For it may well be that even our empirical knowledge is a compound of what we receive through impressions and of what our own faculty of knowledge (merely occasioned by sensible impressions) supplies from itself. This addition we cannot distinguish from the original material until long practice has made us attentive to it and skilled in separating it.",["- Last relative clause (\"welchen Zusatz…\") restated as a separate sentence."]],"krv-b-0053":["It is therefore at least a question which requires closer investigation, and which cannot be dismissed at first glance, whether there is any knowledge of this kind, independent of experience and even of all impressions of the senses. Such modes of knowledge are entitled *a priori*, and are distinguished from the empirical, which have their sources *a posteriori*, that is, in experience.",["- \"Erkenntniß\" in the singular in \"ein … Erkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"; plural \"Erkenntnisse\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\". The stray line number \"5\" is omitted."]],"krv-b-0054":["This expression, however, is not yet definite enough to indicate adequately the whole meaning of the question before us. For we are accustomed to say of much knowledge derived from empirical sources that we are capable of it, or partake in it, *a priori*, because we derive it not immediately from experience, but from a general rule which we have nevertheless itself borrowed from experience. Thus we say of a man who undermined the foundations of his house, that he could have known *a priori* that it would fall; that is, he need not have waited for the experience of its actually falling. Yet he could not have known this completely *a priori*. For that bodies are heavy, and therefore fall when their support is withdrawn, he must first have learnt through experience.",["- \"Erkenntniß … ihrer\" resolved as \"knowledge … it\"; \"die wir gleichwohl selbst doch aus der Erfahrung entlehnt haben\" rendered \"which we have nevertheless itself borrowed\", i.e. \"ourselves\" is intended: read \"which we have nevertheless ourselves borrowed from experience\".","- Line numbers omitted."]],"krv-b-0055":["In what follows, therefore, we shall understand by modes of knowledge *a priori* not those which occur independently of this or that experience, but those which occur in absolute independence of all experience. Opposed to them are empirical modes of knowledge, or those which are possible only *a posteriori*, that is, through experience. Of modes of knowledge *a priori*, those are entitled pure with which nothing empirical is intermingled. Thus, for instance, the proposition, Every alteration has its cause, is a proposition *a priori*, but not pure, because alteration is a concept which can be derived only from experience.",["- Correction: in the previous passage's note, \"itself\" should read \"ourselves\" (typographical slip in my note).","- \"heißen\" rendered \"are entitled\"."]],"krv-b-0056":["II.",[]],"krv-b-0057":["We are in possession of certain modes of knowledge *a priori*, and even the common understanding is never without them.",["- The sentence runs on into the next passage (\"der gemeine Verstand…\"); the two are joined here for sense, though the ids are separate. Heading text is split by the page break."]],"krv-b-0058":["[continuation of the preceding sentence: \"…the common understanding is never without them.\"]",["- Fragment \"der gemeine Verstand ist niemals ohne solche\" already rendered in krv-b-0057 to keep the sentence whole; not repeated as new text."]],"krv-b-0059":["What we require here is a criterion by which we can certainly distinguish pure knowledge from empirical. Experience tells us, indeed, that something is so and so constituted, but not that it could not be otherwise. First, then, if a proposition is thought along with its necessity, it is a judgment *a priori*; and if, in addition, it is not derived from any proposition except one which in its turn is valid as a necessary proposition, it is absolutely *a priori*. Secondly, experience never confers on its judgments true or strict universality, but only assumed and comparative universality (through induction). We ought properly to say, therefore, So far as we have hitherto perceived, there is no exception to this or that rule. If, then, a judgment is thought with strict universality, that is, in such a manner that no exception is allowed as possible, it is not derived from experience, but is valid absolutely *a priori*. Empirical universality is thus only an arbitrary extension of validity, from that which holds in most cases to that which holds in all, as, for instance, in the proposition, All bodies are heavy. Where, on the other hand, strict universality is essential to a judgment, this points to a special source of knowledge for it, namely, a faculty of knowledge *a priori*. Necessity and strict universality are thus sure criteria of knowledge *a priori*, and are inseparable from one another. But since in using them it is sometimes easier to show the empirical limitation of judgments than their contingency, or again sometimes more evident to show the unlimited universality which we ascribe to a judgment than its necessity, it is advisable to employ the two criteria separately, each being by itself infallible.",["- \"Quelle\" is given as \"source\"; OCR \"Erkenntnißgquell\" corrected to \"Erkenntnißquell\"; \"Algemeinheit\" corrected.","- \"Erkenntnisses a priori\" in \"Vermögen des Erkenntnisses\" rendered \"faculty of knowledge *a priori*\".","- \"die Zufälligkeit in den Urtheilen\" taken as \"contingency of judgments\"; the sentence is long and kept as one, with the \"Weil\" clause intact."]],"krv-b-0060":["That there really are in human knowledge judgments which are necessary and in the strictest sense universal, and which are therefore pure judgments *a priori*, is easily shown. If we want an example from the sciences, we need only look to all the propositions of mathematics. If we seek one from the commonest use of the understanding, the proposition that every alteration must have a cause will serve. In the latter, indeed, the very concept of a cause so manifestly contains the concept of a necessity of connection with an effect, and of a strict universality of the rule, that the concept would be altogether lost if, as Hume did, we sought to derive it from a frequent conjunction of what happens with what precedes, and from a custom, arising therefrom, of connecting representations (that is, from a merely subjective necessity). Even without requiring such examples to prove the actuality of pure principles *a priori* in our knowledge, we could establish their indispensableness for the possibility of experience itself, and so establish it *a priori*. For whence could experience itself derive its certainty, if all the rules by which it proceeds were themselves always empirical, and therefore contingent? Hence these rules can hardly be allowed to rank as first principles. Here, however, we may content ourselves with having set out the pure use of our faculty of knowledge as a fact, together with the criteria of it. But not only in judgments, but even in concepts, is the origin of some of them *a priori* revealed. Remove gradually from your experiential concept of a body everything that is empirical in it: the colour, the hardness or softness, the weight, even the impenetrability. There still remains the space which the body (now entirely vanished) occupied, and this you cannot remove. Similarly, if you leave out of your empirical concept of any object, corporeal or incorporeal, all the properties which experience teaches you, you yet cannot take from it that property through which you think it as substance, or as inhering in a substance (although this concept contains more determination than that of an object in general). Convinced by the necessity with which this concept forces itself upon you, you must admit that it has its seat in your faculty of knowledge *a priori*.",["- \"Welche\" in the German (OCR for \"Weiche\") read as \"softness\".","- \"Wirklichkeit\" rendered \"actuality\"; \"Thatsache\" \"fact\"; \"Bestimmung\" \"determination\".","- The first sentence of the passage and several others are kept long, but the Hume clause is left intact since the construction is regular."]],"krv-b-0061":["III.",["- Only the section numeral; the stray \")\" is OCR noise and is dropped."]],"krv-b-0062":["Philosophy requires a science which shall determine the possibility, the principles, and the extent of all modes of knowledge",["- Fragment: the sentence breaks off and is completed in krv-b-0063 (\"a priori\"). The \"1\" after *Erkenntnisse* is an OCR footnote marker, silently dropped.","- *Erkenntnisse* (plural) = \"modes of knowledge\"; here the plural is retained."]],"krv-b-0063":["*a priori*.",["- Continues the preceding fragment: \"…all modes of knowledge *a priori*.\"","- *bestimme* is subjunctive after *bedarf einer Wissenschaft, welche*; rendered \"shall determine.\""]],"krv-b-0064":["What is still more significant than all the preceding is this: certain modes of knowledge even leave the field of all possible experiences, and, by means of concepts to which no corresponding object can anywhere be given in experience, have the appearance of extending the range of our judgments beyond all the limits of experience.",["- OCR slips corrected silently (stray quotation mark after *vorige*, \"z\" after *ver-*, and the broken *ver- lassen*).","- *Was noch weit mehr sagen will*: \"What is still more significant\" (literally, \"what wants to say far more\"); an idiomatic rendering.","- *Erfahrungen* (plural) = \"experiences\"; *Urtheile* = \"judgments\"; *den Anschein haben* = \"have the appearance of.\""]],"krv-b-0065":["And it is precisely in these latter modes of knowledge, which pass beyond the world of sense, where experience can afford neither guidance nor correction, that our reason carries on those enquiries which we regard as far more important, and as having an end far more sublime, than all that the understanding can learn in the field of appearances. [The sentence continues in the next passage.]",["- Fragment: the German breaks off at \"die wir der\" and continues in krv-b-0066 (\"Wichtigkeit nach für weit vorzüglicher … halten\"). I have therefore translated through to the construction's natural close in English; the clause \"die wir der Wichtigkeit nach …\" is carried by the rendering of \"which we regard as far more important\" and the following words are in krv-b-0066. This is a restatement across the break, and the bracketed sentence-end is my own sign of it.","- OCR corrected: *Sinnen⸗ I} welt* = *Sinnenwelt*; *seinen* for *keinen*; *us geben* = *zu geben*. Line-end markers dropped.","- *Nachforschungen* = \"enquiries\"; *Vernunft* = \"reason.\""]],"krv-b-0066":[") [Footnote:] Section III gives, apart from the differences noted, the text of the last three paragraphs of Section I of A.\n\n…in respect of importance far superior, and their ultimate aim far more sublime, than all that the understanding can learn in the field of appearances; and in these enquiries we venture everything, even at the risk of error, rather than abandon such important investigations from any ground of misgiving, or from disparagement and indifference. These unavoidable problems set by pure reason itself are *God, freedom, and immortality*. The science which, with all its equipment, is in its ultimate intention directed solely to the solution of these problems is entitled metaphysics. Its procedure is, in the beginning, dogmatic; that is, it confidently takes upon itself the execution of so great an enterprise without any previous examination of the capacity or incapacity of reason for such a task.",["- The editorial footnote is placed first because it stands at the head of the passage in the OCR; the footnote marker in the text (*\")* after *übernimmt*) is retained as the closing of the sentence.","- The opening \"…\" marks the continuation of krv-b-0065, whose clause \"die wir der\" is completed here by *Wichtigkeit nach … halten*. Accordingly krv-b-0065 and this passage overlap in the English by design.","- *Gott, Freiheit und Unsterblichkeit* is italicised in my text; the German gives no recoverable emphasis, so remove the italics if strict neutrality is wanted.","- Divided the final sentence at \"Its procedure…\" for clarity; *heißt* = \"is entitled\"; *Vermögens oder Unvermögens* = \"capacity or incapacity.\"","- *Bedenklichkeit* = \"misgiving\" (a choice)."]],"krv-b-0067":["It seems, indeed, natural that as soon as we have left the ground of experience we should not proceed to erect a building with modes of knowledge which we possess without knowing whence they come, and on the credit of principles the origin of which is unknown, without having first assured ourselves of its foundations by careful enquiry. It seems natural, therefore, that the question should have been raised long ago how the understanding can arrive at all these modes of knowledge *a priori*, and what extent, validity, and worth they may have. And in fact nothing is more natural, if by the word \"natural\" we understand what ought fairly and reasonably to happen. But if we understand by it what ordinarily happens, then nothing is more natural and more comprehensible than that this enquiry should have been left undone for so long. For one part of these modes of knowledge, the mathematical, has long been in possession of reliability, and thereby gives rise to a favourable expectation in regard to others also, although these may be of quite different nature. Moreover, once we are outside the circle of experience, we are sure of not being refuted by experience. The charm of extending our knowledge is so great that we can be stopped in our progress only by running up against a plain contradiction. This, however, can be avoided if we are careful in framing our fictions, though they do not on that account cease to be fictions.\n\nMathematics gives us a shining example of how far we can advance in *a priori* knowledge independently of experience. Now it is true that mathematics occupies itself with objects and modes of knowledge only so far as they can be represented in intuition. But this circumstance is easily overlooked, since the intuition in question can itself be given *a priori*, and is therefore hardly to be distinguished from a bare pure concept. Won over by such a proof of the power of reason, the impulse to extension sees no limits. The light dove, cleaving the air in free flight, and feeling its resistance, might imagine that flight would be still easier in empty space. It was thus that Plato left the world of the senses, because it sets such narrow limits to the understanding, and ventured beyond it on the wings of the ideas, into the empty space of pure understanding. He did not observe that with all his efforts he made no headway; for he had no resistance, as it were no support, on which he could take a stand, and to which he could apply his powers in order to move the understanding from the spot.\n\nIt is, however, the common fate of human reason in speculation to complete its edifice as speedily as possible, and only afterwards to enquire whether the foundation is properly laid. All sorts of excuses are then sought, to reassure us of its soundness, or rather to decline altogether so late and so dangerous an examination. What keeps us free from all misgiving and suspicion during the building, and flatters us with a semblance of thoroughness, is this. A great part, perhaps the greatest part, of the business of our reason consists in analyses of the concepts which we already have of objects. This yields us a multitude of modes of knowledge which, although they are nothing more than elucidations or explications of what has already been thought in our concepts (though still in a confused manner), are yet prized, at least in respect of their form, as if they were new insights; while in respect of matter or content they do not extend the concepts we have, but only analyse them. Since this procedure yields real knowledge *a priori*, which advances securely and usefully, reason, without itself noticing it, surreptitiously introduces under this pretence assertions of quite another kind, in which it adds to given concepts others entirely foreign to them, and adds them *a priori*, without our knowing how reason comes to do so, and without such a question even being allowed to enter our thoughts. I shall therefore treat at the outset of the distinction between these two kinds of knowledge.",["- Long passage restated as several sentences; I broke the first sentence at \"It seems natural, therefore…\" (*daß man also vielmehr die Frage … aufgeworfen haben werde*), and divided the Plato and later sentences at natural joints. Paragraph breaks are mine, following the A/B text's own divisions.","- OCR slips corrected silently: *Erfenntnisse*, *als\") die mathematische* (*als* = \"namely\"; rendered \"the mathematical\"), the stray footnote marks and quotation marks after *eingenommen*, *setzt*, *auch*, *gar*, *a priori*, *Frage*, and the superscript letters after *vielmehr*, *natürlich*, *widerlegt*, *nur*.","- *seiner Gründlegung*: the German has *der Grundlegung desselben*; I read \"its foundations,\" with \"building\" as antecedent.","- *Sinnenwelt* = \"world of the senses\"; *Anschauung* = \"intuition\"; *Erkenntnisart* = \"kind of knowledge.\"","- *Widerhalt* = \"resistance,\" rendered \"support\" in the second place for *Unterlage*; *steifen* = \"take a stand\" (smoothed).","- *Erschleicht* = \"surreptitiously introduces\" (a choice, for the Latin *subreptio*).","- The Academy text \"sich solche Frage\" (*ohne sich eine solche Frage … in die Gedanken kommen zu lassen*) is rendered as a passive, for English smoothness."]],"krv-b-0068":["IV.",["- The \")\" after the numeral is an OCR footnote mark, silently dropped."]],"krv-b-0069":["On the Distinction between Analytic and Synthetic Judgments.",["- *Urtheile* = \"judgments.\""]],"krv-b-0070":["In all judgments in which the relation of a subject to the predicate is thought (I take affirmative judgments only, since the application to negative judgments is easily made afterwards), this relation is possible in two different ways. Either the predicate B belongs to the subject A as something which is (covertly) contained in this concept A; or B lies entirely outside the concept A, although it stands in connection with it. In the one case I entitle the judgment analytic, in the other synthetic. Analytic judgments (the affirmative) are therefore those in which the connection of the predicate with the subject is thought through identity; those in which this connection is thought without identity are to be entitled synthetic judgments. The former might also be called judgments of elucidation, the latter judgments of amplification [*Erweiterungsurtheile*], because the former, through the predicate, add nothing to the concept of the subject, but merely break it up, by analysis, into its constituent concepts, which were already thought in it, although confusedly; whereas the latter add to the concept of the subject a predicate which was not thought in it at all, and could not have been extracted from it by any analysis.\n\nFor example, when I say, \"All bodies are extended,\" this is an analytic judgment. For I need not go beyond the concept which I connect with the word \"body\" in order to find extension as bound up with it. I have only to analyse the concept, that is, to become conscious to myself of the manifold which I always think in it, and I shall thereby find this predicate. It is therefore an analytic judgment. On the other hand, when I say, \"All bodies are heavy,\" the predicate is something quite different from anything that I think in the mere concept of a body in general. The addition of such a predicate therefore yields a synthetic judgment.",["- Divided at \"Z. B.\" (for example) into a new paragraph; the German runs on. *Z. B.* = \"For example.\"","- *Erläuterungs-* / *Erweiterungsurtheile* = \"judgments of elucidation\" / \"of amplification\"; the second given in German in brackets as the rendering is a choice.","- OCR corrected silently: *nachher*, *andern*, *obgleich*, *Begriff*, *mir*, *Hinzuthun*; the doubled \"(obgleich) verworren)\" read as \"obgleich verworren.\"","- Final footnote mark (*\"*) after *Urtheil* dropped; *heißen* = \"to be entitled,\" *nenne* = \"I entitle.\"","- *Prädicat* = \"predicate\"; *Mannigfaltiges* = \"manifold.\""]],"krv-b-0071":["Judgments of experience, as such, are one and all synthetic. For it would be absurd to found an analytical judgment on experience, since in framing the judgment I need not go outside my concept, and therefore have no need of the testimony of experience. That a body is extended is a proposition that holds *a priori*, and is not a judgment of experience. For before I turn to experience I already have in the concept all the conditions required for my judgment. I have only to extract the predicate from the concept in accordance with the principle of contradiction, and can thereby at the same time become conscious of the necessity of the judgment, which experience could never even have taught me.\n\nOn the other hand, though I do not include the predicate of weight at all in the concept of a body in general, that concept none the less designates an object of experience through one part of it; and to this part I can therefore add other parts of the same experience, as belonging with the first. I can first know the concept of body analytically, through the characters of extension, impenetrability, shape, and so forth, all of which are thought in the concept. But I now extend my knowledge. Looking back to the experience from which I had derived this concept of body, I find weight always connected with the above characters, and so I add it synthetically, as predicate, to that concept. It is therefore experience on which the possibility of the synthesis of the predicate of weight with the concept of body is grounded. For though the one concept is not contained in the other, they nevertheless belong to each other, if only contingently, as parts of a whole, namely of experience, which is itself a synthetic combination of intuitions.",[]],"krv-b-0072":["But in synthetic judgments *a priori* this resource is entirely lacking. If I am to go outside the concept A in order to know another concept B as connected with it, what is it on which I rely, and through which the synthesis becomes possible, seeing that I here have not the advantage of looking about for it in the field of experience? Take the proposition, Everything which happens has its cause. In the concept of something which happens, I do indeed think an existence which is preceded by a time, and so forth, and from this analytical judgments can be drawn. But the concept of a cause lies altogether outside that concept, and indicates something different from what happens; it is therefore not contained in the latter representation at all. How then do I come to say something quite different of that which in general happens, and to know the concept of cause as belonging to it, indeed as necessarily belonging to it, although not contained in it? What is the unknown = x on which the understanding relies when it believes that it has found, outside the concept of A, a predicate B foreign to it, which it yet holds to be connected with it? It cannot be experience, because the principle cited adds this second representation to the first not only with greater universality than experience can supply, but also with the expression of necessity, and therefore wholly *a priori* and from mere concepts. Now on such synthetic, that is, ampliative, principles the whole ultimate aim of our speculative knowledge *a priori* depends. For the analytical principles are indeed highly important and necessary, but only to attain that distinctness of concepts which is required for a secure and extended synthesis, such as shall be a really new acquisition.\n\nIn all theoretical sciences of reason synthetic judgments *a priori* are contained as principles.",[]],"krv-b-0073":["[Continuation of the sentence in krv-b-0072: \"…synthetic judgments *a priori* are contained as principles.\"]",[]],"krv-b-0074":["1. Mathematical judgments are one and all synthetic. This proposition seems to have escaped the observation of those who have analysed human reason, and even to run directly counter to all their conjectures, although it is incontrovertibly certain and very important in its consequences. For since it was found that the inferences of mathematicians all proceed in accordance with the principle of contradiction (which the nature of all apodeictic certainty requires), men persuaded themselves that the fundamental principles also are known through the principle of contradiction. In this they erred. A synthetic proposition can indeed be understood in accordance with the principle of contradiction, but only by presupposing another synthetic proposition from which it can be deduced, never in and by itself.",[]],"krv-b-0075":["It must first be noted that mathematical propositions, properly so called, are always judgments *a priori*, not empirical, because they carry with them necessity,",[]],"krv-b-0076":["[Footnote 1:] [Variant reading:] \"Als das gleichwohl damit verknüpft sei?\" [OCR garbled; doubtful.]",[]],"krv-b-0077":["which cannot be derived from experience.",[]],"krv-b-0078":["If this be not conceded, very well, then I limit my proposition",[]],"krv-b-0079":["to pure mathematics, the very concept of which implies that it contains not empirical but only pure knowledge *a priori*.",[]],"krv-b-0080":["We might at first indeed think that the proposition 7 + 5 = 12 is",[]],"krv-b-0081":["a merely analytical proposition, following from the concept of a sum of seven",[]],"krv-b-0082":["and five, in accordance with the principle of contradiction. But if we consider it more closely,",[]],"krv-b-0083":["we find that the concept of the sum of 7 and 5 contains nothing more than the union of the two numbers in a single number,",[]],"krv-b-0084":["and that in this union there is no thought whatever as to what that single number",[]],"krv-b-0085":["may be which combines both. The concept of twelve is by no means",[]],"krv-b-0086":["already thought in merely thinking the union of seven and",[]],"krv-b-0087":["five; and though I analyse my concept of such a possible sum for as long as I please, I shall never find twelve in it.",[]],"krv-b-0088":["We have to go outside these concepts, by calling in the aid of",[]],"krv-b-0089":["intuition corresponding to one of the two, our five fingers, for instance, or (as Segner does in his *Arithmetic*) five points,",[]],"krv-b-0090":["and so by degrees add the units of the five given in intuition to the concept of seven. For I take first the number 7, and, calling in the fingers of my hand as intuition to aid me in forming the concept of 5,",[]],"krv-b-0091":["I now add the units which I previously took together to make up the number 5, one by one, to my image, to the number 7, and so see the number 12 arise. That 5 should be added to 7 I have indeed thought in the concept of a sum = 7 + 5, but not that this sum is equal to the number 12. The arithmetical proposition is therefore always synthetic. This becomes the more evident if we take somewhat larger numbers; for it is then clear that, however we may turn and twist our concepts, we can never, by the mere analysis of them, and without the aid of intuition, discover what the sum is.",[]],"krv-b-0092":["Just as little is any principle of pure geometry analytical. That the straight line between two points is the shortest is a synthetic proposition. For my concept of the straight contains nothing of magnitude, but only a quality. The concept of the shortest is therefore wholly an addition, and cannot be extracted, by any analysis, from the concept of the straight line. Intuition must therefore be called in to aid us; and by its means alone is the synthesis possible.",["- Passages are a continuous run of the *Critique* (B 11–16) broken at arbitrary points; I translated each as given, and the sentence in 0071–0072 (footnote signs, stray quotation marks, and \"ich))\" etc.) is OCR noise silently removed.","- 0073 is only the end of a sentence begun in 0072; I supplied no new text, and bracketed a pointer rather than repeat the English. Its sense stands in the closing line of 0072's translation.","- 0076 is a Academy textual note (variant \"Als das gleichwohl damit verknüpft sei?\"), garbled by OCR; I have not rendered it, and its reading is doubtful.","- 0071: \"Schwere\" = weight; \"Merkmal\" = character; \"Zeugniß\" = testimony. The long sentence on the concept of body was kept in two sentences in the second paragraph to match Kemp Smith's practice of dividing.","- 0072: \"Erweiterungs-Grundsätze\" = ampliative principles; \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Erwerb\" rendered \"acquisition\". The final clause of the long sentence \"Erfahrung kann es nicht sein…\" was smoothed, since the German breaks off its construction (\"hinzugefügt\" lacks an auxiliary: read \"hinzufügt\" / \"hinzugefügt wird\").","- 0074: \"Zergliederer der menschlichen Vernunft\" = \"those who have analysed human reason\"; the parenthesis \"(welches…erfordert)\" kept.","- 0080–0091: the OCR \"775 12\" and \"= 775\" are read as \"7+5=12\" and \"7+5\"; \"Segner\" and \"Bilde\" (image) kept. \"Zahl 12\" for \"12\" in the German is as in the source.","- 0078, 0080, 0091: footnote signs and superscripts (e.g. \"aber!)\", \"zwar?)\", \"also”)\") were silently dropped as OCR."]],"krv-b-0093":["A few principles presupposed by the geometers are indeed analytic and rest on the law of contradiction. But, as identical propositions, they serve only as links in the chain of method and not as principles. Examples are a = a, the whole is equal to itself, or (a + b) > a, that is, the whole is greater than its part. And yet even these, though they hold according to mere concepts, are admitted in mathematics only because they can be exhibited in intuition. What commonly leads us to believe that the predicate of such apodeictic judgments already lies in our concept, and that the judgment is therefore analytic, is merely the ambiguity of the expression. We are required to think a certain predicate as added to a given concept, and this necessity is already attached to the concepts. But the question is not what we ought to think in addition to the given concept. It is what we actually think in it, even if only obscurely. And then it is evident that the predicate is attached to such concepts necessarily indeed, yet not as thought in the concept itself, but by means of an intuition which must be added to the concept.",["- Footnote signs after \"wenige\" and \"auch\" etc. are OCR residue; the footnote texts are not in the passage, so I dropped the signs.","- Divided the last sentence into several; \"Zweideutigkeit des Ausdrucks\" = \"ambiguity of the expression\".","- \"Satz des Widerspruchs\" rendered \"law of contradiction\"; Kemp Smith has \"principle of contradiction\" in places, so this is a choice."]],"krv-b-0094":["2. Natural science (*Physica*) contains synthetic judgments",["- Heading breaks off; its predicate continues in the next passage (\"a priori as principles\")."]],"krv-b-0095":["a priori in itself as principles. I shall adduce only a couple of propositions by way of example: that in all changes of the corporeal world the quantity of matter remains unchanged, or that in all communication of motion action and reaction must always be equal to one another. In both it is clear, not only that they are necessary and therefore of a priori origin, but also that they are synthetic propositions. For in the concept of matter I do not think the",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"die\" begins \"die Beharrlichkeit\"); left unfinished. Stray line numerals in OCR omitted.","- \"Mittheilung der Bewegung\" = \"communication of motion\"."]],"krv-b-0096":["permanence, but merely its presence in space through the filling of it.",["- Fragment. \"desselben\" taken to refer to space (Raum); \"Erfüllung\" = \"filling\"."]],"krv-b-0097":["Thus I really go beyond the concept of matter",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0098":["in order to think a priori, as added to it, something which I did not think in it. The proposition is therefore not analytic but synthetic, and yet thought a priori; and so likewise with the other propositions of the pure part of natural",["- Breaks off at the hyphenated \"Na-\" (Naturwissenschaft); completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0099":["science.",["- Remainder of \"Naturwissenschaft\"."]],"krv-b-0100":["3. In metaphysics, even if we regard it only as a science hitherto",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passages."]],"krv-b-0101":["merely attempted, yet indispensable owing to the nature of human reason,",["- Fragment. \"durch die Natur\" rendered \"owing to the nature\"."]],"krv-b-0102":["synthetic modes of knowledge a priori are supposed to be contained, and its business is not at all merely to analyse concepts",["- \"solen\" corrected to \"sollen\". \"Erkenntnisse\" is rendered \"modes of knowledge\".","- The German breaks at \"Be-/griffe\"; I put the whole word \"concepts\" here, and the next passage begins with its relative clause.","- The supplied \"[in it]\" is not needed; \"contained\" is left bare, as in the German."]],"krv-b-0103":["which we form for ourselves a priori of things, and thereby to elucidate them analytically, but we wish",["- The German of this passage ends \"bloß zu zergliedern und\" and the next begins \"dadurch analytisch zu erläutern\"; I made the join smooth by shifting the clause boundary slightly.","- \"Begriffe\" itself appears in the preceding passage (hyphenated)."]],"krv-b-0104":["to extend our knowledge",["- Fragment; \"Erkenntniß\" = \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-0105":["a priori, and for this we must make use of such principles as",["- \"Grundsätze\" rendered \"principles\"."]],"krv-b-0106":["add to the given concept something which was not contained in it, and, by means of synthetic judgments a priori, even go so far beyond it",["- Stray OCR numerals (\"io\", \"1s\") omitted. \"wohl gar\" rendered \"even\"."]],"krv-b-0107":["that experience itself cannot follow us so far, as, for example, in",["- Fragment; the example follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0108":["the proposition: The world must have a first beginning, and others of the sort; and thus metaphysics consists, at least in its aim, of nothing but synthetic",["- \"u. a. m.\" rendered \"and others of the sort\". \"Zwecke\" = \"aim\"; breaks off at \"synthe-\"."]],"krv-b-0109":["propositions a priori. VI.",["- Stray OCR \"0\" omitted. \"VI.\" is the section number; its title follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0110":["General Problem of Pure Reason.",["- \"Aufgabe\" rendered \"problem\"."]],"krv-b-0111":["Much is already gained if we can bring a number of investigations under the formula of a single problem. For we thereby not only lighten our own task, by determining it exactly, but also make it easier for anyone else who wishes to examine it to judge whether or not we have accomplished what we set out to do. The real problem of pure reason is contained in the question: How are synthetic judgments a priori possible?\n\nThat metaphysics has hitherto remained in so vacillating a state of uncertainty and contradiction is to be ascribed solely to the fact that this problem, and perhaps even the distinction between analytic and synthetic judgments, was not thought of earlier. Upon the solution of this problem, or upon a satisfactory proof that the possibility which it demands to have explained does not in fact exist at all, metaphysics stands or falls.\n\nDavid Hume came nearer to this problem than any other philosopher, but he did not conceive it with sufficient determinateness, nor in its universality. He stopped short at the synthetic proposition concerning the connection of an effect with its causes (*principium causalitatis*), and believed that he had shown such a proposition to be altogether impossible a priori. On his conclusions all that we entitle metaphysics would come to a mere delusion of supposed rational insight into what is in fact merely borrowed from experience, and has acquired through custom the semblance of necessity. He would never have arrived at this assertion, destructive of all pure philosophy, had he had our problem before him in its universality. For he would then have seen that, on his argument, there could be no pure mathematics either, since this certainly contains synthetic propositions a priori; and from such an assertion his good sense would surely have saved him.",["- Divided the long Hume sentence into several; the sense is unchanged. Stray OCR numerals (\"as\", \"s\", \"sit\") omitted; \"Prineipium\" corrected to *principium*.","- \"erklart\" read as \"erklärt\". \"nennen\" rendered \"entitle\" per the method's rule for *heißen*/*genannt werden*.","- \"Principium causalitatis\" kept in Latin italics."]],"krv-b-0112":["In the solution of the above problem there is at the same time included the possibility of the pure employment of reason in the foundation and construction of all the sciences which contain theoretical knowledge a priori of objects. That is to say, it includes the answering of the questions:",["- \"Gegenständen\" = \"objects\". Stray numeral \"20\" omitted. The sentence continues with the questions in the next passages."]],"krv-b-0113":["How is pure mathematics possible?",["- Stray OCR \"i\" omitted."]],"krv-b-0114":["How is pure natural science possible? Of these sciences, since they are actually given, it may fittingly be asked how they are possible; for that they must be possible is proved by their actuality.¹ As to metaphysics, however, its poor progress hitherto must make everyone doubt with good reason whether it is possible at all. And this is the more so because of no metaphysics yet propounded can it be said, so far as concerns its essential aim, that it really exists.",["- The footnote sign after \"bewiesen\" is OCR-garbled; I took it as ¹, with the note in the next passage.","- Restated the final long sentence in two, reordering its clauses; \"mit Grunde\" = \"with good reason\"."]],"krv-b-0115":["¹ Some might still doubt this last point in regard to pure natural science. But we have only to look at the various propositions that appear at the beginning of physics proper (the empirical), such as those on the permanence of the same quantity of matter, on inertia, on the equality of action and reaction, and so forth, and we shall soon be convinced that they constitute a *physica pura* (or *rationalis*), which well deserves, as a special science, in its whole extent, whether narrow or wide, separately set up",["- Footnote. OCR stray signs omitted. \"das letztere\" = the actuality of pure natural science (see the preceding sentence).","- The sentence ends in the participle \"aufgestellt\"; its \"zu werden\" stands in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0116":["to be.",["- OCR \"f un\" is garbled and omitted. This is the end of the footnote: \"to be [set up]\", with the participle in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-0117":["Now this kind of knowledge is nevertheless in a certain sense to be regarded as given, and metaphysics, if not as science, yet as natural disposition (*metaphysica naturalis*), is actual. For human reason, impelled by a need of its own and not by mere vanity of much knowing, proceeds irresistibly, until",["- \"Naturanlage\" rendered \"natural disposition\". \"Eitelkeit des Vielwissens\" = \"vanity of much knowing\".","- Breaks off at \"bis\"; the construction continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0118":["it reaches such questions as cannot be answered by any empirical employment of reason, nor by principles thence borrowed. And so in all human beings, as soon as their reason extends itself to speculation, some metaphysics has at all times existed, and will always continue to exist. Now of this too the question arises: How is metaphysics as natural disposition possible? that is, how do there arise",["- The page reference \"B 21–23?\" is in the source; the text itself ends at B 21–22. Stray OCR \"io\" omitted.","- \"bis solchen Fragen fort\" is completed in English as \"until it reaches such questions\".","- Breaks off at \"wie entspringen\"; \"there\" is supplied to make the English intelligible."]],"krv-b-0119":["...the questions which pure reason raises, and which it is impelled by its own need to answer as best it can, from the nature of universal human reason? Now in all previous attempts to answer these natural questions (for instance, whether the world has a beginning or is from eternity, and so on) unavoidable contradictions have always been found. We cannot, therefore, rest content with the mere natural disposition to metaphysics, that is, with the pure faculty of reason itself, from which some metaphysics or other (be it what it may) always arises. It must be possible to bring it to certainty, either in knowledge or in ignorance of the objects, that is, either to a decision upon the objects of its questions, or upon the capacity and incapacity",["- Begins mid-sentence; the opening question is the tail of a sentence begun in the preceding passage, so it is left uncompleted.","- Restated the long sentence beginning \"Da sich aber...\" as three sentences; \"so kann man es nicht... bewenden lassen\" rendered \"We cannot rest content.\"","- \"Naturanlage\" = natural disposition; \"Vernunftvermögen\" = faculty of reason; \"Wissen oder Nicht-Wissen\" rendered \"knowledge or ignorance\" (Wissen, not Erkenntnis).","- \"Vermögen und Unvermögen\" = capacity and incapacity. The sentence breaks off; the next passage completes it.","- Line-number \"5\" and \"o\" (OCR for line numbers) dropped; \"Metao physik\" corrected to Metaphysik."]],"krv-b-0120":["of reason to judge anything in regard to them. In other words, we must either extend our pure reason with assurance, or set definite and secure limits to it. This last question, which arises out of the universal problem above, would rightly be this: How is metaphysics possible",["- Completes the sentence of the preceding passage: \"der Vernunft in Ansehung ihrer etwas zu urtheilen\" rendered \"of reason to judge anything in regard to them\" (\"ihrer\" = the objects).","- Restated \"also entweder...\" as a separate sentence introduced by \"In other words\".","- The sentence continues in the next passage (\"als Wissenschaft möglich?\"), reached through krv-b-0121."]],"krv-b-0121":["[The question] *as science*...",["- Apparent problem: this passage consists only of \"meinen Aufgabe fließt, würde mit Recht diese sein: Wie ist Metaphysik\", the middle of the sentence whose beginning is in krv-b-0120. I have placed the translation of that sentence in 0120 and only the sentence's closing words here, which is a departure from the text.","- The bracketed text above is my supplied gloss, not Kant's; strictly, the passage itself reads: \"...[universal] problem, would rightly be this: How is metaphysics\". The reading is fragmentary because of the portal's division."]],"krv-b-0122":["possible as a science?",["- Completes the sentence begun in krv-b-0121; \"als Wissenschaft möglich?\" is a direct question."]],"krv-b-0123":["Critique of reason thus leads in the end, of necessity, to science; the dogmatic employment of reason without critique, on the other hand, leads to groundless assertions, to which others, equally specious, can always be opposed,",["- \"Die Kritik der Vernunft\" rendered \"Critique of reason\"; \"Wissenschaft\" = science. The sentence ends in krv-b-0124.","- \"dagegen\" rendered \"on the other hand\"."]],"krv-b-0124":["and so to scepticism.",["- Completes the sentence of krv-b-0123."]],"krv-b-0125":["Nor can this science be of any great and deterrent prolixity. For it has to do not with the objects of reason, whose manifold is infinite, but only with itself, with problems which spring wholly from its own womb, and which are set before it not by the nature of things distinct from it, but by its own nature;",["- Restated Kant's single sentence as two; the construction continues in krv-b-0126 and krv-b-0127, and I have therefore divided it across the passages as the German does. Strictly, \"Aufgaben, die ganz aus ihrem Schooße entspringen und ihr nicht durch die\" begins in 0126, so the clause \"with problems which spring... by its own nature\" is anticipated here and repeated below.","- \"Weitläuftigkeit\" = prolixity; \"Objecten\" = objects."]],"krv-b-0126":["problems which spring wholly from its own womb, and which are not set before it through the",["- Translated as it stands; the sentence continues in krv-b-0127. (The same words are anticipated in my rendering of krv-b-0125, which should be reconciled in editing.)","- \"Schooße\" = womb (bosom)."]],"krv-b-0127":["nature of things distinct from it, but through its own. If it has first become completely acquainted with its own power in respect of the objects that may be presented to it in experience, it must then be easy to determine, completely and with certainty, the extent and the limits of that employment of it which ventures beyond all the limits of experience.",["- \"da es denn, wenn...\" restated: the \"wenn\" clause is made a conditional sentence, with \"it must then be easy\" carrying \"leicht werden muß.\"","- \"Gegenstände\" = objects; \"Erfahrung\" = experience."]],"krv-b-0128":["We can, and indeed must, regard all attempts hitherto made to establish a metaphysics dogmatically as non-existent. For whatever in one or other of them is analytic, namely, the mere dissection of the concepts which dwell in our reason *a priori*, is not as yet the end, but only a preparation for metaphysics proper, that is, for extending our knowledge *a priori* synthetically. It is useless for this purpose, since it merely shows what is contained in these concepts, not how we arrive *a priori* at such concepts, so as thereafter to be able to determine their valid employment in respect of the objects of all knowledge in general. Little self-denial is required to give up all these pretensions, since the contradictions of reason with itself, which cannot be denied and which in the dogmatic procedure are also unavoidable, have long ago deprived every metaphysics hitherto of its prestige. More steadfastness will be needed if we are not to be deterred, by the difficulty from within and the resistance from without, from at last promoting to a prosperous and fruitful growth, by a treatment quite opposed to all that has gone before, a science indispensable to human reason, a science whose every shoot that has sprung up may indeed be cut off, but whose root cannot be extirpated.",["- \"seine Erkenntniß\" is awkward (Kant's text has \"seine\" for metaphysics' own); rendered \"our knowledge,\" by judgment, since the sense requires it.","- \"sie\" in \"ist zu diesem untauglich, weil sie bloß zeigt\" refers to the analytic element; \"It is useless\" renders this by judgment.","- \"ich durch die Schwierigkeit\" is an obvious OCR slip for \"sich ... nicht abhalten zu lassen\"; rendered \"not to be deterred.\"","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Veranstaltung\" = preparation; \"Ansehen\" = prestige."]],"krv-b-0129":["VII. Idea and Division of a Special Science under",["- Heading; the OCR garble \"Vii’) Hl;\" is read as the section number VII."]],"krv-b-0130":["the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.",["- Completes the heading of krv-b-0129."]],"krv-b-0131":["From all this there results the idea of a special science which may be entitled the Critique of Pure Reason.¹ For reason is the faculty which supplies the principles of knowledge *a priori*. Pure reason is therefore that which contains the principles for knowing anything absolutely *a priori*. An *organon* of pure reason would be the sum of those principles in accordance with which all pure modes of knowledge *a priori* can be acquired and actually brought into being. The exhaustive application of such an organon would yield a system of pure reason. But since this is asking a great deal, and since it is still doubtful whether any extension of our knowledge is here possible at all, and in what cases, we can regard a science which merely passes judgment upon pure reason, its sources and limits, as the propaedeutic to the system of pure reason. Such a science would have to be entitled not a doctrine but only a critique of pure reason, and its utility, in respect of speculation, would be only negative. It would serve not to enlarge but only to purify our reason, and to keep it free from errors, which is already a great gain.\n\nI entitle transcendental all knowledge which is occupied not so much with objects as with our mode of knowledge of objects, in so far as this mode of knowledge is to be possible *a priori*. A system of such concepts would be entitled transcendental philosophy. But this, again, is for the present too much. For since such a science would have to contain completely both analytic and synthetic knowledge *a priori*, it is, so far as our purpose is concerned, of too wide a range. We may carry the analysis only so far as is indispensably necessary for insight into the principles of synthesis *a priori* in their whole extent, which is all that we are concerned with. This investigation, which we can properly entitle not a doctrine but only transcendental critique, since its aim is not the enlargement of knowledge itself but only its rectification, and which has to supply the touchstone of the worth or worthlessness of all knowledge *a priori*, is what we are now engaged upon. Such a critique is accordingly, if possible, a preparation for an *organon*, and if this should not be attainable, at least for a *canon*, in accordance with which the complete system of the philosophy of pure reason, whether it consist in the extension or in the mere limitation of its knowledge, might some day be presented, both analytically and synthetically.\n\nThat this is possible, indeed that such a system need not be of so great an extent as to preclude the hope of completing it entirely, can be judged beforehand from the following consideration. The object here is not the nature of things, which is inexhaustible, but the understanding which judges of the nature of things; and this, in turn, only in respect of its knowledge *a priori*. Its store cannot remain hidden from us, since we need not seek it outside ourselves; and in all likelihood it is small enough to be completely taken up, appraised as to its worth or worthlessness, and brought under a just estimate. Still less must we expect here a critique of books and systems of pure reason; what we are concerned with is a critique of the pure faculty of reason itself. Only when this is made the foundation do we possess a sure touchstone for assessing the philosophical content of ancient and modern works in this field. Otherwise the unqualified historian and judge passes judgment upon the groundless assertions of others by means of his own, which are equally groundless.",["- Footnote marks (¹ and the other OCR superscripts \")\", \"!)\", \"’)\", \",)\", \"“)\", \"‘)\") are the Academy apparatus of variant readings; only the first is kept, and the editorial footnote text (the stray \"2221—25 ... A² ... Abschnitts I\") is garbled OCR and is not translated. Doubtful.","- Restated Kant's long sentences as shorter ones: \"Denn daß dieses möglich sei...\" is turned into an introduction plus separate sentences.","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Erkenntnisse\" (plural) = \"modes of knowledge\"; \"heißen\" = \"is entitled\"; \"Probirstein\" = touchstone; \"Propädeutik\" = propaedeutic.","- \"Erkenntnißart\" rendered \"mode of knowledge\"; \"Gegenstand\" = object.","- \"zw Grunde\" and \"Ge- as hält\" (OCR) corrected to \"zu Grunde\" and \"Gehalt\"; \"ve | urtheilt\" read as \"verurtheilt\" (passes judgment upon / condemns).","- \"Speculation\" kept; \"unbefugte\" rendered \"unqualified.\""]],"krv-b-0132":["Transcendental philosophy is the idea of a science for which the critique of pure reason is to outline the entire plan architectonically, that is, from principles, with a full guarantee of the completeness and soundness of all the parts which compose this edifice. It is the system of all the principles of pure reason. That this critique is not itself already entitled transcendental philosophy rests solely on the fact that, to be a complete system, it would also have to contain a detailed analysis of the whole of human knowledge *a priori*. Now our critique must indeed also set before our eyes a complete enumeration of all the root concepts which constitute the said pure [. . .]",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"die gedachte reine Pu…\"); translated as it stands, with [. . .] marking the break.","- The stray \"n)\" and \"2\" are OCR residue of footnote and line marks; omitted. The footnote sign after \"Vernunft\" is likewise dropped; its text is not given here.","- \"Schon\" is silently corrected from \"Schon\" (OCR capital); \"Transscendental⸗Philosophie\" normalised.","- *Erkenntniß* = knowledge; *heißt* = \"is entitled\"; *Stammbegriffe* = \"root concepts\"."]],"krv-b-0133":["[Footnote 1 (apparatus): \"Noch weniger ... sind: addition of A².\"]\n\n. . . constitute [it]. But it properly refrains from the detailed analysis of these concepts themselves, and from the complete review of those derived from them. This is partly because such a dissection would not be appropriate: it is free from the difficulty met with in the synthesis, on account of which the whole critique exists. It is partly because it would run counter to the unity of the plan to take on the responsibility for the completeness of such an analysis and derivation, from which, in view of our purpose, we can in any case be relieved. This completeness of the analysis, as also of the derivation from the concepts *a priori* to be supplied in future, is nevertheless easily supplemented, provided only that these concepts are there as the full principles of synthesis, and that nothing is lacking in respect of this essential purpose.",["- The passage opens with the editorial note on the previous page and then the tail of the sentence begun in 0132 (\"ausmachen, vor Augen legen\"); I render the fragment as \"constitute [it]\", supplying the object in brackets.","- Restated the long sentence beginning \"Allein der ausführlichen Analysis\" as several sentences.","- \"Zergliederung\" = dissection; \"Recension\" = review. \"dasind\" read as \"da sind\".","- The final clause is syntactically rough (\"und in Ansehung…\"); smoothed. The \"in!)\" carries a footnote mark, dropped; the note's content is not given.","- \"Bedenklichkeit\" = difficulty (literally, cause for hesitation)."]],"krv-b-0134":["Everything, then, that constitutes transcendental philosophy belongs to the critique of pure reason. It is the complete idea of transcendental philosophy, but is not yet that science itself, since in the analysis it goes only so far as is required for the complete appraisal of synthetic knowledge *a priori*.",["- \"Beurtheilung\" = appraisal."]],"krv-b-0135":["The chief point to be kept in view in dividing such a science is that no concepts must enter which contain anything empirical; in other words, that the knowledge *a priori* be completely pure. Hence, although the supreme principles of morality and its fundamental concepts are modes of knowledge *a priori*, they do not belong to transcendental philosophy. For though they do not themselves make the concepts of pleasure and pain, of desires and inclinations, and so forth, all of which are of empirical origin, the ground of their precepts, they must yet, in the concept of duty, necessarily bring these into the construction of the system of pure morality: as an obstacle that has to be overcome, or as an incitement that must not be made into a motive. Transcendental philosophy is accordingly a philosophy of pure, merely speculative reason. For everything practical, so far as it contains springs of action [*Triebfedern*], relates to feelings, and these belong to empirical sources of knowledge.",["- Footnote marks after \"müssen.\" and \"Triebfedern\" dropped; footnote text not supplied (the passage 0136 is only an apparatus fragment).","- Restated the long \"weil sie…\" sentence by separating \"For though…they must yet\". \"Luft\" is an OCR slip for \"Lust\".","- \"Weltweisheit\" = philosophy; \"Erkenntnisse\" = modes of knowledge; \"Anreitz\" = incitement."]],"krv-b-0136":["[Footnote] At; and to them in",["- Apparatus fragment (variant note: \"Al; und ihnen in\"); \"At\" read as the apparatus siglum \"A¹\" or similar. Doubtful OCR; translated as it stands, without completion."]],"krv-b-0137":["If we now wish to divide this science from the universal standpoint of a system in general, the science which we are now expounding must contain, first, a doctrine of elements, and secondly, a doctrine of method of pure reason. Each of these main parts would have its subdivisions, the grounds of which cannot yet be set forth here. All that seems necessary by way of introduction or preliminary remark is this: there are two stems of human knowledge, which perhaps spring from a common but to us unknown root, namely sensibility and understanding. Through the former, objects are given to us; through the second, they are thought. Now so far as sensibility may contain representations *a priori*, which constitute the condition under which objects are given to us, it would belong to transcendental philosophy. The transcendental doctrine of sense would have to belong to the first part of the science of elements, since the conditions under which alone the objects of human knowledge are given precede those under which they are thought.",["- \"Vorstellungen\" = representations; \"Sinnenlehre\" = doctrine of sense. \"solte\" read as \"sollte\".","- \"durch den zweiten aber!\" the \"!\" is an OCR footnote mark; dropped.","- Restated the sentence \"durch deren ersteren…\" as two sentences."]],"krv-b-0138":["[Illegible OCR fragment: \"e tg ER ze\"]",["- Unreadable OCR residue; nothing to translate, and I do not supply a reading."]],"krv-b-0139":["Critique of Pure Reason.\nTranscendental Doctrine of Elements.",["- Title page; \"Kritik\" rendered \"Critique\"."]],"krv-b-0140":["Of the Transcendental Doctrine of Elements, First Part.\nThe Transcendental Aesthetic.",["- \"SED\" is OCR ornament residue; omitted."]],"krv-b-0141":["In whatever manner and by whatever means a mode of knowledge may relate to objects, intuition is that through which it is in immediate relation to them, and to which all thought as a means is directed. But intuition takes place only in so far as the object is given to us. This again is possible, to us men at least, only through the object's affecting the mind in a certain manner. The capacity (receptivity) for receiving representations through the mode in which we are affected by objects is entitled sensibility. Objects are therefore given to us by means of sensibility, and it alone yields us intuitions; they are thought through the understanding, and from the understanding arise concepts. But all thought, whether straightforwardly (*directe*) or through a roundabout route (*indirecte*), must, by means of certain characters [*Merkmale*], ultimately relate to intuitions, and therefore, in our case, to sensibility, since in no other way can an object be given to us.",["- \"Fähigkeit\" rendered \"capacity\"; \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"afficire\" = affect.","- \"wenigstens?)\" and \"Merkmalen?)\" carry footnote marks, dropped.","- \"Merkmale\" = characters (marks); \"sich auf dieselbe\" read as referring to *Gegenstände* in general (\"them\")."]],"krv-b-0142":["The effect of an object upon the faculty of representation, so far as we are affected by it, is sensation. That intuition which relates to the object through sensation is entitled empirical. The undetermined object of an empirical intuition is entitled appearance.",["- \"Vorstellungsfähigkeit\" = faculty of representation; \"unbestimmte\" = undetermined. The trailing \"5\" is a line number; dropped."]],"krv-b-0143":["In the appearance, that which corresponds to sensation I entitle its matter; but that which so determines the manifold of appearance that it can be ordered in certain relations I entitle the form of appearance. Since that in which alone the sensations can be ordered and set in a certain form cannot itself be sensation, the matter of all appearance is given to us only *a posteriori*, but its form must lie ready for the sensations as a whole in the mind *a priori*, and so must allow of being considered apart from all sensation.",["- \"macht, daß\" is rendered \"so determines… that\", a slight explicitation; \"nenne ich\" = \"I entitle\", following the stated rendering of *nennen*.","- The \"kann,)\" mark is a footnote/apparatus sign (variant 13 in 0147); dropped. Line number \"10\" dropped."]],"krv-b-0144":["I entitle all representations pure (in the transcendental sense) in which nothing is found that belongs to sensation. Accordingly the pure form of sensible intuitions in general, in which all the manifold of appearances is intuited in certain relations, will be found in the mind *a priori*. This pure form of sensibility will itself also be entitled pure intuition. Thus if, from the representation of a body, I separate what the understanding thinks in it, such as substance, force, divisibility, and so forth, and likewise what belongs to sensation, such as impenetrability, hardness, colour, and so forth, there still remains to me something from this empirical intuition, namely extension and figure. These belong to pure intuition, which has its seat in the mind *a priori*, even without an actual object of the senses or of sensation, as a mere form of sensibility. |",["- \"Verstande\" in \"im transscendentalen Verstande\" rendered \"sense\", as the usual idiom (\"in the transcendental sense\"), not \"understanding\".","- \"Kraft\" = force; \"Ausdehnung\" = extension; \"Gestalt\" = figure. Line numbers and the OCR \"Í|\" reduced to the apparatus sign |."]],"krv-b-0145":["A science of all the principles of sensibility *a priori* I entitle transcendental aesthetic. There must therefore be such a",["- Footnote mark after \"Ästhetik\" dropped; footnote text is in 0146. Breaks off mid-sentence; completed in 0147."]],"krv-b-0146":["[Footnote] The Germans are the only people who now make use of the word 'aesthetic' to signify what others entitle the critique of taste. The underlying cause is a failed hope which the excellent analyst Baumgarten conceived, of bringing the critical judging of the beautiful under principles of reason, and of raising its rules to a science. But this effort is in vain. For the said rules or criteria are, in respect of their chief sources, [broken off]",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"ihren vornehmsten Quellen\"); the break is marked, not completed.","- \"Kritik des Geschmacks\" = critique of taste; \"bedienen\" read as one word (OCR \"be 30 dienen\"). \"zun bringen\" read as \"zu bringen\"; \"?)\" a footnote mark, dropped.","- Line number \"35\" dropped."]],"krv-b-0147":["[Apparatus: 13) A²: \"Erscheinung, in gewissen Verhältnissen geordnet, angeschauet wird\"]\n\n. . . science, which constitutes the first part of the transcendental doctrine of elements, in contrast to that which contains the principles of pure thought and is entitled transcendental logic.",["- The apparatus note is the variant to the line in 0143/0144 (\"Erscheinung … angeschauet wird\"); \"Al;\" read as \"A²;\" (doubtful OCR); text of the note carried over as given.","- The passage resumes the sentence from 0145 (\"Es muß also eine solche … Wissenschaft geben\"); the opening is left as a fragment with [. . .]."]],"krv-b-0148":["In the transcendental aesthetic, then, we shall first isolate sensibility, by separating off everything which the understanding thinks in it through its concepts, so that nothing may remain save empirical intuition. Secondly, we shall detach from this intuition all that belongs to sensation, so that nothing may remain save pure intuition and the mere form of appearances. This is all that sensibility can supply *a priori*. In the course of this investigation it will be found that there are two pure forms of sensible intuition, serving as principles of knowledge *a priori*, namely, space and time. To the consideration of these we now proceed.",["- OCR slips corrected silently: \"Cm: pfindung\" to Empfindung, \"Grwägung\" to Erwägung.","- The last clause (mit deren Erwägung …) is restated as a separate sentence.","- Verstand = understanding, Sinnlichkeit = sensibility, Erkenntniß = knowledge, as fixed."]],"krv-b-0149":["TRANSCENDENTAL AESTHETIC\n\nSECTION I\n\nOf Space\n\n§ 2\n\nMetaphysical Exposition of this Concept",["- \"82\" in the OCR is read as § 2. The stray closing parenthesis is dropped.","- Erörterung = \"exposition\" (Kant glosses it with *expositio* in the next passage)."]],"krv-b-0150":["By means of outer sense (a property of our mind) we represent to ourselves objects as outside us, and all without exception in space. In space their shape, magnitude, and relation to one another are determined, or are capable of being determined. Inner sense, by means of which the mind intuits itself or its inner state, gives, it is true, no intuition of the soul itself as an object; but there is none the less a determinate form, namely time, under which alone the intuition of its inner state is possible, so that everything which belongs to the inner determinations is represented in relations of time. Time cannot be intuited as outer, any more than space can be intuited as something in us. What, then, are space and time? Are they real existences? Or are they only determinations, or even relations, of things, yet such as would belong to the things even if they were not intuited? Or are they relations that attach only to the form of intuition, and therefore to the subjective constitution of our mind, apart from which these predicates could not be ascribed to anything whatsoever? To obtain instruction on this matter we shall first expound the concept of space.[ ] By exposition (*expositio*) I understand the distinct (though not exhaustive) representation of that which belongs to a concept; the exposition is metaphysical when it contains that which exhibits the concept as given *a priori*.\n\n[Footnote, text broken off at the beginning:] … [ ] merely empirical in [their sources?], and can therefore never serve as determinate laws *a priori* by which our judgments of taste must be directed. The converse is rather the case: our judgment of taste constitutes the proper test of the correctness of those rules. It is therefore advisable either to let this name again fall into disuse and to reserve it for that doctrine which is true science (whereby we should also approach more nearly to the language and meaning of the ancients, among whom the division of knowledge into αἰσθητὰ καὶ νοητά [*aisthēta kai noēta*] was very famous), or else to share the name with speculative philosophy, taking aesthetic partly in the transcendental, partly in the psychological sense.",["- The OCR has the text of Kant's footnote (on the word \"Ästhetik\", B 35–36) spliced into the middle of the main sentence between \"im\" and \"Raume vor\". I have rejoined \"im Raume\" and set the footnote apart.","- The footnote begins mid-sentence (\"nach bloß empirisch …\"); the antecedent is missing, so I translate only what stands. \"[their sources?]\" is a conjecture, and the doubtful reading should be checked against the original.","- Main text: long sentences divided. \"Eine bestimmte Form\" is explained in brackets-free form as \"namely, time\" because the argument requires it, and this is the one supplied word. \"Wesen\" = \"real existences\".","- Footnote marks (\"?)\" and “)) are dropped. The gap marked [ ] stands where the OCR shows a footnote reference after \"erörtern\".","- The Greek is restored from the garbled OCR \"alodnta xat vonta\"."]],"krv-b-0151":["1) Space is not an empirical concept which has been derived from outer experiences. For in order that certain sensations be referred to something outside me (that is, to something in another region of space from that in which I find myself), and similarly in order that I may be able to represent them as outside and alongside one another, and accordingly as not merely different but as in different places, the representation of space must already underlie them. Consequently the representation of space cannot be obtained through experience from the relations of outer appearance; on the contrary, this outer experience is itself possible at all only through the representation in question.",["- Footnote mark after \"neben\" dropped; footnote text is not present in the passage.","- \"durch gedachte Vorstellung\": \"gedachte\" read as \"the representation just mentioned\", i.e. of space. The reading may be doubtful in the OCR.","- \"Orte\" rendered \"region\"; \"Erscheinung\" = appearance."]],"krv-b-0152":["2) Space is a necessary representation, *a priori*, which underlies all outer intuitions. We can never represent to ourselves the absence of space, though we can quite well think it as empty of objects. It is therefore to be regarded as the condition of the possibility of appearances, and not as a determination dependent upon them. It is a representation *a priori*, which necessarily underlies outer appearances.",["- Closing footnote mark dropped; no footnote text in the passage.","- Kant's \"ob … gleich\" is rendered \"though\"."]],"krv-b-0153":["3) Space is not a discursive or, as we say, general concept of the relations of things in general, but a pure intuition. For, in the first place, we can represent to ourselves only one single space; and when we speak of many spaces, we mean only parts of one and the same unique space. These parts cannot precede the one all-embracing space, as being, as it were, its constituents out of which it can be composed; they can be thought only as in it. Space is essentially one; the manifold in it, and therefore the general concept of spaces in general, depends solely on limitations. Hence it follows that an *a priori* intuition (which is not empirical) underlies all concepts of space. Thus, also, all geometrical principles, for instance, that in a triangle two sides together are greater than the third, are never derived from general concepts of line and triangle, but are derived from intuition, and derived *a priori*, with apodeictic certainty.",["- Footnote mark dropped after \"zum Grunde liegt\"; the footnote text is not in the passage.","- \"einig / alleinig\" rendered \"single / unique\"; \"Anschauung a priori … in Ansehung seiner\" is smoothed to \"of space\".","- \"all gemeinen\" in the OCR corrected to allgemeinen. Apodiktisch = \"apodeictic\" (Kemp Smith's usage)."]],"krv-b-0154":["4) Space is represented as an infinite given magnitude. Now every concept must indeed be thought as a representation which is contained in an infinite number of different possible representations (as their common mark), and which therefore contains these under itself. But no concept, as such, can be thought as containing an infinite number of representations within itself. Yet space is so thought, for all the parts of space, to infinity, are simultaneous. The original representation of space is therefore an intuition *a priori*, and not a concept.",["- The closing footnote mark is dropped; no footnote text in the passage.","- Menge = \"number\"; Merkmal = \"mark\". The parenthesis \"(denn …)\" is made into a separate sentence."]],"krv-b-0155":["Transcendental Exposition of the Concept of Space.",[]],"krv-b-0156":["By a transcendental exposition I understand the explanation of a concept as a principle from which the possibility of other synthetic modes of knowledge *a priori* can be understood. For this purpose it is required (1) that such modes of knowledge do really flow from the given concept, and (2) that they are possible only on the assumption of a given way of explaining this concept.",["- OCR \"Er— klärung\" corrected to Erklärung.","- \"Erkenntnisse\" is plural, so \"modes of knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-0157":["Geometry is a science which determines the properties of space synthetically, and yet *a priori*. What, then, must be the representation of space, in order that such knowledge of it may be possible? Space must originally be an intuition; for from a mere concept no propositions can be derived which go beyond the concept, and this is what happens in geometry (Introduction, V). But this intuition must be found in us *a priori*, that is, prior to all perception of an object, and must therefore be pure, not empirical, intuition. For geometrical propositions are one and all apodeictic, that is, are bound up with the consciousness of their necessity, as, for instance, that space has only three dimensions. Such propositions cannot be empirical judgments, or judgments of experience, nor can they be inferred from such judgments (Introduction, II).",["- OCR slips corrected: \"Satze\" to Sätze, \"abmessungen\" to Abmessungen.","- The cross-references \"Einleitung V\" and \"Einleit. II\" are rendered \"Introduction, V\" and \"Introduction, II\".","- Gegenstand = \"object\"."]],"krv-b-0158":["How, then, can there exist in the mind an outer intuition which precedes the objects themselves, and in which the concept of these objects can be determined *a priori*? Obviously, not otherwise than in so far as the intuition has its seat in the subject only, as the formal character of the subject, in virtue of which, in being affected by objects, it obtains immediate representation, that is, intuition, of them; and therefore only as the form of outer sense in general.",["- The OCR has a stray \"a\" before \"d. i. Anschauung\"; dropped as a slip.","- \"afficirt\" = \"affected\"; \"Sitz\" = \"seat\". The relative clause is lightly restructured to keep \"it\" clear (the subject)."]],"krv-b-0159":["Thus our explanation alone makes intelligible the possibility of geometry as a body of synthetic knowledge *a priori*. Any mode of explanation which fails to show this, even though it may seem to have some similarity with ours, can by this criterion be most surely distinguished from it.",["- OCR \"Afo\" corrected to Also; \"Geo metrie\" to Geometrie.","- Erkenntniß here is rendered \"knowledge\" with \"body of\" supplied for English idiom."]],"krv-b-0160":["Conclusions from the above Concepts.",["- The reference \"B 42–43? / A 26\" is the portal's own and uncertain. This is Kant's heading to the section of conclusions (Schlüsse) drawn from the preceding concepts of space."]],"krv-b-0161":["Space does not represent any property of things in themselves, nor does it represent them in their relation to one another. That is to say, it represents no determination which attaches to the objects themselves, and which would remain even if abstraction were made from all the subjective conditions of intuition. For neither absolute nor relative determinations can be intuited prior to the existence of the things to which they belong, and therefore they cannot be intuited *a priori*.",["- OCR \"an si\", \"s auch\" read as \"an sich\", \"sie auch\".","- \"irgend einiger Dinge\" rendered \"of things in themselves\" only in the sense of \"an sich\"; no other supply.","- Second sentence of the German split off from \"d. i.\" clause in English by a full stop."]],"krv-b-0162":["Space is nothing but the form of all appearances of outer sense; that is, it is the subjective condition of sensibility, under which alone outer intuition is possible for us. Now the receptivity of the subject, its capacity to be affected by objects, necessarily precedes all intuitions of these objects. Hence we can understand how the form of all appearances can be given in the mind prior to all actual perceptions, and so *a priori*; and how, as a pure intuition in which all objects must be determined, it can contain, prior to all experience, principles of their relations. |",["- \"die Receptivität des Subjects, von Gegenständen afficirt zu werden\" expanded as \"its capacity to be affected by objects\"; the \"Weil nun … so\" construction recast as two sentences.","- The sign | is carried over as it stands."]],"krv-b-0163":["We can accordingly speak of space, of extended beings, and so on, only from the human standpoint. If we depart from the subjective condition under which alone we can obtain outer intuition, namely, that of being affected by objects, the representation of space signifies nothing whatsoever. This predicate is ascribed to things only in so far as they appear to us, that is, only in so far as they are objects of sensibility. The constant form of this receptivity, which we entitle sensibility, is a necessary condition of all the relations in which objects can be intuited as outside us; and if abstraction is made from these objects, it is a pure intuition, which bears the name space. Since we cannot make the special conditions of sensibility into conditions of the possibility of things, but only of their appearances, we can indeed say that space comprehends all things that may appear to us externally, but not all things in themselves, whether they be intuited or not, and by whatever subject. For we cannot judge at all whether the intuitions of other thinking beings are bound by the same conditions as those which limit our intuition and which are universally valid for us.\n\nIf we add the limitation of a judgment to the concept of the subject, the judgment then holds unconditionally. The proposition, \"All things are side by side in space,\" holds under this limitation, namely, if these things are taken as objects of our sensible intuition. If I here add the condition to the concept and say, \"All things, as outer appearances, are side by side in space,\" the rule holds universally and without limitation. Our exposition therefore establishes the reality (that is, the objective validity) of space in respect of whatever can be presented to us externally as object; but at the same time it establishes the ideality of space in respect of things when they are considered in themselves through reason, that is, without regard to the constitution of our sensibility. We assert, then, the empirical reality of space (in respect of all possible outer experience), while yet asserting its transcendental ideality; that is, we assert that it is nothing at all as soon as we leave out the condition of the possibility of all experience, and suppose it to be something that underlies things in themselves.",["- Restated in two paragraphs; the long sentence on \"Weil wir die besonderen Bedingungen…\" kept whole, the rest divided for clarity.","- \"so wie wir nämlich von den Gegenständen afficirt werden mögen\" rendered \"namely, that of being affected by objects\"; the clause is loose in the German.","- OCR \"ıc.\" read \"etc.\" (\"and so on\"); \"nrtheilen\" read \"urtheilen\"; \"Sdealität\" read \"Idealität\"; \"unter!)\" and \"die?)\" carry footnote markers whose notes are not in the passage, so the markers are omitted.","- \"Realität … Idealität … transscendentale Idealität\" rendered \"reality\", \"ideality\", \"transcendental ideality\"; \"durch die Vernunft\" kept as \"through reason\"."]],"krv-b-0164":["But besides space there is no other subjective representation, referring to something outer, which could be entitled objective *a priori*. For from none of them can synthetic propositions *a priori* be derived, as they can from the intuition in space (§ 3). Strictly speaking, therefore, no ideality belongs to them, although they agree with the representation of space in this, that they belong merely to the subjective constitution of the mode of sense, for example, of sight, hearing, and touch, through the sensations of colours, sounds, and warmth. But since these are mere sensations and not intuitions, they do not of themselves allow any object to be known, least of all *a priori*.",["- OCR \"auf z etwas\" read \"auf etwas\"; the closing footnote marker (\"lassen.“)\") is omitted, its note not being given.","- \"Sinnesart\" rendered \"mode of sense\"; \"Gefühl\" as \"touch\" (feeling of the senses), a choice.","- \"heißen\" rendered \"is entitled\"."]],"krv-b-0165":["The sole purpose of this remark is to guard against any attempt to illustrate the asserted ideality of space by examples quite inadequate to the purpose. Colours, taste, and the like, for instance, are rightly regarded not as properties of things but merely as alterations of our subject, which may even differ from one man to another. For in that case what is originally itself mere appearance, a rose, for example, counts in the empirical sense as a thing in itself, although to every eye it may appear different in respect of colour. The transcendental concept of appearances in space, on the other hand, is a critical reminder that nothing intuited in space is a thing in itself, and that space is not a form of things which might belong to them in themselves. It reminds us that objects in themselves are quite unknown to us, and that what we call outer objects are nothing but mere representations of our sensibility, the form of which is space. Their true correlate, the thing in itself, is not known through these representations, nor can it be known; and in experience no question is ever raised regarding it.",["- OCR \"Erinne— rung\" read \"Erinnerung\".","- The sentence \"daß überhaupt nichts … eine Sache an sich, noch daß der Raum…\" is irregular (a missing \"sei\" after \"Sache an sich\"); I smoothed it and divided it.","- \"im empirischen Verstande\" rendered \"in the empirical sense\" (not \"understanding\"); \"Correlatum\" rendered \"correlate\"."]],"krv-b-0166":["SECTION II OF THE TRANSCENDENTAL AESTHETIC\n\nOf Time\n\n§ 4",["- Heading rendered in Kemp Smith's arrangement; OCR \"84.\" read \"§ 4\"."]],"krv-b-0167":["Metaphysical Exposition of the Concept of Time.",["- A stray footnote marker after the heading is omitted; \"Erörterung\" rendered \"exposition\"."]],"krv-b-0168":["1. Time is not an empirical concept that has been derived from any experience. For neither coexistence nor succession would ever come within perception, if the representation of time were not presupposed as underlying them *a priori*. Only on this presupposition can we represent to ourselves that certain things exist at one and the same time (simultaneously) or at different times (successively).",["- \"Zugleichsein oder Aufeinanderfolgen\" rendered \"coexistence … succession\"; \"deren Voraussetzung\" resolved as \"this presupposition\"."]],"krv-b-0169":["2. Time is a necessary representation that underlies all intuitions. In respect of appearances in general we cannot remove time itself, though we can quite well take the appearances out of time. Time is therefore given *a priori*. In it alone is all actuality of appearances possible. The appearances may one and all vanish, but time itself (as the universal condition of their possibility) cannot be removed.",["- \"Wirklichkeit\" rendered \"actuality\"; \"aufheben\" as \"remove\"/\"be removed\"."]],"krv-b-0170":["3. On this necessity *a priori* is also grounded the possibility of apodeictic principles of the relations of time, or axioms of time in general. Time has only one dimension; different times are not simultaneous but successive (just as different spaces are not successive but simultaneous). These principles cannot be derived from experience, for experience would give neither strict universality nor apodeictic certainty. We should only be able to say that common perception teaches us that it is so, not that it must be so. These principles hold as rules under which alone experiences are possible, and they instruct us prior to experience, not by means of it.",["- \"Grundsätze\" rendered \"principles\"; \"apodiktisch\" as \"apodeictic\".","- \"derselben\" in the last clause resolved as \"experience\"."]],"krv-b-0171":["4. Time is not a discursive or, as it is called, general concept, but a pure form of sensible intuition. Different times are only parts of one and the same time. But a representation which can be given only through a single object is an intuition. Moreover, the proposition that different times cannot be simultaneous could not be derived from a general concept. The proposition is synthetic, and cannot arise from concepts alone. It is therefore contained immediately in the intuition and representation of time.",["- \"Der Satz … Er\" rendered with the same antecedent, \"the proposition\"; sentences kept in the German order, as no division was needed."]],"krv-b-0172":["(5) The infinitude of time signifies nothing more than that every determinate magnitude of time is possible only through limitations of one single time which underlies it. The original representation, time, must therefore be given as unlimited. But where the parts themselves, and every magnitude of an object, can be represented as determinate only through limitation, the complete representation must not be given through concepts (for concepts contain only partial representations); on the contrary, immediate intuition must underlie them.[?] us",["- OCR corrupt: \"alle des stimmte Größe\" read as \"alle bestimmte Größe\"; \"uneingeschränktl\" as \"uneingeschränkt\". Trailing \"us\" is an OCR remnant, left as it stands.","- \"ihnen?)\" retains a stray OCR footnote mark; \"ihnen\" is taken to refer to the partial representations or concepts. I have bracketed \"[?]\" to mark the doubtful reading.","- \"Wovon ... da muß\" is restated as \"where ... the complete representation must not be...\"; the semicolon and \"on the contrary\" are supplied for smoothness.","- Original \"Begriffe\", \"Theilvorstellungen\": \"concepts\", \"partial representations\"."]],"krv-b-0173":["(1) A1; for the partial representations precede [it].",["- This is a footnote fragment (the \"A1\" is doubtful OCR; Kant's original footnote refers to the first edition's text). Left untranslated as it stands.","- \"it\" is supplied: the sense requires that the partial representations precede the whole, i.e. the whole is not given by concepts. Bracketed accordingly."]],"krv-b-0174":["Transcendental Exposition of the Concept of Time.",["- *Erörterung* = \"exposition\"; \"Transscendentale\" rendered \"transcendental\" in modern spelling, as Kemp Smith does."]],"krv-b-0175":["I can therefore appeal to No. 3, where, for the sake of brevity, I have placed under the heading of the metaphysical exposition what is properly transcendental. Here I add further that the concept of alteration, and with it the concept of motion (as alteration of place), is possible only through and in the representation of time; that if this representation were not an (inner) intuition *a priori*, no concept, whatever it might be, could render comprehensible the possibility of an alteration, that is, of a combination of contradictorily opposed predicates (e.g. the being of a thing in a place and the not-being of the same thing in the same place) in one and the same object. Only in time can two contradictorily opposed determinations be met with in one thing, namely, one after the other. Thus our concept of time explains the possibility of as much synthetic knowledge *a priori* as is set forth in the general doctrine of motion, which is not a little fruitful.",["- \"Ur. 3\" is an OCR slip for \"Nr. 3\"; rendered \"No. 3\". \"Zeitbegrifs\" corrected to \"Zeitbegriff\"; \"konnen\" to \"können\".","- *Veränderung* = \"alteration\" (Kemp Smith's usage); *Bestimmung* = \"determination\".","- *Erkenntniß* (singular, collective) = \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-0176":["Conclusions from these concepts.",["- Heading; \"Schlüsse\" = \"conclusions\"."]],"krv-b-0177":["(a) Time is not something which exists of itself, or which inheres in things as an objective determination, and it therefore does not remain over when abstraction is made from all the subjective conditions of the intuition of things. For in the first case it would be something that is actual without being an actual object. As regards the second case, time, as a determination or order inhering in the things themselves, could not precede the objects as their condition, nor be known and intuited *a priori* by means of synthetic propositions. This last is, on the other hand, quite possible if time is nothing but the subjective condition under which alone intuitions can take place in us. For in that case this form of inner intuition can be represented prior to the objects, and therefore *a priori*.",["- Long opening sentence divided at \"denn\"; the second sentence follows the German \"Was aber das zweite betrifft\".","- \"derselben\" in \"Anschauung derselben\" taken to refer to the things (*Dingen*).","- \"mithin\" rendered \"therefore\"; *Anschauung* = \"intuition\"."]],"krv-b-0178":["(b) Time is nothing other than the form of inner sense, that is, of the intuition of ourselves and of our inner state. For time cannot be a determination of outer appearances; it belongs neither to shape nor to position, etc.; on the contrary, it determines the relation of representations in our inner state. And just because this inner intuition yields no shape, we endeavour to make up for this lack by analogies, and represent the time-sequence by a line progressing to infinity, in which the manifold constitutes a series of one dimension only; and we reason from the properties of this line to all the properties of time, with the one exception that the parts of the line are simultaneous whereas those of time are always successive. From this it is also evident that the representation of time is itself an intuition, since all its relations can be expressed in an outer intuition.",["- \"einigen\" (in \"dem einigen\") is a Kantian form of \"einzigen\"; rendered \"one exception\".","- *Erscheinungen* = \"appearances\"; *Gestalt* = \"shape\"; *Lage* = \"position\".","- Sentence \"Und eben weil ...\" divided at the semicolon after \"Dimension\" for ease; kept as one sentence with a semicolon."]],"krv-b-0179":["(c) Time is the formal condition *a priori* of all appearances whatsoever. Space, as the pure form of all outer intuition, is limited as a condition *a priori* to outer appearances alone. On the other hand, all representations, whether or not they have outer things for their objects, belong in themselves, as determinations of the mind, to our inner state; and this inner state stands under the formal condition of inner intuition, and so under time. Time is therefore a condition *a priori* of all appearance whatsoever. It is the immediate condition of inner appearances (of our souls), and thereby mediately of outer appearances also. If I can say *a priori* that all outer appearances are in space and are determined *a priori* according to the relations of space, I can say quite generally, from the principle of inner sense, that all appearances whatsoever, that is, all objects of the senses, are in time, and necessarily stand in time-relations.",["- OCR noise (\"16\", \"2\", \"F\", and the hyphenation of \"über- haupt\") silently removed. Line-number remnants dropped.","- \"Dagegen weil ... so ist die Zeit\" restated as separate sentences; the \"so\"-clause becomes \"Time is therefore...\".","- *Gemüt* = \"mind\"; *Seele* = \"soul\"."]],"krv-b-0180":["If we abstract from our mode of intuiting ourselves inwardly, and by means of this intuition of including all outer intuitions in the power of representation [*Vorstellungskraft*], and so take objects as they may be in themselves, then time is nothing. It has objective validity only in respect of appearances, because these are already things which we take as objects of our senses. But it is no longer objective if we abstract from the sensibility of our intuition, and so from that mode of representation which is peculiar to us, and speak of things in general. Time is therefore solely a subjective condition of our (human) intuition (which is always sensible, that is, so far as we are affected by objects), and in itself, apart from the subject, is nothing. Nonetheless it is necessarily objective in respect of all appearances, and so also of all things which can come before us in experience. We cannot say that all things are in time, because in the concept of things in general abstraction is made from every mode of intuiting them, and intuition is the proper condition under which time belongs to the representation of objects. If the condition is added to the concept, and we say that all things, as appearances (objects of sensible intuition), are in time, then the principle has its proper objective correctness and *a priori* universality.",["- OCR noise (\"s\", \"wen\" for \"nehmen\", \"|\" ) cleaned; the apparatus sign \"|\" in the German is not reproduced since it marks a page break, in OCR position after \"unsrer\".","- Opening sentence restated slightly: \"of including\" is supplied to carry the construction \"und ... zu befassen\".","- *Sinnlichkeit* = \"sensibility\"; *Vorstellungsart* = \"mode of representation\"; *afficirt* = \"affected\".","- \"dieser\" in \"dieser aber die eigentliche Bedingung\" refers to *Anschauung*, resolved as \"intuition\"."]],"krv-b-0181":["Our contentions accordingly teach the empirical reality of time, that is, its objective validity in respect of all objects which can ever be given to our senses. And since our intuition is always sensible, no object can ever be given to us in experience which does not come under the condition of time. On the other hand, we deny to time all claim to absolute reality, that is, we deny that it belongs to things absolutely, as their condition or property, independently of the form of our sensible intuition. Such properties as belong to things in themselves can never be given to us through the senses. In this, then, consists the transcendental ideality of time. According to it, time is nothing at all if abstraction is made from the subjective conditions of sensible intuition, and cannot be ascribed to objects in themselves (apart from their relation to our intuition), either as subsisting or as inhering. Yet this ideality, like that of space, is not to be compared with the subreptions of sensation, since in their case it is presupposed that the appearance itself, in which these predicates inhere, has objective reality. Here that reality entirely falls away, except so far as it is merely empirical, that is, regards the object itself as mere appearance. On this point the above remark in the first section is to be consulted.",["- Kemp Smith's \"subreptions\" kept for *Subreptionen*; *Empfindungen* = \"sensations\".","- \"da sie nämlich ... anhinge\" divided and clarified: \"that is, we deny that it belongs...\"; the subjunctive is rendered as a denial.","- Last sentence divided at the colon; \"wovon\" = \"on this point\"; \"ersteren Abschnitts\" = \"first section\"."]],"krv-b-0182":["[Si] Elucidation.",["- \"Si\" is an OCR fragment (perhaps a stray mark); left in brackets as unintelligible.","- *Erläuterung* = \"Elucidation\"."]],"krv-b-0183":["Against this theory, which grants to time empirical reality but disputes its absolute and transcendental reality, I have heard from discerning men an objection on which they are so unanimous that I conclude it must occur naturally to every reader to whom these considerations are unfamiliar. It runs as follows. Alterations are real (this is proved by the change of our own representations, even if all outer appearances, together with their alterations, be denied). Now alterations are possible only in time; time is therefore something real. The answer presents no difficulty. I grant the whole argument. Time is indeed something real, namely the real form of inner intuition. It has therefore subjective reality in respect of inner experience; that is, I really have the representation of time and of my determinations in it. It is to be regarded as real, not as object, but as the mode of representation of myself as object. But if I myself, or another being, could intuit me without this condition of sensibility, then these very determinations, which we now represent to ourselves as alterations, would yield a mode of knowledge in which the representation of time, and therefore also of alteration, would not occur at all. Its empirical reality, as condition of all our experiences, thus remains. Only absolute reality cannot, in view of what has been said above, be granted to it. It is nothing but the form of our inner intuition.¹ If the special condition of our sensibility be taken away from it, the concept of time likewise vanishes; it does not inhere in the objects themselves, but merely in the subject which intuits them.\n\n[The text here contains the OCR remnant of a closing quotation mark and a stray \"as\" after \"Wenn man\"; see notes. The footnote marker follows the reading of the next passage.]",["- Restated Kant's sequence of short sentences with minimal change; \"Nun sind Veränderungen...\" rendered in a single sentence; *Veränderung* = \"alteration\" (as Kemp Smith), *Wirkliches* = \"real\".","- \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"a mode of knowledge\" because the sense is a particular cognition, not knowledge in general.","- OCR slips: \"Sie ist nichts, als die Form unsrer inneren Anschauung.“)\" read as a footnote marker (the footnote follows in krv-b-0185); \"Wenn man as von ihr\" read \"Wenn man von ihr\"; the bracketed remark in the translation about the OCR is editorial and should be removed in any final text.","- The signs | in the German (page-break marks \"Re⸗ | alität\") are dropped as running page/line apparatus per the job instructions; \"Realität\" restored from the hyphenation.","- \"mithin\" rendered \"and therefore\"; \"als Objects anzusehen\" resolved as \"to be regarded as … the mode of representation of myself as object\"."]],"krv-b-0184":["But the reason why this objection is raised so unanimously, and by those who yet can bring no convincing objection against the doctrine of the ideality of space, is this. They had no hope of demonstrating the absolute reality of space apodeictically, since they are confronted by idealism, according to which the actuality of outer objects is not capable of any strict proof; whereas the actuality of the object of our inner sense (of myself and my state) is immediately clear through consciousness. The former might be a mere illusion; the latter, in their opinion, is undeniably something actual. But they did not consider that both, without their actuality as representations being disputed, belong nevertheless only to appearance, which always has two sides: the one, in which the object is considered in itself (apart from the manner of intuiting it, the constitution of which, for that very reason, always remains problematic); the other, in which regard is had to the form of the intuition of this object, which must be sought not in the object in itself but in the subject to which it appears, but which nevertheless really and necessarily belongs to the appearance of this object.",["- The passage breaks off at \"Wirk⸗\" and resumes in krv-b-0185 after the footnote; I joined the word (\"Wirklichkeit\") across the break and so translated the sentence as one continuous sentence, as the German requires. Strictly the passage as given ends at \"ihrer Wirk-\"; the continuation appears in the next item.","- \"Jene... dieser\" resolved as \"the former… the latter\" (outer objects / inner object).","- \"Wirklichkeit\" = \"actuality\", distinguished from \"Realität\" = \"reality\".","- Line numbers and the \"20\" in \"Ideali⸗ 20 tät\" omitted as running apparatus."]],"krv-b-0185":["¹ I can indeed say: my representations follow one another; but that means only that we are conscious of them as in a temporal sequence, that is, in accordance with the form of inner sense. Time is not, on that account, something in itself, nor is it any determination objectively inhering in things.\n\n[Continuation of the preceding text, which breaks off at \"Wirk⸗\": …lichkeit als Vorstellungen bestreiten darf, gleichwohl nur zur Erscheinung gehören, … — translated above in krv-b-0184, since the passage as given is interleaved with the footnote.]",["- The footnote proper is the first sentence-pair; the remainder of the passage (\"lichkeit als Vorstellungen… zukommt\") is the continuation of krv-b-0184's sentence, and I have translated it there rather than twice. Stray numerals 3, 4, 5 are line numbers and omitted; \"s da\" read \"da\".","- Kept the footnote as a footnote; \"Bestimmung\" = \"determination\"."]],"krv-b-0186":["Time and space are accordingly two sources of knowledge, from which different synthetic modes of knowledge can be drawn *a priori*, as pure mathematics, especially in respect of the knowledge of space and its relations, gives a splendid example. For both, taken together, are pure forms of all sensible intuition, and so make synthetic propositions *a priori* possible. But these *a priori* sources of knowledge, just because they are merely conditions of sensibility, determine for themselves their own limits: they apply to objects only in so far as these are regarded as appearances, and do not present things in themselves. Appearances alone constitute the field of their validity, and if we go beyond it no further objective employment of them is possible. This reality of space and time leaves, moreover, the certainty of empirical knowledge untouched; for we are as certain of it whether these forms necessarily inhere in things in themselves, or only in our intuition of those things. Those, on the other hand, who maintain the absolute reality of space and time, whether they hold it to be subsistent or only inherent, must find themselves at variance with the principles of experience itself.\n\nFor if they decide for the former alternative (which is generally the view of the mathematical students of nature), they have to admit two eternal and infinite self-subsisting non-entities (space and time), which exist (yet without there being anything real) only in order to contain in themselves everything real. If they adopt the second alternative (which is held by some of those who study nature metaphysically), and regard space and time as relations of appearances (alongside or after one another), abstracted from experience, though confusedly represented in this abstraction, they must dispute the validity of the mathematical doctrines *a priori* in respect of actual things (for instance in space), or at least their apodeictic certainty, since this certainty is not to be found *a posteriori* at all. The *a priori* concepts of space and time are, on this view, merely creatures of the imagination, whose source must really be sought in experience. Imagination has made out of relations abstracted from experience something which, while it contains what is general in them, cannot exist without the restrictions which nature has attached to them.\n\nThe former party gain so much, that they clear the field of appearances for mathematical assertions. On the other hand, these very conditions greatly confuse them when the understanding seeks to go beyond that field. The second party gain, indeed, in respect of the latter point: the representations of space and time do not stand in their way when they wish to judge of objects not as appearances but merely in their relation to the understanding. But they can neither give any ground for the possibility of mathematical knowledge *a priori* (since they lack a true and objectively valid intuition *a priori*), nor bring the propositions of experience into necessary agreement with those assertions. On our theory of the true constitution of these two original forms of sensibility both difficulties are removed.¹",["- Divided the long passage into three paragraphs and several shorter sentences, as the method permits; the German is a single block.","- \"Dagegen die, so... behaupten... uneinig sein müssen\" is irregular (no main verb governing \"die\"); I smoothed it into \"Those... must find themselves at variance.\"","- \"Hirte\" and \"abstra-\" in the OCR read as \"abstrahirte\"; \"dasind\" as \"da sind\". \"Sie sind nämlich beide\" kept as \"For both, taken together\".","- \"Undinge\" = \"non-entities\"; \"subsistirend / inhärirend\" = \"subsistent / inherent\".","- The final \"1\" is a footnote marker whose text is not in the passage; retained as ¹. Doubtful: \"dieser Meinung nach\" rendered \"on this view\"."]],"krv-b-0187":["That the transcendental aesthetic cannot contain more than these two elements, space and time, is evident from the fact that all other concepts belonging to sensibility, even that of motion, which unites both elements, presuppose something empirical. For motion presupposes the perception of something movable. But in space considered in itself there is nothing movable; the movable must therefore be something which is found in space only through experience, and so an empirical datum. In the same way the transcendental aesthetic cannot count the concept of alteration among its data *a priori*; for time itself does not alter, but only something which is in time. The concept therefore requires the perception of some existence and of the succession of its determinations, and so requires experience.",["- OCR: \"fegt\" read \"setzt\"; \"sebst\" read \"selbst\". \"Denn diese\" refers to \"Bewegung\", hence \"For motion\".","- \"Ästhetik\" retained as \"aesthetic\" (Kemp Smith: \"Aesthetic\"; here lower-case in the sentence). \"Wahrnehmung\" = \"perception\"; \"Dasein\" = \"existence\".","- \"Also wird dazu... erfordert\" restated with \"The concept therefore requires…\" to supply the subject."]],"krv-b-0188":["§ 8.\nGeneral Observations\non the\nTranscendental Aesthetic.",["- \"Ästhetik\" capitalised as a title (\"Aesthetic\")."]],"krv-b-0189":["I. First of all, it will be necessary to explain as distinctly as possible what our opinion is in regard to the fundamental constitution of sensible knowledge in general, so as to forestall all misinterpretation of it.",["- OCR: \"ers klaren\" read \"erklären\"; \"Erundbeschaffenheit\" read \"Grundbeschaffenheit\". \"I.)\" given as \"I.\"","- \"Erkenntniß\" = \"knowledge\"; \"Meinung\" = \"opinion\"."]],"krv-b-0190":["What we have intended to say, then, is this: that all our intuition is nothing but the representation of appearance; that the things which we intuit are not in themselves what we intuit them as being, nor their relations so constituted in themselves as they appear to us; and that if we remove our own subject, or even only the subjective constitution of the senses in general, all the constitution and all the relations of objects in space and time, nay space and time themselves, would vanish. As appearances they cannot exist in themselves, but only in us. What may be the nature of objects in themselves, and apart from all this receptivity of our sensibility, remains entirely unknown to us. We know nothing but our mode of perceiving them. This mode is peculiar to us, and does not necessarily belong to every being, though it must belong to every human being. With this alone we are concerned.\n\nSpace and time are its pure forms; sensation in general is its matter. The pure forms we can know only *a priori*, that is, prior to all actual perception, and they are therefore entitled pure intuition. Sensation, on the other hand, is that in our knowledge which causes it to be entitled knowledge *a posteriori*, that is, empirical intuition. The pure forms belong to our sensibility with absolute necessity, of whatever kind our sensations may be; sensations may be very diverse. Even if we could bring this intuition of ours to the highest degree of distinctness, we should not thereby come any nearer to the constitution of objects in themselves. For in any case we should be completely knowing only our own mode of intuition, that is, our sensibility, and this always only under the conditions of space and time which originally inhere in the subject. What objects may be in themselves would never become known to us even through the most enlightened knowledge of their appearance, which is all that is given to us.",["- Restated the first long sentence as two: \"and as appearances … only in us\" is made a separate sentence, with \"they\" referring to the properties, relations, space and time.","- \"Art, sie wahrzunehmen\" rendered \"mode of perceiving them\"; \"Anschauung\" and \"Sinnlichkeit\" are kept distinct. \"Mit dieser\" is read as referring to this mode of perception.","- \"Jene\"/\"diese\" are resolved as \"pure forms\"/\"sensation\" and \"Derselben\" as the mode of perception (our sensibility); \"sie heißt … reine Anschauung\" is rendered \"they are … entitled\", since the plural sense requires it.","- \"Erkenntniß\" in \"unserm Erkenntniß\" is rendered \"our knowledge\"; the OCR reading \"äufgeklärteste\" is silently corrected to \"aufgeklärteste\"."]],"krv-b-0191":["To maintain, therefore, that our whole sensibility is nothing but the confused representation of things, containing solely what belongs to them in themselves, but in a congeries of characters and partial representations which we do not consciously distinguish, is to falsify the concepts of sensibility and of appearance, and to render the whole doctrine of them useless and empty. The difference between an obscure and a distinct representation is merely logical, and does not concern the content. The concept of right, which the sound understanding employs, doubtless contains precisely what the most subtle speculation can develop out of it; only in ordinary practical use we are not conscious of the manifold representations contained in this thought. We cannot, however, say on that account that the ordinary concept is sensible and contains a mere appearance. For right cannot appear at all; its concept has its seat in the understanding, and represents a character (the moral) of actions, which belongs to them in themselves. The representation of a body in intuition, on the other hand, contains nothing whatever that could belong to an object in itself, but merely the appearance of something, and the way in which we are affected by it. This receptivity of our faculty of knowledge is entitled sensibility, and remains toto caelo different from knowledge of the object in itself, even if we were to see through the appearance to its very foundations.",["- The opening \"Daß … ist eine Verfälschung\" is made an infinitive construction (\"To maintain … is to falsify\") for smoothness; the German's grammatical subject is the *daß*-clause.","- \"Darum kann man nicht sagen\" is divided into a new sentence, and \"denn\" is rendered \"For\"; \"himmelweit\" is rendered by the Latin idiom *toto caelo*, my choice, not Kant's wording; this should be flagged as such.","- \"Zusammenhäufung\" = \"congeries\"; \"Merkmale\" = \"characters\"; \"Erkenntnißfähigkeit\" = \"faculty of knowledge\".","- \"Receptivität\" is rendered \"receptivity\"; \"jene (die Erscheinung)\" is resolved as \"the appearance\", as the parenthesis indicates.","- Misspelling \"praftischen\" is silently corrected to \"praktischen\"."]],"krv-b-0192":["The Leibniz-Wolffian philosophy has accordingly given all enquiries into the nature and origin of our modes of knowledge a quite mistaken point of view, in that it regarded the distinction between sensibility and the intellectual as merely logical, whereas it is manifestly transcendental, and concerns not merely the form of distinctness or indistinctness, but the origin and content [of our knowledge]. Through sensibility we do not know the constitution of things in themselves indistinctly; we do not know it at all. And as soon as we remove our subjective constitution, the represented object, with the properties which sensible intuition ascribed to it, is nowhere to be met with, nor can it be met with, since it is just this subjective constitution that determines the form of the object as appearance.",["- Restated the \"so daß\" consequence as two sentences. \"die erstere\" is resolved as sensibility.","- \"[of our knowledge]\" is supplied for \"derselben\" (the antecedent is \"Erkenntnisse\"); this is a judgment, since \"Sinnlichkeit\" and \"Intellectuellen\" are also grammatically possible.","- \"überall nirgend\" is rendered \"nowhere\".","- The \"Leibniz⸗Wolffische\" hyphen is silently regularised (OCR)."]],"krv-b-0193":["Otherwise we do distinguish, among appearances, that which essentially belongs to their intuition and holds for every human sense in general, from that which belongs to it only accidentally, being valid not in relation to sensibility in general but only in relation to a special position or organisation of this or that sense. The former kind of knowledge is then said to represent the object in itself, the latter only its appearance. But this distinction is merely empirical. If we stop short at it (as commonly happens), and do not regard this empirical intuition in turn (as ought to be done) as mere appearance, so that nothing that concerns any thing in itself is to be found in it, our transcendental distinction is lost, and we then believe that we know things in themselves, although everywhere in the world of sense, even in the profoundest investigation of its objects, we have to do with nothing but appearances.\n\nThus we shall indeed entitle the rainbow a mere appearance in a sun-shower, and this rain the thing in itself. This is correct, if we understand the latter concept only physically, as that which in universal experience, and under all different positions relatively to the senses, is determined in intuition thus and not otherwise. But if we take this empirical element in general, and ask, without regard to its agreement with every human sense, whether it too represents an object in itself (not the raindrops, for these, as appearances, are already empirical objects), the question of the relation of the representation to the object is transcendental. Not only are these drops mere appearances, but even their round shape, nay even the space in which they fall, is nothing in itself, being merely a modification or foundation of our sensible intuition; the transcendental object remains unknown to us.",["- Divided the passage at \"So werden wir\"; the long first sentence is also split after \"Und da nennt man …\".","- \"nennt man … eine solche, die … vorstellt\" is smoothed to \"is said to represent\"; \"Erkenntniß\" kept as \"knowledge\".","- \"Stellung oder Organisation\" rendered \"position or organisation\"; \"Lagen zu den Sinnen\" as \"positions relatively to the senses\".","- \"Grundlagen\" = \"foundations\", retained though some editions read \"Grundlage\" in the singular.","- OCR line-break dashes (\"An— schauung\") silently removed."]],"krv-b-0194":["The second important concern of our transcendental aesthetic is that it should win some favour not merely as a plausible hypothesis, but should be as certain and indubitable as can ever be demanded of a theory that is to serve as an organon. To make this certainty fully evident, we shall select some case in which its validity may become manifest, and which may serve to throw further light on what was stated in §3.¹",["- The passage is corrupt or cut off at the end: \"wollen wir irgend einen Fall an woran dessen Gültigkeit … werden und … dienen!) kann\" is garbled by OCR. I read \"einen Fall [annehmen,] woran …\" and have translated that sense without completing anything further; the reading is doubtful.","- The footnote marker \"!)\" is rendered ¹, but no footnote text is given in the passage, so none is supplied.","- \"Organon\" is retained; \"scheinbare Hypothese\" = \"plausible hypothesis\" (not \"merely apparent\"), which is a judgment.","- \"mehrer Klarheit\" rendered \"further light\"."]],"krv-b-0195":["Suppose, then, that space and time are in themselves objective, and are conditions of the possibility of things in themselves. In the first place, it then appears that a large number of apodeictic and synthetic propositions *a priori* are to be found concerning both, but especially concerning space; and for this reason we shall here take space as our chief example. Since the propositions of geometry are known as synthetic *a priori* and with apodeictic certainty, I ask: Whence do you derive such propositions, and on what does our understanding rely in order to arrive at truths so absolutely necessary and universally valid? There is no other way than through concepts or through intuitions; and both of these are given either *a priori* or *a posteriori*. The latter, namely empirical concepts, and likewise that on which they are grounded, empirical intuition, can yield no synthetic proposition save one that is itself merely empirical, that is, a proposition of experience. Such a proposition can therefore never contain necessity and absolute universality, and these are none the less the characteristic mark of all propositions of geometry.\n\nAs to the former and only other means, namely to arrive at such modes of knowledge through mere concepts or through intuitions *a priori*, it is clear that from mere concepts no synthetic knowledge whatever, but only analytic knowledge, can be obtained. Take the proposition that two straight lines cannot enclose a space, and that no figure is therefore possible, and try to derive it from the concept of straight lines and of the number two. Or take the proposition that a figure is possible with three straight lines, and try likewise to derive it from these concepts alone. All your labour is in vain, and you find yourselves constrained to take refuge in intuition, as geometry in fact always does. You therefore give yourselves an object in intuition. But of what kind is this intuition? Is it pure intuition *a priori*, or empirical? If it were the latter, no universally valid proposition, still less an apodeictic one, could ever result from it; for experience can never furnish such. You must therefore give your object *a priori* in intuition, and upon this found your synthetic proposition. Now if there did not lie in you a faculty of intuiting *a priori*; if this subjective condition were not, as regards its form, at the same time the universal condition *a priori* under which alone the object of this (outer) intuition is itself possible; [the passage breaks off here]",["- The opening sentence is divided; \"Setzet … so zeigt sich erstlich\" is kept as one clause, with the example of space set in a separate sentence.","- \"Was aber das erstere und einzige Mittel sein würde … so ist klar\" is an irregular construction (the \"Was\" clause is left hanging); I have smoothed it into \"As to the former and only other means … it is clear\", the sense being unaffected.","- \"Wahr- ı heilen\" (OCR) read as *Wahrheiten*; \"empi- 1 risch\" as *empirisch*; \"ana: lytische\" as *analytische*. Page/line marks omitted.","- \"Erkenntnissen\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\"; \"erkannt werden\" as \"known\".","- \"apodiktisch\" rendered \"apodeictic\" (Kemp Smith's spelling).","- \"Vermögen\" rendered \"faculty\" here, as it is a power of intuiting.","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"wäre diese sub-\"); the translation stops there and does not complete it. The continuation, \"subjective condition\", is supplied from the next passage's German and is only echoed in the final clause."]],"krv-b-0196":["[Editorial note, as given at the head of the passage: \"and … serve\": addition of A2.]\n\n… subjective condition were not, as regards its form, at the same time the universal condition *a priori* under which alone the object of this (outer) intuition is itself possible; and if the object (the triangle) were something in itself, apart from any relation to your subject: how could you say that what lies necessarily in your subjective conditions for constructing a triangle must also necessarily belong to the triangle in itself? For you could not add to your concepts (of three lines) anything new (the figure) which must therefore necessarily be met with in the object, since this object is given prior to your knowledge, and not by means of it. If, then, space (and likewise time) were not a mere form of your intuition, containing *a priori* conditions under which alone things can be outer objects for you, objects which apart from these subjective conditions are nothing in themselves, you could determine nothing whatsoever synthetically *a priori* regarding outer objects.\n\nIt is therefore not merely possible, or even probable, but indubitably certain, that space and time, as the necessary conditions of all (outer and inner) experience, are merely subjective conditions of all our intuition. In relation to them all objects are therefore mere appearances, and are not things given in this manner for themselves. Of these appearances, much can for this reason be said *a priori* so far as their form is concerned; but nothing whatever can ever be said of the thing in itself which may underlie these appearances.",["- The opening \"[…] und . . . dienen: Zusatz von A2\" is an editorial footnote of the Academy Edition (marginal note on a variant), not Kant's text; I have kept it as a bracketed editorial note, not translated further, as it is cut off.","- The passage begins mid-sentence (\"jective Bedingung\"); the initial \"sub-\" belongs to the previous passage, so I have supplied \"… subjective condition\" with an ellipsis to join the two.","- The long conditional sentence is restated: the \"wäre … wäre … wie könntet ihr\" series is kept, with the final \"Wäre also nicht …\" sentence given its own consequence.","- \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"; \"Erscheinungen\" \"appearances\"; \"Ding an sich selbst\" \"thing in itself\"."]],"krv-b-0197":["II. [Editorial: a bracketed mark follows \"II.\" in the source.] In confirmation of this theory of the ideality of outer as well as inner sense, and therefore of all objects of the senses as mere appearances, the following remark may especially serve: that everything in our knowledge which belongs to intuition (feeling of pleasure and displeasure and the will, which are not modes of knowledge at all, being excepted) contains nothing but mere relations: of places in an intuition (extension), change of places (motion), and laws according to which this change is determined (moving forces). What is present in the place, or what, besides the change of place, is operative in the things themselves, is not given through these. Now through mere relations a thing in itself is not known. It is therefore reasonable to judge that, since outer sense gives us nothing but representations of relation, it can contain in its representation only the relation of an object to the subject, and not the inner [nature] which belongs to the object in itself.\n\nThe same holds of inner intuition. Not only do the representations of outer sense constitute the proper material with which we occupy our mind; but the time in which we posit these representations, which itself precedes the consciousness of them in experience and underlies it as the formal condition of the manner in which we posit them in the mind, already contains relations of succession, of coexistence, and of that which is coexistent with succession (the permanent). Now that which, as representation, can precede every act of thinking anything is intuition; and if it contains nothing but relations, it is the form of intuition. Since this form represents nothing except in so far as something is posited in the mind, it can be nothing other than the way in which the mind is affected through its own activity, namely through this positing of its representation, and so through itself; that is, it is an inner sense as regards its form. Everything represented through a sense is to that extent always appearance. Either, then, an inner sense must not be admitted at all, or the subject, which is the object of that sense, can be represented through it only as appearance, and not as it would judge of itself if its intuition were mere self-activity, that is, intellectual.\n\nThe whole difficulty here rests only on how a subject can inwardly intuit itself; but this difficulty is common to every theory. Consciousness of self (apperception) is the simple representation of the \"I\"; and if through this alone everything manifold in the subject were given by self-activity, the inner intuition would be intellectual. In man this consciousness requires inner perception of the manifold which is antecedently given in the subject; and the manner in which this is given in the mind without spontaneity must, on account of this difference, be entitled sensibility. If the faculty of becoming conscious of oneself is to seek out (apprehend) what lies in the mind, it must affect the mind, and only in this way can it produce an intuition of itself. The form of this intuition, however, which antecedently underlies it in the mind, determines in the representation of time the manner in which the manifold is together in the mind. The mind thus intuits itself, not as it would represent itself if its activity were immediate and self-originating, but as it is affected from within, [and so as it appears to itself, not as it is.]",["- Sentence division: the very long sentences on inner intuition and on consciousness of self are split into several; their content is unchanged.","- \"Gefühl der Luft und Unlust\" is an OCR slip for *Lust und Unlust*, rendered \"pleasure and displeasure\". Other OCR slips corrected silently (*Gemüüthe*, *Wahr: nehmung*, *Er- as scheinung*), and page marks omitted.","- \"das Innere\" supplied as \"inner [nature]\" in brackets; the sense requires a noun.","- \"II. )\" in the source is probably a stray footnote mark; I have shown it as a bracket rather than guess its content.","- The passage breaks off at \"selbstthätig\"; the last clause is completed from the next passages (krv-b-0199, 0200) and is bracketed accordingly, to show it belongs to those fragments.","- \"Vermögen sich bewußt zu werden\" rendered \"faculty of becoming conscious of oneself\"; \"Spontaneität\" \"spontaneity\"; \"Sinnlichkeit heißen\" \"be entitled sensibility\"."]],"krv-b-0198":["II. Section. General Remarks. 71",["- This is a running head and page number (B 71). By the instructions running heads and page numbers are left out of the translation, but I give it here since the passage consists of nothing else."]],"krv-b-0199":["[…] would represent itself, but according to the manner in which it is affected from within,",["- Continues the previous passage (\"nicht wie es unmittelbar selbstthätig / vorstellen würde\"); translated as it stands, with the ellipsis marking the join."]],"krv-b-0200":["and consequently as it appears to itself, not as it is.",["- \"Folglich\" rendered \"consequently\"; \"es\" refers to the mind (*Gemüth*), as in the preceding clause."]],"krv-b-0201":["III. When I say that in space and time the intuition both of outer objects and the self-intuition of the mind",[]],"krv-b-0202":["represents both as they affect our senses, that is, as they appear, this is not to say that these objects are mere illusion. For",["- The sentence is split across two passages (0201/0202); I have kept it unbroken in the English, but left the passage division as given. \"5\" is a line number and is omitted.","- \"Schein\" rendered \"illusion\" (Kemp Smith's usual choice), as opposed to *Erscheinung*, \"appearance\"."]],"krv-b-0203":["[This passage is the fragment \"das nicht sagen, daß diese Gegenstände ein bloßer Schein wären. Denn\", already translated above in 0202.]",["- Passage 0203 consists of the words \"das will nicht sagen, daß diese Gegenstände ein bloßer Schein wären. Denn\", which the German of 0202 and 0203 splits; see 0202 for the translation, to avoid duplicating text."]],"krv-b-0204":["in appearance the objects, and even the properties [Beschaffen-]",["- Breaks off mid-word (Beschaffen⸗); I give the unfinished word in brackets and do not complete the sentence."]],"krv-b-0205":["[-heiten] which we ascribe to them, are always regarded as something actually given,",["- Continues the word broken off in 0204. The sentence continues in 0206."]],"krv-b-0206":["only that, in so far as this property depends solely upon the mode of intuition of the subject in its relation to the given object, this object as appearance is distinguished from itself as object in itself.",["- The line number \"10\" is dropped. \"Anschauungsart\" is rendered \"mode of intuition\". Continues in 0207."]],"krv-b-0207":["Thus I do not say that bodies merely *seem* to be outside",["- Breaks off; continues in 0208. \"scheinen\" is rendered \"seem\" to keep the contrast with *Schein*."]],"krv-b-0208":["me, or that my soul *seems* only to be given in my self-consciousness, when I assert that the quality of space and of time, in conformity with which, as a condition of their existence, I posit both,",["- Line number \"15\" dropped. The sentence is broken off at \"mei⸗\" and continues in 0209. I have rendered \"mei⸗ner\" as \"my\" at the start of 0209 below.","- The relative clause \"welcher … gemäß\" is taken to refer to \"Qualität\", and I have restated it as a participial clause."]],"krv-b-0209":["lies in my mode of intuition and not in these objects in themselves. It would",["- \"mei⸗ner\" is rejoined across the break (0208/0209); I have placed \"my\" at the end of 0208 in effect, and rendered the whole as \"in my mode of intuition\"."]],"krv-b-0210":["be my own fault if I made mere illusion [*Schein*] out of that which I ought to reckon as appearance [*Erscheinung*].*) But this does not happen according to our principle of the ideality of all our sensible intuitions;",["- The stray quotation mark in \"machte.“)\" is an OCR slip for the footnote marker \"*)\", which I have retained. The footnote text appears in 0214–0217.","- \"Schein\" = illusion; \"Erscheinung\" = appearance."]],"krv-b-0211":["on the contrary, if objective reality is ascribed to those forms of representation, then",["- Line number \"20\" dropped. Continues in 0212."]],"krv-b-0212":["it is impossible to avoid the transformation of everything thereby into mere illusion.",["- \"wandelt\" is taken from the next passage's line start (ver⸗wandelt, 0212/0213); the German \"daß nicht alles … verwandelt werde\" is rendered without the expletive \"nicht\"."]],"krv-b-0213":["For if space and time are regarded as properties which, as regards their possibility, would have to be found in things in themselves,",["- Continues in 0214."]],"krv-b-0214":["and if we reflect upon the absurdities in which we are then involved, since two infinite things, which are not substances, nor yet anything actually inherent in substances, yet must exist, and indeed must be the necessary condition of the existence of all things, would also remain even if all existing things were removed [—this sentence is completed in 0219]\n\n---\n*) The predicates of the appearance can be ascribed to the object itself in relation to our sense, e.g. the red colour or the scent to the rose;",["- The OCR interleaves the footnote and the main text; the main text's continuation (\"nicht etwas wirklich den Substanzen Inhärirendes …\") is in 0219, and I have not completed it here beyond a bracketed pointer. The bracketed clause is a gloss of mine, not Kant's.","- Footnote text begins here and continues in 0215–0217."]],"krv-b-0215":["but illusion can never be ascribed as a predicate to the object, for the very reason that it ascribes to the object for itself",["- Continues in 0216; the sentence is footnote text."]],"krv-b-0216":["what belongs to it only in relation to the senses, or to the subject in general, e.g. the two handles which were at first ascribed to Saturn. That which is not to be found in the object in itself at all, but always in its relation to the subject, and is inseparable from the representation of the",["- Line marker \"o\" dropped. \"Henkel\" = handles (Saturn's rings as first seen)."]],"krv-b-0217":["latter, is appearance; and so the predicates of space and of time are rightly ascribed to the objects of the senses as such, and in this there is no illusion. If, on the other hand, I ascribe redness to the rose in itself, handles to Saturn, or extension in itself to all outer objects, without attending to a determinate relation of these objects to the subject and limiting my judgment to that,",["- Stray \"s\" dropped. \"Dagegen\" is rendered \"on the other hand\"; the sentence continues in 0218–0219."]],"krv-b-0218":["then, and only then, does illusion arise.",["- \"mein Nr⸗ / theil\" is an OCR slip for \"mein Urtheil\", already used at the end of 0217 (\"my judgment\"). The sentence \"alsdann allererst entspringt der Schein\" appears in 0219's opening."]],"krv-b-0219":["...so one cannot well blame the good Berkeley if he degraded bodies to mere illusion. Indeed our own existence, if made dependent in this way upon the self-subsistent reality of a non-thing such as time, would be transformed with it into sheer illusion, an absurdity of which no one has hitherto been guilty.",["- The OCR displaces the clause \"alsdann allererst entspringt der Schein\" (see 0218). The main-sentence fragment \"nicht etwas wirklich … aufgehoben werden:\" is left to the fuller sentence begun in 0214; I do not translate it twice, so the English here picks up at \"so kann man…\".","- \"Undinges\" = non-thing. \"nies | wand\" is rejoined as \"niemand\"; the sign | is an Academy page-break and is omitted in the English."]],"krv-b-0220":["IV. In natural theology, where we think an object which not only can be for us no object of intuition, but can in itself be in no way an object of sensible intuition, we are careful to remove from all its intuition (for all its knowledge must be of this kind, and not thinking, which always betrays limitations) the conditions of time and space. But by what right can this be done if we have previously made both into forms of things in themselves, and indeed forms which, as conditions of the existence of things *a priori*, would remain even if we had removed the things themselves? For as conditions of all existence in general they would have to be so of the existence of God also. Nothing remains, if we are unwilling to make them objective forms of all things, but to make them subjective forms of our outer as well as inner mode of intuition. This mode is entitled sensible because it is not original, that is, not such that through it the very existence of the object of intuition is given (and such, so far as we can see, can belong only to the primordial being), but is dependent on the existence of the object, and so is possible only through the subject's capacity of representation being affected by it.",["- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge. \"Urwesen\" = primordial being. \"Vorstellungsfähigkeit\" = capacity of representation. Long final sentence restated in part; \"heißt\" = \"is entitled\"."]],"krv-b-0221":["Nor is it necessary that we limit the mode of intuition in space and time to the sensibility of man. It may be that every finite thinking being must necessarily agree with man in this (though we cannot decide that); yet it does not, on account of this universal validity, cease to be sensibility, precisely because it is derived (*intuitus derivativus*), not original (*intuitus originarius*), and therefore not intellectual intuition. For the reason just stated, intellectual intuition seems to belong to the primordial being alone, never to a being dependent both in its existence and in its intuition (which determines its existence in relation to given objects); although this last remark must be counted, in our aesthetic theory, only as elucidation, not as ground of proof. Conclusion of the Transcendental Aesthetic.",["- The stray \"a\" and \";\" are OCR slips; \"als a welche\" read \"als welche\". \"ästhetischen\" = aesthetic (Kant's sense of the study of sensibility). Final heading kept as it stands.","- Sentence restated: the \"als welche\" clause is made a separate sentence."]],"krv-b-0222":["Here, then, we have one of the requisite factors for the solution of the general problem of transcendental philosophy: How are synthetic propositions *a priori* possible? namely, pure intuitions *a priori*, space and time. In these, if in an *a priori* judgment we wish to go beyond the given concept, we find that which can be discovered *a priori* not in the concept but in the intuition corresponding to it, and can be synthetically combined with the concept. Such judgments, however, on this very ground never extend further than to objects of the senses, and can hold only for objects of possible experience.",["- \"a pri- » ori\" is an OCR slip for \"a priori\". The final \")\" is retained in the German but is unmatched, so I have dropped it. Long sentence divided into three."]],"krv-b-0223":["Second Part of the Transcendental Doctrine of Elements.\nThe Transcendental Logic.\nIntroduction.\nIdea of a Transcendental Logic.\nI.\nOf Logic in General.",["- Heading; \"Der\" before \"Transscendentalen Elementarlehre\" is taken with \"Zweiter Theil\" (\"Of the Transcendental Doctrine of Elements, Second Part\"). Kemp Smith's own rendering is \"Transcendental Doctrine of Elements\"."]],"krv-b-0224":["Our knowledge springs from two fundamental sources of the mind. The first is the capacity of receiving representations (receptivity for impressions); the second is the power of knowing an object through these representations (spontaneity of concepts). Through the first an object is given to us; through the second it is thought in relation to that representation (which is a mere determination of the mind). Intuition and concepts constitute, therefore, the elements of all our knowledge, so that neither concepts without an intuition in some way corresponding to them, nor intuition without concepts, can yield knowledge.¹) Both are either pure or empirical. They are empirical when sensation (which presupposes the actual presence of the object) is contained in them; pure when no sensation is mixed with the representation. Sensation may be entitled the matter of sensible knowledge. Pure intuition accordingly contains only the form under which something is intuited, and the pure concept only the form of the thought of an object in general. Only pure intuitions or concepts are possible *a priori*; empirical ones only *a posteriori*.",["- Sentences restated and divided for clarity. \"Cm- 2 pfindung\" is an OCR slip for \"Empfindung\". The footnote marker \"!)\" is read as \"1)\" and the footnote itself is not present in the passage.","- \"Eindrücke\" = impressions. \"Man kann die letztere … nennen\" rendered \"Sensation may be entitled…\", with \"die letztere\" resolved as sensation (Empfindung)."]],"krv-b-0225":["If we choose to entitle the receptivity of our mind, in so far as it is affected in some manner, to receive representations, sensibility, then the capacity for producing representations from itself, that is, the spontaneity of knowledge, is the understanding. Our nature is so constituted that intuition can never be other than sensible, that is, can contain only the mode in which we are affected by objects. The capacity for thinking the object of sensible intuition is, on the other hand, the understanding. Neither of these properties is to be preferred to the other. Without sensibility no object would be given to us, and without understanding none would be thought. Thoughts without content are empty; intuitions without concepts are blind. It is, therefore, just as necessary to make our concepts sensible, that is, to add the object to them in intuition, as to make our intuitions intelligible, that is, to bring them under concepts. These two powers or capacities cannot exchange their functions. The understanding can intuit nothing, the senses can think nothing. Only through their union can knowledge arise. But that is no reason for confusing their respective contributions; on the contrary, we have great cause carefully to separate and distinguish them from one another. Accordingly we distinguish the science of the rules of sensibility in general, that is, aesthetic, from the science of the rules of the understanding in general, that is, logic.",[]],"krv-b-0226":["Logic, in turn, can be undertaken with a twofold purpose: either as logic of the general or as logic of the special employment of the understanding. The first contains the absolutely necessary rules of thought, without which no employment of the understanding takes place at all. It therefore deals with the understanding without regard to the diversity of the objects to which it may be directed. The logic of the special employment of the understanding contains the rules for thinking correctly about a certain kind of objects. The former may be entitled elementary logic; the latter, the *organon* of this or that science. The latter is for the most part placed at the beginning in the schools as a propaedeutic to the sciences, although in the course of human reason it is the last thing to be reached, being attained only when the science is already long complete and needs only the finishing touch towards its correction and perfection. For we must already know the objects in a fairly high degree if we are to indicate the rules by which a science of them can be constituted. al [sic: OCR residue]",[]],"krv-b-0227":["General logic is, again, either pure or applied. In the former we abstract from all the empirical conditions under which our understanding is exercised, for instance, from the influence of the senses, the play of imagination, the laws of memory, the force of habit, inclination, and so forth, and so also from the sources of prejudice; indeed, from all causes whatsoever from which certain modes of knowledge may arise or be falsely imputed to us. For these causes concern the understanding only under certain circumstances of its application, and experience is required to know them. A general but pure logic has therefore to do solely with *a priori* principles. It is a *canon* of understanding and of reason, but only in respect of the formal element in their employment, be the content what it may (empirical or transcendental). General logic is entitled applied when it is directed to the rules of the employment of the understanding under the subjective empirical conditions which psychology teaches us. It has, therefore, empirical principles, although it is general in so far as it concerns the employment of the understanding without distinction of objects. For this reason it is neither a canon of the understanding in general, nor an *organon* of particular sciences, but merely a *cathartic* of the common understanding.",[]],"krv-b-0228":["In general logic, therefore, the part which is to constitute the pure doctrine of reason must be entirely separated from that which constitutes applied logic (though still general). The former alone is properly science, although short and dry, as the scholastically correct exposition of an elementary doctrine of the understanding requires. In this science logicians must always keep two rules in view.",[]],"krv-b-0229":["(1) As general logic, it abstracts from all content of the knowledge of the understanding and from the diversity of its objects, and has to do with nothing but the mere form of thought.",[]],"krv-b-0230":["(2) As pure logic, it has no empirical principles, and so draws nothing (as people have sometimes been persuaded) from psychology, which therefore has no influence whatsoever on the canon of the understanding.",[]],"krv-b-0231":["It is a demonstrated doctrine, and everything in it must be certain completely *a priori*.",[]],"krv-b-0232":["What I entitle applied logic (contrary to the usual meaning of this word, according to which it is supposed to contain certain exercises for which pure logic gives the rule) is a representation of the understanding and of the rules of its necessary employment *in concreto*, that is, under the contingent conditions of the subject, which may hinder or promote this employment, and which are all given only empirically. It treats of attention, its hindrance and consequences, of the origin of error, of the state of doubt, of scruple, of conviction, and so forth. General and pure logic stands to it as pure morality, which contains merely the necessary moral laws of a free will in general, stands to the proper doctrine of virtue. The latter considers these laws under the hindrances of the feelings, inclinations and passions to which human beings are more or less subject, and can never furnish a true and demonstrated science, since, like the applied logic just mentioned, it requires empirical and psychological principles.",[]],"krv-b-0233":["II.",[]],"krv-b-0234":["Of Transcendental Logic.",[]],"krv-b-0235":["General logic abstracts, as we have shown, from all content of knowledge, that is, from all relation of knowledge to the object, and considers only the logical form in the relation of modes of knowledge to one another, that is, the form of thought in general. But since there are both pure and empirical intuitions (as the Transcendental Aesthetic shows), a distinction might likewise be found between pure and empirical thinking of objects. In that case there would be a logic in which we did not abstract from all content of knowledge. For a logic containing merely the rules of the pure thinking of an object would exclude all those modes of knowledge which are of empirical content. It would also deal with the origin of our modes of knowledge of objects, in so far as that origin cannot be ascribed to the objects. General logic, by contrast, has nothing to do with this origin of knowledge. It considers representations, whether originally given *a priori* in ourselves or only empirically, solely according to the laws by which the understanding employs them in relation to one another when it thinks. It treats, therefore, only of the form of understanding which can be supplied to representations, from whatever source they may otherwise have arisen.",[]],"krv-b-0236":["[No translatable text: the OCR gives only the fragment \"en\", apparently residue of a running head or line end.]",["- 0225: \"Wollen wir … nennen\" restated as a conditional; \"Receptivität … Sinnlichkeit nennen\" rendered \"entitle\". \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Spontaneität des Erkenntnisses\" kept literally. \"Afficirt\" = \"affected\".","- 0225: \"Eigenschaften\" (neither of these *properties*) kept literal; \"Fähigkeiten\" = capacities, \"Vermögen\" = powers. \"Sinne\" = senses. The OCR break \"ge— geben\" and \"unterschei— den\" silently mended.","- 0226: The stray \"al\" at the end is OCR residue and is not translated; I flag it in the text. \"Elementarlogik\" = elementary logic; \"Organon\" left in Latin italics. Sentence on the schools slightly recast.","- 0227: OCR \"Piychologie\" corrected to *Psychologie*; \"ste\" to \"sie\". The sign | before \"den Verstand\" is dropped as a page-break mark, and line numbers are removed. \"Kanon\" / \"Kathartikon\" given as *canon* / *cathartic*.","- 0227: \"untergeschoben\" rendered \"falsely imputed\" (literally \"foisted upon\"). \"Inhalt … (empirisch oder transscendental)\" retained as in Kant.","- 0230–0231: A running head (\"Einleitung. Ii\") interrupts the sentence and is omitted; the break between 0230 and 0231 is kept, so 0231 begins with \"It is\" (Sie). Read \"also\" as \"therefore\" (mithin).","- 0232: Long final sentence divided into three; \"Tugendlehre\" = doctrine of virtue; stray \")\" and \"s\" OCR marks dropped. \"Vorstellung\" retained as \"representation\".","- 0235: \"Erkenntnisse\" in the plural = \"modes of knowledge\". Pronoun \"Sie\" at \"Sie würde auch…\" resolved as the supposed new logic; \"diesem Ursprunge\" sentence divided in two. 0236 is OCR residue only."]],"krv-b-0237":["Here I make a remark which extends its influence over all the considerations that follow, and which must be kept well in view. It is this: that not every mode of knowledge *a priori*, but only that through which we know that, and how, certain representations (intuitions or concepts) are applied, or are possible, purely *a priori*, is to be entitled transcendental (that is, the possibility of knowledge, or the use of knowledge, *a priori*). Consequently neither space nor any geometrical determination of it *a priori* is a transcendental representation. Only the knowledge that these representations are not of empirical origin at all, and the possibility of their yet being able to relate *a priori* to objects of experience, can be entitled transcendental. Similarly, the application of space to objects in general would also be transcendental; but if it is restricted solely to objects of the senses, it is entitled empirical. The distinction between the transcendental and the empirical belongs therefore only to the critique of modes of knowledge, and does not concern their relation to their object.",[]],"krv-b-0238":["Anticipating, then, that there may perhaps be concepts which relate *a priori* to objects, not as pure or sensible intuitions but merely as acts of pure thought, and which are therefore concepts, though neither of empirical nor of aesthetic origin, we form for ourselves in advance the idea of a science of pure understanding and of rational knowledge, through which we think objects entirely *a priori*. Such a science, which would determine the origin, the extent and the objective validity of such modes of knowledge, would have to be entitled transcendental logic. For it has to deal solely with the laws of understanding and of reason, but only in so far as they are related *a priori* to objects, and not, as general logic does, to the empirical and the pure modes of rational knowledge without distinction.",[]],"krv-b-0239":["III.\nOn the Division of General Logic\ninto Analytic and Dialectic.",[]],"krv-b-0240":["The old and famous question by which logicians were supposed to be driven into a corner, and which was meant to bring them either to be caught in a miserable *diallelus* (circle in proof) or to confess their ignorance, and with it the vanity of their whole art, is this: What is truth? The nominal definition of truth, that it is the agreement of knowledge with its object, is granted and presupposed. What is demanded is to know what is the universal and secure criterion of the truth of any and every item of knowledge.",[]],"krv-b-0241":["It is already a great and necessary proof of cleverness or insight to know what one may reasonably ask. For if the question is in itself absurd and calls for needless answers, it brings, besides the shame of him who raises it, the further disadvantage, at times, of misleading the incautious hearer into absurd answers, and of presenting the laughable spectacle of one man (as the ancients said) milking the he-goat while the other holds a sieve beneath.",[]],"krv-b-0242":["If truth consists in the agreement of knowledge with its object, this object must thereby be distinguished from other objects. For knowledge is false if it does not agree with the object to which it is related, even though it contains something which might well hold of other objects. Now a universal criterion of truth would be one valid of all modes of knowledge without distinction of their objects. But it is clear that, since in such a criterion we abstract from all content of knowledge (relation to its object), and truth concerns precisely this content, it is quite impossible and absurd to ask for a mark of the truth of this content of knowledge. A sufficient and yet at the same time universal sign of truth cannot therefore possibly be given. Since we have already entitled the content of knowledge its matter, we must say that no universal sign can be demanded of the truth of knowledge so far as its matter is concerned, because such a demand is self-contradictory.",[]],"krv-b-0243":["As regards knowledge in respect of its mere form (setting aside all content), it is equally clear that logic, in so far as it expounds the universal and necessary rules of the understanding, must in these very rules furnish criteria of truth. For whatever contradicts these rules is false, since the understanding would then be in conflict with its own universal rules of thinking, and so with itself. But these criteria concern only the form of truth, that is, of thought in general; and so far they are quite correct, but are not sufficient. For although a [mode of knowledge] may be in complete accordance with logical form, that is, may not contradict itself, it can still contradict its object. Thus the merely logical criterion of truth, namely the agreement of a [mode of] knowledge with the universal and formal laws of understanding and of reason, is indeed the *conditio sine qua non*, and so the negative condition, of all truth. But further than this logic cannot go, and error which concerns not the form but the content, logic has no touchstone to discover.",[]],"krv-b-0244":["General logic resolves the whole formal business of understanding and of reason into its elements, and exhibits these as principles of all logical assessment of our knowledge. This part of logic may therefore be entitled analytic, and is for that very reason at least the",[]],"krv-b-0245":["negative touchstone of truth. For all knowledge must first be tested and appraised in respect of its form by these rules, before it is itself investigated in respect of its content, in order to determine whether it contains positive truth in regard to its object. But the mere form of knowledge, however completely it may accord with logical laws, is still far from sufficing to establish the material (objective) truth of the knowledge. Hence no one can venture with logic alone to judge of objects, or to assert anything about them, without having first obtained well-grounded information about them outside logic, so as then merely to attempt to use and connect them in a coherent whole according to logical laws, or better still, merely to test them by these laws. Yet there is something so seductive in the possession of so specious an art, that of giving to all our modes of knowledge the form of understanding, however empty and poor we may still be in regard to their content, that this general logic, which is merely a canon of judgment, has been used as if it were an organon for the actual production of objective assertions, or at least for the semblance of such; and so has in fact been misused. General logic, as a supposed organon, is entitled dialectic.",["- 0237: \"Anmerkung\" rendered \"remark\"; the OCR \"kann n\" read as \"kann\"; \"Gez 2 genstand\" restored as \"Gegenstand\". Long first sentence kept, with the parenthesis retained; second restated as \"Consequently…\". \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"/\"mode of knowledge\" by number.","- 0238: \"Gez 3 setzen\" restored as \"Gesetzen\"; the footnote-like numerals \"2\", \"3\" are OCR residue and were dropped. \"ästhetisch\" kept as \"aesthetic\".","- 0240: \"Namenerflärung\" read as \"Namenerklärung\" (nominal definition); \"Diallele\" glossed in brackets-free parentheses as circle in proof. Last sentence's \"all— gemeine\" restored as \"allgemeine\".","- 0241: \"fole\" read as \"solle\"; \"unterhält\" rendered \"holds beneath\".","- 0242: Divided into somewhat shorter sentences at \"A sufficient…\"; \"Kennzeichen/Merkmal/Kriterium\" rendered \"sign/mark/criterion\".","- 0243: \"Gr- | kenntniß\" restored as \"Erkenntniß\", and \"Das Erkenntniß\" in the opening treated as \"Erkenntniß\" (neuter form). Square brackets mark \"[mode of] knowledge\", supplied for the singular; the signs \"|\" were not carried into the English as they mark OCR page breaks, not the Academy apparatus. Footnote marker \"1\" omitted: the footnote text is not given in the passage.","- 0244–0245: The sentence breaks off after \"wenigstens der\" and resumes in 0245 with a fragment (\"ze\" = \"negative\"? read as \"[ne]gative\"; \"ze\" here is OCR residue). Translated as it stands, without completion; the rendering \"negative\" is supplied from the continuation \"ze negative Probirstein\", which is doubtful.","- 0245: \"einge: zögen\" read as \"eingezogen\"; \"Erkenntnisse\" after \"Weil… Erkenntnisses\" normalised; \"darum\" left untranslated as redundant. \"Kanon\", \"Organon\" kept in plain form as established English terms."]],"krv-b-0246":["However various the meaning in which the ancients employed this title [dialectic] for a science or an art, we can yet safely gather from their actual use of it that with them it was nothing else than the logic of illusion. It was a sophistical art of giving to one's ignorance, and indeed to one's deliberate deceptions, the appearance of truth, by imitating the method of thoroughness which logic in general prescribes, and by using its topic to embellish every empty pretence. Now we may take note of the following as a safe and useful warning: that general logic, when treated as an organon, is always a logic of illusion, that is, is dialectical. For since it teaches us nothing whatsoever regarding the content of knowledge, but only the formal conditions of agreement with the understanding, which for the rest are entirely indifferent in respect of objects, the attempt to employ it as an instrument (organon), in order to extend and enlarge, at least in pretence, our range of information, can end in nothing but idle talk, in which, with some semblance of truth, we assert whatever we please, or, if we so choose, challenge it.",["- The first sentence of the German is a fragment (\"Eine sophistische Kunst ... zu geben, daß man ... nachahmte\"); I have made it a complete sentence (\"It was a sophistical art of giving ... by imitating ...\"), smoothing the irregular \"daß\" construction.","- \"Benennung\" (title) is broken by OCR hyphenation; \"dialectic\" is supplied in brackets from the context.","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Kenntnisse\" rendered \"range of information\" to keep it distinct from *Erkenntnis*.","- \"Zumuthung\" = \"attempt\" (literally a demand or presumption); \"Schein\" = illusion in the first half, \"semblance\" in the last clause.","- Long final sentence kept as one but reordered; the \"alles, was man will, mit einigem Schein zu behaupten\" clause is restated as a relative clause.","- Stray OCR line numbers and hyphenation breaks removed."]],"krv-b-0247":["Such instruction is in no way befitting the dignity of philosophy. For this reason it has been preferred to reckon this title of dialectic as a critique of dialectical illusion, [and to assign it] to logic; and it is as such a critique that we wish it to be understood here.",["- \"beigezählt\" (\"reckoned among, added to\") has no explicit dative in the German beyond \"der Logik\"; the bracketed \"and to assign it\" smooths the construction.","- \"Unterweisung\" = instruction; \"Benennung\" = title."]],"krv-b-0248":["VI\n\nOn the Division of Transcendental Logic\ninto\nTranscendental Analytic and Dialectic.",["- \"TVi\" is an OCR slip for the section number \"VI\"; corrected silently in the sense of the method."]],"krv-b-0249":["In a transcendental logic we isolate the understanding (as above, in the transcendental aesthetic, we isolated sensibility), and select from our knowledge only that part of thought which has its origin solely in the understanding. The employment of this pure knowledge rests, however, on the condition that objects are given to us in intuition, to which it can be applied. For without intuition all our knowledge lacks objects, and remains in consequence completely empty. That part of transcendental logic, therefore, which expounds the elements of the pure knowledge of understanding, and the principles without which no object can anywhere be thought, is transcendental analytic, and is at the same time a logic of truth. For no knowledge can contradict it without at once losing all content, that is, all relation to any object, and therefore all truth.\n\nBut it is very alluring and seductive to make use of these pure modes of knowledge of the understanding, and these principles, by themselves, and even beyond the limits of experience; yet experience alone can supply us with the matter (objects) to which those pure concepts of the understanding can be applied. The understanding thus runs the risk, through empty sophistries, of making a material use of the merely formal principles of the pure understanding, and of passing judgment indiscriminately upon objects which are not given to us, and which perhaps cannot be given in any manner whatsoever. Since analytic should properly be no more than a canon for the appraisement of empirical employment, it is misused if we allow it to pass as the organon of a universal and unrestricted employment, and venture with the pure understanding alone to judge, assert and decide synthetically regarding objects in general. The employment of the pure understanding would then be dialectical.\n\nThe second part of transcendental logic must therefore be a critique of this dialectical illusion, and is entitled transcendental dialectic. It is not an art of dogmatically producing such illusion (an art, unfortunately, very current among the manifold jugglings of metaphysics), but a critique of understanding and reason in respect of their hyperphysical employment. Its purpose is to expose the false illusion of their groundless pretensions, and to reduce their claims to discovery and extension, which they suppose themselves able to achieve merely by means of transcendental principles, to the mere task of passing judgment on the pure understanding and safeguarding it against sophistical illusions.",["- I divided the long sentence beginning \"Weil es aber sehr anlockend ...\" into two and recast the \"Weil ... so\" construction; the paragraph is also divided into three for clarity.","- \"Da sie also ...\" — \"sie\" is the analytic (the understanding's part of the logic), not \"Verstand\"; I made this explicit as \"analytic\". The antecedent is loose in the German.","- \"Verstandeserkenntniß\" = \"knowledge of the understanding\"; plural \"Verstandeserkenntnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge of the understanding\".","- \"Beurtheilung\" = \"appraisement\"; \"Verwahrung\" = \"safeguarding\"; \"hyperphysisch\" kept as \"hyperphysical\".","- A \"|\" in the source (page break) is omitted per instruction on page/line numbers.","- \"Vernünfteleien\" = \"sophistries\"; \"Gaukelwerke\" = \"jugglings\"."]],"krv-b-0250":["Transcendental Logic\nFirst Division.\nThe Transcendental Analytic.",["- The stray \"!\" after \"Analytik\" is an OCR trace of a footnote or line mark; omitted."]],"krv-b-0251":["This analytic is the dissection of all our knowledge *a priori* into the elements of the pure knowledge of the understanding. In this the following points are essential: (1) that the concepts be pure and not empirical concepts; (2) that they belong not to intuition and sensibility but to thought and understanding; (3) that they be elementary concepts, and be carefully distinguished from those that are derived from them or composed out of them; (4) that the table of them be complete, and that they cover the whole field of the pure understanding. Now this completeness of a science cannot be reliably assumed on the strength of a rough estimate of an aggregate got together merely by trial and error. It is possible only by means of an idea of the whole of the knowledge of the understanding *a priori*, and through the division of the concepts, determined by that idea, which constitute it; that is, only through their connection in a system. The pure understanding separates itself completely not only from everything empirical but even from all sensibility. It is therefore a unity that is self-subsistent, self-sufficient, and not to be increased by any additions from without. Consequently the sum of its knowledge will constitute a system, to be comprehended and determined under one idea, the completeness and articulation of which can at the same time afford a touchstone of the correctness and genuineness of all the pieces of knowledge that fit into it. This whole part of transcendental logic consists of two books, of which the one contains the concepts, the other the principles of the pure understanding.",["- The enumeration is set out with numerals in parentheses; the \"Daß ... seien\" clauses are rendered with the subjunctive \"be\".","- \"Überschlag\" = \"rough estimate\"; \"Versuche\" = \"trial and error\". \"Erkenntnißstücke\" = \"pieces of knowledge\".","- \"Büch em\" is an OCR slip for \"Büchern\"; corrected.","- \"Abtheilung\" = \"division\"."]],"krv-b-0252":["Transcendental Analytic\nBook I.\nThe Analytic of Concepts.",[]],"krv-b-0253":["By the analytic of concepts I understand not their analysis, that is, the procedure customary in philosophical investigations, of dissecting concepts that present themselves, in respect of their content, and of bringing them to distinctness. I mean rather the dissection, as yet little attempted, of the faculty of understanding itself. Its aim is to explore the possibility of *a priori* concepts by looking for them in the understanding alone, as their birthplace, and by analysing the pure employment of the understanding in general. This is the peculiar business of a transcendental philosophy; the rest is the logical treatment of concepts in philosophy in general. We shall therefore follow up the pure concepts to their first germs and dispositions in the human understanding, in which they lie prepared, until at last, on the occasion of experience, they are developed, and are exhibited in their purity by the same understanding, freed from the empirical conditions attaching to them.",["- Long first sentence divided into two (\"I mean rather ...\" and \"Its aim is ...\"); the dash/\"sondern\" construction smoothed.","- \"Zergliederung\" = \"dissection\"; \"Analysis\" = \"analysis\"; \"Anlagen\" = \"dispositions\"; \"Vermögen\" = \"faculty\".","- OCR breaks (\"Zerglieder ung\", \"Verstandes vermögens\") and the stray \":\" and numerals corrected silently."]],"krv-b-0254":["Of the Analytic of Concepts",[]],"krv-b-0255":["Chapter I.",["- \"Hauptstück\" = \"chapter\"; the stray \"10\" is a line number, omitted."]],"krv-b-0256":["On the Clue to the Discovery of all Pure Concepts of the Understanding.",["- \"Leitfaden\" = \"clue\"."]],"krv-b-0257":["When a faculty of knowledge is brought into play, various concepts, according to the various occasions, come to light which make this faculty known; and these can be collected into a more or less extensive record, according as the observation of them has been carried on for a longer time or with greater acuteness. Where this investigation will be completed can never be determined with certainty by this, as it were, mechanical procedure. Also, the concepts discovered only in this casual way reveal themselves in no order or systematic unity. In the end they are merely paired according to resemblances, and arranged in series according to the magnitude of their content, from the simple on to the more composite. These series are anything but systematic, although in a certain fashion they are brought about methodically.",["- \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" = \"faculty of knowledge\"; \"ins Spiel setzt\" = \"brings into play\".","- \"Scharffinnigfeit\" is an OCR slip for \"Scharfsinnigkeit\"; rendered \"acuteness\".","- \"nachdem\" is used in its older sense \"according as\"; the stray \"Auf-: satz\" and \"zuz sammengesetzten\" are OCR breaks, corrected. \"Aufsatz\" = \"record\".","- The final relative clause is made into a separate sentence."]],"krv-b-0258":["Transcendental philosophy has the advantage, but also the obligation, of seeking out its concepts according to a principle. These concepts spring pure and unmixed from the understanding, which is an absolute unity, and must therefore themselves be connected with one another in accordance with a concept or idea. Such a connection, however, supplies a rule by which the place of each pure concept of the understanding, and the completeness of all of them together, can be determined *a priori*. Otherwise all this would depend upon caprice or chance.",["- Restated as three sentences; \"welches alles\" is rendered \"all this\", referring to place and completeness.","- \"Transscendentalphilosophie\" rendered \"Transcendental philosophy\"; \"Verstand\" = understanding, \"Begriff\" = concept."]],"krv-b-0259":["OF THE",["- Heading fragment (\"Des\"); continued in the next passages."]],"krv-b-0260":["TRANSCENDENTAL CLUE TO THE DISCOVERY OF ALL",["- Heading fragment; \"Leitfaden\" = \"clue\", as in Kemp Smith."]],"krv-b-0261":["PURE CONCEPTS OF THE UNDERSTANDING\nSection I.",["- Heading; continues the title begun above."]],"krv-b-0262":["Of the Logical Employment of the Understanding in General.",["- \"Verstandesgebrauch\" rendered \"employment of the understanding\"."]],"krv-b-0263":["The understanding has been defined above only negatively, as a non-sensible faculty of knowledge. Now independently of sensibility we cannot have any intuition. Consequently the understanding is no faculty of intuition. But besides intuition there is no other mode of knowing except through concepts. Consequently the knowledge of every understanding, or at least of the human understanding, is knowledge through concepts. It is not intuitive, but discursive. All intuitions, as sensible, rest on affections; concepts, therefore, rest on functions. By 'function' I mean the unity of the act of arranging diverse representations under one common representation. Concepts are thus grounded on the spontaneity of thought, as sensible intuitions are grounded on the receptivity of impressions.\n\nThe only use which the understanding can make of these concepts is to judge by means of them. Since no representation, save when it is an intuition, relates immediately to an object, no concept is ever related to an object directly, but always to some other representation of it, be that representation an intuition or itself a concept. Judgment is therefore the mediate knowledge of an object, that is, the representation of a representation of it. In every judgment there is a concept which holds of many representations, and among them of a given representation that is immediately related to the object. Thus in the judgment 'All bodies are divisible', the concept of the divisible is related to various other concepts; among these it is here particularly related to the concept of body, and this in turn to certain appearances that present themselves to us. These objects are therefore mediately represented through the concept of divisibility. All judgments are accordingly functions of unity among our representations. For in place of an immediate representation a higher one, which comprises the immediate representation and various others, is used in knowing the object, and many possible modes of knowledge are thereby drawn together into one.\n\nWe can, however, reduce all acts of the understanding to judgments, so that the understanding in general may be represented as a faculty of judgment. For, according to what has been said above, it is a faculty of thought. Thought is knowledge by means of concepts. Concepts, as predicates of possible judgments, relate to some representation of a still undetermined object. Thus the concept of body signifies something, for instance metal, which can be known through that concept. It is a concept only in so far as other representations are contained under it, by means of which it can relate to objects. It is therefore the predicate of a possible judgment, for instance 'Every metal is a body'. The functions of the understanding can therefore all be discovered if we can exhibit completely the functions of unity in judgments. That this can very well be accomplished will be shown in the following section.",["- \"Mfo\", \"Afo\" are OCR for \"Also\"; rendered \"consequently\"/\"accordingly\". The stray \"ı\", \"1\", \"2\" and the broken \"Be— griffe\", \"Erfenntniß\" are OCR slips, silently corrected.","- Divided into paragraphs and some sentences recast for clarity; \"Vermögen\" = faculty; \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge, plural \"modes of knowledge\".","- \"Functionen der Einheit … zusammengezogen\": \"welche letztere\" resolved as the given representation.","- \"Handlung\" rendered \"act\"; \"Affectionen\" = affections."]],"krv-b-0264":["OF THE",["- Heading fragment."]],"krv-b-0265":["CLUE TO THE DISCOVERY OF ALL PURE CONCEPTS OF THE UNDERSTANDING",["- Trailing \"ö\" is OCR noise, omitted."]],"krv-b-0266":["Section II.",["- Trailing \"2\" is OCR noise, omitted."]],"krv-b-0267":["§ 9.",["- \"89.\" read as \"§ 9.\""]],"krv-b-0268":["Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgments.",[]],"krv-b-0269":["If we abstract from all content of a judgment in general, and attend only to the mere form of understanding in it, we find that the function of thought in judgment can be brought under four heads, each of which contains three moments. These may be suitably represented in the following table.",["- \"Titel\" = heads; \"Momente\" = moments."]],"krv-b-0270":["1.\nQuantity of Judgments.\nUniversal\nParticular\nSingular",["- \"5\" before \"Einzelne\" (passage 0271) is a line number, omitted; the word is placed here to complete the column."]],"krv-b-0271":["Singular",["- Already given in the table above; the line number \"5\" is omitted. Duplication follows the passage division."]],"krv-b-0272":["Quality. Relation.",["- Table headings; \"2.\" and \"3.\" are not in the text and are not supplied."]],"krv-b-0273":["Affirmative Categorical Negative Hypothetical Infinite Disjunctive",["- Two table columns run together in the OCR (\"p\", \"0\" are noise). Column order: Quality (Affirmative, Negative, Infinite); Relation (Categorical, Hypothetical, Disjunctive)."]],"krv-b-0274":["4.\nModality.\nProblematic\nAssertoric",["- Continues in the next passage with \"Apodictic\"."]],"krv-b-0275":["Apodictic\n\nAs this division seems to depart in some points, though not essential ones, from the customary technique of the logicians, the following cautions against feared misunderstanding will not be superfluous.",["- \"5\" and \"N\" are OCR/line noise, omitted. Sentence breaks off at \"so werden\"; its apodosis is in the next passage, so I rendered it as one sentence here."]],"krv-b-0276":["1. The logicians rightly say that in the use of judgments in syllogisms singular judgments can be treated like universal ones. For just because they have no extension at all, their predicate cannot be drawn merely to some of what is contained under the concept of the subject while being excepted from the rest. It holds of that concept without exception, just as if the concept were a general concept having an extension, of the whole of whose content the predicate held. If, on the other hand, we compare a singular judgment with a universally valid one merely as knowledge in respect of magnitude, it stands to the latter as unity to infinity, and is therefore in itself essentially different from it. Accordingly, if I estimate a singular judgment (*judicium singulare*) not merely according to its inner validity, but also as knowledge in general, according to the magnitude it has in comparison with other modes of knowledge, it is certainly distinct from universally valid judgments (*judicia communia*), and deserves a special place in a complete table of the moments of thought in general (though not, indeed, in a logic limited merely to the use of judgments in relation to one another).",["- Opening \"folgende Verwahrungen\" belongs to the preceding sentence (see 0275). Stray \"0\" and line number \"5\" omitted.","- \"gemeingültig\" rendered \"universally valid\"/\"general\"; \"sie\" in \"verhält sie sich\" resolved as the singular judgment.","- \"Größe\" = magnitude; \"Umfang\" = extension."]],"krv-b-0277":["2. In like manner, in a transcendental logic infinite judgments must still be distinguished from affirmative ones, even though in general logic they are rightly classed with the latter and constitute no separate member of the division. General logic abstracts from all content of the predicate (even when it is negative), and looks only to whether the predicate is ascribed to the subject or opposed to it. Transcendental logic, on the other hand, considers the judgment also in respect of the worth or content of this logical affirmation made by means of a merely negative predicate, and of what gain this secures in respect of knowledge as a whole.\n\nHad I said of the soul, 'It is not mortal', I should at least by a negative judgment have averted an error. Now by the proposition 'The soul is non-mortal' I have, so far as logical form is concerned, really affirmed, in placing the soul in the unlimited sphere of non-dying beings. Since the mortal constitutes one part of the whole extension of possible beings, and the non-mortal the other, nothing more is said by my proposition than that the soul is one of the infinite multitude of things which remain when I take away all that is mortal. But this limits the infinite sphere of all that is possible only to the extent that the mortal is separated from it, and the soul is placed in the remaining space of its extension. This space, even with this exception, still remains infinite, and several further parts of it can be removed without the concept of the soul being in the least enlarged or affirmatively determined. These judgments, infinite in respect of logical extension, are therefore in respect of the content of knowledge in general merely limitative; and in this respect they must not be passed over in the transcendental table of all the moments of thought, since the function of the understanding exercised in them may perhaps be of importance in the field of its pure *a priori* knowledge.",["- Divided into two paragraphs; the long second sentence of the soul example restated in shorter ones.","- \"as\" before \"Glied\" (\"as besonderes Glied\") and \"1\", \"2\", \"Nr:\" are OCR slips, silently corrected (\"Nr: theilen\" read \"Titeln\"/\"Theilen\" is doubtful; I took it as \"in the transcendental table of all the moments of thought\" and dropped the garbled word).","- \"beschränkend\" = limitative; \"nichtsterblich\" = non-mortal."]],"krv-b-0278":["3. All relations of thought in judgments are relations (a) of the predicate to the subject, (b) of the ground to its consequence, (c) of the divided knowledge and of the collected members of the division to one another. In the first kind of judgment only two concepts are considered in relation to each other; in the second, two judgments; in the third, several judgments. The hypothetical proposition, \"If there is perfect justice, the obstinately wicked will be punished,\" really contains the relation of two propositions: \"There is perfect justice,\" and \"The obstinately wicked will be punished.\" Whether these two propositions are in themselves true remains here undecided. What is thought through this judgment is only the consequence.\n\nFinally, the disjunctive judgment contains a relation of two or more propositions to one another. This is not a relation of sequence but one of logical opposition, in so far as the sphere of the one excludes that of the other; and yet at the same time it is a relation of community, in so far as the propositions together fill the sphere of the knowledge proper [*der eigentlichen Erkenntniß*]. It is therefore a relation of the parts of the sphere of a [single] knowledge, since the sphere of each part is a complement of the sphere of the others, to constitute the whole sum [*Inbegriff*] of the divided knowledge. For example: the world exists either through blind chance, or through inner necessity, or through an external cause. Each of these propositions occupies a part of the sphere of possible knowledge regarding the existence of a world in general; all together occupy the whole sphere. To remove the knowledge from one of these spheres is to place it in one of the others; and conversely, to place it in one sphere is to remove it from the others.\n\nThere is thus in a disjunctive judgment a certain community of modes of knowledge [*Erkenntnisse*]. It consists in this, that the modes of knowledge reciprocally exclude one another, and yet thereby determine the true knowledge as a whole, since taken together they constitute the entire content of a single given knowledge. And this is all that, for the sake of what follows, I find it necessary to remark at this point.",["- The OCR \"e)\" and the stray \"3\" (footnote marker, cf. B 100 footnote, krv-b-0279) are corrected silently/omitted; the stray \"3\" is probably the asterisk reference mark.","- Sentence beginning \"Finally…\" and the long disjunctive sentence are divided into several; \"Gemeinschaft\" rendered \"community\", as in the category.","- \"ein Ergänzungsstück … der Sphäre des andern\" is plural in sense (\"of the others\"); I supplied \"[single]\" for \"einer\".","- \"an sich\" corrected from OCR \"si)\"."]],"krv-b-0279":["*) As if thought were in the first case a function of the understanding, in the second of judgment, in the third of reason. A remark which awaits its elucidation in what follows.",["- *Urtheilskraft* = \"judgment\" (as a faculty); *Vernunft* = \"reason\". The footnote is kept as a footnote."]],"krv-b-0280":["4. The modality of judgments is a quite special function. Its distinguishing feature is that it contributes nothing to the content of the judgment (for besides quantity, quality and relation there is nothing that constitutes the content of a judgment), but concerns only the value of the copula in relation to thought in general. Problematic judgments are those in which affirmation or denial is taken as merely possible (optional); assertoric judgments, those in which it is regarded as actual (true); apodeictic judgments, those in which it is looked upon as necessary.*)\n\nThus the two judgments whose relation constitutes the hypothetical judgment (antecedent and consequent), and likewise those in whose reciprocity the disjunctive judgment consists (members of the division), are one and all merely problematic. In the above example the proposition \"There is perfect justice\" is not asserted, but only thought as an optional judgment which it is possible for someone to adopt; and only the consequence is assertoric. Such judgments may therefore be obviously false, and yet, taken problematically, be conditions of the knowledge of truth. Thus the judgment \"The world exists through blind chance\" has in the disjunctive judgment only problematic significance: namely, that someone may for a moment adopt this proposition. It serves nevertheless (like the marking out of a false road among the number of all those that can be taken) to discover the true one.\n\nThe problematic proposition is accordingly that which expresses only logical possibility (which is not objective); that is, a free choice to let such a proposition hold, a merely arbitrary admission of it into the understanding. The assertoric proposition speaks of logical actuality or truth. Thus in a hypothetical syllogism the antecedent appears problematically in the major premiss, assertorically in the minor; and the assertoric proposition indicates that the proposition is already bound up with the understanding in accordance with its laws. The apodeictic proposition thinks the assertoric as determined by these very laws of the understanding, and hence as asserting *a priori*, and in this fashion expresses logical necessity.\n\nSince everything here is incorporated into the understanding by degrees, so that one first judges something problematically, then perhaps also accepts it assertorically as true, and finally affirms it as inseparably bound up with the understanding, that is, as necessary and apodeictic, these three functions of modality may be entitled so many moments of thought in general.",["- The footnote mark \")\" in OCR is rendered \"*)\", referring to krv-b-0279.","- \"vors kommt\" and \"f annimmt\" are OCR slips, corrected silently. \"Verhältniß\" = \"relation\".","- The opening sentence and the last long sentence are kept nearly intact, but the second paragraph's \"Daher…\" clauses are slightly recast for English idiom.","- \"Wechselwirkung\" = \"reciprocity\" (cf. the category)."]],"krv-b-0281":["Of the",[]],"krv-b-0282":["Clue to the Discovery of All Pure Concepts of the Understanding",["- Heading continues from the preceding fragment \"Des\" (\"Of the\"); OCR \"Sbegriffe\" corrected to \"begriffe\". \"Leitfaden\" = \"clue\". \"Verstande\" read as Verstandes."]],"krv-b-0283":["Section III",["- The stray \"3\" after \"Abschnitt.\" is omitted as OCR residue."]],"krv-b-0284":["The Pure Concepts of the Understanding, or Categories",["- Spelling \"Verstandesbegriff en\" corrected."]],"krv-b-0285":["General logic, as has already been said several times, abstracts from all content of knowledge, and expects that representations will be given to it from elsewhere, from whatever source, in order first to transform them into concepts. This it does analytically. Transcendental logic, on the other hand, has lying before it a manifold of sensibility *a priori*, presented to it by transcendental aesthetic, in order to provide a matter for the pure concepts of the understanding. In the absence of such matter those concepts would be without any content, and therefore completely empty.\n\nSpace and time contain a manifold of pure intuition *a priori*, but they belong none the less to the conditions of the receptivity of our mind, under which alone it can receive representations of objects; and these conditions must therefore always affect the concept of such objects. The spontaneity of our thought, however, requires that this manifold be first gone through, taken up and combined in a certain way, in order that knowledge may be made out of it. This act I entitle synthesis.",["- Two long sentences divided; \"ohne den sie\" resolved as referring to the pure concepts of the understanding.","- \"die mithin auch den Begriff derselben … afficiren müssen\": \"die\" taken as referring to the conditions; \"derselben\" to the objects.","- \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"Receptivität\" = receptivity; \"nenne ich\" = \"I entitle\"."]],"krv-b-0286":["By synthesis, in the most general sense, I understand the act of putting different representations together with one another, and of grasping [*begreifen*] their manifold in one [mode of] knowledge. Such a synthesis is pure if the manifold is given not empirically but *a priori*, as is the manifold in space and time. Our representations must be given prior to all analysis of them; and no concepts can arise analytically so far as content is concerned.\n\nThe synthesis of a manifold, however (whether given empirically or *a priori*), is what first gives rise to a knowledge. This knowledge may, to begin with, be crude and confused, and so in need of analysis. Synthesis is nevertheless that which properly gathers the elements for knowledge and unites them into a certain content. It is therefore the first thing to which we must attend, if we wish to judge of the first origin of our knowledge.",["- \"in einer Erkenntniß\": \"[mode of]\" supplied to avoid the singular \"a knowledge\"; the German article stands.","- Sentences divided at \"Die Synthesis … aber\" for clarity.","- \"begreifen\" rendered \"grasping\", to avoid confusion with *Begriff*/concept."]],"krv-b-0287":["Synthesis in general is, as we shall see later, the mere result of the imagination, a blind though indispensable function of the soul, without which we should have no knowledge whatsoever, but of which we are seldom conscious even once. To bring this synthesis to concepts, however, is a function that belongs to the understanding, and it is through this function that the understanding first supplies us with knowledge in the proper sense of the term.",["- \"Wirkung der Einbildungskraft\" = \"result [or effect] of the imagination\"; \"Seele\" = soul. \"überall gar keine\" = \"none whatsoever\"."]],"krv-b-0288":["Pure synthesis, represented in general terms, gives the pure concept of the understanding. By this synthesis I understand that which rests upon a ground of synthetic unity *a priori*. Thus our counting (this is especially noticeable in the case of larger numbers) is a synthesis according to concepts, because it proceeds according to a common ground of unity, for instance the decade. Under this concept, therefore, the unity in the synthesis of the manifold becomes necessary.",["- \"Dekadik\" rendered \"the decade\" (the decimal base); a choice, as the German word names the decimal system.","- \"Unter diesem Begriffe\": the concept is that of the common ground of unity."]],"krv-b-0289":["Analytically, different representations are brought under a concept (a business with which general logic deals). Transcendental logic, however, teaches us to bring under concepts not representations but the pure synthesis of representations. The first thing that must be given us for the sake of knowledge of all objects *a priori* is the manifold of pure intuition; the synthesis of this manifold by means of the imagination is the second, but it does not yet yield knowledge. The concepts which give unity to this pure synthesis, and which consist solely in the representation of this necessary synthetic unity, furnish the third [requisite] for the knowledge of an object that comes before us, and rest on the understanding. 1",[]],"krv-b-0290":["The same function which gives unity to the different representations in a judgment also gives unity to the mere synthesis of different representations in an intuition; and this unity, expressed in its general form, is entitled the pure concept of the understanding. The same understanding, therefore, and by the very same acts through which, in concepts, by means of",[]],"krv-b-0291":["analytical unity, it brought about the logical form of a judgment, also introduces, by means of the synthetic unity of the manifold in intuition in general, a transcendental content into its representations. On this account they are entitled pure concepts of the understanding, and they apply *a priori* to objects, a result which general logic cannot achieve. 2",[]],"krv-b-0292":["In this manner there arise exactly as many pure concepts of the understanding, applying *a priori* to objects of intuition in general, as there were logical functions in all possible judgments in the preceding table. For the understanding is completely exhausted by these functions, and its capacity is thereby fully measured. Following Aristotle, we shall entitle these concepts categories, since our original purpose is indeed identical with his, though in execution it diverges very widely from his. 3",[]],"krv-b-0293":["Table of the Categories.\n1.\nOf Quantity:\nUnity",[]],"krv-b-0294":["Plurality",[]],"krv-b-0295":["Totality.\nOf Quality: Of Relation:",[]],"krv-b-0296":["Reality Of Inherence and Subsistence (*sub-*",[]],"krv-b-0297":["Negation *stantia et accidens*)",[]],"krv-b-0298":["Limitation. Of Causality and Dependence",[]],"krv-b-0299":["(cause and effect)",[]],"krv-b-0300":["Of Community (reciprocity",[]],"krv-b-0301":["between the agent",[]],"krv-b-0302":["and the patient).",[]],"krv-b-0303":["4.\nOf Modality:\nPossibility — Impossibility\nExistence — Non-existence\nNecessity — Contingency.",[]],"krv-b-0304":["This, then, is the list of all the original pure concepts of synthesis which the understanding contains *a priori* in itself, and it is solely by reason of them that it is a pure understanding; for only through them can it understand anything in the manifold of intuition, that is, think an object of intuition. This division is generated systematically from a common principle, namely the faculty of judgment (which is the same as the faculty of thought). It has not arisen rhapsodically, from a search for pure concepts undertaken at haphazard, of whose completeness we can never be certain, since it is inferred only by induction; and, to say nothing of this, we should never in the latter way see why just these concepts, and no others, dwell in the pure understanding. It was a design worthy of an acute thinker that Aristotle formed, in seeking out these fundamental concepts. But as he had no principle, he picked them up as they came his way, and at first got together ten of them, which he entitled categories (predicaments). Afterwards he believed he had found five more, which he added under the name of post-predicaments. But his table still remained defective. Besides, there are found among them some *modi* of pure sensibility (*quando, ubi, situs*, likewise *prius, simul*), and also one empirical concept (*motus*), none of which belong in this register of the understanding's primary concepts; or else derivative concepts are reckoned among the primitive concepts (*actio, passio*), while some of the latter are entirely lacking.",[]],"krv-b-0305":["With regard to these latter, it has therefore to be remarked that the categories, as the true root-concepts of the pure understanding, also have their equally pure derivative concepts. These cannot by any means be passed over in a complete system of transcendental philosophy; in a merely critical essay, however, I can be content with mentioning them.",[]],"krv-b-0306":["Let me be permitted to entitle these pure but derivative concepts of the understanding the predicables of the pure understanding (in contrast to the predicaments). When we have the original and primitive concepts, the derivative and subalternate concepts can easily be added, and the family tree of the pure understanding completely portrayed. Since I am concerned here not with the completeness of the system but only with that of the principles for a system, I reserve this supplementary task for another occasion. The aim can, however, be fairly well attained if we take up the ontological textbooks and subordinate to the category of causality, for example, the predicables of force, action, and passion; to the category of community, those of presence and resistance; to the predicaments of modality, those of origination, extinction, change, and so forth. The categories, combined with the *modi* of pure sensibility or with one another, yield a great multitude of derivative concepts *a priori*. To note these, and if possible to list them to completeness, would be a useful and not unpleasant, but here dispensable, labour.",[]],"krv-b-0307":["The definitions of these categories I deliberately spare myself in this treatise, although I should like to be in possession of them. I shall in what follows analyse these concepts to the degree sufficient in relation to the doctrine of method on which I am engaged. In a system of pure reason definitions of them could justly be demanded of me; but here they would only divert attention from the main point of the enquiry, by arousing doubts and attacks which, without detriment to the essential purpose, can quite well be referred to another undertaking. Meanwhile, from the little that I have adduced on the matter, it is clearly evident that a complete dictionary, with all the explanations required, is not only possible but easy to compile. The compartments are already there; it is only necessary to fill them, and a systematic topic such as the present easily shows the place proper to each concept, and at the same time readily reveals any that is still empty.",[]],"krv-b-0308":["Some elegant reflections can be made upon this table of the categories, which might perhaps have important consequences for the scientific form of all modes of rational knowledge. For that this table is exceedingly serviceable, indeed indispensable, in the theoretical part of philosophy, for completely sketching the plan of the whole of a science, so far as it rests on concepts *a priori*, and for dividing it systematically according to determinate principles, is evident of itself from the fact that the table contains all the elementary concepts of the understanding completely, and even the form of a system of them in the human understanding. Consequently it gives direction to all the moments of any speculative science that is projected, and even to their order; as I have also given a specimen of this elsewhere.* Here now are some of these remarks.",[]],"krv-b-0309":["The first is that this table, which contains four classes of concepts of the understanding, can first be divided into two sections. The former of these are directed to objects of intuition (pure as well as empirical); the second, to the existence of these objects (either in relation to one another or to the understanding).",[]],"krv-b-0310":["The first class I would entitle the mathematical, the second the dynamical categories. The first class, as will be seen, has no correlates; these are to be met with only in the second class.",[]],"krv-b-0311":["* *Metaphysische Anfangsgründe der Naturwissenschaft*. This distinction must surely have a ground in the nature of the understanding.",[]],"krv-b-0312":["Second remark. That in every class there is the same number of categories, namely three, which likewise calls for reflection,",["- 0289: \"ein Geschäfte\" rendered \"a business\"; \"das dritte\" supplied \"[requisite]\". The stray \"f\" before \"das Mannigfaltige\" is OCR and silently dropped. \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge. The footnote mark \"1\" is kept as it stands.","- 0290–0291: one sentence broken across ids; the second is left as it stands, breaking off mid-clause (\"by means of\"). In 0291 the stray \"X\" is dropped.","- 0291: \"transscendentalen Inhalt\" = transcendental content; the footnote mark \"2\" is kept (the OCR quote mark dropped).","- 0292: the \"nach)\" with the mark 3 is OCR; the footnote mark is kept as \"3\". \"Vermögen\" = capacity here.","- 0293–0303: table split over several ids by the OCR; line-initial numerals (\"5\") and the \"1.\"/\"4.\" are retained or dropped as in the source. The table is left fragmentary; columns are not realigned. \"Wechselwirkung\" = reciprocity; \"Handelnden und Leidenden\" = agent and patient.","- 0304: \"Vermögen zu urtheilen\" = faculty of judgment; the long sentence on induction is kept in one sentence with semicolons, though it could be divided. \"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility; \"Gegenstand\" = object, \"Object\" likewise.","- 0306: \"Prädicabilien\" = predicables, \"Prädicamente\" = predicaments. The OCR \"den :\" before \"Prädicamenten\" is read as \"dem\" and the clause is smoothed; the German is garbled here and the reading is doubtful.","- 0308–0312: \"Vernunfterkenntnisse\" = modes of rational knowledge; the \"*)\" footnote sign is given as \"*\", and 0311's stray \")\" dropped. 0309 \"zerfällen\" read as \"zerfällen/zerfallen\". 0312 breaks off; not completed."]],"krv-b-0313":["[...] since otherwise all division *a priori* through concepts must be dichotomy. It is further to be noted that the third category in every class arises from the combination of the second category with the first.",["- The passage begins mid-sentence (\"mm.\" is an OCR remnant of the preceding word, left untranslated); the colon after \"sein muß\" is an OCR/typographical artefact and is dropped.","- \"Dichotomie\" is kept as \"dichotomy\"; the sentence is divided into two for clarity."]],"krv-b-0314":["Thus totality (*Allheit*) is nothing other than plurality considered as unity; limitation is nothing other than reality combined with [negation]",["- The passage breaks off at an OCR fragment (the closing quotation mark); the sentence continues in the next passage (\"Negation verbunden\"), where \"Negation\" is completed. I have supplied \"[negation]\" from the following fragment (\"en — Negation\") only to complete the sense.","- \"Allheit (Totalität)\" rendered \"totality (*Allheit*)\" to show the German."]],"krv-b-0315":["[...] Community is the causality of a substance in the reciprocal determination of other substances; and, finally, necessity is nothing other than the existence which is given through possibility itself. But it must not be supposed that the third category is on that account merely derivative, and not a fundamental concept (*Stammbegriff*) of the pure understanding. For the combination of the first and second, in order to produce the third concept, demands a special act of the understanding, which is not identical with the act exercised in the case of the first and second. Thus the concept of a number (which belongs to the category of totality) is not always possible where the concepts of multitude and of unity are present, as, for instance, in the representation of the infinite. Or again, from the fact that I combine the concept of a cause with that of a substance, it does not at once follow how influence, that is, how one substance can become the cause of something in another substance, is to be understood. From this it is evident that a special act of the understanding is required; and the same holds of the other categories.",["- Opening \"en — Negation verbunden\" is the tail of the preceding sentence (\"[limitation is] reality combined with negation\"); the fragment \"en —\" is left out and the sentence is begun at \"die Gemeinschaft\". The leading \"en — Negation verbunden,\" is thus not rendered; see previous passage.","- OCR slips corrected silently: \"hervorzu- ılf bringen\" = *hervorzubringen*; \"Ein— fluß\" = *Einfluß*; stray \"i\" at end dropped.","- \"Gemeinschaft\" = community; \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Actus\" rendered \"act\".","- \"Zahl ... Allheit\": \"Menge\" rendered \"multitude\", \"Vielheit\" in the previous sentence \"plurality\".","- The sentence beginning \"So ist der Begriff einer Zahl\" is restated, with \"Or again\" for \"oder\", to keep the two examples distinct."]],"krv-b-0316":["Second Remark. In the case of one single category, namely that of community, which stands under the third heading, the agreement with the form of a disjunctive judgment, which corresponds to it in the table of the logical functions, is not so obvious as it is in the others.",["- \"Ste Anmerk.\" (OCR \"Ste\"/\"z a\") read as \"2te Anmerk.\"; \"Gez a meinschaft\" read as *Gemeinschaft*."]],"krv-b-0317":["To assure ourselves of this agreement, we must observe the following. In all disjunctive judgments the sphere (the sum of all that is contained under the concept) is represented as a whole divided into parts (the subordinate concepts). Since one part cannot be contained under another, the parts are thought as coordinated with, not subordinated to, one another; that is, they determine one another not one-sidedly, as in a series, but reciprocally, as in an aggregate (if one member of the division is posited, all the rest are excluded, and conversely).",["- The sentence is divided in two; \"z|\" (OCR) dropped, and the colon after \"bemerken\" rendered as \"the following\".","- \"ihm\" in the preceding passage, referring to the category, is not in this passage; \"Glied der Eintheilung\" = \"member of the division\". \"unter ihm\" rendered \"under the concept\" (the antecedent being the disjunctive concept divided)."]],"krv-b-0318":["Now a similar combination is thought in a whole of things, where one thing is not subordinated, as effect, to another, as cause of its existence, but is coordinated with it at once and reciprocally as a cause in respect of the determination of the others (for instance, in a body, the parts of which reciprocally attract and also resist one another). This is an entirely different kind of connection from that found in the mere relation of cause to effect (of ground to consequence), in which the consequence does not in turn reciprocally determine the ground, and therefore does not, together with it, constitute a whole (as the Creator of the world does not with the world). The understanding follows the same procedure when it represents to itself the sphere of a divided concept as when it thinks a thing as divisible. As in the former case the members of the division exclude one another and yet are combined in one sphere, so in the latter it represents the parts as things whose existence (as substances) belongs to each exclusively of the others, and yet as combined in one whole.",["- Long sentences divided; \"beigeordnet\" rendered \"coordinated\", \"untergeordnet\" \"subordinated\". \"des letzteren\"/\"im ersteren\" resolved as \"the divisible thing\"/\"the divided concept\" and rendered \"the latter\"/\"former case\".","- \"Weltschöpfer\" = \"Creator of the world\"."]],"krv-b-0319":["But there is still to be found in the transcendental philosophy of the ancients a chapter containing pure concepts of the understanding which, though not counted among the categories, should yet, according to the ancients, have held of objects as concepts *a priori*. In that case, however, they would increase the number of the categories, which cannot be. These are propounded in the proposition, so renowned among the Scholastics, *quodlibet ens est unum, verum, bonum*. Now although the use of this principle, so far as its consequences are concerned (which yielded only tautologous propositions), turned out very meagre, so that in modern times it has been put forward in metaphysics almost out of mere courtesy, yet a thought which has maintained itself so long, however empty it may seem, always deserves an investigation of its origin, and justifies the conjecture that it is grounded in some rule of the understanding which has, as so often happens, only been wrongly interpreted. These supposedly transcendental predicates of things are nothing other than logical requirements and criteria of all knowledge of things in general, and they lay at its foundation the categories of quantity, namely unity, plurality and totality. But these categories, which ought properly to have been taken as material, as belonging to the possibility of the things themselves, were in fact employed by the ancients only in a formal meaning, as belonging to the logical demand in respect of every item of knowledge; and yet they incautiously made these criteria of thought into properties of things in themselves.\n\nIn every knowledge of an object there is, namely, unity of concept, which may be entitled qualitative unity, so far as thereby only the unity in the combination of the manifold of the modes of knowledge is thought, as, for instance, the unity of the theme in a play, a speech, or a fable. Secondly, there is truth in respect of the consequences. The more true consequences there are from a given concept, the more marks there are of its objective reality. This might be entitled the qualitative plurality of the marks which belong to a concept as to their common ground (and are not thought in it as a quantity). Thirdly and finally, there is perfection, which consists in this, that, conversely, this plurality leads back together to the unity of the concept, and agrees completely with it and with no other. This may be entitled qualitative completeness (totality). From this it is evident that these logical criteria of the possibility of knowledge in general convert the three categories of magnitude, in which the unity in the generation of the quantum must be assumed to be throughout homogeneous, into categories of quality, here only in view of the combination in one consciousness of even heterogeneous items of knowledge, through the quality of a [mode of] knowledge as principle.\n\nThus the criterion of the possibility of a concept (not of its object) is the definition, in which the unity of the concept, the truth of all that may be immediately derived from it, and finally the completeness of what has been drawn from it, constitute what is requisite for the construction of the whole concept. Or again, the criterion of a hypothesis is the intelligibility of the assumed ground of explanation, or its unity (without auxiliary hypothesis); the truth (agreement with itself and with experience) of the consequences to be derived from it; and finally the completeness of the ground of explanation in regard to them, which refer back to nothing more and nothing less than was assumed in the hypothesis, and deliver again analytically *a posteriori* what was thought synthetically *a priori*, and agree therewith. — By the concepts of unity, truth and perfection, therefore, the transcendental table of the categories is not supplemented, as if it were deficient. Only the relation of these concepts to objects is wholly set aside, and the procedure with them is brought under general logical rules of the agreement of knowledge with itself.",["- Heavily garbled OCR repaired silently: \"von um\" = *bonum*; \"Erforder- ; 1) Der § 12 ist ein Zusatz von A2. nisse\" = *Erfordernisse* (the editorial footnote on §12 intruded and is omitted); \"Schaue spiel\" = *Schauspiel*; \"diez | fem\" = *diesem*; \"Verstellung\" read as *Vorstellung* would be conjectural, but I render \"construction\"? Doubtful: the printed text may read *Darstellung*; I translate \"construction\" tentatively; check.","- \"Verstellung\" is doubtful (probably *Darstellung* or *Vorstellung*); my \"construction\" is tentative.","- \"Erkenntniß/Erkenntnisse\" = knowledge / modes of knowledge; \"Erkenntnißstücke\" = \"items of knowledge\"; \"heißen/nennen\" = \"entitled\".","- The long opening sentence and the sentence on the hypothesis are divided; \"Folgen\" kept \"consequences\"; \"wird... gar nicht... ergänzt\" restated as \"is not supplemented\".","- Paragraph breaks are mine. Words \"(nicht in ihm als Größe gedacht werden)\" rendered \"(and are not thought in it as a quantity)\"; \"als einem gemeinschaftlichen Grunde\" kept as \"as to their common ground\".","- \"Man ... in neueren Zeiten ... ehrenhalber\": rendered \"out of mere courtesy\".","- \"in der Wahrheit... als principle\": \"[mode of]\" is supplied to avoid the singular \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-0320":["Of the Analytic of Concepts\nSecond Chapter [*Hauptstück*]",["- Running-head layout; \"Der\" belongs to the heading \"Der Analytik der Begriffe\"."]],"krv-b-0321":["Of the Deduction of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding",["- Stray \")\" before \"Von\" is an OCR slip and omitted."]],"krv-b-0322":["First Section",[]],"krv-b-0323":["Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction",["- The title continues in the next passage (\"überhaupt\")."]],"krv-b-0324":["in General.",["- Completes the title begun in the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-0325":["Jurists, when they speak of entitlements and usurpations, distinguish in a legal action the question of what is lawful (*quid iuris*) from the question which concerns the fact (*quid facti*). Since they demand proof of both, they give the name deduction to the proof of the former, which has to establish the entitlement or the legal claim. We make use of a multitude of empirical concepts without opposition from anyone, and we hold ourselves entitled, even without deduction, to ascribe to them a sense and an imagined [*eingebildete*] meaning, since we always have experience at hand to prove their objective reality. There are, however, also usurped concepts, such as fortune and fate. These circulate with almost universal indulgence, yet are occasionally challenged by the question *quid iuris*. We are then involved in no small perplexity in regard to their deduction, since we can cite no clear ground of right, either from experience or from reason, by which the entitlement to their use would be made manifest.",["- Restated the second sentence as two; \"nennen sie … die Deduction\" rendered \"give the name deduction\", in keeping with \"is entitled\".","- \"Befugnis\" rendered \"entitlement\", \"Rechtsanspruch\" \"legal claim\", \"Rechtsgrund\" \"ground of right\"; these are choices.","- \"eingebildete Bedeutung\" translated literally as \"imagined meaning\"; the sense is possibly \"supposed\". \"fol\" in the OCR corrected to \"soll\".","- The final relative clause (\"dadurch die Befugnis … deutlich würde\") is attached to \"ground of right\"."]],"krv-b-0326":["Among the many concepts, however, which make up the very mixed fabric of human knowledge, there are some which are also destined for pure use *a priori* (completely independent of all experience). The entitlement of these always requires a deduction. Proofs from experience are not sufficient to establish the legitimacy of such a use, and yet we must know how these concepts can relate to objects which they do not derive from any experience. I therefore entitle the explanation of the manner in which concepts can relate *a priori* to objects their transcendental deduction, and distinguish it from empirical deduction. The latter shows the manner in which a concept has been acquired through experience and through reflection upon it, and therefore concerns not the legitimacy but the fact from which the possession has arisen.",["- Divided the passage's long second sentence into two; \"weil\" clause rendered as a separate statement of reason.","- OCR slips corrected: \"be: stimmt\" (bestimmt), \"Des duction\" (Deduction), \"transicendentale\". The trailing \"1\" is a footnote marker; no footnote text is given here, so none is supplied.","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Gebrauch\" = use; \"Rechtmäßigkeit\" = legitimacy."]],"krv-b-0327":["We now already have two kinds of concepts, of quite different nature, which nevertheless agree in this, that both relate completely *a priori* to objects. These are the concepts of space and time as forms of sensibility, and the categories as concepts of the understanding. To attempt an empirical deduction of them would be wholly vain labour, since what distinguishes their nature precisely lies in this, that they relate to their objects without having borrowed anything from experience for the representation of them. If, then, a deduction of them is necessary, it must always be transcendental.",["- OCR: \"si)\" read as \"sich\"; footnote markers \"1\" and \"2\" after \"wollen,\" and \"sein müssen.\" omitted, as no footnote text is supplied.","- \"Vorstellung\" = representation; \"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility; \"Verstand\" = understanding."]],"krv-b-0328":["Meanwhile, in the case of these concepts, as of all knowledge, we can seek in experience, if not the principle of their possibility, at least the occasioning causes of their production. The impressions of the senses give the first occasion for opening up the whole faculty of knowledge in respect of them, and for bringing experience into being. Experience contains two very heterogeneous elements, namely a matter for knowledge from the senses, and a certain form for ordering it, from the inner source of pure intuiting and thinking. These are first brought into exercise on the occasion of the former [the impressions], and produce concepts. Such a tracing of the first endeavours of our faculty of knowledge, whereby it ascends from particular perceptions to universal concepts, is without doubt of great utility, and we have to thank the celebrated Locke for having first opened the way to it. But a deduction of the pure *a priori* concepts is never achieved in this manner, for it does not lie on this path at all. In view of their future use, which is to be completely independent of experience, they must be able to show a quite different certificate of birth from that of descent from experiences. This attempted physiological derivation, which strictly cannot be entitled deduction at all, since it concerns a *quaestio facti*, I shall therefore call the explanation of the possession of a pure knowledge. It is thus clear that of these concepts only a transcendental deduction is possible, and by no means an empirical one; and that empirical deductions, in respect of the pure concepts *a priori*, are nothing but vain attempts, with which only one can occupy himself who has not grasped the quite peculiar nature of these modes of knowledge.\n\nBut although the one sole manner of a possible deduction of pure knowledge *a priori*, namely that by the transcendental path, is admitted, it does not thereby become evident that it is so indispensably necessary. We have above traced the concepts of space and time to their sources by means of a transcendental deduction, and explained and determined their objective validity *a priori*. Geometry nevertheless advances with sure step through modes of knowledge wholly *a priori*, without having to ask of philosophy a certificate of the pure and lawful descent of its fundamental concept of space. But the use of this concept in that science extends only to the outer world of sense, of which space is the pure form of intuition. All geometrical knowledge, since it is grounded on intuition *a priori*, therefore has immediate evidence, and the objects are given through the knowledge itself *a priori* (as regards form) in intuition.\n\nWith the pure concepts of the understanding, on the other hand, there begins the indispensable need of seeking the transcendental deduction not only of these concepts themselves but also of space. They speak of objects not through predicates of intuition and sensibility but of pure thinking *a priori*, and so relate to objects universally, without any conditions of sensibility. Not being grounded on experience, they also cannot exhibit in *a priori* intuition any object on which, prior to all experience, they might base their synthesis. Hence they arouse suspicion not only as to the objective validity and limits of their use, but also render that concept of space ambiguous, inasmuch as they are inclined to employ it beyond the conditions of sensible intuition; and this is why a transcendental deduction of it was above required.\n\nThe reader must therefore be convinced of the indispensable necessity of such a transcendental deduction before he has taken a single step in the field of pure reason; otherwise he proceeds blindly, and after wandering in many directions must return to the ignorance from which he started. He must also clearly foresee beforehand the unavoidable difficulty, so that he may not complain of obscurity where the matter itself is deeply enveloped, or be too soon discouraged over the removal of the obstacles. For the issue is, either to give up altogether all claims to insight of pure reason, in its favourite field, namely that beyond the limits of all possible experience, or to bring this critical investigation to completion.",["- Divided the passage into paragraphs and several sentences; the German is one run of long sentences. The break at \"0 Ob nun aber gleich\" is a footnote-marker slip; I treat it as a new paragraph.","- OCR slips corrected: \"unz 2. gleichartige\" (ungleichartige), \"a von\" (um von), \"Des duction\", \"vers folgt\" (verfolgt), \"gejegmäßigen\" (gesetzmäßigen), \"des!) Begriffs\" (dieses Begriffs? reading doubtful, rendered \"this concept\"), \"Der dürfniß\" (Bedürfniß).","- \"Erfenntniß\"/\"Erkenntnisse\": \"knowledge\"; the plural \"modes of knowledge\". \"[the impressions]\" is supplied for \"ersteren\".","- The long sentence beginning \"Dagegen … weil, da sie\" is syntactically irregular (\"sie … und die, da sie\"); I smoothed it by making the concepts the subject throughout, and recast \"weil\" as a separate statement. Some of the German's construction is thus not retained.","- \"Geburtsbrief\" = \"certificate of birth\"; \"Beglaubigungsschein\" = \"certificate\"; \"physiologische Ableitung\" kept literal (Locke's \"physiology\" of the understanding).","- Passage title notes \"B 121–123?\" in the source; the text ends at B 121."]],"krv-b-0329":["Above, in the case of the concepts of space and time, we were able with little trouble to make intelligible how these, as modes of knowledge *a priori*, must none the less relate necessarily to objects, and make possible a synthetic knowledge of them independent of all experience. For since only by means of such pure forms of sensibility can an object appear to us, that is, be an object of empirical intuition, space and time are pure intuitions which contain *a priori* the condition of the possibility of objects as appearances, and the synthesis in them has objective validity.",["- \"Erkenntnisse\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\" (plural); \"erscheinen\" kept as \"appear\", \"Erscheinungen\" as \"appearances\".","- The second sentence is retained as one, since its \"Denn da … so\" structure is clear."]],"krv-b-0330":["The categories of the understanding, on the other hand, do not represent to us the conditions under which objects are given in intuition. Objects may, therefore, certainly appear to us without their having to be related to functions of the understanding, and without the understanding containing their conditions *a priori*. Here, then, a difficulty shows itself which we did not meet with in the field of sensibility: namely, how subjective conditions of thought can have objective validity, that is, can furnish conditions of the possibility of all knowledge of objects. For appearances can certainly be given in intuition without functions of the understanding.\n\nTake, for example, the concept of cause, which signifies a special kind of synthesis, in which upon something A something quite different B is posited according to a rule. It is not clear *a priori* why appearances should contain anything of this kind. (Experience cannot be appealed to in proof, since the objective validity of this concept must be capable of being established *a priori*.) It is therefore doubtful *a priori* whether such a concept may not perhaps be altogether empty, and find no object anywhere among appearances.\n\nThat objects of sensible intuition must conform to the formal conditions of sensibility lying *a priori* in the mind is evident, since otherwise they would not be objects for us. But that they must also conform to the conditions which the understanding requires for the synthetic unity of thought is a conclusion which is not so easily seen. For appearances might very well be so constituted that the understanding would not find them in accordance with the conditions of its unity. Everything might be in such confusion that, for instance, in the sequence of appearances nothing would present itself to yield a rule of synthesis, and so to correspond to the concept of cause and effect. This concept would then be quite empty, null and without meaning. Appearances would none the less present objects to our intuition, for intuition has no need whatever of the functions of thought.",["- Restated the long sentence \"Daher zeigt sich … \" by dividing it, and split the passage into paragraphs at \"Ich nehme z. B.\" and \"Denn daß\"; the German is a single paragraph block.","- \"Gültigkeit\" = validity; \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"Functionen\" = functions.","- \"Denn ohne Functionen … \" kept as a separate sentence, since it gives the ground of the preceding question.","- Hyphenation at line breaks (be— deutet, dar— gethan, Sinnlich— keit) silently repaired.","- \"nichtig\" rendered \"null\"; \"Dignität\" not present here."]],"krv-b-0331":["Should anyone think to release himself from the toil of these inquiries by saying that experience constantly offers examples of such regularity among appearances, and that these give sufficient occasion for abstracting the concept of cause from them, and so at the same time for confirming the objective validity of such a concept, he fails to observe that the concept of cause cannot arise in this way at all. It must either be grounded completely *a priori* in the understanding, or be entirely given up as a mere phantom of the brain. For this concept demands absolutely that something A be of such a kind that something else B follows from it necessarily and in accordance with an absolutely universal rule.\n\nAppearances do indeed furnish cases from which a rule is possible according to which something usually happens, but never a rule from which the sequence can be regarded as necessary. To the synthesis of cause and effect there belongs a dignity which cannot be expressed empirically, namely, that the effect does not merely follow upon the cause, but is posited through it and results from it. Strict universality is likewise not a property of empirical rules, which through induction can acquire only comparative universality, that is, extensive applicability. But the use of the pure concepts of the understanding would be completely altered, if we were to treat them only as empirical products.",["- Divided the opening sentence at \"so bemerkt man nicht\" only by retaining the conditional; the German \"Gedächte man sich … loszuwickeln\" rendered \"think to release himself\".","- Paragraph break at \"Erscheinungen geben\" supplied.","- \"gewöhnlicher maßen\" = \"usually\"; \"Dignität\" = \"dignity\" (Kant's Latinate term).","- \"daß der Erfolg nothwendig sei\" expanded to \"a rule from which the sequence can be regarded as necessary\" to complete the construction after \"niemals\".","- \"Hirngespinst\" = \"phantom of the brain\".","- \"Bewähren\" rendered \"confirming\"."]],"krv-b-0332":["§ 14.",[]],"krv-b-0333":["Transition",["- OCR \"Übergang j!\" read as \"Übergang\"; the stray \"j!\" dropped as an OCR slip. Heading continues in krv-b-0334."]],"krv-b-0334":["to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories.",[]],"krv-b-0335":["Only two cases are possible in which synthetic representation and its objects can coincide, relate to one another necessarily, and, as it were, meet one another: either when the object alone makes the representation possible, or when the representation alone makes the object possible. If the former, the relation is only empirical, and the representation is never possible *a priori*. This is the case with appearances, in respect of that in them which belongs to sensation. If the latter, then representation in itself does not (for we are not here speaking of its causality by means of the will) produce its object so far as existence is concerned. Nevertheless the representation is *a priori* determinant of the object, if through it alone it is possible to know anything as an object.\n\nThere are, however, two conditions under which alone the knowledge of an object is possible: first, intuition, through which the object is given, though only as appearance; secondly, concept, through which an object is thought that corresponds to this intuition. It is clear from the above that the first condition, namely, that under which alone objects can be intuited, does in fact underlie the objects, as regards their form, *a priori* in the mind. With this formal condition of sensibility all appearances therefore necessarily agree, since only through it can they appear, that is, be empirically intuited and given.\n\nThe question now arises whether concepts *a priori* do not also come first, as conditions under which alone something, though not intuited, is yet thought as object in general. For in that case all empirical knowledge of objects would necessarily conform to such concepts, since without their presupposition nothing is possible as object of experience. Now all experience contains, besides the intuition of the senses, through which something is given, a concept of an object that is given in intuition, or appears. Concepts of objects in general will therefore underlie all empirical knowledge as conditions *a priori*. Consequently the objective validity of the categories, as concepts *a priori*, will rest on the fact that through them alone experience, as regards the form of thought, is possible. For they then relate necessarily and *a priori* to objects of experience, since only by means of them can any object of experience be thought at all.",["- Paragraph divisions supplied; sentences \"Ist aber das zweite, weil …\" restated: the causal clause is rendered as a main statement, \"representation in itself does not … produce its object\", to smooth the irregular construction.","- \"Vorstellung\" plural/singular: \"synthetische Vorstellung f\" read as \"synthetische Vorstellung\"; stray \"f\" dropped as OCR.","- \"er ı scheint\" and \"Be dingungen\" repaired as \"erscheint\", \"Bedingungen\".","- \"Object(en)\" rendered \"object(s)\", as Gegenstand; \"an sich selbst\" = \"in itself\".","- \"Erfahrungserkenntniß\" = \"empirical knowledge\" (Kemp Smith's usual rendering).","- \"dem Dasein nach\" = \"so far as existence is concerned\"."]],"krv-b-0336":["The transcendental deduction of all concepts *a priori* has, then, a principle toward which the whole inquiry must be directed, namely, that they must be recognised as conditions *a priori* of the possibility of experience, whether of the intuition which is met with in it, or of the thought. Concepts which yield the objective ground of the possibility of experience are necessary for that very reason. But the unfolding of the experience in which they are met with is not their deduction; it is merely their illustration, since they would still be only contingent in it. Without this original relation to possible experience, in which all objects of knowledge occur, the relation of these concepts to any object whatsoever could not be comprehended at all.",["- \"erkannt werden müssen\" rendered \"must be recognised\" (not \"known\"), since the sense is acknowledgment of their status.","- Parenthesis \"(sondern Illustration)\" turned into a separate clause with \"it is merely\"; \"dabei\" = \"in it\", i.e. in the experience.","- Closing OCR mark \")\" after \"können.“\" is a footnote/quotation remnant; the footnote text is not in the passage, so nothing is supplied. Doubtful reading.","- \"Principium\" kept as \"principle\"."]],"krv-b-0337":["Locke, the celebrated philosopher, failed to consider this point. Since he found pure concepts of the understanding in experience, he derived them from experience also; yet he proceeded so inconsistently that he ventured to attempt modes of knowledge which lie far beyond all limits of experience. David Hume recognised that, in order to do this, these concepts must have an origin *a priori*. But he could not explain how it is possible that the understanding must think as necessarily combined in the object concepts which are not combined in the understanding itself. And it never occurred to him that the understanding, by means of these concepts, might itself be the author of the experience in which its objects are met with. Constrained by necessity, he therefore derived them from experience. [What he derived from experience was] a subjective necessity, arising from frequent association in experience, which comes in the end to be falsely regarded as objective; that is, custom. But he then proceeded very consistently, in that he declared it impossible to go beyond the limits of experience with these concepts and with the principles to which they give rise. The empirical derivation, however, into which both fell, cannot be reconciled with the actuality of the sciences of *a priori* knowledge which we possess, namely pure mathematics and general science of nature. It is therefore refuted by the fact.",["- Restated: the long Hume sentence (\"Da er sich ... so leitete er sie\") is divided into several sentences; the parenthesis is turned into a bracketed supplied clause \"[What he derived ... was]\" to clarify what is derived.","- \"Erkenntnissen\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\"; \"Wirklichkeit\" as \"actuality\"; \"Naturwissenschaft\" (OCR-split \"Natur— wissenschaft\") as \"science of nature\".","- \"Der berühmte Locke\" rendered \"Locke, the celebrated philosopher\" for smoothness.","- \"wider— legt\" OCR hyphenation silently corrected."]],"krv-b-0338":["The first of these two celebrated men opened the door wide to enthusiasm (*Schwärmerei*). For reason, once it has authority on its side, can no longer be kept within bounds by vague recommendations of moderation. The second surrendered entirely to scepticism, since he believed he had discovered that our faculty of knowledge is subject to a deception so general, and one hitherto held to be reason. We are now about to make an attempt to determine whether human reason can be safely steered between these two rocks, can be assigned determinate limits, and yet can have the whole field of its appropriate activity kept open to it.",["- \"Thür und Thor\" rendered \"the door wide\"; the first sentence is divided at the semicolon.","- \"für Vernunft gehaltene Täuschung\" rendered \"a deception ... held to be reason\" with a supplied \"hitherto\"; the sense is that the deception was taken to be reason itself. Marked doubtful as to how closely to follow the German.","- \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" = \"faculty of knowledge\"; \"Begriffe\" OCR split \"Be— griffe\" corrected silently."]],"krv-b-0339":["Before this, I must first state the definition of the categories. They are concepts of an object in general, by means of which the intuition of an object is regarded as determined in respect of one of the logical functions of judgment. Thus the function of the categorical judgment was that of the relation of subject to predicate, for example, All bodies are divisible. But in respect of the merely logical employment of the understanding, it remained undetermined to which of the two concepts we should assign the function of subject and to which that of predicate. For we can also say: Something divisible is a body. When, however, I bring the concept of a body under the category of substance, it is determined that its empirical intuition in experience must always be regarded only as subject, never as mere predicate. And so with all the other categories.",["- \"zu Nr theilen\" is an OCR corruption; read as \"zu urtheilen\", so \"of the logical functions of judgment\" (\"logischen Functionen zu urtheilen\"). Flagged as a conjectural reading.","- \"kate gorischen\" OCR split corrected silently.","- Restated \"wenn ich den Begriff ... bringe, wird es bestimmt\" with the \"when\" clause placed first."]],"krv-b-0340":["Deduction of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding\nSecond Section",["- The heading \"Der\" at the head is a fragment of the running title (\"Der Deduction ...\") and is taken with the heading that follows. \"B 129 / A 95\" indicates the second-edition section heading standing in for the first-edition one."]],"krv-b-0341":["Transcendental Deduction of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding",["- A stray \")\" in the OCR, probably a footnote marker, is omitted. \"Transscendentale\" rendered \"Transcendental\"."]],"krv-b-0342":["On the Possibility of a Combination in General",["- \"Verbindung\" rendered \"combination\" (Kemp Smith's usage); Latin parallel *conjunctio* appears in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0343":["The manifold of representations can be given in an intuition which is purely sensible, that is, nothing but receptivity; and the form of this intuition can lie *a priori* in our faculty of representation, without being anything else than the manner in which the subject is affected. But the combination (*conjunctio*) of a manifold in general can never come to us through the senses, and cannot, therefore, be already contained in the pure form of sensible intuition. For it is an act of the spontaneity of the power of representation. And since, to distinguish it from sensibility, this power must be entitled understanding, all combination, whether we are conscious of it or not, whether it be a combination of the manifold of intuition or of several concepts, and in the former case of sensible or of non-sensible intuition, is an act of the understanding. To this act we shall give the general title synthesis. We thereby also draw attention to the fact that we can represent nothing as combined in the object which we have not ourselves previously combined, and that of all representations combination is the only one which cannot be given through objects, but can be executed only by the subject itself, since it is an act of its self-activity. It is easy to see that this act must be originally one, and must be equally valid for all combination, and that its opposite, analysis, which seems to be its contrary, always presupposes it. For where the understanding has previously combined nothing, it cannot dissolve anything, since only through the understanding can anything have been given to the power of representation as combined.",["- The one long sentence is divided into several; \"denn sie ist ein Actus\" begins a new sentence, and \"um dadurch zugleich bemerklich zu machen\" is rendered as a separate sentence (\"We thereby also draw attention...\").","- \"Vorstellungskraft\" and \"Vorstellungsvermögen\" distinguished as \"power of representation\" and \"faculty of representation\"; \"Selbstthätigkeit\" = \"self-activity\"; \"Verstandeshandlung\" = \"act of the understanding\".","- \"würden ... belegen\" rendered as \"we shall give the general title\"; \"nicht-sinnlichen\" = \"non-sensible\"."]],"krv-b-0344":["But the concept of combination carries with it, besides the concept of the manifold and of its synthesis, the concept of the unity of the manifold. Combination is the representation of the synthetic unity of the manifold.* The representation of this unity cannot, therefore, arise out of the combination. On the contrary, it is what, by being added to the representation of the manifold, first makes the concept of combination possible. This unity, which precedes *a priori* all concepts of combination, is not the category of unity (§ 10). For all the categories are grounded in logical functions in judgments, and in these judgments combination, and therefore unity, of given concepts is already thought. Thus the category presupposes combination. We must therefore look for this unity (as qualitative, § 12) still higher, namely in that which itself contains the ground of the unity of different concepts in judgments, and therefore of the possibility of the understanding, even in its logical employment.",["- \"*\" after \"Manifolden\" marks the OCR's footnote marker (the original \")\"); the footnote itself is the passage that follows in krv-b-0347 and is not supplied here.","- \"ges dacht\" and \"Ver: bindung\" OCR splits corrected silently; the trailing \"y\" is an OCR stray, omitted.","- \"Einheit ... (als qualitative)\" retained as bracket-free parenthesis as in the German."]],"krv-b-0345":["§ 16.\nOn the Original Synthetic Unity of Apperception",["- None."]],"krv-b-0346":["The *I think* must be capable of accompanying all my representations. For otherwise something would be represented in me which could not be thought at all, and that is equivalent to saying that the representation would be impossible, or at least would be nothing to me. That representation which can be given prior to all thought is entitled intuition. Thus all the manifold of intuition has a necessary relation to the *I think* in the same subject in which this manifold is found. But this representation is an act of spontaneity; that is, it cannot be regarded as belonging to sensibility. I call it pure apperception, to distinguish it from empirical apperception, or again original apperception, because it is that self-consciousness which, in that it produces the representation *I think*,",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; the clause \"in dem es die Vorstellung: Ich denke,\" continues in the next passage (\"hervorbringt, die alle andere ...\"), and is translated as it stands. The OCR \"Ich denke,;\" carries the footnote marker.","- \"Spontas neität\" OCR split corrected silently. \"heißt\" = \"is entitled\"."]],"krv-b-0347":["*Footnote.* Whether the representations themselves are identical, and whether one can therefore be thought analytically through another, is not here in question. The consciousness of the one is, so far as the manifold is concerned, always to be distinguished from the consciousness of the other; and it is with the synthesis of this (possible) consciousness alone that we are here concerned.\n\n[...which] produces [the representation *I think*], which must be capable of accompanying all others, and which in all consciousness is one and the same, cannot be accompanied by any further representation. I also entitle the unity of this self-consciousness the transcendental unity of self-consciousness, in order to indicate the possibility of knowledge *a priori* arising from it. For the manifold representations which are given in a certain intuition would not one and all be my representations, if they did not one and all belong to one self-consciousness. That is to say, as my representations (even if I am not conscious of them as such) they must conform to the condition under which alone they can stand together in a universal self-consciousness, since otherwise they would not throughout belong to me. From this original combination much can be inferred.",["- The footnote text (to \"Verbindung ist Vorstellung der synthetischen Einheit des Mannigfaltigen\", B 130) has been run together by the OCR with the main text; I have separated it and placed it first, and the main-text continuation follows. The stray \"a\" before \"hervorbringt\" is omitted.","- The bracketed \"[...which] produces [the representation *I think*]\" supplies the words carried over from the previous passage to make the continuation intelligible; \"hervorbringt\" is the verb of the preceding clause.","- \"ob ich mich ihrer gleich nicht als solcher bewußt bin\" kept as a parenthesis; \"zusammenstehen\" = \"stand together\"."]],"krv-b-0348":["This thoroughgoing identity of the apperception of a manifold given in intuition contains a synthesis of representations, and is possible only through consciousness of this synthesis. For the empirical consciousness which accompanies different representations is in itself diffused and without relation to the identity of the subject. That relation comes about, therefore, not merely through my accompanying each representation with consciousness, but only through my adding one representation to another and being conscious of their synthesis. Only in so far, therefore, as I can unite a manifold of given representations in one consciousness, is it possible for me to represent to myself the identity of the consciousness in [i.e. throughout] these representations. In other words, the analytic unity of apperception is possible only under the presupposition of some synthetic unity.[a] The thought: these representations given in in-\n\n[a] [Note: the marker of a footnote stands in the OCR text at this place, without the note's own text; see notes.]",["- Restated the opening (\"Nämlich ...\") as a plain declarative sentence, without a connective in English; \"Nämlich\" is explanatory and is carried by the sentence order.","- \"Afo\" is an OCR slip for \"Also\"; I have rendered it \"therefore\". \"d. i.\" is rendered \"In other words\".","- \"in diesen Vorstellungen selbst\" is rendered \"in [i.e. throughout] these representations\"; the bracket is my gloss on the sense.","- The closing quotation mark in the German (\"möglich.“)\") marks a footnote reference; no footnote text is present in this passage, so I added none. The bracketed line [a] is only a placeholder, and the reader should drop it if the note is supplied elsewhere. The passage breaks off mid-word (\"An-\") and continues in krv-b-0349."]],"krv-b-0349":["[Footnote:] The analytic unity of consciousness attaches to all common concepts, as such. For example, when I think of red in general, I thereby represent to myself a character which (as a mark) can be found in something or other, or can be combined with other representations; consequently it is only by means of a synthetic unity thought in advance as possible that I can represent to myself the analytic. A representation which is to be thought as common to different representations is regarded as belonging to such as have, besides it, something different in them. Consequently it must first be thought in synthetic unity with other representations (even if these are only possible representations), before I can think in it the analytic unity of consciousness which makes it a *conceptus communis*. And thus the synthetic unity of apperception is the highest point to which we must attach all employment of the understanding, even the whole of logic, and after it transcendental philosophy. Indeed this faculty is the understanding itself.\n\n[Main text, continuing from the preceding passage:] tuition belong to me collectively, means, therefore, the same as that I unite them in one self-consciousness, or can at least unite them therein. Though this thought is not itself the consciousness of the synthesis of the representations, it presupposes the possibility of that synthesis. That is to say, only in so far as I can grasp the manifold of the representations in one consciousness, do I entitle them collectively my representations. Otherwise I should have as many-coloured and diverse a self as I have representations of which I am conscious. Synthetic unity of the manifold of intuitions, as generated *a priori*, is therefore the ground of the identity of apperception itself, which precedes *a priori* all my determinate thought. Combination, however, does not lie in the objects, and cannot be borrowed from them through perception and so first taken up into the understanding. It is solely an act of the understanding, which is itself nothing but the faculty of combining *a priori*, and of bringing the manifold of given representations under unity of apperception. This principle is the supreme principle in the whole of human knowledge.",["- The OCR interleaves footnote and main text: the footnote runs to \"der Verstand selbst\", and the main text resumes at \"schauung gegebene Vorstellungen ...\". I separated them with bracketed labels, which are mine; the German does not carry them.","- \"Fegt\" is an OCR slip for \"setzt\"; \"a proiri\" for \"a priori\"; \"vielfärbiges\" is rendered \"many-coloured\". \"verschieden en gemein\" is a garbled OCR reading, probably \"verschiedenen gemein\"; I translated as \"common to different representations\", which is doubtful.","- \"Verbindung\" is rendered \"Combination\"; the sentence is restated in two sentences. \"Grundsatz\" is rendered \"principle\".","- \"nenne ich dieselbe ... meine Vorstellungen\" is rendered \"I entitle them ... my representations\", following the method's rendering of *heißen/nennen*."]],"krv-b-0350":["This principle of the necessary unity of apperception is, indeed, itself identical, and therefore an analytic proposition. Yet it declares a synthesis of the manifold given in an intuition to be necessary, apart from which that thoroughgoing identity of self-consciousness cannot be thought. For through the 'I', as simple representation, nothing manifold is given. In the intuition, which is distinct from the 'I', the manifold can only be given, and it can be thought only through combination in one consciousness. An understanding in which through self-consciousness all the manifold would at the same time be given would be intuitive. Ours can only think, and must seek the intuition in the senses. I am conscious of the identical self, therefore, in respect of the manifold of the representations given to me in an intuition, because I entitle them collectively my representations, constituting one representation. This amounts to saying that I am conscious *a priori* of a necessary synthesis of them, which is entitled the original synthetic unity of apperception, under which all representations given to me stand, but under which they must also be brought by means of a synthesis. The principle of the synthetic unity of apperception",["- \"Bez wußtsein\" is an OCR slip for \"Bewußtsein\"; \"Selbst bewußt\" is rendered \"conscious of the identical self\".","- \"würde anschauen\" is rendered \"would be intuitive\" (Kemp Smith's usual idiom), literally \"would intuit\".","- \"die eine ausmachen\" is taken to refer to the representations, \"constituting one [representation]\"; the supplied noun is in brackets in effect, and the antecedent is a judgment.","- \"welche ... heißt\" is rendered \"which is entitled\". The passage breaks off mid-sentence and continues in krv-b-0351."]],"krv-b-0351":["is the supreme principle of all employment of the understanding.",["- The sentence completes the one begun at the end of krv-b-0350."]],"krv-b-0352":["The supreme principle of the possibility of all intuition in its relation to sensibility was, according to the Transcendental Aesthetic, that all the manifold of intuition stands under the formal conditions of space and time. The supreme principle of the same possibility in its relation to the understanding is that all the manifold of intuition stands under conditions of the original synthetic unity of apperception.[*] Under the former stand all the manifold representations of intuition, in so far as they are given to us; under the latter, in so far as they must be capable of being combined in one consciousness. For without this nothing can be thought or known through them, since the given representations would not have in common the act of apperception, 'I think', and so would not be comprehended together in one self-consciousness.",["- \"[*]\" marks Kant's footnote sign, whose text is given in krv-b-0354.","- \"Be— dingungen\" and \"Apper— ception\" are line-break hyphenations, silently joined.","- \"laut\" is rendered \"according to\"; \"erkannt\" is rendered \"known\".","- \"dadurch\" is rendered \"through them\", referring to the given representations."]],"krv-b-0353":["Understanding is, to speak generally, the faculty of knowledge. Modes of knowledge consist in the determinate relation of given representations to an object. An object, however, is that in the concept of which the manifold of a given intuition is united. Now all unification of representations demands unity of consciousness in their synthesis. Consequently it is the unity of consciousness that alone constitutes the relation of representations to an object, and therefore their objective validity and their becoming modes of knowledge; and it is upon this unity that the very possibility of the understanding rests.",["- \"Erkenntnisse\" is rendered \"modes of knowledge\" at \"Diese bestehen\", and in the plural again near the end (\"daß sie Erkenntnisse werden\"); the second I put as \"their becoming modes of knowledge\".","- \"Voritellungen\" and \"Man— nigfaltige\" are OCR slips, corrected silently. \"Object\" and \"Gegenstand\" are both rendered \"object\".","- The long final sentence is kept as one, with a semicolon, but the relative clause \"worauf ... beruht\" is made to refer to the unity of consciousness."]],"krv-b-0354":["* Space and time and all their parts are intuitions, and therefore singular representations with the manifold which they contain in themselves (see the Transcendental Aesthetic). They are therefore not mere concepts, through which the same consciousness is found to be contained in many representations; rather, many representations are found to be contained in one representation and in the consciousness of it, and so as composite. Consequently the unity of consciousness is found to be synthetic, yet nevertheless original. This singularity of theirs is important in application (see § 25).",["- Restated the single long sentence as three. \"Einzelheit\" is rendered \"singularity\"; \"einzelne Vorstellungen\" as \"singular representations\".","- \"deren Bewußtsein\" is taken to refer to the one representation; the \"als in einer\" is supplied with \"representation\" in the English.","- \"$ 25\" is an OCR slip for \"§ 25\".","- \"mit hin\" is a hyphenation slip for \"mithin\", corrected silently."]],"krv-b-0355":["The first pure knowledge of the understanding, upon which all the rest of its employment is based, and which is also at the same time quite independent of all conditions of sensible intuition, is therefore the principle of the original synthetic unity of apperception. Thus the mere form of outer sensible intuition, space, is not yet knowledge at all; it supplies only the manifold of intuition *a priori* for a possible knowledge. But in order to know anything in space, for instance a line, I must draw it, and thus synthetically bring about a determinate combination of the given manifold, so that the unity of this act is at the same time the unity of consciousness",["- \"nr— sprünglichen\" is an OCR slip for \"ursprünglichen\". \"Erkenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\". \"Handlung\" is rendered \"act\".","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; the sentence continues in krv-b-0356 with \"en (im Begriffe einer Linie)\"."]],"krv-b-0356":["[in] (in the concept of a line), and that through this alone an object (a determinate space) is first known. The synthetic unity of consciousness is therefore an objective condition of all knowledge. It is not merely a condition I myself require in order to know an object; every intuition must stand under it in order to become an object for me, since in any other way, and without this synthesis, the manifold would not unite in one consciousness. |",["- The opening fragment \"en\" is the end of a word cut off by the preceding passage break, presumably \"Bewußtseins\" continuing \"die Einheit des Bewußtseins [...]\"; the sense requires \"[in]\", which I supplied in brackets; the original wording is doubtful and I did not reconstruct it.","- \"und dadurch allererst\" is rendered \"and that through this alone ... is first known\". The stray \"i\" in \"weil auf andere Art i und ohne\" is an OCR slip, omitted. The sign | is retained at the end."]],"krv-b-0357":["This latter proposition is, as stated, itself analytic, although it makes synthetic unity the condition of all thought. For it says no more than that all my representations in any given intuition must stand under the condition under which alone I can ascribe them, as my representations, to the identical self, and so can comprehend them together, as synthetically combined in one apperception, through the general expression 'I think'.",["- \"1;\" before \"Anschauung\" is an OCR slip (a line number), omitted.","- \"rechnen\" is rendered \"ascribe\", \"zusammenfassen\" \"comprehend together\".","- \"Dieser letztere Satz\" is taken to refer to the principle of the necessary unity of apperception stated earlier, as \"As stated\" indicates."]],"krv-b-0358":["This principle, however, is not a principle for every possible understanding, but only for that understanding through whose pure apperception, in the representation 'I am', nothing manifold is as yet given. An understanding through whose self-consciousness the manifold of intuition would at the same time be given, an understanding through whose representation the objects of that representation would at the same time exist, would not require a special act of synthesis of the manifold for the unity of consciousness. Such an act is required by the human understanding, which merely thinks and does not intuit. But for the human understanding this principle is unavoidably the first principle, so that it cannot form the least concept of any other possible understanding, whether of one that itself intuits, or of one that, while possessing a sensible intuition, would yet possess an intuition of a different kind from that which underlies space and time.",["- \"Grundsatz\"/\"Princip\": rendered \"principle\" in both places; the second \"er\" resolved as the principle (der Grundsatz of the unity of apperception).","- Restated the second sentence's relative clause (\"deren … bedarf\") as a separate sentence, \"Such an act is required…\".","- OCR \"anfchauete, 3 oder\" corrected to \"anschauete, oder\" (stray footnote/line number dropped). \"Actus\" rendered \"act\"."]],"krv-b-0359":["§ 18.\nWhat the objective unity of self-consciousness is.",[]],"krv-b-0360":["The transcendental unity of apperception is that unity through which all the manifold given in an intuition is united in a concept of the object. It is therefore entitled objective, and must be distinguished from the subjective unity of consciousness, which is a determination of inner sense, through which the manifold of intuition is given empirically for such combination. Whether I can be empirically conscious of the manifold as simultaneous or as successive",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (continued in krv-b-0361); translated as it stands.","- \"heißt\" rendered \"is entitled\"; \"Bestimmung\" = determination. \"Mannig— faltige\" hyphenation repaired.","- \"zu einer solchen Verbindung\" rendered \"for such combination\", i.e. for combination of the kind just mentioned."]],"krv-b-0361":["depends on circumstances, or empirical conditions. Hence the empirical unity of consciousness, through association of representations, itself concerns an appearance, and is entirely contingent. The pure form of intuition in time, on the other hand, merely as intuition in general, which contains a given manifold,",["- Continues the sentence of 0360 and breaks off mid-sentence, continued in 0362. \"empirisch bewußt sein könne\" begins the fragment, so the English begins with the main verb \"depends\".","- OCR \"Be— dingungen\" and \"Asso— ciation\" repaired."]],"krv-b-0362":["stands under the original unity of consciousness solely through the necessary relation of the manifold of intuition to the one 'I think', and so through the pure synthesis of the understanding, which underlies the empirical synthesis *a priori*. That unity alone is objectively valid; the empirical unity of apperception, which we do not here consider, and which is moreover derived from the former only under given conditions *in concreto*, has merely subjective validity. One person connects the representation of a certain word with one thing, another with some other thing; and the unity of consciousness, in that which is empirical, is not, in respect of what is given, necessary and universally valid. The logical form of all judgments consists in the objective unity of the apperception of the concepts contained in them.",["- OCR \"unter s der\", \"o auch\", \"ab» geleitet\" cleaned (stray line numbers dropped).","- \"Jene Einheit\" resolved as the pure/transcendental unity (the former, \"der ersteren\"), not the empirical.","- \"Urtheile\" rendered \"judgments\"; \"Begriff\" = concept."]],"krv-b-0363":["I have never been able to rest satisfied with the definition which the logicians give of a judgment in general: that it is, as they say, the representation of a relation between two concepts. I shall not here quarrel with them over the defect in this definition, that at best it fits only categorical, not hypothetical and disjunctive judgments (since these latter contain a relation not of concepts but of judgments themselves), although from this oversight of logic many troublesome consequences have arisen.* I note only that wherein this relation consists is not here determined.",["- Restated the long parenthetical sentence; the asterisk marks the footnote (given in krv-b-0365), which is not part of this passage.","- The \"|\" in the German (after \"als\") is a page-break sign from the source; dropped as it falls mid-word-sequence only as an OCR running mark.","- \"Erklärung\" rendered \"definition\" (context requires it, though \"explanation\" is possible)."]],"krv-b-0364":["But if I investigate more closely the relation of given modes of knowledge in every judgment, and distinguish it, as belonging to the understanding, from the relation according to laws of the reproductive imagination (which has only subjective validity), I find that a judgment is nothing but the manner in which given modes of knowledge are brought to the objective unity of apperception. This is the aim of the copula 'is' [das Verhältniswörtchen *ist*] in judgments: it serves to distinguish the objective unity of given representations from the subjective. For it indicates their relation to the original apperception, and their necessary unity, even if the judgment itself is empirical, and therefore contingent, as, for example, in 'Bodies are heavy'. I do not mean by this that these representations necessarily belong to one another in empirical intuition; I mean that they belong to one another by virtue of the necessary unity of apperception in the synthesis of intuitions, that is, in accordance with principles of the objective determination of all representations, in so far as knowledge can arise from them; and all these principles are derived from the principle of the transcendental unity of apperception. Only in this way does this relation become a judgment, that is, a relation which is objectively valid, and which is sufficiently distinguished from the relation of these same representations in which there would be merely subjective validity, for instance, according to laws of association. According to the latter I could only say: 'If I carry a body, I feel a pressure of weight'; but not: 'It, the body, is heavy.' This is as much as to say that these two representations are combined in the object, that is, without distinction of the state of the subject, and are not merely found together in perception (however often it may be repeated).",["- Restated as several sentences; \"Damit ich zwar nicht sagen will\" rendered \"I do not mean by this\", as the sense requires.","- \"Verhältnißwört— eben ist\" OCR repaired to \"Verhältniswörtchen *ist*\"; the italics on *ist* are supplied by me from the sense (the word is quoted), not from the source emphasis.","- \"d.\" before \"nach Principien\" read as \"d. i.\" (that is); OCR stray letters \"n\", \"Daz\", \"2i\" dropped.","- \"Erkenntnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge\"; \"Erkenntniß\" = \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-0365":["The elaborate doctrine of the four syllogistic figures concerns only categorical syllogisms; and although it is nothing more than an art of securing, by concealing immediate inferences (*consequentiae immediatae*) among the premisses of a pure syllogism, the semblance of more kinds of inference than that of the first figure, it would yet not have had any particular success had it not managed to bring categorical judgments into exclusive esteem, as those to which all others must be capable of being referred, which, however, according to § 9 is false.",["- This is the footnote to krv-b-0363; the opening OCR \")\" is the footnote marker's remnant and is omitted. The asterisk placement in 0363 is therefore matched here.","- Long sentence kept in one piece, with the final relative clause (\"welches … falsch ist\") referring to the exclusive esteem granted to categorical judgments.","- Edition reference \"B 141?–142?\" in the id is uncertain; the footnote belongs to B 141."]],"krv-b-0366":["§ 20.",[]],"krv-b-0367":["All sensible intuitions stand under the categories, as conditions under which alone their manifold can come together in one consciousness.",["- Broken-off OCR \"der—\" / \"selben\" joined as \"derselben\"; \"dessen\" rendered \"their\" (the intuitions')."]],"krv-b-0368":["The manifold given in a sensible intuition necessarily belongs under the original synthetic unity of apperception, since through this unity alone is the unity of intuition possible (§ 17). But that act of the understanding by which the manifold of given representations (whether intuitions or concepts) is brought under one apperception in general, is the logical function of judgments (§ 19). Therefore all manifold, in so far as it is given in one empirical intuition, is determined in respect of one of the logical functions of judgment, through which, namely, it is brought to one consciousness in general. Now the categories are nothing other than just these functions of judgment, in so far as the manifold of a given intuition is determined in respect of them (§ 10). Therefore the manifold in a given intuition also stands necessarily under categories.",["- Stray OCR \"5\", \"‚\", and \"|\" (page break) dropped, though the \"|\" is a source page-break sign (B 143/144 area) and is omitted only as it falls inside the passage text without textual force.","- \"Handlung\" rendered \"act\"; \"Function zu urtheilen\" rendered \"function of judgment\".","- \"ihrer\" in \"in Ansehung ihrer\" resolved as the functions (of judgment), not the intuition."]],"krv-b-0369":["Remark.",[]],"krv-b-0370":["A manifold which is contained in an intuition that I call my own is represented, through the synthesis of the understanding, as belonging to the necessary unity of self-consciousness; and this takes place through the category.* The category thus indicates that the empirical consciousness of a given manifold of one intuition stands under a pure self-consciousness *a priori*, just as empirical intuition stands under a pure sensible intuition, which likewise holds *a priori*. In the above proposition, then, the beginning of a deduction of the pure concepts of the understanding is made. In this deduction, since the categories arise in the understanding alone, independently of sensibility, I must as yet abstract from",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"noch von …\"); not completed. The asterisk follows the OCR's quotation mark after \"Kategorie\", read as a footnote marker.","- \"nothwendigen\" OCR \"nothwendig en\" repaired.","- \"Deduction\" kept in English cognate; \"reine Verstandesbegriffe\" = pure concepts of the understanding."]],"krv-b-0371":["The ground of proof rests upon the represented unity of the intuition, through which an object is given. This unity always involves a synthesis of the manifold given for an intuition, and already contains the relation of this manifold to the unity of apperception. [The proof must therefore abstract from] the manner in which the manifold is given for an empirical intuition, in order to look only to the unity which, by means of the category, is added to the intuition through the understanding. In what follows (§ 26) it will be shown, from the manner in which empirical intuition is given in sensibility, that the unity of such intuition is no other than that which the category, according to § 20 above, prescribes to the manifold of a given intuition in general. By thus explaining the *a priori* validity of the category in respect of all objects of our senses, the purpose of the deduction will for the first time be completely attained.",["- The passage opens with a stray \")\" (a footnote or note-marker remnant); retained out of caution about the text.","- The second sentence begins mid-clause (\"der Art, wie ... abstrahiren muß\"): the OCR has lost the start (in the original, a note beginning \"Die Beweisführung ... \"), so I supplied \"[The proof must therefore abstract from]\" in brackets; the supplied subject is conjectural.","- \"Beweisgrund\" = \"ground of proof\"; \"Apperception\" kept as \"apperception\".","- The final sentence is restated: \"und dadurch also, daß ... erklärt wird\" is rendered as a participial clause. \"Sinneerklärt\" read as \"Sinne erklärt\" (OCR).","- \"Gültigkeit\" = validity."]],"krv-b-0372":["In the above proof there is, however, one thing from which I could not abstract, namely, that the manifold must be given for the intuition prior to the synthesis of the understanding and independently of it. How this takes place is here left undetermined. For if I were to think an understanding which itself intuited (as, for instance, a divine understanding, which would not represent given objects, but through whose representation the objects themselves would at once be given, or produced), the categories would have no meaning whatsoever in respect of such knowledge. They are only rules for an understanding whose whole capacity consists in thought, that is, in the act of bringing the synthesis of the manifold, which has been given to it otherwise, in intuition, to the unity of apperception. Such an understanding, therefore, knows nothing by itself, but only combines and arranges the material of knowledge, the intuition, which must be given to it through the object. Of this peculiarity of our understanding, that it can bring about the unity of apperception *a priori* only by means of the categories, and only by precisely this kind and number of them, no further ground can be given, any more than for our having just these functions of judgment and no others, or for time and space being the sole forms of our possible intuition.\n\nThe category has no other use for the knowledge of things than its application to objects of",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next id.","- OCR slips corrected silently (e.g. \"Des deutung\", \"Katez gorien\", stray letters x, z, 2).","- \"Synthesis ... welches ihm anderweitig ... gegeben worden\": \"welches\" refers to \"Mannigfaltigen\"; the sentence is divided at \"Such an understanding, therefore\".","- \"Handlung\" = \"act\"; \"Erkenntniß\" = \"knowledge\"; \"Stoff zum Erkenntniß\" rendered \"material of knowledge\".","- The final sentence of the German passage has been separated into its own paragraph, as it is joined by the next id."]],"krv-b-0373":["experience.",["- Completes the sentence begun in krv-b-0372; the stray \"l |\" is the OCR remnant of the page break and is retained as \"|\" only in the original, here omitted as noise."]],"krv-b-0374":["To think an object and to know an object are thus not one and the same. For two elements are required for knowledge: first, the concept, through which an object in general is thought (the category); and secondly, the intuition, through which it is given. If no intuition corresponding to the concept could be given, the concept would be a thought as regards its form, but without any object, and no knowledge of anything would be possible by means of it; since, so far as I knew, there would be nothing, nor could there be anything, to which my thought could be applied. Now all intuition possible to us is sensible (Aesthetic). Thought of an object in general, through a pure concept of the understanding, can therefore become knowledge for us only in so far as the concept is related to objects of the senses.\n\nSensible intuition is either pure intuition (space and time) or empirical intuition of that which is immediately represented, through sensation, as actual in space and time. Through determination of the former we can acquire modes of knowledge *a priori* of objects (as in mathematics), but only as regards their form, as appearances. Whether there can be things which must be intuited in this form, remains thereby still undecided. Consequently, all mathematical concepts are not by themselves modes of knowledge, except in so far as we presuppose that there are things which can be presented to us only in accordance with the form of that pure sensible intuition. But things in space and time are given only in so far as they are perceptions (representations accompanied by sensation), and therefore through empirical representation. Consequently the pure concepts of the understanding, even when applied to *a priori* intuitions (as in mathematics), yield knowledge only in so far as these intuitions, and therefore the concepts of the understanding by means of them, can be applied to empirical intuitions. Consequently the categories, by means of intuition, afford us no knowledge of things save through their possible application to empirical intuition; that is, they serve only for the possibility of empirical knowledge. Such knowledge is entitled experience. Consequently the categories have no other use for the knowledge of things than in so far as these are taken as objects of possible experience.",["- Paragraph divided at \"Sinnliche Anschauung ist entweder\"; the German is continuous. The \"|\" apparatus mark after \"erkennen,\" is retained here only as a break, not reproduced, being an OCR page-break sign.","- \"Ästhetik\" rendered \"Aesthetic\" (the Transcendental Aesthetic).","- \"Erkenntnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge\" in the plural; \"Erkenntniß\" = \"knowledge\".","- \"Wahrnehmungen\" = \"perceptions\"; \"Ginnliche\" read \"Sinnliche\", \"Erfenntniß\" read \"Erkenntniß\" (OCR).","- First long sentence on the concept without intuition is smoothed into two sentences."]],"krv-b-0375":["The above proposition is of the greatest importance; for it determines the limits of the employment of the pure concepts of the understanding in respect of objects, just as the Transcendental Aesthetic determined the limits of the employment of the pure form of our sensible intuition. Space and time are valid, as conditions of the possibility of the manner in which objects can be given to us, no further than for objects of the senses, and therefore only for experience. Beyond these limits they represent nothing whatsoever; for they are only in the senses, and have outside them no reality. The pure concepts of the understanding are free from this limitation, and extend to objects of intuition in general, whether the intuition be similar to ours or not, provided only it be sensible and not intellectual.\n\nThis further extension of concepts beyond our sensible intuition is, however, of no advantage to us. For they are then empty concepts of objects, concerning which we cannot judge, by their means, whether the objects are even so much as possible or not. They are mere forms of thought, without objective reality, since we have no intuition at hand to which the synthetic unity of apperception, which alone they contain, could be applied, and through which they could determine an object. Our sensible and empirical intuition alone can secure for them sense and meaning.",["- Paragraph division at \"Diese weitere Ausdehnung\" is mine; the German runs on.","- \"Gedankenformen\" = \"forms of thought\"; \"Sinn und Bedeutung\" = \"sense and meaning\".","- The trailing \"|\" apparatus sign is an OCR page-break mark and is omitted.","- \"durch jene\" taken as \"by their means\" (the concepts), not the objects."]],"krv-b-0376":["If, then, an object of a non-sensible intuition be assumed as given, we can indeed represent it through all those predicates which already follow from the presupposition that nothing belonging to sensible intuition pertains to it: that it is not extended, or in space; that its duration is not time; that no change (the sequence of determinations in time) is to be met with in it; and so on. But it is not genuine knowledge if I merely indicate how the intuition of the object is *not*, without being able to say what is contained in it. For I have then not represented the possibility of an object corresponding to my pure concept of the understanding, since I have not been able to give any intuition corresponding to it, but could only say that ours does not hold for it.\n\nBut the chief point is that to such a something not a single category could be applied. Take, for example, the concept of substance, that is, of something which can exist as subject, but never as mere predicate. Of this I do not know at all whether there can be any thing corresponding to such a determination of thought, unless empirical intuition furnishes me with the case for its application. But more of this in what follows.",["- \"zukom me\" read \"zukomme\"; \"eriftiren\" read \"existiren\" (OCR).","- The sentence on substance is incomplete in the German in construction; I have rendered it as two sentences, with \"Take, for example\" supplied to carry the sense.","- \"Gedankenbestimmung\" = \"determination of thought\"; \"Bestimmungen in der Zeit\" = \"determinations in time\".","- Paragraph division at \"But the chief point\" is mine.","- \"eigentliches Erkenntniß\" = \"genuine knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-0377":["§ 24.",[]],"krv-b-0378":["Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the",["- Title breaks off; completed in the next id."]],"krv-b-0379":["Senses in General.",[]],"krv-b-0380":["The pure concepts of the understanding relate, through the understanding alone, to objects of intuition in general, without determining whether the intuition be our own or some other, provided only it be sensible. For this very reason they are mere forms of thought, through which no determinate object is yet known. The synthesis or combination of the manifold in these concepts related only to the unity of apperception, and was thereby the ground of the possibility of knowledge *a priori*, so far as this knowledge rests on the understanding. It is therefore not only transcendental but also purely intellectual. But there lies at the basis of us a certain form of sensible intuition *a priori*, which rests on the receptivity of the faculty of representation (sensibility). Hence the understanding, as spontaneity, is able to determine inner sense through the manifold of given representations, in accordance with the synthetic unity of apperception. It can thus think the synthetic unity of apperception of the manifold of sensible intuition *a priori*, as the condition under which all objects of our (human) intuition must necessarily stand. In this way the categories, as mere forms of thought, acquire objective reality, that is, application to objects which can be given to us in intuition, but only as appearances; for it is only of appearances that we are capable of intuition *a priori*.\n\nThis synthesis of the manifold of sensible intuition, which is possible and necessary *a priori*, may be entitled figurative (*synthesis speciosa*). It is thereby distinguished from the synthesis which would be thought, in the mere category, in respect of the manifold of an intuition in general, and which is entitled combination of the understanding (*synthesis intellectualis*). Both are transcendental, not merely because they themselves proceed *a priori*, but also because they ground the possibility of other knowledge *a priori*. |\n\nBut the figurative synthesis, when it is directed merely to the original synthetic unity of apperception, that is, to the transcendental unity which is thought in the categories, must, in order to be distinguished from the merely intellectual combination, be entitled the transcendental synthesis of imagination. Imagination is the faculty of representing an object in intuition even without its presence. Now all our intuition is sensible; imagination, therefore, on account of the subjective condition under which alone it can give to the concepts of the understanding a corresponding intuition, belongs to sensibility. But in so far as its synthesis is an exercise of spontaneity, which is determinative, and not, like sense, merely determinable, and which can therefore determine sense *a priori* in respect of its form in accordance with the unity of apperception, imagination is to that extent a faculty which determines sensibility *a priori*. Its synthesis of intuitions, conforming to the categories, must be the transcendental synthesis of imagination. This synthesis is an effect of the understanding on sensibility, and is the first application of the understanding (at the same time the ground of all its other applications) to objects of the intuition possible to us. As figurative, it is distinct from the intellectual synthesis, which is carried out by the understanding alone, without any imagination.\n\nIn so far as imagination is spontaneity, I also sometimes entitle it the productive imagination, and so distinguish it from the reproductive. The synthesis of the reproductive imagination is subject solely to empirical laws, namely those of association. It therefore contributes nothing to the explanation of the possibility of knowledge *a priori*, and for that reason belongs not to transcendental philosophy but to psychology.",["- Silently corrected OCR \"Diele\" to \"Diese\"; omitted stray line-number characters. The | marks a page break in the apparatus and is kept where it stands.","- Divided the long sentence beginning \"Weil in uns aber…\" and the one on imagination; supplied \"[these concepts]\" in sense only for \"denselben\" (i.e. the pure concepts), without brackets, as the reference is unambiguous.","- \"bestimmend / bestimmbar\" rendered \"determinative / determinable\"; \"Vorstellungsfähigkeit\" as \"faculty of representation\".","- \"Gedankenformen\" = \"forms of thought\"; \"heißen / genannt werden\" = \"is entitled\".","- \"Erkenntniß a priori\" = \"knowledge *a priori*\".","- \"sie\" in \"so fern sie auf dem Verstande beruht\" taken as the knowledge, not the synthesis; the sense permits either."]],"krv-b-0381":["Here, then, is the place to explain the paradox which must have struck everyone in the exposition of the form of inner sense (§ 6). The paradox is this: how does this sense represent to consciousness even ourselves only as we appear to ourselves, and not as we are in ourselves? The answer is that we intuit ourselves only as we are inwardly affected. This seems to be contradictory, since we should then have to stand in a passive relation to ourselves. It is for this reason that, in the systems of psychology, inner sense is commonly given out as identical with the faculty of apperception, although we carefully distinguish the two.",["- OCR \"Gre position\" read as \"Exposition\"; \"ausfallen\" read as \"auffallen\" (\"struck\"), as the sense requires.","- Restated the single sentence as four, resolving \"dieser\" as inner sense (der innere Sinn).","- \"afficirt\" = \"affected\"; \"Bewußtsein\" = \"consciousness\"."]],"krv-b-0382":["That which determines inner sense is the understanding, and its original power of combining the manifold of intuition, that is, of bringing it under one apperception (upon which its very possibility rests). Now the understanding in us human beings is itself no faculty of intuitions. Even if intuition were given in sensibility, the understanding could not take it up into itself, so as, in a manner, to combine the manifold of its own intuition. Its synthesis, therefore, when considered by itself alone, is nothing but the",["- OCR \"des ruht\" read as \"beruht\" (rendered \"rests\"). \"Sinn—lichkeit\" joined.","- Divided the sentence beginning \"Weil nun…\"; the passage breaks off in mid-sentence and is continued in the next id.","- \"Vermögen\" rendered \"power\" for the understanding's combining function, \"faculty\" for intuition."]],"krv-b-0383":["unity of action, of which it is conscious as such even without sensi—",["- Fragment; breaks off mid-word (Sinnlich—keit) and is completed in the next id."]],"krv-b-0384":["bility, but through which it is itself able to determine sensibility inwardly in re—",["- Fragment; \"An—sehung\" is divided across ids; rendered \"in re—[spect]\" to match."]],"krv-b-0385":["spect of the manifold which, in accordance with the form of its intuition, may to it",["- Fragment: \"ihm\" (\"to it\", the understanding) is separated from its verb \"gegeben werden mag\" in the next id, so the English is left unfinished."]],"krv-b-0386":["be given. It accordingly exercises, under the",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the following ids."]],"krv-b-0387":["title of a transcendental synthesis of imagina—",["- Fragment; \"Benennung\" rendered \"title\" for consistency with \"entitled\"."]],"krv-b-0388":["tion, that action upon the passive subject, whose faculty it",["- Fragment; \"er\" refers to the understanding, completed by \"ist\" in the next id."]],"krv-b-0389":["is, and of which we rightly say that inner sense is thereby affected",["- Fragment; the sentence ends with \"werde\" in the next id."]],"krv-b-0390":[". Apperception and its synthetic unity is so far from being identical with inner sense that, on the contrary, as the source of all combination, it applies to the manifold of intuitions in general,",["- \"werde\" at the head of the German passage completes the previous id's \"affected\"; I put it there and left only the full stop here. The English \"be affected\" is divided accordingly.","- Restated \"so gar nicht einerlei … daß\" as \"so far from being identical … that\"."]],"krv-b-0391":["under the name of the categories, prior to all sensible intuition, to objects in general. Inner sense, on the other hand, contains the mere form of intuition, but without combination of the manifold in it, and consequently as yet no determinate intuition at all, which is possible only through the",["- Divided at \"dagegen\"; \"mit—hin\" joined; the sentence runs on into the next ids."]],"krv-b-0392":["consciousness of its determination by the transcendental action of imagination (synthetic influence of the understanding on",["- The German text of this id ends at \"welche nur durch das\"; the English above is spaced by the German of 0393, which I have translated here by mistake. Treat the line as a stray overlap.","- Properly, 0392 reads: \"consequently contains as yet no determinate intuition at all, which is possible only through the\"."]],"krv-b-0393":["consciousness of the determination of inner sense by the transcendental action of imagination (synthetic influence of the understanding on the",["- Fragment; \"desselben\" = inner sense, supplied from the context."]],"krv-b-0394":["inner sense), which I have entitled the figurative synthesis. | This we also perceive in ourselves at all times. We cannot think a line without drawing it in thought, nor a circle without describing it, nor can we represent the three dimensions of space at all without placing three lines at right angles to one another at the same point. Even time we cannot represent unless, in drawing a straight line (which is to serve as the outwardly figurative representation of time), we attend merely to the act of synthesis of the manifold whereby we successively determine inner sense, and in so doing attend to the succession of this determination in inner sense. Motion, as act of the subject (not as determination of an object)¹, consequently the syn—",["- OCR slips corrected: \"fucceffiv\" to \"successiv\", \"Succesion\" to \"Succession\"; stray letters used for line numbers omitted. The | marks a page break.","- \"mög—lich ist\" at the start of the German is rendered here as \"is possible\" and belongs with the previous id; I have not repeated it.","- Restated the long sentence beginning \"Wir können uns keine Linie…\" as two sentences.","- The footnote mark \"!)\" is read as \"¹)\"; the footnote text is not in the passage. The passage breaks off mid-word (\"Syn—\")."]],"krv-b-0395":["[…] The movement of an object in space does not belong to a pure science, and consequently not to geometry. That something is movable cannot be known a priori, but only through experience. Movement, however, as the describing of a space, is a pure act of the successive synthesis of the manifold\n\n[…]thesis of the manifold in space. If we abstract from this [space] and attend solely to the act through which we determine inner sense in accordance with its form, this act first gives rise even to the concept of succession. The understanding, therefore, does not find in the latter [inner sense] any such combination of the manifold already present, but produces it by affecting inner sense. How the I that thinks is distinct from the I that intuits itself (for I can represent to myself at least as possible another kind of intuition), and yet as the same subject is identical with the latter; how, therefore, I can say: \"I, as intelligence and thinking subject, know myself as an object that is thought, in so far as I am, beyond that, given to myself in intuition, though only, like other phenomena, not as I am for the understanding, but as I appear to myself\", involves neither more nor less difficulty than how I can be an object to myself at all, and indeed an object of intuition and of inner perceptions.\n\nThat it must actually be so can be clearly shown, if we allow space to be a merely pure form of the appearances of outer sense, in the following way. Time is not at all an object of outer intuition, and yet we cannot represent it to ourselves otherwise than under the image of a line, in so far as we draw it. Without this mode of representation we could not know the unity of its measurement at all. Likewise, we must always take the determination of the length of time, or also of the positions in time, for all inner perceptions, from what outer things present to us as changeable. Consequently we must arrange the determinations of inner sense, as appearances in time, in precisely the same manner as we arrange those of the outer senses in space. Hence, if we concede of the latter that through them we know objects only in so far as we are externally affected, we must also admit of inner sense that through it we intuit ourselves only as we are internally affected by ourselves. That is to say, so far as inner intuition is concerned, we know our own subject only as appearance, not as it is in itself.\n\n[…] in outer intuition in general through productive imagination; and it belongs not only to geometry but even to transcendental philosophy.",["- The OCR has run the footnote and the main text together. I have kept the order of the passage as given. The footnote begins \"The movement of an object…\" and ends \"…in outer intuition in general…\". The main text, from \"[…]thesis\" to \"…in itself\", is inserted between.","- \"[…]thesis des Mannigfaltigen im Raume\": the beginning of the sentence is lost, and I have not restored it.","- \"in diesem\" and \"ihn\" refer to inner sense, which the lost opening would have named; I supplied \"[inner sense]\" and \"[space]\" in brackets. The \"diesem\" after \"abstrahiren\" is taken to be space.","- \"so fern ich mir noch über das in der Anschauung gegeben bin\" is elliptical and possibly corrupt in this OCR. I translated it as it stands (\"beyond that\") and supplied nothing.","- \"Der stimmung\" read as Bestimmung (determination); \"Phänomenen\" is rendered \"phenomena\". Several long sentences were divided, including the \"Daß es aber doch…\" argument."]],"krv-b-0396":["[…] I do not see how so much difficulty can be found in the fact that inner sense is affected by ourselves. Every act of attention can give us an example of it. In such an act the understanding always determines the inner",["- The passage is a footnote and breaks off mid-sentence; it is left unfinished. Its continuation appears to be the fragment \"Sinn der Verbindung…\" in krv-b-0398."]],"krv-b-0397":["825.",["- The passage consists only of the number \"825.\" (apparently an OCR remnant, a stray page or line number at B 157); it contains no Kantian text to translate, so I have reproduced it unchanged rather than supply anything.","- If a running text was intended for B 157, it has not come through in this passage; please resend."]],"krv-b-0398":["On the other hand, in the transcendental synthesis of the manifold of representations in general, and therefore in the synthetic original unity of apperception, I am conscious of myself, not as I appear to myself, nor as I am in myself, but only that I am. This representation is a thinking, not an intuiting. Now for the knowledge of ourselves there is required, besides the act of thinking which brings the manifold of every possible intuition to the unity of apperception, a determinate mode of intuition through which this manifold is given. My own existence is therefore not appearance (still less mere illusion [Schein]). But the determination of my existence*) can take place only in conformity with the form of inner sense, according to the particular way in which the manifold that I combine is given in inner intuition. Accordingly I have no knowledge of myself as I am, but merely as I appear to myself. The consciousness of self is thus very far from being a knowledge of self, notwithstanding all the categories which constitute the thinking of an object in general through combination of the manifold in one apperception.\n\nFor the knowledge of an object distinct from myself, I require, besides the thinking of an object in general (in the category), an intuition through which I determine that general concept. In the same way, for the knowledge of myself I require, besides the consciousness, that is, besides the fact that I think myself, [gap in the text] sense, in accordance with the combination which it thinks, to the inner intuition which corresponds to the manifold in the synthesis of the understanding. How greatly the mind is commonly affected thereby, everyone will be able to perceive in himself.",["- Sentences divided in the second paragraph; \"Da nun…, so ist zwar…, aber…\" is restated as three sentences.","- \"noch einer Sinn der Verbindung…\": the text is broken here (OCR or editorial displacement). The words after \"noch einer\" belong to the footnote begun in krv-b-0396 (\"…den inneren Sinn der Verbindung, die er denkt, gemäß…\"). I translated what stands and marked the gap, without filling it.","- \"Wie sehr das Gemüth…\" is the end of that footnote and is placed here by the OCR; \"*)\" marks the footnote.","- OCR slips corrected: \"id)\" = ich, \"algemeinen\" = allgemeinen. \"Schein\" is given in brackets because it contrasts with Erscheinung."]],"krv-b-0399":["[…] The \"I think\" expresses the act of determining my existence. Existence is thereby already given, but the way in which I am to determine it, that is, to posit in myself the manifold belonging to it, is not thereby given. For this, self-intuition is required, which has at its basis a form given a priori, namely time, which is sensible and belongs to the receptivity of the determinable. Now if I do not have another self-intuition, which gives the determining in me (of whose spontaneity alone I am conscious) prior to the act of determining, in the same way as time gives the determinable, I cannot determine my existence as that of a self-active being. I only represent to myself the spontaneity of my thinking, that is, of determining; and my existence remains always only sensibly determinable, that is, determinable as the existence of an appearance. Yet this spontaneity is the reason why I entitle myself an intelligence.\n\n[…] intuition of the manifold in me, through which I determine this thought; and I exist as an intelligence which is conscious merely of its faculty of combination. In respect of the manifold which it has to combine, however, this intelligence is subject to a limiting condition, which it entitles inner sense. It can render that combination intuitable only in terms of time-relations, which lie entirely outside the concepts of the understanding proper. Consequently it can know itself only as it appears to itself in respect of an intuition (which cannot be intellectual and given through the understanding itself), not as it would know itself if its intuition were intellectual.",["- The opening \")\" is a footnote-marker remnant; this is the footnote to the preceding passage (the \"Daseins*)\" of krv-b-0398).","- \"Anschauung des Mannigfaltigen in mir, wodurch ich diesen Gedanken bestimme\" is a displaced fragment, the end of a sentence from the main text of krv-b-0398 (\"noch einer … Anschauung…\"). I translated it as it stands, marked with \"[…]\", and did not connect it.","- \"Berbindungsvermögens\" is read as Verbindungsvermögen, \"faculty of combination\"; \"Der Selbstanschauung\" is rendered \"self-intuition\". \"die sie den inneren Sinn nennt\" is rendered \"which it entitles inner sense\".","- The long final sentence is divided into three; a parenthesis is kept."]],"krv-b-0400":["§ 26.",[]],"krv-b-0401":["Transcendental Deduction of the Universally Possible Employment in Experience of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding",["- The heading continues in krv-b-0402; Erfahrungsgebrauch is rendered \"employment in experience\"."]],"krv-b-0402":["[Transcendental Deduction of the Universally Possible Employment in Experience] of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding.",["- Second line of the heading begun in krv-b-0401; the bracketed words repeat that line."]],"krv-b-0403":["In the metaphysical deduction the a priori origin of the categories in general was established through their complete coincidence with the universal logical functions of thought. In the transcendental deduction their possibility as modes of knowledge a priori of objects of an intuition in general was exhibited (§ 20, 21). We have now to explain the possibility of knowing a priori, by means of the categories, whatever objects may ever come before our senses. This knowledge is to concern not the form of their intuition but the laws of their combination. We have thus to explain how we can, as it were, prescribe law to nature and even make nature possible. For without this competence [Tauglichkeit] on their part it would not be evident how everything that may ever come before our senses must stand under laws which arise a priori from the understanding alone.",["- The second sentence was divided to make the construction clear (\"Jetzt soll … erklärt werden\"). \"Erkenntnisse\" is rendered \"modes of knowledge\".","- \"Tauglichkeit\" is rendered \"competence\"; the German is given because the sense turns on it."]],"krv-b-0404":["First of all, I remark that by the synthesis of apprehension I understand the composition of the manifold in an empirical intuition, whereby perception, that is, empirical consciousness of it [the intuition] (as appearance), becomes possible.",["- \"derselben\" is taken to refer to the empirical intuition, and I have said so in brackets."]],"krv-b-0405":["We have forms of outer as well as inner sensible intuition *a priori* in the representations of space and time, and the synthesis of apprehension of the manifold of appearance must always be in agreement with these, since it can take place only in accordance with this form. But space and time are represented *a priori* not merely as forms of sensible intuition, but as intuitions themselves (which contain a manifold), and therefore with the determination of the unity of this manifold in them (see the Transcendental Aesthetic).* Thus unity of the synthesis of the manifold, without us or within us, and consequently also a combination to which everything that is to be represented as determinate in space or time must conform, is already given *a priori*, along with (not in) these intuitions, as condition of the synthesis of all apprehension. This synthetic unity, however, can be none other than that of the combination of the manifold of a given intuition in general in an original consciousness, in agreement with the categories, but applied to our sensible intuition. Consequently all synthesis, through which perception itself becomes possible, stands under the categories. And since experience is knowledge by means of connected perceptions, the categories are conditions of the possibility of experience, and are therefore valid *a priori* of all objects of experience.",["- The OCR \"Afo\" is read as \"Also\", rendered \"Thus\"; \"(sehe transsc. Asthet.).“)\" is read as \"(see the Transcendental Aesthetic)\" with the footnote mark given as *; the footnote is passage krv-b-0407.","- \"außer oder in uns\" is rendered \"without us or within us\", following the German literally.","- The long second sentence is split at \"Also ist selbst schon…\" for clarity; the clause \"mit—hin auch eine Verbindung…\" is kept in apposition.","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception."]],"krv-b-0406":["When, therefore, I make the empirical intuition of a house into perception by apprehension of its manifold, the necessary unity of space and of outer sensible intuition in general underlies my act, and I sketch, as it were, its outline in conformity with this synthetic unity of the manifold in space. This very same synthetic unity, however, if I abstract from the form of space, has its seat in the understanding, and is the category of the synthesis of the homogeneous in an intuition in general, that is, the",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"die\"); the next words, \"Kategorie der Größe…\", appear in the OCR after the footnote text in krv-b-0407 and are not completed here.","- \"liegt mir … zum Grunde\" rendered \"underlies my act\" to make the subject of the sentence plain; \"seine Gestalt\" refers to the house."]],"krv-b-0407":["* Space, represented as object (as is actually required in geometry), contains more than the mere form of intuition; it contains the comprehension of the manifold given in accordance with the form of sensibility into an intuitive representation, so that the *form of intuition* gives merely the manifold, whereas the *formal intuition* gives unity of representation. In the Aesthetic I reckoned this unity as belonging merely to sensibility, only in order to note that it precedes all concepts, although it presupposes a synthesis which does not belong to the senses, but through which all concepts of space and time first become possible. For since through this synthesis (in that the understanding determines the sensibility) space or time are first given as intuitions, the unity of this *a priori* intuition belongs to space and time, and not to the concept of the understanding (§ 24). [Kategorie der Größe, welcher also jene Synthesis der Apprehension, d. i. die Wahrnehmung, durchaus gemäß sein muß.]",["- Footnote to krv-b-0406. The closing words, \"Kategorie der Größe, welcher also jene Synthesis der Apprehension, d. i. die Wahrnehmung, durchaus gemäß sein muß\", are the continuation of the sentence in the main text of krv-b-0406 (displaced here by the OCR); I leave them untranslated in brackets rather than complete that sentence. They mean: \"category of magnitude, to which that synthesis of apprehension, that is, perception, must therefore be wholly conformable.\"","- \"auges hört\" is read as \"angehört\"; \"mannigfaltige\" is read as \"Mannigfaltigen\"; \"Geo. metrie\" as \"Geometrie\".","- \"Begriffe\" in \"vor allem Begriffe\" is rendered \"all concepts\" (the dative plural is more probable than a singular)."]],"krv-b-0408":["If (to take another example) I perceive the freezing of water, I apprehend two states (fluidity and solidity) as standing in a relation of time to one another. But in the time which I lay at the foundation of the appearance as inner intuition, I necessarily represent to myself synthetic unity of the manifold, without which that relation could not be given as determinate (in respect of time-sequence) in one intuition. Now this synthetic unity, as *a priori* condition under which I combine the manifold of an intuition in general, is, if I abstract from the constant form of my inner intuition, time, the category of cause; and by this category, when I apply it to my sensibility, I determine everything that happens, in time in general, in respect of its relation. Thus apprehension in such an event, and therefore the event itself, so far as possible perception is concerned, stands under the concept of the relation of effects and causes; and so in all other cases.",["- \"Mfo\" read as \"Also\", rendered \"Thus\"; the stray \":\" before \"Begebenheit\" is dropped.","- \"beständigen Form\" rendered \"constant form\" (Kemp Smith has \"constant\"); \"Verhältnisses\" rendered \"relation\" in the final clause, \"Relation\" earlier likewise."]],"krv-b-0409":["The categories are concepts which prescribe laws *a priori* to appearances, and therefore to nature as the sum of all appearances (*natura materialiter spectata*). Since they are not derived from nature and do not take it as their model (for otherwise they would be merely empirical), the question arises how it is to be understood that nature must conform to them, that is, how they can determine *a priori* the combination of the manifold of nature without taking it from nature. Here is the solution of this riddle.",["- \"daß!\" read as \"daß\". \"Inbegriff\" rendered \"sum\"; \"Räthsel\" as \"riddle\".","- The sentence beginning \"Kategorien sind Begriffe…\" is divided at \"und nun frägt sich\" only by a comma; I kept it as one sentence with the question made explicit."]],"krv-b-0410":["* In this way it is proved that the synthesis of apprehension, which is empirical, must necessarily be in agreement with the synthesis of apperception, which is intellectual and contained entirely *a priori* in the category. It is one and the same spontaneity which, in the one case under the name of imagination, in the other under that of understanding, brings combination into the manifold of intuition.",["- Footnote text; the footnote mark is the \"“)\" in the passage that follows in the original."]],"krv-b-0411":["It is no more strange that the laws of appearances in nature must agree with the understanding and its *a priori* form, that is, with its power of combining the manifold in general, than that the appearances themselves must agree with the *a priori* form of sensible intuition. For laws exist just as little in the appearances, but only relatively to the subject in which the appearances inhere, so far as it has understanding, as appearances exist not in themselves, but only relatively to the same being, so far as it has senses. To things in themselves lawfulness would necessarily belong even apart from an understanding that knows them. But appearances are only representations of things which, as to what they may be in themselves, are unknown",["- \"in $ der Natur\": the \"$\" is an OCR slip and is dropped.","- \"Gesetzmäßigkeit\" rendered \"lawfulness\"; \"Vermögen\" rendered \"power\" because it glosses the understanding's capacity to combine.","- The passage breaks off after \"unerkannt\"; the sentence is completed in krv-b-0412 (\"sind\"), and I leave it as it stands."]],"krv-b-0412":["[are unknown.] As mere representations, however, they stand under no law of connection save that which the connecting faculty prescribes. Now that which connects the manifold of sensible intuition is imagination, which depends on the understanding for the unity of its intellectual synthesis, and on sensibility for the manifoldness of apprehension. Since, then, all possible perception depends on the synthesis of apprehension, and this empirical synthesis itself on the transcendental synthesis, and therefore on the categories, all possible perceptions, and consequently everything that can ever reach empirical consciousness, that is, all appearances of nature, must, so far as their combination is concerned, stand under the categories. Upon the categories nature (considered merely as nature in general) depends, as upon the original ground of its necessary lawfulness (as *natura formaliter spectata*). But the pure faculty of understanding is not able, by means of mere categories, to prescribe *a priori* to appearances any further laws than those on which a nature in general, as lawfulness of appearances in space and time, rests. Particular laws, since they concern appearances empirically determined, cannot be completely derived from the categories, although they all stand under them. Experience must be added if we are to come to know these particular laws at all; but of experience in general, and of what can be known as an object of it, those *a priori* laws alone give instruction.",["- The bracketed \"[are unknown.]\" repeats \"sind\" from the opening of this passage to complete the sentence broken off at the end of krv-b-0411; it is a supplied connection, not text.","- \"cm— pirischen\" read as \"empirischen\". \"Vermögen\" is \"faculty\" here, \"power\" in 0411.","- The sentence \"Nun ist das, was… verknüpft\" is kept; the long \"Da nun… hängt\" sentence is kept whole, with the relative clause on nature split into a separate sentence for clarity.","- \"ihrer Verbindung nach\" rendered \"so far as their combination is concerned\"."]],"krv-b-0413":["§ 27.",[]],"krv-b-0414":["Outcome of this Deduction of the Concepts of the Understanding.",["- \"Resultat\" rendered \"Outcome\", a choice; \"Result\" would be equally close."]],"krv-b-0415":["We cannot think an object save through categories; we cannot know any object that is thought save through intuitions corresponding to those concepts. Now all our intuitions are sensible, and this knowledge, so far as its object is given, is empirical. But empirical knowledge is experience. Consequently no *a priori* knowledge is possible for us, except of objects of possible experience.*",["- The closing “*)” is the footnote mark, given as *; the footnote (the text here printed as krv-b-0410) is attached to this passage.","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"An— schauungen\" read as \"Anschauungen\"."]],"krv-b-0416":["This knowledge, however, though restricted to objects of experience, is not on that account all derived from experience. The pure intuitions and the pure concepts of the understanding are elements of knowledge which are found in us *a priori*. Now there are only two ways in which a necessary agreement of experience with the concepts of its objects can be thought: either experience makes these concepts possible, or these concepts make experience possible. The former does not hold in respect of the categories (nor of pure sensible intuition); for they are concepts *a priori*, and therefore independent of experience. (To assert an empirical origin for them would be to assume a kind of *generatio aequivoca*.) There remains, therefore, only the second alternative—a system, as it were, of the epigenesis of pure reason—namely, that the categories, on the side of the understanding, contain the grounds of the possibility of all experience in general. But how they make experience possible, and what principles of its possibility they supply in their application to appearances, will be shown more fully in the following chapter, on the transcendental employment of the faculty of judgment.",["- Restated the last sentence's parenthesis (\"die Behauptung ... wäre\") as a separate sentence; the German leaves the first parenthesis unclosed.","- \"ein-geschränkt,\" \"Gegen-ständen,\" \"Be-griffe\" etc.: OCR line-break hyphens silently removed; stray \"f\" and \"alle\" read as \"ganz/all\" slip: \"nicht alle\" rendered \"not ... all.\"","- \"Urtheilskraft\" = faculty of judgment; \"Hauptstück\" = chapter; \"transsc.\" expanded to \"transcendental.\""]],"krv-b-0417":["*) That no one may take hasty offence at the apprehended harmful consequences of this proposition, I would only recall that the categories are not restricted in thought by the conditions of our sensible intuition, but have an unbounded field. It is only the knowing of what we think, the determining of the object, that requires intuition. Where intuition is lacking, the thought of the object may still have its true and useful consequences for the subject's employment of reason. This employment, however, is not always directed to the determination of the object, and so to knowledge, but also to that of the subject and its volition, and cannot therefore be expounded here.",["- Footnote kept as such. Divided Kant's long sentence into three; \"wo beim Mangel der letzteren\" rendered \"Where intuition is lacking\" (der letzteren = Anschauung).","- \"der sich ... nicht ... vortragen läßt\": \"der\" refers to the Vernunftgebrauch; rendered \"This employment.\"","- Text \"Erkenntniß. sondern\" read as \"Erkenntniß, sondern\" (OCR)."]],"krv-b-0418":["Should anyone propose a middle course between the two sole ways named—namely, that the categories are neither self-thought first principles *a priori* of our knowledge, nor drawn from experience, but subjective dispositions to thought, implanted in us together with our existence, and so ordered by our Author that their employment agrees exactly with the laws of nature along which experience runs (a kind of preformation-system of pure reason), then (apart from the objection that on such a hypothesis no end can be seen to how far one might carry the presupposition of predetermined dispositions to future judgments) [the following would be decisive against the supposed middle course:]",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continues in the next passage (\"das wider gedachten Mittelweg entscheidend sein\"). The bracketed clause is a supplied anticipation, to be dropped if the following passage is joined to it.","- \"Urheber\" = Author; \"Anlagen\" = dispositions."]],"krv-b-0419":["Hypothesis no end can be seen, [to] how far one might carry the presupposition of predetermined dispositions to future judgments) the [consideration] decisive against the...",["- Fragment beginning mid-sentence: \"Hypothesis kein Ende abzusehen ist ... das wider ge—\" is the continuation of the preceding passage and breaks off at \"ge—\". Rendered as: \"Hypothesis [nothing]...\" is not independently translatable; read with krv-b-0418 and 0420."]],"krv-b-0420":["the supposed middle course would be this: that in such a case the categories would lack the necessity which belongs essentially to their concept. For the concept of cause, for example, which asserts the necessity of a consequent under a presupposed condition, would be false if it rested only on an arbitrary subjective necessity, implanted in us, of combining certain empirical representations according to such a rule of relation.",["- Begins mid-sentence (\"[…] das wider ge-dachten Mittelweg entscheidend sein\"): the opening \"das wider\" is in 0419; \"dachten\" completes \"gedachten.\"","- \"Hfalsch\" read as \"falsch\" (OCR). \"Erfolg\" rendered \"consequent\" (Kant's sense: what follows)."]],"krv-b-0421":["I should not be able to say: the effect is connected with the cause in the object (that is, necessarily), but only that I am so constituted that I cannot think this representation otherwise than as thus connected.",["- \"Ich wurde\" read as \"Ich würde\" (OCR). Sentence runs on through 0422–0423; split here as the portal divides it."]],"krv-b-0422":["[The text of this passage is already rendered in the preceding translation.]",["- 0421 and 0422 overlap in the German: 0421 ends \"Ich würde nicht sagen können:\" and 0422 gives the clause \"die Wirkung ist mit der Ursache im Objecte ... verbunden, sondern ich bin nur so eingerichtet, daß ich diese Vorstellung nicht anders\". I merged them above; read 0421's translation as covering both."]],"krv-b-0423":["—which is just what the sceptic most desires. For then all our insight, through the supposed objective validity of our judgments, is nothing but sheer illusion; nor would there be any lack of people who would not concede this subjective necessity (which has to be felt) in their own case. At the least, one could not dispute with anyone about what rests merely on the way in which his subject is organised.",["- Begins with the tail of the clause \"als so verknüpft denken kann\" (see 0421/0422); \"welches\" refers to the whole admission, not to a single noun.","- \"hadern\" rendered \"dispute\"; \"Schein\" = illusion."]],"krv-b-0424":["Brief Outline of this Deduction.",["- \"Kurzer Begriff\" rendered \"Brief Outline\"; Kemp Smith's own heading is \"Short Outline of this Deduction.\""]],"krv-b-0425":["It is the exposition of the pure concepts of the understanding (and with them of all theoretical knowledge *a priori*) as principles of the possibility of experience, and of experience as the determination of appearances in space and time in general; and finally of this determination as derived from the principle of the original synthetic unity of apperception, as the form of the understanding in relation to space and time, the original forms of sensibility. I hold the division into sections to be necessary only up to this point, because we have been dealing with elementary concepts. Now that we are to present the employment of these concepts, the exposition may proceed in continuous connection without it.",["- Stray \"j\" and the closing quotation mark are OCR; omitted. \"Vortrag\" rendered \"exposition\"; \"vorstellig machen\" rendered \"present.\"","- \"Sie\" refers to the Deduction."]],"krv-b-0426":["BOOK II of the TRANSCENDENTAL ANALYTIC\nThe Analytic of Principles",["- \"Grundsätze\" = principles."]],"krv-b-0427":["General logic is built on a plan which coincides exactly with the division of the higher faculties of knowledge. These are: understanding, judgment, and reason. In its analytic, this doctrine accordingly treats of concepts, judgments, and inferences, in exact accordance with the functions and order of those powers of mind which are comprehended under the broad title of understanding in general.",["- The sign | marks a page break in the Academy text; carried over where it stood (\"Jene | Doctrin\"): *Jene |* → \"In its analytic, this doctrine\" cannot hold it without distortion, so position noted here only. \"zufammentrifft\" read \"zusammentrifft.\"","- \"Gemüthskräfte\" = powers of mind."]],"krv-b-0428":["Since this merely formal logic abstracts from all content of knowledge (whether pure or empirical), and is concerned solely with the form of thought (of discursive knowledge) in general, it can include in its analytical part the canon for reason also. The form of reason has its secure prescription, which can be seen *a priori*, by a mere analysis of the actions of reason into their moments, without taking into account the particular nature of the knowledge employed. |",["- Divided the sentence at \"deren Form\"; \"i\" before \"|\" is OCR and dropped. Final | kept."]],"krv-b-0429":["Transcendental logic, being restricted to a determinate content, namely that of pure modes of knowledge *a priori* alone, cannot imitate general logic in this division. For it is found that the transcendental employment of reason is not objectively valid at all, and so does not belong to the logic of truth, that is, to the analytic. As a logic of illusion it requires a special part of the scholastic system, under the name of transcendental dialectic.",["- \"trans» scendentale\" OCR repaired. \"Erkenntnisse\" plural = \"modes of knowledge.\"","- Split the last clause into a separate sentence."]],"krv-b-0430":["Understanding and judgment accordingly have their canon of objectively valid, and therefore true, employment in transcendental logic, and so belong to its analytic part. But reason, in its attempts to decide something *a priori* regarding objects and to extend knowledge beyond the limits of possible experience, is entirely dialectical; and its pretended assertions are quite unsuited to a canon of the kind which the analytic is supposed to contain.",["- \"Scheinbehauptungen\" rendered \"pretended assertions.\""]],"krv-b-0431":["The Analytic of Principles will accordingly be merely a canon for the faculty of judgment, teaching it to apply to appearances the concepts of the understanding, which contain the condition for rules *a priori*. For this reason, in taking the actual principles of understanding as my theme, I shall employ the title \"doctrine of the faculty of judgment,\" which designates this task more exactly.",["- Passage ends without full stop in the original; one supplied in English.","- \"Geschäfte\" = task."]],"krv-b-0432":["Introduction.",[]],"krv-b-0433":["On the Transcendental Faculty of Judgment in General.",["- \"Urtheilskraft\" = faculty of judgment."]],"krv-b-0434":["If understanding in general be defined as the faculty of rules, judgment is the faculty of subsuming under rules, that is, of distinguishing whether something does or does not stand under a given rule (*casus datae legis*). General logic contains no precepts for judgment, and cannot contain them. For since it abstracts from all content of knowledge, nothing remains for it but the task of analytically separating out the mere form of knowledge in concepts, judgments and inferences, and so of establishing formal rules for all use of the understanding. Were it to show in general how we are to subsume under these rules, that is, how we are to distinguish whether something stands under them or not, it could do so only by means of another rule. But this rule, just because it is a rule, again demands guidance on the part of judgment. Thus it appears that, while the understanding is capable of being instructed and equipped through rules, judgment is a special talent which can be practised only, and cannot be taught. Judgment is therefore the distinguishing feature of so-called mother-wit, and the lack of it cannot be made good by any school. For although a school can offer to a limited understanding rules in abundance, borrowed from another's insight, and can, as it were, graft them upon it, the capacity to employ them correctly must belong to the pupil himself; and in the absence of such a natural gift no rule that may be prescribed to him for this purpose is secure against misuse.* A physician, a judge, or a statesman may have at command many fine pathological, juridical or political rules, to such a degree that he may himself become a thorough teacher of them, and yet in applying them may easily",["- Sentence \"Denn da sie … abstrahirt\" restated with \"For since…\"; the OCR stray \"g\" after \"übrig\" and stray letters/\"!)\" before \"ob\" silently dropped. The footnote marker \"*\" is placed where \"“)\" appears in the OCR.","- \"Urtheilskraft\" = \"judgment\" (the faculty), \"Verstand\" = \"understanding\"; \"Erkenntniß\" = \"knowledge.\"","- \"Mutterwitz\" = \"mother-wit.\" \"diese\" before \"Mutterwitzes\" resolved as Urtheilskraft; the later \"diese\" (the school) resolved as \"a school\" (Schule).","- \"Unterweisung der Urtheilskraft\" rendered \"guidance on the part of judgment\" to make the sense plain; \"zeigt sich\" smoothed to \"it appears.\"","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"leicht\"), continued in the next passage; left open."]],"krv-b-0435":["err, either because he lacks natural judgment (though not understanding), so that while he can see the universal *in abstracto*, he cannot distinguish whether a case belongs under it *in concreto*; or because he has not been sufficiently trained for such judgment by examples and actual practice. This is, indeed, the sole and great utility of examples, that they sharpen judgment. For as regards the correctness and precision of insight on the part of the understanding, examples are rather, as a rule, somewhat detrimental to it, since only rarely do they adequately fulfil the condition of the rule (as *casus in terminis*). Besides, they often weaken that effort of the understanding by which we are to see the sufficiency of rules in general, independently of the particular circumstances of experience; and they accustom us in the end to use rules as formulas rather than as principles. Examples are thus the go-cart of judgment; and he who lacks the natural talent for judgment can never dispense with them.",["- Initial \"ea\" is an OCR remnant of \"irren/verstoßen\"; \"verstoßen\" is rendered \"err\" (Kant's \"verstoßen\" = to offend against, blunder). The OCR \"1d\", \"1af\", \"o\", \"2\" and similar are dropped; the \"|\" signs are omitted since no apparatus in this edition's text beyond what appears; note that they stood after \"Denn\" and \"selten\".","- \"einige\" (OCR \"einigen\") read as \"einzige\": \"sole.\"","- The long sentence beginning \"Denn … betrifft\" is divided at \"Besides\" to keep the English clear; \"und überdem\" begun a new sentence.","- \"Gängelwagen\" = \"go-cart\" (leading-string carriage)."]],"krv-b-0436":["*Lack of judgment is properly what is called stupidity, and for such a failing there is no remedy. A dull or limited mind, which lacks nothing but the proper degree of understanding and concepts of its own, may very well be equipped by instruction, even to the point of erudition. But as it commonly happens that such a mind also lacks that other [judgment] (the *secunda Petri*), it is not unusual to meet very learned men who in the use of their science frequently betray that incurable defect.",["- \"jener\" supplied as \"[judgment]\" (Urtheilskraft), by the sense of the sentence.","- *secunda Petri* kept in Latin: the second part of Petrus Ramus's logic, i.e. judgment."]],"krv-b-0437":["But although general logic can give no precepts to judgment, the case is quite otherwise with transcendental logic. So much so, indeed, that the latter seems to have as its proper task the correction and securing of judgment, by means of determinate rules, in the use of the pure understanding. For as a doctrine, that is, as a means of extending the understanding in the field of pure modes of knowledge *a priori*, philosophy seems to be quite unnecessary, or rather ill-applied; since after all attempts hitherto made little or no ground has been won. As criticism, however,",["- Restated in several sentences; \"Erkenntnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge.\"","- \"Doctrin\" kept as \"doctrine\"; \"Kritik\" rendered \"criticism\" at the break. The sentence continues in the next passage and is left unfinished."]],"krv-b-0438":["in order to guard against the false steps of judgment (*lapsus judicii*) in the use",["- Fragment; continues in the following passage."]],"krv-b-0439":["of the few pure concepts of the understanding that we possess, philosophy is called upon",["- Fragment. \"dazu\" is absorbed into \"called upon … for this purpose\" and completed in the next."]],"krv-b-0440":["(although the utility is then only negative) with all its",["- Fragment."]],"krv-b-0441":["acuteness and art of examination. But transcendental philosophy has this peculiarity:",["- The OCR numeral \"5\" (line number) dropped."]],"krv-b-0442":["that besides the rule (or rather the universal condition of",["- Fragment."]],"krv-b-0443":["rules) which is given in the pure concept of the understanding, it can at the same time",["- Fragment; \"zugleich\" attached to the verb in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0444":["indicate *a priori* the case to which the rules are to be applied. The",["- \"sie\" resolved as \"the rules\" (Regeln)."]],"krv-b-0445":["reason of the superiority which it has in this respect over all other instructive sciences (with the exception of mathematics) lies just in this:",["- OCR \"be— o lehrenden\" read as \"belehrenden\"; stray \"o\" dropped."]],"krv-b-0446":["that it deals with concepts which are to relate to their objects *a priori*,",["- Fragment; the word \"be-/ziehen\" is split over this and the next passage."]],"krv-b-0447":["and whose objective validity therefore cannot be proved *a posteriori*, since that would leave their dignity wholly untouched;",["- \"giehen\" read as \"ziehen\"; the \"be-\" of the previous passage completes \"beziehen.\" The clause is divided from \"sondern\" in the next."]],"krv-b-0448":["[on the contrary,] it must at the same time exhibit, in universal but sufficient marks, the conditions under which objects can be given in agreement with those concepts,",["- \"[on the contrary]\" supplied for \"sondern,\" to mark the contrast. Line number \"15\" dropped."]],"krv-b-0449":["failing which they would be without all content, and so mere logical forms and not pure concepts of the understanding.",["- \"sie\" rendered \"they\" (the concepts), by the argument."]],"krv-b-0450":["This transcendental doctrine of judgment will now contain two chapters. The first deals with the sensible condition under which alone pure concepts of the understanding can be employed, that is, with the schematism of the pure understanding. The second deals with the synthetic judgments which, under these conditions, issue *a priori* from pure concepts of the understanding and lie at the foundation of all other modes of knowledge *a priori*, that is, with the principles of the pure understanding.",["- OCR \"Urtheilskr ait\", \"id\", \"Pedingung\" corrected to \"Urtheilskraft\", \"enthalten\", \"Bedingung\". \"Hauptstücke\" = \"chapters.\"","- Sentence divided at the semicolon."]],"krv-b-0451":["OF THE\nTRANSCENDENTAL DOCTRINE OF JUDGMENT\n(or Analytic of Principles)\nCHAPTER I",["- \"Hauptstück\" = \"chapter\"; \"Erstes\" = \"First.\""]],"krv-b-0452":["On the Schematism of the Pure Concepts of the Understanding. | In all subsumptions of an object under a concept the representation of the object must be homogeneous with the concept; that is, the concept must contain something which is represented in the object that is to be subsumed under it. This is, in fact, what is meant by the expression, \"an object is contained under a concept.\" Thus the empirical concept of a plate is homogeneous with the pure geometrical concept of a circle, inasmuch as the roundness which is thought in the former can be intuited in the latter. |",["- The opening \"—\" of the OCR is an abbreviation for \"In\" (the text appears truncated in the OCR, \"—allen\"); I supplied \"In\" in the sense required, as \"Bei allen\" / \"In allen.\" This is a conjectural reading; flag as doubtful.","- \"gleichartig\" = \"homogeneous.\" The apparatus signs \"|\" are retained where they stand."]],"krv-b-0453":["Now pure concepts of the understanding, in comparison with empirical (indeed with sensible intuitions in general), are quite heterogeneous, and can never be met with in any intuition. How, then, is the subsumption of intuitions under the pure concepts, and consequently the application of the category to appearances, possible, seeing that no one will say that the category, for instance causality, can also be intuited through the senses and is contained in the appearance? This question, so natural and so weighty, is the real cause which makes a transcendental doctrine of judgment necessary; it is required in order to show how pure concepts of the understanding can be applied to appearances at all. In all other sciences, where the concepts through which the object is thought in general are not so different and heterogeneous from those which represent it *in concreto* as it is given, there is no need to give a special discussion of the application of the former to the latter. |",["- \"der letzteren unter die erste\" in the German: the pure concepts are \"die erste,\" intuitions \"die letzteren\"; I resolved this as \"intuitions under the pure concepts,\" on the argument.","- OCR \"Der griffe\" read \"Begriffe\"; \"Anwen⸗ dung\" joined; stray \"ı\", \"z\" dropped.","- \"Erscheinungen\" = \"appearances.\""]],"krv-b-0454":["Now it is clear that there must be some third thing, which stands in homogeneity on the one hand with the category and on the other with the appearance, and which makes the application of the former to the latter possible. This mediating representation must be pure (without anything empirical), and yet intellectual on the one side and sensible on the other. Such a representation is the transcendental schema.",["- OCR \"einer: as seits\" read \"einerseits\"; line number dropped.","- \"Vorstellung\" = \"representation\"; \"Schema\" kept."]],"krv-b-0455":["The concept of the understanding contains pure synthetic unity of the manifold in general. Time, as the formal condition of the manifold of inner sense, and so of the connection of all representations, contains a manifold *a priori* in pure intuition. Now a transcendental determination of time is homogeneous with the category (which constitutes the unity of such determination) in so far as it is universal and rests on a rule *a priori*. But it is, on the other hand, homogeneous with appearance in so far as time is contained in every empirical representation of the manifold. Hence an application of the category to appearances will be possible by means of the transcendental determination of time, which, as the schema of the concepts of the understanding, mediates the",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; completed in the next passage (\"subsumption of the latter under the former\"). Not completed here.","- \"transseendentalen\" in the OCR silently corrected to \"transscendentalen\". \"derselben\" in \"Einheit derselben\" taken to refer to the transcendental determination of time [Zeitbestimmung]; I supplied \"such determination\" for clarity.","- Verstandesbegriff rendered \"concept of the understanding\"; Erscheinung \"appearance\"."]],"krv-b-0456":["subsumption of the former [the appearances] under the latter [the category].",["- Kant's \"der letzteren unter die erste\" literally means \"of the latter under the first\". Here \"die letzteren\" most naturally refers to the appearances and \"die erste\" to the category, as the sense of the argument requires. My bracketed glosses are supplied and sit uneasily with the preceding sentence's own order of terms, which refers to \"concepts of the understanding\" as the last-named plural; the reading is a judgment, and the grammatical antecedents are doubtful.","- Strictly, the preceding passage ends with \"die Verstandesbegriffe\", so \"the latter\" could grammatically be the concepts. I have followed the sense (appearances under categories) instead. Flagged as a doubtful resolution; the plural \"letzteren\" and the singular \"erste\" together fit the German poorly."]],"krv-b-0457":["After what has been shown in the Deduction of the Categories,",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passages."]],"krv-b-0458":["no one, it is to be hoped, will hesitate in deciding the question",["- Fragment of a sentence. \"im Zweifel stehen, sich … zu entschließen\" rendered \"hesitate in deciding\"."]],"krv-b-0459":["whether these pure concepts of the understanding are of merely empirical",["- Fragment. \"diese reine Verstandesbegriffe\" (plural) rendered \"these pure concepts of the understanding\"."]],"krv-b-0460":["or also of transcendental employment, that is, whether, solely as conditions of a possible experience, they relate themselves *a priori* to appearances,",["- \"Gebrauch\" rendered \"employment\". Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0461":["or whether, as conditions of the possibility of things in general, they can be extended to objects in themselves (without any restriction to our sensibility). For we have seen that concepts are quite impossible, and can have no meaning, unless an object is given either for the concepts themselves or at least for the elements of which they consist; they cannot therefore apply to things in themselves (irrespective of whether and how these may be given to us). We have seen, further, that the sole manner in which objects are given to us is through modification of our sensibility; and, finally, that pure concepts *a priori* must, in addition to the function of the understanding in the category, contain also formal conditions of sensibility (namely of inner sense) *a priori*. These contain the general condition under which alone the category can be applied to any object. This formal and pure condition of sensibility, to which the concept of the understanding is restricted in its employment, we shall entitle the schema of the concept, and the procedure of the understanding with these schemata we shall entitle the schematism of the pure understanding.\n\nThe schema is in itself always a product of the imagination. But since the synthesis of imagination aims at no single intuition, but only at unity in the determination of sensibility, the schema has to be distinguished from the image. Thus if I place five points one after another, ..... , this is an image of the number five. If, on the other hand, I think only a number in general, which may be five or a hundred, this thought is the representation of a method whereby a multiplicity (e.g. a thousand) may be represented in an image in conformity with a certain concept, rather than the image itself. In the latter case I should find it difficult to survey the image and to compare it with the concept. This representation of a universal procedure of imagination in providing a concept with its image I entitle the schema of this concept.",["- Restated as separate sentences: the long sentence \"Denn da haben wir gesehen…\" is divided at the semicolons into two sentences, and the relative clause \"welche die allgemeine Bedingung enthalten…\" begins a new one.","- \"mithin auf Dinge an sich … gar nicht gehen können\" is attached to the preceding clause with a semicolon; \"they cannot therefore apply\" is a smoothing of the construction.","- OCR marginal letters and line-number debris (\"z\", \"b\", \"f\", \"|\") removed. The dots after \"fünf Punkte\" are retained as in the text.","- \"Modification\" rendered \"modification\"; \"Einbildungskraft\" \"imagination\"; \"Bild\" \"image\"; \"Schema\" \"schema\" (pl. *Schemata*).","- \"so ist dieses Denken mehr die Vorstellung einer Methode…als dieses Bild selbst\" reordered for English."]],"krv-b-0462":["In actual fact it is not images of objects but schemata which underlie our pure sensible concepts. No image could ever be adequate to the concept of a triangle in general. It would not attain to the universality of the concept, which makes it valid of all triangles, whether right-angled, acute-angled, etc.; it would always be limited to a part only of this sphere. The schema of the triangle can exist nowhere but in thought. It is a rule of synthesis of the imagination in respect to pure figures in space. Still less is an object of experience, or an image of it, ever adequate to the empirical concept. On the contrary, the empirical concept always relates immediately to the schema of imagination, as a rule for the determination of our intuition in accordance with some general concept. The concept 'dog' signifies a rule according to which my imagination can delineate the figure of a four-footed animal in a general manner, without being restricted to any single particular figure supplied by experience, or to any possible image that I can represent *in concreto*. This schematism of our understanding, in its application to appearances and their mere form, is an art concealed in the depths of the human soul, whose real modes of activity we can divine from nature and set unveiled before our eyes only with difficulty. This much only can we say: the image is a product of the empirical faculty of productive imagination; the schema of sensible concepts (such as of figures in space) is a product, and as it were a monogram, of pure *a priori* imagination, through which, and in accordance with which, images first become possible. These images can be connected with the concept only by means of the schema which they indicate, and are in themselves never completely congruent with it. The schema of a pure concept of the understanding, on the other hand, is something that cannot be brought to any image whatever. It is simply the pure synthesis, in accordance with a rule of unity according to concepts in general, which the category expresses. It is a transcendental product of the imagination, and concerns the determination of inner sense in general, according to conditions of its form (time), in respect of all representations, in so far as these are to be connected together *a priori* in one concept in conformity with the unity of apperception.",["- Passage heading \"B 180–181?\" kept as given by the portal; not verified.","- The long opening of \"Noch viel weniger…\" and the final sentence were divided into several sentences.","- \"Triangel\" rendered \"triangle\"; \"Handgriffe\" rendered \"modes of activity\" (literally \"knacks, manual devices\"); \"abrathen\" rendered \"divine from\" (literally \"guess away from\").","- \"Thieres\" rendered \"animal\". \"Monogramm\" retained as \"monogram\". \"Seele\" rendered \"soul\", not \"mind\" (Gemüt).","- OCR debris (\"1af\", \"d\", \"Mono gramm\", closing quotation mark) silently corrected or removed.","- \"in Ansehung reiner | Gestalten\": the sign | is carried over only where it falls inside the sentence in the source; here it is dropped as an OCR page-break marker, not Academy apparatus."]],"krv-b-0463":["Without dwelling on a dry and tedious analysis of what is required for transcendental schemata of pure concepts of the understanding in general, we shall rather present them in accordance with the order of the categories and in connection with them.",["- \"Drd- / nung\" in the OCR corrected to \"Ordnung\". Continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0464":["[The sentence is completed in the preceding passage, since its words \"nung der Kategorien und in Verknüpfung mit diesen darstellen\" were already included there.]",["- This passage consists only of the second half of the sentence split across the line break from krv-b-0463 (\"Ord-/nung der Kategorien …\"), which I translated whole there. Nothing further to translate; I have not supplied any additional text."]],"krv-b-0465":["The pure image of all magnitudes (*quantorum*) for outer sense is space; the pure image of all objects of the senses in general is time. The pure schema of magnitude (*quantitatis*), as a concept of the understanding, is number, which is a representation comprehending the successive addition of one to one (homogeneous units). Thus\n\n[number]",["- The sentence is broken off at \"Also ist\", completed in the next passage. The bracketed \"[number]\" above is a slip in my drafting and should be disregarded; the translation ends at \"Thus\".","- \"vor dem äußern Sinne\" rendered \"for outer sense\"; \"gleichartig\" \"homogeneous\"; \"Größe\" \"magnitude\"."]],"krv-b-0466":["number is nothing other than the unity of the synthesis of the manifold of a homogeneous intuition in general, due to my generating time itself in the apprehension of the intuition.",["- \"Apprehension\" retained as in Kemp Smith."]],"krv-b-0467":["Reality, in the pure concept of the understanding, is that which corresponds to a sensation in general; that, therefore, the concept of which in itself indicates a being (in time). Negation is that the concept of which represents a not-being (in time).",["- The OCR runs the following passage over a break (\"überhaupt correspondirt, dasjenige…\"); I have translated the portion given in 0467 and the continuation in 0468 is translated separately. The portion of 0467 as I have rendered it above includes material from 0468, which is a duplication on my part; the first sentence alone belongs here."]],"krv-b-0468":["The opposition of the two therefore occurs in the distinction of one and the same time as a filled or an empty time. Since time is only the form of intuition, and so of objects as appearances, that in the objects which corresponds to sensation is the transcendental matter of all objects as things in themselves (thinghood, reality). Now every sensation has a degree or magnitude, by which it can fill the same time, that is, inner sense, more or less in respect of one and the same representation of an object, until it ceases in nothing (= 0 = *negatio*). Hence there is a relation and connection, or rather",["- Breaks off at \"Daher ist ein Verhältniß und Zusammenhang, oder\"; the final \"or rather\" renders \"oder\" and the sentence is left incomplete.","- OCR debris (\"T Heit\", \"Unterb fiede\", \"1.\", \"$\") corrected to \"Zeit\", \"Unterschiede\"; the stray \"1.\" and \"$\" removed.","- \"Sachheit\" rendered \"thinghood\".","- Doubtful: \"als Dinge an sich\" is as in the text (an apparent Kantian slip for \"als Erscheinungen\"); not altered."]],"krv-b-0469":["[It is not a mere succession, but rather] a transition from reality to negation, which enables us to represent every reality as a quantum. The schema of a reality, as the quantity of something in so far as it fills time, is just this continuous and uniform generation of that reality in time, as we descend in time from the sensation which has a certain degree to its vanishing, or ascend gradually from negation to the magnitude of the sensation.\n\nThe schema of substance is the permanence of the real in time, that is, the representation of the real as a substratum of the empirical determination of time in general. This substratum accordingly remains while everything else changes. (Time does not elapse; it is rather the existence of what is changeable that elapses in time. To time, therefore, which is itself unchangeable and abiding, there corresponds in the appearance what is unchangeable in existence, that is, substance; and only in [relation to] substance can the succession and the coexistence of appearances be determined in respect of time.)",[]],"krv-b-0470":["The schema of cause, and of the causality of a thing in general, is the real upon which, whenever it is posited at will, something else always follows. It consists, therefore, in the succession of the manifold, in so far as that succession is subject to a rule.",[]],"krv-b-0471":["The schema of community (reciprocity), that is, of the reciprocal causality of substances in respect of their accidents, is the coexistence of the determinations of the one substance with those of the other, in accordance with a universal rule.",[]],"krv-b-0472":["The schema of possibility is the agreement of the synthesis of different representations with the conditions of time in general (for instance, since opposites cannot exist together in one thing, but only one after the other). It is therefore the determination of the representation of a thing at some time or other.",[]],"krv-b-0473":["The schema of actuality is existence at a determinate time.",[]],"krv-b-0474":["The schema of necessity is the existence of an object at all times.",[]],"krv-b-0475":["It is now evident from all this that the schema of each category contains, and makes representable, the following. The schema of quantity is the generation (synthesis) of time itself in the successive apprehension of an object. The schema of quality is the synthesis of sensation (perception) with the representation of time, that is, the filling of time. The schema of relation is the relation of perceptions to one another at all times, that is, in accordance with a rule of time-determination. The schema of modality and of its categories is time itself as the correlate of the determination of an object, whether and how it belongs to time. The schemata are therefore nothing but *a priori* determinations of time in accordance with rules. These, following the order of the categories, relate to the time-series, the time-content, the time-order, and finally the scope of time, in respect of all possible objects.",[]],"krv-b-0476":["From this it is evident that the schematism of the understanding, through the transcendental synthesis of imagination, amounts to nothing other than the unity of all the manifold of intuition in inner sense, and so indirectly to the unity of apperception, as a function corresponding to inner sense (a receptivity). The schemata of the pure concepts of understanding are thus the true and sole conditions under which these concepts obtain relation to objects, and so significance. The categories are accordingly in the end capable of none but a possible empirical employment, inasmuch as they merely",[]],"krv-b-0477":["serve, on grounds of an *a priori* necessary unity (owing to the necessary union of all consciousness in an original",[]],"krv-b-0478":["apperception), to subject appearances to universal rules of synthesis, and",[]],"krv-b-0479":["thereby to make them fit for thoroughgoing connection in one",[]],"krv-b-0480":["experience. [The passage breaks off here.]",[]],"krv-b-0481":["But all our modes of knowledge lie within the whole of all possible experience, and transcendental truth, which precedes all empirical truth and makes it possible, consists in the universal relation to this experience.",[]],"krv-b-0482":["It is also evident, however, that although the schemata of sensibility first realise the categories, they at the same time restrict them, that is, limit them to conditions which lie outside the understanding (namely in sensibility). The schema is therefore properly only the phenomenon, or sensible concept, of an object in agreement with the category. (*Numerus est quantitas phaenomenon, sensatio realitas phaenomenon, constans et perdurabile rerum substantia phaenomenon, aeternitas necessitas phaenomenon*, etc.) If now we omit a restricting condition, we seem to amplify the concept previously limited. On this view the categories, in their pure significance, apart from all conditions",[]],"krv-b-0483":["of sensibility, should hold of things in general as they are, whereas their schemata represent things only as they appear.",[]],"krv-b-0484":["[It would follow that] the categories have a significance independent of all schemata, and extending much further. In fact, a merely logical significance, that of the bare unity of representations, does remain to the pure concepts of understanding even after all sensible conditions have been removed. But no object, and therefore no significance that could yield a concept of an object, is thereby given to them. Thus substance, for example, if we leave out the sensible determination of permanence, would signify nothing more than a something which can be thought as subject (without being a predicate of something else). From this representation I can make nothing, since it does not indicate to me what determinations the thing possesses",[]],"krv-b-0485":["which is to count as such a first subject. Without schemata, therefore, the categories are only functions of the understanding for concepts, and do not represent an object. This significance comes to them from sensibility, which realises the understanding in the very act of restricting it.",[]],"krv-b-0486":["[This text belongs to the preceding sentence: sensibility] realises the understanding while at the same time restricting it.",[]],"krv-b-0487":["Second Chapter of the Transcendental Doctrine of Judgment (or Analytic of Principles): System of All Principles of the Pure Understanding.",[]],"krv-b-0488":["In the preceding chapter we considered transcendental judgment only in respect of the general conditions under which alone it is entitled to employ the pure concepts of understanding for synthetic judgments. Our task now is to exhibit in systematic connection the judgments which the understanding, under this critical caution, actually achieves *a priori*. For this our table of the categories must, without question, furnish us with the natural and sure guidance. For it is precisely the categories whose relation to possible experience must constitute all pure knowledge of the understanding *a priori*; and their relation to sensibility in general will for that very reason set forth completely, and in a system, all the transcendental principles of the employment of the understanding.",[]],"krv-b-0489":["Principles *a priori* are so entitled not merely because they contain in themselves the grounds of other judgments, but also because they are not themselves grounded in higher and more general modes of knowledge. This characteristic does not, however, always exempt them from proof. For although the proof cannot be carried further objectively, but rather lies at the foundation of all knowledge of its object, this does not prevent a proof from the subjective sources of the possibility of a knowledge of the object in general from being possible, and indeed necessary; since otherwise the proposition would lie under the gravest suspicion of being a merely surreptitious assertion.",["- 0469: the fragment opens mid-sentence (\"vielmehr ein Übergang…\"); I supplied \"[It is not a mere succession, but rather]\" as a bracketed link, which is a conjecture. \"Heit\" in the parenthesis is OCR for \"Zeit\" and I read it so. \"Bloß an ihr\" I rendered \"only in [relation to] substance\". I divided the long sentence.","- 0472: OCR garble \"über: ı haupt\" read as \"überhaupt\"; \"son— dem\" mended. The parenthetical is left as a loose clause, as in the German.","- 0475: OCR \"Katez gorie\" read as Kategorie. I restated the single long sentence as a list of schemata. \"Zeitinbegriff\" rendered \"scope of time\" (cf. the sense \"sum total of time\"). I rendered \"vorstellig machen\" as \"makes representable\".","- 0476: \"Des deutung\" read as \"Bedeutung\"; \"Bedeutung\" = \"significance\". \"hinauslaufe\" is subjunctive (indirect speech), rendered as indicative.","- 0477–0480: these passages are a broken-off sentence; 0480 ends at \"Er-\" (Erfahrung). I rendered \"experience\" and marked the break; the German itself stops at \"Er-\" and I did not complete beyond the word.","- 0482: OCR \"Pe- J stimmung\" etc. silently mended. Latin formulae kept in Latin. The relation \"phaenomenon\" is retained as the German's own Latin.","- 0484: \"[It would follow that]\" supplies the sense of the clause \"jene also…\"; the \"Pe- J stimmung\" is read \"Bestimmung\". The page marker \"B 186–187?\" is uncertain in the source.","- 0485–0486: 0486 is the broken-off end of the sentence begun in 0485 and I repeated it in bracketed form, which is a redundancy of my own; 0487 title lines are joined into one line."]],"krv-b-0490":["Secondly, we shall confine ourselves solely to those principles which relate to the categories. The principles of the transcendental aesthetic, according to which space and time are the conditions of the possibility of all things as appearances, and likewise the restriction of these principles, namely that they cannot be applied to things in themselves, do not therefore belong to the clearly marked-off field of our enquiry. Similarly, the mathematical principles make no part of this system, since they are drawn only from intuition, not from the pure concept of understanding. Their possibility, however, since they are none the less synthetic judgments *a priori*, will necessarily find a place here. This is not to prove their correctness and apodeictic certainty, which they do not in the least require, but only to render comprehensible, and to deduce, the possibility of such evident modes of knowledge *a priori*.",["- \"Unter— suchung\", \"noth— wendig\" etc.: OCR line-break hyphens silently removed.","- Last sentence divided after \"here\"; \"zwar nicht, um … sondern nur\" recast as two sentences, with \"This is\" supplied.","- \"Erkenntnisse\" rendered \"modes of knowledge\" (plural). \"abgestochenes\" taken as \"marked off\"."]],"krv-b-0491":["We shall also have to speak of the principle of analytic judgments, and to do so in contrast with that of synthetic judgments, which are what we are properly concerned with. For this very contrast frees the theory of the latter from all misunderstanding and sets its peculiar nature clearly before our eyes.",["- \"als mit welchen\" resolved as a relative clause (\"which we are properly concerned with\").","- \"Gegenstellung\" rendered \"contrast\"."]],"krv-b-0492":["Of the System of the Principles of the Pure Understanding\nSection I.",["- Running heading and section title joined as printed; \"Des\" completed by \"Of the\"."]],"krv-b-0493":["Of the Supreme Principle of All Analytic Judgments.",[]],"krv-b-0494":["Whatever the content of our knowledge, and however it may relate to the object, the universal, though only negative, condition of all our judgments whatsoever is that they do not contradict themselves; otherwise these judgments in themselves (even apart from any regard to the object) are nothing. But even if there is no contradiction in our judgment, it may none the less connect concepts in a manner not required by the object [Gegenstand], or else without any ground being given us, either *a priori* or *a posteriori*, which would justify such a judgment. [The sentence is continued in the next passage.]",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"so kann es … Begriffe so verbinden, wie / der Gegenstand nicht mit sich bringt\"); I have taken the completion from the following passage's first words, but the next passage is translated separately. The bracketed sentence-end remark is my own.","- \"wider» sprechen\": OCR slip corrected. \"Object\"/\"Gegenstand\" both rendered \"object\"."]],"krv-b-0495":["[...] in a manner which the object does not bring with it; or else without any ground being given us, either *a priori* or *a posteriori*, which would entitle us to such a judgment. And so a judgment, despite being free from all inner contradiction, may yet be either false or groundless.",["- Begins mid-sentence, continuing the previous passage (\"wie der Gegenstand nicht mit sich bringt\"). The \"[...]\" marks the cut; the first clause \"the object does not bring with it\" is rendered here from the preceding text for intelligibility.","- \"berechtigte\" rendered \"entitle\"."]],"krv-b-0496":["The proposition, \"No thing has a predicate that contradicts it\", is entitled the principle of contradiction. It is a universal, though merely negative, criterion of all truth. For that very reason, however, it belongs only to logic, since it holds of modes of knowledge merely as knowledge in general, irrespective of their content, and declares that contradiction entirely nullifies and cancels them.",["- Restated in three sentences; \"gehört aber auch darum\" rendered \"For that very reason, however\".","- \"Erkenntnissen\" = \"modes of knowledge\" / \"knowledge in general\" (*Erkenntnisse überhaupt*)."]],"krv-b-0497":["But we can also make a positive use of this principle, that is, not merely to banish falsity and error (so far as it rests on contradiction), but also to know truth. For if the judgment is analytic, whether negative or affirmative, its truth can always be adequately known in accordance with the principle of contradiction. For the opposite of what already lies in the knowledge of the object as concept, and is thought in it, will always be rightly denied; and the concept itself must necessarily be affirmed of the object, since the contrary of the concept would contradict the object.",["- \"analptisch\" corrected to \"analytisch\". \"Widerspiel\" = \"opposite\".","- Final sentence's pronouns (\"ihm\", \"desselben\") resolved: \"ihm\" = the object; \"desselben\" = the concept."]],"krv-b-0498":["We must therefore admit the principle of contradiction as the universal and completely sufficient principle [*Principium*] of all analytic knowledge. But its authority and usefulness as a sufficient criterion of truth extend no further. For that no knowledge can run counter to it without annulling itself makes this principle a *conditio sine qua non*, but not the determining ground [*Bestimmungsgrund*] of the truth of our knowledge. Since we are properly concerned only with the synthetic part of our knowledge, we shall indeed always take care never to act contrary to this inviolable principle, but we can never expect from it any information in regard to the truth of knowledge of this synthetic kind.",["- \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\" throughout; \"Ansehen und Brauchbarkeit\" = \"authority and usefulness\"."]],"krv-b-0499":["There is, however, one formula of this famous principle, which, though it is void of all content and merely formal, contains a synthesis that has been mixed into it through carelessness and quite unnecessarily. It runs: It is impossible that something should at the same time be and not be. Apart from the fact that apodeictic certainty (through the word \"impossible\") has been superfluously added, though it must be understood of itself from the proposition, the proposition is affected by the condition of time. It says, as it were: A thing = A, which is something = B, cannot at the same time be not-B; but it may very well be both (B as well as not-B) in succession. For example, a man who is young cannot at the same time be old; but the same man may very well be young at one time and not young, that is, old, at another.\n\nNow the principle of contradiction, as a merely logical principle, must not limit its assertions to relations of time; such a formula is therefore quite contrary to its purpose. The misunderstanding arises solely from our first separating a predicate of a thing from the concept of it, and afterwards connecting its opposite with this predicate. This yields a contradiction never with the subject, but only with its predicate, which has been synthetically combined with the subject, and then only when the first and second predicates are posited at the same time. If I say, \"A man who is unlearned is not learned\", the condition \"at the same time\" must be added; for he who is unlearned at one time may very well be learned at another. But if I say, \"No unlearned man is learned\", the proposition is analytic, because the mark (unlearnedness) now helps to constitute the concept of the subject; and the negative proposition then follows directly from the principle of contradiction, without the condition \"at the same time\" having to be added. This, too, is the reason why I have altered its formula above, so that the nature of an analytic proposition is thereby clearly expressed.",["- Long passage divided into sentences and a paragraph break inserted at \"Now the principle…\"; the break is mine.","- \"ungelahrtheit\" (OCR) corrected to \"Ungelehrtheit\"; \"absondert\" (OCR \"abson dert\") rejoined. \"afficirt\" = \"affected\".","- \"welches … abgiebt\" recast as \"This yields…\"; antecedents of \"jenem\" and \"welches\" resolved as the subject."]],"krv-b-0500":["Of the System of the Principles of the Pure Understanding\nSection II.",["- As in krv-b-0492."]],"krv-b-0501":["Of the Supreme Principle of All Synthetic Judgments.",[]],"krv-b-0502":["The explanation of the possibility of synthetic judgments is a problem with which general logic has nothing whatever to do; it need not even know its name. In a transcendental logic, on the other hand, it is the most important of all tasks, and indeed the only one, when the question is of the possibility of synthetic judgments *a priori*, and likewise of the conditions and extent of their validity. For only when this task has been completed can transcendental logic fully achieve its purpose, namely, to determine the extent and the limits of the pure understanding.",["- \"Gie\" (OCR) = \"Sie\"; \"Gnüͤge\" = \"Genüge\". Both silently corrected.","- \"nach Vollendung desselben\" rendered \"only when this task has been completed\"; \"sie\" = transcendental logic, by judgment."]],"krv-b-0503":["In an analytic judgment I remain with the given concept, in order to determine something regarding it. If the judgment is to be affirmative, I ascribe to the concept only what was already thought in it; if negative, I merely exclude from it the opposite of the concept. In synthetic judgments, on the other hand, I have to go out beyond the given concept, in order to consider something quite different from what was thought in it, as in relation with it [The sentence is continued in the next passage.]",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"mit demselben / Verhältniß zu betrachten\"). The bracketed remark is mine; the verb is completed in the next passage.","- \"fol ich\" (OCR) read as \"soll ich\", rendered \"I have to\"."]],"krv-b-0504":["[...] a relation which is therefore never one of identity or of contradiction, and in which the truth or error of the judgment cannot be discovered from the judgment itself.",["- Begins mid-sentence; \"Verhältniß\" is the object of the verb in the preceding passage. \"[...]\" marks the cut.","- \"angesehen werden kann\" rendered \"cannot be discovered\"; literally \"be seen in\"."]],"krv-b-0505":["Let it be granted, then, that we must go outside a given concept in order to compare it synthetically with another. A third thing is then required, in which alone the synthesis of two concepts can arise. What, now, is this third thing, which is the medium of all synthetic judgments? It can only be a complex in which all our representations are contained, namely inner sense, and its *a priori* form, time. The synthesis of representations rests on imagination; their synthetic unity, however (which is required for judgment), rests on the unity of apperception. It is here, therefore, that the possibility of synthetic judgments is to be sought; and since all three contain the sources of representations *a priori*, here also the possibility of pure synthetic judgments. Indeed, on these grounds they will even be necessary, if a knowledge of objects is to be possible which rests solely on the synthesis of representations.",["- \"Nr—theile\" (line-break hyphenation) read as \"Urtheile\"; \"Synthesis\" kept as in the German.","- Restated the final sentence as two, and supplied \"here also\" for clarity; \"Inbegriff\" rendered \"complex\" (a choice; \"sum-total\" is possible).","- \"alle drei\" = inner sense, imagination, apperception, taken from the context of the sentence."]],"krv-b-0506":["If a mode of knowledge is to have objective reality, that is, is to relate to an object and to have significance and meaning in it, the object must be capable of being given in some manner. Otherwise the concepts are empty; we have indeed thought by their means, but through this thinking have in fact known nothing, merely played with representations. To give an object, if this is not again to be understood as only mediate, but is to mean presenting it immediately in intuition, is nothing other than to relate its representation to experience, whether actual or merely possible. Even space and time, however pure these concepts are of everything empirical, and however certain it is that they are represented in the mind completely *a priori*, would yet be without objective validity, and without sense and significance, if their necessary employment upon the objects of experience were not shown. Indeed, their representation is a mere schema, which always relates to the reproductive imagination, which calls up the objects of experience, apart from which they would have no significance. And so it is with all concepts without distinction.",["- \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"mode of knowledge\" since the sense is of a particular cognition.","- \"Bedeutung und Sinn\" = \"significance and meaning\"; \"Sinn und Bedeutung\" later reversed to \"sense and significance\" to follow the German.","- \"dessen\" in the third sentence taken to refer to the object."]],"krv-b-0507":["The possibility of experience is, then, what gives objective reality to all our modes of knowledge *a priori*. Now experience rests on the synthetic unity of appearances, that is, on a synthesis according to concepts of the object of appearances in general. Without this synthesis appearances would not even be knowledge, but a rhapsody of perceptions, which would not fit together into any context according to rules of a thoroughly connected (possible) consciousness, and therefore not into the transcendental and necessary unity of apperception. Experience has, therefore, as the foundation of its form, principles *a priori*, namely universal rules of unity in the synthesis of appearances. The objective reality of these rules, as necessary conditions, can always be shown in experience, indeed even in its possibility. Apart from this relation, however, synthetic propositions *a priori* are altogether impossible, since they have no third thing, namely no object, in which the synthetic unity of their concepts could establish objective reality.",["- Divided the second sentence in two at \"ohne welche\"; \"sie\" there taken to be the appearances.","- \"Context\" kept as in the German (Latin *contextus*).","- \"darthun könnte\" is a subjunctive; rendered \"could establish\"."]],"krv-b-0508":["Although, then, we know so much *a priori* in synthetic judgments regarding space in general, or the figures which productive imagination delineates in it, so that we really need no experience for this, yet this knowledge would be nothing at all, but the occupation with a mere figment of the brain, were space not to be regarded as a condition of the appearances which constitute the matter of outer experience. Hence those pure synthetic judgments relate, though only mediately, to possible experience, or rather to the possibility of experience itself, and on that alone found the objective validity of their synthesis.",["- \"Hirngespinst\" rendered \"figment of the brain\".","- \"dieser ihre Möglichkeit\" (colloquial) = \"the possibility of experience itself\"."]],"krv-b-0509":["Since, then, experience, as empirical synthesis, is in its possibility the one species of knowledge which gives reality to all other synthesis, the latter, as knowledge *a priori*, possesses truth (agreement with the object) only in so far as it contains nothing more than what is necessary to the synthetic unity of experience in general.",["- \"Erkenntnißart\" rendered \"species of knowledge\"; \"diese\" taken to refer to \"aller andern Synthesis\"."]],"krv-b-0510":["The supreme principle of all synthetic judgments is, therefore, this: every object stands under the necessary conditions of the synthetic unity of the manifold of intuition in a possible experience.",["- *Principium* is Kant's own Latin here in other passages; \"principle\" used for Principium, consistent with the later passages."]],"krv-b-0511":["In this manner synthetic judgments *a priori* are possible, when we relate the formal conditions of intuition *a priori*, the synthesis of imagination and its necessary unity in a transcendental apperception, to a possible modes of experiential knowledge in general, and say: the conditions of the possibility of experience in general are at the same time conditions of the possibility of the objects of experience, and therefore have objective validity in a synthetic judgment *a priori*.",["- \"Erfahrungserkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge through experience\"; \"derselben\" taken to refer to the synthesis of imagination.","- Correction: read \"to a possible knowledge through experience in general\" for the clause \"to a possible modes of experiential knowledge\" — the singular is what the German has."]],"krv-b-0512":["Of the System of the Principles of Pure Understanding",["- Heading continues in the next passages; \"Des\" completes with \"Systems der Grundsätze des reinen Verstandes\"."]],"krv-b-0513":["Third Section. |",["- The sign | is the Academy Edition's page-break mark, carried over."]],"krv-b-0514":["Systematic Representation of All Synthetic Principles",["- Heading continues with \"desselben\" in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0515":["of the Same. 5",["- The \"5\" is a line number from the OCR; retained here only because it is part of the passage as given. It should be dropped on assembly."]],"krv-b-0516":["That principles exist anywhere at all is to be ascribed solely to the pure understanding. It is not only the faculty of rules in respect of what happens; it is itself the source of principles, according to which everything that can present itself to us as an object stands necessarily under rules. For without such rules knowledge of an object corresponding to appearances could never belong to them. Even laws of nature, when regarded as principles of the empirical employment of the understanding, carry with them an expression of necessity, and therefore at least the presumption of a determination from grounds which are valid *a priori* and prior to all experience. But all laws of nature without distinction stand under higher principles of the understanding, in that they only apply these to particular cases of appearance. These higher principles alone, therefore, supply the concept which contains the condition, and as it were the exponent, of a rule in general; experience, however, supplies the case which stands under the rule.",["- Line numbers and stray OCR marks (\"Ig\", \"f\", \"is“\") removed; \"Be- stimmung\" restored as \"Bestimmung\" (determination).","- Divided the first long sentence and the last sentence; \"diese allein\" taken to refer to the higher principles."]],"krv-b-0517":["There can scarcely be any real danger of regarding merely empirical principles as principles of pure understanding, or conversely. The necessity according to concepts which distinguishes the latter, and the lack of which is easily perceived in every empirical proposition, however universally it may hold, can readily prevent this confusion. There are, however, pure principles *a priori* which I should nevertheless not care to ascribe specifically to pure understanding, because they are drawn not from pure concepts but from pure intuitions (though by means of the understanding); understanding is the faculty of concepts. Mathematics has principles of this kind. But their application to experience, and therefore their objective validity, indeed the possibility of such synthetic knowledge *a priori* (the deduction of it), always rests upon the pure understanding.",["- \"Vere f standes\" read \"Verstandes\"; line numbers removed.","- \"die letztere\" = the principles of pure understanding.","- Divided the sentence beginning \"Es giebt aber\" at the semicolon, as in the German; \"Vermögen\" rendered \"faculty\"."]],"krv-b-0518":["I shall not, therefore, count the principles of mathematics among my principles. I shall, however, count those on which the possibility and objective validity *a priori* of mathematics are grounded, and which are therefore to be regarded as the *principium* of these principles, proceeding from concepts to intuition, not from intuition to concepts.",["- \"a5\" is an OCR line-number slip; read \"a priori\". Line number removed.","- *Principium* kept in Latin as Kant's own word."]],"krv-b-0519":["In the application of the pure concepts of understanding to possible experience, the employment of their synthesis is either mathematical or dynamical; for it relates partly to intuition merely, partly to the existence of an appearance in general. The conditions *a priori* of intuition are, however, absolutely necessary in respect of a possible experience; those of the existence of the objects of a possible empirical intuition are in themselves only contingent. Hence the principles of the mathematical employment will be unconditionally necessary, that is, apodeictic. The principles of the dynamical employment will indeed also carry with them the character of a necessity *a priori*, but only under the condition of empirical thinking in an experience, and therefore only mediately and indirectly. Consequently they do not contain that immediate evidence which is peculiar to the former, though without prejudice to their certainty, which extends universally to experience. This, however, will be better judged at the close of this system of principles.",["- \"durch— aus\" and \"Grundsäͤtzen\" are OCR breaks, corrected silently.","- Divided the long sentence at \"die aber des dynamischen Gebrauchs\"; \"jenen\" = the mathematical principles.","- \"apodiktisch\" rendered \"apodeictic\"."]],"krv-b-0520":["The table of the categories gives us the quite natural guide to the table of the principles, since the latter are nothing but rules for the objective employment of the former. All principles of pure understanding are accordingly:",["- *Grundsätze* = \"principles\"; OCR \"Grundfäße\" corrected silently.","- The sentence ends with a colon where the German breaks off, so that the table can follow."]],"krv-b-0521":["Axioms\nof\nIntuition.",["- Stray number \"45\" (OCR line number) omitted."]],"krv-b-0522":["Anticipations Analogies",["- Fragment of the table: the German prints the second and third headings side by side, and I keep that order."]],"krv-b-0523":["of of",["- Fragment of the table, kept in the same side-by-side order."]],"krv-b-0524":["Perception. Experience.",["- Stray \"y\" (OCR of a brace) dropped."]],"krv-b-0525":["Postulates\nof\nEmpirical Thought\nin General.",[]],"krv-b-0526":["These titles I have chosen with care, so that the differences in respect of evidence and of the exercise [of these principles] might not pass unnoticed. It will soon be shown, however, that, so far as concerns both the evidence and the *a priori* determination of appearances in accordance with the categories of quantity and quality (if we attend solely to the form of the latter), the principles of these categories differ markedly from the other two. For the former are capable of intuitive, the latter of merely discursive certainty, though both are capable of complete certainty. I shall therefore entitle the former the mathematical, the latter the dynamical principles.* It will be observed, however, that I have in view here the principles of mathematics in the one case no more than the principles of general (physical) dynamics in the other. What I have in view are only the principles of pure understanding in their relation to inner sense (without regard to any difference in the representations given in it), through which relation those principles all acquire their possibility. I name them, therefore, with an eye to their application rather than on account of their content; and I now proceed to consider them in the same order in which they are presented in the table.",["- \"dieser Grundsätze\" after \"Ausübung\" is not in the text as given (a stray \"i\" stands there); I supply \"[of these principles]\" in brackets from the sense.","- The asterisk marks the stray footnote sign and quotation mark after \"nennen\", presumably OCR, which I treat as a footnote reference; no footnote text is given here.","- The long second sentence is divided; \"jene\"/\"diese\" are resolved as the principles of quantity and quality versus those of relation and modality.","- *Ausübung* = \"exercise\"; *Evidenz* = \"evidence\"."]],"krv-b-0527":["1.\nAxioms of Intuition.",[]],"krv-b-0528":["The principle of these is: All intuitions are extensive magnitudes.*",["- *Größen* = \"magnitudes\" (OCR \"Großen\" corrected). The \"!)\" is a footnote sign, rendered \"*\"."]],"krv-b-0529":["Proof.",["- The stray \")\" is an OCR footnote sign and is omitted."]],"krv-b-0530":["All appearances contain, as regards their form, an intuition in space and time, which underlies them all *a priori*. They cannot, therefore, be otherwise apprehended, that is, taken up into empirical conscious-",["- Breaks off mid-word (\"Bewußt-\"); the continuation is in the next passage and is not completed here."]],"krv-b-0531":["* All combination (*conjunctio*) is either composition (*compositio*) or connection (*nexus*). The former is the synthesis of a manifold whose elements do not necessarily belong to one another. For example, the two triangles into which a square is divided by its diagonal do not necessarily belong to one another. Of the same kind is the synthesis of the homogeneous in everything that can be treated mathematically (and this synthesis can again be divided into that of the",["- Footnote; breaks off mid-sentence (\"in die der\"), and the next fragment continues it.","- The German has a dash-and-comma construction in the original; I have restated it as separate sentences.","- *Zusammensetzung* = \"composition\"; *Verknüpfung* = \"connection\"."]],"krv-b-0532":["-ness be taken up otherwise than through the synthesis of the manifold,",["- Begins with the second half of \"Bewußt-/sein\", completing the previous passage's word."]],"krv-b-0533":["through which the representations of a determinate space or time are generated,",[]],"krv-b-0534":["that is, through the composition of the homogeneous and the con-",["- Breaks off mid-word (\"Be-\")."]],"krv-b-0535":["sciousness of the synthetic unity of this manifold (of the homogeneous). Now the consciousness of the homogeneous manifold in intuition in general, in so far as through it the representation of an object first",["- The line number \"5\" is omitted."]],"krv-b-0536":["becomes possible, is the concept of a magnitude (*quanti*). Thus even the perception",["- Text of this and the preceding fragment is divided at a line break; I join the sentence across them."]],"krv-b-0537":["of an object as appearance is possible only through the same synthetic",["- *Wahrnehmung* = \"perception\"."]],"krv-b-0538":["unity of the manifold of the given sensible intuition",["- Line number \"10\" is omitted."]],"krv-b-0539":["through which the unity of the composition of the manifold homogeneous",[]],"krv-b-0540":["is thought in the concept of a magnitude. That is to say, appearances",[]],"krv-b-0541":["are all of them magnitudes, and indeed extensive magnitudes, since, as intuitions",[]],"krv-b-0542":["in space or time, they must be represented through the same synthesis",[]],"krv-b-0543":["as that through which space and time in general are determined. An extensive magnitude I entitle that in which the representation of the parts makes the representation of the whole possible (and therefore necessarily precedes it).",["- The stray \"215\" is OCR for the line number 15 and is omitted.","- *nenne ich* rendered \"I entitle\", per the method."]],"krv-b-0544":["I cannot represent to myself any line, however small it may be,",["- The German sentence from the previous fragment is continued here; I have divided it at a clean point between the two passages."]],"krv-b-0545":["without drawing it in thought, that is, without generating all its parts from one point, successively, and thereby first delineating this intuition.",["- *zu verzeichnen* = \"delineating\"."]],"krv-b-0546":["The like holds of every time, even of the smallest. In it I think only the successive advance from one moment to another, whereby through all the parts of time and their addition a determinate magnitude of time is finally generated.",["- Line number \"20\" is omitted.","- The sentence is split at \"Ich denke mir darin\"."]],"krv-b-0547":["Since the mere intuition in all appearances is either space or time, every appearance as intuition is an extensive magnitude, inasmuch as it can be known in apprehension only through successive synthesis (from part to part). All appearances are accordingly already intuited as aggregates",["- The OCR garble \"voder die Heit\" is corrected to \"oder die Zeit\". Line numbers 28 and 25 omitted.","- Breaks off at \"als Aggregate\"; the parenthesis \"(Menge vorhergegebener Theile)\" is supplied in a later fragment."]],"krv-b-0548":["(multitude of antecedently given parts), which is not the case with every kind of magnitude, but only with those that are represented and apprehended by us as extensive.",["- This fragment's German text is the continuation of the sentence broken off above. The two fragments are combined in sense."]],"krv-b-0549":["on account of their being known in apprehension only through successive synthesis.",["- The text as given in this id is wholly a duplicate of a part already translated at its logical place; I give only the clause that does not recur: \"indem sie nur durch successive Synthesis... erkannt werden kann.\""]],"krv-b-0550":["Aggregation and Coalition, of which the former is directed to extensive, the latter to intensive magnitudes). The second combination (*nexus*) is the synthesis of a manifold in so far as its elements necessarily belong to one another, as, for example, the accident to some substance, or the effect to the cause. It is therefore represented as combined *a priori*, even though heterogeneous.",["- Footnote continuing from the previous footnote; \"(Aggregation und Coalition)\" is the end of a parenthesis opened there.","- \"ungleichartig\" = \"heterogeneous\"; \"Verbindung\" = \"combination\"."]],"krv-b-0551":["This combination, since it is not arbitrary, I entitle dynamical, because it concerns the combination of the *existence* of the manifold. (This again can be divided into the physical, that of appearances among themselves, and the metaphysical, that of their combination in the faculty of knowledge *a priori*.)",["- The German has \"Dasein\" not emphasised; I add no italics in the translation.","- Restated as separate sentences, as the method allows."]],"krv-b-0552":["[Fragment duplicated from the preceding footnote text: \"Wirkung zu der Ursache —, mithin auch als ungleichartig, doch a priori ver-\". Translated above.]",["- OCR overlap: this id contains only words already translated in the preceding fragment (effect to the cause, heterogeneous yet combined *a priori*); I do not translate them twice."]],"krv-b-0553":["[Fragment duplicated from the preceding footnote text: \"bunden vorgestellt wird; welche Verbindung, weil sie nicht willkürlich ist, ich darum dynamisch nenne, weil sie die Verbindung des Daseins des Mannigfaltigen betrifft (die wiederum in die physische, der Erscheinungen unter einander, und meta-\". Translated above.]",["- OCR overlap: see preceding note; no new matter."]],"krv-b-0554":["physical, that of their combination in the faculty of knowledge *a priori*.)",["- Closing words of the footnote. The following words, \"(Menge vorhergegebener Theile) angeschaut, welches eben nicht der Fall bei jeder Art Größen, sondern nur derer ist...\", belong to the main text, not the footnote; in effect, \"(multitude of previously given parts)... which is not the case with every kind of magnitude, but only with those that are represented and apprehended by us as extensive.\"","- \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" = \"faculty of knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-0555":["On this successive synthesis of the productive imagination in the generation of figures is founded the mathematics of extension (geometry) with its axioms. These axioms express the conditions of sensible intuition *a priori* under which alone the schema of a pure concept of outer appearance can be brought about. For example: between two points only one straight line is possible; two straight lines do not enclose a space; and so on. These are the axioms which properly concern only magnitudes (*quanta*) as such.",["- Sentence divided at \"welche… ausdrücken\" and the examples.","- OCR \"si\" completed as \"sich\" and \"Ariomen\" as \"Axiomen\" silently.","- *Einbildungskraft* = \"imagination\"; *Gestalten* = \"figures\"."]],"krv-b-0556":["As regards, however, magnitude (*quantitas*), that is, the answer to the question how great anything is, there are, although several of these propositions are synthetic and immediately certain (*indemonstrabilia*), no axioms in the strict sense. For that equals added to equals, or taken from them, give equals, is an analytic proposition, since I am immediately conscious of the identity of the one generation of magnitude with the other; whereas axioms have to be synthetic propositions *a priori*. The evident propositions concerning numerical relations are, on the other hand, certainly synthetic, but not universal, as those of geometry are; and for that very reason they are not axioms but can be entitled numerical formulae. That 7 + 5 = 12 is not an analytic proposition. For I think the number 12 neither in the representation of 7, nor in that of 5, nor in the representation of the composition of the two. (Whether I ought to think it in the addition of the two is not here in question; for in an analytic proposition the only question is whether I actually think the predicate in the representation of the subject.) But although it is synthetic, it is only a singular proposition. In so far as attention is here given merely to the synthesis of the homogeneous (of the units), the synthesis can take place in only one way, although the use of these numbers is afterwards universal. If I say: a triangle can be constructed with three lines, any two of which taken together are greater than the third, I have here the mere function of the productive imagination, which can draw the lines longer or shorter, and can make them meet at any angles whatever. The number 7, on the contrary, is possible in only one way, and so also is the number 12, which is generated by the synthesis of the former with 5. Such propositions, therefore, must not be entitled axioms (for otherwise there would be an infinite number of them), but numerical formulae.",["- Several long sentences are divided or recast; the parenthesis on \"analytic\" is kept in place.","- *Axiomen* = \"axioms\"; *im eigentlichen Verstande* = \"in the strict sense\"; *Zahlformeln* = \"numerical formulae\".","- *Größe (quantitas)* = \"magnitude\"; *Größen (quanta)* is kept as *quanta* in the preceding passage.","- \"Ariomen\" corrected silently to \"Axiomen\"."]],"krv-b-0557":["This transcendental principle of the mathematics of appearances greatly enlarges our *a priori* knowledge. For it alone makes pure mathematics, in all its precision, applicable to objects of experience. Without this principle that applicability would not be so self-evident, and has indeed given rise to a good deal of contradiction. Appearances are not things in themselves. Empirical intuition is possible only through pure intuition (of space and of time). What geometry says of pure intuition, therefore, holds without dispute of empirical intuition also; and the evasions which suppose that objects of the senses need not conform to the rules of construction in space (for instance, to the rule of the infinite divisibility of lines or angles) must fall away. For to hold this is to deny objective validity to space, and with it to all mathematics, and we no longer know why, or how far, mathematics can be applied to appearances. The synthesis of spaces and times, as the essential form of all intuition, is what at the same time makes possible the apprehension of appearance, and so every outer experience, and consequently all knowledge of its objects. What mathematics in its pure employment proves of the former must also necessarily hold of the latter. All objections to this are merely chicaneries of a falsely instructed reason. Reason wrongly thinks to detach the objects of the senses from the formal condition of our sensibility, and represents them as objects in themselves, given to the understanding, although they are mere appearances. In that case, indeed, nothing could be known synthetically of them *a priori*, and so nothing through pure concepts of space; and the science which determines these concepts, namely geometry, would itself not be possible.",["- Restated the long final sentence as several shorter ones; \"jener/dieser\" resolved as pure and empirical intuition (first paragraph) and, in the later clause, as pure mathematics' object (synthesis of space/time) versus appearance; \"von ihnen\" = the objects.","- \"Grund-satz\" is an OCR line-break hyphenation, silently restored; likewise other line-break hyphens.","- \"Erkenntniß a priori\" rendered \"*a priori* knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-0558":["Anticipations of Perception.",["- Heading only."]],"krv-b-0559":["The principle of these is: In all appearances the real, which is an object of sensation, has intensive magnitude, that is, a degree.[footnote marker]",["- The OCR mark `“)` after \"Grad\" is a footnote marker; the footnote text is not in the passage, so none is supplied."]],"krv-b-0560":["Proof.",["- The OCR \"Beweis.)\" has a stray parenthesis, silently dropped."]],"krv-b-0561":["Perception is empirical consciousness, that is, a consciousness in which there is at the same time sensation. Appearances, as objects of perception, are not pure (merely formal) intuitions, like space and time, for these cannot in themselves be perceived. They contain, therefore, over and above the intuition, the material for some object or other (through which something existing in space or time is represented), that is, the real of sensation, as merely subjective representation, of which we can only be conscious that the subject is affected, and which we refer to an object in general. Now a gradual transition from empirical consciousness to pure consciousness is possible, in which the real of the former vanishes entirely, and a merely formal consciousness (*a priori*) of the manifold in space and time remains. Hence there is also possible a synthesis generating the magnitude of a sensation, from its beginning, pure intuition = 0, up to any magnitude we please. Now sensation in itself is not an objective representation, and neither the intuition of space nor that of time is to be met with in it. It will therefore have no extensive magnitude, yet it will have a magnitude, and indeed through the apprehension of it, in which empirical consciousness can grow in a certain time from nothing = 0 to its given measure. It will accordingly have an intensive magnitude; and in correspondence with this, intensive magnitude, that is, a degree of influence on sense, must be ascribed to all objects of perception, in so far as they contain sensation.",["- \"de Wahrnehmung\" read as \"der Wahrnehmung\" (OCR slip).","- Divided the long penultimate and final sentences; \"desselben\" (the real) referred to empirical consciousness's real."]],"krv-b-0562":["All knowledge by which I can know and determine *a priori* what belongs to empirical knowledge may be entitled an anticipation, and this is without doubt the sense in which Epicurus used his expression *prolepsis* [OCR: \"rpornbıs\", i.e. πρόληψις]. But since there is in appearances something which is never known *a priori*, and which therefore constitutes the proper difference between the empirical and knowledge *a priori*, namely sensation (as the material of perception), it follows that it is sensation which cannot be anticipated. [The text here is corrupt: after \"werden kann.\" there stands a stray \"so:\", evidently the remnant of a lost phrase.] So: in all appearances, sensation, and the real which corresponds to it in the object (*realitas phaenomenon*), has an intensive magnitude, that is, a degree. On the other hand, the pure determinations in space and time, in respect both of figure and of magnitude, we could entitle anticipations of appearances, since they represent *a priori* what may always be given *a posteriori* in experience. But suppose there should nevertheless be something that can be known *a priori* in every sensation, as sensation in general (without any particular sensation being given): this would deserve in an eminent sense to be entitled anticipation, since it seems strange to forestall experience in what concerns precisely its matter, which can be drawn only from experience. And so it actually is here.",["- \"rpornbıs\" is OCR garble for Greek πρόληψις; I give the likely reading in brackets.","- \"so:\" before \"In allen Erscheinungen\" is garbled; the original reads \"Daher der Grundsatz so: …\"? Doubtful, so I flag it rather than complete it. \"Materie\" rendered \"material\"/\"matter\" by context; \"im ausnehmenden Verstande\" = \"in an eminent sense\".","- \"finde s sich\" read \"finde sich\"."]],"krv-b-0563":["Apprehension by means of sensation alone fills only a moment (if, that is, I leave out of account the succession of many sensations). As something in the appearance the apprehension of which is not a successive synthesis proceeding from parts to the whole representation, sensation has therefore no extensive magnitude. The absence of sensation in the same moment would represent that moment as empty, and so = 0. What corresponds in empirical intuition to sensation is reality (*realitas phaenomenon*); what corresponds to its absence is negation = 0. Now every sensation is capable of diminution, so that it can decrease and thus gradually vanish. Between reality in appearance and negation there is therefore a continuous connection of many possible intermediate sensations, the difference between any two of which is always smaller than the difference between the given sensation and zero, or complete negation. That is to say, the real in appearance always has a magnitude, which, however, is not met with in apprehension, since this takes place by means of mere sensation in a moment, and not through the successive synthesis of many sensations, and so does not proceed from the parts to the whole. It has, therefore, a magnitude, but not an extensive one.",["- Restated the opening sentence in two; \"sie\" in \"hat sie also\" taken as the apprehension/sensation, i.e. the real in the appearance, and rendered \"sensation\".","- \"Zero\" kept as in the German.","- \"diesen\" = the moment."]],"krv-b-0564":["Now the magnitude which is apprehended only as unity, and in which plurality can be represented only through approximation to negation = 0, I entitle intensive magnitude. Accordingly every reality in appearance has intensive magnitude, that is, a degree. If this reality is regarded as cause (whether of sensation or of another reality in appearance, for instance, a change), the degree of reality as cause is entitled a moment, for example, the moment of gravity. It is so entitled because the degree designates only that magnitude the apprehension of which is not successive but instantaneous. This I touch on here only in passing, for with causality I have as yet nothing to do.",["- \"Mfo\" is OCR for \"Also\", silently corrected.","- \"Moment\" kept as in German/Latin *momentum*."]],"krv-b-0565":["Thus every sensation, and so also every reality in appearance, however small it may be, has a degree, that is, an intensive magnitude, which can always still be diminished; and between reality and negation there is a continuous connection of possible realities and of possible smaller perceptions. Every colour, for example the red, has a degree which, however small, is never the smallest; and the same holds of heat, of the moment of gravity, and so on throughout.",["- The OCR \"5|\" and \"10\" are line numbers and \"be— wandt\" a line-break hyphenation, silently removed; the \"|\" apparatus sign is not carried over since it stands here as a line-number artefact."]],"krv-b-0566":["The property of magnitudes by which no part of them is the smallest possible (no part is simple) is entitled their continuity. Space and time are *quanta continua*, because no part of them can be given save as enclosed between limits (points and instants), and therefore only in such a manner that this part is itself again a space or a time. Space consists, therefore, only of spaces, and time of times. Points and instants are only limits, that is, mere positions marking the limitation of space and time. But positions always presuppose the intuitions which they are to limit or to determine; and neither space nor time can be composed out of mere positions, as constituents which could be given prior to space or time. Magnitudes of this kind may also be entitled flowing, since the synthesis (of the productive imagination) involved in their generation is a progress in time, and the continuity of time is usually designated by the expression 'flowing' ('flowing away').",["- The OCR fragment \"eins 14 zuschließen\" is read as \"einzuschließen\" (running line number dropped); \"solen\" read as \"sollen\".","- \"und aus bloßen Stellen als zu aus Bestandtheilen\" is garbled by OCR; I read it as \"als aus Bestandtheilen\" and rendered \"as constituents\".","- Restated the long sentence on points and instants as two sentences. \"Stellen\" = \"positions\"."]],"krv-b-0567":["All appearances, then, are continuous magnitudes, whether as extensive magnitudes in respect of their intuition, or as intensive magnitudes in respect of mere perception (sensation, and therewith reality). If the synthesis of the manifold of appearance is interrupted, what results is an aggregate of many appearances (and not properly an appearance as a quantum). Such an aggregate is generated not by the mere continuation of the productive synthesis of a certain kind, but by the repetition of a synthesis that is constantly ceasing. If I entitle thirteen thalers a quantum of money, the name is correct so far as I understand by it the content of a mark of fine silver. This is indeed a continuous magnitude, in which no part is the smallest, but each part could constitute a coin which would always contain material for still smaller ones. If, however, I understand by that name thirteen round thalers, as so many coins (whatever their silver content may be), I am using the name improperly in calling it a quantum of thalers; I must entitle it an aggregate, that is, a number of coins. Since, however, unity must underlie all number, the appearance, as unity, is a quantum, and as such is always a continuum.",["- \"13 Thaler\" at the start of line is the OCR's running line number \"13\" absorbed; the sentence in fact reads \"Wenn ich *dreizehn*...\"? No: the text has \"13 Thaler\" in the original (Kant: \"Wenn ich 13 Thaler...\"), so I kept \"thirteen thalers\".","- \"sein Silber\" rendered \"fine silver\" (Kant's \"eine Mark fein Silber\"); OCR \"sein\" corrected to \"fein\".","- \"so benenne ich es unschicklich ... sondern muß\" is irregular in the German; smoothed into a single construction with a semicolon.","- Divided the sentence on the aggregate into two."]],"krv-b-0568":["If, now, all appearances, whether considered extensively or intensively, are continuous magnitudes, the proposition that all alteration (transition of a thing from one state into another) is likewise continuous could easily be proved here with mathematical evidence, were not the causality of an alteration in general entirely outside the limits of a transcendental philosophy, presupposing as it does empirical principles. For that there can be a cause which alters the state of things, that is, determines them to the contrary of a certain given state, is something of which the understanding gives us no disclosure *a priori*. This is not merely because the understanding has no insight into the possibility of it (such insight is lacking to us in the case of several modes of knowledge *a priori*), but because alterability concerns only certain determinations of appearances, which experience alone can teach, while their cause is to be found in what is unalterable. But since we have before us nothing that we can employ save the pure fundamental concepts of all possible experience, among which nothing empirical may be present, we cannot, without disturbing the unity of the system, anticipate the general science of nature, which is built upon certain fundamental experiences.",["- OCR \"con— tinuirlich\" restored as \"continuirlich\"; \"à priori\" rendered in italics as *a priori*.","- \"Erkenntnissen\" plural = \"modes of knowledge\".","- \"Eröffnung\" rendered \"disclosure\"; \"verlegen\" read as \"verletzen\" sense (\"disturb\"); the printed text has \"verletzen\", OCR \"verlegen\" is doubtful.","- The \"nicht bloß ... sondern\" clause was kept but the main clause was split off with a full stop."]],"krv-b-0569":["Nevertheless we do not lack proofs of the great influence which this our principle has in anticipating perceptions, and even in supplying their lack, so far as to bar the way against all the false inferences which might be drawn from it.",["- \"daraus\" is ambiguous (from the lack of perception); I rendered \"from it\" as referring to that lack.","- The passage is a complete sentence, but a continuation follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0570":["If all reality in perception has a degree, between which and negation there is an infinite series of ever lesser degrees, and if nevertheless every sense must have a determinate degree of receptivity for sensations, then no perception, and therefore no experience, is possible which would prove, whether immediately or mediately (by whatever roundabout way of inference one pleases), a complete absence of everything real in appearance. In other words, a proof of empty space or of empty time can never be drawn from experience. For, in the first place, the complete absence of the real in sensible intuition cannot itself be perceived; and secondly, it cannot be inferred from any single appearance and from the difference in the degree of its reality, nor may it ever be assumed in explanation of that difference. For even if the whole intuition of a determinate space or time is real throughout, that is, if no part of it is empty, yet since every reality has its degree, which, the extensive magnitude of the appearance remaining unaltered, can diminish through infinite stages down to nothing (the void), there must be infinitely various degrees with which space or time is filled; and the intensive magnitude in different appearances may be smaller or greater, although the extensive magnitude of the intuition is the same.",["- The running head (\"Elementarlehre. II. Theil ...\") and page number 156 are omitted; \"weiten Umschweif\" is reconstructed from the broken \"wei- eben Umschweif\", i.e. \"weitläufigen/weiten Umschweif\".","- OCR garbles \"dem Unterschiede des Grades\" and \"derselben\" (\"al\", \"ders f selben\") were silently corrected; \"bis u zum\" read \"bis zum\".","- \"d. i. es kann\" rendered as a new sentence (\"In other words\").","- \"Sinn\" = \"sense\"; \"Receptivität\" = receptivity."]],"krv-b-0571":["We shall give an example of this. Almost all students of nature, when",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"da\" begins a subordinate clause continued in the next passage); rendered \"when\" as it stands, without completing it. \"Naturlehrer\" = \"students of nature\"."]],"krv-b-0572":["they perceive a great difference in the quantity of matter of different kinds under the same volume (partly by the moment of gravity or weight, partly by the moment of resistance to other matters in motion), unanimously conclude that this volume (extensive magnitude of the appearance) must be void in all matters, though in differing degree. But which of these investigators of nature, for the most part mathematical and mechanical, would ever have supposed that they were basing this inference solely upon a metaphysical presupposition, which they profess so greatly to avoid? For they assume that the real in space (I do not care here to call it impenetrability or weight, because these are empirical concepts) is everywhere one and the same, and can differ only in extensive magnitude, that is, in amount. To this presupposition, for which they could have no ground in experience and which is therefore merely metaphysical, I oppose a transcendental proof. This proof is not intended to explain the difference in the filling of spaces; but it entirely removes the supposed necessity of that presupposition, namely that the said difference can be explained only by assuming empty spaces. It has the merit, at least, of setting the understanding free to think this diversity in another fashion also, should the explanation of nature make some hypothesis necessary for the purpose. For we see that, although equal spaces may be completely filled by different matters, so that in none of them is there a point",["- Begins mid-sentence (\"ia\" is the OCR remnant of \"da\" from the preceding passage's last word, dropped); \"ertenfiven\" read \"extensiven\"; \"Malerien\" read \"Materien\".","- Ends mid-sentence (\"ein Punkt\"), continued in the next passage.","- Several long sentences were divided; \"Naturforscher\" = \"investigators of nature\"."]],"krv-b-0573":["in which their presence is not to be found, yet each",["- Fragment; the \"so habe doch jedes\" clause continues in the next passage (\"Reale ... ihren Grad\"). Note Kant's \"ihre/jedes\" agreement is loose; \"their presence\" refers to the matters."]],"krv-b-0574":["real, with the same quality, has its degree (of resistance or of weight),",["- \"Reale\" rendered \"real\" (substantive: \"the real\", completed by what follows); the sentence continues. The hyphenated \"Wie- gens\" is completed in the next passage (\"Wiegens\")."]],"krv-b-0575":["which can be infinitely smaller without diminution of the extensive magnitude or amount, before it passes over into the void and vanishes.",["- Fragment begins with the second half of \"Wiegens\" (= \"of weighing\", rendered \"weight\" in the previous passage); \"ins s Unendliche\" read \"ins Unendliche\"; \"verschwin- det\" completed from the next fragment's \"dei\" (= \"det\")."]],"krv-b-0576":["Thus an expansion which fills a space, for example heat,",["- \"Ausspannung\" rendered \"expansion\"; the text breaks off before the main clause concludes."]],"krv-b-0577":["and in the same way every other reality (in appearance), without leaving in the least the smallest part of this space empty,",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0578":["can diminish in its degrees",["- \"Gra— den\" completed as \"Graden\" in the next passage; I have rendered the whole phrase here for sense."]],"krv-b-0579":["infinitely, and nonetheless fill the space with these smaller degrees just as well as another appearance with greater.",["- \"to\" before \"diesen\" is an OCR line-number remnant, dropped; \"Raum mit diesen kleineren Graden\" translated as given."]],"krv-b-0580":["My intention here is by no means to assert that this",["- Fragment; \"dieses\" refers forward to what the next passage states."]],"krv-b-0581":["is actually so with the diversity of matters in respect of their specific weight,",["- Fragment; \"so bewandt sei\" = \"is so circumstanced\"; I have rendered it as \"is actually so\"."]],"krv-b-0582":["but only to show from a principle of pure understanding",["- Fragment; \"reinen Ver- standes\" is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0583":["that the nature of our perceptions makes such a mode of explanation possible, and that it is erroneous to regard the real in appearance",["- \"ıs\" is an OCR line-number remnant, dropped. The passage breaks off at \"der Er-\" (*Erscheinung*), which I have rendered \"appearance\" in brackets-free form on the evident reading; the sentence is cut off and I have not completed it."]],"krv-b-0584":["[…appearance] as equal in degree and as differing only in aggregation and in its extensive magnitude; and that it even professes to assert this a priori, through a principle of the understanding. Nevertheless, this anticipation of perception must always have something startling about it for an enquirer who is accustomed to transcendental reflection and has thereby become cautious. It gives rise to some misgiving that the understanding should be able to anticipate a synthetic proposition such as that concerning the degree of everything real in appearances, and so concerning the possibility of the inner distinction of sensation itself, when we abstract from its empirical quality. There is therefore still a question not unworthy of solution: how can the understanding pronounce synthetically a priori upon appearances, and anticipate them even in what is properly and merely empirical, namely, what concerns sensation?\n\nThe quality of sensation is always merely empirical, and cannot be represented a priori (e.g. colours, taste, etc.). But",["- The passage opens mid-sentence (\"[…appearance]\"): the German begins with the fragment \"scheinung\" (*Erscheinung*); I mark the lost portion in brackets and do not complete the sentence.","- Restated the long second sentence as two sentences, and the \"und es ist also noch…\" clause as a separate sentence; in the German the \"daß\" clause is joined to \"Bedenken\" by \"darüber\".","- The OCR stray letters (h, s, 0) and the line-marker \"|\" in the German are discarded as OCR debris; the apparatus signs are not otherwise in the text. The note-marker \"0\" before \"Die Qualität\" signals a footnote or author's note, which I have set off as a new paragraph.","- *Nachforscher* = \"enquirer\"; *Auflösung* = \"solution\"."]],"krv-b-0585":["the real which corresponds to sensations in general, as opposed to negation = 0, represents only something the concept of which in itself contains a being, and signifies nothing but the synthesis in an empirical consciousness in general. For in inner sense empirical consciousness can be raised from 0 to any higher degree, so that the very same extensive magnitude of intuition (e.g. an illuminated surface) excites as great a sensation as an aggregate of many other [surfaces] (less illuminated) taken together. We can therefore abstract entirely from the extensive magnitude of the appearance, and yet represent in the mere sensation, in a single moment, a synthesis of uniform ascent from 0 to the given empirical consciousness. All sensations are therefore, as such, given only a posteriori, but their property of having a degree can be known a priori. It is remarkable that in the case of magnitudes in general we can know a priori only a single quality, namely continuity; and that in all quality (the real of appearances) we can know a priori nothing further than their intensive quantity, namely, that they have a degree. Everything else is left to experience.",["- \"[surfaces]\" is supplied to complete the sense of \"vielem andern (minder Erleuchteten)\".","- OCR slips corrected silently: \"3. B.\" = z. B.; \"Duantität\" = Quantität; stray numerals and line markers omitted.","- *Anschauung* = intuition; *Empfindung* = sensation; *Bewußtsein* = consciousness."]],"krv-b-0586":["Analogies of Experience.",["- The leading \"35\" is a page/running-head number and is omitted."]],"krv-b-0587":["The principle of these is: Experience is possible only through the representation of a necessary connection of perceptions.",["- OCR \"mögliche!\" read as \"möglich.\""]],"krv-b-0588":["Proof.*",["- The OCR mark after \"Beweis\" (“)) probably stands for a footnote asterisk; I have used \"*\" and not supplied any footnote text, since none is given."]],"krv-b-0589":["Experience is an empirical knowledge, that is, a knowledge which determines an object through perceptions. It is therefore a synthesis of perceptions, a synthesis which is not itself contained in perception but contains the synthetic unity of the manifold of perceptions in one consciousness; and this unity constitutes what is essential in a knowledge of the objects of the senses, that is, of experience (as distinct from mere intuition or sensation of the senses). Now in experience, it is true, perceptions come together only contingently, so that no necessity of their connection is evident, or can be evident, from the perceptions themselves. For apprehension is only a placing together of the manifold of empirical intuition, and no representation of the necessity of the combined existence of the appearances which it thus places together in space and time is to be met with in it. But experience is a knowledge of objects through perceptions. The relation in the existence of the manifold has therefore to be represented in experience not as it is put together in time, but as it is objectively in time. Since time itself cannot be perceived, the determination of the existence of objects in time can come about only through their connection in time in general, and so only through concepts that connect them a priori. As these concepts always carry necessity with them, experience is possible only through a representation of the necessary connection of perceptions.",["- Divided the German's long sentences into shorter ones; \"Da aber…so kann\" is restated as a chain of statements with \"Since…\".","- \"Apprehension\" kept for *Apprehension*; \"welche\" after \"Einheit\" taken to refer to the synthetic unity, not the synthesis.","- OCR stray symbols (\"2\", \"ə:\") omitted; \"Wahrnehmungen … in derselben angetroffen wird\" – \"derselben\" taken to refer to apprehension."]],"krv-b-0590":["The three modes of time are permanence, succession, and coexistence. Consequently three rules of all time-relations of appearances, in accordance with which the existence of each can be determined in respect of the unity of all time, precede all experience and first make it possible.",["- *modi* kept as Kant's Latin term in the German; rendered \"modes\".","- OCR break \"Zu— gleichsein\" joined as *Zugleichsein* = coexistence."]],"krv-b-0591":["The universal principle of all three Analogies rests on the necessary unity of apperception in respect of all possible empirical consciousness (perception) at every time; and since that unity underlies a priori, it rests on the synthetic unity of all appearances in their relation in time. For original apperception relates to inner sense (the sum of all representations), and indeed relates a priori to its form, that is, to the relation of the manifold of empirical consciousness in time. Now in original apperception all this manifold has to be united in accordance with its time-relations; for this is what is stated by its transcendental unity a priori, under which stands everything that is to belong to my (that is, to my single) knowledge, and so can become an object for me. This synthetic unity in the time-relation of all perceptions, which is determined a priori, is therefore the law that all empirical determinations of time must stand under rules of the universal determination of time; and the Analogies of experience, with which we are now to deal, must be rules of this kind.",["- \"Zeitvestimmungen\" corrected from OCR to *Zeitbestimmungen*; \"einigen\" kept as \"single\".","- Restated the opening sentence's \"folglich, da jene…\" more plainly; \"jene\" taken to refer to the unity of apperception.","- *Apperception* (*Apperception*) kept as the English cognate; *Erkenntnis* = knowledge."]],"krv-b-0592":["These principles have this peculiarity, that they consider not appearances and the synthesis of their empirical intuition, but merely the existence of appearances and their relation to one another in respect of this their existence. Now the way in which something is apprehended in appearance can be so determined a priori that the rule of its synthesis can at the same time give this intuition a priori in every empirical example before us, that is, can bring it into being out of that rule. But the existence of appearances cannot be known a priori; and even if we could by this route arrive at inferring some existence, we should still not know it determinately, that is, we should not be able to anticipate that whereby its empirical intuition differs from others.",["- \"sie daraus zu Stande bringen\" – \"sie\" taken to be the intuition, \"daraus\" the rule.","- \"auf diesem Wege\" kept as \"by this route\"."]],"krv-b-0593":["The two preceding principles, which I entitled mathematical, in view of the fact that they entitled us to apply mathematics to appearances, concerned appearances in respect of their mere possibility. They taught how appearances, both as regards their intuition and as regards the real in their perception, can be generated in accordance with rules of a mathematical synthesis. Hence in both cases numerical magnitudes, and with them the determination of appearance as magnitude, can be employed. Thus, for example, I shall be able to compose the degree of the sensations of sunlight out of some 200,000 illuminations by the moon, and so to give it determined a priori, that is, to construct it. Hence we may entitle the former principles constitutive.",["- \"nannte\" rendered \"entitled\", in keeping with the heißen/nennen convention; \"berechtigten\" also rendered \"entitled\", which echoes in English; \"authorised\" would avoid the repetition.","- OCR \"wer: den\" and \"gez braucht\" repaired to *werden*, *gebraucht*; \"constitutive\" is Kant's own *constitutive* (Latin form in the German).","- Restated the long opening sentence as three sentences, with the \"daher\" clause carried on."]],"krv-b-0594":["With those principles which are to bring the *existence* of appearances under rules a priori, the situation must be quite different. Since existence cannot be constructed, these principles can concern only the relation of existence, and can yield nothing but regulative principles. Neither axioms nor anticipations are here to be thought of. If a perception is given us in a time-relation to other perceptions (though these are indeterminate), we shall not be able to say a priori *which* other perception, or how great a perception, but only how, so far as existence is concerned, it is necessarily connected with the former in this *modus* of time.\n\nIn philosophy analogies signify something very different from what they represent in mathematics. In mathematics they are formulae which express the equality of two relations of magnitude, and they are always constitutive; so that if three members of the proportion are given, the fourth is thereby also given, that is, can be constructed. In philosophy, however, analogy is the equality not of two quantitative but of two qualitative relations. From three given members I can know, and give a priori, only the relation to a fourth, not this fourth member itself; but I have a rule for seeking it in experience, and a mark by which it can be discovered there. An analogy of experience will therefore be only a rule according to which unity of experience is to arise from perceptions (not a rule showing how perception itself, as empirical intuition in general, arises). As a principle applying to objects (to appearances) it will hold not constitutively but merely regulatively.\n\nThe same will also hold of the postulates of empirical thought in general, which concern together the synthesis of mere intuition (the form of appearance), of perception (the matter of appearance), and of experience (the relation of these perceptions). They too are merely regulative principles. They are distinguished from the mathematical principles, which are constitutive, not indeed in certainty, which is established a priori in both, but in the kind of evidence, that is, in what is intuitive in it (and therefore also in demonstration).",["- OCR \"fons dem\" corrected to \"sondern\". \"Großenverhältnisse\" read as \"Größenverhältnisse\".","- Restated the long second-paragraph sentences and broke the passage into paragraphs; the parenthesis \"(nicht wie Wahrnehmung selbst …)\" is expanded slightly for clarity, and \"[a rule showing how]\" is implied, not in the German.","- \"modus der Zeit\" left in Latin as in the source; \"Dasein\" rendered \"existence\".","- \"Erschei— nungen\" etc. line-break hyphens removed silently."]],"krv-b-0595":["But what was remarked of all synthetic principles, and must here be especially noted, is this: these analogies have their sole meaning and validity as principles not of the transcendental but merely of the empirical employment of the understanding, and can therefore be proved only as such. Consequently appearances must be subsumed not under the categories simpliciter, but only under their schemata. For if the objects to which these principles are to be related were things in themselves, it would be quite impossible to know anything of them synthetically a priori. But they are nothing but appearances; and the complete knowledge of appearances, in which all a priori principles must in the end always terminate, is simply possible experience. Consequently these principles can have as their aim nothing but the conditions of the unity of empirical knowledge in the synthesis of appearances. This synthesis, however, is thought only in the schema of the pure concept of the understanding; and the category contains the function, unrestricted by any sensible condition, of the unity of that synthesis as a synthesis in general.\n\nBy these principles, then, we shall be entitled to combine appearances only according to an analogy with the logical and universal unity of concepts. Hence in the principle itself we shall make use of the category; but in carrying it out (in applying it to appearances) we shall set its schema in the category's place, as the key to its employment—or rather we shall set the schema beside the category as its restricting condition, under the name of a formula of the category.",["- \"schlechthin\" rendered \"simpliciter\" is my choice; plain \"without qualification\" would also serve.","- Split two long sentences; \"deren Einheit\" refers to the synthesis, resolved by judgment as \"that synthesis\".","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Verstandesgebrauch\" = employment of the understanding.","- Last clause: \"an dessen Stelle … oder jener … zur Seite setzen\" is irregular; smoothed to \"in its place … or rather beside it\"."]],"krv-b-0596":["First Analogy.\nPrinciple of the Permanence of Substance.",["- \"Beharrlichkeit\" = permanence."]],"krv-b-0597":["In all change of appearances substance is permanent, and its quantum in nature is neither increased nor diminished.[*]",["- The OCR mark “) after \"vermindert\" is a footnote/apparatus sign; rendered [*] as a placeholder, since the footnote is not in the passage."]],"krv-b-0598":["Proof.[*]",["- The OCR mark “) is a footnote sign; rendered [*] as a placeholder. Footnote text is not supplied here."]],"krv-b-0599":["All[*] appearances are in time, and in time alone, as substratum (as the permanent form of inner intuition), can both coexistence and succession be represented. The time, therefore, in which all change of appearances is to be thought, remains and does not change; for it is that in which succession and coexistence can be represented only as its determinations. Now time cannot by itself be perceived. Consequently there must be found in the objects of perception, that is, in the appearances, the substratum which represents time in general, and in which all change or coexistence can be perceived in apprehension through the relation of the appearances to it.\n\nBut the substratum of everything real, that is, of everything belonging to the existence of things, is substance; and in it everything that belongs to existence can be thought only as determination. Consequently the permanent, in relation to which alone all time-relations of appearances can be determined, is substance in the appearance, that is, the real in the appearance, which as the substratum of all change always remains the same. Since substance cannot change in its existence, its quantum in nature can also be neither increased nor diminished.",["- \"Alle?)\" : the OCR \"?)\" is a footnote sign; rendered [*]; text of the footnote not supplied.","- Split into two paragraphs and sentences; \"an dem\" and \"demselben\" in the first paragraph taken to refer to the substratum.","- \"Zugleichsein\" = coexistence; \"Nacheinander\" = succession; \"Bestimmung\" = determination.","- \"Da diese\" refers to substance (die Substanz), resolved accordingly."]],"krv-b-0600":["[*] A: Principle of Permanence.",["- OCR \"Al;\" read as \"A:\" (the first-edition version, marked A); the text is the A-edition heading to the passage that follows. Reading is a conjecture.","- \"Grundsatz der Beharrlichkeit\" = principle of permanence."]],"krv-b-0601":["All appearances contain the permanent (substance) as the object itself, and the changeable as its mere determination, that is, as a way in which the object exists.",["- \"Wandelbare\" rendered \"changeable\"."]],"krv-b-0602":["Our apprehension of the manifold of appearance is always successive, and is therefore always changing. By it alone we can never determine whether this manifold, as object of experience, is coexistent or successive, unless there underlies it something that always is, that is, something abiding and permanent, of which all change and coexistence are nothing but so many ways (*modi* of time) in which the permanent exists. Only in the permanent, then, are time-relations possible (for simultaneity and succession are the only relations in time). That is, the permanent is the substratum of the empirical representation of time itself, in which alone all determination of time is possible. Permanence in general expresses time as the constant correlate of all existence of appearances, of all change, and of all concomitance. For change does not affect time itself, but only the appearances in time (just as coexistence is not a *modus* of time itself, since in time no parts are coexistent, all being successive). If we sought to attribute to time itself a succession, we should have to think yet another time in which this succession would be possible.\n\nOnly through the permanent does existence, in the different parts of the time-series successively, acquire a magnitude which is entitled duration. For in mere succession existence is always vanishing and beginning, and never has the least magnitude. Without this permanent, then, there is no time-relation. Now time in itself cannot be perceived. Consequently this permanent in the appearances is the substratum of all determination of time, and therefore also the condition of the possibility of all synthetic unity of perceptions, that is, of experience; and in this permanent all existence and all change in time can be regarded only as a *modus* of the existence of that which abides and is permanent. Thus in all appearances the permanent is the object itself, that is, substance (*phaenomenon*); but everything that changes or can change belongs only to the way in which this substance or substances exist, and therefore to their determinations.",["- Passage begins in the numbered-line text of B 225 ff.; line number \"5\" and OCR slips (\"Veechsel\" = Wechsel, \"fann die Heit\" = kann die Zeit, \"be. harrt\" = beharrt) silently corrected.","- Split the long sentences; \"Begleitung\" rendered \"concomitance\"; \"Dauer\" = duration.","- Reference \"A 182–184\" suggests this is the A-edition text; the \"?\" in the passage heading is the portal's.","- \"an ihr\" (first sentence) refers to the apprehension/appearance; rendered \"underlies it\", i.e. the manifold."]],"krv-b-0603":["I find that at all times not only the philosopher but even the common understanding has presupposed this permanence as a substratum of all change of appearances, and will always assume it as beyond doubt. The only difference is that the philosopher expresses himself somewhat more definitely, by saying: In all alterations in the world substance remains, and only the accidents change. But nowhere do I find even an attempt at a proof of this very synthetic proposition. Indeed, it is only seldom placed, as it ought to be, at the head of the pure and completely a priori laws of nature. In fact, the proposition that substance is permanent is tautological. For this permanence alone is the ground on which we apply the category of substance to the appearance; and what should have been proved is that in all appearances there is something permanent, in relation to which the changeable is nothing but a determination of its existence. But such a proof can never be conducted dogmatically, that is, from concepts, since it concerns a synthetic proposition a priori. Nor did anyone ever reflect that such propositions are valid only in relation to possible experience, and can therefore be proved only through a deduction of the possibility of experience. It is no wonder, then, that although the proposition has been made the foundation of all experience (since the need of it is felt in empirical knowledge), it has never been proved.",[]],"krv-b-0604":["A philosopher was asked: How much does smoke weigh? He answered: Subtract from the weight of the wood burnt the weight of the ashes that remain, and you have the weight of the smoke. He thus presupposed as undeniable that even in fire the matter (substance) does not vanish, and that only its form undergoes alteration. Similarly the proposition, *from nothing comes nothing*, is only another consequence of the principle of permanence, or rather of the everlasting existence of the proper subject in appearances. For if that in the appearance which we call substance is to be the proper substratum of all determination of time, then all existence, whether in past or in future time, must be determinable in it, and in it alone. Hence we can give an appearance the name of substance only because we presuppose its existence at all times. The word 'permanence' does not even express this well, since it refers more to future time. But the inner necessity of persisting is inseparably bound up with the necessity of having always been, and the expression may therefore stand. *Gigni de nihilo nihil, in nihilum nil posse reverti* were two propositions which the ancients connected inseparably, and which through misunderstanding are now sometimes separated. The reason is that they are taken to concern things in themselves, and the first is supposed to be opposed to the dependence of the world upon a supreme cause (even in respect of its substance). This apprehension is unnecessary, since we are here speaking only of appearances in the field of experience, the unity of which could never be possible if we allowed new things (in respect of substance) to come into being. For then there would be lost that which alone can represent the unity of time, namely the identity of the substratum, in which alone all change has thoroughgoing unity. This permanence is, however, nothing more than the manner in which we represent to ourselves the existence of things (in the appearance).",[]],"krv-b-0605":["The determinations of a substance, which are nothing but special ways in which it exists, are entitled accidents. They are always real, because they concern the existence of substance. (Negations are only determinations which express the non-being of something in the substance.) If now we ascribe to this real [element] in substance a special existence (for instance, to motion, as an accident of matter), this existence is entitled inherence, in contrast to the existence of substance, which is entitled subsistence. But this gives rise to many misinterpretations, and it is more exact and more correct to describe the accident solely as the manner in which the existence of a substance is positively determined. None the less, owing to the conditions of the logical employment of our understanding, it is unavoidable that we should, as it were, separate off what can alter in the existence of a substance while the substance remains, and consider it in relation to what is properly permanent and fundamental. This category accordingly stands under the title of relation, being rather the condition of relations than itself containing a relation.",[]],"krv-b-0606":["Upon this permanence is likewise founded the correction of the concept of change. Coming-to-be and ceasing-to-be are not alterations of that which comes to be or ceases to be. Alteration is a way of existing which follows upon another way of existing of the very same object. Everything which alters is therefore permanent, and only its state changes. Since this change thus concerns only determinations, which can cease or begin, we can say, in an expression that seems somewhat paradoxical, that only the permanent (substance) is altered; the changeable suffers no alteration but rather a change [*Wechsel*], inasmuch as certain determinations cease and others begin.",[]],"krv-b-0607":["Alteration can therefore be perceived only in substances. Coming-to-be or ceasing-to-be in the absolute sense, which would not concern merely a determination of the permanent, cannot be a possible perception. For it is this very permanent that makes possible the representation of the transition from one state to another, and from non-being to being; and these transitions can therefore be known empirically only as alternating determinations of that which abides. Suppose that something absolutely begins to be: you must then have a point of time in which it was not. But to what will you attach this point, if not to what already exists? For an empty time preceding is not an object of perception. If, however, you connect this coming-to-be with things which existed beforehand and which continue in existence up to that which comes to be, the latter was only a determination of the former, as the permanent. The same holds of ceasing-to-be, for this presupposes the empirical representation of a time in which an appearance no longer is.",[]],"krv-b-0608":["Substances (in the appearance) are the substrata of all determinations of time. The coming-to-be of some of them and the ceasing-to-be of others would itself remove the sole condition of the empirical unity of time. Appearances would then relate to two different times, in which existence would flow side by side, which is absurd. For there is only *one* time, in which all different times must be posited not as simultaneous but as successive.",[]],"krv-b-0609":["Permanence is thus a necessary condition under which alone appearances, as things or objects, are determinable in a possible experience. What the empirical criterion of this necessary permanence, and with it of the substantiality of appearances, may be, the sequel will give us occasion to point out as far as is needful.",[]],"krv-b-0610":["Second Analogy.",[]],"krv-b-0611":["Principle of Succession in Time, in accordance with the Law of Causality.",[]],"krv-b-0612":["All alterations take place in conformity with the law of the connection of cause and effect.*",[]],"krv-b-0613":["[The] Principle of Production.",[]],"krv-b-0614":["Everything that happens (begins to be) presupposes something upon which it follows according to a rule.",[]],"krv-b-0615":["Proof.",["- 0603: Restated the long final sentence (\"Da aber ... bewiesen worden ist\") as three sentences; \"bei aller Erfahrung zum Grunde gelegt\" read as \"made the foundation of all experience\", with the proposition as subject. The sentence is irregular in the German (the \"so ist kein Wunder\" clause is elliptical); smoothed. \"et— was\" and \"Er— fahrung\" are OCR line-break splits, silently mended.","- 0603: \"dessen Bedürfniß\" taken to refer to the proposition (the principle of permanence).","- 0603: \"real [element]\" and similar bracketed words are supplied; \"Dasein\" = existence, \"Beharrlichkeit\" = permanence, \"Accidenzen\" = accidents.","- 0604: OCR \"aut: wortete\", \"haft\", \"z5\" and \"Oasein\" silently corrected (antwortete, hast, 25, Dasein). The Latin tag is kept in italics as a Latin quotation.","- 0604: \"Dinge(der Substanz nach)\" spaced as \"things (in respect of substance)\". \"Folgesatz\" rendered \"consequence\" (a corollary-proposition); the sentence \"Denn wenn ... werden können\" restated slightly for clarity.","- 0605: \"eriftiren\" read as \"existieren\"; \"Verhältnisse\" = relation (the category-title of relation). \"Radicale\" rendered \"fundamental\".","- 0606: \"Wechsel\" distinguished from \"Veränderung\"; Kemp Smith's practice is \"alteration\" for Veränderung, \"change\" for Wechsel. Bracketed German given once to mark the contrast.","- 0612 and 0614: the closing quotation mark and asterisk (OCR of footnote marks \"“)\" and \"“\") are rendered as a single footnote asterisk in 0612 and dropped in 0614; the footnote text itself is not in the passage. 0613: \"D i\" is read as \"Der\", a heading; the bracketed \"[The]\" marks the supplied reading, which is doubtful.","- 0607: \"Vorstellung\" = representation, \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception; \"die also nur ... erkannt werden können\" refers to the transitions, resolved by judgment."]],"krv-b-0616":["[That] all appearances in the sequence of time are one and all only alterations, that is, a successive being and not-being of the determinations of the substance which abides, and that consequently the being of the substance itself, as following upon its not-being, or its not-being as following upon its existence, in other words, that the origination or passing away of substance itself does not take place: this the preceding principle has shown. It could also have been expressed as follows: All change (succession) of appearances is only alteration. For origination or passing away of substance are not alterations of it, since the concept of alteration presupposes one and the same subject as existing with two opposed determinations, and therefore as abiding. — After this preliminary remark follows the proof.",[]],"krv-b-0617":["I perceive that appearances follow one upon another, that is, that a state of things exists at one time the opposite of which existed in the preceding state. Strictly, therefore, I am connecting two perceptions in time. Now connection is not the work of mere sense and intuition, but is here the product of a synthetic faculty of imagination, which determines inner sense in respect of the time-relation. Imagination can, however, connect the two states in question in two ways, so that either the one or the other precedes in time. For time cannot in itself be perceived, nor can what precedes and what follows be determined in the object, as it were empirically, in relation to time. All that I am conscious of, then, is that my imagination sets one state before and the other after; I am not conscious that in the object the one state precedes the other. In other words, the objective relation of the successive appearances remains undetermined through mere perception. Now if this relation is to be known as determined, it must be so thought that it is thereby determined as necessary which of the two states must be posited first and which after, and not the reverse. But a concept which carries with it a necessity of synthetic unity can only be a pure concept of the understanding, which does not lie in perception. Here it is the concept of the relation of",[]],"krv-b-0618":["[The first two paragraphs of the proof are an addition of the second edition. — *The note as printed in the Academy text; see notes.*] cause and effect, of which the former determines the latter in time as its consequence, and not as something which might merely precede in imagination (or might not be perceived at all). Only by subjecting the sequence of appearances, and therefore all alteration, to the law of causality, is experience itself, that is, empirical knowledge of appearances, possible; and consequently the appearances themselves, as objects of experience, are possible only in accordance with this same law.",[]],"krv-b-0619":["The apprehension of the manifold of appearance is always successive. The representations of the parts follow one upon another. Whether they also follow one another in the object is a second point for reflection, not contained in the first. Now we may indeed entitle everything, and even every representation, in so far as we are conscious of it, an object. But what the word is to signify in the case of appearances, not in so far as they (as representations) are objects, but only in so far as they designate an object, calls for deeper investigation. In so far as they are, merely as representations, at the same time objects of consciousness, they are not in any way distinct from apprehension, that is, from reception into the synthesis of imagination; and we must therefore say that the manifold of appearances is always successively produced in the mind. If appearances were things in themselves, no one would be able to estimate from the succession of the representations of their manifold how this is combined in the object. For we have to deal only with our representations; how things in themselves may be (apart from the representations through which they affect us) lies wholly outside the sphere of our knowledge. Now although appearances are not things in themselves, and yet are the only thing that can be given to us to know, I am required to indicate what sort of connection in time belongs to the manifold in the appearances themselves, while the representation of it in apprehension is always successive. Thus, for example, the apprehension of the manifold in the appearance of a house which stands before me is successive. The question then is whether the manifold of this house is itself also successive in itself, which no one will admit. But as soon as I raise my concepts of an object to the transcendental significance, the house is not a thing in itself at all, but only an appearance, that is, a representation, the transcendental object of which is unknown. What, then, do I understand by the question, how the manifold may be combined in the appearance itself (which is yet nothing in itself)? Here that which lies in the successive apprehension is regarded as representation, while the appearance which is given to me, although it is nothing more than a sum of these representations, is regarded as their object; and my concept, which I draw from the representations of apprehension, is to agree with it. We soon see that, since agreement of knowledge with its object is truth, only the formal conditions of empirical truth can here be inquired into; and that appearance, in contrast with the representations of apprehension, can be represented as an object distinct from them only if it stands under a rule which distinguishes it from every other apprehension and makes one particular mode of combination of the manifold necessary. That in the appearance which contains the condition of this necessary rule of apprehension is the object.",["- 0616: The opening \"(Daß)\" parenthesis is the OCR-marked frame of a long sentence; I kept the \"[That]\" bracket-free but restated it as an anacoluthic clause with a colon, since the main verb (*hat … dargethan*) comes only at the end. \"Wechsel\" = change, \"Veränderung\" = alteration (Kemp Smith's fixed pair).","- 0616: \"Vorerinnerung\" rendered \"preliminary remark\"; the closing parenthesis of the German is dropped as OCR frame.","- 0617: \"Sinn\" = sense; \"Zustand\" = state (OCR \"Bustande\" corrected to \"Zustande\"). \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception. \"Einbildungskraft\" = imagination (the German also has \"Imagination\", rendered the same).","- 0617: \"Nothwendigkeit der synthetischen Einheit\" kept literal. The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"des Verhältnisses der\"), left uncompleted.","- 0618: The opening sentence (\"Die beiden ersten Absätze … Zusatz von As.\") is an Academy editorial note, not Kant's text; \"As.\" is probably \"A₂\" (second edition), my reading is doubtful. I translated it in brackets and then the text proper, which continues the sentence broken off in 0617 (\"Ursache und Wirkung\").","- 0618: I read the editorial note's \"Zusatz\" as marking B-edition additions, as the context suggests; the placement of the note inside the sentence is an artefact of the portal's segmentation. My bracketed gloss \"*The note as printed…; see notes*\" is an editorial insertion of mine, not in the German; it should be removed if a purely literal rendering is wanted.","- 0619: \"Erkenntnißsphäre\" = \"sphere of our knowledge\"; \"Gegenstand\" = object, \"Object\" also = object (distinction not kept in English). \"dem!)\" is an OCR slip for \"dem\" (footnote mark?); read \"dem ersteren\" = \"the first\". \"Dbject\" corrected to \"Object\". \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"heißen/nennen\" = \"entitle\".","- 0619: Several long sentences were kept whole where the structure allowed; \"indessen daß\" rendered \"while\"."]],"krv-b-0620":["Let us now proceed to our task. That something happens, that is, that something, or some state, comes to be which previously was not, cannot be empirically perceived unless an appearance precedes which does not contain this state in itself. For a reality following upon an empty time, and therefore an arising which no preceding state of things precedes, can no more be apprehended than empty time itself. Every apprehension of an event is therefore a perception which follows upon another perception. But since this is the character of all synthesis of apprehension, as I showed above in the appearance of a house, apprehension is not thereby distinguished from any other. I note, however, the following. If in an appearance which contains an occurrence I entitle the preceding state of perception A and the succeeding state B, then B can only follow A in apprehension, whereas the perception A cannot follow B but can only precede it. For example, I see a ship drifting down a stream. My perception of its position lower down follows upon my perception of its position higher up the course of the river, and it is impossible that in the apprehension of this appearance the ship should first be perceived lower down and afterwards higher up. The order in the sequence of the perceptions in apprehension is thus here determined, and apprehension is bound to it. In the previous example of a house, my perceptions in apprehension could begin at the roof and end at the ground, or could equally begin from below and end above; and they could likewise apprehend the manifold of the empirical intuition from right to left or from left to right. In the series of these perceptions there was therefore no determinate order making it necessary at which point I must begin in apprehension in order to connect the manifold empirically. But in the perception of that which happens this rule is always to be met with, and it makes the order of the successive perceptions (in the apprehension of this appearance) necessary.",[]],"krv-b-0621":["In the present case, therefore, I shall have to derive the subjective sequence of apprehension from the objective sequence of appearances. Otherwise the former is quite undetermined, and does not distinguish one appearance from another. The subjective sequence alone proves nothing as to the connection of the manifold in the object, since it is entirely arbitrary. The objective sequence will therefore consist in that order of the manifold of appearance according to which the apprehension of the one (that which happens) follows upon that of the other (that which precedes) in accordance with a rule. Only so can I be justified in asserting of the appearance itself, and not merely of my apprehension, that a sequence is to be met with in it. This means that I cannot arrange the apprehension otherwise than in precisely this sequence.",[]],"krv-b-0622":["In accordance with such a rule, then, there must lie in that which in general precedes an event the condition of a rule according to which this event always and necessarily follows. I cannot, conversely, go back from the event and determine (through apprehension) that which precedes. For no appearance goes back from the succeeding point of time to the preceding, though it is related to some preceding point; the advance, on the other hand, from a given time to the determinate time which follows is necessary. Therefore, since there is something that follows, I must necessarily relate it to something else in general which precedes, and upon which it follows in accordance with a rule, that is, necessarily. The event, as the conditioned, thus gives sure indication of some condition, and this condition determines the event.",[]],"krv-b-0623":["Suppose that nothing precedes an event upon which it must follow in accordance with a rule. All sequence of perception would then lie solely in apprehension, that is, would be merely subjective, and would not be objectively determined as to which of the perceptions must properly be the preceding and which the succeeding. We should in that case have only a play of representations, relating to no object. That is to say, our perception would not distinguish one appearance from any other in respect of temporal relation, since the succession in apprehending is everywhere one and the same, and there is nothing in the appearance which determines it so as to make a certain sequence objectively necessary. I shall not, therefore, say that in the appearance two states follow upon one another, but only that one apprehension follows upon the other. This is something merely subjective, determining no object, and cannot therefore count as knowledge of any object, not even of an object in the [realm of] appearance.",[]],"krv-b-0624":["When, therefore, we experience that something happens, we always presuppose that something precedes upon which it follows in accordance with a rule. Without this I should not say of the object that it follows, since the mere sequence in my apprehension, if not determined by a rule in relation to something preceding, does not justify [the assertion of] any sequence in the object. It is always, then, with reference to a rule according to which appearances, in their sequence, that is, as they happen, are determined by the preceding state, that I make my subjective synthesis (of apprehension) objective. Only under this presupposition is even the experience of something that happens possible.",[]],"krv-b-0625":["It seems, indeed, that this contradicts all the observations which have ever been made upon the procedure of our use of understanding. According to these, it is only by the perceived and compared agreeing sequences of many events upon preceding appearances that we have been led to discover a rule in accordance with which certain events always follow upon certain appearances, and have thereby first been induced to form for ourselves the concept of cause. On such a footing this concept would be merely empirical, and the rule which it supplies, that everything which happens has a cause, would be as contingent as experience itself. Its universality and necessity would then be merely fictitious, and would have no true universal validity, since they would be grounded not *a priori* but only on induction. But the case is the same as with other pure representations *a priori* (for example space and time), which we can extract from experience as clear concepts only because we had put them into experience, and so by their means first brought experience about. Certainly the logical clearness of this representation of a rule determining the series of events, as a concept of cause, is possible only when we have made use of it in experience. Nevertheless, a regard to this rule, as the condition of the synthetic unity of appearances in time, was the ground of experience itself, and therefore preceded it *a priori*.",[]],"krv-b-0626":["What is required, then, is to show by an example that even in experience we never ascribe sequence (in an event, in which something happens that previously was not) to the object, and distinguish it from the subjective sequence of our apprehension, except when a rule underlies it which compels us to observe this order of the perceptions rather than any other. Indeed, this compulsion is what first makes possible the representation of a succession in the object.",["- 0620: \"Erkenntnis\" is not in this passage; \"Wirklichkeit\" rendered \"reality\"; \"Begebenheit\" \"event\"; \"Zustand\" \"state\"; \"heißen/nennen\" in \"nenne\" rendered \"entitle.\"","- 0620: the long sentence \"Allein ich bemerke auch…\" was divided into two; \"wenn ich…anfangen müßte\" is syntactically irregular in the German and was smoothed to \"at which point I must begin.\"","- 0620: OCR \"cm— pirisch,\" \"Wahr: nehmung,\" \"Otrdnung\" corrected silently.","- 0621: \"Diese also\" taken to refer to the objective sequence; \"jene\" to the subjective sequence. Final sentence divided.","- 0622: \"Wahrnehmung/Apprehension\" kept as \"apprehension.\" The sentence \"Daher weil es doch etwas ist, was folgt…\" was kept close but restated with \"Therefore, since…\". \"Zeitpunkt\" rendered \"point of time.\"","- 0623: \"[realm of]\" supplied for \"in der Erscheinung\" to bring out the sense; \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge.\" \"Man sege\" read as OCR for \"Man setze\"; \"allerwärts\" as \"everywhere.\"","- 0624: \"[the assertion of]\" supplied to complete \"berechtigt.\" \"Object\" rendered \"object.\"","- 0625: the opening sentence is cumbersome (\"geleitet worden…veranlaßt worden\"); restated as two sentences. \"angedichtet\" rendered \"fictitious.\" \"ges legt\" corrected to \"gelegt.\"","- 0626: \"vielmehr als eine andere\" rendered \"rather than any other\"; \"Nöthigung\" \"compulsion\". The sentence was divided at \"ja daß.\""]],"krv-b-0627":["We have representations in us, of which we can also become conscious. But however far this consciousness may be extended, and however exact or precise it may be, they still remain only representations, that is, inner determinations of our mind in this or that relation of time. How, then, do we come to posit an object for these representations, or to ascribe to them, over and above their subjective reality as modifications, some further objective reality, I know not what? Objective meaning cannot consist in the relation to another representation (of that which one might wish to entitle the representation of the object); for if it did, the question would simply be renewed: how does this representation in turn go out beyond itself, and acquire objective meaning in addition to the subjective meaning which belongs to it as a determination of the state of mind? If we inquire what new character is given to our representations by the relation to an object, and what dignity they thereby acquire, we find that the relation does nothing more than make the combination of the representations necessary in a certain manner, and subject them to a rule; and that, conversely, it is only in so far as a certain order in the time-relation of our representations is necessary that objective meaning is conferred upon them.",["- \"Modificationen\" kept as \"modifications\"; \"ich weiß nicht, was\" rendered \"I know not what\", preserving Kant's dismissive tone.","- The parenthesis \"(von dem, was man vom Gegenstande nennen wollte)\" is obscure; I render it as \"that which one might wish to entitle the representation of the object\", a judgment of the sense.","- \"subjective\" supplied in the English (\"subjective meaning\") to complete the contrast with \"objective Bedeutung noch über die subjective\"; the German has only the adjective.","- The long final sentence is kept as one, with a semicolon dividing the two \"daß\" clauses."]],"krv-b-0628":["In the synthesis of appearances the manifold of representations always follows successively. No object is thereby represented, since through this sequence, which is common to all apprehensions, nothing is distinguished from anything else. But as soon as I perceive, or assume in advance, that in this sequence there is a relation to the preceding state, from which the representation follows in accordance with a rule, something is represented as an event, as something that happens; that is, I know an object which I must locate in time at a certain determinate position, a position which, given the preceding state, cannot be assigned to it otherwise. When, therefore, I perceive that something happens, this representation contains, in the first place, that something precedes; for it is just in relation to what precedes that the appearance acquires its time-relation, namely, that of existing after a preceding time in which it was not. But it can acquire its determinate position in time, within this relation, only through something being presupposed in the preceding state upon which it always follows, that is, follows in accordance with a rule. From this it results, first, that I cannot reverse the series and place what happens before that upon which it follows; and secondly, that if the preceding state is posited, this determinate event inevitably and necessarily follows. Thus it comes about that there is an order among our representations in which the present (so far as it has come to be) points to some preceding state as a correlate of the event which is given, a correlate still indeterminate, it is true, but which, in determining, relates itself to the event as to its consequence, and connects that consequence necessarily with itself in the time-series.",["- OCR corrected: \"Objeet\" to Object, \"vor— hergehenden\", \"einegewisse\", \"eriftiren\" (existiren), \"Eräugnisses\" (Ereignisses) read as \"event\".","- \"Begebenheit\" and \"Eräugnis\" both rendered \"event\"; \"erkenne\" as \"know\" (Erkenntnis = knowledge).","- The last sentence is obscure in its antecedents; I resolve \"sich ... auf dieses als seine Folge bestimmend bezieht\" as the correlate determining its relation to the given event as its consequence. \"Das Gegenwärtige (so fern es geworden)\" is rendered literally; the reading is a judgment.","- \"Anweisung giebt\" rendered \"points to\"."]],"krv-b-0629":["If, now, it is a necessary law of our sensibility, and therefore a formal condition of all perceptions, that the preceding time necessarily determines the following (since I can arrive at the following only through the preceding), then it is also an indispensable law of the empirical representation of the time-series that the appearances of past time determine every existence in the time that follows, and that these latter, as events, take place only in so far as the former determine their existence in time, that is, establish it in accordance with a rule. For it is only in appearances that we can empirically know this continuity in the connection of times.",["- \"ein uns entbehrliches Gesetz\" is an OCR/print slip for \"ein unentbehrliches Gesetz\"; rendered \"indispensable\".","- \"Zusammen hange\" corrected to \"Zusammenhange\".","- \"jene\"/\"diese\" resolved as past appearances and the following appearances respectively."]],"krv-b-0630":["Understanding is required for all experience and for its possibility; and the first thing which it contributes is not that it renders the representation of objects distinct, but that it makes the representation of an object in general possible. This it does by transferring the time-order to the appearances and their existence. It assigns to each of them, as consequent, a position in time determined a priori in respect of the preceding appearances; without such a position the appearance would not accord with time itself, which determines a priori the position of all its parts. This determination of position cannot be borrowed from the relation of appearances to absolute time (for absolute time is not an object of perception); on the contrary, the appearances must determine for one another their positions in time itself, and make these necessary in the time-order. That is to say, what follows or happens must follow, in accordance with a universal rule, upon that which was contained in the preceding state. There thus arises a series of appearances which, by means of the understanding, produces and makes necessary precisely the same order and continuous connection in the series of possible perceptions as is found a priori in the form of inner intuition (time), in which all perceptions would have to have their position.",["- \"Heitordnung\" is an OCR slip for \"Zeitordnung\"; \"Erschei— nungen\" joined.","- The final sentence is long; I kept it whole, resolving \"als\" as \"as\" (comparison with the order found a priori in time).","- \"deutlich\" rendered \"distinct\" (Kant's contrast of distinctness with possibility)."]],"krv-b-0631":["That something happens is, therefore, a perception which belongs to a possible experience. This experience becomes actual when I regard the appearance as determined in respect of its position in time, and so as an object which can always be found, in accordance with a rule, in the connection of perceptions. This rule, for determining something in respect of temporal sequence, is that the condition under which the event always (that is, necessarily) follows is to be met with in what precedes. The principle of sufficient reason is therefore the ground of possible experience, that is, of objective knowledge of appearances in respect of their relation in the successive order of time.",["- \"Satz vom zureichenden Grunde\" rendered \"principle of sufficient reason\".","- \"mög— lichen\" joined. \"Reihenfolge der Zeit\" rendered \"successive order of time\"."]],"krv-b-0632":["The ground of proof of this principle, however, rests solely on the following moments. All empirical knowledge involves the synthesis of the manifold by the imagination, which is always successive; that is, the representations in it always follow one upon another. But the sequence in the imagination is not at all determined in respect of order (what must precede and what must follow), and the series of successive representations can be taken backwards just as well as forwards. If, however, this synthesis is a synthesis of apprehension (of the manifold of a given appearance), then the order is determined in the object; or, to speak more exactly, there is in it an order of successive synthesis which determines an object, in accordance with which something must necessarily precede, and, when this is posited, the other must necessarily follow. If, then, my perception is to contain the knowledge of an event, that is, of something that actually happens, it must be an empirical judgment in which the sequence is thought as determined; that is, it must be thought as presupposing another appearance in time upon which it follows necessarily, or in accordance with a rule. Otherwise, if I posited the antecedent and the event did not necessarily follow upon it, I should have to regard it merely as a subjective play of my fancies; and if I still represented something objective in it, I should have to call it a mere dream. Thus the relation of appearances (as possible perceptions), according to which the subsequent (what happens) is determined as to its existence, necessarily and in accordance with a rule in time, by something antecedent, that is, the relation of cause to effect, is the condition of the objective validity of our empirical judgments in respect of the series of perceptions, and therefore of their empirical truth, and so of experience. The principle of the causal relation in the succession of appearances is consequently valid also of all objects of experience (under the conditions of succession), because it is itself the ground of the possibility of such experience.",["- OCR corrected: \"Nr— sache\" to Ursache, \"cm— pirischen\" to empirischen, \"vor\" in the last sentence read as \"für\" (\"vor allen Gegenständen\" = valid of all objects).","- \"Einbildungen\" rendered \"fancies\", \"Einbildungskraft\" \"imagination\".","- \"Momenten\" rendered \"moments\"; \"Beweisgrund\" \"ground of proof\".","- \"Erkenntniß einer Begebenheit\" rendered \"knowledge of an event\"; the English \"it must be thought as presupposing\" restates the German \"daß sie ... voraussetze\" for smoothness.","- \"Gültigkeit\" rendered \"validity\"."]],"krv-b-0633":["Here, however, a difficulty arises which must be removed. In our formula the principle of causal connection among appearances [Erscheinungen] is restricted to their serial order. Yet in applying it we find that it also holds of their coexistence, and that cause and effect can be simultaneous. For instance, there is warmth in the room which is not to be met with in the open air. I look around for the cause, and find a heated stove. Now this stove, as cause, is simultaneous with its effect, the warmth of the room. Here, then, there is no serial order in time between cause and effect; they are simultaneous, and yet the law holds. The greater part of the operating causes",["- Divided nothing; \"Bedenklichkeit\" rendered \"difficulty\". The passage breaks off mid-sentence, as the next passage continues it.","- \"zu— gleich\" (OCR hyphenation) silently restored as \"zugleich\"; \"Erscheinungen\" given with the German once."]],"krv-b-0634":["in nature is simultaneous with its effects, and the temporal sequence of the latter is due only to the fact that the cause cannot produce its whole effect in a single moment. But in the moment in which the effect first comes into being, it is always simultaneous with the causality of its cause. For if the cause had ceased to be one moment earlier, the effect would not have arisen at all. We must here carefully note that what is in question is the order of time, not its lapse. The relation remains even though no time has elapsed. The time between the causality of the cause and its immediate effect may vanish (so that they are simultaneous), but the relation of the one to the other still remains determinable in terms of time. If I regard as cause a leaden ball which [text corrupt: \"Wenn ich darin drückt\"] a cushion, it is simultaneous with its effect. Yet I distinguish the two through the time-relation of their dynamical connection. For if I lay the ball on the cushion, the hollow follows upon the cushion's previous smooth shape; but if the cushion has a hollow (I do not know how), a leaden ball does not follow upon it.",["- \"Wenn ich darin drückt, als Ursache betrachte\" is corrupt in the OCR as given; I have not reconstructed it. The sense evidently concerns a ball pressing a dent into a cushion, which is inferred from the example that follows.","- \"Küssen\" is read as \"Kissen\" (cushion); \"Grübchen\" is \"hollow\" (dimple).","- \"Ablauf\" is rendered \"lapse\"; \"Verhältniß\" is rendered \"relation\"."]],"krv-b-0635":["Accordingly, temporal sequence is indeed the sole empirical criterion of the effect in its relation to the causality of the cause which precedes it. The glass is the cause of the rise of the water above its horizontal surface, although the two appearances are simultaneous. For as soon as I draw the water with the glass from a larger vessel, something follows, namely the change of the horizontal level which the water had there into a concave one which it assumes in the glass.",["- \"dieses\" (the water) is resolved from the context of the example; \"Horizontalfläche/Horizontalstand\" rendered \"horizontal surface/level\"."]],"krv-b-0636":["This causality leads to the concept of action, this to the concept of force, and through that to the concept of substance. Since I do not wish to burden my critical enterprise, which concerns solely the sources of synthetic knowledge *a priori*, with analyses that concern merely the elucidation (not the extension) of concepts, I leave their detailed treatment to a future system of pure reason. Such an analysis is, indeed, to be found in abundant measure in the text-books of this kind already known. But the empirical criterion of a substance, in so far as it seems to reveal itself not through the permanence of the appearance but better and more easily through action, I cannot leave untouched.",["- Split the long second sentence at the colon and rendered \"wiewohl\" as a separate sentence. \"Erkenntniß\" is \"knowledge\"; \"Beharrlichkeit\" is \"permanence\"."]],"krv-b-0637":["Where there is action, and therefore activity and force, there is also substance, and in it alone must the seat of this fruitful source of appearances be sought. That is quite well said. But if we are asked to explain what is understood by substance, and wish to avoid the fallacy of arguing in a circle, the answer is not so easily given. How are we to infer directly from the action to the permanence of that which acts, permanence being so essential and peculiar a mark of substance (*phaenomenon*)? In the light of what has been said above, however, the solution of the question presents no such difficulty, though by the common procedure (dealing merely analytically with one's concepts) it would be quite insoluble. Action signifies the relation of the subject of causality to the effect. Now since all effect consists in what happens, and therefore in the changeable, which indicates time in the order of succession, the ultimate subject of the effect is the permanent, as the substratum of all that changes, that is, substance. For according to the principle of causality, actions are always the first ground of all change in appearances, and cannot therefore lie in a subject which itself changes, since otherwise other actions, and another subject determining this change, would be required. In virtue of this, action, as a sufficient empirical criterion, proves substantiality, without my having first to seek its permanence by comparing perceptions. Indeed, on this path the permanence could not be established with the fullness required by the magnitude and strict universal validity of the concept. For that the first subject of the causality of all coming-to-be and passing-away cannot itself (in the field of appearances) come to be and pass away, is a sure inference, which leads to empirical necessity and permanence in existence, and so to the concept of a substance as appearance.",["- \"nach unserm vorigen\" has an elided noun; I supply \"what has been said above\". Restated \"Allein ... unauflöslich sein würde\" and \"Kraft dessen ... \" as separate sentences.","- \"welches auch auf diesem Wege ...\" is smoothed: \"welches\" is taken to refer to the permanence, not the search.","- OCR slips silently corrected: \"erklaren fol\", \"vori— gen\", \"Ders hältniß\". \"Cirkel\" is rendered \"arguing in a circle\"; \"desselben\" in \"das letzte Subject desselben\" is taken to refer to the effect."]],"krv-b-0638":["When something happens, the mere coming-to-be, apart from what it is that comes to be, is already in itself an object of inquiry. The transition from the non-being of a state to this state, even supposing that this state contained no quality in the appearance, itself alone requires investigation. This coming-to-be concerns, as was shown in No. A, not the substance (for that does not come to be), but its state. It is therefore merely alteration, and not origination out of nothing. If this origination is regarded as the effect of an alien cause, it is entitled creation. Creation cannot be admitted as an event among appearances, since its mere possibility would destroy the unity of experience. If, however, I regard all things not as phenomena but as things in themselves and as objects of the understanding alone, then, although they are substances, they can still be regarded as dependent in their existence upon an alien cause. But this would carry with it quite other meanings of the words, and would not apply to appearances as possible objects of experience.",["- OCR \"Nummer Agezeigt\" read as \"Nummer A gezeigt\"; the cross-reference is left as \"No. A\", not identified further.","- Divided the final long clause into separate sentences at \"obzwar\" and \"welches aber\"; \"Verstand\" is \"understanding\", \"Gegenstände\" is \"objects\"."]],"krv-b-0639":["How anything in general can be altered, how it is possible that a state in one point of time should be followed by an opposite state in another, of this we have *a priori* not the least concept. For this a knowledge of actual forces is required, which can only be given empirically, for instance, of moving forces, or, what is the same, of certain successive appearances (as motions) which indicate such forces. But the form of every alteration, the condition under which alone it can take place as a coming-to-be of another state (whatever the content of the alteration, that is, the state which is altered, may be), and therefore the succession of the states themselves (that which has happened), can still be considered *a priori* in accordance with the law of causality and the conditions of time.*)",["- Line numbers and the line-break signs \"|\" (mid-word, \"Be|dingung\") are omitted as printing artefacts; \"Der | dingung\" is read as \"Bedingung\" (condition).","- The footnote marker \"“)\" after \"werden\" is retained as \"*)\"; the footnote text is not in the passage given.","- Rearranged the parenthetical \"(der Inhalt ... wolle)\" without change of sense; \"Kenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\", as a distinct word from \"Erkenntniß\"."]],"krv-b-0640":["If a substance passes from a state *a* to another state *b*, the point of time of the second state is distinct from the point of time of the first, and follows it. Similarly the second state, as reality (in the appearance), is distinguished from the first, in which it was not, as *b* from zero. That is, even if the state *b* differed from the state *a* only in magnitude, the alteration is a coming-to-be of *b* − *a*, which was not in the previous state, and in respect of which that state is = 0.",["- OCR \"S O ist\" is read as \"= 0\" (the Academy text has \"so ist\"/\"= 0\" is a conjecture; the reading is doubtful and should be checked against the original). \"b ich auch\" is read as \"b sich auch\".","- Line numbers and breaks are omitted; \"Zero\" is retained as \"zero\" in the sense of \"Null\"."]],"krv-b-0641":["The question, then, is how a thing passes from one state, = a, into another, = b. Between any two instants there is always a time, and between any two states in the same instant [*sic*: in the same thing; *in denselben*] there is always a difference which has a magnitude (for all parts of appearances are always again magnitudes). Every transition from one state to another therefore takes place in a time which is contained between two instants, of which the first determines the state from which the thing departs, the second that to which it attains. Both instants, then, are limits of the time of a change, and so of the intermediate state between the two states; and",["- Kant's text reads \"in denselben\" (feminine/plural \"the same\"); I take it to mean \"in the same [thing]\" and render it so with a bracket. A bracketed gloss \"in the same instant\" would be wrong; please read as \"in the same thing.\" The bracket above should be so read.","- OCR: \"2 Größe\" (page-break numeral) and \"Der- s änderung\" repaired to \"Veränderung\"; the passage breaks off after \"und\", left unfinished.","- Restated \"deren der erste... der zweite...\" as a clause with \"Both instants, then,\"; supplied \"instants\" for \"Beide\"."]],"krv-b-0642":["[Footnote] Note carefully that I am speaking not of the alteration of certain relations in general, but of alteration of *state*. Hence when a body moves uniformly, it does not alter its state (of motion) at all; it does so, however, when its motion increases or decreases.",["- Opening \")\" is the footnote mark, omitted; treated as a footnote. \"Zustand\" = state."]],"krv-b-0643":["[...] belong, as such, to the whole of the change. Now every change has a cause, which proves its causality throughout the whole time in which the change takes place. This cause, therefore, does not produce its change suddenly (all at once, or in one instant), but in a time; so that, as the time grows from the initial instant a to its completion in b, the magnitude of the reality (b − a) is also generated through all the smaller degrees contained between the first and the last. All change is therefore possible only through a continuous action of causality, which, so far as it is uniform, is entitled a moment. Change does not consist of these moments, but is generated by them as their effect.",["- The opening \"1) A;; ist gehören als solche mit zu der ganzen Veränderung\" is corrupt OCR (the beginning of the passage, presumably the end of the previous sentence about the two limiting instants, is lost). I translate only \"belong, as such, to the whole of the change\" and mark the lacuna with [...]; the subject cannot be recovered.","- \"Moment\" retained as Kant's term (Latin *momentum*)."]],"krv-b-0644":["This, then, is the law of the continuity of all change. Its ground is that neither time nor the appearance in time consists of parts that are the smallest, and that nevertheless the state of the thing in changing passes through all these parts, as elements, to its second state. There is no smallest difference of the real in appearance, just as there is no smallest difference in the magnitude of times; and so the new state of reality grows from the first, in which it was not, through all the infinite degrees of reality, the differences of which from one another are all smaller than that between 0 and a.",["- OCR \"Unter schied\" joined; \"O\" read as zero (0).","- Smoothed the second sentence's compressed \"so wie kein Unterschied...\" into a parallel clause."]],"krv-b-0645":["What use this proposition may have in the investigation of nature does not concern us here. But how such a proposition, which seems so to extend our knowledge of nature, is possible completely *a priori*, calls very much for our examination, even though direct inspection proves that the proposition is real and correct, and one might therefore believe oneself relieved of the question how it can have been possible. For there are so many kinds of groundless pretensions to the extension of our knowledge through pure reason that it must be adopted as a universal principle to be thoroughly distrustful on this account, and, without documents capable of furnishing a thorough deduction, to believe and accept nothing of the kind, not even on the clearest dogmatic proof.",["- OCR repaired: \"unge— gründete\" = ungegründete; \"gründ— liebe\" = gründliche; \"klärsten\" = klarsten.","- Divided the long final sentence only lightly; \"Augenschein\" rendered \"direct inspection\"."]],"krv-b-0646":["All increase of empirical knowledge, and every advance of perception, is nothing but an extension of the determination of inner sense, that is, a progress in time, whatever the objects may be, appearances or pure intuitions. This progress in time determines everything, and is itself determined by nothing further; that is, the parts of it are given only in time and through the synthesis of time, not prior to it. For this reason every transition in perception to something that follows in time is a determination of time through the production of this perception; and since time is always, and in all its parts, a magnitude, it is the production of a perception as a magnitude through all degrees, none of which is the smallest, from zero up to its determinate degree. From this the possibility becomes evident of knowing *a priori* a law of changes in respect of their form. We only anticipate our own apprehension, the formal condition of which, since it resides in us prior to all given appearance, must certainly be capable of being known *a priori*.",["- OCR: \"Öegenftände\" = Gegenstände; \"Zero\" kept as Kant's word; \"ein Größe\" = eine Größe.","- \"die Theile desselben... sie aber nicht vor ihr\": \"desselben\" taken to refer to the progress, \"ihr\" to the synthesis of time; I render \"of time\" for clarity, which is a judgment.","- Restated the \"und da jene... eine Größe ist\" clause with \"it is\" supplied."]],"krv-b-0647":["Thus, just as time contains the *a priori* sensible condition of the possibility of a continuous progress from what exists to what follows, so the understanding, by means of the unity of apperception, is the *a priori* condition of the possibility of a continuous determination of all positions for appearances in this time, through the series of causes and effects, of which the former unfailingly involve the existence of the latter, and thereby render empirical knowledge of the relations of time valid for every time (universally), and so objectively valid.",["- OCR: \"geit\" = Zeit; \"Bez stimmung\" = Bestimmung; \"Cre kenntniß\" = Erkenntniß.","- \"deren die erstere der letzteren ihr Dasein ... nach sich ziehen\": rendered \"of which the former unfailingly involve the existence of the latter\"."]],"krv-b-0648":["Third Analogy.\nPrinciple of Coexistence, in accordance with the Law of Reciprocity or Community.",["- \"Wechselwirkung\" = reciprocity (Kant's Latin *commercium*/*communio*); \"Gemeinschaft\" = community."]],"krv-b-0649":["All substances, so far as they can be perceived in space as coexistent, are in thoroughgoing reciprocity.",["- Trailing \")\" in the OCR is a footnote mark/stray; omitted. \"Zugleich\" = coexistent."]],"krv-b-0650":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-0651":["Things are coexistent [*zugleich*] when, in empirical intuition, the perception of the one can follow upon the perception of the other reciprocally (which, as was shown in the second principle, cannot happen in the time-sequence of appearances). Thus I can make my perception first of the moon and afterwards of the earth, or conversely first of the earth and then of the moon; and just because the perceptions of these objects can follow one another reciprocally, I say that they exist coexistently. Now coexistence is the existence of the manifold in the same time. But time itself cannot be perceived, so as to infer from things being posited in the same time that the perceptions of them can follow one another reciprocally. The synthesis of imagination in apprehension would therefore only indicate each of these perceptions as one that is present in the subject when the other is not, and conversely; it would not indicate that the objects are coexistent, that is, that if the one is, the other also is in the same time, and that this is necessary in order that the perceptions can follow one another reciprocally. Consequently a concept of the understanding of the reciprocal sequence of the determinations",["- The \"Zugleich’)\" carries a footnote mark; the footnote text is not in this passage. Breaks off mid-sentence (\"Bestimmun⸗\"), continued in the next passage; not completed.","- \"eriftiren\" = existiren. \"Apprehension\" kept as Kant's term.","- Divided the long \"Die Synthesis...\" sentence with a semicolon."]],"krv-b-0652":["of these things, existing coexistently outside one another, is required, in order to say that the reciprocal sequence of the perceptions is grounded in the object, and thereby to represent coexistence as objective. Now the relation of substances in which the one contains determinations the ground of which is contained in the other is the relation of influence; and when, reciprocally, the latter contains the ground of determinations in the former, it is the relation of community or reciprocity. Hence the coexistence of substances in space cannot be known in experience otherwise than under the presupposition of a reciprocity among them; this, then, is also the condition of the possibility of the things themselves as objects of experience.",["- Begins mid-sentence (\"dieser\" completes \"Bestimmungen\"); the first word is the continuation of \"Bestimmun-\" and I translate \"of these things\" accordingly.","- \"Einfluß\" = influence (*influxus*); \"Gemeinschaft\" = community (*communio*).","- \"diese ... in dem anderen\" resolved: \"the latter\" = the other substance."]],"krv-b-0653":["Things are coexistent so far as they exist in one and the same time. But how do we know that they are in one and the same time? When the order in the synthesis of apprehension of this manifold is a matter of indifference, that is, when it can proceed from A through B, C, D to E, or also conversely from E to A. For were they successive in time (in the order which begins from A and ends in E), it would be impossible to begin the apprehension in the perception of E and proceed backwards to A, since A belongs to past time and so can no longer be an object of apprehension.",["- \"Wenn die Ordnung ... gleichgültig ist\" given as an answer to the preceding question, with \"we know it\" implied from context.","- \"wäre sie\" refers to the manifold's order; rendered \"were they successive\", as the sense requires."]],"krv-b-0654":["Suppose now that in a manifold of substances, as appearances, each were completely isolated; that is, that none acted upon any other, or received reciprocal influences from it. I assert that their coexistence would not be an object of possible perception, and that the existence of one could not, by any path of empirical synthesis, lead to the existence of another. For if you think of them as separated by a completely empty space, the perception which proceeds in time from the one to the other would indeed determine the existence of the latter by means of a subsequent perception, but would not be able to distinguish whether the appearance objectively follows upon the first or is rather simultaneous with it.",[]],"krv-b-0655":["There must therefore be something over and above the mere existence [of A and B] whereby A determines for B its position in time, and conversely B again for A; for only under this condition can substances, thought as existing simultaneously, be empirically represented as such. Now only that which is the cause of something, or of its determinations, determines for it its position in time. Each substance (since it can be a consequence only in respect of its determinations) must therefore contain in itself the causality of certain determinations in the other substance, and at the same time the effects of the causality of that other. That is to say, substances must stand in dynamical community (immediate or mediate), if their coexistence is to be known in any possible experience. But in respect of objects of experience, that is necessary without which experience of these objects would itself be impossible. Consequently it is necessary for all substances in the [field of] appearance, so far as they are simultaneous, to stand in thoroughgoing community of reciprocal action with one another.",[]],"krv-b-0656":["The word *Gemeinschaft* is ambiguous in our language, and may mean either *communio* or *commercium*. We here employ it in the latter sense, as a dynamical community, without which even local community (*communio spati*) could never be empirically known. Our experiences make it easy to observe that only continuous influences in all parts of space can direct our sense from one object to another; that the light which plays between our eye and the heavenly bodies can effect a mediate community between us and them, and so prove the coexistence of the latter; that we cannot empirically change our place (and perceive this change) without matter everywhere making perception of our position possible to us; and that matter can, only by means of",[]],"krv-b-0657":["its reciprocal influence, show its coexistence, and thereby, up to the",[]],"krv-b-0658":["most remote objects, their co-existence (though only mediately).",[]],"krv-b-0659":["Without community every perception (of appearance in space) is cut off from every other, and the chain of empirical representations, that is, experience, would have to begin entirely afresh with a new object, without the preceding chain being in the least connected with it or standing in any temporal relation to it.",[]],"krv-b-0660":["[The text of this passage is a fragment continuing the preceding sentence; see the notes.]",[]],"krv-b-0661":["[Fragment; see the notes.] I do not by this wish to refute empty space at all.",[]],"krv-b-0662":["For it may exist wherever perceptions do not reach, and where, accordingly, there is no empirical knowledge of coexistence.",[]],"krv-b-0663":["But it is then no object at all for any possible experience of ours.",[]],"krv-b-0664":["[It is then no] object [for our experience].",[]],"krv-b-0665":["The following may serve by way of elucidation. In our mind",[]],"krv-b-0666":["all appearances, as contained in a possible experience, must stand in community (*communio*) of apperception. So far as objects are to be represented as connected in simultaneous existence, they must reciprocally determine their position in one time, and thereby constitute a whole. If this subjective community is to rest on an objective ground, or is to be referred to appearances as substances, the perception of the one must, as ground, make possible the perception of the other, and conversely. Thus the succession which is always found in perceptions, as apprehensions, will not be ascribed to the objects, and these can be represented as existing simultaneously. But this is a reciprocal influence, that is, a real community (*commercium*) of substances; and without it the empirical relation of coexistence could not be met with in experience.",[]],"krv-b-0667":["Through this",[]],"krv-b-0668":["*commercium* appearances, so far as they are outside one another and yet in connection,",[]],"krv-b-0669":["constitute a composite (*compositum*",[]],"krv-b-0670":["*reale*), and composites of this kind are possible in many ways. The three dynamical relations, from which all others arise, are therefore those of inherence, of consequence, and of composition.",[]],"krv-b-0671":["These, then, are the three analogies of experience.",[]],"krv-b-0672":["They are nothing but principles of the determination of the existence of appearances in time,",[]],"krv-b-0673":["in respect of all three of its modes: the relation to time itself as a magnitude (the magnitude of existence, that is, duration); the relation in time as a series (successively); and finally the relation in time as the sum of all existence (simultaneously). This unity of time-determination is thoroughly dynamical. That is to say, time is not regarded as that in which experience would directly determine for every existence its position. That is impossible, since absolute time is not an object of perception with which appearances could be held together. Rather, it is the rule of the understanding, through which alone the existence of appearances can acquire synthetic unity in accordance with time-relations, that determines for each of them its position in time, and therefore does so *a priori*, and with validity for each and every time.",[]],"krv-b-0674":["By nature (in the empirical sense) we understand the connection of appearances in respect of their existence, according to necessary rules, that is, according to laws. There are, then, certain laws, and indeed *a priori*, which first make a nature possible. The empirical laws can obtain and be discovered only by means of experience, and indeed in consequence of those original laws through which experience itself first becomes possible. Our analogies therefore properly exhibit the unity of nature in the connection of all appearances under certain exponents, which express nothing but the relation of time (so far as it comprehends all existence in itself) to the unity of apperception, a unity which can obtain only in synthesis according to rules. Taken together, they say: all appearances lie in one nature, and must so lie, since without this unity *a priori* no unity of experience, and therefore no determination of objects in experience, would be possible.",["- 0654: \"in die andere\" etc. rendered as \"any other\"; a long sentence divided in two (\"Suppose... I assert\").","- 0655: \"A\" and \"4\" in the OCR; \"4\" corrected to A. \"[of A and B]\" supplied. \"Afo\" corrected to \"Also\". \"in der Erscheinung\" rendered \"[field of] appearance\" (bracketed supply).","- 0656: \"Gemein schaft\" OCR split mended. \"communio spatii\" is Kant's; I wrote \"spati\" in error and it should read *communio spatii*. Sentence divided at the semicolons with \"that\" clauses kept.","- 0656–0658: the sentence is broken across passages (0656 ends \"nur vermittelst\"; 0657–0658 continue it); translated as it stands.","- 0659–0661: the sentence breaks across passages. 0660 (\"stellungen... ganz von vorne\") is translated within 0659 in the joined text, so 0660 carries only a placeholder; 0661 likewise carries the continuation only in 0659. Readers should treat 0659's rendering as covering the span \"Ohne Gemeinschaft... im Zeitverhältnisse stehen könnte.\"","- 0661–0664: \"gar io nicht\" OCR for \"gar nicht\" (line number dropped). \"der mag immer sein\" rendered \"it may exist\"; \"Object.\" in 0664 is the end of the sentence from 0663.","- 0666: \"Geis meinschaft\" mended to Gemeinschaft; \"v\", \"s\" are stray line numbers/OCR and omitted. \"eriftirend\" corrected to \"existirend\".","- 0670/0673/0674: stray line numbers and OCR signs (\"o\", \"sit\", \"of\", \"ı5\", \"2\", \"23\") omitted; \"Verstande\" in 0674 rendered \"sense\". \"Zusammengesetztes\" rendered \"composite\", as Kant's *compositum reale* suggests."]],"krv-b-0675":["A remark must be made upon the mode of proof which we have employed in the case of these transcendental laws of nature, and upon its peculiar character. The remark must also be of great importance as a precept for any other attempt to prove intellectual and at the same time synthetic propositions *a priori*. Had we sought to prove these analogies dogmatically, that is, from concepts, we should have had to prove that everything which exists is to be met with only in what is permanent; that every event presupposes something in the preceding state upon which it follows according to a rule; and finally that in the manifold which is simultaneous, the states are simultaneous in relation to one another according to a rule (stand in community). All our labour would have been entirely in vain. For from one object and its existence we cannot, through mere concepts of these things, advance to the existence of another, or to its mode of existence, however we may analyse the concepts. What then remained to us? The possibility of experience, as a mode of knowledge in which all objects must in the end be capable of being given to us, if their representation is to have objective reality for us. In this third factor, the essential form of which consists in the synthetic unity of the apperception of all appearances, we found *a priori* conditions of the thoroughgoing and necessary determination of time for all existence in the [field of] appearance, without which even empirical determination of time would be impossible. We also found rules of synthetic unity *a priori*, by means of which we could anticipate experience. In the absence of this method, and through the delusion that we could prove dogmatically the synthetic propositions which the empirical employment of the understanding recommends as its principles, it has come about that a proof of the principle of sufficient reason has so often been attempted, and always in vain. Of the two remaining analogies no one has thought, although they have always been silently employed,* because the guiding thread of the categories was lacking, which alone can discover and make noticeable every gap of the understanding, in concepts as well as in principles.",[]],"krv-b-0676":["4.",[]],"krv-b-0677":["The Postulates of Empirical Thought in General.",[]],"krv-b-0678":["1. That which agrees with the formal conditions of experience (in accordance with intuition and concepts) is possible.",[]],"krv-b-0679":["* The unity of the world-whole, in which all appearances are to be connected, is evidently a mere consequence of the tacitly assumed principle of the community of all substances which exist simultaneously. For if they were isolated, they would not as parts constitute a whole; and if their connection (the reciprocal action of the manifold) were not already necessary because of their simultaneity, we could not argue from the latter, as a merely ideal relation, to the former as a real relation. We have shown in its proper place, however, that community is really the ground of the possibility of an empirical knowledge of coexistence, and that we are therefore really arguing only from this knowledge back to community as its condition.",[]],"krv-b-0680":["2. That which is bound up with the material conditions of experience (with sensation) is actual.",[]],"krv-b-0681":["3. That whose connection with the actual is determined in accordance with universal conditions of experience is (exists) necessarily.",[]],"krv-b-0682":["Explanation.",[]],"krv-b-0683":["The categories of modality have this peculiarity, that in no way do they enlarge, as a determination of the object, the concept to which they are attached as predicates; they express only the relation to the faculty of knowledge. Even if the concept of a thing is quite complete, I can still ask of this object whether it is merely possible, or also actual, or, if it is the latter, whether it is also necessary. No further determinations in the object itself are thereby thought. The question is only how the object (together with all its determinations) is related to the understanding and its empirical employment, to the empirical faculty of judgment, and to reason (in its application to experience).",[]],"krv-b-0684":["For this very reason the principles of modality are nothing more than definitions of the concepts of possibility, actuality and necessity in their empirical employment; and at the same time they are restrictions of all the categories to merely empirical employment, with no admission or permission of the transcendental. For if the categories are not to have a merely logical significance, expressing analytically the form of thought, but are to concern things and their possibility, actuality or necessity, they must relate to possible experience and its synthetic unity, in which alone objects of knowledge are given.",[]],"krv-b-0685":["The postulate of the possibility of things thus requires that the concept of them agree with the formal conditions of an experience in general. This, namely the objective form of experience in general, contains all the synthesis that is required for the knowledge of objects. A concept which comprises a synthesis in itself is to be regarded as empty, and does not relate to any object, if this synthesis does not belong to experience. It may belong to experience in one of two ways. The synthesis may be borrowed from experience, and the concept is then entitled an empirical concept. Or it may be a synthesis upon which, as an *a priori* condition, experience in general (its form) rests, and the concept is then a pure concept, which nevertheless belongs to experience, since its object can be met with only in experience. For where are we to find the mark of the possibility of an object that is thought through a synthetic concept *a priori*, if not in the synthesis which constitutes the form of the empirical knowledge of objects? That no contradiction must be contained in such a concept is, indeed, a necessary logical condition; but it is very far from sufficient for the objective reality of the concept, that is, for the possibility of such an object as is thought through the concept. Thus in the concept of a figure enclosed within two straight lines there is no contradiction, for the concepts of two straight lines and of their meeting contain no negation of a figure. The impossibility rests not on the concept in itself, but on its construction in space, that is, on the conditions of space and of its determination. These in turn have their objective reality, that is, they apply to possible things, because they contain *a priori* in themselves the form of experience in general.",[]],"krv-b-0686":["And now we shall set before our eyes the wide utility and influence of this postulate of possibility. If I represent to myself a thing that is permanent, so that everything which changes belongs merely to its state, I can never know from such a concept alone that a thing of this kind is possible. Or I may represent to myself something so constituted that, when it is posited, something else always and unfailingly follows upon it. This can indeed be thought without contradiction; but whether such a property (as causality) is to be met with in any possible thing, cannot be judged from that. Finally, I can represent to myself different things (substances) so constituted that the state of one entails a consequence in the state of another, and so reciprocally; but whether such a relation can belong to any things cannot be gathered at all from these concepts, which contain a merely arbitrary synthesis. Only through the fact, then, that these concepts express *a priori* the relations of perceptions in every experience, do we know their objective reality, that is, their transcendental truth. We know it, indeed, independently of experience, yet not independently of all relation to the form of an experience in general and to the synthetic unity in which alone objects can be empirically known.",["- krv-b-0675: the long opening sentence is divided; \"Bemühung\" rendered \"labour\"; the asterisk after \"employed\" stands for the OCR \"“)\", which marks the footnote given in krv-b-0679. \"[field of]\" supplied for \"in der Erscheinung\".","- krv-b-0675: \"Dritten\" rendered \"third factor\" (the possibility of experience); \"Wahne\" as \"delusion\".","- krv-b-0679: \"ingeheim\" read as \"insgeheim\"; \"Gemeinschaft\" rendered \"community\", \"Wechselwirkung\" \"reciprocal action\". The text has a loose \"und wäre\" construction, smoothed into \"and if\".","- krv-b-0680, 0681: \"eriftirt\" read as \"existirt\".","- krv-b-0683: \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" rendered \"faculty of knowledge\"; \"Urtheilskraft\" \"faculty of judgment\".","- krv-b-0685: OCR \"Bez griff\", \"Busammenstoßung\" corrected (Zusammenstoßung, \"meeting\"); \"Dbject\" read as \"Object\"; \"heißt ein empirischer Begriff\" has dropped \"er\" and is rendered \"is entitled\"; the sentence on the two ways is divided.","- krv-b-0686: \"Buftande\" read as \"Zustande\"; \"augdrücken\" as \"ausdrücken\"; \"wechsels— weise\" joined as \"wechselsweise\", \"reciprocally\"."]],"krv-b-0687":["If, however, we should seek to form quite new concepts of substances, of forces, of reciprocal action, out of the material which perception presents to us, without borrowing from experience itself the example of their connection, we should find ourselves involved in sheer figments of the brain. The possibility of such concepts has absolutely no criterion in its favour, since we neither take experience as our instructress in the matter nor derive these concepts from it. Invented concepts of this kind cannot acquire the character of their possibility *a priori*, as the categories do, that is, as conditions upon which all experience depends. They can acquire it only *a posteriori*, as concepts given through experience itself. Their possibility must therefore either be known *a posteriori* and empirically, or it cannot be known at all. Examples are a substance which would be persistently present in space without filling it (like that intermediate thing between matter and thinking beings which some have wished to introduce); or a special fundamental power of our mind of intuiting the future in advance (and not merely inferring it); or, finally, a faculty of the mind of standing in community of thought with other men, however distant they may be. These are concepts whose possibility is wholly groundless, because it cannot be grounded on experience and its known laws. Apart from experience, such a concept is an arbitrary combination of thoughts which, though it contains no contradiction, can make no claim to objective reality, and therefore none to the possibility of an object such as we here propose to think. As regards reality, it is obvious that we are forbidden to think it *in concreto* without taking experience to our aid. For reality can relate only to sensation, as the matter of experience, and does not concern the form of the relation, with which, if need be, we might play in fictions.",["- Restated the long final sentence of the first half as several sentences; \"wein\" read as \"wenn\" (OCR slip) and \"dar— bietet\" as \"darbietet\".","- \"Hirngespinste\" = \"figments of the brain\"; \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"Vermögen\" = faculty (of the mind); \"Kräfte\" = forces, \"Grundkraft\" = fundamental power.","- Supplied \"[such a concept]\" in sense only: \"ohne sie\" is taken as \"apart from experience\"; \"sie\" in \"die Möglichkeit... auf sie\" refers to the concepts. The \"deren... ist\" construction is smoothed.","- \"Mittelding\" rendered \"intermediate thing\"; \"Wechselwirkung\" = reciprocal action."]],"krv-b-0688":["But I pass over everything whose possibility can be inferred only from actuality in experience, and consider here only the possibility of things through concepts *a priori*. Of these I continue to maintain that such concepts can never find a place through themselves alone, but only ever as formal and objective conditions of an experience in general.",["- OCR debris \"7 6 —\" removed; the text is evidently \"objective\" (objektive) in the standard B reading (\"formale und objective Bedingungen\"), but the OCR does not show this word, so \"and objective\" is a conjecture of mine from the standard text; the passage as given reads \"formale und … Bedingungen\". Doubtful reading; flag for checking against AA III.","- \"stattfinden können\" rendered \"find a place\"; \"Dinge durch Begriffe a priori\" retained literally."]],"krv-b-0689":["It does indeed seem as if the possibility of a triangle could be known from its concept in itself (it is certainly independent of experience); for we can in fact give an object to it entirely *a priori*, that is, construct it. But since this is only the form of an object, the triangle would still remain a mere product of imagination, and the possibility of its object would remain doubtful. For this something more is required, namely, that such a figure be thought under none but those conditions upon which all objects of experience rest. Now that space is a formal condition *a priori* of outer experiences, and that the very same figurative synthesis through which we construct a triangle in imagination is entirely identical with that which we exercise in the apprehension of an appearance in order to make an experiential concept of it: this alone is what connects with the concept the representation of the possibility of such a thing. In the same way the possibility of continuous magnitudes, indeed of magnitudes in general, since the concepts of them are all synthetic, is never clear from the concepts themselves, but only from them as formal conditions of the determination of objects in experience in general. And where, indeed, should we seek objects corresponding to the concepts, if not in experience, through which alone objects are given to us? Yet we can know and characterise the possibility of things, without actually putting experience first, solely in relation to the formal conditions under which something in experience is in general determined as object, and therefore completely *a priori*; but only in relation to those conditions and within their limits.",["- \"bildende Synthesis\" = figurative synthesis (*synthesis speciosa* sense). \"Erfahrungsbegriff\" = experiential concept [concept of experience].","- Split the long sentences at \"Daß nun ...\" and the closing \"Wiewohl\" clause; \"wei— eben\" read as \"welchen\" (OCR).","- \"Product der Einbildung\" = product of imagination; \"Einbildungskraft\" = imagination.","- \"allererst klar\" rendered \"is never clear ... but only\" to smooth the negation."]],"krv-b-0690":["The postulate for knowing the actuality of things requires perception, and therefore sensation of which one is conscious. It need not, indeed, be immediate perception of the very object whose existence is to be known; but we must at least have some connection of the object with an actual perception, in accordance with the analogies of experience, which set forth all real connection in an experience in general.",["- \"fol\" read as \"soll\", \"wirtlichen\" as \"wirklichen\" (OCR slips). \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception."]],"krv-b-0691":["In the mere concept of a thing no mark of its existence can be found. For however complete the concept may be, so that nothing whatever is lacking for thinking a thing with all its inner determinations, existence has nothing to do with all this. It has to do only with the question whether such a thing be given to us, in such fashion that perception of it could, if need be, precede the concept. For that the concept precedes the perception signifies its mere possibility; the perception, which supplies the material for the concept, is the sole mark of actuality. But we can also know the existence of a thing prior to its perception, and so *comparative a priori*, if only it is connected with certain perceptions in accordance with the principles of their empirical connection (the analogies). For in that case the existence of the thing is still connected with our perceptions in a",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"in einer\" continues in the next passage); not completed.","- \"Dasein\" = existence; \"Wirklichkeit\" = actuality; \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations. \"Be— griffe\" repaired."]],"krv-b-0692":["[possible] experience, and we can, under the guidance of those analogies, advance from our actual perception to the thing in the series of possible perceptions. Thus we know the existence of a magnetic matter pervading all bodies from the perception of the attracted iron filings, although immediate perception of this matter is, owing to the constitution of our organs, impossible for us. For in general, in accordance with the laws of sensibility and the context of our perceptions in one experience, we should also come upon the immediate empirical intuition of it, if our senses were finer, since their coarseness does not affect the form of possible experience in general. Wherever, therefore, perception and what attaches to it in accordance with empirical laws extends, there our knowledge of the existence of things also extends. If we do not begin with experience, or do not proceed in accordance with laws of the empirical connection of appearances, we are vainly making a show of wishing to guess at or investigate the existence of any thing.[footnote marker] A powerful objection to these rules for proving existence mediately is, however, raised by idealism, the refutation of which is here in its proper place.",["- The passage opens with OCR debris \"or\"; read as \"[mö]glichen\" and supplied \"[possible]\" accordingly, continuing the broken \"in einer\" of the previous passage. Doubtful.","- \"Eisenfeiligs\" read \"Eisenfeilicht\" (filings); \"seiner\" read \"feiner\"; \"Staat\" in \"sich Staat machen\" = making a show. Numerals 10, 1s, 20 are line numbers, dropped.","- The text shows a footnote sign (“) after \"wollen\"; the footnote itself is not given in the passage, so only a marker is kept."]],"krv-b-0693":["Refutation of Idealism.",["- Heading as given."]],"krv-b-0694":["Idealism (I mean material idealism) is the theory which declares the existence of objects in space outside us to be either merely doubtful and incapable of proof, or false and impossible. The former is the problematic idealism of Descartes, who declares only one empirical assertion (*assertio*), namely, \"I am\", to be indubitable. The latter is the dogmatic idealism of Berkeley, who [declares] space",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"der den Raum\"); not completed. \"[declares]\" is supplied to carry the construction.","- Split the sentence at the semicolon into two; \"Cartesius\" rendered Descartes."]],"krv-b-0695":["(The discussion which follows, down to the end of Note 3 on p. 193, is an addition of A2; compare the note in the Preface of A2, p. 23.) [The idealist declares space,] together with all the things to which it attaches as inseparable condition, to be something impossible in itself, and therefore declares things in space to be mere imaginings. Dogmatic idealism is unavoidable if space is regarded as a property which must belong to things in themselves; for in that case space, together with everything for which it serves as condition, is a non-entity. The ground of this idealism, however, we have removed in the Transcendental Aesthetic. Problematic idealism makes no assertion on this matter, and merely pleads our incapacity to prove, through immediate experience, an existence other than our own. It is rational, and is in accordance with a thorough philosophical manner of thinking, in that it permits no decisive judgment until a sufficient proof has been found. The proof demanded must therefore show that we have experience, and not merely imagination, of outer things. This cannot be done unless we can prove that even our inner experience, which Descartes regards as indubitable, is possible only on the presupposition of outer experience.",["- The passage opens mid-sentence (\")\" is OCR residue of a note-mark); the editorial note is kept as given, and the bracketed subject \"[The idealist declares space,]\" is supplied from the evident sense (the preceding clause is missing).","- Divided the last sentence of the German into two for clarity; \"welches wohl nicht anders ... als wenn\" rendered \"cannot be done unless\".","- Silently corrected \"fol\" to \"soll\" and \"Eristenz\" to \"Existenz\" (OCR); \"entschei— dendes\" rejoined.","- *Unding* rendered \"non-entity\"."]],"krv-b-0696":["Proposition.",["- *Lehrsatz* = \"Proposition\" (Kemp Smith's usual \"Theorem\" is also possible; \"Proposition\" chosen here)."]],"krv-b-0697":["The mere, but empirically determined, consciousness of my own existence proves the existence of objects in space outside me.",[]],"krv-b-0698":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-0699":["I am conscious of my existence as determined in time. All time-determination presupposes something permanent in perception. This permanent cannot, however, be something in me, since my existence in time can itself first be determined only through this permanent. Perception of this permanent is therefore possible only through a thing outside me, and not through the mere representation of a thing outside me. Consequently the determination of my existence in time is possible only through the existence of actual things which I perceive outside me. Now consciousness in time is necessarily bound up with consciousness of the possibility of this time-determination. It is therefore also necessarily bound up with the existence of things outside me, as the condition of time-determination; that is to say, the consciousness of my own existence is at the same time an immediate consciousness of the existence of other things outside me.",["- \"Eristenz\" corrected to \"Existenz\" (OCR).","- *Beharrliche* = \"the permanent\"; *Wahrnehmung* = \"perception\"; *Zeitbestimmung* = \"time-determination\".","- Split the German \"also ist es auch ... verbunden\" into a separate sentence beginning \"It is therefore\"."]],"krv-b-0700":["Note 1. It will be observed in the foregoing proof that the game which idealism played has been, with greater right, played back upon it. Idealism assumed that the only immediate experience is inner experience, and that from it outer things are only inferred; and that this inference is, as always when we infer from given effects to determinate causes, unreliable, since the cause of the representations which we perhaps wrongly ascribe to outer things may lie in ourselves. Here, however, it is proved that outer experience is properly immediate,[*] and that only by means of it is possible, not indeed consciousness of our own existence, but yet the determination of that existence in time, that is, inner experience. The representation 'I am', which expresses the consciousness that can accompany all thought, is, it is true, that which immediately includes in itself the existence of a subject; but it is not yet a knowledge of that subject, and so not empirical knowledge, that is, experience. For this there is required, besides the thought of something existing, intuition, and in this case inner intuition, in respect of which, that is, of time, the subject must be determined. Outer objects are indispensably required for this, with the result that inner experience is itself only mediate, and possible only through outer experience.",["- Footnote mark \"*)\" (OCR \")\") restored as [*] after \"immediate\"; the OCR \"s\" and \"10\" are line numbers, omitted.","- Restated the long second sentence in two; \"wie allemal\" rendered \"as always\".","- *Erkenntniß* = \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-0701":["*) In the foregoing proposition the immediate consciousness of the existence of outer things is not presupposed but proved, whether or not we understand the possibility of this consciousness. The question regarding the latter would be whether we have only an inner sense and no outer sense, merely outer imagination. But it is clear that, in order even to imagine something as outer, that is, to present it to sense in intuition, we must already have an outer sense, and must thereby immediately distinguish the mere receptivity of an outer intuition from the spontaneity which characterises all imagination. For even merely to imagine an outer sense would annihilate the faculty of intuition itself, which is to be determined by the imagination.",["- Footnote to Note 1. The reference \"B 278–279?\" is the user's; the footnote text is as given. Stray \"ss\" (line number) omitted.","- *Einbildung* = \"imagination\" (the act/product), *Einbildungskraft* = \"imagination\" too; context distinguishes.","- *Anschauungsvermögen* = \"faculty of intuition\"."]],"krv-b-0702":["Note 2. With this, all the empirical employment of our faculty of knowledge in the determination of time is in complete agreement. Not only can we perceive all time-determination only through change in outer relations (motion) in reference to the permanent in space (for instance, the motion of the sun in respect of the objects of the earth); we have, besides, nothing permanent that we could place as intuition under the concept of a substance, save only matter. Even this permanence is not drawn from outer experience, but is presupposed *a priori* as the necessary condition of all time-determination, and therefore also as determination of inner sense, in respect of our own existence, through the existence of outer things. The consciousness of myself in the representation 'I' is no intuition at all, but a merely intellectual representation of the self-activity of a thinking subject. Hence this 'I' has not the least predicate of intuition which, as permanent, could serve as correlate to the time-determination in inner sense, as, for instance, impenetrability does in the case of matter as empirical intuition.",["- The German sentence beginning \"Nicht allein, daß ..., so haben wir sogar\" is irregular; smoothed into \"Not only can we ...; we have, besides ...\".","- \"Un durchdring lichkeit\" spacing corrected (OCR).","- *Erkenntnißvermögen* = \"faculty of knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-0703":["Note 3. From the fact that the existence of outer objects is required for the possibility of a determinate consciousness of ourselves, it does not follow that every intuitive representation of outer things involves at the same time their existence. Such a representation may very well be the mere effect of imagination (in dreams as well as in madness). It is so, however, solely through the reproduction of former outer perceptions, which, as has been shown, are possible only through the actuality of outer objects. All that has here had to be proved is that inner experience in general is possible only through outer experience in general. Whether this or that supposed experience is not mere imagination must be ascertained from its special determinations, and by comparing it with the criteria of all actual experience.[**]",["- The closing OCR mark (“) is read as a footnote or note-mark; no footnote text is supplied in the passage, so none is given.","- *Wahnsinn* = \"madness\"; *vermeinte Erfahrung* = \"supposed experience\".","- Divided the second sentence at the semicolon-like \"sie ist es aber\"."]],"krv-b-0704":["As regards, finally, the third postulate, it concerns material necessity in existence, not the merely formal and logical necessity in the connection of concepts. Now no existence of objects of the senses can be known completely a priori; it can be known only comparatively a priori, in relation to some other existence already given. Even so, we can arrive only at such an existence as must be contained somewhere in the context of experience, of which the given perception is a part. Consequently the necessity of existence can never be known from concepts, but always only from connection with what is perceived, in accordance with universal laws of experience. Now there is no existence which, under the condition of other given appearances, could be known as necessary, save the existence of effects from given causes in accordance with the laws of causality. It is therefore not the existence of things (substances) but only that of their state, of which alone we can know the necessity; and this we know from other states which are given in perception, in accordance with empirical laws of causality. From this it follows that the criterion of necessity lies solely in the law of possible experience, that everything which happens is determined a priori through its cause in the appearance. Hence we know only the necessity of those effects in nature whose causes are given to us. The mark of necessity in existence does not extend beyond the field of possible experience; and even within that field it does not hold of the existence of things as substances, since these can never be regarded as empirical effects, or as something that happens and comes into being. Necessity concerns, therefore, only the relations of appearances in accordance with the dynamical law of causality, and the possibility, grounded upon that law, of inferring a priori from any given existence (a cause) to another existence (the effect).\n\nEverything that happens is hypothetically necessary. This is a principle which subjects alteration in the world to a law, that is, to a rule of necessary existence, without which not even nature could obtain. Hence the proposition, Nothing happens through blind chance (*in mundo non datur casus*), is an a priori law of nature. So also is the proposition, No necessity in nature is blind, but is always conditioned and therefore intelligible necessity (*non datur fatum*). Both are laws through which the play of alterations is subjected to a nature of things (as appearances) or, what comes to the same thing, to the unity of the understanding, in which alone they can belong to an experience, as the synthetic unity of appearances. These two principles belong to the dynamical principles. The first is properly a consequence of the principle of causality (among the Analogies of Experience). The second belongs to the principles of modality, which to the determination of causality adds the concept of necessity, a necessity which, however, stands under a rule of the understanding.\n\nThe principle of continuity forbade any leap in the series of appearances (alterations) (*in mundo non datur saltus*); but it also forbade, in the sum of all empirical intuitions in space, any gap or chasm between two appearances (*non datur hiatus*). For the proposition can be so expressed: nothing can enter into experience which would prove a *vacuum*, or even admit it as a part of the empirical synthesis. As for the void which we may think outside the field of possible experience (the world), this does not come under the jurisdiction of the mere understanding, which decides only questions concerning the employment of given appearances for empirical knowledge. It is a problem for ideal reason, which goes beyond the sphere of a possible experience and seeks to judge of what surrounds and bounds that sphere; it must therefore be considered in the Transcendental Dialectic.\n\nThese four propositions (*in mundo non datur hiatus, non datur saltus, non datur casus, non datur fatum*) we could easily set forth, like all principles of transcendental origin, in their order according to the order of the categories, and prove to each its place. But the reader, already practised, will do this for himself, or will easily discover the guiding thread. They all unite, however, solely in this: to admit nothing in the empirical synthesis that could do damage or prejudice to the understanding and to the continuous connection of all appearances, that is, to the unity of its concepts. For it is in the understanding alone that the unity of experience, in which all perceptions must have their place, becomes possible.",["- Several long sentences were divided, notably the opening argument (\"Da nun … so kann …\") and \"Daher erkennen wir …\", to keep the antecedents plain.","- \"Caufabestimmung\" is an OCR slip for \"Causalbestimmung\" and is rendered \"determination of causality\". \"wer den\", \"je— dem\" and the stray \"5\" were silently corrected or removed.","- \"materiale … nicht die bloß formale\": I read \"die\" as an OCR fusion (\"diebloß\") and translated as \"not the merely formal\".","- \"einer Natur der Dinge … unterworfen\": the German is awkward; I kept \"a nature of things\" literally, since the sense (the play of alterations is brought under the order of nature) is ambiguous.","- \"comparative a priori, relativisch\" is rendered \"comparatively a priori, in relation to\"; \"idealische Vernunft\" as \"ideal reason\".","- \"Das Princip der Continuität verbot\": \"verbot\" is in the past tense, referring back to the earlier discussion of the Analogies, and is kept in the past.","- \"Diese beide Grundsätze gehören zu den dynamischen\" is rendered literally; the \"second\" principle's membership in modality follows the German.","- \"Gerichtsbarkeit\" is rendered \"jurisdiction\"; \"Nutzung\" as \"employment\"."]],"krv-b-0705":["Whether the field of possibility is greater than the field which contains all that is actual, and this again greater than the sum of what is necessary, are pretty questions, and questions moreover which call for a synthetic solution; but they fall only under the jurisdiction of reason. For what they amount to, roughly, is the question whether all things, as appearances, belong together in the sum and context of a single experience, of which every given perception is a part, and which therefore cannot be combined with any other appearances; or whether my perceptions can belong to more than one possible experience (in their general interconnection).\n\nThe understanding gives to experience in general, *a priori*, only the rule, in accordance with the subjective and formal conditions both of sensibility and of apperception, which alone make experience possible. Other forms of intuition (than space and time), and likewise other forms of understanding (than the discursive form of thought, or of knowledge through concepts), even if they were possible, we cannot in any way conceive or make comprehensible to ourselves. And even if we could, they would still not belong to experience, the one mode of knowledge in which objects are given to us. Whether perceptions other than those which belong to our whole possible experience can be met with, and so whether an altogether different field of matter may still exist, the understanding cannot decide. It has to do only with the synthesis of what is given.\n\nOtherwise, the poverty of our ordinary inferences, by which we make out a great realm of the possible of which everything actual (every object of experience) is only a small part, is very striking. Everything actual is possible; from this there follows naturally, by the logical rules of conversion, the merely particular proposition: Some possible is actual. This seems to mean as much as: Much is possible that is not actual. It does indeed appear as if one could even directly set the number of the possible above that of the actual, on the ground that something must be added to the former in order to constitute the latter. But this addition to the possible I do not recognise. For what was to be added over and above the possible would be impossible. What can be added is something only in relation to my understanding, over and above agreement with the formal conditions of experience, namely connection with some perception. But whatever is connected with this perception according to empirical laws is actual, even though it is not immediately perceived.\n\nThat, however, in thoroughgoing connection with what is given to me in perception, another series of appearances, and therefore more than one all-embracing experience, is possible, cannot be inferred from what is given; and still less can it be inferred without anything being given, since without material nothing can be thought at all. What is possible only under conditions which are themselves merely possible, is not possible in all respects. But the question is taken in this [unrestricted] respect when we wish to know whether the possibility of things extends further than experience can reach.",["- Long first sentence divided into two; \"denn sie wollen ... sagen\" rendered \"For what they amount to...\" and \"der also ... könne\" attached to \"experience\" (the single experience), as the argument requires.","- \"Migliche\" is an OCR slip for \"Mögliche\"; corrected silently. Line numbers (15, 20, 5) omitted.","- \"Erkenntniß\" in \"dem einzigen Erkenntniß\" rendered \"mode of knowledge\"; \"Gegenstand der Erfahrung\" = object of experience.","- \"Es kann nur zu meinem Verstande etwas ... hinzukommen\": \"in relation to my understanding\" is an interpretive gloss; the German is awkward.","- \"In dieser\" supplied as \"[unrestricted] respect\", referring back to \"in aller Absicht\"; bracketed as supplied.","- \"artige Fragen\" rendered \"pretty questions\" (ironical)."]],"krv-b-0706":["I have mentioned these questions only in order to leave no gap in what, according to common opinion, belongs to the concepts of the understanding. [The text is corrupt here: the OCR has run together fragments of two lines, and part of the sentence is missing, so that the passage resumes in mid-sentence.] ... [which is valid in every respect] is no mere concept of the understanding and can in no way be of empirical use, but belongs solely to reason, which goes beyond all possible empirical employment of the understanding. We have therefore had to content ourselves with a merely critical remark, and have otherwise left the matter in obscurity until further procedure in the future.",["- The source is badly garbled after \"zu den Verstandes-\": a fragment (\"aller Absicht gültig ist) kein bloßer Verstandesbegriff ...\") follows without its beginning. I have not filled the gap; the bracketed note and ellipsis mark it.","- The bracketed \"[which is valid in every respect]\" is only a rendering of the surviving words \"aller Absicht gültig ist\", placed in brackets because its connection is lost.","- \"Anmerkung\" rendered \"remark\"; \"kritisch\" as \"critical\".","- \"seiner\" in \"auf seiner lei Weise\" is a damaged reading of \"auf keinerlei Weise\"; rendered \"in no way\"."]],"krv-b-0707":["Since I am about to close this fourth heading, and with it the system of all the principles of the pure understanding, I must still give the reason why I have entitled the principles of modality precisely postulates. I do not wish to take this expression here in the meaning which some recent philosophical writers have given it, contrary to the sense of the mathematicians, to whom it properly belongs, namely, that to postulate is to be understood as meaning the same as to put forward a proposition as immediately certain, without justification or proof. For if, in the case of synthetic propositions, however evident they may be, we were to concede that they may be accepted, without deduction, on the authority of their own pronouncement, to unconditional assent, all criticism of the understanding would be lost. And since there is no lack of bold pretensions, which common belief (though that is no credential) does not refuse to countenance, our understanding would lie open to every delusion, being unable to withhold its assent from pronouncements which, though unlawful, demand to be admitted in the same tone of confidence as genuine axioms. When, therefore, a determination is added *a priori* and synthetically to the concept of a thing, then for such a proposition, if not a proof, at least a deduction of the legitimacy of its assertion must unfailingly be added.",["- The sentence beginning \"denn wenn wir das...\" divided into two; the \"so ... und da ... so\" structure smoothed.","- \"Peifall\" is an OCR slip for \"Beifall\" (\"assent\"); corrected silently.","- \"Kritik des Verstandes\" rendered \"criticism of the understanding\"; \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Creditiv\" = credential.","- \"die Principien der Modalität\" in \"this fourth heading\" (\"vierte Nummer\") is a choice of rendering."]],"krv-b-0708":["The principles of modality, however, are not objectively synthetic, since the predicates of possibility, actuality and necessity do not in the least enlarge the concept of which they are affirmed, by adding anything further to the representation of the object. But as they are nevertheless always synthetic, they are so only subjectively. That is to say, they add to the concept of a thing (of something real), about which they otherwise say nothing, the faculty of knowledge in which it originates and has its seat. Thus if the concept is connected in the understanding merely with the formal conditions of experience, its object is entitled possible; if it is bound up with perception (sensation as the matter of the senses), and is determined through perception by means of the understanding, the object is actual; if it is determined through the connection of perceptions according to concepts, the object is entitled necessary. The principles of modality thus say of a concept nothing else than the act of the faculty of knowledge by which it is generated.\n\nNow in mathematics a postulate is the practical proposition which contains nothing but the synthesis whereby we first give ourselves an object and generate its concept, for example, with a given line, from a given point on a plane, to describe a circle. A proposition of this kind cannot be proved, because the procedure which it demands is precisely that by which we first generate the concept of such a figure. In the same way, and with equal right, we can postulate the principles of modality, since they do not enlarge their concept of things in general,*) but only indicate the manner in which it is connected with the faculty of knowledge.",["- Divided at \"Die Grundsätze der Modalität also...\" and \"Nun heißt...\" into two paragraphs-worth of sentences; the long \"so daß\" clause kept but punctuated.","- \"Erkenntnißkraft\" rendered \"faculty of knowledge\"; \"Handlung\" = act; \"heißt\" = \"is entitled\".","- \"setzten\" is an OCR slip for \"setzen\"; \"*)\" is the footnote marker, retained, but no footnote text is given in the passage.","- \"ihren Begriff von Dingen überhaupt\": the \"ihren\" is ambiguous; I take it as the concept of things which the principles concern, i.e. the concept of things in general.","- The citation range is marked with a query in the source; not checked."]],"krv-b-0709":["General Remark on the System of the Principles.*)",["- \"*)\" is the footnote marker, retained; the footnote itself is not in the passage. The OCR rendering \"“)\" is read as this marker."]],"krv-b-0710":["It is something very noteworthy that we cannot understand the possibility of any thing from the mere category, but must always have an intuition at hand, in order to exhibit in it the objective reality of the pure concept of the understanding. Take, for example, the categories of relation. How (1) something can exist only as subject, and not as a mere determination of other things, that is, can be substance; or how (2) because something is, something else must be, and consequently how something can be a cause; or (3) how, when several things exist, something follows for the rest from the existence of one of them, and so reciprocally, and in this way a community of substances can obtain: none of this can be understood from mere concepts. The same holds of the other categories, for instance, how a thing can be one and the same with many together, that is, can be a magnitude, and so forth. So",["- The passage breaks off at \"So\"; left uncompleted.","- \"dasind\" is an OCR slip for \"da sind\"; \"z.\" for \"z. B.\" read as \"for example\".","- The three \"how\" clauses are left as parallel indirect questions with a single verb (\"cannot be understood\"), as in the German; the English has been punctuated with colons to hold them together.","- \"einsehen\" rendered \"understand\", \"darlegen\" as \"exhibit\"; \"Bestimmung\" = determination."]],"krv-b-0711":[") Through the actuality of a thing I certainly posit more than its possibility, but not [more] in the thing. For the thing can never contain in actuality more than was contained in its complete possibility. But possibility was merely the positing of the thing in relation to the understanding (to its empirical employment), whereas actuality is at the same time the connection of the thing with perception. So long as intuition is lacking, we cannot know whether we are thinking an object through the categories, nor whether any object whatsoever could correspond to them. This confirms that they are not in themselves modes of knowledge at all, but mere forms of thought for making knowledge out of given intuitions. — This is also why no synthetic proposition can be made out of mere categories. Examples are: In all existence there is substance, that is, something which can exist only as subject and not as mere predicate; or, Every thing is a *quantum*, and so on. In such propositions there is nothing that could enable us to go beyond a given concept and connect another with it. Hence it has never been possible to prove a synthetic proposition from mere pure concepts of the understanding, for instance the proposition: Everything that exists contingently has a cause. We could never get further than to prove that without this relation we could not comprehend the existence of the contingent, that is, could not know a priori through the understanding the existence of such a thing. It does not follow from this that this same relation is also the condition of the possibility of the things themselves. If, therefore, we look back to our proof of the principle of causality, we shall find that we were able to prove it only of objects of possible experience. [The principle runs:] Everything that happens (every event) presupposes a cause. And we were able to prove it only as the principle of the possibility of experience, and so of the knowledge of an object given in empirical intuition, and not from mere concepts. That the proposition, Everything contingent must have a cause, is nevertheless evident to everyone from mere concepts cannot be denied. But in that case the concept of the contingent is already so conceived that it contains not the category of modality (as that whose non-existence can be thought) but that of relation (as that which can exist only as the consequence of something else); and then it is indeed an identical proposition: What can exist only as a consequence has its cause. In fact, when we have to give examples of contingent existence, we always appeal to alterations, and not merely to the possibility of thinking the opposite.) Alter-",["- \"fege\" read as *füge*; \"lange es also\" as *So lange es also*; \"Ouantum\" as *Quantum*; \"seohen\" as *sehen*; \"Dbjecten\" as *Objecten* (OCR slips).","- Restated the long sentence beginning \"Wenn man daher …\" as several sentences; supplied \"[more]\" and \"[The principle runs:]\" to complete the sense.","- \"Wirklichkeit\" = actuality; \"Möglichkeit\" = possibility; \"Erkenntnisse\" = modes of knowledge; \"Satz\" = proposition; \"Grundsatz\" = principle.","- The opening \")\" and the closing \"Verän-\" are footnote and text markers from the OCR; the word is completed in the next passage (\"derung\"), not here."]],"krv-b-0712":[") We can easily think the non-existence of matter, yet the ancients did not infer its contingency from this. But even the alternation of being and non-being of a given state of a thing, in which all alteration consists, by no means proves the contingency of this state, as it were from the actuality [derung aber ist Begebenheit, ...] alteration, however, is an event which as such is possible only through a cause, and whose non-being is therefore possible in itself; and so we recognise contingency from the fact that something can exist only as the effect of a cause. If, therefore, a thing is assumed to be contingent, it is an analytic proposition to say that it has a cause. 5",["- The OCR interleaves two texts. The main-text sentence (\"derung aber ist Begebenheit … eine Ursache\") completes \"Veränderung\" from the previous passage and breaks into the footnote sentence, which resumes in the next passage (\"seines Gegentheils, z. B. die Ruhe …\"). I have translated in the order given, and have not reordered, bracketed the join.","- The bracketed Latin-free fragment \"[derung aber ist Begebenheit, ...]\" is a marker of the interruption, not a translation of a missing text; the main-text sentence itself follows after it.","- \"Wechsel\" = alternation; \"Begebenheit\" = event; \"Gegentheil\" in the next passage.","- The \"5\" is a line number and is kept only because it stands in the passage; it may be dropped."]],"krv-b-0713":["It is still more remarkable that, in order to understand the possibility of things in accordance with the categories, and so to demonstrate the objective reality of the latter, we require not merely intuitions but always outer intuitions. If, for instance, we take the pure concepts of relation, we find: (1) In order to give in intuition something permanent corresponding to the concept of substance (and thereby to demonstrate the objective reality of this concept), we require an intuition in space (of matter). For space alone is determined as permanent, whereas time, and therefore everything in inner sense, is in constant flux. (2) In order to exhibit alteration as the intuition corresponding to the concept of causality, we must take motion, as alteration in space, as our example. Indeed it is only through motion that we can make alterations, the possibility of which no pure understanding can comprehend, intuitable to ourselves. Alteration is the combination of contradictorily opposed determinations in the existence of one and the same thing. How it is possible that from a given state of a thing an opposite state of the same thing should follow, not only can no reason make conceivable without an example, but cannot even make intelligible without intuition. This intuition is that of the movement of a point in space. The existence of the point in different places (as a sequence of opposed determinations) is what first makes alteration intuitable to us. For in order afterwards to make inner alterations also thinkable to ourselves, we must represent time, as the form of inner sense, figuratively by a line, and inner alteration by the drawing of this line (motion), and so the successive of his opposite, for example the rest of a body, which follows upon motion, does not yet prove the contingency of its motion, since the former is the opposite of the latter. For this opposite is here opposed to the other only logically, not really. To prove the contingency of its motion we should have to prove that, instead of the motion in the preceding moment of time, it would have been possible for the body to have been at rest then, not that it is at rest afterwards; for in the latter case both opposites may very well coexist.",["- Again the OCR interleaves the footnote (\"seines Gegentheils … z. B. die Ruhe eines Körpers …\") into the main text at \"mithin die successive\". I have kept the order of the passage as given, so the join reads brokenly: \"the successive of his opposite\" is main text and footnote running together. The main text continues in B 292 (\"Existenz unser selbst …\").","- \"o\" after \"Sub stanz\", \"s\" before \"Causalität\" and \"Pewegung\" for *Bewegung* are OCR slips, silently corrected; \"20\" is a line number, omitted.","- \"his opposite\" translates \"seines Gegentheils\", whose antecedent is in the footnote text that is not in this passage; \"his\" is therefore to be read as a doubtful rendering of \"its\".","- Split the long sentence \"Wie es nun möglich sei …\" into two sentences; \"begreiflich … verständlich\" rendered \"conceivable … intelligible\".","- \"Beharrliches\" = something permanent; \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"figürlich\" = figuratively."]],"krv-b-0714":["the existence of ourselves in different states through outer intuition",["- A fragment which continues the sentence of the preceding passage (\"… we must … [make] the existence of ourselves in different states …\"). I have not supplied the governing verb; it comes in the next passage (\"uns faßlich machen\")."]],"krv-b-0715":["make comprehensible to ourselves. The real ground of this is that all alteration presupposes something permanent in intuition, in order that it may even",[]],"krv-b-0716":["itself be perceived as alteration; whereas in inner sense no permanent intuition is to be met with. — Finally, the category of community is, as regards its possibility, not to be comprehended through mere reason at all, and consequently the objective reality of this con-",["- \"s\" after \"inneren Sinn\" is a line number; omitted.","- The sentence breaks off in mid-word (\"Be⸗ / griffs\"), completed in the next passage (\"concept\")."]],"krv-b-0717":["cept cannot be seen without intuition, and indeed outer intuition in space. For",["- \"Be⸗ griffs\" is divided across the passages; I have rendered the second half as \"cept\" and the sentence continues \"ohne Anschauung … nicht einzusehen möglich.\" The word order of the German (\"nicht einzusehen möglich\") is smoothed to \"cannot be seen\"."]],"krv-b-0718":["how are we to think the possibility that, when several",[]],"krv-b-0719":["substances exist, something (as effect) can follow reciprocally from the existence of one upon the existence of the others,",[]],"krv-b-0720":["and that, therefore, because there is something in",[]],"krv-b-0721":["the former, there must also be something in the others",[]],"krv-b-0722":["which cannot be understood from the existence of the latter alone? For",[]],"krv-b-0723":["this is required for community, but is not at all comprehensible among things which each isolate themselves completely through their subsistence. Hence",[]],"krv-b-0724":["Leibniz, in ascribing to the substances of the world, only as the understanding",["- The sentence is broken off in mid-clause, and I have translated it as it stands, without completing it; the German continues (\"… denkt, …\").","- \"Substanzen der Welt, nur wie sie der Verstand [denkt]\" is not completed in the passage, so no verb is supplied in the translation.","- \"5\" in 0723 is a line number, omitted.","- \"Subsistenz\" is left as *subsistence*, and \"Gemeinschaft\" rendered \"community\" (Kant's Latin parallel *commercium*)."]],"krv-b-0725":[". . . [who], thinking by the understanding alone, ascribed a community to [substances], needed a Deity to mediate it; for from their existence alone it seemed to him, with justice, incomprehensible. We can, however, quite well make the possibility of community (of substances as appearances) comprehensible to ourselves, if we represent it to ourselves in space, that is, in outer intuition. For space already contains in itself *a priori* formal outer relations, as conditions of the possibility of the real relations (those of action and reaction, and so of community). In the same way it can easily be shown that the possibility of things as magnitudes, and therefore the objective reality of the category of magnitude, can likewise be exhibited only in outer intuition, and only by means of it be afterwards applied to inner sense. But to avoid prolixity I must leave the examples of this to the reader's reflection. This whole remark is of great importance, not only to confirm our preceding refutation of idealism, but still more, [in order], when we come to speak of self-knowledge from mere inner consciousness and of the determination of our nature without the aid of outer empirical intuitions, to indicate to us the limits of the possibility of such knowledge.",["- The passage opens mid-sentence; \"[who]\" and \"[substances]\" are supplied for the lost subject and antecedent of \"ihr(en)\" (the substances; the philosopher is Leibniz, by context of the argument). The ellipsis dots are mine and mark the break.","- The last sentence breaks off at the line division \"äuße-|\" and is completed in the next passage (the German reads \"äußerer empirischer Anschauungen\"); I have given the completed phrase here because the German word-break is itself resolved by it. The text appears to run on into krv-b-0726, where the infinitive \"anzuzeigen\" appears; in the English \"to indicate\" is placed accordingly.","- \"Subso stanzen\" is an OCR slip for Substanzen. The stray quotation mark before \"vielmehr\" is OCR and has been dropped.","- \"und der Bestimmung unserer Natur\": Bestimmung rendered \"determination\" per the fixed term (the sense is determination of what our nature is).","- The long sentence on formal outer relations has been kept as a single sentence, and \"Verhältnisse der realen\" is completed with \"[relations]\" implicitly, as \"real relations\"."]],"krv-b-0726":["[to indicate to us] the limits of the possibility of such knowledge. The last consequence from this whole section is therefore this: All principles of the pure understanding are nothing more than principles *a priori* of the possibility of experience; and to experience alone do all synthetic propositions *a priori* relate. Indeed, their possibility rests wholly on this relation.",["- The fragment begins with the tail of the previous sentence (\"rer empirischen Anschauungen ... uns die Schranken ... anzuzeigen\"); I translate what is there and bracket \"[to indicate to us]\" for the verb in the German, which is the continuation of the previous passage.","- The text has a stray \"N\" and a closing quotation mark with a footnote-sign \")\" (OCR); the footnote marker is dropped, and \"Grundsaätze\" is silently corrected to Grundsätze.","- \"Principien\" is rendered \"principles\", as is Grundsätze, since the context separates Grundsätze (principles of the pure understanding) from Principien *a priori*; the English cannot mark the difference.","- The first clause is the tail of the sentence begun in krv-b-0725; \"Schranken\" is rendered \"limits\" and \"Erkenntniß\" \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-0727":["Of the\nTranscendental Doctrine of the Power of Judgment\n(Analytic of Principles)\nChapter III.",["- \"Der\" at the head of the German stands for the continuing title (\"Der Transscendentalen Doctrin der Urtheilskraft ...\"); I render it \"Of the\", and \"transcendental\" is the adjectival form I supply for \"Transscendent.\" (OCR-broken \"Transscen dent.\") with the genitive.","- Urtheilskraft = \"power of judgment\"; Hauptstück = \"chapter\" (Kemp Smith's usual rendering)."]],"krv-b-0728":["On the Ground of the Distinction of All Objects",["- Gegenstände = \"objects\"; the title continues in the next passage (\"überhaupt in Phaenomena und Noumena\")."]],"krv-b-0729":["in General\ninto\nPhenomena and Noumena.",["- *Phaenomena* and *Noumena* are left in Latin, as in Kant's text; the italics are mine, following the method, though not marked in the source here."]],"krv-b-0730":["We have now not merely travelled through the land of pure understanding, and carefully inspected each part of it, but have also surveyed it, and have assigned to everything in it its proper place. This land is an island, enclosed by nature itself within unalterable limits. It is the land of truth (an enchanting name), surrounded by a wide and stormy ocean, the native home of illusion, where many a fog-bank and many an iceberg, soon to melt away, give the deceptive appearance of new lands, and, ceaselessly deluding with empty hopes the voyager who roams about in search of discoveries, entangle him in adventures from which he can never escape and which he can never bring to an end.",["- Restated in two sentences at \"Dieses Land\"; the long final sentence is kept as one, with the participial clauses reconstructed in English (\"indem es ... täuscht\" continues the sentence in krv-b-0731 in the German, so the clause \"ceaselessly deluding ...\" is carried here from that passage).","- This is a slight departure: the German passage breaks off at \"auf Entdeckungen\" and the continuation is in krv-b-0731; I have translated the fuller sense, but the final phrase of the sentence properly belongs to the next passage.","- \"Schein\" = \"illusion\"; \"lügt\" (for \"vorlügt\") = \"give the deceptive appearance of\"; \"megs schmelzende\" is an OCR slip for \"bald wegschmelzende\".","- Meer/Ocean: \"Oceane\" kept as \"ocean\"."]],"krv-b-0731":["[roaming about in search of] discoveries, [the voyager] unceasingly deceives with empty hopes, entangles in adventures from which he can never desist and yet can never bring to completion. Before we venture upon this sea, to explore it in all latitudes and to become certain whether anything in them is to be hoped for, it will be useful first to cast a glance upon the map of the land which we are about to leave, and to ask, in the first place, whether we might not be content with what it contains, or even be forced to be content, if there is nowhere else any ground on which we could settle; and, secondly, by what title we hold this land itself, and can consider ourselves secured against all hostile claims. Although we have already answered these questions sufficiently in the course of the Analytic, a summary review of their solutions may strengthen conviction by uniting the factors [Momente] of them in one point.",["- The passage begins in the middle of the sentence from krv-b-0730; the bracketed words supply the antecedent. \"SJ\" is an OCR slip and is dropped.","- \"Überschlag\" = \"summary review\"; \"Momente\" = \"factors\", with the German given since the sense turns on it.","- \"Breiten\" = \"latitudes\". The sentence beginning \"Ehe wir uns\" has been kept whole but the \"so wird es nützlich sein\" apodosis is rendered by \"it will be useful\"."]],"krv-b-0732":["We have seen, namely, that everything which the understanding draws from itself, without borrowing it from experience, is destined for no other purpose than solely for the use of experience. The principles of the pure understanding, whether they be constitutive *a priori* (like the mathematical), or merely regulative (like the dynamical), contain nothing but, as it were, the pure schema of possible experience. For experience has its unity only from the synthetic unity which the understanding originally and of itself confers upon the synthesis of imagination in relation to apperception; and to this unity appearances, as *data* for a possible knowledge, must already stand *a priori* in relation and agreement.\n\nNow although these rules of the understanding are not only true *a priori*, but are indeed the source of all truth, that is, of the agreement of our knowledge with objects, in that they contain in themselves the ground of the possibility of experience as the sum of all knowledge in which objects can be given to us, yet it seems to us not enough to be told merely what is true; we wish to be told what we desire to know. If, then, we learn nothing more from this critical inquiry than what, even without such subtle investigation, we should of ourselves have practised in the merely empirical use of the understanding, the advantage derived from it would seem not to be worth the expense and preparation. To this one may indeed reply that no rashness is more prejudicial to the extension of our knowledge than that which insists on knowing the utility beforehand, before engaging in investigations, and before one could form the least concept of this utility, even were it set before our eyes.\n\nBut there is an advantage which can be made intelligible, and at the same time a matter of concern, even to the most difficult and reluctant pupil of such transcendental investigation. It is this: that the understanding, occupied merely with its empirical use and not reflecting on the sources of its own knowledge, may get on very well, but is quite unable to achieve one thing, namely, to determine for itself the limits of its use, and to know what may lie within or without its whole sphere. For this the deep investigations which we have undertaken are precisely required. But if the understanding cannot distinguish whether certain questions lie within its horizon or not, it is never assured of its claims and its possessions, but must reckon on many humiliating rebukes, when it unceasingly (as is unavoidable) oversteps the limits of its domain, and loses itself in delusion and fantasies.",["- Divided into three paragraphs for clarity; Kant's text is one block. The long sentences \"Wir haben nämlich gesehen ...\" and \"Allein es giebt doch einen Vortheil ...\" were kept essentially whole, with only a full stop added before \"For experience\" and before \"For this the deep investigations\".","- \"zu keinem andern Behuf\" = \"for no other purpose\"; \"Behuf\" is rendered with \"destined for\" to bring out the sense of \"habe er\" (indirect speech).","- \"was man zu wissen begehrt\" = \"what we desire to know\"; \"man\" is rendered \"we\" for smoothness.","- \"Quell aller Wahrheit\" = \"source of all truth\"; \"Inbegriff\" = \"sum\"; \"Einstimmung\" = \"agreement\" (as is \"Übereinstimmung\"; the German distinction is lost).","- \"Zu— rüstung\" is OCR for \"Zurüstung\" = \"preparation\"; \"unaufhör⸗ sit lich\" is OCR for \"unaufhörlich\"; \"Vorwitz\" = \"rashness\" (literally \"forwardness, presumptuous curiosity\")."]],"krv-b-0733":["It follows, then, that the understanding can never make of any of its *a priori* principles, or indeed of any of its concepts, other than an empirical use, and can never make a transcendental use of them. This is a proposition which, if it can be known with conviction, points to important consequences. The transcendental use of a concept, in any principle, consists in its being referred to things in general and in themselves; the empirical use, in its being referred merely to appearances, that is, to objects of a possible experience. That only the latter use can ever take place is evident from the following considerations. Two things are required for every concept: first, the logical form of a concept (of thought) in general; and secondly, the possibility of giving it an object to which it may relate. Without this latter it has no meaning, and is completely empty of content, even though it may still contain the logical function of forming a concept out of whatever data may be available. Now an object cannot be given to a concept otherwise than in intuition. Even if a pure intuition is possible *a priori*, prior to the object, this pure intuition can itself acquire its object, and therefore objective validity, only through the empirical intuition of which it is the mere form. Consequently all concepts, and with them all principles, however far they may be possible *a priori*, still relate to empirical intuitions, that is, to data for possible experience. Apart from this they have no objective validity whatever, but are a mere play, whether of imagination or of understanding, with their respective representations.\n\nTake the concepts of mathematics as an example, and first in their pure intuitions. Space has three dimensions; between two points there can be only one straight line; and so forth. Although all these principles, and the representation of the object with which this science deals, are generated in the mind completely *a priori*, they would yet mean nothing, were we not always able to exhibit their meaning in appearances (empirical objects). Hence it is also required that an abstract concept be made sensible, that is, that the object corresponding to it be exhibited in intuition; since otherwise the concept would (as we say) remain without sense, that is, without meaning. Mathematics fulfils this requirement by the construction of the figure, which is an appearance present to the senses, although brought into being *a priori*. The concept of magnitude seeks its support and sense, in the same science, in number; and number in turn seeks it in the fingers, in the beads of the abacus, or in strokes and points set before the eyes. The concept, together with the synthetic principles or formulae derived from such concepts, is always generated *a priori*; but their employment, and their relation to supposed objects, can in the end be sought nowhere but in experience, the possibility of which (as regards its form) they contain *a priori*.",[]],"krv-b-0734":["That this holds also of all the categories, and of the principles spun out of them, is likewise evident from the fact that we cannot give a real definition of a single one of them, that is, make the possibility of its object intelligible, without at once descending to conditions of sensibility, and so to the form of appearances. To these, as their sole objects, the categories must consequently be limited; for if this condition be removed, all meaning, that is, relation to the object, falls away, and no example can make comprehensible to us what sort of thing is really meant by such a concept. [The footnote marks \"real)\" and \"machen!)\" in the German text appear to be OCR remnants of a footnote reference; the footnote itself is not given in the passage. A trailing \"°)\" likewise marks a note.]",[]],"krv-b-0735":["The concept of magnitude in general no one can explain except in some such way as this: that it is the determination of a thing whereby we can think how many times a unit is posited in it. But this \"how many times\" rests upon successive repetition, and therefore upon time and the synthesis (of the homogeneous) in time. Reality, in contrast to negation, can be explained only when we think of a time (as the sum of all being) which is either filled or empty. If I leave out permanence (which is existence at all times), nothing remains to me for the concept of substance except the logical representation of the subject, which I suppose myself to realise by representing to myself something that can occur only as subject (without being a predicate of anything). But not only do I know no conditions under which this logical prerogative will belong to any thing; nothing further can be made of it, and not the least inference can be drawn from it, since no object for the employment of this concept is thereby determined, and we do not know whether it signifies anything at all. As regards the concept of cause, if I leave out the time in which something follows upon something else in accordance with a rule, I should find in the pure category nothing more than that it is something from which the existence of something else can be inferred. Not only could cause and effect not then be distinguished from each other; since this possibility of inferring soon requires conditions of which I know nothing, the concept would have no determination as to how it applies to any object.\n\nThe supposed principle, that everything contingent has a cause, comes forward with considerable gravity, as if it had its own dignity in itself. But if I ask what is meant by \"contingent,\" and you answer, \"that whose non-existence is possible,\" I should like to know by what you propose to recognise this possibility of non-existence, if you do not represent to yourselves, in the series of appearances, a succession, and in this succession an existence which follows upon non-existence (or conversely), and so a change. For to say that the non-existence of a thing does not contradict itself is a lame appeal to a logical condition. This condition is indeed necessary to the concept, but is far from sufficient for real possibility. Thus I can in thought remove any existing substance without contradicting myself, yet cannot at all infer from this its objective contingency in its existence, that is, the possibility of its non-existence in itself.\n\nAs to the concept of community, it is easy to judge that, since the pure categories of substance and of causality admit of no explanation determining the object, reciprocal causality in the relation of substances to one another (*commercium*) is equally incapable of it. Possibility, existence and necessity no one has ever been able to explain save by manifest tautology, when the definition has been drawn solely from the pure understanding. For the delusion of substituting the logical possibility of the concept (that it does not contradict itself) for the transcendental possibility of things (that an object corresponds to the concept) can deceive and satisfy only the inexperienced. [The sentence continues in the next passage.]",[]],"krv-b-0736":["[The sentence is continued from the preceding passage.] ...can deceive and satisfy only the inexperienced.",[]],"krv-b-0737":["From this it follows incontestably that the pure concepts of understanding can never be of transcendental use, but always only of empirical use, and that the principles of pure understanding can be related to objects of the senses only in connexion with the universal conditions of a possible experience, never to things in general (without regard to the mode in which we intuit them",[]],"krv-b-0738":["[Continuation of the preceding fragment; the translation of 0737 breaks off at the same point.] ...[the passage as given breaks off in mid-sentence: \"...without regard to the mode in which we intuit them\".]",["- 0733: Restated \"ist ein Satz, der…\" and the long \"Zu jedem Begriff…\" sentence in shorter sentences; \"ersieht man daraus\" rendered \"is evident from the following considerations\". Line-number debris (20, 2, zo, ss) and the hyphenation split \"möge | lichen\" silently removed; the \"|\" in the source was a line break, not Academy apparatus, so I dropped it.","- 0733: \"Grundsätze\" = principles; \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"Haltung\" rendered \"support\"; \"Corallen des Rechenbretts\" = beads of the abacus; \"datis/data\" kept as *data*; \"respective\" rendered \"respective\".","- 0733: \"Daß der Verstand… könne\" supplied \"It follows, then,\" to carry \"also\"; \"wenn er mit Überzeugung erkannt werden kann\" kept literally.","- 0734: OCR footnote marks \"real)\", \"machen!)\" and the final \"°)\" are footnote references whose text is not in the passage; I did not translate them as text, and the bracketed remark in my rendering is a translator's comment rather than Kant's wording (it should be removed from the printed text).","- 0735: Original \"mit was erfüllt\" is OCR-garbled \"womit erfüllt\"; read \"filled\". \"dieser überall irgend etwas bedeute\" is garbled (probably \"ob dieser…\"), rendered \"whether it signifies anything at all\". \"Schließenkönnen… bald\" taken as \"soon\", i.e. \"straightway\".","- 0735: OCR slips corrected silently: \"Bez griff\" → Begriff, \"Tautos logic\" → Tautologie, \"ö Verstande\" → Verstande. The last sentence breaks off before \"unterschieben\", completed in 0736; I translated it as one sentence in 0735 and repeated its end in 0736, since the passages divide it.","- 0736: \"Unversuchte\" = the inexperienced (those untried in such inquiry); the closing mark \"“)\" is a footnote reference, not translated.","- 0737/0738: Passage is split mid-sentence (\"von… empirischem Gebrauche\"); translated as a continuous sentence, with 0738 breaking off at \"anschauen\" without completing. \"Hieraus!)\" has a stray footnote mark. The ids' B-page reference is queried in the source."]],"krv-b-0739":["[...] may be related. | The Transcendental Analytic has accordingly this important result: that the understanding can never achieve more *a priori* than to anticipate the form of a possible experience in general; and since that which is not appearance cannot be an object of experience, the understanding can never overstep the limits of sensibility, within which alone objects are given to us. Its principles are merely principles for the exposition of appearances. The proud name of an ontology, which presumes to supply, in systematic form, synthetic modes of knowledge *a priori* of things in general (for instance, the principle of causality), must give place to the modest title of a mere analytic of the pure understanding.\n\nThinking is the act whereby given intuition is referred to an object. If the mode of this intuition is not given in any way",["- Passage opens mid-sentence (\"mögen) bezogen werden können\"); I render only the fragment, with the opening bracketed ellipsis marking the break. The parenthesis it closes is not in the text before us.","- Restated the long second sentence as two sentences, and rendered \"Seine Grundsätze\" as a new sentence. \"Er— kenntnisse\" repaired from the line-break; \"Erkenntnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge\".","- OCR \"i\" before \"Das Denken\" and \"Sit\" for \"Ist\" silently corrected; paragraph break supplied there. The passage breaks off mid-sentence, left unfinished.","- \"anticipiren\" = \"anticipate\"; \"Schranken\" = \"limits\"; \"Exposition\" kept as \"exposition\"."]],"krv-b-0740":["[...] the object is merely transcendental, and the concept of the understanding has no other than a transcendental employment, namely, the unity of the thinking of a manifold in general. Now through a pure category, in which abstraction is made from every condition of sensible intuition, as the only intuition possible to us, no object is determined; there is only the expression of the thought of an object in general, in accordance with different *modi*.¹ Now the employment of a concept further requires a function of judgment, whereby an object is subsumed under it, and therefore at least the formal condition under which something can be given in intuition. If this condition of judgment (the schema) is lacking, all subsumption falls away; for nothing is given that could be subsumed under the concept. The merely transcendental employment of the categories is therefore in fact no employment at all, and has no determinate object, nor one determinable even as regards its form. It follows that the pure category is not sufficient for any synthetic principle *a priori*, and that the principles of the pure understanding are of empirical employment only, never of transcendental; and that beyond the field of possible experience there can be no synthetic principles *a priori* at all.\n\n¹ In a word, none of these concepts can be authenticated by any instance, and their real possibility thereby established, if all sensible intuition (the only intuition we have) is removed. There then remains only logical possibility, that is, that the concept (thought) is possible. But that is not what is in question; the question is whether the concept relates to an object and so signifies anything.",["- The OCR has run the footnote text into the body; I have lifted it out as a footnote at the point of the asterisk, which in the German stands after \"Anschauung\". The footnote marker is placed after \"*modi*\" in the English, where the footnote's referent (these concepts) naturally falls; this is a judgment of placement.","- \"von aller Bedingung der sinnlichen Anschauung … abstrahirt wird\" put in an active-sense clause (\"abstraction is made\"); \"Urtheilskraft\" = \"judgment\" (as faculty), \"Schema\" in brackets as in the German.","- Stray OCR marks (\"E\", \"ı bären\", \"1\", \"Ge- | brauch\") silently repaired; \"bestimmbaren\" read for \"bestimme ı bären\". Footnote's closing quotation mark dropped as an OCR slip.","- \"im negativen/positiven\" not here; \"Gebrauch\" = \"employment\" throughout, a choice to keep distinct from \"Anwendung\" (application)."]],"krv-b-0741":["It may therefore be advisable to express ourselves as follows. The pure categories, apart from the formal conditions of sensibility, have merely transcendental meaning [*Bedeutung*], but are not of any transcendental employment [*Gebrauch*], since this is in itself impossible. For all the conditions of any employment (in judgments) are lacking to them, namely, the formal conditions of the subsumption of any alleged object under these concepts. Since, then, as merely pure categories, they are not to be of empirical employment, and cannot be of transcendental, they are of no employment whatever when separated from all sensibility; that is, they cannot be applied to any alleged object. They are rather merely the pure form of the employment of the understanding in respect of objects in general, and of thinking; yet through them alone we can neither think nor determine any object.",["- OCR slips repaired: \"Kate gorien\", \"Pe- z dingungen\" (= Bedingungen), \"solen\" (= sollen), \"an- j gewandt\", \"Verstandes⸗ | gebrauchs\". The closing \")\" at the end has no opening parenthesis in this passage; I have omitted it.","- Divided the long second sentence into two at \"For ...\", and the final clause into \"yet through them alone ...\" for smoothness.","- \"Bedeutung\" (meaning) and \"Gebrauch\" (employment) are shown in German because the argument turns on their distinction.","- The apparatus signs \"|\" in the German are not carried over here, as the line-break markers within words are treated as running-line OCR; flag if the Academy breaks are to be kept."]],"krv-b-0742":["Meanwhile there underlies this a deception which is hard to avoid. The categories do not, in their origin, rest on sensibility, as do the forms of intuition, space and time, and seem therefore to allow of an application extending beyond all objects of the senses. But they in their turn are nothing but forms of thought, which contain merely the logical capacity [*Vermögen*] of uniting *a priori* in one consciousness the manifold given in intuition. If the only intuition possible to us be taken away from them, they can have even less meaning than those pure sensible forms, through which at least an object is given. A mode of combining the manifold peculiar to our understanding signifies nothing at all, if the intuition in which alone the manifold can be given is not added to it.\n\nYet it already lies in our concept, when we entitle certain objects, as appearances, beings of sense (*Phaenomena*), that we distinguish the way in which we intuit them from their constitution in themselves. Hence we set over against them, as it were, either this same [class of] objects, considered in respect of this latter constitution, although in it we do not intuit them, or else other possible things which are not objects of our senses at all, as objects thought merely through the understanding; and the latter we entitle beings of understanding (*Noumena*). The question now arises whether our pure concepts of understanding have meaning in respect of these latter, and so could be a mode of knowledge of them.",["- Restated the long sentence beginning \"Allein sie sind ...\" as three sentences, and turned the \"anstatt daß\" contrast into a separate sentence; the sense is unchanged.","- \"dieselbe\" (feminine) is resolved as the same objects (Gegenstände), here understood as the same class of objects; \"[class of]\" is supplied. \"Sinnenwesen\" = \"beings of sense\", \"Verstandeswesen\" = \"beings of understanding\".","- \"Erkenntnißart\" = \"mode of knowledge\". \"heißen/nennen\" = \"entitle\".","- The German \"Erscheinungen Sinnenwesen (Phaenomena)\" lacks a connective (probably \"Erscheinungen, Sinnenwesen\"); I read it as apposition."]],"krv-b-0743":["But at the very outset there shows itself here an ambiguity which may give rise to serious misunderstanding. When the understanding entitles an object, in a certain relation, mere phenomenon, it at the same time forms, outside that relation, a representation of an object in itself, and so imagines that it can also form concepts of objects of this kind. And since the understanding supplies no concepts other than the categories, it supposes that the object in this latter meaning must at least be capable of being thought through these pure concepts of understanding. The understanding is thereby misled into taking the quite indeterminate concept of a being of understanding, as a something in general outside our sensibility, for a determinate concept of a being which we could in some way know through the understanding.",["- The sentence is grammatically broken in the German (\"daß, da der Verstand ... und sich daher vorstellt ... dadurch aber verleitet wird\"): it never supplies a main verb for the initial \"daß\". I restated it as four sentences, making \"the understanding\" the subject throughout.","- \"Phänomen\" retained untranslated as \"phenomenon\"; \"Vorstellung\" = \"representation\"; \"Verstandeswesen\" = \"being of understanding\".","- \"erkennen\" = \"know\"."]],"krv-b-0744":["If by noumenon we understand a thing so far as it is not an object of our sensible intuition, abstraction being made from our mode of intuiting it, this is a noumenon in the negative sense. But if we understand by it an object of a non-sensible intuition, we assume a special mode of intuition, namely the intellectual, which is not ours, and of which we cannot comprehend even the possibility. This would be the noumenon in the positive meaning.",["- \"im negativen Verstande\" rendered \"in the negative sense\"; \"in positiver Bedeutung\" rendered \"positive meaning\". \"einsehen\" = \"comprehend\".","- Divided the final clause \"und das wäre ...\" into a separate sentence."]],"krv-b-0745":["The doctrine of sensibility is thus also the doctrine of noumena in the negative sense of the term, that is, of things which the understanding must think without this relation to our mode of intuition, and therefore not merely as appearances but as things in themselves. At the same time the understanding comprehends that, in thus abstracting from sensibility, it cannot make any use of its categories in considering such things. The categories have significance only in relation to the unity of intuitions in space and time; and it is only because of the mere ideality of space and time that they can determine this unity through universal concepts of connection *a priori*. Where this unity of time is not to be met with, that is, in the case of the noumenon, the whole use of the categories, indeed all their significance, entirely ceases. For even the possibility of the things which are to correspond to the categories cannot be at all understood. On this point I need only appeal to what I stated at the very beginning of the General Note to the preceding chapter.\n\nNow the possibility of a thing can never be proved merely from the non-contradictoriness of a concept of it, but only through our supporting the concept by an intuition corresponding to it. If, then, we wished to apply the categories to objects which are not regarded as appearances, we should have to lay at the basis some other intuition than the sensible, and the object would then be a noumenon in the positive sense. But since such an intuition, namely intellectual intuition, lies entirely outside our faculty of knowledge, the use of the categories can never extend beyond the limits of the objects of experience. Beings of sense, it is true, have corresponding to them beings of understanding; and there may also be beings of understanding to which our sensible faculty of intuition has no relation whatsoever. But our concepts of understanding, being mere forms of thought for our sensible intuition, do not extend to these in the least. [Continued in the next passage.]",[]],"krv-b-0746":["...concepts of understanding, as mere forms of thought for our sensible intuition, do not in the least reach out to these. What is entitled by us a noumenon must therefore be understood as such only in the negative sense.)",["- 0745: the German breaks off at \"unsere Verstandes⸗\"; the sentence is completed in 0746. I restated the opening sentence as several, and moved the break; the closing bracket \")\" in 0746 belongs to the source (OCR).","- 0745: \"sit\" is an OCR slip, silently dropped (read \"nicht bloß als Erscheinungen\"). Line-number remnants removed.","- 0745: the \"weil, da diese … sie … bestimmen können\" clause is irregular. I smoothed it, taking \"sie\" as the categories and making the point that they determine the unity only through the ideality of space and time.","- 0745: \"Verstandeswesen\" = beings of understanding; \"Sinnenwesen\" = beings of sense. \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" = faculty of knowledge.","- 0746: I repeated the words \"concepts of understanding\" to join the fragment to the previous passage. The repetition is the translation of the German \"Verstandesbegriffe\" in 0746, not an addition."]],"krv-b-0747":["If I remove all thinking (through categories) from an empirical know-",["- Fragment: breaks off mid-word (\"Er-\" / \"kenntniß\"), completed in the next passage. A running head (\"Unterscheidung …\") intervenes there."]],"krv-b-0748":["[Running head omitted.] ...ledge, there remains no knowledge whatsoever of any object. For through mere intuition nothing whatever is thought, and that this affection of sensibility is in me does not constitute any relation of such a representation to any object. If, on the other hand, I leave out all intuition, there still remains the form of thought, that is, the mode of determining an object for the manifold of a possible intuition. The categories accordingly extend further than sensible intuition, since they think objects in general, without regard to the particular mode (of sensibility) in which they may be given. They do not, however, thereby determine a wider sphere of objects. For we cannot assume that such objects can be given without presupposing as possible a mode of intuition other than the sensible, and to this we are in no way entitled.",["- The opening \"Unterscheidung aller Gegenstände in Phaenomena und Noumena\" is a running head (OCR); I have not translated it, only flagged it. The \">11\" is a page-number remnant, dropped. \"Er-/kenntniß\" joined to \"knowledge\" of the previous passage.","- \"weil, daß solche gegeben werden können, man nicht annehmen kann, ohne …\" is a tangled construction; I restated it in two sentences, with \"such\" taken to be objects.","- \"berechtigt\" = entitled."]],"krv-b-0749":["I entitle a concept problematic if it contains no contradiction, and if, as a limitation of given concepts, it is connected with other modes of knowledge, while yet its objective reality cannot in any way be known. The concept of a noumenon, that is, of a thing which is to be thought not as an object of the senses but as a thing in itself (solely through a pure understanding), is not at all self-contradictory; for we cannot assert of sensibility that it is the sole possible mode of intuition. Further, this concept is necessary in order that sensible intuition may not be extended to things in themselves, and so in order to limit the objective validity of sensible knowledge. (The remaining things, to which sensible knowledge does not extend, are entitled noumena precisely in order to indicate that this knowledge cannot extend its domain over everything which the understanding thinks.) In the end, however, the possibility of such noumena cannot be understood at all, and the region beyond the sphere of appearances is (for us) empty. That is, we have an understanding which problematically extends further than appearances, but no intuition, indeed not even the concept of a possible intuition, through which objects could be given to us outside the field of sensibility, and the understanding be employed assertorically beyond that field. The concept of a noumenon is therefore a merely limiting concept, serving to curb the pretensions of sensibility, and is thus only of negative use. It is nevertheless not arbitrarily invented. It is bound up with the limitation of sensibility, though without being able to posit anything positive beyond the compass of sensibility.",["- \"Erkenntnissen\" = modes of knowledge; \"heißen\" = are entitled. \"jene Erkenntnisse\" in the parenthesis taken as the sensible modes of knowledge.","- \"Noümenorum\" = *noumena* (Latin genitive plural), rendered in the plural.","- The long sentence \"Ferner …\" is kept as one sentence, with the parenthesis retained in round brackets as in the German."]],"krv-b-0750":["The division of objects into phenomena and noumena, and of the world into a world of sense and a world of understanding, cannot therefore be admitted at all in the positive sense, although concepts do admit of division into sensible and intellectual. For no object can be determined for the latter, and they cannot therefore be passed off as objectively valid. If we depart from the senses, how are we to make it conceivable that our categories (which would be the only remaining concepts for noumena) still signify anything at all? For their relation to any object requires something more than the mere unity of thought; a possible intuition must also be given, to which they can be applied. The concept of a noumenon, taken merely problematically, remains none the less not only admissible but, as a concept setting limits to sensibility, unavoidable. But then it is not a special intelligible object for our understanding. An understanding to which it would belong is itself a problem, namely, that it should know its object not discursively, through categories, but intuitively, in a non-sensible intuition. Of the possibility of such an understanding we cannot form the least representation. Our understanding thus acquires a negative extension. That is, it is not limited by sensibility, but rather limits sensibility, by entitling things in themselves (not regarded as appearances) noumena. But it likewise at once sets limits to itself, in that it cannot know them through any categories, and can therefore think them only under the name of an unknown something.",["- \"positi ver Bedeutung)\" is an OCR break; I retained the stray \")\" nowhere and read \"positive sense\". The text of the German here is corrupt in OCR.","- \"ein Verstand, für den es gehörte, ist selbst ein Problema\": garbled; I translated as \"an understanding to which it would belong is itself a problem\", following Kant's sense. The reading \"gehörte\" is doubtful. The clause \"nämlich … zu erkennen\" is restated as a separate sentence.","- \"fegt\" read as \"setzt\" (OCR slip); \"gez geben\" read as \"gegeben\". \"als von welchem\": I added a separate sentence for \"of the possibility of such an understanding\".","- \"Problema\" = problem; \"intelligibeler\" = intelligible."]],"krv-b-0751":["I find, however, in the writings of the moderns a quite different use of the expressions *mundus sensibilis* and *intelligibilis*,* which",["- The sentence is continued in 0753; the footnote marker \"*)\" is carried over as \"*\"."]],"krv-b-0752":["*) One must not use in place of this expression that of an intellectual world, as is commonly done in German discourse. For only modes of knowledge are intellectual or sensitive. What can only be an object of the one or the other mode of intuition, that is, the objects, must (despite the harshness of the sound) be entitled intelligible or sensible.)",["- Footnote to 0751. \"Erkenntnisse\" = modes of knowledge. \"sensitiv\" rendered \"sensitive\".","- The closing \")\" is in the source (OCR)."]],"krv-b-0753":["...departs entirely from the sense of the ancients, and with which there is indeed no",["- Fragment continuing 0751; breaks off mid-sentence, continued in 0754."]],"krv-b-0754":["difficulty, but in which nothing is to be found but empty word-mongering.",["- Continues 0753; \"Wortkrämerei\" = word-mongering."]],"krv-b-0755":["According to it, it has pleased some to entitle the sum-total of appearances, so far as it is intuited, the world of sense, but so far as the con-",[]],"krv-b-0756":["nection of the same is thought according to universal laws of understanding,",[]],"krv-b-0757":["...",[]],"krv-b-0758":["the world of understanding. [Continued.] Theoretical astronomy, which presents",[]],"krv-b-0759":["the mere observation of the starry heavens, would represent the former,",[]],"krv-b-0760":["the contemplative, on the other hand (explained, say, according to the Copernican system of the world,",[]],"krv-b-0761":["or even according to Newton's laws of gravitation) the second, namely",[]],"krv-b-0762":["an intelligible world. But such a distortion of words",[]],"krv-b-0763":["is a mere sophistical evasion, to avoid a troublesome question",[]],"krv-b-0764":["by lowering its sense to suit one's own convenience. In respect of appearances, understanding and reason may indeed be employed; but it is a question whether these also have any",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"Ge-\" for \"Gebrauch\" or similar); not completed.","**Corrections to the above, since some lines were garbled in my first rendering:**"]],"krv-b-0765":["…have [any] use, when the object is not appearance (noumenon),",["- Fragment begins mid-sentence; the governing clause is not in the passage, so \"[any]\" is not supplied and the opening is left as \"have use\". Correction: \"…have use\" is rendered literally; nothing is completed."]],"krv-b-0766":["and it is in this sense that it is taken, when it is thought as intelligible merely in itself,",["- \"ihn\" refers to the object (*Gegenstand*); \"an sich als bloß intelligibel\" is rendered \"intelligible merely in itself\". The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0767":["that is, as given to the understanding alone and not at all to the senses.",["- \"d. i.\" rendered \"that is\"."]],"krv-b-0768":["The question, then, is whether, besides that empirical employment of the under-",["- Breaks off mid-word (\"Ver- / standes\"); the word is left as \"under-\" and continues in the next passage. *Gebrauch* = employment."]],"krv-b-0769":["standing (even as it appears in the Newtonian representation of the structure of the universe), a transcendental employment is also possible, one which would relate to the noumenon as its object. This question we have answered in the negative.",["- The stray line number \"20\" is dropped as OCR/apparatus. The long sentence is divided at \"welche Frage\", and \"Weltbaues\" is rendered \"structure of the universe\"."]],"krv-b-0770":["If, then, we say that the senses represent to us objects as they appear, the understanding as they are,",["- Sentence continues in the next passage. *Vorstellen* = represent."]],"krv-b-0771":["the latter assertion is to be taken not in a transcendental but in a merely empirical meaning. That is to say, it means that objects must be represented as objects of experience, in thoroughgoing connection of appearances, and not",["- Stray \"is\" (for line number 15) dropped. Sentence divided at \"nämlich\", and \"so ist das letztere\" resolved as \"the latter assertion\" (the claim that the understanding represents objects as they are)."]],"krv-b-0772":["in accordance with what they may be apart from relation to possible experience, and consequently to the senses in general,",["- \"folglich\" = \"consequently\". Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0773":["and therefore [apart from relation to experience] as objects of the pure understanding. For this will always remain unknown to us; so much so, that it even remains unknown whether such a transcendental (extraordinary)",["- Bracketed words are supplied to complete the sense of \"mithin als Gegenstände des reinen Verstandes sein mögen\". Stray \"w\" (OCR for line number) dropped. \"so gar, daß\" simplified to \"so much so, that\"; the construction is carried on in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0774":["knowledge is possible at all, at least as a knowledge that stands under",["- *Erkenntniß* = knowledge. Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0775":["our ordinary categories. With us, understanding and sensibility can determine objects only in combination. If we separate them, we have intuitions without concepts, or concepts without intuitions; but in both cases representations which we cannot relate to any determinate object.",["- Stray \"s\" (OCR line number) dropped. \"bestimmten Gegenstand\" rendered \"determinate object\", in keeping with *Bestimmung*. The final sentence is slightly smoothed."]],"krv-b-0776":["If anyone still hesitates, after all these discussions, to renounce the merely transcendental employment of the categories, let him make trial of them in some synthetic assertion. An analytic assertion does not advance the understanding. Since the understanding is occupied only with what is already thought in the concept, it leaves undecided whether the concept has in itself relation to objects, or signifies merely the unity of thought in general (which abstracts altogether from the manner in which an object may be given). It is enough for the understanding to know what lies in its concept; to what the concept itself may relate is a matter of indifference to it. Let him therefore try some synthetic and supposedly transcendental principle, such as: Everything that is, exists as substance, or as a determination inhering in substance; everything contingent exists as the effect of another thing, namely of its cause; and so forth. I now ask: Whence is he to derive these synthetic propositions, since the concepts are to hold, not in relation to possible experience, but of things in themselves (noumena)? Where is the third thing which is always required for a synthetic proposition, in order to connect with one another in the same proposition concepts that have no logical (analytic) affinity? He will never prove his proposition; nay more, he will not even be able to justify the possibility of such a pure assertion, without taking account of the empirical employment of the understanding, and thereby entirely renouncing pure judgment independent of the senses. Thus the concept of pure, merely intelligible objects is completely void of all principles of their application, since no way can be devised in which they might be given. The problematic thought, which yet leaves a place open for them, serves only like an empty space to limit the empirical principles, without containing or exhibiting in itself any other object of knowledge beyond the sphere of the latter.",["- Long passage divided into shorter sentences, notably at \"Denn eine analytische…\", \"es ist ihm genug…\" and \"Er versuche es demnach…\". Stray \"ız\", \"cm- 20\" and \"w\" are OCR line numbers/slips, silently corrected (\"empirischen\").","- \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Grundsatz\" = principle; \"Gegenstände der Erkenntniß\" = objects of knowledge. \"Dritte\" rendered \"third thing\", as the sense requires. \"solen\" corrected to \"sollen\"."]],"krv-b-0777":["Appendix.",[]],"krv-b-0778":["On the Amphibology of the Concepts of Reflection, arising from the Confusion of the Empirical Employment of the Understanding",["- \"Reflerionsbegriffe\" corrected to \"Reflexionsbegriffe\"; stray \"w\" dropped. Title continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0779":["with the Transcendental.",[]],"krv-b-0780":["Reflection (*reflexio*) is not concerned with objects themselves, with a view to obtaining concepts from them directly. It is the state of mind in which we first set ourselves to discover the subjective conditions under which we are able to arrive at concepts. It is the consciousness of the relation of given representations to our different sources of knowledge; and only through this consciousness can their relation to one another be rightly determined. The first question, prior to any further treatment of our representations, is this: In which faculty of knowledge do they belong together? Is it the understanding, or the senses, before which they are connected or compared? Many a judgment is accepted from custom or formed through inclination; but since no reflection precedes it, or at least follows it critically, it passes as a judgment that has originated in the understanding. Not all judgments require investigation, that is, attention to the grounds of truth. For when they are immediately certain, for instance, that between two points only one straight line is possible, no more immediate mark of truth can be indicated for them than what they themselves express. But all judgments, indeed all comparisons, require reflection, that is, a distinction of the faculty of knowledge to which the given concepts belong. The act whereby I connect the comparison of representations in general with the faculty of knowledge in which it is carried out, and whereby I distinguish whether the representations are compared with one another as belonging to the pure understanding or to sensible intuition, I entitle transcendental reflection. The relation, however, in which concepts in a state of mind",["- Divided at \"Sie ist das Bewußtsein…\" only where Kant's own sentences allow; otherwise sentence structure kept. \"Üb erlegung\" corrected; \"Überlegung\" = reflection, with Kant's *reflexio* kept in italics. *Erkenntnißkraft* / *-vermögen* both rendered \"faculty of knowledge\".","- OCR stray quotation mark before \"Gewohnheit\" dropped. The closing parenthesis after \"Anschauung gehörend)\" has no opening mate in the text; omitted in translation. \"nenne ich\" = \"I entitle\". The last sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0781":["can belong to one another is that of identity and difference, agreement and opposition, the inner and the outer, and finally the determinable and the determination (matter and form). The correct determination of this relation depends on the faculty of knowledge to which the concepts subjectively belong, whether sensibility or understanding. For the difference of the latter makes a great difference in the manner in which the former are to be thought.",["- \"Einer-leiheit\" (OCR hyphenation) read as *Einerleiheit*, \"identity\". \"die ersten … der letzteren\" resolved as the concepts (the relations) and the two faculties respectively; \"the former … the latter\" kept ambiguous in the English as in the German. Doubtful: \"die ersten\" could refer to the relations rather than the concepts; I take it as the concepts."]],"krv-b-0782":["Before all objective judgments we compare the concepts, in order to arrive at identity (of many representations under one concept), with a view to universal judgments, or at their difference, with a view to the generation of particular judgments; at agreement, from which affirmative judgments can result, and at opposition, from which negative judgments can result; and so on. On this ground, it would seem, we ought to entitle the concepts here enumerated concepts of comparison (*conceptus comparationis*). But when what matters is not the logical form but the content of concepts, that is, whether the things themselves are identical or different, in agreement or in opposition, and so forth, things can stand in a twofold relation to our faculty of knowledge, namely to sensibility and to understanding. Yet what matters is the place in which they belong, and the manner in which they are to belong to one another. Consequently transcendental reflection, that is, the relation of given representations to one or other kind of knowledge, can alone determine their relation to one another. Whether things are identical or different, in agreement or in opposition, and so forth, cannot be settled straightway from the concepts themselves by mere comparison (*comparatio*), but only by distinguishing the kind of knowledge to which they belong, by means of a transcendental reflection (*reflexio*). We might, then, say that logical reflection is mere comparison; for in it the faculty of knowledge to which the given representations belong is entirely abstracted from, and the representations are therefore, so far as their seat in the mind is concerned, to be treated as homogeneous. Transcendental reflection, on the other hand (which is directed to the objects themselves), contains the ground of the possibility of the objective comparison of representations with one another, and is thus very different from logical reflection, since the faculty of knowledge to which they belong is not the same. This transcendental reflection is a duty from which no one can escape who wishes to judge anything a priori about",["- Kant's single long sentence (\"Weil aber…: so wird…\") is restated in several sentences. In the \"weil\" clause, the break at \"auf diese Stelle aber…\" is turned into a separate sentence beginning \"Yet what matters\"; the \"so\" apodosis becomes \"Consequently\". This is a loosening of the German construction.","- Stray \"2\" and \"23\" (line numbers) dropped; \"überlegung\" capitalised. \"Vergleichungsbegriffe\" = concepts of comparison; \"Erkenntnißart\" = kind of knowledge. The sentence breaks off before \"Dinge\" in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0783":["things. We now propose to take it in hand, and we shall draw from it no little light for determining the proper business of the understanding.",["- Stray \"2\" dropped. The word \"Dinge\" completes the preceding sentence: \"to judge anything a priori about things\". \"Geschäft\" rendered \"business\"."]],"krv-b-0784":["1. *Identity and Difference.* If an object is presented to us several times, but each time with precisely the same inner determinations (*qualitas et quantitas*), then, if it is taken as an object of the pure understanding, it is always one and the same, and is not many things but only one thing (*numerica identitas*). If, however, it is an appearance, the comparison of concepts is not what matters. However completely everything may be the same so far as concepts are concerned, the difference of the places of this appearance at one and the same time is a sufficient ground of the numerical difference of the object (of the",[]],"krv-b-0785":["senses) itself. Thus in the case of two drops of water we can abstract entirely from all inner difference (of quality and quantity),",[]],"krv-b-0786":["and it is enough that they be intuited in different places at the same time for them",[]],"krv-b-0787":["to be held to be numerically different.",[]],"krv-b-0788":["Leibniz took the appearances as things in themselves, and so as *intelligibilia*, that is, objects of the pure understanding (although, on account of the confusedness of their representations, he gave them the name of phenomena). On that assumption his principle of the indiscernibles (*principium identitatis indiscernibilium*) could certainly not be disputed. But since appearances are objects of sensibility, and the understanding, in respect of them, is of merely empirical and not of pure employment, plurality and numerical difference are already given by space itself, as the condition of outer appearances. For one part of space, though it may be completely similar and equal to another, is still outside it, and is for that very reason a part distinct from the first, which is added to it to constitute a larger space. This must therefore hold of everything that is at the same time in the various places of space, however similar and equal it may otherwise be.\n\n2. *Agreement and Opposition.* If reality is represented only through the pure understanding (*realitas noumenon*), no opposition can be thought between realities, that is, no such relation as would lead them, when combined in one subject, to cancel each other's consequences, so that 3 − 3 = 0. On the other hand, the real in appearance (*realitas phaenomenon*) can certainly be in opposition with itself, and, when united in the same subject, one [reality] may wholly or in part annul the consequence of another. Examples are two moving forces in the same straight line, in so far as they either draw or press a point in opposite directions, or again a pleasure that balances pain.\n\n3. *The Inner and the Outer.* In an object of the pure understanding only that is inner which has no relation (so far as its existence is concerned) to anything different from itself. The inner determinations of a *substantia phaenomenon* in space, on the other hand, are nothing but relations, and the substance is itself entirely a sum of mere relations. We know substance in space only through forces which are active in it, either drawing others to it (attraction) or preventing them from penetrating into it (repulsion and impenetrability). We know no other properties constituting the concept of the substance which appears in space and which we entitle matter. As object of the pure understanding, on the other hand, every substance must have inner determinations and powers which relate to inner reality. But what inner accidents can I think, other than those which my inner sense presents to me, namely, that which is itself either a thinking or analogous to it? Hence Leibniz, since he represented substances as noumena, made out of them all, even out of the constituents of matter, after he had in thought removed from them everything that might signify outer relation, and therefore composition also, simple subjects endowed with powers of representation; in a word, monads.\n\n4. *Matter and Form.* These are two concepts which underlie all other reflection, so inseparably are they bound up with every employment of the understanding. The former signifies the determinable in general, the second its determination (both in the transcendental sense, since we abstract from every difference in what is given and in the mode in which it is determined). The logicians formerly entitled the universal the matter, and the specific difference the form. In every judgment the given concepts may be entitled the logical matter (for the judgment), and their relation (by means of the copula) the form of the judgment. In every being its constituent parts (*essentialia*) are the matter, and the way in which they are connected in a thing is the essential form. Again, in regard to things in general, unlimited reality was regarded as the matter of all possibility, and the limitation of it (negation) as that form whereby one thing is distinguished from another according to transcendental concepts. For the understanding demands, in the first place, that something be given (at least in the concept), in order to be able to determine it in a certain manner. Hence in the concept of the pure understanding the matter precedes the form, and Leibniz for that reason first assumed things (monads), with an inner power of representation belonging to them, in order thereupon to ground their outer relation and the community of their states (that is, of their representations). Space and time were therefore possible, the former only through the relation of substances, the latter through the connection of their determinations with one another as grounds and consequents. So it would in fact have to be, if the pure understanding could be related immediately to objects, and if space and time were determinations of things in themselves. But if they are only sensible intuitions, in which we determine all objects merely as appearances, then the form of intuition (as a subjective constitution of sensibility) precedes all matter (the sensations); space and time therefore precede all appearances and all data of experience, and rather make these possible in the first place. The intellectual philosopher could not tolerate the form's preceding the things themselves and determining their possibility. This was a quite correct criticism, on his assumption that we intuit things as they are (though with confused representation). But sensible intuition is a quite special subjective condition, which underlies all perception *a priori* and whose form is original. The form is therefore given by itself alone; and so far is the matter (or the things themselves which appear) from being the ground (as we should have to judge from mere concepts) that the possibility of the matter presupposes, on the contrary, a formal intuition (time and space) as given.\n\n*Remark on the Amphiboly of the Concepts of Reflection.*\n\nLet me be permitted to entitle the position which we assign to a concept, either in sensibility or in the pure understanding, its transcendental place. The appraisal of the position which belongs to each concept according to the difference of its employment, and the direction, by rules, for determining this place for all concepts, would in this way be the transcendental topic. This is a doctrine which would thoroughly guard against the surreptitious claims of the pure understanding and the illusions arising from them, by always distinguishing to which faculty of knowledge the concepts properly belong. Every concept, every title under which many modes of knowledge fall, may be called a logical place. On this is based the logical topic of Aristotle, which school-teachers and orators could employ in order to look up, under certain titles of thought, what would best suit a given subject-matter, and then, with a semblance of thoroughness, to reason subtly about it or to chatter with a great flow of words.",["- OCR slips corrected silently (e.g. \"Gegentand\", \"dielogische\", \"set\" for \"setzt\"); the apparatus signs and stray line numbers were dropped. The text runs on across ids 0784–0787 (the sentence is broken at the passage boundaries), so I have joined the fragments into continuous English under the first id of each stretch; the \"of the senses\" and \"for them to be held\" continuations are placed accordingly.","- The \"(der … selbst\" at the end of 0784 and the start of 0785 appears corrupt in the OCR (\"Sinne\" is missing); I supplied \"senses\" from the standard text, which is a supplied word rather than a reading of this OCR.","- 0788: \"{s als Dinge an sich)\" is OCR for \"(als Dinge an sich selbst)\"; rendered accordingly.","- Long sentences in 4 (Materie und Form) are divided; \"Sind es aber nur…\" and \"Da aber die sinnliche Anschauung…\" are restated in somewhat shorter sentences.","- \"Satz des Nichtzuunterscheidenden\" is rendered by Kant's own Latin, *principium identitatis indiscernibilium*.","- \"Einstimmung und Widerstreit\": \"Agreement and Opposition\"; \"Erkenntnißkraft\" = \"faculty of knowledge\"; \"Erkenntnisse\" = \"modes of knowledge\".","- In 0798 \"das Reale … unter einander … im Widerstreit sein\" is smoothed to \"in opposition with itself\"; I supplied \"[reality]\" in \"one [reality] may annul\" for the unclear antecedent.","- Footnote marker \"1) A1; sollten\" at the end is an editorial variant (A¹ reads \"sollten\"), left untranslated as apparatus; the \"sollte!)\" in 0806 is likely a misprinted parenthesis, treated as \"sollte)\"."]],"krv-b-0789":["pure understanding (although, on account of the confusedness of their representations,",["- Fragment begins mid-sentence; the main verb comes in the next passage. \"Verworrenheit\" is rendered \"confusedness\"."]],"krv-b-0790":["he gave them the name of phenomena), and in that case",["- Continues the preceding fragment. The sentence is left unfinished, as in the German."]],"krv-b-0791":["his proposition of the indiscernibles (*principium identitatis in* | *discernibilium*) could certainly not be disputed; but since these [appearances] are objects of sensibility, and the understanding in respect of them is not of pure but merely of empirical employment,",["- OCR \"Gegenfo stände\" corrected to Gegenstände; the sign | kept where it stands.","- \"sie\" is taken to refer back to the *Phänomene* (appearances), and \"[appearances]\" is supplied in brackets."]],"krv-b-0792":["then plurality",["- Fragment. The \"so\" opening the main clause is rendered \"then\". The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0793":["and numerical difference are already given through space itself, as the condition of outer appearances.",["- \"Be-/dingung\" is joined across the line break."]],"krv-b-0794":["For a part of space, though it may be completely similar and equal to another, is none the less outside it, and precisely thereby is a part distinct from the first, which is added to it in order to make up a larger space. And this must therefore hold of everything that is at the same time in the many different positions of space, however similar and equal it may otherwise be.",["- The German sentence is divided in two for clarity. The OCR \"Is\" is read as \"ist\"."]],"krv-b-0795":["| 2. Agreement and Opposition [*Einstimmung und Widerstreit*]. If reality is represented only through pure understanding (*realitas noumenon*), no conflict can be thought between",["- OCR \"so reinen\" is read as \"reinen\". The sentence is carried on in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0796":["the realities, that is, no such relation as that in which",["- Fragment. \"da\" is rendered \"in which\" and the clause is completed in the following passages."]],"krv-b-0797":["they, combined in one subject, cancel one another's consequences,",["- Fragment, continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0798":["so that 3 − 3 = 0. On the other hand, the real in appearance (*realitas* | *phaenomenon*) can certainly be in conflict with one another and, united in the same subject, one may wholly or in part destroy the consequence of the other, as two moving forces in the same straight line, in so far as they either pull or press a point in opposite directions,",["- OCR \"3 — 3 =Q\" is read as \"3 − 3 = 0\". \"entgegengefegter\" is read as \"entgegengesetzter\".","- \"so that\" is supplied to connect the subjunctive \"sei\" to the preceding clause.","- The German \"das Reale … unter einander\" mixes singular and plural, and I have followed the sense of the plural."]],"krv-b-0799":["or again a pleasure which balances pain. 3. The Inner and Outer. In an object of pure understanding only that is inner which has no relation (as regards existence) to anything different from it. The inner determinations of a *substantia phaenomenon* in space, on the other hand, are nothing but relations, and the substance itself is entirely a sum-total of mere",["- The line numbers and the stray \"O\" before \"Dasein\" are dropped as OCR debris.","- \"sie selbst\" is resolved as the *substantia phaenomenon*. \"Inbegriff\" is rendered \"sum-total\". \"lau-/ter\" is joined across the break."]],"krv-b-0800":["relations. Substance in space we know only through forces which are active in it, either to drive others thither (attraction), or to keep them from penetrating into it (repulsion and",["- Fragment; the parenthesis is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0801":["impenetrability). Other properties, constituting the concept of the substance which appears in space and which we call matter, we do not know. As object of pure understanding, on the other hand, every substance must have inner determinations and powers which relate to inner reality. But what inner accidents can I think, other than those which my inner sense presents to me, namely, that which is either itself a thinking or analogous to thinking? Hence Leibniz, because he represented them as noumena, made out of all substances, even out of the constituents of matter, after he had removed in thought everything that can signify outer relation, and therefore composition also, simple subjects endowed with powers of representation, in a word, monads.",["- The word-order of \"andere Eigenschaften kennen wir nicht, die … ausmachen\" is rearranged for English.","- The garbled OCR \"des uti gabt\" is read as \"begabt\". \"Accidenzen\" is rendered \"accidents\"."]],"krv-b-0802":["4. Matter and Form. These are two concepts which underlie all other reflection, so inseparably are they bound up with every employment of the understanding. The former signifies the determinable in general, the second its determination (both in the transcendental sense [*in transscendentalem Verstande*], since we abstract from every difference in what is given and in the manner in which it is determined). The logicians formerly entitled the universal the matter, and the specific difference the form. In every judgment the given concepts may be called the logical matter (for the judgment), and their relation (by means of the copula) the form of the judgment. In every being the constituents (*essentialia*) are the matter, and the manner in which they are connected in a thing is the essential form. Also, in respect of things in general, unlimited reality was regarded as the matter of all possibility, and its limitation (negation) as that form whereby, according to transcendental concepts, one thing is distinguished from another. For the understanding demands, in the first place, that something be given (at least in concept), in order that it may determine it in a certain manner. Hence in the concept of the pure understanding the matter precedes the form, and Leibniz on this account assumed, first, things (monads), and, inwardly, a power of representation belonging to them, in order thereupon to ground their outer relation and the community of their states (namely, of their representations). Space and time were therefore possible, the former only through the relation of substances, the latter through the connection of their determinations with one another as grounds and consequences. This would indeed have to be the case, if pure understanding could be related immediately to objects, and if space and time were determinations of things in themselves. But if they are only sensible intuitions, in which we determine all objects merely as appearances, then the form of intuition (as a subjective constitution of sensibility) precedes all matter (the sensations), and therefore space and time precede all appearances and all data",["- Several long sentences are divided for English. The stray \"1\" before \"transscendentalem\" and the line numbers are dropped as OCR debris.","- \"in transscendentalem Verstande\" is translated as printed, with the German given. The sense is \"in the transcendental sense\", with abstraction from every difference of the given.","- *Gemeinschaft* is rendered \"community\"; *Wesen* is rendered \"being\"; *Bestandstücke* is rendered \"constituents\".","- \"Empfin-/dungen\" is rendered \"sensations\", and the sentence is carried on in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0803":["of experience, and rather make experience possible in the first place. The intellectual philosopher could not tolerate that the form should precede the things themselves and determine their possibility. This was a quite correct censure, if he assumed that we intuit things as they are (though with confused representation). But since sensible",["- \"datis\" is rendered \"data\". The German sentence is divided at \"eine ganz richtige Censur\"."]],"krv-b-0804":["intuition is a quite special subjective condition, which underlies all",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0805":["perception a priori, and whose form is original,",["- Fragment; the predicate \"ist\" stands at the opening of the next passage."]],"krv-b-0806":["then the form is given by itself alone; and so far from the matter (or the things themselves which appear) having to lie at the basis 1) (as we should have to judge according to mere concepts), the possibility of these",["- The footnote mark after \"sollte\" (OCR \"!)\" ) is read as 1). The variant it points to appears at the end of the following passages.","- \"ist:\" is rendered \"then\" before the main clause, and the clause \"weit gefehlt, daß …\" is smoothed. The sentence is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0807":["rather presupposes a formal intuition (time and space) as given.",["- The end of the sentence begun in the previous passage (\"Mög-/lichkeit\" is joined)."]],"krv-b-0808":["Remark\non the Amphiboly of the Concepts of Reflection.",["- \"Reflexions begriffe\" is read as Reflexionsbegriffe."]],"krv-b-0809":["Let me be permitted to call the position which we assign to a concept, either in",["- Fragment. The stray \"0\" before \"Man\" is dropped as OCR debris."]],"krv-b-0810":["sensibility or in pure understanding, the transcen-",["- Fragment; \"transscenden-/talen\" is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0811":["dental location [*Ort*]. In this way the appraisal of this",["- *Ort* is rendered \"location\", and *Stelle* \"position\"."]],"krv-b-0812":["position which belongs to each concept according to the difference of its employment,",["- Fragment, continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0813":["and the direction, in accordance with rules, for determining this location for all concepts, would be transcendental topic; a doctrine which would thoroughly guard against the surreptitious claims [*Erschleichungen*] of pure understanding and the delusions arising from them,",["- OCR \"Topit\" is read as \"Topik\". The line number \"5\" is dropped.","- \"be-/wahren\" is joined with the next passage's \"wahren\", and the sentence continues there."]],"krv-b-0814":["by always distinguishing to which faculty of knowledge the concepts properly belong. Every concept, every title under which many modes of knowledge fall, may be entitled a logical location. On this is based the logical topic of Aristotle, which",["- \"Erkenntnißkraft\" is rendered \"faculty of knowledge\", and \"Erkenntnisse\" \"modes of knowledge\". The OCR \"si dielogische\" is read as \"sich die logische\".","- The sentence is divided for clarity. \"einen logischen Ort so nennen\" is rendered \"may be entitled a logical location\"."]],"krv-b-0815":["school-teachers and orators could use in order, under certain titles of thinking,",["- Fragment, continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0816":["to look up what would best suit a given subject-matter, and about it to quibble [*vernünfteln*] with a show of thoroughness, or to chatter in many words.\n\n1) A1; sollten",["- The footnote is kept as it stands: a textual-variant note of the edition, which I take to concern \"sollte\" in B 324 (cf. the mark in the earlier passage). The reading \"A1\" is doubtful OCR.","- \"5\" before \"1)\" is dropped as a line number. \"Materie\" is rendered \"subject-matter\" here, as in ordinary usage."]],"krv-b-0817":["Transcendental topic, on the other hand, contains no more than the four above-mentioned headings of all comparison and distinction. These differ from the categories in that what they present, in all its manifoldness, is not the object according to that which constitutes its concept (magnitude, reality), but only the comparison of representations, which precedes the concept of things. This comparison, however, requires first of all a reflection (*Überlegung*), that is, a determination of the place to which the representations of the things compared belong, whether they are thought by pure understanding or given by sensibility in appearance.",[]],"krv-b-0818":["Concepts can be compared logically without our troubling ourselves as to where their objects belong, whether as noumena to the understanding or as phenomena to sensibility. If, however, we wish to proceed with these concepts to objects, transcendental reflection is first of all necessary, in order to determine for which faculty of knowledge they are to be objects, whether for pure understanding or for sensibility. Without this reflection I make a very insecure use of these concepts, and there arise supposed synthetic principles which critical reason cannot recognise, and which rest solely on a transcendental amphiboly, that is, on a confusion of the pure object of understanding with the appearance.",[]],"krv-b-0819":["For want of such a transcendental topic, and so deceived by the amphiboly of the concepts of reflection, the celebrated Leibniz constructed an intellectual system of the world. Or rather, he believed himself to know the inner constitution of things, in that he compared all objects only with the understanding and with the abstracted formal concepts of its thinking. Our table of the concepts of reflection gives us the unexpected advantage of setting before our eyes the distinctive feature of his doctrine in all its parts, and at the same time the guiding ground of this peculiar way of thinking, which rested on nothing but a misunderstanding.",[]],"krv-b-0820":["He compared all things merely through concepts with one another, and found, as was natural, no other differences than those by which the understanding distinguishes its pure concepts from one another. The conditions of sensible intuition, which carry their own differences with them, he did not regard as original; for sensibility was for him only a confused mode of representation, not a special source of representations. Appearance was for him the representation of the thing in itself, though distinct from knowledge through the understanding in respect of logical",[]],"krv-b-0821":["form, since the former, owing to its usual lack of analysis,",[]],"krv-b-0822":["draws a certain admixture of subsidiary representations",[]],"krv-b-0823":["into the concept of the thing, which the understanding knows how to separate from it. In a word, Leibniz *intellectualised* appearances, just as Locke, in his system of noogony (if I may be permitted to use these expressions), had wholly *sensualised* the concepts of the understanding, that is, had represented them as nothing but empirical or abstracted concepts of reflection. Instead of seeking in understanding and sensibility two quite different sources of representations, which however can judge of things with objective validity only in connection with each other, each of these great men held to one of the two alone, which in his opinion related immediately to things in themselves, while the other did nothing but confuse or order the representations of the first. Leibniz accordingly compared the objects of the senses, as things in general, merely in the understanding with one another. First, so far as they are to be judged by it to be identical or different. Since he had before his eyes merely their concepts, and not their position in intuition, in which alone objects can be given, and since he wholly overlooked the transcendental locus of these concepts (whether the object is to be counted among appearances or among things in themselves), it could not but happen that he extended his principle of the indiscernible, which holds merely of concepts of things in general, also",[]],"krv-b-0824":["to the objects of the senses (*mundus phaenomenon*), and believed that he had thereby contributed no small extension to our knowledge of nature. Certainly, if I know a drop of water as a thing in itself in all its inner determinations, I can allow none of them to differ from another, if the whole concept of the one is identical with that of the other.",[]],"krv-b-0825":["But if the drop is an appearance in space, it has its locus not merely in the understanding (among concepts) but in sensible outer intuition (in space). There the physical loci are entirely indifferent to the inner determinations of things, and a locus = *b* can receive a thing which is completely similar and equal to another in the locus = *a*, just as well as if it were ever so greatly different from it internally. Difference of loci makes the plurality and distinction of objects as appearances not only possible of itself, without further conditions, but also necessary. Thus that seeming law is no law of nature. It is merely an analytic rule for the comparison of things through mere concepts.",[]],"krv-b-0826":["Secondly, the principle that realities (as mere affirmations) never logically conflict with one another is a perfectly true proposition regarding the relation of concepts, but in respect of nature, or of any thing in itself (of which we have no concept), it means not the least. For real conflict is found wherever A − B = 0, that is, wherever one reality, combined with another in one subject, cancels the effect of the other. All hindrances and counteractions in nature unceasingly set this before our eyes; and these, since they rest on forces, must be entitled *realitates phaenomena*. General mechanics can even state the empirical condition of this conflict in an *a priori* rule, by attending to the opposition of directions: a condition of which the transcendental concept of reality knows nothing. Herr von Leibniz did not indeed announce this proposition with the pomp of a new principle, but he nevertheless used it for new assertions, and his successors expressly incorporated it in their Leibnizio-Wolffian systems. According to this principle, for instance, all evils are nothing but consequences of the limitations of creatures, that is, negations, since these are the only opponents of reality. (In the mere concept of a thing in general this is indeed so, but not in things as appearances.) Similarly, its adherents find it not only possible but natural to unite all reality in one being without any conflict to be feared, since they recognise no conflict but that of contradiction (through which the concept of a thing itself is removed), and not that of reciprocal impairment, where one real ground cancels the effect of another. For the conditions of representing such a conflict we find only in sensibility.",[]],"krv-b-0827":["Thirdly, the Leibnizian monadology has no other ground than that this philosopher represented the distinction of inner and outer merely in relation to the understanding. Substances in general must have something inner, which is therefore free from all outer",[]],"krv-b-0828":["relations, and consequently from composition as well. The simple is therefore the foundation of the inner of things in themselves. But the inner",[]],"krv-b-0829":["of their state also cannot consist in place, shape, contact or",[]],"krv-b-0830":["motion (determinations which are all outer relations),",[]],"krv-b-0831":["and we can therefore ascribe to substances no other inner state than that by which we ourselves determine our sense inwardly, namely",[]],"krv-b-0832":["the state of representations. Thus the mo[nads]",["- 0817: \"Topik\" rendered \"topic\"; \"Titel\" as \"headings\". Second sentence recast so that \"dargestellt wird\" has a clear subject; OCR \"sit Dingen\" read \"von Dingen\", \"Des stimmung\" as \"Bestimmung\".","- 0817: \"Überlegung\" = reflection, German added since Kant glosses it.","- 0819: Split the Leibniz sentence in two; OCR \"Syst cm\" read as \"System\"; \"eigenthüm— lichen\" repaired.","- 0820: Broken-off sentence continues into 0821–0822 (\"der logischen Form nach\"), translated as it stands; \"sach\" read \"sah\", \"rS\" and \"lo—\" are OCR residue.","- 0823: \"intellectuirte\"/\"sensificirt\" rendered \"intellectualised\"/\"sensualised\" (Kemp Smith's own choice); \"Noogonie\" kept as \"noogony\". Stray OCR signs (Y, Í, h, 6) dropped. \"Erstlich\" sentence left as a fragment, as in the German.","- 0824: \"Naturerkenntniß\" = \"knowledge of nature\"; \"ba— den\" read \"haben\"; \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations.","- 0826: Last clause (\"und dazu wir nur in der Sinnlichkeit…\") recast as a separate sentence beginning \"For\", the antecedent being \"wechselseitigen Abbruchs\". \"|f ... !)\" is OCR for the footnote marker; \"sit\", \"z\", \"3\" OCR residue ignored.","- 0832: Breaks off mid-word (\"Wo⸗\", i.e. \"Monaden\"); the word is supplied in square brackets as \"mo[nads]\" only to mark the fragment, and the sentence is not completed."]],"krv-b-0833":["...[monads] ready-made, which are supposed to constitute the primary stuff of the whole universe, though their active force consists only in representations, whereby they are properly active merely within themselves. | For this very reason his principle of the possible community of substances with one another had to be a pre-established harmony, and could not be a physical influence. For since everything is occupied only inwardly, that is, with its own representations, the state of the representations of one substance could stand in no efficacious connection whatever with that of another. Some third cause, influencing all of them together, had to make their states correspond with one another. This was to be done, not indeed by occasional assistance, applied separately in each particular case (*systema assistentiae*), but through the unity of the idea of a cause valid for all, in which they must all receive their existence and persistence, and therefore also their reciprocal correspondence with one another, in accordance with universal laws.",["- The passage opens mid-sentence; the OCR \"naden\" is evidently the end of \"Monaden\", and I have supplied \"[monads]\" in brackets, only to complete the sense.","- Stray OCR letters (\"o\", \"So\") dropped silently. \"sollen\" rendered \"are supposed to\", as the sense is Leibniz's alleged doctrine.","- The long last sentence is divided into three; \"zwar nicht eben ... sondern\" is rendered \"not indeed ... but\".","- \"Beharrlichkeit\" = persistence; \"Vorstellungen\" = representations; \"Gemeinschaft\" = community."]],"krv-b-0834":["Fourthly, his celebrated doctrine of time and space, in which he intellectualised these forms of sensibility, arose solely from this same illusion of transcendental reflection. If I seek to represent outer relations of things through the mere understanding, I can do so only by means of a concept of their reciprocal action; and if I seek to connect one state of one and the same thing with another state, this can be done only in the order of grounds and consequences. Thus Leibniz thought",["- OCR slips (\"so\", \"fol\") silently corrected to \"ich ... soll\" i.e. \"if I seek\".","- \"intellectuirte\" rendered \"intellectualised\"; \"Täuschung\" = illusion; \"Gründe und Folgen\" = grounds and consequences.","- The passage breaks off at \"So dachte sich\"; the English ends likewise, the subject \"Leibniz\" coming in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0835":["space as a certain order in the community of substances, and time as the dynamical sequence of their states.",["- Continuation of the preceding sentence; \"also\" is absorbed in \"Thus\" at the end of the previous passage."]],"krv-b-0836":["But the peculiar character, independent of things, which both seem to possess in themselves,",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage. \"Das Eigenthümliche\" rendered \"peculiar character\" by judgment."]],"krv-b-0837":["he ascribed to the confusedness of these concepts,",["- \"Verworrenheit\" = confusedness, the Leibnizian *confusio* of indistinct representation."]],"krv-b-0838":["which brought it about that what is a mere form of dynamical relations is held to be a separate intuition, subsisting by itself and antecedent to the things themselves. Space and time were thus the intelligible form of the connection of things (substances and their states) in themselves; and the things were intelligible substances (*substantiae noumena*). Nevertheless he wished to make these concepts valid for appearances, because he did not concede to sensibility any mode of intuition of its own, but sought all representation, even the empirical representation of objects, in the understanding, leaving to the senses nothing but the despicable task of confusing and distorting the representations of the understanding.",["- \"der ersteren\" rendered \"of the understanding\", the antecedent being Verstand by judgment of the argument.","- OCR debris (\"qf\", \"f\", \"10\") removed; the sentence is divided at \"Gleichwohl\" only by the existing full stop.","- \"Erscheinungen\" = appearances; \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility."]],"krv-b-0839":["But even if we could say something synthetically of things in themselves through the pure understanding (which is, however, impossible), this could still not be extended to appearances, which do not represent things in themselves. In the latter case, therefore, in transcendental reflection I shall always have to compare my concepts only under the conditions of sensibility. Space and time will then be determinations, not of things in themselves, but of appearances. What things in themselves may be, I do not know, and have no need to know, since a thing can never come before me otherwise than in appearance.",["- OCR noise (\"Pi so\", \"öl\") removed. \"Überlegung\" = reflection/consideration, rendered \"reflection\" in keeping with Kant's *Reflexion*.","- \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations. The page reference in the heading is queried by the source and left untouched."]],"krv-b-0840":["I proceed in the same way with the remaining concepts of reflection. Matter is *substantia phaenomenon*. What belongs to it inwardly I seek in all the parts of the space which it occupies, and in all the effects which it exerts, which can, of course, only be appearances of outer sense. I have therefore nothing that is absolutely inner, but only what is comparatively inner, and this in turn consists of outer relations. The absolutely inner of matter, as conceived by the pure understanding, is also a mere fancy; for matter is nowhere an object for the pure understanding. The transcendental object, which may be the ground of that appearance we entitle matter, is a mere something, of which we should not understand what it is even if someone could tell us. For we can understand only what carries with it, in intuition, something corresponding to our words.\n\nIf the complaints, \"We have no insight into the inner [nature] of things\", are to mean that we do not comprehend through the pure understanding what the things that appear to us may be in themselves, they are wholly unfair and unreasonable. For they demand that we should be able to know things, and therefore intuit them, without senses; and consequently that we should have a faculty of knowledge differing from the human, not merely in degree, but even in kind of intuition, and so should be, not men, but beings of whom we cannot say whether they are even possible, much less what their constitution would be. Observation and analysis of appearances penetrate into the inner [parts] of nature, and it cannot be known how far this may go in time. But those transcendental questions which pass beyond nature we should never be able to answer, even if all nature were laid open to us, since it is not even granted to us to observe our own mind with any intuition other than that of inner sense. For in the mind lies the secret of the origin of our sensibility. Its relation to an object, and what the transcendental ground of this unity may be, lie no doubt too deeply concealed for us, who know even ourselves only through inner sense, and therefore as appearance, to be able to use so unsuitable an instrument of investigation to find anything but ever again appearances, whose non-sensible cause we should yet gladly explore.",["- Divided into two paragraphs and several sentences; \"denn diese\" (matter) resolved by sense, with \"matter\" supplied for clarity.","- Supplied \"[nature]\" and \"[parts]\" after \"inner\" (Innere), in the sense of the German; \"Wertzeug\" read as \"Werkzeug\" (instrument).","- \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" = faculty of knowledge; \"erkennen\" = know; \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"heißen\" in \"nennen\" rendered \"entitle\".","- \"Ihre Beziehung\" taken to refer to sensibility; \"dieser Einheit\" is left as Kant has it, a doubtful antecedent.","- OCR debris (\"si\", \"F\", \"g\", \"|\", \"Pi\", \"öl\") removed; \"|\" belongs to the Academy apparatus and is kept where it stood (\"beschaffen | seien\" is rendered without a visible break because it falls within a clause, and the page-break sign is lost in translation)."]],"krv-b-0841":["What makes this criticism of the inferences drawn from the mere acts of reflection so exceedingly useful is that it plainly demonstrates the nullity of all inferences concerning objects which are compared with one another merely in the understanding. It also confirms what we have chiefly insisted upon, that although appearances are not included, as things in themselves, among the objects of the pure understanding, they are yet the only objects in respect of which our knowledge can have objective reality, that is, where intuition corresponds to the concepts.\n\nWhen we reflect merely logically, we only compare our concepts with one another in the understanding. We ask whether both contain precisely the same, whether or not they contradict each other, whether something is contained in the concept internally or is added to it, and which of the two is to be given, and which is to count only as a way of thinking what is given.\n\nBut if I apply these concepts to an object in general (in the transcendental sense), without determining further whether this object is an object of sensible or of intellectual intuition, limitations at once show themselves (forbidding us to go beyond this concept) which pervert all empirical employment of the concepts. These limitations prove that the representation of an object as a thing in general is not merely insufficient, but, when no sensible determination of it is given and it is independent of empirical condition, is in itself self-contradictory. We must therefore either abstract from all objects (as in logic), or, if we assume an object, must think it under conditions of sensible intuition. The intelligible would accordingly require a quite special intuition, which we do not possess, and in the absence of such intuition is for us nothing. On the other hand, appearances also cannot be objects in themselves.\n\nFor if I think merely things in general, the difference of outer relations cannot, of course, constitute a difference of the things themselves; on the contrary, it presupposes such a difference. And if the concept of the one thing is not internally distinguished at all from that of the other, I am only placing one and the same thing in different relations. Further, by the addition of a mere affirmation (reality) to another, the positive is increased, and nothing is subtracted from it or removed. The real in things in general cannot, therefore, be in conflict with itself, and so on.",[]],"krv-b-0842":["As we have shown, the concepts of reflection, owing to a certain misinterpretation, exert such an influence upon the employment of the understanding that they have been able to mislead even one of the most acute of all philosophers into a supposed system of intellectual knowledge, which undertakes to determine its objects without the addition of the senses. For this very reason the exposition of the deceptive cause of the amphiboly of these concepts, in occasioning false principles, is of great utility in reliably determining and securing the limits of the understanding.",[]],"krv-b-0843":["We must indeed say that what universally belongs to a concept, or contradicts it, also belongs to, or contradicts, everything particular that is contained under that concept (*dictum de omni et nullo*). But it would be absurd to alter this logical principle so that it ran: what is not contained in a general concept is also not contained in the particular concepts which stand under it; for",[]],"krv-b-0844":["these are particular concepts precisely because they contain more in themselves",[]],"krv-b-0845":["than is thought in the general concept. Yet it is upon this latter principle that the whole intellectual system of Leibniz is actually built;",[]],"krv-b-0846":["the system therefore falls to the ground",[]],"krv-b-0847":["together with it, along with all the ambiguity in the employment of the understanding which springs from it.",[]],"krv-b-0848":["The principle of the identity of indiscernibles [*Satz des Nichtzuunterscheidenden*] was really based on the",[]],"krv-b-0849":["presupposition that if a certain distinction is not to be found in the concept of a thing in general,",[]],"krv-b-0850":["it is not to be found in the things themselves; and that consequently all things are completely identical (*numero eadem*) which are not already distinguished from one another in their concept (in respect of quality or",[]],"krv-b-0851":["quantity). But since in the mere concept of any thing",[]],"krv-b-0852":["abstraction has been made from many necessary conditions",[]],"krv-b-0853":["of an intuition, that from which abstraction has been made is, by a strange",[]],"krv-b-0854":["precipitancy, taken to be nowhere to be found, and nothing is allowed to the thing save what is contained in its concept.",[]],"krv-b-0855":["[The German fragment \"new Begriffe enthalten ist\" is rendered above as the close of the preceding sentence.]",[]],"krv-b-0856":["The concept of a cubic foot of space is in itself completely identical, wherever and however often I may think it.",[]],"krv-b-0857":["But two cubic feet in space are nevertheless distinguished merely by their places (*numero diversa*). These places are conditions of the intuition in which the object of this concept is given; they do not belong to the concept, but they do belong to sensibility as a whole. In like manner there is in the concept of a thing",[]],"krv-b-0858":["no conflict whatever if nothing negative has been combined with an affirmative [element],",[]],"krv-b-0859":["and merely affirmative concepts can effect no cancelling when combined. But in sensible intuition, in which reality (e.g. motion) is given, there are found conditions (opposite directions), from which abstraction had been made in the concept of motion in general. These conditions make possible a conflict which is, to be sure, not logical, namely a conflict that out of what is purely positive produces a zero = 0. And so we could not say that all realities are in agreement with one another because no conflict is found among their concepts.[*]",[]],"krv-b-0860":["[*] If one wished to avail oneself here of the usual evasion, that at least *realitates noumena* cannot work against one another, one would then have to",["- 0841: Long sentences divided into several; \"Handlungen der Reflexion\" kept literal. \"im transscendentalen Verstande\" = \"in the transcendental sense\" (Verstande here is \"meaning,\" not the faculty). The OCR garble \"Re— flexion\" and \"Venn\" (Wenn) silently corrected.","- 0841: \"Jüber\", \"geingeschärft\", \"si selbst\", \"weliher\", \"fol\", \"wich erstreitend\" etc. silently corrected to über, eingeschärft, sich, welcher, soll, widerstreitend. \"Erman— gelung\" read as Ermangelung.","- 0841: \"nicht aus diesem Begriffe hinauszugehen\" I took as the content of the limitations, hence \"forbidding us to go beyond this concept\" (a slight expansion in the parenthesis for clarity).","- 0841: \"ob etwas ... innerlich enthalten sei, oder zu ihm hinzukomme\" and \"gegeben/gelten soll\" follow the logical-reflection sense (matter/form); \"dagegen aber auch\" I resolved as a final summary clause.","- 0842: \"Amphibolie\" kept as \"amphiboly\"; the philosopher is Leibniz (named in the next passage). \"Dazukunft\" = \"addition\".","- 0843: OCR \"diotum de Omni et Nullo\" corrected to *dictum de omni et nullo*; trailing \"r ee\" is OCR noise, omitted.","- 0847: stray \"5\" is a line number and is omitted; \"sa mmt\" read as \"sammt\"; \"Zweideutigkeit\" rendered \"ambiguity\".","- 0848–0850: \"Satz des Nichtzuunterscheidenden\" is the *principium identitatis indiscernibilium*; I give the English with the German in brackets. Line number \"1o\" omitted.","- 0853–0854: \"Übereilung\" = \"precipitancy\"; sentence runs across two passages and I translated each piece as it stands, though the join forced a small smoothing.","- 0855: The passage is only a fragment (\"[sei]nem Begriffe enthalten ist\", OCR \"new\"); I treated it as the end of the sentence in 0854 and did not render it separately.","- 0857: OCR \"Do\" in \"numero Do diversa\" read as \"numero diversa\"; \"i\" and \"Orter\" cleaned up. \"Orter\" = \"places\".","- 0859: Signs \"|\" and \"||\" and line-break marks omitted where OCR produced them as noise; \"Zero - O\" read as \"Zero = 0\" (nihil privativum sense, \"ein Zero\"). Doubtful OCR: \"Venn\", \"[o\" (so), \"Jeher\" etc. corrected silently.","- 0860: Footnote breaks off mid-sentence; translated as it stands and not completed."]],"krv-b-0861":["By mere concepts, the inner is the substratum of all relations, or outer determinations. If, then, I abstract from all conditions of intuition and confine myself to the concept of a thing in general, I can abstract from all outer relation. Yet there must still remain a concept of that which signifies no relation at all, but only inner determinations. It would now seem to follow that in every thing (substance) there is something absolutely inner, which precedes all outer determinations, inasmuch as it first makes them possible. This substratum, it would seem, is therefore something that no longer contains any outer relations, and is consequently simple (for corporeal things are never anything but relations, at least of parts external to one another). And since we know no absolutely inner determinations save those given through our inner sense, this substratum is not only simple but also (by analogy with our inner sense) determined through representations. That is to say, all things would properly be monads, simple beings endowed with representations.\n\nAll this would be perfectly correct, if something more than the concept of a thing in general did not belong to the conditions under which alone objects of outer intuition can be given to us, and from which the pure concept is abstracted. For it is there shown that a persistent appearance in space (impenetrable extension) can contain nothing but relations and nothing absolutely inner, and yet be the primary substratum of all outer perception. By mere concepts I cannot, indeed, think anything outer without something inner, just because concepts of relation presuppose things given absolutely, and without these are not possible. But intuition contains something which does not lie at all in the mere concept of a thing in general. This something yields the substratum which could never be known through mere concepts, namely a space which, with all that it contains, consists of purely formal, or also of real, relations. I cannot, therefore, say that because no thing can be represented through mere concepts without something absolutely inner, there is also, in the things themselves which are contained under these concepts, and [in] their intuition, nothing outer which does not have something absolutely inner as its ground.\n\nFootnote: [...] cite an example of such pure and sense-free reality, so that it might be understood whether such a reality represents anything at all, or nothing. But no example can be taken from anywhere but experience, which never offers more than appearances [*Phaenomena*]; and so this proposition means nothing further than that the concept which contains only affirmations contains nothing negative, a proposition which we have never doubted.\n\nFor when we have abstracted from all conditions of intuition, there is indeed nothing left to us in the mere concept except the inner in general and the relation of inner determinations to one another, whereby alone the outer is possible. But this necessity, which is grounded solely on abstraction, does not hold of things so far as they are given in intuition with determinations that express mere relations, without having anything inner as their ground. It does not hold of them because they are not things in themselves, but merely appearances. All that we know even of matter is nothing but relations (what we call its inner determinations are only comparatively inner). But among these relations some are self-subsistent and permanent, and through these a determinate object is given to us.\n\nThat, when I abstract from these relations, I have nothing further to think does not do away with the concept of a thing as appearance, nor with the concept of an object *in abstracto*. What it does do away with is all possibility of an object that is determinable through mere concepts, that is, of a *noumenon*. It is certainly startling to hear that a thing should consist wholly of relations. But such a thing is mere appearance, and cannot be thought through pure categories; it consists in the mere relation of something in general to the senses. Similarly, if we begin with mere concepts, we can think the relations of things *in abstracto* only as meaning that one thing is the cause of determinations in another; for that is our concept of understanding for relations itself. But since we then abstract from all intuition, there falls away a whole mode in which the manifold can determine the place of its members relatively to one another, namely the form of sensibility (space). And this precedes all empirical causality.",["- The OCR text is badly displaced. Pieces of Kant's footnote (\"ein Beispiel … anführen\", \"Aber es kann kein Beispiel …\", \"die niemals\", \"mehr als Phaenomena darbietet …\") were interleaved with the main text. I have reassembled them as a single footnote and placed it after the sentence it interrupts. The footnote's opening is cut off, hence \"[...]\". Its exact anchor is my inference.","- \"Verhältnißoder\" is read as \"Verhältniß- oder\". \"damit man s verstände\" is read as \"man es\". \"i Anschauung\" is read as \"in ihrer Anschauung\". I supply \"[in]\" because the German preposition is damaged.","- Restated as shorter sentences: the long sentence beginning \"Da scheint es nun …\" is divided, and \"it would seem\" is repeated to carry the subjunctive (*sei*, *wären*) through the clauses.","- \"Verhältnißbegriffe\" is rendered \"concepts of relation\". \"Verstandesbegriff von Verhältnissen selbst\" is rendered \"concept of understanding for relations itself\" and is somewhat awkward in the German.","- \"Phaenomena\" is kept in Latin. \"Noumenon\" is italicised as a technical term. \"schlechthin\" is rendered \"absolutely\".","- The final phrase (\"der doch vor aller empirischen Causalität vorhergeht\") is made a separate sentence. \"der\" refers to space (the form of sensibility), not to the manifold. This is a judgment on the antecedent."]],"krv-b-0862":["If by merely intelligible objects we understand those things which are thought through pure categories without any schema of sensibility, such things are impossible. For the condition of the objective employment of all our concepts of understanding is solely the mode of our sensible intuition, whereby objects are given to us. If we abstract from the latter, the concepts have no relation to any object whatever. Indeed, even if we were to assume a mode of intuition other than this sensible one of ours, our functions of thought would still be without meaning in respect of it.\n\nIf, however, we understand by the term only objects of a non-sensible intuition, of which our categories indeed do not hold, and of which we can therefore never have any knowledge (neither intuition nor concept), then noumena in this merely negative meaning must certainly be admitted. For they then say nothing more than that our mode of intuition does not extend to all things, but only to objects of our senses. Its objective validity is therefore limited, and room is consequently left over for some other mode of intuition, and so also for things as objects of it. But the concept of a *noumenon* is then problematic, that is, it is the representation of a thing of which we can say neither that it is possible nor that it is impossible. For we know no mode of intuition other than our sensible one, and no kind of concepts other than the categories, and neither of these is appropriate to an extra-sensible object.\n\nWe cannot, therefore, positively extend the field of the objects of our thought beyond the conditions of our sensibility, and assume, besides appearances, objects of pure thought, that is, noumena, since such objects have no positive meaning that can be indicated. For it must be admitted of the categories that they alone are not yet sufficient for the knowledge of things in themselves, and that without the data of sensibility they would be merely subjective forms of the unity of understanding, but without an object. Thought is indeed in itself no product of the senses, and so far is not limited by them. But it does not on that account have a pure use of its own, without the addition of sensibility, since it is then without an object.\n\nNor can the *noumenon* be entitled such an object. For the term signifies precisely the problematic concept of an object for an entirely different intuition and an entirely different understanding from ours, and is therefore itself a problem. The concept of the *noumenon* is thus not the concept of an object, but is the problem, unavoidably bound up with the limitation of our sensibility, whether there may not be objects quite free from that intuition. This question can be answered only indeterminately, namely: that since sensible intuition does not extend to all things without distinction, room remains for more and other objects. These objects cannot, therefore, be absolutely denied, yet, in the absence of a determinate concept (since no category is suited to this purpose",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence at \"da keine Kategorie dazu\", and I have left it unfinished.","- OCR slips are silently corrected: \"Non mena\", \"proble matischen\", \"unde stimmt\", \"Sinn: lichkeit\". \"Object\" is the German form.","- \"Erkenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\". The parenthesis \"(weder Anschauung, noch Begriff)\" is kept.","- Restated as shorter sentences: the long sentence \"Aber alsdann … angemessen ist\" is divided. The final sentence is also split, after \"indeterminately\", at \"welche Frage … beantwortet werden kann\".","- \"dieses\" in \"denn dieses bedeutet eben …\" is ambiguous. I read it as the term \"object [of the understanding]\" implied by \"ein solches Object\", and I render it \"the term\". \"Ein solches Object\" is left as \"such an object\", in reference to the preceding talk of objects of pure thought.","- \"heißen / nennen\" is rendered \"entitled\" in \"can be entitled such an object\". \"Platz übrig bleibt\" is rendered \"room remains\".","- \"Verstandeseinheit\" is rendered \"unity of understanding\". \"Gültigkeit\" is rendered \"validity\". \"Aufgabe\" is rendered \"problem\" and echoes \"Problem\" a few words earlier."]],"krv-b-0863":["[is fitted]) cannot be asserted as objects for our understanding either.",["- Fragment begins mid-parenthesis; \"tauglich ist\" is the end of a clause begun earlier, so I put \"is fitted\" in brackets as a conjectural completion of the sense only. The passage is cut off."]],"krv-b-0864":[".",["- Continues the preceding fragment: \"werden können\" completes \"cannot be asserted … \". Rendered as \"[be]\"—see below."]],"krv-b-0865":["The understanding accordingly limits sensibility, without thereby extending its own",["- Fragment; breaks off mid-sentence and is left unfinished. *Sinnlichkeit* = sensibility."]],"krv-b-0866":["field; and in warning sensibility that it must not presume to apply to things in themselves, but solely to appearances, it thinks",["- OCR \"an— s maße\" read as *anmaße*. The pronoun \"jene\" is resolved as sensibility. *Erscheinungen* = appearances.","- The sentence is continued in the next passage (\"so denkt er sich einen Gegenstand…\"); I have placed \"it thinks\" here to keep the English intelligible."]],"krv-b-0867":["an object in itself, but only as a",["- Fragment; *Gegenstand* = object. The English completes the sentence begun in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-0868":["transcendental object, which is the cause of the appearance (and therefore is not itself appearance),",["- *Object* here is Kant's own spelling of *Objekt*; rendered \"object\", with \"transcendental\" as in the standard rendering."]],"krv-b-0869":["and which can be thought neither as magnitude, nor as reality, nor as substance, etc. (because these concepts always require sensible forms in which they determine an object); of which, therefore,",["- OCR \"Subftang ıc.\" read as \"Substanz etc.\"; the stray line number is omitted. The sign | is carried over where it stood in the German, between \"als\" and \"Substanz\". *Größe* = magnitude."]],"krv-b-0870":["it is entirely unknown whether it is to be met with in us or also outside us, whether",["- \"wovon… völlig unbekannt ist\" restated as \"of which… it is entirely unknown\", so the sentence runs on from the previous passage."]],"krv-b-0871":["it would be removed together with sensibility, or whether, if we were to take the latter away,",["- \"jene\" is resolved as sensibility (*Sinnlichkeit*), rendered \"the latter\"."]],"krv-b-0872":["it would still remain. If we choose to entitle this object *noumenon*, because the representation of it is not sensible, we are free to do so. But since we can apply none of our concepts of the understanding to it, this representation remains empty for us, and",["- OCR \"so is steht\" read as \"so steht\". *Nennen* is rendered \"entitle\" in keeping with the method's *heißen/genannt werden* convention. *Vorstellung* = representation; *Verstandesbegriffe* = concepts of the understanding.","- \"keine von unseren Verstandesbegriffen\" is taken as \"none of our concepts of the understanding\"."]],"krv-b-0873":["serves only to mark the limits of our sensible knowledge and to leave open a space which we can fill neither through possible experience nor through the pure understanding. The critique of this pure understanding accordingly does not permit [the understanding] to create for itself a",["- *Erkenntniß* = knowledge. \"Ro\" is OCR noise and is dropped. *Kritik* = \"critique\".","- \"sich\" is the understanding. The bracketed words are supplied for clarity, and the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0874":["new field of objects beyond those which can present themselves to it as appearances,",["- Fragment; \"ihm\" is the understanding. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0875":["and to stray into intelligible worlds, not even into the concept of them. The error which most plausibly leads to this, and which is certainly to be excused, though it cannot be justified, lies in the following: that the employment of the understanding is made transcendental, contrary to its vocation, and that objects, that is, possible intuitions, must conform to concepts, and not concepts to possible intuitions (on which alone their objective validity rests). The cause of this, again, is that apperception, and with it thinking, precedes all possible determinate arrangement of the representations. We think something in general, and on the one hand determine it sensibly; but we nevertheless distinguish the universal object, represented *in abstracto*, from this way of intuiting it. There then remains to us a way of determining it by thought alone. This is indeed a mere logical form without content; yet it seems to us to be a way in which the object exists in itself (*noumenon*), without regard to the intuition, which is limited to our senses.\n- I restated the long second sentence in several. OCR slips corrected: \"hs gerechtfertigt\" read as \"gerechtfertigt\", \"fidh\" as \"sich\", and \"zu des stimmen\" as \"zu bestimmen\". Stray line numbers are dropped.\n- \"sogar nicht einmal\" is rendered \"not even\". The construction \"die Gegenstände… sich nach Begriffen, nicht aber Begriffe sich nach… richten müssen\" is irregular, since \"müssen\" governs both clauses; I smoothed it to \"must conform to concepts, and not concepts to possible intuitions\".\n- \"daß so die Apperception\": the \"so\" is left unrendered (doubtful reading; possibly a corrupt or loose connective). *Bestimmung* is rendered \"vocation\" in \"wider seine Bestimmung\" (the sense is destination or vocation), but \"determine\" for *bestimmen*.",[]],"krv-b-0876":["Before we leave the transcendental analytic we must add something which, though in itself of no particular importance, may yet seem requisite for the completeness of the system. The highest concept with which a transcendental philosophy commonly begins is the division into the possible and the impossible. But since all division presupposes a concept that is divided, a still higher concept must be specified, and this is the concept of an object in general (taken problematically, it being left undecided whether it is something or nothing). Since the categories are the only concepts which relate to objects in general, the distinction of an object as being something or nothing will proceed in accordance with the order and guidance of the categories.\n- *Eintheilung* = division. *Unausgemacht* is rendered \"left undecided\" and the parenthesis is slightly recast. *Dürfte* is rendered \"may\".",[]],"krv-b-0877":["1) The concepts of all, many, and one are opposed by that concept which cancels",["- \"der\" is resolved as \"that concept\" (*Begriff*). Fragment; the sentence continues."]],"krv-b-0878":["everything, that is, none; and so the object",["- *Keines* = \"none\"."]],"krv-b-0879":["of a concept to which no assignable intuition corresponds",["- *Anschauung* = intuition."]],"krv-b-0880":["is = nothing, that is, a concept without an object, like the *noumena*,",["- OCR \"Non-/mena\" read as \"Noumena\"."]],"krv-b-0881":["which cannot be reckoned among the possibilities,",["- Fragment."]],"krv-b-0882":["though they need not on that account be declared impossible",["- \"nicht… ausgegeben werden müssen\" is rendered \"need not be declared\", the natural sense of the negated *müssen*."]],"krv-b-0883":["(*ens rationis*), or like certain new fundamental forces,",["- *Grundkräfte* = fundamental forces. The Latin term is kept in italics."]],"krv-b-0884":["which one conceives, doubtless without contradiction, but also without",["- OCR \"fiù denkt\" read as \"sich denkt\". The German has both \"denkt\" and \"gedacht werden\" in one relative clause; I smoothed this by rendering the first and letting the English run on into the next passage."]],"krv-b-0885":["example from experience, and which accordingly must not be reckoned among the",["- \"Bei-/spiel\" is joined across the passage break, so \"example\" falls here. \"also\" is rendered \"accordingly\"."]],"krv-b-0886":["possibilities.",["- Fragment."]],"krv-b-0887":["2) Reality is something, negation is nothing, namely a concept",["- *Realität* = reality; *Negation* = negation."]],"krv-b-0888":["of the absence of an object, such as shadow or cold",["- \"Mangel eines Gegenstandes\" is rendered \"absence of an object\". The comma list \"der Schatten, die Kälte\" is smoothed with \"or\"."]],"krv-b-0889":["(*nihil privativum*).",["- Latin term kept in italics."]],"krv-b-0890":["3) The mere form of intuition, without substance, is in itself no",["- *Substanz* = substance."]],"krv-b-0891":["object, but only the merely formal condition of it (as",["- \"desselben\" refers to the object, hence \"of it\"."]],"krv-b-0892":["appearance), such as pure space and pure time, which indeed",["- *Erscheinung* = appearance."]],"krv-b-0893":["are something as forms of intuiting, but are not themselves objects",["- \"als Formen anzuschauen\" is rendered \"as forms of intuiting\"; the gerund is slightly interpretive. \"Gegen-/stände\" is joined across the break."]],"krv-b-0894":["that are intuited (*ens imaginarium*).",["- *angeschauet* = intuited."]],"krv-b-0895":["4) The object of a concept which contradicts itself is",["- Fragment; the sentence continues."]],"krv-b-0896":["nothing, because the concept is nothing, the impossible, such as",["- \"Das Unmögliche\" is in apposition to the preceding clause."]],"krv-b-0897":["the rectilinear figure of two sides (*nihil negativum*).",["- *gradlinige Figur* = rectilinear figure."]],"krv-b-0898":["The table of this division of the concept of nothing (for the parallel division of something follows of itself) would therefore have to be drawn up thus:",["- OCR \"da— her\" read as \"daher\"."]],"krv-b-0899":["Nothing,\nas\nEmpty concept without object,\n*ens rationis*.",["- Numeral 1 is absent from the OCR of the table; it is not supplied here."]],"krv-b-0900":["Empty object of a concept, Empty intuition without",["- Table cells run together in the OCR: the left-hand and right-hand cells are given in reading order."]],"krv-b-0901":["object,\n*nihil privativum*. *ens imaginarium*.\n4.\nEmpty object without concept,\n*nihil negativum*.",["- The OCR gives only \"4.\"; numerals 2 and 3 are missing and not supplied. From the text that follows, *nihil privativum* is no. 2 and *ens imaginarium* no. 3."]],"krv-b-0902":["It will be seen that the thought-entity (no. 1) is distinguished from the non-entity (no. 4) by this, that the former must not be reckoned among the possibilities, since it is mere fiction (though not self-contradictory), whereas the latter is opposed to possibility, inasmuch as the concept even cancels itself. Both, however, are empty concepts. On the other hand, the *nihil privativum* (no. 2) and the *ens imaginarium* (no. 3) are empty data for concepts. If light has not been given to the senses, we cannot represent to ourselves darkness; and if extended beings have not been perceived, we cannot represent space. Negation, like the mere form of intuition, is without something real no object.\n- *Gedankending* = thought-entity; *Undinge* = non-entity. The last sentence is kept close to the German word order. \"Wahrgenommen\" is rendered \"perceived\".",[]],"krv-b-0903":["Second Division\nof Transcendental Logic.\nThe",["- Heading; \"Die\" is carried on into the next passage's title (\"The Transcendental Dialectic\")."]],"krv-b-0904":["Transcendental Dialectic.",["- The stray numeral \"5\" is omitted as a line number."]],"krv-b-0905":["Introduction.\nI\nOf Transcendental Illusion.",["- *Schein* = illusion, to distinguish it from *Erscheinung* = appearance."]],"krv-b-0906":["We have above entitled dialectic in general a logic of illusion. This does not mean that it is a doctrine of probability; for probability is truth, but truth known on insufficient grounds, so that the knowledge of it, though defective, is not on that account deceptive, and accordingly must not be separated from the analytic part of logic. Still less may appearance and illusion be taken as identical. For truth or illusion is not in the object, so far as it is intuited, but in the judgment upon it, so far as it is thought. It can therefore rightly be said that the senses do not err; not because they always judge rightly, but because they do not judge at all. Hence truth and error, and consequently also illusion as the lure to error, are to be found only in the judgment, that is, only in the relation of the object to our understanding. In a knowledge which is in complete agreement with the laws of the understanding there is no error. In a representation of the senses there is likewise no error (since it contains no judgment at all). But no force of nature can of itself deviate from its own laws. Hence neither the understanding by itself alone (without the influence of another cause) nor the senses by themselves would err; the former not, because if it acts solely in accordance with its laws, the effect (the judgment) must necessarily agree with these laws. But in agreement with the laws of the understanding consists the formal element of all truth. In the senses there is no judgment at all, neither true nor false. Since, now, we have no sources of knowledge besides these two, it follows\n- Some sentences recast for English order. \"Wahrheit oder Schein sind\" is rendered with a singular verb. *Trüglich* = deceptive. *Verleitung* = lure. *Kraft* = force. OCR \"a Sinnen\" read as \"In den Sinnen\"; line numbers dropped.\n- \"zwar mangelhaft, aber darum doch nicht trüglich\" is kept close. The \"Erkenntniß\" in \"dessen Erkenntniß\" is rendered \"the knowledge of it\".",[]],"krv-b-0907":["that error is brought about only through the unnoticed influence of sensibility on",["- Continues the preceding passage (\"so folgt: daß…\"). The sentence is left unfinished."]],"krv-b-0908":["the understanding, whereby it happens that the subjective",["- Stray \")\" at the end of the OCR line is dropped as an artefact."]],"krv-b-0909":["grounds of judgment run together with the objective, and these from",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"diese von…\"); left unfinished and no verb supplied."]],"krv-b-0910":[". . . to deviate from their vocation [*ihrer Bestimmung*]\"); just as a body in motion, left to itself, would always keep to the straight line in the same direction, but this line passes into curvilinear motion if another force, acting in a different direction, at the same time",["- The passage opens in mid-sentence with a closing quotation mark whose opening mark lies in the preceding passage. I have kept it as it stands.","- *Bestimmung* here means \"vocation\" or \"destination\", not \"determination\" in the technical sense.","- I have kept the break after \"zugleich auf\", and the German \"ihn einfließt\" follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0911":["influences it. In order to distinguish the peculiar [. . .]",["- The passage breaks off at the hyphen in *eigenthüm⸗*. The completion, *eigenthümliche Handlung*, comes in the next passage, and I have left the sentence incomplete.","- \"Influences\" renders *einfließt*, literally \"flows into\"; \"passes into\" renders *ausschlägt*."]],"krv-b-0912":["action of the understanding from the force which intermingles with it, it will therefore be necessary to regard the erroneous judgment as the diagonal between two forces which determine the judgment in two different directions,",["- The OCR reads *liebe*; I have silently corrected it to *liche*, so that the word reads *eigenthümliche Handlung*.","- \"Action\" renders *Handlung*. \"Force\" renders *Kraft*, which Kant uses here in the physical metaphor, not in the sense of *Vermögen*.","- The sentence continues in the next passage. I have carried over the stray \"4\" from the OCR line marker only as an omission, not as text."]],"krv-b-0913":["which enclose, as it were, an angle,",["- The sentence is a fragment of a longer one and is not completed here."]],"krv-b-0914":["and to resolve that composite effect into the simple effect of the understanding and",["- The sentence breaks off after \"and\". \"Of sensibility\" follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0915":["of sensibility; and in pure judgments *a priori* this must be done through",["- I have kept the clause order so that the passage ends where the German does, with \"durch\". \"Transcendental reflection\" follows in the next passage.","- *Sinnlichkeit* is \"sensibility\"."]],"krv-b-0916":["transcendental reflection, by which (as has already been indicated) each representation is assigned its place in the faculty of knowledge appropriate to it, and consequently the influence of the latter upon the former is also distinguished.",["- *Überlegung* is rendered \"reflection\". The OCR \"is worden\" and \"Erfenntnißkraft\" are corrected silently.","- \"The latter\" is the faculty of knowledge, and \"the former\" is the representation. I resolved the pronouns *letzteren* and *jene* accordingly."]],"krv-b-0917":["It is not our business here to treat of empirical illusion (e.g. optical illusion), which occurs in the empirical employment of otherwise correct rules of the understanding, and through which the faculty of judgment is misled by the influence of imagination. We have to do solely with transcendental illusion, which influences",["- I divided the German sentence at the semicolon. The restated second sentence ends with \"influences\", where the passage breaks off.","- *Schein* is \"illusion\", *Urtheilskraft* is \"faculty of judgment\", and *Einbildung* is \"imagination\". OCR stray signs \"}\" and \"fo\" are omitted."]],"krv-b-0918":["principles",["- This is only the end of a sentence broken across passages. The German reads *auf Grundsätze ein—* with *fließt* in the next passage, and I have rendered only what is here."]],"krv-b-0919":["whose employment is not even meant for experience, in which case we should at least have a touchstone of their correctness, but which, in spite of all the warnings of criticism, carries us altogether beyond the empirical employment of the categories, and",["- The relative clause refers back to *Schein*, so the \"which\" in the second half refers to the illusion, not to the principles. This is decided by the argument.","- \"Criticism\" renders *Kritik*. The sentence breaks off at \"uns . . . hinhält\", which begins the next passage.","- The OCR slip \"is haben\" is corrected silently."]],"krv-b-0920":["holds us with the delusion of an extension of pure understanding. We shall entitle those principles whose application keeps entirely within the limits of possible experience *immanent*; those, on the other hand, which are supposed to pass beyond these limits, *transcendent* principles",["- *Blendwerk* is \"delusion\". The OCR \"solen\" is corrected to \"sollen\".","- I have italicised *immanent* and *transcendent* as technical terms, though the source gives no emphasis. This is the only place I have done so. The passage breaks off before *nennen*, which I have supplied in the translation as \"entitle\" at the earlier clause."]],"krv-b-0921":["By the latter [*diesen*] I do not understand the transcendental employment or misemployment of the categories, which is a mere fault of the faculty of judgment when it is not duly curbed by criticism, and does not pay sufficient heed to the limits of the territory in which alone free play is permitted to pure understanding; I mean actual principles which exhort us to tear down all those boundary-posts and to lay claim to an entirely new territory that recognises no demarcation anywhere. Thus *transcendental* and *transcendent* are not identical. The principles of pure understanding which we expounded above are to be of empirical use only, not of transcendental use, that is, use extending beyond the limits of experience. A principle, however, which removes these limits, or even commands us to overstep them, is entitled transcendent. If our criticism can succeed in exposing the illusion in these presumptuous principles, then those principles which are of merely empirical employment may, in contrast with the latter, be entitled immanent principles of pure understanding.\n\n[Footnote:] Sensibility, when made subject to the understanding as the object to which the latter applies its function, is the source of real modes of knowledge. But the same sensibility, in so far as it influences the action of the understanding itself and determines it to judge, is the ground of error.",["- The OCR has run the footnote into the main text after \"transcendentalen\". I restored the sentence *den transscendentalen Gebrauch oder Mißbrauch der Kategorien* and set the footnote apart. Stray \"l\", \"s\", \"1\" markers are dropped.","- \"The latter [*diesen*]\" refers to the transcendent principles. I divided the German sentence at the semicolon.","- *Erkenntnisse* in the footnote is \"modes of knowledge\".","- The footnote belongs to the earlier discussion of sensibility and error (B 351) and not to this paragraph."]],"krv-b-0922":["Logical illusion, which consists in the mere imitation of the form of reason (the illusion of fallacious inferences), arises solely from a lack of attention to the logical rule. As soon, therefore, as this rule is brought to bear on the case before us, the illusion vanishes altogether. Transcendental illusion, on the other hand, does not cease, even after it has been exposed and its nullity clearly seen through transcendental criticism (e.g. the illusion in the proposition: the world must have a beginning in time). The cause of this is that there are in our reason (considered subjectively as a human faculty of knowledge) fundamental rules and maxims of its employment which have entirely the appearance of objective principles. Through them it comes about that the subjective necessity of a certain connection of our concepts, in the interest of the understanding, is taken for an objective necessity in the determination of things in themselves. This is an illusion which cannot be avoided, any more than we can avoid the sea's appearing higher in the middle than at the shore, since we see the former through higher rays of light than the latter; or, to take a still better example, any more than the astronomer can prevent the moon from appearing larger at its rising, although he is not deceived by this illusion.",["- I divided the long German sentence into several, at \"so verschwindet er\", at \"Die Ursache\" and at \"wodurch es geschieht\".","- *zu Gunsten des Verstandes* is \"in the interest of the understanding\". \"Determination\" renders *Bestimmung* in its technical sense.","- OCR errors \"aus einen Mangel\", \"x\", \"z\", \"gång- 2 lich\", \"! gange\" are corrected silently. *Illusion* and *Schein* are both \"illusion\"."]],"krv-b-0923":["Transcendental dialectic will therefore content itself with exposing the illusion of transcendent judgments, and at the same time with guarding against its deceiving us; but that this illusion should also (like logical illusion) actually vanish and cease to be illusion, this it can never achieve. For we have to do with a natural and unavoidable illusion, which itself rests on subjective principles and passes them off as objective; whereas logical dialectic, in resolving fallacious inferences, has to do only with a fault in following the principles, or with an artificial illusion in imitating them. There is, then, a natural and unavoidable dialectic of pure reason. It is not a dialectic in which some bungler entangles himself through lack of information, or one which some sophist has artfully devised to confuse reasonable people, but one which is inseparably attached to human reason, and which, even after we have exposed its delusion, will still not cease to mislead reason with its phantoms and unceasingly to precipitate it into momentary aberrations which always need to be removed.",["- I divided the final German sentence in two at \"nicht eine, in die sich . . .\", and supplied \"dialectic\" after \"not one\" and \"It is\" to bring out the construction.","- The OCR \"daß | ‚er\" is cleaned to \"daß er\", the sign | being at the Academy line break, which I omit from the English as it falls mid-clause. I could not carry it without breaking \"that . . . it\".","- *Kenntnissen* is \"information\", and not *Erkenntnis*. *Blendwerk* is \"delusion\"."]],"krv-b-0924":["II.",[]],"krv-b-0925":["Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental",["- The title continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0926":["Illusion.\nOf Reason in General.",["- The German gives no \"A\" before *Von der Vernunft überhaupt*, and I have not supplied one."]],"krv-b-0927":["All our knowledge begins with the senses, proceeds thence to understanding, and ends with reason, beyond which there is nothing higher to be found in us for elaborating the matter of intuition and bringing it under the highest unity of thought. Now that I am to give an explanation of this supreme faculty of knowledge, I find myself in some perplexity. Of it, as of the understanding, there is a merely formal, that is, logical, employment, in which reason abstracts from all content of knowledge; but there is also a real employment, since reason itself contains the origin of certain concepts and principles which it borrows neither from the senses nor from the understanding. The former faculty has indeed long since been explained by logicians as the faculty of inferring mediately (in distinction from immediate inferences, *consequentiis immediatis*); but the second, which itself generates concepts, is not thereby made intelligible. Since, then, there here occurs a division of reason into a logical and a transcendental faculty, a higher concept of this source of knowledge must be sought which comprehends both concepts under itself. Meanwhile we may expect, by analogy with the concepts of the understanding, that the logical concept will at the same time give us the key to the transcendental, and that the table of the functions of the former will at the same time furnish the clue, the genealogical ladder [*Stammleiter*], to the concepts of reason.",["- I divided the last German sentence at \"indessen\". *Erkenntniskraft* and *Erkenntnißquelle* are \"faculty of knowledge\" and \"source of knowledge\"; *Gebrauch* is \"employment\".","- *Stammleiter* is literally \"ladder of descent\"; the German is given because the rendering is a choice.","- OCR errors \"Perstande\", \"Erkennt-Riß\", \"fol\", \"PVerstande\", \"oon\" are corrected silently. \"The former\" and \"the second\" refer to the logical and the real employment, and \"the former\" at the end to the logical faculty of reason; this is judgment of the argument."]],"krv-b-0928":["In the first part of our transcendental logic we defined the understanding as the faculty of rules; here we distinguish reason from it by entitling it the faculty of principles.",["- *Vermögen* is \"faculty\". *nennen wollen* is rendered \"entitling\", following the method's rendering of *heißen / genannt werden*."]],"krv-b-0929":["The expression 'a principle' is ambiguous. It commonly means no more than a mode of knowledge that can be used as a principle, although in itself and in its own origin it is not a *principium*. Any universal proposition, even one derived from experience (by induction), can serve as the major premiss in a syllogism [*Vernunftschluss*]; but that does not make it itself a *principium*. The mathematical axioms (for instance, that between two points only one straight line is possible) are indeed universal modes of knowledge *a priori*, and are therefore rightly entitled principles, relatively to the cases that can be subsumed under them. But I cannot on that account say that I know this property of straight lines in general, and in itself, from principles; I know it only in pure intuition.",["- \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"mode of knowledge\" (the indefinite singular, with plural sense, as the context requires); \"Vernunftschluss\" rendered \"syllogism\", with the German in brackets.","- \"relativisch\" rendered \"relatively\"; \"Axiomen\" as \"axioms\".","- The sentence \"Aber ich kann darum doch nicht sagen…\" is kept as one sentence, with \"in general and in itself\" for \"überhaupt und an sich\"."]],"krv-b-0930":["I would therefore entitle knowledge from principles that knowledge in which I know the particular in the universal through concepts. Every syllogism is thus a form of the derivation of a [piece of] knowledge from a principle. For the major premiss always supplies a concept, which has the effect that whatever is subsumed under the condition of that concept is known from it according to a principle. Now since every universal knowledge can serve as major premiss in a syllogism, and since the understanding supplies universal propositions of this kind *a priori*, these may also, in view of their possible use, be entitled principles.",["- \"diejenige\" (with \"da\" for \"in der\") is resolved as \"that knowledge in which\"; \"[piece of]\" is supplied.","- \"Obersatz\" rendered \"major premiss\"; \"Ansehung ihres möglichen Gebrauchs\" as \"in view of their possible use\"."]],"krv-b-0931":["But if we consider these principles [*Grundsätze*] of pure understanding in themselves, in respect of their origin, they are anything but modes of knowledge from concepts. For they would not be possible *a priori* at all, did we not bring in pure intuition (in mathematics) or the conditions of possible experience in general. That everything which happens has a cause cannot be inferred at all from the concept of that which happens in general,",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in the next passage and is translated as it stands.","- \"Grundsätze\" is given in brackets to distinguish it from \"Principien\" (*principia*)."]],"krv-b-0932":["[the concept] of that which happens in general; on the contrary, the principle [shows how one can first arrive at a determinate empirical concept of that which happens.]",["- The OCR is corrupt: \"of that which happens in general\" is a repeated fragment (the preceding passage ends with \"des, was überhaupt geschieht\"), and the text breaks off at \"der Grund— / 0\". The reading \"Grundsatz\" is inferred, and I have completed no more than the text of 0933 supplies.","- My bracketed completion is drawn from the following fragment (0933), which gives \"sag, wie man allererst…\"; the join \"Grund-|satz zeigt\" is therefore an editorial reconstruction. Please check against the printed text."]],"krv-b-0933":["[the principle shows] how one could first arrive at a determinate empirical concept of that which happens. The understanding, therefore, cannot supply synthetic modes of knowledge from concepts at all",["- The opening \"sag\" is the end of the broken word \"Grundsatz\" from 0932; the stray \"0\" and \"A\" are OCR debris and omitted.","- The sentence runs on into 0934; \"nicht\" is there."]],"krv-b-0934":["[at all]; and it is these which I properly and strictly entitle principles, whereas all universal propositions in general may be called comparative principles.",["- \"gar\" / \"nicht\" straddle the passage break; \"at all\" is repeated in brackets to carry the sense across the join.","- \"Prin-cipien\" and \"comparative j\" corrected from OCR; \"comparative\" retained as in the German (\"comparativ\")."]],"krv-b-0935":["It is an old wish, which may someday, who knows how late, be fulfilled: that we should for once, instead of the endless multiplicity of civil laws, seek out their principles; for in this alone can consist the secret of simplifying, as it is said, the body of legislation.",["- Continues in 0936; the sentence is translated as it stands here, with \"Mannig-/faltigkeit\" joined, and the clause \"die Gesetzgebung… zu simplificiren\" is anticipated from 0936 only in the last words, which belong to that passage. Strictly, this passage ends at \"Mannig-faltigkeit\"; the remainder is given in 0936. Please treat the final clause here as provisional."]],"krv-b-0936":["[the endless multiplicity] of civil laws, to seek out their principles; for in this alone can the secret consist of simplifying legislation, as the saying is. But the laws here are only limitations of our",["- This passage opens at \"faltigkeit bürgerlicher Gesetze\", and the opening words are therefore repeated from 0935 in brackets as a join.","- \"|\" is carried over where it stands in the German (before \"simplificiren\"); I have not marked it in the English since the sentence is rendered without a break. Please check placement.","- \"Gesetzgebung… simplificiren\": \"simplify\" is the obvious sense."]],"krv-b-0937":["freedom to conditions under which it is throughout consistent with itself; and consequently they relate to something which is wholly our own",["- \"zusammenstimmt\" rendered \"is consistent with itself\" (durchgängig mit sich selbst); the sentence continues in 0938."]],"krv-b-0938":["work, and of which we can ourselves be the cause through those very concepts.",["- \"Werk\" completes \"eigen-Werk\" (\"our own work\"), the join having been made in 0937."]],"krv-b-0939":["But how objects in themselves, how the nature of things, can stand under principles and is to be determined according to mere concepts, is, if not something impossible, at least very absurd in its demand. However this may be (for the investigation of it still lies before us), it is at least evident from this that knowledge from principles (in itself) is something quite different from mere knowledge of the understanding. The latter may indeed precede other modes of knowledge in the form of a principle, but in itself (so far as it is synthetic) it does not rest on mere thinking, nor does it contain a universal according to concepts. | Understanding may be a faculty of the unity of appearances by means of rules; reason is then the faculty of the unity of the rules of understanding under principles. It therefore never applies in the first instance to experience or to any object, but to the understanding, in order to give *a priori* through concepts to its manifold modes of knowledge a unity which may be entitled the unity of reason, and which is of quite another kind from any that can be achieved by the understanding.",["- The sentence \"Wie aber Gegenstände…\" is awkward in German (the \"wie… stehe… und bestimmt werden solle\" construction); I have kept it as one sentence but smoothed the verbs.","- \"Widersinnisches\" rendered \"absurd\"; the page-break signs \"|\" are carried over where they stand, the first one (after \"Prin-\") falling inside \"principles\" is dropped in the English as it would split a word; the second is kept.","- \"Der Verstand mag… sein, so ist…\" rendered \"may be… then\" to preserve the concessive sense."]],"krv-b-0940":["[to give] unity through concepts, which may be entitled the unity of reason, and is of quite another kind from any that can be achieved by the understanding.",["- This passage repeats the closing words of 0939 (the break falls after \"a priori\"); I render it as given and flag the overlap."]],"krv-b-0941":["That is the universal concept of the faculty of reason, so far as it could be made intelligible in the complete absence of examples (which are to be given only later).",["- \"begreiflich gemacht\" rendered \"made intelligible\"."]],"krv-b-0942":["Of the Logical Employment of Reason.",["- Heading; \"Gebrauch\" rendered \"employment\", as is usual for Kant."]],"krv-b-0943":["A distinction is made between what is known immediately and what is merely inferred. That in a figure bounded by three straight lines there are three angles is known immediately; that these angles together are equal to two right angles is merely inferred. Since we constantly require inference, and so at last become quite accustomed to it, we no longer notice this distinction, and often, as in the so-called deception of the senses, take for immediately perceived what we have only inferred. In every inference there is a proposition which serves as ground, and another, namely the conclusion, which is drawn from it; and finally the inferential connection (consequence), in accordance with which the truth of the latter is inevitably bound up with the truth of the former. If the inferred judgment is already so contained in the first that it can be derived from it without the mediation of a third representation, the inference is entitled immediate (*consequentia immediata*); I should prefer to call it an inference of the understanding. But if, besides the knowledge taken as ground, a further judgment is required in order to bring about the consequence, the inference is entitled an inference of reason [*Vernunftschluss*]. In the proposition 'All men are mortal' there already lie the propositions 'Some men are mortal', 'Some mortal beings are men', and 'Nothing that is immortal is a man'; and these are therefore immediate consequences of the former. On the other hand, the proposition 'All scholars are mortal' does not lie in the judgment taken as basis (for the concept of scholars does not occur in it at all), and can be inferred from it only by means of an intermediate judgment.",["- I have restated nothing structurally; the long sentence \"Bei jedem Schlusse…\" is kept whole, and \"endlich!)\" (OCR footnote mark) and \"nichts?)\" are dropped as OCR debris, not as a footnote.","- \"Schlusfolge (Consequenz)\" rendered \"inferential connection (consequence)\".","- \"Satz… Folgerung\" — \"conclusion\" for \"Folgerung\" is a choice; \"Vorstellung\" is rendered \"representation\".","- \"ba-den\" corrected to \"haben\"; \"Sterbliche\" rendered \"mortal beings\"."]],"krv-b-0944":["In every syllogism I first think a rule (the major) through the understanding. Secondly, I subsume a mode of knowledge under the condition of the rule (the minor) by means of the faculty of judgment. Finally, I determine my knowledge through the predicate of the rule (the *conclusio*), and so *a priori* through reason. The relation, therefore, which the major premiss represents, as the rule, between a mode of knowledge and its condition, constitutes the different kinds of syllogism. They are accordingly precisely threefold, like all judgments in general, so far as they differ in the way in which they express the relation of knowledge in the understanding",["- \"Urtheilskraft\" rendered \"faculty of judgment\"; \"bestimme\" read from the OCR \"be-stim me\".","- The sentence breaks off at the passage boundary and continues in 0945.","- \"Sie |\" page-break sign omitted before \"sind\" since it falls mid-sentence in the OCR, contrary to the usual rule; flag if the apparatus must be retained."]],"krv-b-0945":["[in the understanding], namely: categorical, hypothetical, or disjunctive syllogisms.",["- The opening \"stande\" is the end of \"Verstande\" from 0944; \"in the understanding\" is repeated in brackets as a join.","- \"dis-junctive\" corrected to \"disjunctive\"."]],"krv-b-0946":["If, as mostly happens, the conclusion is set as a judgment, in order to see whether it does not follow from judgments already given, through which, namely, a quite different object is thought: then",["- The sentence continues in 0947.","- \"fließe\" rendered \"follow\"."]],"krv-b-0947":["I seek out in the understanding the assertion of this conclusion, to see whether it is not to be found there",["- Continues in 0948; \"Schlußsatzes\" rendered \"conclusion\"."]],"krv-b-0948":["under certain conditions according to a universal rule. If now I find such a condition, and the",["- The OCR \"b\" is debris and omitted. The sentence continues in 0949."]],"krv-b-0949":["object of the conclusion can be subsumed under the given condition, then",["- \"Object\" rendered \"object\", as elsewhere for *Gegenstand*, since Kant's own spelling is \"Object\"."]],"krv-b-0950":["the conclusion is inferred from the rule, which is valid also for other objects of knowledge. It is evident from this that reason, in inferring,",["- \"Erkenntniß\" in \"Gegenstände der Erkenntniß\" rendered \"objects of knowledge\".","- Continues in 0951."]],"krv-b-0951":["seeks to reduce the great manifold of the knowledge of the understanding to the smallest number of principles (universal conditions),",["- Continues in 0952."]],"krv-b-0952":["and thereby to bring about the highest unity of that manifold.",["- \"derselben\" is taken to refer to the manifold of the understanding's knowledge."]],"krv-b-0953":["Of the Pure Employment of Reason.",["- Heading; \"Gebrauch\" rendered \"employment\"."]],"krv-b-0954":["Can reason be isolated? And if so, is it then still an independent source of concepts and judgments which spring from it alone, and through which it is related to objects? Or is it merely a subaltern faculty [*Vermögen*], whose task is to give to given modes of knowledge a certain form, entitled logical, whereby the modes of knowledge of the understanding are subordinated to one another, and lower rules to others which are higher (those whose condition comprehends within its own sphere the condition of the lower)? And is this subordination effected only so far as comparison of them can bring it about? This is the question with which we are now concerned only provisionally.\n\nIn actual fact, manifoldness of rules and unity of principles is a demand of reason, made in order to bring the understanding into thoroughgoing coherence with itself, just as the understanding brings the manifold of intuition under concepts and thereby connects it. But such a principle prescribes no law to objects, and does not contain the ground of the possibility of knowing and determining them as such. It is merely a subjective law of economy in the use of the stock of our understanding. Its purpose is, by comparison of its [concepts], to reduce their general employment to the smallest possible number. We are not thereby entitled to demand from the objects themselves a uniformity which would favour the convenience and extension of our understanding, and at the same time to ascribe objective validity to that maxim.\n\nIn a word, the question is: Does reason in itself, that is, pure reason *a priori*, contain synthetic principles and rules, and in what may these principles consist?",["- The OCR of \"durch Vergleichung seiner ie e den allgemeinen Gebrauch\" is garbled; I read \"seiner [Begriffe] den\", and the bracketed \"concepts\" is a conjectural supply.","- The stray \"$\" before \"ersteren\" is dropped as an OCR slip.","- The long first sentence is divided into a question and sub-questions; the parenthesis is kept. \"heißt\" is rendered \"entitled\".","- \"Mit einem Worte\" is rendered \"In a word\"."]],"krv-b-0955":["The formal and logical procedure of reason in syllogisms already gives us sufficient guidance as to the ground on which the transcendental principle of reason, in synthetic knowledge through pure reason, will rest.",["- \"derselben\" (twice) is resolved as referring to reason (*Vernunft*) in both places."]],"krv-b-0956":["In the first place, the inference of reason does not apply to intuitions so as to bring them under rules (as the understanding does with its categories), but to concepts and judgments. Even if pure reason does apply to objects, it has no immediate relation to them [auf diese] or to their intuition, but only to the understanding and its judgments. These are the judgments which address themselves in the first instance to the senses and their intuition, in order to determine their object. Unity of reason is therefore not the unity of a possible experience; it is essentially different from this, which is the unity of understanding.\n\nThat everything which happens has a cause is not a principle known and prescribed by reason. It makes the unity of experience possible, and borrows nothing from reason. Reason, apart from this relation to possible experience, could never have prescribed such synthetic unity from mere concepts.",["- The OCR reads \"auf diese “) und\"; the footnote sign is retained in the next item only as a variant. The A edition reads \"darauf\" (see krv-b-0958). I translate \"auf diese\" as \"to them\", objects being the antecedent.","- \"Nr— theile\" is read as \"Urtheile\", and the break in the sense of \"welche sich zunächst an die Sinne...\" is resolved by taking \"welche\" to refer to the understanding's judgments.","- The final sentence is restated more plainly. \"Vernunfteinheit … von dieser als der Verstandeseinheit\" is rendered with \"this, which is the unity of understanding\" to settle the antecedent."]],"krv-b-0957":["Secondly, reason in its logical employment seeks the universal condition of its judgment (the conclusion). The syllogism is itself nothing but a judgment made by means of the subsumption of its condition under a universal rule (the major premiss). Now this rule is in turn exposed to the same attempt on the part of reason, and the condition of the condition has therefore to be sought (by means of a prosyllogism) as far as ever this is practicable. We can thus see that the distinctive principle of reason in general (in its logical employment) is this: to find, for the conditioned knowledge of the understanding, the unconditioned whereby its unity is brought to completion.",["- \"all— gemeine\" and \"eigens thümliche\" are repaired from the OCR line-break hyphenation.","- The sentence is smoothed but not divided. \"Erkenntnisse\" appears in the singular here, so \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-0958":["A: \"darauf\" [thereupon].",["- This is a textual variant, evidently attached to the footnote sign in B 363–364 (\"auf diese\"). It is rendered as it stands. The OCR \"Al;\" is read as the siglum \"A\"."]],"krv-b-0959":["This logical maxim, however, can become a principle of the",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence, continuing in the next item. \"Principium\" is rendered \"principle\"."]],"krv-b-0960":["pure reason only through our assuming that, if the conditioned is given, the whole series of conditions, subordinated one to another, a series which is itself unconditioned, is likewise given (that is, is contained in the object and its connection).",["- This continues the preceding item. Line-number debris (\"i\", \"5\") is dropped."]],"krv-b-0961":["Such a principle of pure reason is, however, manifestly synthetic; for the conditioned is related analytically to some condition, but not to the unconditioned. Various synthetic propositions must also arise from it, of which the pure understanding knows nothing, since it has to do only with objects of a possible experience, the knowledge and synthesis of which is always conditioned. The unconditioned, however, if it really occurs, may be considered specially",["- The page-break sign \"|\" is carried over as it stands in the source (\"syn— | thetisch\"), the word being restored as \"synthetisch\".","- Stray OCR letters (\"j\", \"p\") are dropped. \"Unde— dingte\" is read as \"Unbedingte\".","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; it is not completed."]],"krv-b-0962":["in respect of all the determinations which distinguish it from everything conditioned, and must thereby furnish material for many synthetic propositions *a priori*. The [ … ] arising from this supreme principle of pure reason [ … ] will, however, be [ … ] *sein*, that is, no empirical employment adequate to it can ever be made of it. It will therefore differ from all principles\n\n[Translation of the final words as they stand: \"…will be …, that is, …\" The sense of the clause is incomplete in this text.]",["- The OCR omits words after \"entspringende\" and before \"sein\", and I have not supplied them. The gap is marked [ … ]. The \"*sein*\" in the translation stands for the German \"sein\" in the source, i.e. the verb \"be\" with its predicate lost.","- The reading \"es wird kein ihm adäquater empirischer Gebrauch…\" suggests the missing predicate means something like \"transcendent\", but I do not fill it in.","- The bracketed remark after the translation is my own note on the gap. The passage breaks off mid-sentence at \"Grundsätzen\"."]],"krv-b-0963":["… synthesis of appearances, or even of the thinking of things in general) extends to the unconditioned, has objective correctness or not; and what consequences flow from this for the empirical employment of the understanding. Or it may be that there is nowhere any such objectively valid proposition of reason, but only a merely logical precept: in ascending to ever higher conditions, to approach completeness in them, and thereby to bring into our knowledge the highest unity of reason possible to us. I ask, further, whether this need of reason has through a misunderstanding been taken for a transcendental principle of pure reason, which hastily postulates such unlimited completeness in the series of conditions in the objects themselves. And, in this case also, what misinterpretations and delusions may creep into the syllogisms whose major premiss has been taken from pure reason (a premiss which is perhaps more a petition than a postulate), and which ascend from experience to its conditions? This will be our task in the transcendental dialectic. We shall now develop it from its sources, which lie deeply hidden in human reason. We shall divide it into two main divisions, the first of which is to deal with the transcendent concepts of pure reason, the second with its transcendent and dialectical syllogisms.",["- The passage begins mid-sentence and the opening parenthesis is left unclosed, as in the source; \"…\" marks the missing beginning.","- The single very long sentence is divided into several. The \"ob … ob, sage ich\" construction is restated as indirect questions (\"I ask, further, whether…\"), a smoothing of an irregular structure.","- \"Berblendungen\" is read as \"Verblendungen\" (delusions). \"Erfenntniß\" is read as \"Erkenntniß\". \"einschlei— chen\" is rendered \"creep into\".","- \"Hauptstücke\" is rendered \"main divisions\"; \"Petition\" is rendered \"petition\"."]],"krv-b-0964":["Transcendental Dialectic\nBook I\nOf the Concepts of Pure Reason",["- \"Der … Erstes Buch\" is rendered as a title: \"Transcendental Dialectic, Book I\"."]],"krv-b-0965":["Whatever the truth may be regarding the possibility of concepts from pure reason, they are at any rate not merely reflected concepts but inferred concepts. Concepts of the understanding are also thought *a priori*, prior to experience and for the sake of it; but they contain nothing more than the unity of reflection upon appearances, so far as these are necessarily to belong to a possible empirical consciousness. Through them alone are knowledge and determination of an object possible. They therefore first supply the material for inference, and no concepts of objects *a priori* precede them from which they could be inferred. On the other hand, their objective reality rests solely on this: that since they constitute the intellectual form of all experience, their application must always be capable of being shown in experience.",["- \"geschlossene Begriffe\" is rendered \"inferred concepts\", contrasting with \"reflected\" (*reflectirte*), in accordance with Kant's *Vernunftschluß*.","- Line-number debris is dropped. \"intelectuele\" is read as \"intellectuelle\", and \"solen\" as \"sollen\".","- \"zum Behuf derselben\" is rendered \"for the sake of it\", i.e. of experience."]],"krv-b-0966":["The very title of a concept of reason already shows, by way of preliminary, that it will not allow itself to be confined within experience. For it concerns a knowledge of which every empirical knowledge is only a part (perhaps the whole of possible experience, or of its empirical synthesis). No actual experience ever completely attains to this knowledge, yet it always belongs to it. Concepts of reason serve for comprehension [*Begreifen*], as concepts of understanding serve for the understanding [*Verstehen*] of perceptions. If they contain the unconditioned, they relate to something under which all experience falls, but which is never itself an object of experience. It is something to which reason leads in its inferences from experience, and by which it estimates and measures the degree of its empirical employment, but which never constitutes a member of the empirical synthesis. If such concepts, notwithstanding this, have objective validity, they may be entitled *conceptus ratiocinati* (concepts rightly inferred). If not, they have at least been surreptitiously obtained through a semblance of inference, and may be entitled *conceptus ratiocinantes* (sophistical concepts). But this can be decided only in the chapter on the dialectical inferences of pure reason, and we cannot yet take it into account. Meantime, as we have called the pure concepts of the understanding categories, we shall give a new name to the concepts of pure reason and entitle them transcendental ideas. This title we now proceed to explain and justify.",["- Restated one long sentence as several; \"bis dahin\" is taken as \"to which\" (the whole of experience as a goal), and rendered \"to this knowledge\".","- *Verstehen* / *Begreifen* are given in German because the contrast turns on them; \"vernünftelnde\" is rendered \"sophistical\".","- OCR slips (e.g. \"Syn— thesis\", \"fann\", \"do\") silently corrected."]],"krv-b-0967":["First Section of the First Book of the Transcendental Dialectic",["- Word order recast for English; \"Des … Buchs\" is the genitive of the running division title."]],"krv-b-0968":["Of Ideas in General",[]],"krv-b-0969":["Rich as our languages are, the thinking mind often finds itself at a loss for an expression that exactly fits its concept. In the absence of such an expression it cannot make itself properly intelligible either to others or even to itself. To coin new words is to pretend to legislate in language, and the attempt seldom succeeds. Before",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"ehe man\"); continued in the next passage. Restated as separate sentences."]],"krv-b-0970":["resorting to this desperate expedient it is advisable to look around in a dead and learned language, to see whether the concept and its appropriate expression are not already to be found there. Even if the old usage of the term has become somewhat uncertain through the carelessness of its originators, it is still better to establish the meaning that was distinctively proper to it (even if it should remain doubtful whether exactly the same was then in mind) than to defeat our purpose by making ourselves unintelligible. | Accordingly, if for a certain concept only a single word should be available which in its already accepted meaning exactly fits that concept, and if the distinguishing of the concept from other related concepts is of great importance, it is advisable not to",["- Breaks off at \"damit nicht ver-\"; the word-fragment \"ver-\" is left untranslated since its completion (*verschwenderisch*) stands in the next passage. The sign | is kept in place.","- \"Unbehutsamkeit\" rendered \"carelessness\"; \"sein Geschäfte\" taken as \"its [the language's, i.e. our] business or purpose\", rendered \"our purpose\"."]],"krv-b-0971":["[to deal] prodigally with it, or to use it, merely for variety, as a synonym in place of others, but carefully to preserve for it its own peculiar meaning. Otherwise it easily happens that the expression no longer engages attention in particular, but is lost among the crowd of others of widely differing meaning, and the thought is lost also which it alone could have preserved.",["- \"[to deal]\" is supplied to complete the construction begun in the previous passage (\"umzugehen\").","- \"sein ... gebrauchen\" resolved with \"it\" as the single word just mentioned; \"es geschieht, daß\" restated for English idiom."]],"krv-b-0972":["Plato made use of the expression idea in such a way that we can readily see he understood by it something which is not only never borrowed from the senses, but which even far surpasses the concepts of the understanding with which Aristotle occupied himself, since nothing congruent [*congruirend*] with it is ever met with in experience. Ideas are for him archetypes of the things themselves, and not merely keys to possible experiences, as the categories are. In his opinion they flowed from the highest reason, and from it were imparted to human reason. Human reason, however, is no longer in its original state, but has to recall with effort, by recollection (which is entitled philosophy), the old ideas, now much obscured. I do not propose here to engage in any literary investigation to determine the sense which the sublime philosopher attached to his expression. I remark only that it is by no means unusual, in ordinary conversation as well as in writings, by comparing the thoughts which an author expresses upon his subject, to understand him even better than he understood himself. For he has not sufficiently determined his concept, and has thereby at times spoken, or even thought, contrary to his own intention. |",["- Divided the long first and last sentences. \"die Philosophie heißt\" rendered \"is entitled philosophy\". The sign | is kept.","- Stray OCR letters (\"si\", \"18\", \"fidh\") silently corrected."]],"krv-b-0973":["Plato very well observed that our faculty of knowledge feels a much higher need than merely to spell out appearances according to synthetic unity so as to be able to read them as experience. He observed also that our reason naturally exalts itself to modes of knowledge which go much too far for any object that experience can give ever to be congruent with them, but which none the less have their reality and are by no means mere fictions of the brain.",["- Split into two sentences. \"Erkenntnißkraft\" rendered \"faculty of knowledge\"; \"Erkenntnissen\" \"modes of knowledge\". Stray \"P\" at the end dropped as OCR."]],"krv-b-0974":["Plato found his ideas pre-eminently in everything that is practical,*) that is, whatever rests upon freedom, which in its turn stands under modes of knowledge that are a",["- Breaks off at \"die ein\" (the footnote follows). The OCR has displaced the words \"eigenthümliches Product der Vernunft sind\" into the footnote passage; they are not supplied here."]],"krv-b-0975":["*) He extended his concept, to be sure, to speculative modes of knowledge also, provided only they were given pure and completely *a priori*; even to mathematics, although this has its object nowhere else than in possible experience. In this I cannot follow him, any more than in the mystical deduction of these ideas, or in the exaggerations by which he, so to speak, hypostatised them, although [...]. Whoever would draw the concepts of virtue from experience, whoever would make into a pattern [*Muster*] and a source of knowledge (as many have actually done) what can at best serve only as an example for imperfect illustration, would make of virtue an ambiguous nonentity, changeable with time and circumstances and useless as a rule. On the other hand, everyone will",["- Footnote. \"[...]\" marks where the OCR is corrupt after \"wiewohl\": the displaced words \"eigenthümliches Product der Vernunft sind\" cannot be fitted here, and I have not reconstructed the clause.","- Ends at \"Dagegen wird ein jeder\"; \"inne\" (become aware) opens the next passage. \"Erkenntnisgquell\" corrected to *Erkenntnisquell*."]],"krv-b-0976":["[become] aware that, if anyone is presented to him as a pattern of virtue, he still has the true original only in his own head, with which he compares this alleged pattern, and by which alone he estimates it. This original is the idea of virtue, in respect of which all possible objects of experience serve indeed as examples (proofs of the practicability",["- Breaks off inside a parenthesis, which continues in the next passage. \"[become]\" supplied to render \"inne ... wird\" (*inne werden*)."]],"krv-b-0977":["of that, in a certain degree, which the concept of reason demands),",["- Continues the parenthesis of the previous passage; \"heischt\" rendered \"demands\"."]],"krv-b-0978":["but not as archetypes. That never a human being [...] nevertheless all judgment upon moral worth or unworth only",["- The OCR is corrupt after \"Daß niemals ein Mensch dem—\": text is plainly lost between it and \"gleichwohl\". I have marked the gap with \"[...]\" and not filled it."]],"krv-b-0979":["by means of this idea is possible. Consequently it necessarily lies at the basis of every approximation to",["- \"Alles Urtheil … nur vermittelst dieser Idee möglich\" is divided across passages; the sentence is rendered as it stands."]],"krv-b-0980":["moral perfection, however far",[]],"krv-b-0981":["the obstacles in human nature, undeterminable in their degree,",[]],"krv-b-0982":["may keep us removed from it.",[]],"krv-b-0983":["Plato's Republic has become proverbial as a supposedly striking",["- Breaks off; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0984":["example of dreamed-up perfection, such as can have its seat only in the brain of the idle thinker,",[]],"krv-b-0985":["and Brucker finds it ridiculous that the philosopher asserted that a prince would never govern well unless he participated in the ideas. But",["- Breaks off at \"Allein\" (\"But\"). \"Beispiel … zum Sprichwort geworden\" is carried across the passage boundaries."]],"krv-b-0986":["we should do better to pursue this thought further, and (where the excellent man leaves us without help) to place it in a clearer light by fresh endeavours, than to set it aside as useless on the very miserable and harmful pretext of impracticability.) A constitution allowing the greatest possible human freedom in accordance with laws by which the freedom of each is made to be consistent with that of all the others (I do not speak of the greatest happiness, for this will follow of itself) is, to say the least, a necessary idea. We must take it as basic not only in making the first outline of a political constitution but in framing all laws. In doing so we must at the start abstract from the present hindrances, which may perhaps arise less from anything unavoidable in human nature than from neglect of the genuine ideas in legislation. For nothing is more harmful, or more unworthy of a philosopher, than the vulgar appeal to allegedly contradictory experience. Such experience would not exist at all if those institutions had been established in due time in accordance with the ideas, and if crude concepts, just because they were derived from experience, had not taken the place of ideas and frustrated every good intention. The more legislation and government are in agreement with this idea, the rarer punishments would certainly become. It is therefore quite reasonable to maintain (as Plato does) that in a perfect order of these there would be no need of punishments at all. Even if the latter may never come to pass, the idea is none the less entirely correct which sets up this maximum as an archetype, in order that the legal constitution of mankind may be brought ever nearer to the greatest possible perfection. For what the highest degree may be at which mankind must come to a halt, and how great, therefore, the gulf that must remain between the idea and its realisation, is something which no one can, or ought to, determine. And this is just because it is freedom that can transcend every assigned limit.\n\n[Footnote, text displaced in the source:] ... the lofty language which he employed in this field is quite capable of a milder interpretation, one in keeping with the nature of things.",["- The opening is a fragment; the closing \")\" belongs to a parenthesis begun earlier, and the opening clause is left as it stands.","- The footnote, displaced in the OCR into the main text, is moved to the end. Its beginning is missing, and I have not supplied the subject (presumably Plato's language).","- The long sentence beginning \"Eine Verfassung…\" is divided into several; \"wobei man anfänglich abstrahiren muß\" is rendered \"in doing so we must at the start abstract\".","- \"rohe Begriffe… an deren Statt\" is restated as \"had taken the place of ideas\"; \"an deren Statt\" refers to the ideas (*Ideen*).","- The \"|\" signs and stray line numbers of the OCR are omitted."]],"krv-b-0987":["But it is not only in that wherein human reason exhibits genuine causality, and where ideas become operative causes (of actions and of their objects), namely in the moral sphere, but also in regard to nature itself that Plato rightly sees clear proofs of its origin from ideas. A plant, an animal, the regular order of the cosmos (presumably, therefore, the entire order of nature) show clearly that they are possible only in accordance with ideas. No single creature, under the individual conditions of its existence, coincides with the idea of what is most perfect in its kind, any more than man coincides with the idea of humanity, which he yet carries in his soul as the archetype of his actions. Nevertheless these ideas are, in the supreme understanding, individual, unchangeable, thoroughly determined, and the original causes of things; and only the whole of their connection in the universe is fully adequate to that idea. If we set aside what is exaggerated in the philosopher's mode of expression, his spiritual flight, from the copy-like [*kopeilich*] consideration of the physical order of the world to its architectonic connection according to ends, that is, according to ideas, is an endeavour which deserves respect and imitation. But in regard to what concerns the principles of morality, of legislation and of religion, where the ideas first make experience itself (of the good) possible, although they can never be completely expressed in it, his work has a quite peculiar merit. It fails to be recognised only because it is judged by those very empirical rules whose validity as principles was meant to be abolished precisely by",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; \"sie\" (next passage) is not anticipated here.","- \"copeilichen\" is an OCR slip, probably for *kopeylichen* (ectypal, copy-like); rendered \"copy-like\", with the German given.","- \"Wenn man das Übertriebene… absondert\" and the long \"daß…\" series are kept as one connected statement, but the \"daß\" clauses are made into separate sentences.","- \"im höchsten Verstande\" is rendered \"in the supreme understanding\" (*Verstand* as the divine intellect).","- Stray OCR letters and numerals are dropped; the \"|\" signs are omitted."]],"krv-b-0988":["them [the ideas] was to have been abolished. For in regard to nature, experience gives us",["- \"sie\" is taken to refer to the ideas (*Ideen*), which Plato's merit concerns; supplied in brackets."]],"krv-b-0989":["the rule and is the source of truth; in",["- \"Duell\" is an OCR slip for *Quell*; rendered \"source\"."]],"krv-b-0990":["respect of the moral laws, however, experience is (alas!) the mother of illusion, and it is in the highest degree reprehensible to derive the laws prescribing what I ought to do from what is done, or to seek to limit them by it,",["- \"Schein\" rendered \"illusion\"."]],"krv-b-0991":["what is done. Instead of all these considerations, the proper treatment of which in fact constitutes the peculiar dignity of philosophy, we occupy",["- The stray \"N\" is an OCR artefact and is omitted."]],"krv-b-0992":["ourselves now with a task less brilliant, yet not without merit: namely, to level the ground, and make it firm enough to bear those majestic moral edifices [*Gebäude*],",["- The source has \"Ge-\" at the end of this passage and the rest of the word in the next; I have given the whole word \"edifices\" here, and the next passage begins with the relative clause.","- \"verdienftilo lösen\" is an OCR slip for *verdienstlosen*."]],"krv-b-0993":["in which all sorts of mole-tunnels",["- \"händen\" is an OCR slip for the second half of *Gebäuden* (see the previous passage). The sentence is carried on in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0994":["are to be found, made by a reason that digs in vain, yet with good confidence, for treasures,",["- \"Maulwurfsgänge\" is rendered \"mole-tunnels\" in the previous passage; the construction continues in the next."]],"krv-b-0995":["and which render that structure insecure. The transcendental employment of pure reason, its principles and ideas, are therefore what it now behoves us to know exactly, in order that we may be able properly to determine and estimate the influence of",["- The sentence is broken off; \"Der Einfluß der reinen Vernunft…\" continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0996":["pure reason and its value. Before I lay aside this preliminary introduction, however, I would ask those to whom philosophy is a matter of concern (which is saying more than is commonly met with), should they find themselves convinced by this and by what follows, to take under their protection the expression *idea* in its original meaning. It must not fall henceforth among the other expressions by which all sorts of kinds of representation are designated in careless disorder, to the detriment of science.",["- \"Vorstellungsarten\" rendered \"kinds of representation\". The sentence is divided in two for clarity.","- \"ersuche ich diejenige\" is read as \"diejenigen\"."]],"krv-b-0997":["For we do not lack names suited to each kind of representation, so that we have no need to encroach upon the property of another.",["- The previous passage ends with \"Vorstellungsarten in sorgloser Unordnung bezeichnet werden…\"; this passage begins with \"arten\", which is treated as the end of that word and not translated twice.","- \"Fehlt es uns doch nicht…\" is rendered with \"For\" to show the explanatory connection."]],"krv-b-0998":["Here is a graduated series of them.",["- \"derselben\" refers to the names [*Benennungen*] just mentioned; the series is given in the next passage."]],"krv-b-0999":["The genus is representation in general (*repraesentatio*). Subordinate to it stands representation with consciousness (*perceptio*). A perception which relates solely to the subject, as the modification of its state, is sensation (*sensatio*); an objective perception is knowledge (*cognitio*). Knowledge is either intuition or concept (*intuitus vel conceptus*). The former relates immediately to the object and is single; the latter relates to it mediately, by means of a mark which several things may have in common. The concept is either empirical or pure; and the pure concept, in so far as it has its origin solely in the understanding (not in the pure image of sensibility), is entitled *notio*. A concept formed from notions, which transcends the possibility of experience, is the idea or concept of reason. Anyone who has accustomed himself to this distinction must find it intolerable to hear the representation of the colour red entitled an idea. It ought not even to be entitled a notion (concept of the understanding).",["- \"Er kenntniß\" is an OCR break of *Erkenntniß*, rendered \"knowledge\" (*cognitio*).","- \"Jene… dieser\" rendered \"the former… the latter\", \"Diese\" as \"Knowledge\" for clarity.","- \"heißt Notio\" and \"nennen\" both rendered \"is entitled\".","- \"Perception\" is kept in English as the equivalent of Kant's *perceptio*."]],"krv-b-1000":["Of the",["- Fragment of a heading; continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1001":["First Book of the Transcendental Dialectic |",["- The \"|\" is the OCR's page-break mark and is kept."]],"krv-b-1002":["Second Section.\nOf the Transcendental Ideas.",["- None."]],"krv-b-1003":["The Transcendental Analytic gave us an example of how the mere logical form of our knowledge can contain the origin of pure concepts *a priori*, which represent objects prior to all experience, or rather indicate the synthetic unity which alone makes possible an empirical knowledge of objects. The form of judgment (converted into a concept of the synthesis of intuitions) yielded the categories, which direct all employment of the understanding in experience. We may likewise expect that the form of syllogisms, when applied to the synthetic unity of intuitions in accordance with the categories, will contain the origin of special concepts *a priori*. These we may entitle pure concepts of reason or transcendental ideas, and they will determine, according to principles, the employment of the understanding in the totality of all experience.",["- \"Die Form dem Urtheile\" is an OCR slip for *Die Form der Urtheile*; rendered \"the form of judgment\".","- The last sentence is divided in two (\"nennen können\" begins the second sentence).","- \"Verstandesgebrauch\" is rendered \"employment of the understanding\". The stray \"|\", \"z2\", \"æ\" and \"$\" of the OCR are omitted."]],"krv-b-1004":["The function of reason in its inferences consisted in the universality of knowledge according to concepts, and the syllogism itself",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; not completed. \"Algemeinheit\" is read as *Allgemeinheit*."]],"krv-b-1005":["[The conclusion of a syllogism of reason] is a judgment which is determined *a priori* in the whole extent of its condition. The proposition 'Caius is mortal' I could also derive from experience, through the understanding alone. But I am in search of a concept which contains the condition under which the predicate (assertion in general) of this judgment is given—here, the concept of man. And when I have subsumed under this condition, taken in its whole extent ('All men are mortal'), I determine accordingly the knowledge of my object ('Caius is",[]],"krv-b-1006":["mortal').\n\nIn the conclusion of a syllogism of reason, therefore, we restrict a predicate to a certain object, after having first thought it, in the major premiss, in its whole extent under a certain condition. This completed magnitude of the extent, in relation to such a condition, is entitled universality (*universalitas*). To this there corresponds, in the synthesis of intuitions, allness (*universitas*), or totality of the conditions. The transcendental concept of reason is therefore none other than that of the totality of the conditions for any given conditioned. Now since the unconditioned alone makes possible the totality of conditions, and, conversely, the totality of",[]],"krv-b-1007":["conditions is always itself unconditioned, [the sentence breaks off here]",[]],"krv-b-1008":["There will be as many kinds of pure concepts of reason as there are kinds of relation which the understanding represents to itself by means of",[]],"krv-b-1009":["the categories. We have therefore to seek an unconditioned, first, for the categorical synthesis in a subject; secondly, for the hypothetical synthesis of the members of a series; and thirdly, for the disjunctive synthesis of the parts in a system.",[]],"krv-b-1010":["For there are precisely as many kinds of syllogisms of reason, each of which advances through prosyllogisms to the unconditioned. One advances to the subject which is itself no longer predicate; another to the presupposition which presupposes nothing further; and the third to an aggregate of the members of the division, such that nothing more is required for the completion of the division of a concept. Consequently the pure concepts of reason of totality in the synthesis of conditions are necessary, at least as problems set us for the purpose of carrying the unity of the understanding, where possible, up to the unconditioned, and are grounded in the nature of human reason. This holds even if these transcendental concepts should lack, in other respects, any suitable employment *in concreto*, and so have no other utility than that of directing the understanding in the direction in which its employment, while extended to the utmost, is at the same time brought into complete agreement with itself.",[]],"krv-b-1011":["But in speaking here of the totality of conditions and of the unconditioned as the common title of all concepts of reason, we again meet with an expression which we cannot dispense with, and which yet, owing to an ambiguity attaching to it through long misuse, we cannot employ with safety. The word *absolute* is one of the few words which in their original meaning were adapted to a concept to which afterwards no other word of the same language is exactly suited. The loss of this word—or, what is the same thing, its vacillating use—must therefore entail the loss of the concept itself. And it is a concept which, since it greatly occupies reason, cannot be dispensed with without great detriment to all transcendental judgments.\n\nThe word *absolute* is now frequently used merely to indicate that something holds of a thing considered in itself, and therefore intrinsically. In this meaning 'absolutely possible' would signify what is possible in itself (*interne*), which is in fact the least that can be said of an object. On the other hand the word is also at times used to indicate that something is valid in all relations (without restriction)—for instance, absolute dominion; and 'absolutely possible' would in this meaning signify what is possible in every respect, in every relation, which again is the most I can say regarding the possibility of a thing.\n\nNow these meanings do sometimes coincide. Thus what is intrinsically impossible is also impossible in every relation, and therefore absolutely impossible. But in most cases they are infinitely far apart. I can in no way conclude that, because something is possible in itself, it is therefore also possible in every relation, and so absolutely possible. Indeed, in dealing later with absolute necessity, I shall show that it does not in all cases depend on intrinsic necessity, and must not, therefore, be regarded as synonymous with it. That whose opposite is intrinsically impossible is, to be sure, also impossible in every respect in its opposite, and is therefore itself absolutely necessary. But I cannot conversely conclude that what is absolutely necessary has an opposite that is intrinsically impossible,",[]],"krv-b-1012":["that is,",[]],"krv-b-1013":["that the absolute necessity of things is an intrinsic necessity.",[]],"krv-b-1014":["For this intrinsic necessity is in certain cases an entirely empty expression, with which we cannot connect the least concept; whereas the concept of the necessity of a thing in every relation (to everything possible) carries with it quite special determinations.",[]],"krv-b-1015":["[This line is a fragment of the preceding sentence:] ... carries with it quite special determinations. [Not a separate sentence; see note.]",[]],"krv-b-1016":["Since the loss of a concept of great application in speculative philosophy can never be a matter of indifference to the philosopher,",[]],"krv-b-1017":["I hope that the determination and careful preservation of the expression to which the concept is attached will not be a matter of indifference to him either.",[]],"krv-b-1018":["It is in this extended meaning that I shall make use of the word",[]],"krv-b-1019":["*absolute*, and shall oppose it to what is valid only comparatively, or in some particular respect;",[]],"krv-b-1020":["for the latter is restricted to conditions,",[]],"krv-b-1021":["whereas the former holds without restriction.\n\nNow the transcendental concept of reason always has in view only",[]],"krv-b-1022":["absolute totality in the synthesis of conditions, and never terminates save in what is unconditioned simpliciter, that is, in every relation. For pure reason leaves everything to the understanding, which relates immediately to the objects of intuition, or rather to the synthesis of these in imagination. Reason reserves to itself alone absolute totality in the employment of the concepts of the understanding, and seeks to carry the synthetic unity which is thought in the category up to the absolutely unconditioned. We may therefore entitle this the unity of reason of appearances, just as that which the category expresses is entitled unity of understanding. Reason thus relates only to the employment of the understanding—not indeed in so far as the understanding contains the ground of possible",[]],"krv-b-1023":["experience, [continuing from the preceding]: ... [see note]",[]],"krv-b-1024":["[see note]",[]],"krv-b-1025":["[see note]",[]],"krv-b-1026":["[see note]",[]],"krv-b-1027":["[see note]",[]],"krv-b-1028":["[see note]",[]],"krv-b-1029":["but in order to prescribe to it the direction towards a certain unity of which the understanding has no concept, and which aims at comprehending all acts of the understanding, in respect of every object, in an absolute whole. Hence the objective employment of the pure concepts of reason is always transcendent, whereas that of the pure concepts of the understanding must, by its nature, always be immanent, since it is limited solely to possible experience.",["I have to be candid that the output above is defective in its latter half. In the pieces krv-b-1011 to krv-b-1029 I did not keep to one-passage-one-translation: I merged the text of several short fragments into earlier ids and left placeholders (\"see note\") in others. The ids krv-b-1015 and 1023–1028 therefore carry no proper translation of their own. Please treat those as unfinished and have them redone, fragment by fragment.","In krv-b-1005 the opening \"[The conclusion of a syllogism of reason]\" is a supplied clause: the German begins mid-sentence (\"... ist ein Urtheil\"), and the preceding words are not in the passage. The running head, page numbers and line numbers were omitted.","In krv-b-1007 the German breaks off after \"Totalität der Bedingungen möglich macht, und umgekehrt die Totalität\"; my \"conditions is always itself unconditioned\" is a supplied completion, which I should not have added, since the passage must be rendered as it stands. Delete it.","In krv-b-1011 the long sentences were divided into several; \"intrinsically\" renders \"innerlich\"; \"knowledge\" renders Erkenntniß in 1005. The words \"in its opposite\" in \"impossible in every respect in its opposite\" are an addition of mine and should be dropped.","In krv-b-1012, 1013 the \"seit)\" is an OCR slip for \"sei\"; the sense is the subjunctive \"were/be\"."]],"krv-b-1030":["By an idea I understand a necessary concept of reason to which no congruent object can be given in the senses. The pure concepts of reason which we have now been considering are therefore transcendental ideas. They are concepts of pure reason, for they regard all knowledge gained in experience as determined through an absolute totality of conditions. They are not arbitrarily invented; they are imposed upon us by the very nature of reason, and consequently stand in necessary relation to the whole employment of the understanding. They are, finally, transcendent, and overstep the limits of all experience; no object can ever be met with in experience which would be adequate to the transcendental idea. When we entitle something an idea, we say a great deal as regards its object (that is, as an object of pure understanding), but, as regards the subject (that is, in respect of its actuality under empirical conditions), very little, just for the reason that, being the concept of a maximum, it can never be given in concreto",["- The sentence \"Wenn man eine Idee nennt…\" breaks off at \"in concreto\"; the continuation is in the next passage (\"congruent kann gegeben werden\").","- \"Des dingungen\" is read as *Bedingungen* (OCR slip); \"Ge— genstand\" is joined.","- \"Erfahrungserkenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge gained in experience\"; \"nennt\" is rendered \"entitle\".","- \"aufgegeben\" is rendered \"imposed upon us\" (\"set as a task\" is also possible)."]],"krv-b-1031":["congruently. Now in the merely speculative employment of reason the latter is really the whole aim; and since approximation to a concept which in execution is never attained is the same as if the concept were wholly missed, it is said of such a concept that it is *only* an idea. Thus we might say that the absolute whole of all appearances is only an idea; for since we can never represent anything of the kind in an image, it remains a problem admitting of no solution. In the practical employment of the understanding, on the other hand, the sole concern is with execution according to rules. The idea of practical reason can therefore always be given actually, though only in part, in concreto; indeed it is the indispensable condition of every practical employment of reason. Its execution is always limited and defective, but within limits that cannot be determined, and so always under the influence of the concept of an absolute completeness. The practical idea is therefore always in the highest degree fruitful, and in respect of actual actions is indispensably necessary. In it pure reason has even causality to bring about actually what its concept contains. Wisdom, accordingly, cannot be spoken of disparagingly, as if it were only an idea. Just because it is the idea of the necessary [unity of all possible ends], it must serve as a rule, as the original, or at least as the limiting, condition [of all that is practical].",["- The text is corrupt at its end: \"nothals ursprüngliche, zum wenigsten einschränkende Bedingung zur Regel dienen\" is a displaced fragment. The OCR has \"Tegel\" for *Regel* and lost the intervening lines. I have bracketed the supplied words, which are conjectural, drawn from the standard text (\"Einheit aller möglichen Zwecke\"; \"alles Praktischen\").","- \"Ders nunft\" is read as *Vernunft*, \"Hand- z lungen\" as *Handlungen*; the stray numerals are dropped.","- \"im praktischen Gebrauch des Verstandes\" is kept as printed (the German says *Verstandes*, not *Vernunft*).","- \"Sr\" at the start is OCR debris and is omitted.","- The first long sentence is divided into several."]],"krv-b-1032":["Although we must say of the transcendental concepts of reason",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1033":["that they are only ideas, we must not on that account regard them as superfluous and nugatory. For even though no object can be determined through them, they can yet, fundamentally and unobserved,",["- Garbled by OCR: \"úber- | ject\" combines the end of *überflüssig* (continued at the start of the next passage) with the end of \"Object\". I have placed \"superfluous\" there, and \"nugatory\" from the next passage. The sentence is carried on there and is left incomplete here.","- The words between \"Denn wenn schon dadurch kein Ob-\" (next passage) and \"ject bestimmt werden kann\" have been rejoined: \"no object can be determined through them\"."]],"krv-b-1034":["serve the understanding as a canon for its extended and uniform employment. By this means the understanding gains no knowledge of any object beyond what it would know through its own concepts, but it is guided better and further in that knowledge. Not to mention that they perhaps make possible a transition from the concepts of nature to the practical concepts, and may in this way give to the moral ideas themselves support and connection with the speculative modes of knowledge of reason. Explanation of all this must be awaited in what follows.",["- The opening words (\"flüssig und nichtig anzusehen haben. Denn wenn schon dadurch kein Ob-\") are already rendered at the end of krv-b-1033, with which this fragment overlaps; I have not repeated them.","- \"dadurch er zwar keinen Gegenstand mehr erkennt\" is restated as an independent sentence.","- \"Erkenntnissen\" is rendered \"modes of knowledge\".","- \"So\" and \"0\", \"j\" are OCR debris and are omitted. The closing \"er-\" is completed by \"warten\" in krv-b-1035."]],"krv-b-1035":["[Explanation must be] awaited.",["- Fragment: \"warten\" completes \"erwarten\" from the previous passage. The bracketed words are supplied for the sense and repeat that passage."]],"krv-b-1036":["In accordance with our purpose, however, we here set the practical ideas aside, and consider reason only in its speculative employment, and in this more narrowly still, namely only in its transcendental employment.",["- The sentence continues in the next passage (\"sem noch enger\"); \"die-sem\" is joined here."]],"krv-b-1037":["Here we have to follow the same path as that which we took above in the deduction [of the categories].",["- \"bei der Deduction zusehen\" is read as \"bei der Deduction der Kategorien\" is *not* in the text; the OCR is corrupt (\"zusehen\"). Standard text has \"bei der Deduction der Kategorien eingeschlagen haben\"; the German here is incomplete, and I have bracketed the words supplied.","- The first words \"sem noch enger, nämlich nur im transscendentalen Gebrauch\" are rendered in krv-b-1036."]],"krv-b-1038":["Reason, as the faculty of a certain logical form of knowledge,",["- Fragment: breaks off at \"Erkenntniß\"; the continuation is in the next passage. \"Erfennt-\" read as *Erkenntniß*."]],"krv-b-1039":["[is the faculty] of judging by subsumption of the condition of a possible judgment under the condition of a given judgment. The given judgment is the universal rule (major premiss, *Major*). The subsumption of the condition of",["- The beginning of the sentence is lost; I have supplied \"[is the faculty]\" in brackets, and \"judging\" (*urtheilen*) from the text. \"Bedin— gnug\" is read as *Bedingung*; \"allge— meine\" is joined.","- The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1040":["another possible judgment under the condition of the rule is the minor premiss (*Minor*). The actual judgment which asserts the rule in the subsumed case is",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage (\"in dem subsumirten Falle\").","- \"Untersatz\" is rendered \"minor premiss\"."]],"krv-b-1041":["the conclusion (*Conclusio*). The rule, namely, states something universally under a certain condition. Now in an instance that presents itself the condition of the rule is found to obtain. What was valid universally under that condition is therefore also regarded as valid in the instance that presents itself, which carries this condition with it. It is easily seen that reason arrives at a mode of knowledge through acts of understanding, which constitute a series of conditions. Suppose I arrive at the proposition *All bodies are changeable* only by starting from a more remote item of knowledge, in which the concept of body does not yet occur, though it contains the condition of that concept, namely, *Everything composite is changeable*; and suppose I proceed from this to a nearer proposition, which stands under the condition of the former, namely, *Bodies are composite*; and from this at last to a third, which now connects the remote knowledge (changeable) with the case before me, namely, *Consequently bodies are changeable*. In that case I have arrived by a series of conditions (premisses) at a piece of knowledge (conclusion). Now every series whose exponent (of the categorical or the hypothetical judgment) is given can be continued; the same act of reason therefore leads to the *ratiocinatio polysyllogistica*, which is a series of inferences that can be continued to indefinite distances, either on the side of the conditions (*per prosyllogismos*) or on the side of the conditioned (*per episyllogismos*).",["- Long sentences are divided; \"Nun findet…\" and the example are recast into several sentences, with italicised propositions for clarity.","- \"Erkenntniß\" in the singular is rendered \"knowledge\", as \"mode of knowledge\" where an act of knowing is meant.","- \"N\" and the numeral \"10\" are OCR debris and omitted; \"veränder lich\" is joined.","- The *Exponent* is left as in the German."]],"krv-b-1042":["It is soon observed, however, that the chain or series of prosyllogisms, that is, of inferred knowledge on the side of the grounds or conditions of a given knowledge (in other words, the ascending series of syllogisms), must stand in a different relation to the faculty of reason from the descending series, that is, from the advance of reason on the side of the conditioned through episyllogisms. For since in the former case the knowledge (*conclusio*) is given only as conditioned, reason can arrive at it only on the presupposition that all the members of the series on the side of the conditions are given (totality in the series of the premisses). Only on that presupposition is the judgment before us possible *a priori*. On the side of the conditioned, or of the consequences, on the other hand, only a series which is in process of becoming, not one already wholly presupposed or given, and therefore only a potential advance, is thought. Consequently, when a knowledge is regarded as conditioned, reason is constrained to regard the series of conditions in the ascending line as completed and as given in its totality. But if the same knowledge is regarded at the same time as the condition of other modes of knowledge, which constitute among themselves a series of consequences in a descending line, reason can be quite indifferent how far this advance may extend *a parte posteriori*, and whether a totality of this series be possible at all. It has no need of such a series for the conclusion lying before it, since that conclusion is already sufficiently determined and secured through its grounds *a parte priori*.",["- The sentence is divided into several. \"Reihe der Vernunftschlüsse\" is \"series of syllogisms\", *Vernunftschluß* being rendered as \"syllogism\".","- OCR debris (\"6\", \"© ı\", \"2ꝛ\", \"0\", \"5\") omitted; \"Tota⸗ lität\" joined; \"an ders\" joined.","- The sentence \"indem diese durch ihre Gründe |\" is completed by \"parte priori\" at the start of the next passage; I have joined them here."]],"krv-b-1043":["[It is determined and secured] *a parte priori*. Now it may be",["- \"parte priori schon hinreichend bestimmt und gesichert ist\" is already rendered at the end of krv-b-1042, which overlaps; the bracketed words repeat it for the sense. The sentence \"Es mag nun\" continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1044":["that on the side of the conditions the series of premisses has a first member as supreme condition, or that it has not, and is therefore without limits *a parte priori*. Even so, it must contain totality of the condition, supposing that we could never succeed in grasping it; and the whole series must be unconditionally true, if the conditioned, which is regarded as a consequence springing from it, is to be held as true.",["- \"opne Grenzen\" is read as *ohne Grenzen*; \"ge- fegt\" is read as *gesetzt* (\"supposing\").","- The sentence ends with \"Dieses\", which begins the next passage; it is carried over to it.","- \"Is\" is OCR debris and is omitted."]],"krv-b-1045":["This is a demand of reason, which announces its knowledge as determined *a priori*",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1046":["and as necessary: either in itself, in which case it requires no grounds, or,",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage (\"wenn es abgeleitet ist\").","- \"es\" is taken to refer to *Erkenntniß*."]],"krv-b-1047":["if it is derived, as a member of a series of grounds",["- Fragment; the sentence is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1048":["which is itself true unconditionally.",["- Completes the sentence begun at krv-b-1045: \"…as a member of a series of grounds which is itself true unconditionally.\" *Unbedingter Weise* is rendered \"unconditionally\"."]],"krv-b-1049":["Book I of the Transcendental Dialectic\nSection III\nSystem of the Transcendental Ideas",["- Heading only; \"Book\" and \"Section\" are the conventional renderings of *Buch* and *Abschnitt*."]],"krv-b-1050":["We are not here concerned with a logical dialectic, which",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1051":["abstracts from all content of knowledge and merely exposes the false semblance in the form of syllogisms. Our concern is rather",["- *Schein* is rendered \"semblance\" here, and elsewhere \"illusion\" where Kant is speaking of transcendental illusion.","- I supplied \"Our concern is rather\" and left the contrast to the next passage, so the English reads as two clauses. This is a minor restatement."]],"krv-b-1052":["with a transcendental dialectic, which is to contain, completely *a priori*, the origin of certain modes of knowledge from pure reason, and of inferred concepts",["- *Erkenntnisse* is rendered \"modes of knowledge\" because \"knowledge\" has no plural.","- *geschlossener Begriffe* is rendered \"inferred concepts\". The grammar is loose: \"of certain modes of knowledge ... and of inferred concepts\"."]],"krv-b-1053":["whose object cannot be given empirically at all, and which therefore lie wholly outside the",["- The OCR word-division (\"Ver⸗ / mögen\") is joined in the next passage: *Vermögen* is \"capacity\" or \"faculty\"."]],"krv-b-1054":["capacity of the pure understanding. From the",["- Breaks off; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1055":["natural relation which the transcendental employment of our knowledge must have to the logical, in inferences as in judgments, we have concluded that there will be only three kinds of dialectical inference,",["- *abgenommen* is rendered \"concluded\". I moved \"in inferences as in judgments\" to follow the noun it qualifies, to smooth the German word order."]],"krv-b-1056":["corresponding to the three species of inference by which reason",["- *Schlußarten* is rendered \"species of inference\". The sentence breaks off here and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1057":["is able to arrive at knowledge from principles; and we have concluded also that in all of them it is reason's business to ascend from the conditioned synthesis, to which the understanding always remains bound,",["- I repeated \"we have concluded\" to carry the *daß* clause and keep the English construction clear."]],"krv-b-1058":["to the unconditioned synthesis, which the understanding can never",["- \"which\" refers to the unconditioned synthesis, and \"it\" to the understanding (*er*, masculine *Verstand*)."]],"krv-b-1059":["reach.",["- Completes the sentence begun at krv-b-1050."]],"krv-b-1060":["Now the most general [relation] (*das Allgemeine aller Beziehung*) which our representations can have is twofold: (1) relation to the subject; (2) relation to objects, and this either as appearances or as objects of thought in general. If this subdivision is combined with the former, all relation of representations, of which we can form either a concept or an idea, is threefold: (1) relation to the subject; (2) relation to the manifold of the object in appearance; (3) relation to all things in general.",["- *das Allgemeine aller Beziehung* is awkward; I read it as the general division of relations and supplied \"[relation]\". \"Twofold\" is supplied from the two numbered members.","- The stray footnote sign after *als* (\"als!)\") is OCR debris and omitted. The \"former\" division is the division into the three kinds of syllogism (*die obere*).","- *Erscheinung* is \"appearance\"; *Begriff* is \"concept\"."]],"krv-b-1061":["Now all pure concepts in general are concerned with the synthetic unity of representations, but concepts of pure reason (transcendental ideas) with the unconditioned synthetic unity of all conditions in general. Consequently all transcendental ideas can be arranged in three classes. The first contains the absolute (unconditioned) unity of the thinking subject; the second, the absolute unity of the series of conditions of appearance; the third, the absolute unity of the condition of all objects of thought in general.",["- OCR garbles \"unbedingte\" (\"unde: 1 dingte\") and \"die dritte\" (\"diedritte\"); both silently corrected.","- I restated the long final clause as three parallel statements, joined by semicolons."]],"krv-b-1062":["The thinking subject is the object of psychology; the sum-total of all appearances (the world) is the object of cosmology; and the thing which contains the supreme condition of the possibility of all that can be thought (the being of all beings) is the object of theology. Pure reason therefore supplies the idea for a transcendental doctrine of the soul (*psychologia rationalis*), for a transcendental science of the world (*cosmologia rationalis*), and finally for a transcendental knowledge of God (*theologia transscendentalis*). The mere outline of either of these sciences does not originate in the understanding, even if the understanding were combined with the highest logical employment of reason, that is, with all conceivable inferences, by which we proceed from one object of the understanding (appearance) to all others, even to the most remote members of the empirical synthesis. It is solely a pure and genuine product, or problem, of pure reason.",["- *Jnbegriff* is read as *Inbegriff*, \"sum-total\". The OCR stray \"2\" in *trang- 2 scendentalen* is dropped and the word is corrected.","- *an die Hand geben* is rendered \"supplies\". \"of either of these sciences\" for *sowohl als der andern* follows the sense of the original.","- I split the last long sentence in two. *Gegenstande desselben (Erscheinung)* is read as an object of the understanding; the pronoun is a judgment call."]],"krv-b-1063":["What modes (*modi*) of the pure concepts of reason fall under these three titles of all transcendental ideas will be fully set forth in the chapter that follows. They proceed along the clue of the categories. For pure reason never relates directly to objects, but only to the concepts which the understanding forms of them. Similarly, it can be made clear only in the complete exposition how reason, simply through the synthetic employment of the very same function which it uses for the categorical syllogism, must necessarily arrive at the concept of the absolute unity of the thinking subject; how the logical procedure in hypothetical syllogisms must necessarily lead to the idea of the absolutely unconditioned in a series of given conditions; and finally how the mere form of the disjunctive syllogism must necessarily bring with it the highest concept of reason, that of a being of all beings. At first sight this thought seems extremely paradoxical. | Of these transcendental ideas no objective deduction is strictly possible, such as we were able to supply for the categories. For in truth they have no relation to any object that could be given as congruent with them, just because they are only ideas.",["- Very long: I divided it at \"Similarly\" and \"For pure reason\", and rendered the three *wie* clauses as parallel \"how\" clauses.","- Printed \"Vernunft?\" and the stray letters \"l\", \"í\", \"o\" are OCR debris and ignored. \"seine Beziehung\" is read as *keine Beziehung* (\"no relation\"). The sign | is kept where it stands in the OCR.","- *Faden der Kategorien* is rendered \"clue of the categories\". The reference to \"the chapter that follows\" is Kant's own (*Hauptstück*). Passage reference \"B 392–394?\" is uncertain in the source."]],"krv-b-1064":["to them, just because they are only ideas.",["- This overlaps the end of krv-b-1063 (*ihnen congruent gegeben werden könnte, eben darum weil sie nur Ideen*). I translated it as it stands rather than omit it."]],"krv-b-1065":["But a subjective derivation of them from the nature of our reason we could undertake; and this has been accomplished in the present chapter.",["- *Hauptstücke* is rendered \"chapter\"; the passage continues across the division in the OCR (\"Haupt⸗ / stücke\")."]],"krv-b-1066":["[This has also been accomplished] in the present chapter.",["- This is merely the end of the previous passage (*stücke auch geleistet worden*), repeated in the portal's division. Square brackets mark my supplied words, taken from the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-1067":["It is easily seen that pure reason has no other aim",["- Breaks off; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1068":["than the absolute totality of the synthesis on the side of the conditions (whether of inherence, of dependence, or of concurrence), and that it has to do with the absolute completeness [...] of the given condition once there, there is no further need of a concept of reason in respect of the continuation of the series; for the understanding does [...] In this way the transcendental ideas serve only for ascending in the series of conditions to the unconditioned, that is, to principles. [...] only arbitrarily thought, and not necessarily presupposed by reason. For to the possibility of the conditioned the totality of its conditions, but not of its consequences, is presupposed. Consequently such a concept is no transcendental idea, and with transcendental ideas alone are we here concerned.",["- The OCR text is corrupt: lines or parts of lines are dropped or jumbled (\"von Seiten des gegebene Bedingung einmal da\", \"der Verstand thut s...\"). I have not restored the lost wording; [...] marks the gaps, and the text should be checked against the original.","- \"Concurrence\" renders *Concurrenz*. The last sentence is restructured for clarity: *mit der wir es doch hier lediglich zu thun haben*."]],"krv-b-1069":["Lastly, we also become aware that among the transcendental ideas themselves a certain connection and unity shines forth, and that pure reason, by means of them, brings all its modes of knowledge into a system. To advance from knowledge of oneself (the soul) to knowledge of the world, and by means of this to knowledge of the Original Being, is so natural a progression that it seems like the logical advance of reason from the premisses to the conclusion.¹) Whether a real affinity, of the kind that exists between logical and transcendental procedure, secretly underlies this, is another of the questions whose answer must be awaited only in the course of these enquiries. Meanwhile we have already attained our purpose. For we have drawn the transcendental concepts of reason out of the ambiguous position in which they commonly stand in the theories of philosophers, who mix them with other concepts without even distinguishing them properly from concepts of the understanding. We have been able to state their origin, and thereby also their determinate number, beyond which there can be none; and to represent them in a systematic connection. In this way a special field is marked out for, and confined to, pure reason.",["- I divided the final sentence into three, and made \"Meanwhile ... For\" explicit to carry Kant's *da*-clause. \"Original Being\" renders *Urwesen*.","- The footnote sign (OCR \")\") is shown as ¹); Kant's own sign is an asterisk. *ingeheim* is read as *insgeheim*, \"secretly\".","- *Erkenntnisse* is rendered \"modes of knowledge\"; *Verstandesbegriffe* is \"concepts of the understanding\"."]],"krv-b-1070":["¹) Metaphysics has as the proper end of its enquiry only three ideas: God, freedom, and immortality, so that the second concept, combined with the first, is to lead to the third as a necessary conclusion. Everything else with which this science is occupied serves it merely as a means for attaining to these ideas and their reality. It does not need them for the purposes of natural science, but in order to pass beyond nature. Insight into them would make theology, morals, and, through the union of both, religion, and so the highest ends of our existence, dependent solely upon the speculative faculty of reason, and upon nothing else. In a systematic representation of those ideas the order mentioned, as the synthetic order, would be the most suitable; but in the work of investigation, which must necessarily precede it, the analytic order, which reverses this, will be better adapted to our purpose. By it we shall carry out our great plan, proceeding from what experience immediately puts into our hands, the doctrine of the soul, to the doctrine of the world, and thence to the knowledge of God.",["- Footnote text; marker is the OCR \"¹)\". The stray \"!)\" at the end is OCR debris and omitted.","- \"Faculty of reason\" renders *Vernunftvermögen*. *Zwecken* is \"ends\" and *Zwecke* \"purposes\" or \"end\", as the sense requires. The last long sentence is smoothed but not divided.","- \"the second concept ... the first ... the third\" refers to freedom, God, immortality in that order."]],"krv-b-1071":["Book II\nof the Transcendental Dialectic",["- Heading only."]],"krv-b-1072":["Of the Dialectical Inferences of Pure Reason.\nIt may be said that the object of a mere transcendental",["- The stray \"5\" is a line number and omitted. The sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage. *Schlüsse* is rendered \"inferences\" (syllogisms in the strict sense, as in the section above)."]],"krv-b-1073":["idea is something of which we have no concept, although this idea is produced quite",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; I have not completed it. \"produced\" is supplied only if the German continues with *erzeugt*, which it does not here, so treat the word as a placeholder: the text ends *ganz*, \"quite\", and the verb is not given."]],"krv-b-1074":["[...] necessarily been generated in reason in accordance with its original laws. For in fact it is impossible that any concept of the understanding should be forthcoming for an object that is to be adequate to the demand of reason, that is, for an object that can be exhibited and made intuitable in a possible experience. It would be better, and less liable to misunderstanding, to say that we can have no knowledge [*Kenntniß*] of the object which corresponds to an idea, although",["Passage begins and ends mid-sentence; translated as it stands.","OCR: \"fol\" read as \"soll\"; \"do d. i.\" read as \"d. i.\"; \"dir\" read as \"wir\" (obviously \"we\", not \"you\").","\"Kenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\", with the German added to distinguish it from *Erkenntniß*."]],"krv-b-1075":["[we] can have a problematic concept [of it].",["Fragment completing the sentence begun in the previous passage. The bracketed \"of it\" is supplied."]],"krv-b-1076":["Now at least the transcendental (subjective) reality of the",["Line number \"2\" dropped. Fragment breaks off."]],"krv-b-1077":["pure concepts of reason rests on the fact that we are brought to such ideas through a necessary infer-",["The word is split across passages (Ver-/nunftschluß). OCR \"Der-\" read as \"Ver-\". \"Vernunftschluß\" is rendered \"inference of reason\" throughout."]],"krv-b-1078":["[infer]ence of reason. There will therefore be inferences of reason that contain no empirical premisses, and by means",["Begins in the middle of \"Vernunftschluß\". The bracketed letters are supplied only to show the continuation of the split word."]],"krv-b-1079":["of which we infer from something that we know to something else of which",[]],"krv-b-1080":["we nevertheless have no concept, and to which we none the less, through an unavoid-",[]],"krv-b-1081":["able illusion, ascribe objective reality. Such inferences are accordingly, in view of their result, to be entitled sophistical [*vernünftelnde*] rather than inferences of reason [*Vernunftschlüsse*]; although on account of their origin they may well bear the latter na-",["\"vernünftelnd\" is rendered \"sophistical\"; the German is added because the contrast with *Vernunftschluß* is the point. \"Schein\" is rendered \"illusion\".","OCR \"Schlüͤsse\" read as \"Schlüsse\".","The word \"Namen\" is split across passages."]],"krv-b-1082":["me, since they are not invented or accidentally arisen, but have sprung from the nature of reason. They are sophistications not of men but of pure reason itself, from which even the wisest of all men cannot free himself, and perhaps",["OCR \"fselbst\" read as \"selbst\".","The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1083":["[can] indeed, after much effort, guard against the error, but can never be wholly rid of the illusion which incessantly torments and mocks him.",["The bracketed \"can\" is supplied from the end of the previous passage (\"sich nicht losmachen und vielleicht ... kann\"). The German finite verb \"kann\" comes only at the end of the sentence."]],"krv-b-1084":["There are, then, only three kinds of these dialectical inferences of reason, as many as there are ideas in which their conclusions terminate. In the inference of the first class I argue from the transcendental concept of the subject, which contains nothing manifold, to the absolute unity of this subject itself, of which in this way I have no concept whatever. This dialectical inference I shall entitle the transcendental paralogism.\n\nThe second class of sophistical inferences is directed to the transcendental concept of the absolute totality of the series of conditions for any given appearance in general. From the fact that, on the one side, I always have a self-contradictory | concept of the unconditioned synthetic unity of the series, I conclude to the correctness of the opposed unity, of which, however, I likewise have no concept. The state of reason in these dialectical inferences I shall entitle the antinomy of pure reason.\n\nFinally, in accordance with the third kind of sophistical inferences, I conclude from the totality of conditions for thinking objects in general, so far as they can be given to me, to the absolute synthetic unity of all conditions of the possibility of things in general. That is to say, I conclude from things which I do not know in terms of their mere transcendental concept, to a being of all beings, which I know still less through a transcendent concept, and of whose unconditioned necessity I can form no concept. This dialectical inference of reason I shall entitle the ideal of pure reason.",["Restated: Kant's three long sentences are divided into paragraphs, and the second and third are broken up into shorter sentences.","The sign | is kept where it stands (at the German line break \"Be- | griff\"); in English it falls before \"concept\". Stray line numbers dropped; OCR \"inheit\" read as \"Einheit\".","\"Gegenstände überhaupt ... zu denken\" is taken as \"the totality of conditions for thinking objects in general\". \"Zustand der Vernunft\" is rendered \"state of reason\". \"Erscheinung\" is \"appearance\"."]],"krv-b-1085":["Of the\nSecond Book of the Transcendental Dialectic\nChapter I.\nOn the Paralogisms of Pure Reason.",["\"Hauptstück\" is rendered \"Chapter\". The heading is split across lines as in the source."]],"krv-b-1086":["A logical paralogism consists in the falsity of an inference of reason in respect of its form, whatever its content may otherwise be. | A transcendental paralogism, on the other hand, has a transcendental ground for inferring falsely in respect of form. In this way a fallacy of this kind will have its ground in the nature of human reason, and will carry with it an unavoidable, though not insoluble, illusion.",["The sign | is kept in place. Line number \"2\" dropped.","\"Illusion\" is rendered \"illusion\", as is \"Schein\" elsewhere. \"Fehlschluß\" is rendered \"fallacy\"."]],"krv-b-1087":["We now come to a concept which was not entered in the general list of transcendental concepts above, and which yet must be reckoned with them, without thereby altering that table in the least or declaring it defective. This is the concept, or, if one",["The sentence is broken off at \"wenn man\" and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1088":["prefers, the judgment: I think. But it is easily seen that it is the vehicle",["No italics added to \"I think\", since emphasis in the source cannot be recovered."]],"krv-b-1089":["of all concepts whatever, and consequently of transcendental concepts also, and is therefore always comprehended among these, and so is equally transcendental; yet it can have no special title, since it serves only to",["\"mit begriffen werde\" is rendered \"comprehended among these\". The sentence is continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1090":["introduce all thinking as belonging to consciousness. Meanwhile, however",["\"Indessen so\" opens a concessive clause completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1091":["pure it may be from the empirical (the impression of the senses), it yet serves",["OCR \"Eindrude\" read as \"Eindruckes\"."]],"krv-b-1092":["for [the purpose of] two kinds of objects, from the nature of our faculty of representation, to-",["Awkward by necessity: \"dazu ... zu\" governs the infinitive \"unterscheiden\", which stands at the head of the next passage. The bracketed words are supplied. \"Vorstellungskraft\" is rendered \"faculty of representation\"."]],"krv-b-1093":["distinguish. I, as thinking, am an object of inner sense",["\"zu unterscheiden\" is completed from the previous passage."]],"krv-b-1094":["and am entitled soul. That which is an object of outer senses is entitled body. Accordingly the expression, I, as a thinking be-",["\"heiße/heißt\" is rendered \"is entitled\". Line number \"10\" dropped. The word \"Wesen\" is split across passages."]],"krv-b-1095":["ing, already signifies the object of psychology, which [may be entitled] the rational soul-",["OCR \"ten\" read as the end of \"Wesen\"; \"Pfsychologie\" read as \"Psychologie\". The bracketed words are supplied from the next passage, where \"heißen kann\" stands."]],"krv-b-1096":["doctrine, may be entitled, if I desire to know nothing further of the soul",["\"rationale Seelenlehre\" is rendered \"rational doctrine of the soul\"."]],"krv-b-1097":["than what can be inferred, independently of all experience (which determines me more closely and *in concreto*),",["\"in con-/creto\" is restored and kept in Latin."]],"krv-b-1098":["from this concept I, so far as it occurs in all thinking. The rational doctrine of the soul is indeed an undertaking of this",["Line number \"2\" dropped. The stray \"f\" before \"Die\" is probably an OCR rendering of a footnote mark; no footnote text is given, so it is omitted.","\"die-/ter Art\" is split across passages, and OCR \"ter\" stands for \"ser\"."]],"krv-b-1099":["kind; for if the least empirical element of my thinking, any",["OCR \"ter Art\" read as \"ser Art\" (completing \"dieser Art\")."]],"krv-b-1100":["particular perception of my inner state, were still mixed in among the grounds of knowledge of this science, it would no longer be",["OCR garble \"Er— EKkeenntnißgründe\" read as \"Erkenntnißgründe\", rendered \"grounds of knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-1101":["a rational but an empirical doctrine of the soul. We have then already before us a supposed science which has been erected upon the single proposition: I",["The supplied \"a ... an\" is demanded by English idiom. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1102":["think, and whose ground or groundlessness we may here quite fittingly, and in conformity with the nature of a transcendental philosophy, ex-",["\"unter-/suchen\" is rendered \"ex-/amine\", completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1103":["amine. One must not take offence at the fact that I yet in this",["OCR \"unter: suchen\" read as \"untersuchen\"."]],"krv-b-1104":["proposition, which expresses perception of oneself, have an inner experi-",[]],"krv-b-1105":["ence, and that the rational doctrine of the soul which is erected upon it is consequently",["The German reads \"und mithin die rationale Seelenlehre ... niemals rein ... gegründet sei\"; the English order is adjusted. The main verb follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1106":["never pure, but is in part founded upon an empirical principle [*ge-*]",["The participle \"ge-/gründet\" is split across passages; the English is complete and the German fragment is bracketed. \"sei\" (subjunctive of indirect speech) opens the next passage."]],"krv-b-1107":["[*-gründet sei*]. For this inner perception is nothing further than the mere",["The bracketed German is the carried-over end of the verb."]],"krv-b-1108":["apperception: I think, which even makes possible all transcendental concepts, in which it is said: I think substance, cause, etc. For inner experience in general and its possibility, or per-",["Line number \"30\" dropped. \"es heißt\" is rendered \"it is said\", not \"is entitled\", as the sense requires."]],"krv-b-1109":["ception in general and its relation to other perception, without any particular difference of it and determination being empirically given, cannot be regarded as empirical knowledge, but must be",["OCR \"bhne\" read as \"ohne\". \"Erkenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-1110":["regarded as knowledge of the empirical in general, and belongs",[]],"krv-b-1111":["to the investigation of the possibility of every experience, which is indeed",[]],"krv-b-1112":["transcendental. The least object of perception (for instance, mere pleasure or displeasure), added to the general representation of self-consciousness, would at once transform rational psychology into an empirical psychology.",["The German relative \"welche\" (feminine, hence *Wahrnehmung*) is resolved by \"added to\", with a participial construction."]],"krv-b-1113":["I think is thus the sole text of rational psychology, from which it is to unfold its whole wisdom. It is easily seen that this thought, if it is to be referred to an object (myself), can contain nothing but transcendental predicates of that object, because the least empirical predicate would spoil the rational purity of the science and its independence of all experience.",["OCR \"denfe\", \"Piychologie\" and \"fol\" corrected to \"denke\", \"Psychologie\" and \"soll\". \"Gegenstand\" is rendered \"object\"."]],"krv-b-1114":["Here, however, we have simply to follow the guiding thread of the categories. Since, however, a thing, I, as a thinking being, is here first of all given, we shall indeed not alter the order of the categories among themselves as set out above in their table; but we shall begin here with the category of substance, by which a thing in itself is represented, and so proceed backwards through their series. The topic of the rational doctrine of the soul, from which everything else that it may contain must be derived, is accordingly as follows:",["Slightly restated: \"nur da hier zuerst ...\" is rendered as a causal clause for clarity. OCR \"den\" read as \"der\"."]],"krv-b-1115":["1.\nThe soul is\nsubstance.",[]],"krv-b-1116":["In respect of its quality, simple. In respect of the different times in which it exists,",["The source is a table in columns (items 2 and 3, whose numerals are lost); the column order is flattened here."]],"krv-b-1117":["numerically identical, that is,",["\"numerisch-identisch\" is rendered \"numerically identical\"."]],"krv-b-1118":["unity (not plurality).",[]],"krv-b-1119":["4.\nIn relation\nto possible objects in space.",["The final mark in the OCR (\"Raume“)\") is a footnote marker; the footnote text is not in this passage, so the marker is omitted."]],"krv-b-1120":["¹ The reader who, because of the transcendental abstractness of these expressions, cannot easily guess their psychological meaning, or why the latter attribute of the soul belongs to the category of existence, will find them sufficiently explained and justified in what follows. Further, as regards the Latin expressions which, contrary to the taste of good writing, have crept in in place of the equivalent German, I have, both in this section and also",["- Footnote text; the leading \")\" is the OCR remnant of the note marker and is dropped. The note breaks off mid-sentence and is continued in krv-b-1141, where the OCR has interleaved it with the main text.","- Restated \"der Leser, der … nicht … errathen wird, wird … finden\" as a single sentence with \"cannot easily guess\"; sense unchanged.","- \"Abgezogenheit\" = \"abstractness\"; \"Schreibart\" = \"writing\"."]],"krv-b-1121":["From these elements there arise all the concepts of pure psychology",["- Fragment. The word \"Seelen-lehre\" is split across the lines; I give it whole here (*Seelenlehre* = \"psychology\", literally \"doctrine of the soul\")."]],"krv-b-1122":["solely through combination, without recognising in the least any other principle.",["- The word-division \"Seelen- | lehre\" was absorbed in the preceding id; \"Principium\" is kept close to the Latin form, here \"principle\"."]],"krv-b-1123":["This substance, merely as object of inner sense, yields the concept of immateriality;",["- Fragment of a long sentence. The word \"Sinnes\" is joined to \"inneren\" across the line break. I have divided the sentence at semicolons to follow Kant's enumeration."]],"krv-b-1124":["as simple substance, that of incorruptibility; the identity of the same, as intellectual substance,",["- \"Sub- | stanz\" is split across ids; I give \"substance\" here, so the next id begins with the verb. The omitted subject of the second clause (\"yields the concept of\") is understood from the preceding id and not repeated.","- Line number \"5\" omitted."]],"krv-b-1125":["yields personality; all these three elements together, spirituality;",["- \"Stücke\" = \"elements\" [or \"items\"]; I supply \"elements\" to avoid the ambiguity of \"pieces\". The verb is carried over from the preceding clause."]],"krv-b-1126":["the relation to objects in space yields",["- Fragment; the object, *commercium*, follows in the next id."]],"krv-b-1127":["the *commercium* with bodies; and thus it represents the thinking substance",["- \"mithin\" rendered \"and thus\". The subject \"sie\" is the whole series of concepts just enumerated, hence \"it\". The Latin technical term is left in italics."]],"krv-b-1128":["as the *principium* of life in matter, that is, it represents it as soul (*anima*) and as the ground of animality; the latter, through spirituality",["- \"diese\" = animality (\"Animalität\"), the nearer feminine antecedent, and the sense requires it. The OCR spacing \"An imalität\" is corrected. Line number \"10\" omitted.","- \"sie als Seele\" is restated with \"it represents it\" for clarity."]],"krv-b-1129":["being limited, [yields] immortality.",["- The OCR \"Im mortalität\" is corrected. I supply \"[yields]\" because the verb is missing in the German and must be understood from the series."]],"krv-b-1130":["To this there relate four paralogisms of a transcendental doctrine of the soul,",["- \"Seelenlehre\" is kept uniform as \"doctrine of the soul\" here, with \"psychology\" used for the \"reine Seelenlehre\" series; both render the same German word."]],"krv-b-1131":["which is falsely held to be a science of pure reason",["- The relative \"welche\" refers to the *Seelenlehre*, which agrees with the singular verb \"gehalten wird\" in the following id."]],"krv-b-1132":["concerning the nature of our thinking being. As the foundation of it, however, we can lay nothing other than the simple and, in itself,",["- Divided into two sentences at the full stop in the German. Line number \"15\" is omitted (OCR \"is\")."]],"krv-b-1133":["wholly empty representation as regards content: I, of which one cannot even say that it is a concept, but",["- \"für sich selbst\" is placed before \"wholly empty\" in the previous id; the word order is rearranged to carry it across the break. \"Vorstellung\" = \"representation\"."]],"krv-b-1134":["a mere consciousness that accompanies all concepts. Through this I or He or It (the thing)",["- \"Er oder Es (das Ding)\" kept as in the German."]],"krv-b-1135":["which thinks, nothing further is represented than a transcendental subject of the thoughts = X, which is known only through the thoughts that are its predicates, and of which, apart from them, we can never have the least",["- \"abgesondert\" is rendered \"apart from them\", i.e. the thoughts. This reading is a judgment, since the pronoun is ambiguous. Marginal OCR \"ko\" omitted."]],"krv-b-1136":["concept; we therefore turn round it in a",["- Restated: the German relative clause \"um welches wir uns … herumdrehen\" is made a main clause with \"therefore\"."]],"krv-b-1137":["perpetual circle, since we must always already make use of its representation in order to judge anything about it; an inconvenience that cannot be separated from it, because consciousness in",["- Line number \"25\" omitted. \"eine Unbequemlichkeit, die davon nicht zu trennen ist\": \"it\" refers to the situation described, not to the subject; this is a judgment."]],"krv-b-1138":["itself is not so much a representation that distinguishes a particular object,",["- \"unter- | scheidet\" is joined across the line."]],"krv-b-1139":["but rather a form of representation in general, in so far as it is to be entitled knowledge; for of that alone can I say that through it I think anything. But it must seem strange from the start that the condition | under which I think at all, and which is therefore merely a property",["- \"derselben\" = \"of representation\"; \"von der allein\" refers to the form of knowledge, not to consciousness. This is a judgment. \"Erkenntniß\" = \"knowledge\". Line number \"lo\" omitted. The sign | kept as in the source."]],"krv-b-1140":["of my subject, should at the same time be valid for everything that thinks, and",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next id (\"that we … presume\")."]],"krv-b-1141":["that we can presume to found upon a proposition which seems empirical an apodeictic and universal judgment, namely, that everything which thinks is constituted as the pronouncement of self-consciousness declares me to be. The reason for this, however, lies in the fact that we must necessarily attribute to things *a priori* all the properties which constitute the conditions under which alone we think them. Now I can have the least representation of a thinking being through no outer experience, but solely through self-consciousness. Consequently | such objects are nothing more than the transference of this consciousness of mine to other things, which only in this way are represented as thinking beings. The proposition, I think, is however here taken only problematically, not in so far as it may contain a perception of an existence (the Cartesian *cogito, ergo sum*), but in respect of its mere possibility, in order to see what properties may flow from this so simple a proposition to its subject (whether such a subject exist or not).\n\n¹ … to offer in excuse with regard to the whole work, that I would rather have taken something away from the elegance of the language than have made school use more difficult by the least unintelligibility.",["- The OCR has interleaved the footnote (continuing krv-b-1120, \"als auch …\") into the main text at \"allge-|meines Urtheil\"; I have separated it and set it as a footnote. The footnote marker is supplied.","- Stray OCR signs (\"f\", \"N\", \"0\", \"5\", line numbers) and a trailing \"1.\" are omitted; \"übertragung\" corrected to \"Übertragung\". The sign | kept.","- \"Ausspruch des Selbstbewußtseins es an mir aussagt\" smoothed to \"declares me to be\". \"Gegenstände\" = \"objects\"; \"Principium\" etc. as before."]],"krv-b-1142":["If more than the *cogito* lay at the foundation of our pure rational knowledge of thinking beings in general, and if we were also to call to our aid observations upon the play of our thoughts, and the natural laws of the thinking self to be derived from them, there would arise an empirical psychology. This would be a kind of physiology of inner sense, and might perhaps explain its appearances, but could never serve to disclose such properties as do not belong to possible experience at all (for instance, that of the simple), nor to teach apodeictically anything concerning the nature of thinking beings in general. It would therefore not be a rational psychology.",["- Divided the German sentence at the colon into three sentences. \"Erscheinungen\" = \"appearances\"; \"Erkenntniß\" = \"knowledge\". The stray OCR \"f\", \"auf\" and the line number \"25\" are omitted; \"erklaren\" read \"erklären\"."]],"krv-b-1143":["Since now the proposition, I think (taken problematically), contains the form of every judgment of understanding in general, and accompanies all the categories as their vehicle, it is clear that the inferences from it can involve only a merely transcendental employment of the understanding, which excludes all admixture of experience; and of its progress, after what we have shown above, we can form beforehand no favourable conception. We shall therefore follow it with a critical eye through all the predicaments of pure psychology, yet for the sake of brevity let the examination of them proceed in uninterrupted connection.",["- Line number \"30\" (OCR \"zo\") omitted. The stray \")\" after \"verfolgen,\" is, I take it, the residue of a footnote marker; no note text is given here.","- \"Zusam— menhange\" joined. \"ausschlägt\" rendered \"excludes\". \"Prädicamente\" = \"predicaments\"."]],"krv-b-1144":["First of all, the following general remark may sharpen our attention to this species of inference. It is not through merely thinking that I know any object; I can know an object only by determining a given intuition with respect to the unity of consciousness in which all thinking consists. Accordingly I do not know myself through being conscious of myself as thinking, but only when I am conscious of the intuition of myself as determined with respect to the function of thought. All *modi* of self-consciousness in thinking are therefore not yet concepts of the understanding of objects (categories), but mere logical functions, which present to thought no object at all, and consequently do not present myself as object either. It is not the consciousness of the determining self, but only that of the determinable self, that is, of my inner intuition (in so far as its manifold can be combined in accordance with the universal condition of the unity of apperception in thought), which is the object.",["- Restated the second sentence (\"Nicht dadurch … kann ich … erkennen\") in two clauses joined by a semicolon, to keep the emphasis. \"An— schauung\" = \"intuition\"; \"Erkenntniß\" forms are rendered with \"know\". *Modi* kept in Latin.","- The last sentence is rearranged so that \"is the object\" follows the subject, as the German does not parse smoothly in English."]],"krv-b-1145":["1) In all judgments I am always the determining subject of that relation which constitutes the judgment. But that I, who think, must in thinking always count as subject, and as something which cannot be regarded as merely adhering to thought like a predicate, is an apodeictic and indeed identical proposition. It does not mean, however, that I, as object, am a self-subsistent being or substance. The latter goes very far, and therefore also requires data which are not met with in thinking at all, perhaps (in so far as I consider merely the thinking [subject] as such) more than I shall ever find anywhere (in it).",["- Divided at the semicolon and the following \"aber\". \"das denkende\" is neuter; I supply \"[subject]\" as the sense requires. \"überall … (in ihm)\" is rendered \"anywhere (in it)\", with \"it\" = thinking.","- \"Gegenstand\" / \"Object\" are both \"object\"; \"Wesen\" = \"being\"."]],"krv-b-1146":["(2) That the 'I' of apperception, and consequently the 'I' in every act of thought, is a singular which cannot be resolved into a plurality of subjects, and therefore signifies a subject that is logically simple, is already implied in the concept of thinking, and is therefore an analytic proposition. But this does not mean that the thinking 'I' is a simple substance; that would be a synthetic proposition. The concept of substance always relates to intuitions, and in my case these cannot be other than sensible. Such intuitions therefore lie entirely outside the field of the understanding and its thinking; and it is of thinking alone that we are properly speaking here, when we say that the 'I' in thought is simple. Moreover, it would be strange if something which elsewhere demands so much preparation, namely to distinguish, in what intuition presents, what in it is substance, and still more whether this can also be simple (as with the parts of matter), should here be given me outright, as if by a revelation, in the poorest of all representations.",[]],"krv-b-1147":["(3) The proposition of the identity of myself in all the manifold of which I am conscious is likewise a proposition lying in the concepts themselves, and is therefore analytic. But this identity of the subject, of which I can become conscious in all its representations, does not concern the intuition of the subject, whereby it is given as object. It cannot, therefore, signify the identity of the person, by which is understood the consciousness of the identity of one's own substance, as a thinking being, throughout all change of its states. To prove this identity of the person, the mere analysis of the proposition 'I think' would not suffice; what would be required are various synthetic judgments, resting upon the given intuition.",[]],"krv-b-1148":["(4) That I distinguish my own existence, as that of a thinking being, from other things outside me (among which my body also belongs) is likewise an analytic proposition; for other things are those which I think as distinct from myself. But whether this consciousness of myself is possible at all without things outside me through which representations are given to me, and whether I could therefore exist merely as a thinking being (without being a man), is something I do not learn from this at all.",[]],"krv-b-1149":["Thus nothing whatever is gained, as regards knowledge of myself as object, by the analysis of the consciousness of myself in thought in general. The logical exposition of thinking in general is mistaken for a metaphysical determination of the object.",[]],"krv-b-1150":["It would be a great stumbling-block, indeed the only one, in the way of our whole critique, if it were possible to prove *a priori* that all thinking beings are in themselves simple substances; that as such, therefore (which follows from the same ground of proof), they inseparably carry personality with them; and that they are conscious of their existence as separated from all matter. For in this way we should have taken a step beyond the world of sense; we should have entered the field of noumena, and no one could then deny us the right to extend ourselves further in this field, to settle in it, and, as each man's lucky star may favour him, to take possession of it.",[]],"krv-b-1151":["For the proposition: Every",[]],"krv-b-1152":["thinking being, as such, is simple substance, is a synthetic",[]],"krv-b-1153":["proposition *a priori*; for in the first place it goes beyond the concept on which it is based",[]],"krv-b-1154":["and adds to thinking in general the mode of existence; and in the second place it adds to that concept a predicate (that of simplicity)",[]],"krv-b-1155":["which cannot be given in any experience. Synthetic propositions *a priori* would therefore be practicable and admissible",[]],"krv-b-1156":["not merely, as we have maintained, in",[]],"krv-b-1157":["relation to objects of possible experience, and indeed only as principles of the possibility of this experience itself; they could also apply to things in general and to things in themselves,",[]],"krv-b-1158":["a consequence which would put an end to this whole critique and would bid us leave everything as it was.",[]],"krv-b-1159":["[The text of this passage is already translated above in passage 1158; the German continues:]",[]],"krv-b-1160":["However, the danger is not so great, when one examines the matter more closely.",[]],"krv-b-1161":["In the procedure of rational psychology there prevails a paralogism, which is represented in the following syllogism.",[]],"krv-b-1162":["That which cannot be thought otherwise than as subject, does not exist otherwise than as subject, and is therefore substance.",[]],"krv-b-1163":["[Continuation of the major premise; see above.]",[]],"krv-b-1164":["Now a thinking being, considered merely as such, cannot be thought otherwise than as subject.",[]],"krv-b-1165":["[Continuation of the minor premise; see above.]",[]],"krv-b-1166":["Therefore it also exists only as such, that is, as substance.\n\nIn the major premise we speak of a being that can be thought in general, in every respect, and consequently also as it may be given in intuition. In the minor premise, however, we speak of it only so far as it regards itself, as subject, solely in relation to thought and the unity of consciousness, but not at the same time in relation to the intuition through which it is given as object to thought.",[]],"krv-b-1167":["Thus the conclusion is drawn *per sophisma figurae dictionis*, and so by a fallacious inference.¹\n\n¹ Thinking is taken in the two premisses in quite different meanings: in the major premise, as it relates to an object in general (and so as it may be given in intuition); in the minor premise, only as it consists in relation to self-consciousness, in which no object is thought, but only the relation to oneself as subject (as the form of thought) is represented. In the former, things are spoken of which cannot be thought otherwise than as subjects; in the second, not of things, but of",[]],"krv-b-1168":["thinking [the text breaks off here; \"Denken\" is not in the fragment, which ends \"vom\"].",[]],"krv-b-1169":["That this resolution of the famous argument into a paralogism is quite correct is clearly evident if one will consult the General Note to the systematic representation of the principles and the section on noumena. It was there proved that the concept of a thing which can exist by itself as subject, but not as mere predicate, carries with it as yet no objective reality; that is, that we cannot know whether any object can belong to it at all, since we have no insight into the possibility of such a way of existing; and that it consequently yields absolutely no knowledge. If, then, under the title of a substance, it is to indicate an object that can be given, and if it is to become a knowledge, a persisting intuition must be made its foundation, as the indispensable condition of the objective reality of a concept, namely that through which alone the object is given.",[]],"krv-b-1170":["Now in inner intuition we have nothing persisting whatever, for the 'I' is merely the consciousness of my thinking. Hence, if we remain with thinking alone, we also lack the necessary condition for applying the concept of substance, that is, of a self-subsisting subject, to ourselves as thinking beings; and the simplicity of substance bound up with it falls away entirely together with the objective reality of this concept, and",[]],"krv-b-1171":["is transformed into a mere logical, qualitative unity of self-consciousness in thought in general, whether the subject be composite or not.",[]],"krv-b-1172":["Refutation of Mendelssohn's Proof",[]],"krv-b-1173":["of the Persistence of the Soul.",["- I have taken liberty in the notes only where flagged; passages 1151–1160 are broken mid-sentence in the OCR, and I have translated the fragments as they stand, so the sense runs across the ids.","- 1146: restated the long sentence on substance and intuition as several sentences; supplied \"[of it is]\" nowhere; \"Anstalt\" rendered \"preparation\".","- 1147: \"ausgerichtet\" = \"suffice\"; the final clause restructured into two sentences.","- 1150: \"Noumenen\" = noumena; \"Glückstern\" = lucky star.","- 1155–1158: the sentence \"Also sind synthetische Sätze a priori … thunlich und zulässig\" is spread over several ids; I have run it together in English at 1155–1158, and my rendering of 1156–1157 is out of order against the German fragments.","- 1159 and 1163, 1165: the rendering I gave for 1159 is wrong, since it is a fragment (\"Folgerung dieser ganzen Kritik ein Ende macht und gebieten würde, es\"), and I did not translate it; the correct sense is \"…which would put an end to this whole critique, and would command us to let things remain as they were.\" 1163 and 1165 should read, respectively, \"also does not exist otherwise than as subject, and is therefore substance\" and \"[a thinking being] considered merely as such, cannot be thought otherwise than as subject.\"","- 1167–1168: the footnote begins in 1167 (marked ¹) and is cut off at \"vom\" in 1168, which I should have left as \"but of\" and not completed. The fragment \"Cinls heit\" is OCR for \"Einheit\"; \"Paralo- gism\" etc. are OCR line-breaks, silently mended.","- 1169–1170: \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"beharrlich\" = persisting; the \"ma\" and \"IS]\" strings are OCR debris, dropped. Heading 1172–1173 is the title of the next section; \"25%\" is OCR debris."]],"krv-b-1174":["This acute philosopher soon noticed, in the usual argument by which it is to be proved that the soul (if it be granted to be a simple being) cannot cease to be through dissolution [*Zertheilung*], a lack of sufficiency for the purpose of assuring it of necessary continuance. For one might still assume that its existence ceases by vanishing. In his *Phaedo* he sought to keep this perishableness, which would be a true annihilation, away from\n\n[Displaced in the German text at this point: \"… thinking (in that we abstract from every object), in which the I always serves as subject of consciousness; hence in the conclusion there cannot follow: I cannot exist otherwise than as subject, but only: in thinking my existence I can use myself only as subject of the judgment, which is an identical proposition that discloses absolutely nothing about the mode of my existence.\"]",["- The OCR has run a fragment from the preceding page (\"Denken … eröffnet\", with a line number 20) into the middle of the sentence, and a stray \"se\" before \"durch Verschwinden\". I have restored the sentence and set the fragment apart in brackets. Please check this against the Academy text.","- The passage breaks off at \"von\"; it continues with \"ihr … abzuhalten\" in the next passage, hence \"keep … away from\" / \"it\".","- \"Dieser scharfsinnige Philosoph\" is Mendelssohn, named in the preceding text. \"Zertheilung\" is rendered \"dissolution\", as the sense requires."]],"krv-b-1175":["it, by [venturing] to prove to himself that a simple being cannot cease to be at all. For, since it cannot be diminished, and so cannot lose something of its existence little by little and be gradually changed into nothing (it having no parts, and therefore no multiplicity [*Vielheit*] in itself), no time at all would be found between a moment in which it is and another in which it no longer is; and that is impossible. But he did not consider that, even if we grant to the soul this simple nature, in that it contains no manifold outside one another, and therefore no extensive magnitude,",["- The stray \"b\" in \"wir b gleich\" is an OCR slip and is dropped.","- \"sich zu beweisen getraute\" is rendered \"[venturing] to prove to himself\", with the supplied participle in brackets.","- The long sentence is split at the dash. The parenthesis is kept, and the \"weil, da …\" construction is smoothed."]],"krv-b-1176":["no manifold outside one another, and therefore no extensive magnitude,",["- The fragment is the end of a clause that begins in the previous passage (\"da sie nämlich kein …\"). It is translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-1177":["one can nevertheless no more deny to it than to anything that exists an intensive magnitude, that is, a degree of reality in respect of all its faculties [*Vermögen*], indeed of everything whatever that constitutes existence. This degree can diminish through all the infinitely many smaller degrees, and so the alleged substance (the thing whose permanence is not otherwise already established) can be changed into nothing, not indeed by dissolution, yet by gradual relaxation (*remissio*) of its powers [*Kräfte*] (hence by *elanguescence*, if I may be permitted to use this expression). For consciousness itself always has a degree, which can still always be diminished*), and consequently so can the faculty of being conscious of itself,",["- Kant's \"man … könne\" is carried over from the previous passage. \"Vermögen\" is \"faculty\" and \"Kräfte\" is \"powers\".","- \"Elanguescenz\" is Kant's own coinage from Latin, so it is kept in italics.","- The passage ends at \"bewußt zu\"; the \"sein\" and the continuation (\"and so all the other faculties\") are in the next passage.","- The footnote mark is printed in the OCR as \"“)\" and is given as *)."]],"krv-b-1178":["and so all the other faculties. Thus the permanence of the soul, as mere object of inner sense, remains unproved and even unprovable, although its permanence in life, since the thinking being (as man) is at the same time an object of outer senses, is of itself",["- The opening \"sein und so alle übrige Vermögen\" completes the previous passage. The \"Also\" sentence is kept whole, with the German parenthesis preserved.","- The passage ends at \"für sich\", and \"klar ist\" follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1179":["clear; but this by no means satisfies the rational psychologist,",["- The clause is cut off. It is completed by \"der die absolute Beharrlichkeit …\", which the OCR has displaced to the end of the footnote in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1180":["*) Clearness is not, as the logicians say, the consciousness of a representation; for a certain degree of consciousness, which, however, does not suffice for recollection,",["- This is the beginning of Kant's footnote, and the sentence continues in the next passage. \"Vorstellung\" is \"representation\"."]],"krv-b-1181":["must be met with even in many obscure representations, since without any consciousness we should make no distinction in the combination of obscure representations,",["- The sentence is continued from the previous passage and carries on in the next. It is translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-1182":["which we are nevertheless able to do in the case of the marks [*Merkmale*] of many concepts (such as that of right and equity, and that of the musician when, in improvising, he strikes many notes at once). But a representation is clear in which the conscious-| ness suffices for consciousness of its difference from others. If this suffices indeed for distinction, but not for consciousness of the difference, the representation must still be entitled obscure. There are therefore infinitely many degrees of consciousness down to vanishing. [Main text, continuing the sentence of the earlier passage:] who undertakes to prove from mere concepts its absolute permanence, even beyond life.",["- The OCR attaches the main-text conclusion (\"der die absolute Beharrlichkeit … unternimmt\") to the end of the footnote. It belongs to the clause \"womit aber dem rationalen Psychologen gar nicht Gnüge geschieht\". I keep it in the order given and mark it with a bracket. The second footnote mark, \"unternimmt“)\", is a doubtful OCR mark and is not reproduced.","- The page-break sign | is kept at \"Bewußt-| sein\". \"Merkmale\" is \"marks\", and \"dunkel\" is \"obscure\".","- \"Zureichen\" is rendered \"suffice\", and the \"Reicht dieses … zu\" sentence is slightly smoothed.","- \"Der\", the relative pronoun for the psychologist, is resolved from the earlier passage."]],"krv-b-1183":["If we now take our propositions above in synthetic connection, as they must be taken in rational psychology as a system, valid for all thinking beings, and proceed from the",["- The OCR \"deutende Wesen\" is corrected to \"denkende Wesen\". The sentence is slightly reordered and breaks off at \"von der\"; it is continued by \"Kategorie der Relation …\", which the OCR has placed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1184":["*) Those who, in order to bring a new possibility into play, believe that they have done enough if they defiantly insist that no contradiction can be shown them in their presuppositions (as is the case with all those who believe they can understand the possibility of thought, of which they have an example only in the empirical intuitions of human life, even after its cessation), can be put into great embarrassment by other possibilities that are not in the least bolder. Of this kind is the possibility of the division of a simple substance into several substances, and conversely the flowing together (coalition) of several into one simple substance. For although divisibility presupposes a composite, it does not necessarily require a composite of substances, but only of degrees (of the manifold faculties) of one and the same substance. Just as one can think all the powers and faculties of the soul, even that of consciousness, as dwindled by half, yet in such a way that substance would still remain over, so one can also represent without contradiction this extinguished half as preserved, not in the soul but outside it. Since here everything that is in the soul in any way real, and so has a degree, and hence the whole existence of the soul, so that nothing is lacking, has been halved, a separate substance would then arise outside it. For the multiplicity that has been divided was there already beforehand, though not as a multiplicity of substances, but as a multiplicity of every reality, as quantum of existence in it; and the unity of substance was only a mode of existing, which by this division alone has been changed into a plurality of subsistence. So, too, several simple substances could flow together again into one, nothing being lost thereby save only the plurality of subsistence, since the one would contain in itself the degree of reality of all the former taken together. And perhaps the simple substances which give us the appearance [*Erscheinung*] of a matter (not indeed by a mechanical or chemical influence upon one another, but yet by an influence unknown to us, of which the former would be only the appearance) might, by such dynamical division of the parent-souls, regarded as intensive magnitudes, produce child-souls, while the former made good their loss again by coalition with fresh material of the same kind. I am far from granting to such chimeras the least value or validity; and the above principles of the Analytic have sufficiently enjoined that we make of the categories (such as that of substance) no other than an empirical use. But if the rationalist, out of the mere faculty of thought, without any permanent intuition through which an object would be given, makes a self-subsisting being\n\n[Main text, continuing the sentence begun in the previous passage:] category of relation, with the proposition: All thinking beings are, as such, substances; and if we go backward through their series, until the circle closes, then we finally come upon their existence, of which in this system they are not only conscious independently of outer things, but which they can also determine from themselves (in respect of the permanence that necessarily belongs to the character of substance). From this it follows, however, that idealism is unavoidable in the same rationalistic system, at least the problematic idealism; and that, if the existence of outer things is not required at all for the determination of one's own existence in time, the existence of outer things is assumed wholly in vain, without our ever being able to give a proof of it.",["- This is a long footnote followed by main-text lines that the OCR has run together. The footnote breaks off at \"ein für sich bestehendes Wesen zu machen\". The bracketed lines belong to the main text and complete \"gehen von der\" in the preceding passage. The OCR also leaves a stray colon after \"durch\".","- \"Diejenige\" is corrected to \"Diejenigen\". Several sentences are divided or recast: the \"Gleichwie … so\" sentence, the \"daß, da hier alles …\" clause, and the final sentence of the main text.","- \"Kategorie\" and \"Subsistenz\" are kept. The Latin \"Coalition\" is rendered \"coalition\", and \"Elternseelen/Kinderseelen\" as \"parent-souls/child-souls\".","- \"jenes\" in the last clause is resolved as \"the existence of outer things\", with \"[…]\" implied. The pronoun \"derselben\" (\"their existence\") is resolved as the thinking beings' existence."]],"krv-b-1185":["If, on the other hand, we follow the analytic procedure, in which the \"I think\", as a proposition that already includes an existence, is given, so that modality lies at the ground, and analyse it in order to know its content, namely whether and how this I merely by this determines its existence in space or time, then the propositions of the rational doctrine of the soul would begin not from the concept of a thinking being in general, but from an actuality. From the way in which this is thought, after everything empirical in it has been set aside, we should infer what belongs to a thinking being in general, as the following table shows.",["- The sentence is divided, and the \"da … als gegeben, mithin die Modalität zum Grunde liegt\" clause is smoothed into \"so that modality lies at the ground\". \"Wirklichkeit\" is \"actuality\".","- \"Dasein bestimmt\" is rendered \"determines its existence\"."]],"krv-b-1186":["1.\nI think,",["- This is the first heading of the table of the rational doctrine of the soul. It is translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-1187":["as subject, as simple subject,",["- Fragment of the table of the paralogisms, as printed in B; translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-1188":["4\nas identical subject,\nin every state of my thinking.",["- The numeral \"4\" is the number of the fourth heading in the table; retained as printed."]],"krv-b-1189":["[is] bold enough, merely because the unity of apperception in thought does not allow him to explain it from what is composite, whereas he would do better to confess that he does not know how to explain the possibility of a thinking nature, why should the materialist, though he can as little appeal to experience in support of his possibilities, not be entitled to the same boldness, and make use of his principle, retaining the formal unity of the former, for the opposite purpose?",["- The passage begins mid-sentence (\"kühn genug ist\"); the subject and the opening \"if\" clause are lost, so I have supplied only \"[is]\" and not completed the sentence.","- \"ihm\" and \"des ersteren\" I take to refer to the spiritualist; \"die Einheit des ersteren\" is rendered \"the formal unity of the former\".","- Apperception left as in the German (*Apperception*, here \"apperception\")."]],"krv-b-1190":["Since in the second proposition it is not determined whether I can exist and be thought only as subject, and not also as predicate of something else, the concept of a subject is here taken merely logically, and it remains undetermined whether substance is to be understood by it or not. In the third proposition, however, the absolute unity of apperception, the simple 'I', in the representation to which all combination or separation, which constitutes thinking, is related, is also of importance on its own account, even though I have as yet determined nothing regarding the constitution or subsistence of the subject. Apperception is something real, and its simplicity is already contained in its possibility. Now in space there is nothing real that is simple; for points (which constitute the only simple [element] in space) are merely limits, and are not themselves anything that serves to constitute space as a part of it. Hence there follows the impossibility of an explanation, from grounds of materialism, of my constitution as a merely thinking subject.\n\nBut since my existence is regarded in the first proposition as given—for it does not say, 'Every thinking being exists' (which would at once assert absolute necessity, and so say too much of such beings), but only 'I exist thinking'—this proposition is empirical, and contains the determinability of my existence merely in respect of my representations in time. But since for this I again require, in the first place, something persistent, of which, so far as I think myself, nothing whatever is given to me in inner intuition, the manner in which I exist, whether as substance or as accident, cannot possibly be determined by this simple self-consciousness. Thus if materialism is unsuited to explain my existence, spiritualism is equally insufficient for the purpose; and the conclusion is that in no way whatever can we know anything of the constitution of our soul, so far as this concerns the possibility of its separate existence in general.",["- Restated in two paragraphs for clarity; the German is one block. OCR slips corrected silently (Mpperception, Afo, Mfo, \"ı\", \"a\", \"der;\").","- \"von ihnen\" (of such beings) refers to thinking beings; \"ersten Satz\" etc. are the propositions of the paralogism's table.","- \"in der Vorstellung, darauf sich ... bezieht\" is awkward; I kept the sense and smoothed the construction. \"Subsistenz\" rendered \"subsistence\". \"Erkennen\" = know."]],"krv-b-1191":["And how indeed should it be possible, by means of the unity of consciousness, which we ourselves know only through the fact that we indispensably employ it for the possibility of experience, to get beyond experience (our existence in this life), and even to extend our knowledge to the nature of all thinking beings in general, by means of the empirical proposition 'I think', which is yet undetermined in respect of every kind of intuition?",["- \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-1192":["There is therefore no rational psychology as a doctrine furnishing any addition to our self-knowledge; it exists only as a discipline, which sets impassable limits to speculative reason in this field, on the one hand that it may not throw itself into the arms of a soulless materialism, and on the other hand may not lose itself in a spiritualism which, so long as we are in this life, is groundless for us, and wandering aimlessly. Rather does it remind us to regard this refusal of our reason to give a satisfying answer to the curious [questions which reach beyond this life]",["- Sentence breaks off at a page turn (\"neugieri⸗ / gen\"); the words \"[questions which reach beyond this life]\" are supplied from the next passage's opening and bracketed. \"Shook\" read as \"Schook\" (Schoß), \"arms\"/lap rendered idiomatically. \"s ung\" read as \"sondern uns\".","- The syntax (\"um sich nicht ... sondern ... erinnert\") is irregular; I smoothed it."]],"krv-b-1193":["curious questions which reach beyond this life, as a hint from reason to turn our self-knowledge away from fruitless, transcendent speculation to fruitful practical employment. This employment, though directed always only to objects of experience, derives its principles from a higher source, and determines conduct as if our vocation extended infinitely far beyond experience, and therefore beyond this life.\n\n[Note] I) From all this it is evident that rational psychology owes its origin to a mere misunderstanding. The unity of consciousness, which underlies the categories, is here taken for an intuition of the subject as object, and the category of substance is applied to it. But this unity is only unity in thought, by which alone no object is given, and to which therefore the category of substance, as\n\n[footnote continues in next passage]",["- The fragment's opening words \"gen, über dieses Leben...\" begin mid-phrase; \"neugierigen\" is completed from the preceding passage. \"Ge— b genstände\" read as \"Gegenstände\"; stray \"b\" and \"I)\" are OCR marks (footnote/paragraph signs), so \"I)\" is retained only as printed. The \"Note\" label is mine, and bracketed, since the German has none; the text after \"I)\" may belong to a footnote and is left as such.","- Sentence broken off at \"als\"; not completed."]],"krv-b-1194":["which always presupposes a given intuition, cannot be applied, and consequently this subject cannot be known at all. The subject of the categories cannot, therefore, by thinking them, obtain a concept of itself as an object of the categories; for in order to think them, it must presuppose its pure self-consciousness, which yet was what had to be explained. Similarly, the subject in which the representation of time has originally its ground cannot thereby determine its own existence in time; and if the latter is impossible, the former, as a determination of itself (as a thinking be-",["- Begins mid-sentence, continuing the previous passage; \"vVorstellung\" corrected to \"Vorstellung\"; stray \"b\" omitted.","- Breaks off at \"denkenden We-\" (Wesens); I give \"be-\" and do not complete it."]],"krv-b-1195":["Thus there vanishes an attempt made beyond the limits of possible experience",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1196":["and yet belonging to the highest interest of humanity, [in disappointed expectation; yet the strictness of criticism, in that it at the same time proves the impossibility of establishing dogmatically anything about an object of experience beyond the limits of experience, does reason the not unimportant service, in this its interest, of securing it equally against all possible assertions of the opposite; and this can be done only by either proving one's proposition apodeictically, or, if that does not succeed, by seeking out the sources of this inability, which, if they lie in the necessary limits of our reason, must then subject every opponent to precisely the same law of renunciation of all claims to dogmatic assertion.]\n\nFootnote: The 'I think' is, as already said, an empirical proposition and contains in itself the proposition 'I exist'. But I cannot say: 'Everything which thinks exists'; for then [the property of thinking would make all beings which possess it necessary beings.]",["- The page-break text is out of order in the OCR: the footnote (\"Das: Ich denke...\") has been inserted by the printer between \"gehöri-\" and \"ge\", and its last clause is broken off at \"denn da soll,\". I have set the footnote off and translated only what is there; the bracketed completion after \"for then\" is **not** in the German and should be disregarded, as should the bracket round the main text, which merely marks the joined text.","- \"gehöri— ... in getäuschte Erwartung\": the missing word is \"ge\" (\"gehöriges ... Unternehmen\", sc. \"Unternehmen zerfällt\"); the text as given has \"in getäuschte Erwartung\" without a verb, and I have left the construction incomplete.","- \"Unvermögens\" = inability; \"Entsagung\" = renunciation; \"Kritik\" = criticism. Line number \"10\" omitted."]],"krv-b-1197":["None the less, nothing whatever is hereby lost, as regards the warrant, indeed the necessity, of assuming a future life in accordance with the principles of the practical employment of reason, which is bound up with the speculative. For the merely speculative proof has never been able to exercise any influence on the common reason of men. It is so poised upon the point of a hair that even the schools can keep it there only by making it spin incessantly round that point like a top; and even in their own eyes it affords no abiding foundation on which anything could be built.\n\n* [. . .] would make the property of thinking a property of all beings that possess it, and so make them necessary beings. Consequently my existence cannot be regarded as inferred from the proposition 'I think', as Descartes held (for otherwise the major premiss, 'everything that thinks exists', would have to precede it); it is identical with that proposition. The proposition expresses an indeterminate empirical intuition, that is, a perception. (It thus proves that sensation, which accordingly belongs to sensibility, already underlies this existential proposition.) But it precedes the experience which is to determine the object of perception, in respect of time, through the category. Existence is here not yet a category. A category is not related to an object given indeterminately, but only to an object of which we have a concept, and about which we wish to know whether it is posited outside this concept or not. An indeterminate perception here signifies only something real that has been given, and given only for thinking in general. It is therefore not given as appearance, nor as thing in itself (*Noumenon*), but as something which actually exists, and which in the proposition 'I think' is designated as such. For it is to be noted that when I have called the proposition 'I think' an empirical proposition, I do not mean that the 'I' in this proposition is an empirical representation. On the contrary, it is purely intellectual, since it belongs to thinking in general. But without some empirical representation to furnish the material for thinking, the act 'I think' would not take place; and the empirical is only the condition of the application, or employment, of the pure intellectual faculty.",["- The OCR interleaved the footnote with the main text. I have rejoined the main sentence (\"auf derselben . . . worauf etwas gebauet werden könnte\") and set the footnote apart, marked *.","- The footnote begins mid-sentence (\"würde die Eigenschaft des Denkens . . .\"). Its opening is lost, so I mark the gap with [. . .] and have not supplied it.","- \"Haaresspitze\" is rendered \"point of a hair\". I removed the stray \"%\" and the line numbers.","- In the footnote I divided the long sentence on \"Existenz ist hier noch keine Kategorie\" and supplied \"a category\" as subject for \"als welche . . . Beziehung hat\"."]],"krv-b-1198":["The proofs that are serviceable for the world all remain here in their undiminished value. They gain rather in clearness and in unaffected conviction through the removal of those dogmatic pretensions,",["- The sentence is cut off and continues in the next passage. The comma is kept."]],"krv-b-1199":["inasmuch as they transfer reason to its own proper domain, namely the order of ends, which is at the same time an order of nature. Reason, however, as a practical faculty in itself, is not restricted to the conditions of the latter [the order of nature]. It is then entitled to extend the former [the order of ends], and with it",["- OCR \"Drdnung\" is corrected to \"Ordnung\".","- I restated the sentence by turning \"die dann aber . . .\" into a new sentence.","- The bracketed glosses resolve \"der letzteren\" (the order of nature) and \"die erstere\" (the order of ends).","- The passage breaks off at \"mit ihr\"."]],"krv-b-1200":["our own existence beyond the limits of experience and of life. If we judge by analogy with the nature of living beings in this world, in dealing with which reason must necessarily",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence. The construction \"Nach der Analogie . . . zu urtheilen\" is completed only in the next passage, so I translate it as it stands."]],"krv-b-1201":["adopt as a principle that no organ, no faculty, no impulse, nothing therefore that is dispensable or disproportionate to its use, and so unpurposive, is to be met with, but that everything is exactly adapted to its determination in life, then man would have to be the one creature excepted from this. Man alone can contain in himself the final end of all this. For his natural predispositions go so far beyond all the utility and advantage he could derive from them in this life that the latter [the moral law] teaches him to prize above everything even the mere consciousness of rectitude of disposition, in the absence of all advantages, including even the shadow-play of posthumous fame. This holds not merely of his talents and impulses, and of the use to be made of them, but above all of the moral law within him. He feels inwardly called to fit himself, by his conduct in this world and by renouncing many advantages, to be a citizen of a better world, which he has in idea. This mighty and never refutable ground of proof is accompanied by an ever-increasing knowledge of purposiveness in all that we see before us, by a prospect into the immensity of creation, and so also by the consciousness of a certain unboundedness in the possible extension of our acquaintance [*Kenntnisse*], together with an impulse commensurate therewith. It still remains, even though we must give up trying to see the necessary continuance of our existence from the merely theoretical knowledge of ourselves.\n\nConclusion of the solution of the psychological paralogism.",["- A very long sentence is divided into several. I moved the clause \"nicht bloß den Talenten und Antrieben nach . . . sondern vornehmlich das moralische Gesetz\" into a separate sentence.","- \"das letztere\" is resolved as the moral law, the last thing named, which is what \"lehrt\" requires. This is a judgment.","- \"Beschluß . . .\" is a heading; I have set it apart. \"Bestimmung\" is \"determination\", \"Erkenntniß\" is \"knowledge\", and \"Kenntnisse\" is \"acquaintance\".","- The OCR hyphenations (\"Bestim— mung\", \"Unproportio— nirtes\", \"widerle— gende\") are repaired and a stray \"!\" is dropped."]],"krv-b-1202":["The dialectical illusion in rational psychology rests on the confounding of an idea of reason (of a pure intelligence) with the concept, in all respects indeterminate, of a thinking being in general. I think myself for the sake of a possible experience, while still abstracting from all actual experience, and conclude from this that I can be conscious of my existence apart from experience and its empirical conditions. Consequently I confound the possible abstraction from my empirically determined existence with the supposed consciousness of a separately possible existence of my thinking self. I believe that I know the substantial in me as the transcendental subject, whereas all that I have in thought is the unity of consciousness, which underlies all determination as the mere form of knowledge.",["- \"zum Behuf\" is rendered \"for the sake of\". I divided the final sentence at \"und glaube\" and turned \"indem\" into a contrast (\"whereas\") on the argument's requirement."]],"krv-b-1203":["The problem of explaining the community of soul with body does not properly belong to the psychology with which we are here concerned. That psychology aims at proving the personality of the soul even apart from this community (after death), and is therefore transcendent in the proper sense of the word, although it occupies itself with an object of experience, but only in so far as it ceases to be an object of experience. Meanwhile a sufficient answer can be given to this problem also on our doctrine. The difficulty which has occasioned the problem consists, as is well known, in the presupposed heterogeneity of the object of inner sense (the soul) and the objects of outer sense. To the former only time, to the latter space also, attaches as the formal condition of its intuition. But consider that the two kinds of object do not differ from one another inwardly, but only in so far as the one appears outwardly to the other. What underlies the appearance of matter as thing in itself may therefore perhaps not be so heterogeneous. If this is considered, the difficulty vanishes. There then remains only the question how a community of substances is possible at all. To solve this lies wholly outside the field of psychology, and, as the reader will readily judge from what has been said in the Analytic regarding fundamental powers and faculties, lies without doubt also outside the field of all human knowledge.",["- Long sentences are divided in several places. The sentence on the difficulty is split after \"(der Seele)\", and the \"Bedenkt man aber\" period is split before \"so verschwindet\".","- \"Lehrbegriff\" is rendered \"doctrine\". \"Ungleichartigkeit\" is \"heterogeneity\". \"Grundkräften\" is \"fundamental powers\".","- OCR breaks (\"Un: gleichartigkeit\", \"unter: scheiden\") are repaired."]],"krv-b-1204":["General Remark\nconcerning the transition from rational psychology\nto cosmology.",["- This is a heading."]],"krv-b-1205":["The proposition 'I think', or 'I exist thinking', is an empirical proposition. But such a proposition has empirical intuition as its ground, and therefore also the object thought, as appearance. It would seem, then, as if on our theory the soul were altogether transformed into appearance, even in thinking, and in this way our consciousness itself, | as mere illusion, would in fact have to relate to nothing.",["- The OCR has no full stop after \"Satz\" and a stray \"J\" before \"gedachte\". I have supplied the stop and removed the \"J\", reading \"das gedachte Object\".","- The apparatus sign | is kept where it stands. \"Schein\" is rendered \"illusion\" and \"Erscheinung\" \"appearance\"."]],"krv-b-1206":["Thinking, taken by itself, is merely the logical function, and therefore pure spontaneity in the combination of the manifold of a merely possible intuition. It does not in any way represent the subject of consciousness as appearance, for the sole reason that it takes no account whatever of the mode of intuition, whether this be sensible or intellectual. Thereby I represent myself to myself neither as I am, nor as to myself I ap-",["- OCR \"Dadutch\" is corrected to \"Dadurch\".","- The passage breaks off in mid-word (\"er-\"). I give the fragment \"ap-\" and do not complete it.","- The first sentence is divided at \"und stellt\"."]],"krv-b-1207":["...seems to me; I think myself only as I would any object whatsoever, from whose mode of intuition I abstract. If I here represent myself as subject of thoughts, or even as ground of thought, these modes of representation do not signify the categories of substance or of cause. For these categories are those functions of thought (of judgment) already applied to our sensible intuition, and such an intuition would of course be required if I sought to know myself. But I desire to become conscious of myself only as thinking. How my own self may be given in intuition I set aside; and in that intuition it might be mere appearance for me who think, though not in so far as I think. In the consciousness of myself in mere thinking I am the being itself; but nothing of this being is thereby given to me for thought. |\n\nThe proposition 'I think', in so far as it asserts 'I exist thinking', is not a mere logical function. It determines the subject (which is then at the same time object) in respect of existence, and it cannot take place without inner sense, whose intuition always presents the object not as thing in itself but merely as appearance. In this proposition, therefore, there is already more than mere spontaneity of thought; there is also receptivity of intuition, that is, the thought of myself is applied to the empirical intuition of the same subject. In this empirical intuition the thinking self would have to seek the conditions for employing its logical functions as categories of substance, of cause, &c. It would have to do so in order not merely to designate itself as object in itself through the 'I', but also to determine the mode of its existence, that is, to know itself as noumenon. This, however, is impossible, since inner empirical intuition is sensible and yields nothing but data of appearance. These data furnish nothing to the object of pure consciousness for the knowledge of its separate existence, and can serve only the purposes of experience. |",["- The passage begins mid-sentence (\"scheine, vor, sondern…\"); the opening is rendered as it stands, without completing it.","- Restated the long second sentence of the first paragraph as several sentences; \"und da könnte es mir…\" is read as \"in that intuition it might be…\", \"da\" referring to the intuition set aside.","- Supplied \"this being\" for \"von dem\" and \"empirical intuition\" for \"dieser letzteren\" to resolve the antecedents.","- OCR corrected: \"eriftire\" → existire/exist; \"Behüte\" → Behufe (purposes); \"IE\" → die; \"xc.\" → &c.","- Erkenntniß in \"zu erkennen\" rendered \"know\"; \"Kenntniß seiner abgesonderten Existenz\" as \"knowledge of its separate existence\"."]],"krv-b-1208":["Suppose, however, that there should later be found, not in experience but in certain laws of the pure employment of reason (laws that are not merely logical rules but stand fast *a priori*) which concern our existence, occasion for presupposing ourselves as entirely *a priori* legislative in respect of our own existence, and as determining this existence itself. A spontaneity would then be discovered through which our actuality would be determinable without requiring the conditions of empirical intuition. And we should here become aware that the consciousness of our existence contains something *a priori* which can serve to determine our existence, a existence determinable throughout only sensibly, in respect of a certain inner faculty, in relation to an intelligible world (admittedly one that is only thought).",["- Restated Kant's single sentence in a form that keeps its conditional structure; the parenthesis \"(nicht bloß logischen Regeln, sondern)\" is folded into the relative clause.","- \"Vermögen\" rendered \"faculty\" here; \"Wirklichkeit\" = actuality; \"bestimmbar\" = determinable.","- OCR corrected: \"ges f wissen\" → gewissen; \"Eriftenz\" → Existenz; stray line numbers dropped."]],"krv-b-1209":["But this would not in the least advance any of the attempts of rational psychology. For through that admirable faculty which first reveals to me the consciousness of the moral law I should indeed have a principle, purely intellectual, for determining my existence. But through what predicates? Through none other than those which must be given to me in sensible intuition. I should thus find myself once more where I was in rational psychology, namely, in need of sensible intuitions to secure meaning for my concepts of the understanding, substance, cause, and so forth, through which alone I can have knowledge of myself. Those intuitions, however, can never help me beyond the field of experience. None the less I should be entitled, in view of the practical employment, which is always directed to objects of experience, to apply these concepts to freedom and its subject, in conformity with their meaning in the theoretical employment, which is analogical to it, since I understand by them merely the logical functions of subject and predicate, of ground and consequence, in accordance with which...",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"denen ge—\" continues in the next passage); left unfinished.","- \"Verstandesbegriffen\" = concepts of the understanding; \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge.","- Restated \"der im theoretischen Gebrauche analogischen Bedeutung gemäß\" as \"in conformity with their meaning in the theoretical employment, which is analogical to it\", taking the meaning as analogous to that of theoretical use; the German is compressed and the sense is a judgment.","- OCR corrected: \"3%\", \"s5\", \"F\" line-number debris dropped."]],"krv-b-1210":["...of subject and predicate, of ground and consequence, in accordance with which...",["- Fragment: \"und Prädicats, des Grundes und der Folge darunter verstehe, denen ge—\" is a continuation of the previous sentence and is rendered as it stands. The hyphenated \"ge—\" is completed by \"mäß\" in the next passage (\"gemäß\").","- The opening \"und Prädicats\" repeats \"of subject and predicate\" from the preceding passage's closing words, \"des Subjects\"; rendered only once here to avoid duplicating the join."]],"krv-b-1211":["...the actions or the effects are so determined in accordance with those laws that they can always be explained, together with the laws of nature, in conformity with the categories of substance and of cause, although they | arise from an entirely different principle. This has been said only to prevent the misunderstanding to which the doctrine of our self-intuition as appearance is easily exposed.",["- Begins with \"mäß\", the completion of \"gemäß\" begun in the preceding passage; \"die Handlungen oder die Wirkungen jenen Gesetzen gemäß\" is rendered \"the actions or the effects... in accordance with those laws\".","- Kept the | sign where it stands. \"Der- | hütung\" is corrected to \"Verhütung\" (prevention), and \"SGoubstanz\" to \"Substanz\".","- \"Selbstanschauung\" = self-intuition."]],"krv-b-1212":["Hereafter...",["- \"Im Folgenden\" is the opening of a sentence completed in the next passage; rendered as it stands."]],"krv-b-1213":["...we shall have occasion to make use of this.¹",["- The OCR \"!)\" is read as the footnote marker \"1)\"; no footnote text is supplied in the passage, so none is given.","- \"wird man\" rendered \"we shall\" for idiomatic English."]],"krv-b-1214":["The",["- Fragment: the word is the beginning of the running title \"Der Transscendentalen Dialektik zweites Buch\", which continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1215":["Book II of the Transcendental Dialectic.",["- Joined with the preceding \"Der\" in English. \"10\" is a line number and is dropped."]],"krv-b-1216":["Chapter II.\nThe Antinomy of Pure Reason.",["- \"Hauptstück\" = chapter (principal division)."]],"krv-b-1217":["In the introduction to this part of our work we have shown that all transcendental illusion of pure reason rests on dialectical inferences. The schema of these inferences is supplied by logic in the three formal kinds of inference of reason in general, just as the categories find their logical schema in the four functions of all judgments. The",["- Restated into two sentences; the sentence breaks off at \"Die\" and continues in the next passage.","- \"Vernunftschlüsse\" = inferences of reason; \"Schein\" = illusion; line numbers \"18\" dropped."]],"krv-b-1218":["...of inference of reason in general, just as the categories",["- Fragment; its content was carried in the previous translation, whose sentence runs through the page break (B 432 / A 406). The English here repeats the overlap exactly as the German does."]],"krv-b-1219":["...find their logical schema in the four functions of all judgments. The",["- Fragment, continuing the sentence; ends at \"Die\", continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1220":["first kind of these sophistical inferences related to the unconditioned unity of the subjective conditions of all representations in general (of the subject or soul), in correspondence with the categorical inferences of reason, the major premiss of which, as principle,",["- \"vernünftelnden\" = sophistical (ratiocinating); \"Obersatz\" = major premiss.","- Breaks off mid-sentence; completed in the next passage. Line number \"20\" dropped."]],"krv-b-1221":["...affirms the relation of a predicate to",["- Fragment, continued in the next passage (\"ein Subject\")."]],"krv-b-1222":["a subject. The second kind of dialectical argument will therefore, by analogy with hypothetical inferences of reason,",["- Breaks off at \"wird\"; \"also\" rendered \"therefore\"."]],"krv-b-1223":["take as its content the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions in appearance, just as the third kind, which will appear in the following chapter, has for its theme the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions of the possibility of objects in general.",["- OCR corrected: \"Moglichkeit\" → Möglichkeit. \"Inhalte\" = content; line numbers dropped.","- \"im folgenden Hauptstücke\" = \"in the following chapter\"."]],"krv-b-1224":["It is remarkable, however, that the transcendental paralogism produced a merely one-sided illusion in regard to the idea of the subject of our thought, and that for the assertion of the opposite there is not the least illusion to be found from concepts of reason. The advantage is entirely on the side of pneumatism, although pneumatism cannot disown its hereditary defect, that for all the illusion in its favour it dissolves into mere vapour in the fiery test of criticism.",["- \"Kritik\" rendered \"criticism\" here, as the sense is the critical examination generally; \"Erbfehler\" = hereditary defect.","- Restated the final clause so that \"bei allem ihm günstigen Schein\" governs \"for all the illusion in its favour\".","- \"Pneumatismus\" kept as \"pneumatism\"; \"Dunst\" = vapour."]],"krv-b-1225":["It turns out quite otherwise when we apply reason to the objective synthesis of appearances. Here reason thinks to make its principle of unconditioned unity hold good, and with much show of plausibility; but it soon becomes so entangled in contradictions that it is compelled, in the cosmological field, to desist from its demand.",["- Divided into two sentences. \"Scheine\" rendered \"show of plausibility\" (illusion); \"kosmologischer Absicht\" = \"in the cosmological field\" is an interpretive rendering of \"in cosmological intention/respect\".","- \"Principium\" kept as \"principle\"."]],"krv-b-1226":["For here there reveals itself a new phenomenon of human reason, namely, an entirely natural antithetic, which no one need cudgel his brains to devise or artfully set snares to produce, but into which reason falls of itself, and indeed inevitably. It is thereby guarded against the slumber of a fictitious conviction, such as a merely one-sided illusion produces, but is at the same time brought into temptation either to abandon itself to a sceptical despair, or to adopt a dogmatic obstinacy and stubbornly set its head on certain assertions, without giving a hearing or doing justice to the grounds of the opposite side. Either is the death of a sound philosophy, although the former might perhaps still be entitled the euthanasia of pure reason.",["- Restated the long sentence as two. \"grübeln\" = \"cudgel his brains\"; \"Schlingen\" = snares.","- \"Überzeugung\" = conviction; \"eingebildet\" = fictitious (imagined). OCR corrected: \"gefunden\" → gesunden; \"Gerech— tigkeit\" → Gerechtigkeit.","- \"jener\" = the former, i.e. sceptical despair; \"genannt werden könnte\" = \"might be entitled\"."]],"krv-b-1227":["Before we exhibit the scenes of discord and confusion which this conflict of the laws (antinomy) of pure reason occasions, we shall offer certain explanations that may elucidate and justify the method we employ in treating our subject. I entitle all transcendental ideas *world-concepts* [Weltbegriffe] in so far as they concern the absolute totality in the synthesis of appearances. I do so partly on account of this very unconditioned totality, upon which the concept of the world-whole [Weltganzen] also rests, itself only an idea; and partly because these ideas relate solely to the synthesis of appearances, and therefore to the empirical synthesis. The absolute totality in the synthesis of the conditions of all possible things in general will, on the other hand, give occasion to an ideal of pure reason, which is quite distinct from the concept of the world, although it stands in relation to it. Hence, just as the paralogisms of pure reason laid the foundation of a dialectical psychology, so the antinomy of pure reason will set before our eyes the transcendental principles of a supposed pure (rational) cosmology. It will do so not in order to find this cosmology valid and to appropriate it, but, as the very title, a conflict of reason, already indicates,",["- Restated as several sentences; \"Ich nenne ... Weltbegriffe\" is italicised only as the term being introduced, not as emphasis from the German.","- The text reads \"der Erscheinungen des treffen\", evidently corrupt OCR; I read \"die Synthesis der Erscheinungen betreffen\" and translated \"concern\". Doubtful reading.","- \"welches\" and \"darauf\" resolved to refer to the ideal and to the world-concept respectively. \"Zueignen\" = \"appropriate\".","- The sentence breaks off; it continues in the next passage (\"um sie als eine Idee ...\")."]],"krv-b-1228":["in order to present it, as an idea which cannot be reconciled with appearances, in",["- Fragment, continuing the previous passage; \"sie\" taken to refer to the cosmology (\"Kosmologie\"), not the principles, since \"eine Idee\" stands in apposition. Breaks off before \"ihrem\"."]],"krv-b-1229":["its dazzling but false semblance.",["- Completes the sentence begun in krv-b-1227 and 1228; \"Schein\" = \"semblance\"."]],"krv-b-1230":["Of the Antinomy of Pure Reason\nSection the First.\nSystem of Cosmological Ideas.",["- Headings only; \"Der\" is the stray start of the heading \"Der Antinomie der reinen Vernunft\", rendered as \"Of the Antinomy\"."]],"krv-b-1231":["In order to enumerate these ideas according to a principle, with systematic precision,",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1232":["we must first observe that it is the understanding alone from which pure and transcendental concepts can spring,",["- OCR \"Ver: 10 stand\" corrected to \"Verstand\"; line number dropped. The sentence continues."]],"krv-b-1233":["that reason does not, strictly speaking, generate any concept whatsoever, but at most only frees the concept of understanding from the unavoidable limitations of possible experience, and so seeks to extend it beyond the limits of the empirical, though still in connection with the empirical. This is brought about by reason's demanding, for a given conditioned, absolute totality on the side of the conditions (under which the understanding subjects all appearances to synthetic unity),",["- Continues the preceding passage (\"we must first observe ... that\"). \"Einschränkungen\" repaired from the OCR split \"Ein— schränkungen\". The sentence breaks off before \"und dadurch ...\"."]],"krv-b-1234":["and thereby converting the category into a transcendental idea, in order",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passages."]],"krv-b-1235":["to give absolute completeness to the empirical synthesis by continuing it up to the unconditioned (which is never met with in experience, but only in the idea)",["- \"Unde- dingten\" corrected to \"Unbedingten\". The parenthesis is kept in place; \"angetroffen\" is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1236":["[is met with]. Reason demands",["- Square brackets: \"getroffen wird\" is the carry-over of \"an-/getroffen wird\" from the previous passage, which I have already translated there, so I have supplied it here only to mark the split. Strictly the German of this passage is \"getroffen wird. Die Vernunft fordert\". Doubtful editorial handling."]],"krv-b-1237":["this in accordance with the principle: if the conditioned is given, the",["- Fragment; the sentence continues."]],"krv-b-1238":["whole sum of the conditions, and consequently the absolutely unconditioned, is also given, through which alone the conditioned was possible. Thus, in the first place, the transcendental ideas will be really nothing but categories extended to the unconditioned,",["- \"schlechthin Unbedingte\" = \"absolutely unconditioned\". \"Also\" rendered \"Thus\"."]],"krv-b-1239":["and they will admit of being arranged in a table ordered according to the titles of the latter. In the second place,",["- \"Zwei-/tens\" joined and completed with the \"aber\" of the next passage; the sentence continues."]],"krv-b-1240":["however, not all the categories will serve for this purpose, but only those in which the synthesis constitutes a series, namely a series of conditions subordinated to one another (not co-ordinated) for a",["- \"diejenige\" read as \"diejenigen\" (plural), as the sense requires. \"beigeordnet\" = \"co-ordinated\". The sentence continues."]],"krv-b-1241":["conditioned. Absolute totality is demanded by reason only in so far as it concerns the ascending series of conditions for a given conditioned. It is therefore not demanded when we are speaking of the descending line",["- Sentence break after \"Bedingten\" is mine for the fragment's start; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1242":["of consequences, or of the aggregate of co-ordinated conditions of these consequences. For conditions are already presupposed in respect of the given conditioned, and are to be regarded as given together with it. The consequences, on the other hand, do not make their conditions possible but rather presuppose them; and therefore in advancing to the consequences (or in descending from the given condition to the conditioned) we may be unconcerned whether the series ceases or not. The question of its totality is in no way a presupposition of reason.",["- The long \"Denn ... anstatt daß, da ...\" sentence is restated in three sentences, with the irregular \"anstatt daß, da\" construction smoothed."]],"krv-b-1243":["Thus we necessarily think a time that has completely elapsed up to the given moment as also given (even if not determinable by us). As regards the future, however, since it is not the condition of our arriving at the present, it is quite indifferent, in seeking to comprehend the present, how we choose to treat future time, whether we are to make it end somewhere or run on to infinity. Let there be the series m, n, o, in which n is given as conditioned in respect of m, but at the same time as the condition of o. Let the series ascend from the conditioned n to m (l, k, i, etc.), and likewise descend from the condition n to the conditioned o (p, q, r, etc.). Then I must presuppose the former series in order to regard m as given, and n is possible, according to reason (the totality of conditions), only by means of that series. Its possibility does not, however, rest upon the subsequent series o, p, q, r, which therefore cannot be regarded as given, but only as *dabilis* [capable of being given].",["- OCR \"mals\" read \"m als\"; \"T\" in \"(p, q, T etc.)\" corrected to \"r\", as the later series \"o, p, q, r\" shows. \"im gleichen\" = \"likewise\".","- \"dabilis\" kept in Latin, with the gloss supplied in brackets."]],"krv-b-1244":["I shall entitle the synthesis of a series on the side of the conditions, that is, proceeding from the condition nearest to the given appearance and so on to the more remote conditions, the *regressive* synthesis; and that which on the side of the conditioned advances from the nearest consequence to the more remote, the *progressive* synthesis. The former proceeds *in antecedentia*, the latter *in consequentia*. The cosmological ideas, therefore, are occupied with the totality of the regressive synthesis, and proceed *in antecedentia*, not *in consequentia*. When the latter is done, we have an arbitrary, not a necessary, problem of pure reason; for in order completely to comprehend what is given in the appearance we require grounds, but not consequences.",["- \"regressive\" and \"progressive\" italicised as terms being defined. \"Begreiflichkeit\" = \"comprehension\" here, rendered by \"comprehend\"."]],"krv-b-1245":["In order now to construct the table of the ideas in accordance with the table of the categories, we take first the two original quanta of all our intuition, time and space. Time is in itself a series (and the formal condition of all series), and hence in it, in respect of a given present, the *antecedentia*, as conditions (the past), are to be distinguished *a priori* from the *consequentia* (the future).",["- Latin \"consequentibus\" (ablative) rendered as the nominative \"*consequentia*\" for readability in English. The sentence continues in the next passage with \"Folglich\", already taken up here by \"hence\" only for the previous clause; \"unterscheiden\" completed from the split \"unterschei-/den\"."]],"krv-b-1246":["Consequently the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1247":["series of conditions of a given conditioned refers only to all past time. According to the idea of reason, the whole elapsed time is necessarily thought as given, as the condition of the given moment. As regards space, however, there is in it, in itself, no",["- Stray \"s\" before \"Zeit\" (a line number) dropped. The sentence continues."]],"krv-b-1248":["distinction of progressus from regressus, because it constitutes an aggregate but",["- \"progressus\" and \"regressus\" left in Latin as in Kant."]],"krv-b-1249":["not a series, its parts all being simultaneous. The present moment I could regard, in respect of past time, only as conditioned, never as its condition, since this moment arises only through the time that has flowed past (or rather through the passing of the preceding time). But since the",["- OCR \"Dien gegenwärtigen ... „0 Zeit\" read as \"Den gegenwärtigen Zeitpunkt ... vergangenen Zeit\", with line number dropped. \"konnte\" read as \"könnte\". Doubtful reading in the OCR."]],"krv-b-1250":["parts of space are not subordinated to one another but co-ordinated,",["- Join of \"Aber da die\" in the previous passage with this fragment: \"Aber da die / Theile ...\" translated across both; \"But since the\" at the end of 1249 anticipates this."]],"krv-b-1251":["so one part is not the condition of the possibility of another, and space does not, like time, constitute a series in itself. Yet the synthesis of the manifold parts of space, whereby we apprehend it, is successive, takes place therefore in time, and contains a series. And since in this series of aggregated spaces (for instance, the feet in a rod) those that are thought as added beyond a given one are always the condition of the limit of the preceding, the measuring of a",["- Passages 1250–1252 are out of order in my output because the OCR text is displaced; here I have translated the German of 1251/1252 together, as the text of 1252–1253 runs on. This is an error in arrangement: see the following entry."]],"krv-b-1252":["[Duplicate of the content rendered in 1251: \"so ist ein Theil nicht die Bedingung der Möglichkeit des andern, und er macht nicht, so wie die Zeit an sich selbst eine Reihe aus. Allein die Synthesis der mannigfaltigen Theile des Raumes, wodurch wir ihn apprehendiren, ist doch successiv ...\" — see 1251.]",["- I merged the content of 1252 and the start of 1253 into the previous entry by mistake; the correct division is: 1251 ends \"co-ordinated,\"; 1252 runs \"so one part ... whereby we apprehend it,\"; 1253 runs \"is successive ... the measuring of a\". Please treat that division as authoritative."]],"krv-b-1253":["[See 1251–1252. Corrected division: \"is nevertheless successive, takes place therefore in time, and contains a series. And since in this series of aggregated spaces (for instance, the feet in a rod) those that are thought as added beyond a given one are always the condition of the limit of the preceding, the measuring of a\"]",["- \"Ruthe\" = \"rod\" (a measure of length). OCR \"0 din gnug\" read as \"Bedingung\". Doubtful reading."]],"krv-b-1254":["space is also to be regarded as the synthesis of a series of the conditions for a",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1255":["given conditioned; only that the side of the conditions is not, in itself, distinguished from",["- Fragment; continues."]],"krv-b-1256":["the side towards which the conditioned lies, and consequently regressus and progressus in space seem to be one and the same.",["- \"einerlei\" = \"one and the same\". Joined with \"schieden ist\" from 1257's opening; the sentence continues as \"folglich ... zu sein scheint\", spanning the next passage."]],"krv-b-1257":["Since, however, a part of space is not given through the other,",["- Breaks off in mid-sentence (\"durch den andern ge-\", i.e. \"gegeben\" or similar); I have rendered it as \"given\" but the completion is conjectural and must be checked against the next passage. The preceding sentence's ending (\"unterschieden ist, folglich ... zu sein scheint\") is carried in 1256."]],"krv-b-1258":["…give, but is only limited, we must regard every limited space, in so far, as conditioned, as presupposing another space as the condition of its limit, and so on. In respect of the limi-",["- Breaks off at both ends. The opening (\"geben\") has lost its subject and governing clause, and I have not supplied them. \"Be-\" is left as \"limi-\", to be completed by the next passage."]],"krv-b-1259":["tation, then, the advance in space is also a regress, and the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the synthesis in the series",["- A stray \"So\" in the OCR is dropped. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1260":["of conditions applies to space also, and I can ask as well about the absolute totality of appearance in space as about that of the time | that has elapsed. Whether, however, an answer to this is in any way possible,",["- \"verflofje- | neu\" is corrected to \"verflossenen\". The | is kept where it stands."]],"krv-b-1261":["will have to be determined later. Secondly, reality in space, that is, matter, is a conditioned. Its inner conditions are its parts, and the parts of the parts are its remote conditions, so that a regressive synthesis occurs here, the absolute totality of which reason demands. This can occur only through a completed division, by which the reality of matter vanishes either into nothing or at least into that which is no longer matter, namely, the simple. Consequently there is here also a series of conditions and a progress to the unconditioned.",["- The stray numeral \"5\" is dropped. I divided the long second sentence after \"demands\", turning the relative clause \"welche … stattfinden kann\" into a separate sentence. \"Die Realität im Raume\" is rendered \"reality in space\"."]],"krv-b-1262":["Thirdly, as regards the categories of real relation among appearances, the category of substance with its accidents is not suited to a transcendental idea; that is, reason has no ground for proceeding regressively to conditions in respect of it. For accidents (in so far as they inhere in one and the same substance) are coordinate with one another and do not constitute a series. In relation to substance they are not, properly speaking, subordinate to it, but are the mode of existence of the substance itself. What might still seem to be an idea of transcendental reason here would be the concept of the substantial. But this means nothing other than the concept of an object in general, which subsists in so far as we think in it merely the transcendental subject without any predicates. Here, however, we are speaking only of the unconditioned in the series of appearances. It is therefore clear that the substantial cannot constitute a member of this series. The same holds of substances in community. They are mere aggregates and have no exponent of a series, since they are not subordinate to one another as conditions of their possibility. This might well be said of spaces, whose limit was never determined through itself but always through another space. There remains, then, only the category of causality, which presents a series of causes for a given effect. In this series we can ascend from the effect, as the conditioned, to the causes, as conditions, and so answer the question of reason.",["- Stray OCR numerals and letters (\"1\", \"zoi\", \"2\", \"gez 2 gebenen\") are removed. \"ein- ander\", \"Sub— stantiale\", \"Kate— gorie\" and \"konnte\" (for \"könnte\") are corrected. \"Gegenstande\" is read as \"gegebenen\" in \"zu einer gegebenen Wirkung\".","- I divided the sentence beginning \"Allein da dieses…\" and the one on substances in community, which Kant runs on with parentheses and dashes. The \"welches\" before \"man wohl von den Räumen sagen konnte\" is resolved to refer to the non-subordination just stated.","- The passage ends the third paragraph; the heading's page reference is uncertain."]],"krv-b-1263":["Fourthly, the concepts of the possible, the actual and the necessary lead to no series, except in so far as the contingent in existence must always be regarded as conditioned, and, in accordance with the rule of the understanding, points to a condition under which it is necessary. This condition must in turn be referred to a higher condition, until reason finds unconditioned necessity only in the totality of this series.",["- A stray \"a\" is dropped. I divided the sentence at \"Bedingung\", and \"darunter es nothwendig ist, diese auf eine höhere Bedingung zu weisen\" is smoothed. The sense is that the condition in turn needs a higher one."]],"krv-b-1264":["There are accordingly no more than four cosmological ideas, corresponding to the four titles of the categories, if we select those which necessarily carry with them a series in the synthesis of the manifold.",["- \"Titel\" is rendered \"titles\", i.e. the four headings of the table of categories."]],"krv-b-1265":["1.\nThe absolute completeness\nof the\ncomposition\nof the given whole of all appearances.",[]],"krv-b-1266":["The absolute completeness  The absolute completeness",["- The OCR gives the two middle-column headings of the table side by side. Their numerals (2, 3) are missing."]],"krv-b-1267":["of the division  of the origination",["- Two table columns run together in the OCR. I have kept them side by side."]],"krv-b-1268":["of a given whole  of an appearance",["- Again two columns of the table side by side."]],"krv-b-1269":["in the appearance, in general.\n4.\nThe absolute completeness\nof the dependence of the existence\nof the alterable in the appearance.",["- \"in der Erscheinung überhaupt\" joins the ends of two columns: \"…in the appearance\" (division) and \"…in general\" (origination of an appearance). The OCR has run them together, and I have not separated them."]],"krv-b-1270":["In the first place it is to be remarked that the idea of absolute totality concerns nothing but the exposition of appearances, and therefore not the pure concept of the understanding of a whole of things in general. Appearances are here, then, regarded as given, and reason demands the absolute completeness of the conditions of their possibility, in so far as these constitute a series. It thus demands an absolutely (that is, in every respect) complete synthesis, by which the appearance can be expounded in accordance with laws of the understanding.",["- \"über: haupt\" is corrected to \"überhaupt\". I divided the final sentence after \"ausmachen\". \"Exposition\" and \"exponirt\" are rendered \"exposition\" and \"expounded\", following Kant's Latin."]],"krv-b-1271":["Secondly, what reason really seeks in this synthesis of conditions, continued in series and indeed regressively, is solely the unconditioned, as it were the completeness in the series of premisses, which together presuppose no further premiss. This unconditioned is always contained in the absolute totality of the series, when we represent it to ourselves in imagination. But this absolutely completed synthesis is again only an idea; for we cannot know, at least in advance, whether such a synthesis is possible in the case of appearances. If we represent everything through mere pure concepts of the understanding, without conditions of sensible intuition, we can say outright that for a given conditioned the whole series of conditions subordinate to one another is also given; for the former is given only through the latter. In the case of appearances, however, we find a special limitation in the manner in which conditions are given, namely, through the successive synthesis of the manifold of intuition, which is to be complete in the regress. Whether this completeness is sensibly possible is still a problem. But the idea of this completeness lies in reason, irrespective of the possibility or impossibility of connecting with it empirical concepts adequate to it. Now the unconditioned is necessarily contained in the absolute totality of the regressive synthesis of the manifold in appearance (in accordance with the guidance of the categories, which represent this synthesis as a series of conditions for a given conditioned). We may leave undecided whether and how this totality can be brought about. Reason, then, takes the way of setting out from the idea of totality, although what it really has in view as its final aim is the unconditioned, whether of the whole series or of a part of it.",["- A stray \"10%\" is dropped, and \"volendete\" is read as \"vollendete\". The long final sentence is restated as two, and \"Also da … so nimmt\" is smoothed.","- \"Einbildung\" is rendered \"imagination\". \"Nach Anleitung der Kategorien\" is rendered \"in accordance with the guidance of the categories\", the parenthesis being kept."]],"krv-b-1272":["This unconditioned may now be thought in two ways. Either it consists solely in the whole series, in which all members without exception would be conditioned and only the whole of them absolutely unconditioned; and then the regress is entitled infinite. Or the absolutely unconditioned is only a part of the series, to which the remaining members are subordinate, but which itself stands under no other condition.†) In the former case the series is *a parte priori* without limits (without beginning), that is, infinite, and nevertheless given as a whole; but the regress in it is never completed, and can be called infinite only *potentialiter*. In the second case there is a first member of the series. In respect of elapsed time this is entitled the beginning of the world; in respect of space, the limit of the world; in respect of the parts of a whole given within its limits, the simple; in respect of causes, absolute self-activity (freedom); in respect of the existence of alterable things, absolute natural necessity.",["- \"d. i. uns endlich\" is an OCR slip for \"unendlich\"; I read \"infinite\". Stray numerals \"25\" are dropped.","- The footnote mark is shown as †). The note itself is in the next passage. I divided the first sentence at \"dann heißt der Regressus unendlich\". \"Heißt\" is rendered \"is entitled\"."]],"krv-b-1273":["We have two expressions, world and nature, which sometimes run into each other. The first signifies the mathematical whole of all ap-",["- Breaks off mid-word (\"Er-\" for \"Erscheinungen\"). The sentence continues after the footnote in the next passage. The stray \"30\" is dropped."]],"krv-b-1274":["†) The absolute whole of the series of conditions for a given conditioned is always unconditioned, because outside it there are no further conditions in respect of which it could be conditioned. But this absolute whole of such a series is only an idea, or rather a problematic concept, whose possibility must be investigated, and indeed in relation to the manner in which the unconditioned, as the proper transcendental idea with which we are concerned, may be contained in it.\npearances and the totality of their synthesis, in the great as well as in the small, that is, as well in the progress of this synthesis by composition,",["- The OCR puts the footnote first and then the continuation of the main text (\"scheinungen …\"), which completes \"ap-\" from the previous passage. I kept that order. \"Dag\" is corrected to \"Das\", and the stray \"&*\" is dropped."]],"krv-b-1275":["as by division. The same world, however, is entitled nature‡) in so far as",["- The footnote mark after \"Natur\" is shown as ‡). The sentence breaks off and is not completed. \"Genannt\" is rendered \"is entitled\"."]],"krv-b-1276":["it is regarded as a dynamical whole, and we look not to the aggregation in space or time, in order to bring it as a magnitude to [zu Stande]",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; *zu Stande* is completed by *zu bringen* at the start of the next passage, and I have not completed it.","- *Größe* = magnitude; *Erscheinungen* in the next passage = appearances."]],"krv-b-1277":["[to bring about], but to the unity in the existence of appearances. In this case the condition of that which happens is entitled the cause; the unconditioned causality of the cause, in the appearance, is entitled freedom; the conditioned causality, on the other hand, is entitled, in the narrower sense, natural cause. The conditioned in existence in general is entitled contingent, and the unconditioned necessary. The unconditioned necessity of appearances can be entitled natural-",["- \"[to bring about]\" supplies the *zu bringen* that opens this passage, to join it to the preceding fragment.","- *heißen* = \"is entitled\" throughout. *Im engeren Verstande* = \"in the narrower sense\" (*Verstand* here is not \"understanding\").","- The sentence is cut at the hyphen *Natur-*; its verb *heißen* stands in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1278":["necessity.",["- The German is *nothwendigkeit heißen*. The verb is already rendered in the previous passage (\"can be entitled\") so that the English reads continuously."]],"krv-b-1279":["The ideas with which we are now occupied I have above entitled cosmological ideas. I did so partly because by 'world' is understood the sum of all appearances, and our ideas are directed only to the unconditioned among appearances; and partly also because the word 'world', in the transcendental sense, signifies the absolute totality of the sum | of existing things, and we direct our attention solely to the completeness of the synthesis (though, strictly speaking, only in the regress to the conditions). In view, further, of the fact that these ideas are one and all transcendent, and that, while they do not overstep their object, namely appearances, in respect of kind, but have to do solely with the sensible world (not with noumena), they yet carry the synthesis to a degree which surpasses all possible experience, we may, in my opinion, quite fittingly entitle them all world-",["- Restated: one long sentence divided after \"synthesis\"; the \"partly … partly\" construction kept. The sign | is carried over where it stands; the OCR \"eriftirender\" is read as *existirender* (existing).","- *Inbegriff* = sum; *der Art nach* = \"in respect of kind\"; *Noümenis* = noumena.","- The verb *nennen* stands in the next passage; I have placed \"entitle\" here so that the English reads continuously. The sentence breaks at *Weltbe-*, hence \"world-\"."]],"krv-b-1280":["concepts [*Weltbegriffe*]. As regards the difference between the mathematically unconditioned and the dynamically unconditioned, at which the regress aims, I should [entitle] the first two, in the narrower sense, world-concepts (of the world in the great and in the small), and the other two transcendent concepts of nature",["- \"[entitle]\" is supplied because the verb *nennen* stands after the footnote, in the next passage.","- *Weltbegriffe* given in German because the sense turns on it."]],"krv-b-1281":["Footnote: *Nature*, taken adjectivally (*formaliter*), signifies the interconnection of the determinations of a thing according to an inner principle of causality. By nature, on the other hand, taken substantively (*materialiter*), is understood the sum of appearances, in so far as these, by virtue of an inner principle of causality, are thoroughly interconnected. In the former sense we speak of the nature of fluid matter, of fire, and so forth, and use the word only adjectivally; whereas when we speak of the things of nature, we have in mind a self-subsisting whole.\n\n[Text resumed:] [entitle]. This distinction is for the present not of any great importance, but it may become more so in the course of the argument.\n\n- The OCR has put the end of the main-text sentence (*nennen. Diese Unterscheidung …*) inside the footnote; I have separated it and marked it as the continuation of the text.\n- *Bestimmungen* = determinations; *bestehendes Ganzes* = \"self-subsisting whole\", a judgment of sense.\n- \"[entitle]\" repeats the supplied verb of the previous passage, since *nennen* is the verb for \"transcendent concepts of nature\".",[]],"krv-b-1282":["--- notes\n- Left untranslated: \"Der Antinomie der reinen Vernunft\" appears to be a running head, and my instructions omit these.",[]],"krv-b-1283":["Section II.",["- The \"5\" is a line number and is omitted."]],"krv-b-1284":["The Antithetic of Pure Reason.",["- *Antithetik* rendered \"Antithetic\", matching \"thetic\" in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1285":["If a thetic is any body of dogmatic doctrines, then by antithetic I understand not dogmatic assertions of the opposite, but the conflict of modes of knowledge that are seemingly dogmatic (*thesin cum antithesi*), without our ascribing to either a superior claim to approval over the other. The antithetic is thus not concerned at all with one-sided assertions. It considers universal modes of knowledge of reason only in respect of their conflict with one another and of the causes of that conflict. The transcendental antithetic is an investigation into the antinomy of pure reason, its causes and its outcome. If we employ our reason not merely in applying the principles of the understanding to objects of experience, but venture to extend it beyond the limits of experience, there arise pseudo-rational doctrines [*vernünftelnde Lehrsätze*]. These can neither hope for confirmation in experience nor fear refutation by it. Each of them is not only free from contradiction in itself, but even finds in the nature of reason conditions of its necessity; only, unhappily, the opposite assertion has on its side grounds just as valid and necessary.",["- Line numbers embedded in the text (10, 18, 20) are omitted. The OCR \"waz gen\" is read as *wagen*.","- *Erkenntnisse* = \"modes of knowledge\"; *Verstandesgrundsätze* = principles of the understanding.","- *vernünftelnde* = \"pseudo-rational\" (reasoning that merely has the form of reason); the German is given because the choice is mine.","- The last sentence is kept whole but its relative clauses are lightly smoothed."]],"krv-b-1286":["The questions which naturally present themselves in connection with such a dialectic of pure reason are therefore these. (1) In what propositions, precisely, is pure reason unavoidably subject to an antinomy? (2) On what causes does this antinomy rest? (3) Whether, and in what manner, there nevertheless remains open to reason, in face of this contradiction, a path to certainty.",["- The German questions are in indirect form (*ob … sei*, *ob und auf welche Art …*); I have made the first two direct questions and left the third in the German's indirect form.","- Line numbers (25, 30) are omitted."]],"krv-b-1287":["A dialectical proposition of pure reason must accordingly have this to distinguish it from all sophistical propositions: first, that it does not concern an arbitrary question, raised only for some chosen purpose, but one which every human reason must necessarily encounter in its progress; and secondly, that with its",["- The passage breaks off at *mit sei-* (*seinem*), completed in the next passage. The sentence is divided between the passages as in the German.","- *Lehrsatz* = \"proposition\"."]],"krv-b-1288":["opposite it should bring not merely an artificial illusion which, once one",["- The OCR \"new\" is read as *nem* of *seinem*. The verb *führe* (\"should carry\") stands in the next passage; I have placed \"bring\" here for continuity, and the sentence stays unfinished.","- *Schein* = illusion."]],"krv-b-1289":["sees through it, at once vanishes, but a natural and unavoidable illusion, which, even when one is no longer deceived by it, still deludes, though it does not defraud, and which can therefore be rendered harmless but never extirpated.",["- *täuscht, obschon nicht betrügt*: \"deludes, though not defrauds\" keeps the distinction between *täuschen* and *betrügen*."]],"krv-b-1290":["Such a dialectical doctrine will relate not to the unity of the understanding in empirical concepts, but to the unity of reason in mere ideas. The conditions of this unity, since they have first, as a synthesis according to rules, to conform to the understanding, and yet at the same time, as the absolute unity of that synthesis, to agree with reason,",["- The sentence is broken off at the end of the passage and continues in the next two; I have not completed it.","- A stray \"b\" after *Ideen* in the OCR (apparently a footnote mark, with no footnote present) is omitted.","- *Verstandeseinheit* / *Vernunfteinheit* = unity of the understanding / of reason. *congruiren* = \"conform / agree\"."]],"krv-b-1291":["if they are adequate to the unity of reason, will be too great for the",["- The sentence breaks off here (*für den*) and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1292":["understanding, and if they are suited to the understanding, too small for reason; whence a conflict must arise which cannot be avoided, however one may set about it. These pseudo-rational assertions accordingly open a dialectical arena, in which the side that has permission to make the attack keeps the upper hand, and the side that is constrained to proceed merely on the defensive¹ is certain to succumb. Hence also doughty knights, whether\n\nFootnote marker: see the next passage.",["- Stray OCR marks (\"l\" before *Diese*) are omitted. I divided the construction *wenn … adäquat … wenn … angemessen* into two parallel conditionals.","- The final sentence (*Daher auch rüstige Ritter, sie | mögen …*) breaks across passages; the pronoun *sie* is absorbed into \"whether\".","- *Kampfplatz* = arena. *vernünftelnde* as before."]],"krv-b-1293":["they champion the good or the bad cause, are sure to bear off the garland of victory if only they take care that they have the privilege of making the last attack and are not obliged to withstand a fresh onslaught from their opponent. One can easily imagine that this arena has been entered often enough by each side, that many victories have been won by both, but that for the last, which decided the matter, care was always taken that the champion of the good cause should alone keep the field, by his opponent's being forbidden to take up arms any further. As impartial umpires we must set quite aside whether it is the good or the bad cause for which the combatants are fighting, and let them first settle their affair between themselves. Perhaps, when they have wearied one another more than harmed one another, they will themselves perceive the nullity of their quarrel and part as good friends. This method of watching a conflict of assertions, or",["- OCR \"legten\" is read as *letzten* (\"last\"). The stray \"!\" before *Diese* is omitted.","- The sentence breaks off at *oder* and is completed in the passage that follows the footnote.","- *Kampfrichter* = \"umpires\"; *Streithandel* = \"quarrel\"."]],"krv-b-1294":["Footnote ¹: A¹: *der sich bloß vertheidigungsweise zu führen* [\"who … to conduct himself merely defensively\"].",["- The siglum is read from the OCR \"Al;\" as *A¹:* (a variant from the first edition), but this is uncertain.","- The clause is a fragment of a variant reading, with the finite verb absent, and is translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-1295":["rather of occasioning it itself, not in order finally to decide in favour of the one party or the other, but in order to investigate whether its object may not perhaps be a mere illusion [*Blendwerk*], after which each grasps in vain, and in which he can gain nothing even if he were not resisted at all: this procedure, I say, may be entitled the sceptical method. It is entirely distinct from scepticism, a principle of artificial and scientific ignorance, which undermines the foundations of all knowledge, in order, so far as possible, to leave nowhere any reliability or security in it. For the sceptical method aims at certainty. It seeks, in a dispute of this kind, honestly meant on both sides and conducted with understanding, to discover the point of misunderstanding, in order, as wise legislators do, to draw from the embarrassment of judges in lawsuits instruction for themselves as to what is defective and not exactly determined in their laws. The antinomy which reveals itself in the application of laws is, for our limited wisdom, the best test of nomothetic; and it is by this means that reason, which in abstract speculation does not easily become aware of its false steps, is made attentive to the moments to be considered in determining its principles.",["- Restated: the long second sentence (*Denn die sceptische Methode …*) is divided in two, and the last sentence is recast to make *dadurch* explicit (\"by this means\").","- *Nomothetik* is left as \"nomothetic\" (the art of legislation).","- *Erkenntniß* = knowledge; *Bestimmung* = determination. *Blendwerk* is given in German because the sense turns on it.","- The OCR in \"Scepticis— mus\" is silently joined."]],"krv-b-1296":["This sceptical method is, however, essentially proper to transcendental philosophy alone. In any other field of enquiry it can at a pinch be dispensed with, but not in this one. In mathematics its employment would be absurd. No false assertions can there conceal themselves and so be made invisible, since the proofs must always proceed under the guidance of pure intuition, and by way of a synthesis that is at every step evident. In experimental philosophy a doubt that causes delay may indeed be useful. But at least no misunderstanding is possible that cannot easily be removed, and in experience the ultimate means of deciding the dispute must lie, whether they be found early or late. Morals can give its principles, one and all, *in concreto*, together with their practical consequences, at least in possible experiences, and can thereby avoid the misunderstanding due to abstraction. Transcendental assertions, on the other hand, which lay claim to insights extending even beyond the field of all possible experiences, are not in the position that their abstract synthesis could be given in some intuition *a priori*. Nor are they so constituted that the misunderstanding could be discovered by means of",["- Restated the long final sentence in two sentences (at \"Dagegen\"/\"Transcendental assertions\" the construction is kept, but the earlier sentences are split for clarity). The text breaks off in mid-sentence, as given.","- \"Experimentalphilosophie\" rendered \"experimental philosophy\"; \"Aufschub\" given as \"delay\" (a doubt that suspends or defers judgment)."]],"krv-b-1297":["some experience. Transcendental",["- Continues the preceding passage; the sentence is completed in the next."]],"krv-b-1298":["reason therefore admits of no other touchstone than the attempt to unite its assertions with one another, and so first of all to allow them free and unhindered conflict among themselves. This conflict we now propose to institute.*)",["- \"Probirstein\" = touchstone; \"mithin zuvor\" resolved as \"and so first of all\". The footnote marker is kept."]],"krv-b-1299":["*) The antinomies follow one another in the order of the above-mentioned",["- The OCR has \")\" for the footnote marker \"*)\"; restored. The sentence is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1300":["transcendental ideas.",["- Completes the footnote begun in the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-1301":["Of the Antinomy",["- A fragment of the heading (\"Der Antinomie der reinen Vernunft\"); the remainder is not in the passage."]],"krv-b-1302":["First Conflict",["- *Erster Widerstreit*; \"Widerstreit\" is rendered \"conflict\" and the English is made to agree with the usage of the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-1303":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-b-1304":["The world has a beginning in time, and is also enclosed within limits in space.",["- The OCR fragment \"sin!\" is a stray printing artefact and is omitted."]],"krv-b-1305":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1306":["For let us assume that the world has no beginning in time. Then up to every given point of time an eternity has elapsed, and therefore an infinite series of successive states of things [Zustände der Dinge] … that it can never be completed through successive synthesis. Hence an infinite elapsed world-series is impossible, and a beginning of the world is therefore a necessary condition of its existence. This was the first point to be proved.",["- The OCR text is defective after \"Zustände der Dinge\": words are missing between \"Dinge\" and \"Reihe, daß sie…\" (apparently the clause about the infinity of a series). I have not filled the gap; the sign \"…\" marks it.","- \"des weisen\" corrected to \"zu beweisen\"; the long sentence is divided into several."]],"krv-b-1307":["In regard to the second [point] let us again assume the opposite. The world will then be an infinite given whole of simultaneously existing things. Now the magnitude of a quantum which is not given, within certain limits, in any intuition,*) we can think in no other way than through the synthesis of its parts; and the totality of such a quantum only through the completed synthesis, or through repeated addition of unity to itself.**) Accordingly, in order to think the world, which fills all spaces, as a whole, there would have to be",["- \"[point]\" is supplied; *das zweite* refers to the second part of the thesis (limits in space). Line numbers are dropped. The OCR markers \"*)\" and \"“)\" are rendered as \"*)\" and \"**)\".","- \"gegeben wird, auf keine andere Art…\" is restated in two sentences; the passage breaks off in mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1308":["*) We can intuit an undetermined quantum as a whole, if it is enclosed within limits, without having to construct its totality by measurement, that is, by the successive synthesis of its parts. For the limits already determine its completeness, in that they cut off everything further.",["- Footnote. \"Unbestimmtes Quantum\" = \"undetermined quantum\"; \"Mehrere\" rendered \"everything further\"."]],"krv-b-1309":["**) The concept of totality is in this case nothing other than the representation of the completed synthesis of its parts. For since we cannot abstract the concept from the intuition of the whole (which in this case is impossible), we can grasp it only through the synthesis of the parts, carried to the completion of the infinite, at least in idea.",["- Footnote. Broken words (\"Anschau-ung\", \"Un-endlichen\") repaired; the running heads (\"der reinen Vernunft … der transscendentalen Ideen\") are omitted. The sentence is divided at \"weil\"."]],"krv-b-1310":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-b-1311":["The world has no beginning and no limits in space, but is infinite in respect of both time and space.",[]],"krv-b-1312":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1313":["For suppose that it has a beginning. Since the beginning is an existence which is preceded by a time in which the thing is not, a time must",["- The sentence is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1314":["have preceded in which the world was not, that is, an empty time. Now in an empty time no coming-to-be of any thing is possible, because no part of such a time has, as compared with any other, any distin-",["- The word \"unterscheidende\" is split between this passage and the next; I follow the split (\"distin-\" / \"guishing\")."]],"krv-b-1315":["guishing condition of existence as against non-existence (whether we suppose that it arises of itself or through some other cause). Hence many a series of things may indeed begin in the world, but the world itself cannot have a beginning, and is therefore infinite in respect",["- OCR \"Nifo\" read as \"Also\"; rendered \"Hence\". The sentence is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1316":["of past time.",[]],"krv-b-1317":["As regards the second point, let us first assume the opposite, namely that the world is finite and limited in space; it then",["- *befindet* is split across the passage break (\"be-\" / \"findet\"); in English the verb falls into the next passage."]],"krv-b-1318":["is found in an empty space which is not limited. There would therefore be met with not only a relation of things in space, but also a relation of things to space. Now since the world is an absolute whole, outside which no object of intuition, and therefore",["- Verbal fusion: the OCR \"findet sie sich\" is rendered \"is found\" to agree with the preceding fragment. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1319":["no correlate of the world is to be met with, with which the world would stand in relation […] the successive synthesis of the parts of an infinite world would have to be regarded as completed, that is, an infinite time would have to be regarded as elapsed in the enumeration of all coexisting things; which is impossible. Accordingly an infinite aggregate of actual things cannot be regarded as a given whole, and therefore also not as given simultaneously. A world is consequently not infinite in extension in space, but is enclosed within its limits; which was the second point.",["- The OCR is corrupt here. After \"im Verhältniß\" the text jumps to a clause belonging to the Thesis proof (\"die successive Synthesis … einer unendlichen Welt als vollendet\"); the intervening matter is missing or displaced. I mark the break with \"[…]\" and do not supply anything.","- \"womit dieselbe im Verhältniß\" is completed minimally (\"would stand in relation\") only for grammar; this is doubtful. Line number \"5\" dropped."]],"krv-b-1320":["Remark on the",[]],"krv-b-1321":["I. On the Thesis.",["- OCR \"J.\" read as \"I.\""]],"krv-b-1322":["In these conflicting arguments I have not sought for illusions in order to bring forward (as one says) a lawyer's proof, which takes advantage of the opponent's carelessness and gladly lets his appeal to a misunderstood law stand, in order to build its own unlawful claims on the refutation of it. Each of these proofs is drawn from the nature of the matter, and the advantage that the fallacies of the dogmatists on both sides might give us has been set aside.",["- \"Blendwerke\" = illusions (deceptive shows); \"Advocatenbeweis\" = lawyer's proof. The long second sentence is kept in one piece, as the English bears it. \"Dog matiker\" repaired."]],"krv-b-1323":["I could also have proved the thesis, apparently [*dem Scheine nach*], by having put forward beforehand, after the habit of the dogmatists, a faulty concept of the infinity of a given magnitude. A magnitude is infinite if none greater than it (that is, greater than the multitude of a given unit contained in it) is possible. Now no multitude is the greatest, since one or more units can always still be added. Hence an infinite given magnitude, and therefore also a world infinite (alike in respect of the elapsed series and of extension) […] stood, the relation of the world to empty space would be a relation of it to no object. Such a relation, however, and therefore also the limitation of the world by empty space, is nothing. The world is therefore not limited at all in respect of space; that is, it is infinite in extension.*)",["- The OCR is corrupt and displaced. \"unstehe\" at the join is a fusion of the end of the Thesis note (\"…unmöglich\") with a clause from the Antithesis proof (\"… stehe, so würde …\"); the sentence is not recoverable, so \"[…]\" marks the break and nothing is supplied.","- The passage-end running head \"ersten Antinomie\" is omitted; footnote marker \"“)\" restored as \"*)\". Line number dropped."]],"krv-b-1324":["II. On the Antithesis.",[]],"krv-b-1325":["*) Space is merely the form of outer intuition (formal intuition); it is not a real object which can be outwardly intuited. Space, prior to all the things which determine it (fill or limit it), or which rather yield an empirical intuition conforming to its form, is, under the name of absolute space, nothing but the mere possibility of outer appearances, in so far as these either exist in themselves or can be added to given appearances. Empirical intuition is not, therefore, composed of appearances and space (of perception and empty intuition). The one is not the correlate of the other in a synthesis; they are connected in one and the same empirical intuition, as its matter and its form. If we try to posit one of these two constituents apart from the other (space outside all appearances), there arise all sorts of empty determinations of outer intuition, which are yet not possible perceptions. Examples are the motion or rest of the world in infinite empty space, a determination of the relation of the two to each other which can never be perceived, and is therefore the predicate of a mere thought-entity [*Gedankending*].",["- \"Gallen\" in the German is an OCR slip for \"allen\". Corrected silently.","- Restated the second sentence's parenthetical \"(erfüllen oder begrenzen)\" and the \"oder die vielmehr\" clause as a plain relative construction.","- \"Gedankending\" is rendered \"thought-entity\", with the German added."]],"krv-b-1326":["The proof of the infinity of the given world-series and of the world-whole rests on this: that in the contrary case an empty time, and likewise an empty space, would have to constitute the limit of the world. Now I am not unaware that subterfuges are sought against this consequence, by pretending that a limit of the world in time and space is quite possible without our having to assume an absolute time before the beginning of the world, or an absolute space extended beyond the actual world; and this is impossible. With the latter part of this opinion of the philosophers of the Leib— possible: it is therefore limited on both sides. I could have conducted my proof in this way. But this concept does not agree with what is understood by an infinite whole. By it we do not represent how great the whole is; its concept is therefore not the concept of a maximum. What is thought through it is only its relation to a unit which may be assumed at will, in respect of which it is greater than all number. According as the unit is assumed greater or smaller, the infinite would be greater or smaller. But the infinity, since it consists merely in the relation to this given unit, would always remain the same, although the absolute magnitude of the whole would not thereby be known at all, and of this there is here no question.",["- The text is displaced and broken after \"Leib–\". The continuation (\"nizischen Schule ganz wohl zufrieden…\") appears in the next-but-two passage, and the words before \"möglich\" are lost. I have translated what stands and filled nothing.","- \"welches unmöglich ist\" is left as a short independent clause. Its antecedent (the assumption of an absolute time or space) is made explicit by the preceding semicolon.","- \"Maximum\" is retained in Latin."]],"krv-b-1327":["The true (transcendental) concept of infinity is that the successive synthesis of unity in the measuring of a quantum can never be completed.*) From this it follows with complete certainty that an eternity of actual successive states, up to a given (the present) point of time, cannot have elapsed, and that the world must therefore have a beginning.",["- \"niez mals\" is an OCR slip for \"niemals\". Corrected silently.","- The footnote sign appears as “) in the source and is rendered *). The footnote text is in the following passages.","- \"Quantum\" is retained in Latin.","- \"Durchmessung\" is rendered \"measuring\"."]],"krv-b-1328":["As regards the second part of the thesis, the difficulty of a series which is infinite and yet has elapsed does indeed disappear; for the manifold of a world infinite in extension is given simultaneously. But in order to think the totality of such a multitude, since we cannot appeal to limits which of themselves constitute this totality in intuition, we must give an account of our concept,",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence, and I have not completed it. The sentence continues in the later passage beginning \"geben, der in solchem Falle…\". The words \"Rechenschaft geben\" are divided between the two.","- \"Thesis\" is Kant's term for the first of the opposed propositions, and I have left it as is."]],"krv-b-1329":["*) This thereby contains a multitude (of a given unit) which is greater than all number, and this is the mathematical concept of the infinite. —nizian school, quite well satisfied. Space is merely the form of outer intuition, but not a real object which can be outwardly in—",["- This is a footnote, but the passage also contains displaced running text. Its first words (\"nizischen Schule ganz wohl zufrieden\") belong to the sentence begun in the Leib– fragment above (\"Ich bin … zufrieden\"). The remainder repeats the footnote of passage 1325 and continues in the next passage.","- \"Dieses\" in the footnote refers to the infinite whole mentioned in the footnote's anchor in the preceding passage. I kept \"This\" as in the German.","- The passage ends mid-word (\"an—\"), and I have left it so."]],"krv-b-1330":["—tuited, and no correlate of appearances, but the form of appearances itself. Space, therefore, cannot occur absolutely (by itself alone) as something determining in the existence of things, since it is no object at all, but only the form of possible objects. Things, therefore, as appearances, do indeed determine space,",["- The passage begins mid-word (\"geschauet\" completing \"an—\" of the preceding passage). I have represented this by the dash.","- A stray \"$\" in the OCR was dropped."]],"krv-b-1331":["that is, among all the possible predicates of space (magnitude and relation) they bring it about that this or that belongs to reality. But conversely, space, as something subsisting for itself, cannot determine the reality of things in respect of magnitude or shape, since it is in itself nothing real. A space (be it full or empty)*) can therefore well be limited by appearances, but appearances cannot be limited by an empty space outside them. The same holds of time. All this being granted, it is gleich—",["- The passage breaks off mid-word (\"gleich-\"). The next passage begins \"wohl\", so the whole word is *gleichwohl*, \"none the less\". I left the German as it stands.","- \"Wirklichkeit\" is rendered \"reality\" and \"Gestalt\" \"shape\".","- The footnote sign *) refers to the note printed elsewhere in this batch (the note on empty space within the world)."]],"krv-b-1332":["[gleich]wohl [none the less] indisputable that these two non-entities [*Undinge*], the empty space outside and the empty time before the world, must absolutely be assumed, if one assumes a limit of the world, whether in respect of space or of time.",["- The opening \"wohl\" completes \"gleich-\" from the preceding passage, and I bracketed the joined word.","- \"Undinge\" is rendered \"non-entities\", with the German."]],"krv-b-1333":["For as regards the way of escape by which men seek to evade the consequence according to which we say that, if the world has limits (in time and space), the infinite void must determine the magnitude of the existence of actual things, it consists, in secret, only in this. Instead of a world of sense, one thinks, heaven knows what intelligible world. Instead of the first beginning (an existence preceded by a time of non-being) one thinks an existence in general which presupposes no other condition in the world. Instead of the limit of extension one thinks bounds [*Schranken*] of the world-whole; and so one gets out of the way of time and space. But here we are concerned only with the *mundus phaenomenon* and its magnitude, and in dealing with it we can in no way abstract from the conditions of sensibility in question [*gedachten*],",["- \"Sin nenwelt\" is an OCR slip for \"Sinnenwelt\", and \"ingeheim\" is for \"insgeheim\". Both corrected.","- Restated the long sentence on the evasion as a series of short sentences, preserving the parallel \"instead of … one thinks\".","- \"gedachten Bedingungen\" is rendered \"conditions … in question\", i.e. the conditions of sensibility just mentioned, since \"thought\" here would mislead.","- \"Grenze\" is \"limit\" and \"Schranken\" \"bounds\", to keep the distinction.","- The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1334":["without destroying its essence. The world of sense, if it is limited",["- The sentence breaks off. Its predicate (\"ist, liegt nothwendig in dem unendlichen Leeren\") appears as displaced text at the end of passage 1341."]],"krv-b-1335":["*) It is easily seen that what is meant here is that empty space, in so far as it is limited by appearances, and therefore space within the world, does not at least contradict the transcendental principles, and may therefore, so far as these are concerned, be admitted (although its possibility is not on that account straightway asserted). —[to] give [an account of our concept], which in such a case cannot proceed from the whole to the determinate multitude of the parts, but must demonstrate the possibility of a whole through the successive synthesis of the parts. Since this synthesis would have to constitute a series never to be completed, one cannot think a totality prior to it, and consequently not through it either. For the concept of totality itself is, in this case, the representation of a completed synthesis of the parts; and this completion, and therefore also the concept of it, is impossible.",["- The passage combines a footnote with displaced text from the main argument (the sentence begun in passage 1328: \"müssen wir von unserem Begriffe Rechenschaft geben, der …\"). I have shown the join with a dash and bracketed supplied words.","- A stray \"5\" (a line number) was dropped.","- \"Totalität\" is rendered \"totality\", and \"Vollendung\" \"completion\"."]],"krv-b-1336":["Of the Antinomy |",["- The heading is a fragment. Its remaining words, \"der reinen Vernunft\", and the phrase \"der transscendentalen Ideen\" appear displaced at the end of passage 1341. The full title is the Antinomy of Pure Reason."]],"krv-b-1337":["Second Conflict",["- The OCR gives \"Widerstreit m\". The stray \"m\" is an obvious slip and was dropped. \"Widerstreit\" is rendered \"conflict\"."]],"krv-b-1338":["Thesis. |",["- None."]],"krv-b-1339":["Every composite substance in the world consists of simple parts, and there exists nowhere anything save the simple, or that | which is composed of it.",["- \"eriftirt\" is an OCR slip for \"existirt\". Corrected.","- The sign | is carried over as it stands."]],"krv-b-1340":["Proof.",["- The stray \"5%\" (a line number) was dropped."]],"krv-b-1341":["For suppose that composite substances did not consist | of simple parts: then, if all composition were removed in thought, | no composite part, and (since there are no simple parts) no simple part either, and so nothing at all, would remain, and consequently no substance would have been given. Either, therefore, it is impossible to remove all composition in thought, or after its re- | moval there must [remain] something subsisting without any composition, that is, the simple, is, lies necessarily in the infinite void. If one wishes to leave this out, and with it space in general as a condition of the possibility of appearances a priori, the whole world of sense falls away. In our problem this alone is given to us. The *mundus intelligibilis* is nothing but the universal concept of a world in general, in which one abstracts from all conditions of the intuition of it, and in respect of which, consequently, no synthetic proposition, whether affirmative or negative, is possible. | of pure reason of the transcendental ideas.",["- \"läßt ich\" is an OCR slip for \"läßt sich\". Corrected.","- The text is corrupt in order. After \"das Einfache,\" the OCR drops in a stray \"j ist, liegt nothwendig in dem unendlichen Leeren\". This is the end of the sentence begun in passage 1334 (\"Die Sinnenwelt, wenn sie begrenzt ist, liegt nothwendig …\"). I translated the displaced words where they stand, without joining them.","- The verb after \"Bestehendes\" is missing in the German. I supplied \"[remain]\" from the preceding \"übrig bleiben\", and the sentence is otherwise broken off.","- The final \"der reinen Vernunft der transscendentalen Ideen\" is the displaced running title, belonging to the Antinomy heading above.","- \"Substanz\" is rendered \"substance\", \"Zusammensetzung\" \"composition\".","- \"a priori\" is left as in the source (not italicised, following the German)."]],"krv-b-1342":["Antithesis.",["- None."]],"krv-b-1343":["No composite thing in the world consists of simple parts, and nothing simple exists anywhere in it.",["- Thesis and antithesis are run together in one sentence, as in the source text."]],"krv-b-1344":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1345":["Suppose that a composite thing (as substance) consists of simple parts. All external relation, and therefore all composition out of substances, is possible only in space. Hence the space which the composite occupies must consist of as many parts as the composite does. Now space does not consist of simple parts, but of spaces. Therefore every part of the composite must occupy a space. But the absolutely first parts of everything composite are simple. Therefore the simple occupies a space. Now everything real that occupies a space contains in itself a manifold of elements external to one another, and is therefore composite. And as a real composite it is composed not of accidents (for these cannot be external to one another without substance) [text breaks off] ... remain. In the former case, however, the composite would again not consist of substances (since in substances composition is only a contingent relation, without which they must subsist as beings persisting by themselves). Now since this case contradicts the supposition, only the second remains: namely, that the substantial composite in the world consists of simple parts.",["- The passage is corrupt. The sentence beginning \"Da nun alles Reale…\" breaks off at \"nicht aus Accidenzen (…)\", and \"übrig bleiben\" follows with its subject lost. I marked the break and did not complete it. \"Im ersteren Falle\" therefore has no visible antecedent.","- \"Mfo\" read as \"Also\" and \"Busammengesetzten\" as \"Zusammengesetzten\". I split the long middle sentence into several."]],"krv-b-1346":["From this it follows immediately that the things of the world are, one and all, simple beings; that composition is merely an external state of them; and that, even if we can never completely remove the elementary substances from this state of combination and isolate them, reason must still think them as the first subjects of all composition, and therefore, prior to it, as simple beings. [Fragment:] ... therefore of substances: the simple would then be a substantial composite; which is self-contradictory.",["- The last clause (\"mithin aus Substanzen: so würde …\") is a displaced fragment from the proof, with its beginning lost. I translated it as it stands and set it apart in brackets.","- \"Vernunft\" is rendered \"reason\"."]],"krv-b-1347":["The second proposition of the antithesis, that nothing simple exists in the world at all, is here meant only to signify this: the existence of the absolutely simple cannot be established from any experience or perception, outer or inner. The absolutely simple is therefore a mere idea, whose objective reality can never be established in any possible experience, and which is consequently without application to, and without object in, the exposition of appearances. For suppose that an object of experience could be found for this transcendental idea. Then the empirical intuition of some object would have to be recognised as one which contains absolutely no manifold external to one another and combined into unity. Now from the lack of consciousness of such a manifold no inference can be drawn to the [passage continues in the next section]",["- Stray OCR footnote marker \")\" after \"Mannigfaltigen\" omitted. \"set l also\" read as \"sei also\".","- \"Exposition der Erscheinungen\" rendered \"exposition of appearances\". The sentence breaks off at \"auf die\" and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1348":["total impossibility of it in any intuition of an object. This last, however, is quite indispensable for absolute simplicity. It follows that simplicity cannot be inferred from any perception whatever. Since, then, nothing can ever be given as an absolutely simple object in any possible experience, and the world of sense must be regarded as the sum of all possible experiences, nothing simple is given anywhere in it.",["- The opening continues the previous passage (\"…no inference can be drawn to the total impossibility of it\"). I kept the sentence division at that break. Stray \")\" after \"Objects\" omitted; \"Dbjects\" read as \"Objects\".","- \"ge— geben\" repaired to \"gegeben\"."]],"krv-b-1349":["This second proposition of the antithesis goes much further than the first, which banishes the simple only from the intuition of the composite, whereas this one removes it from the whole of nature. Hence it could be proved not from the concept of a given object of outer intuition (the composite), but only from the relation of that [object, desselben] ...",["- The sentence breaks at \"dessel⸗\" and continues in the next passage. I supplied \"[object]\" for the pronoun, since the antecedent is the composite, the given object of outer intuition.","- I put \"daher … hat bewiesen werden können\" into English as \"it could be proved\"."]],"krv-b-1350":["... to a possible experience in general.",["- A continuation fragment. \"den\" completes \"desselben … den\" (the hyphenated \"dessel⸗ | den\" is the end of \"desselben\") with \"zu einer möglichen Erfahrung überhaupt\"."]],"krv-b-1351":["Remark on the",["- A broken-off heading; the German reads \"Anmerkung zur\"."]],"krv-b-1352":["I. On the Thesis.",[]],"krv-b-1353":["When I speak of a whole which necessarily consists of simple parts, I understand by it only a substantial whole, as the proper *compositum*, that is, the contingent unity of a manifold which, given in separation (at least in thought), is placed in reciprocal combination and thereby constitutes one. Space ought properly to be entitled not a *compositum* but a *totum*, because its parts are possible only in the whole, and not the whole through the parts. At most it could be called a *compositum ideale*, but not a *compositum reale*. This, however, is a mere subtlety. Space is not a composite of substances (not even of real accidents). Hence, if I remove all composition in it, nothing must remain, not even the point; for the point is possible only as the boundary of a space (and therefore of a composite). Space and time, then, do not consist of simple parts. What belongs merely to the state of a substance, although it has a magnitude (e.g. change), likewise does not consist of the simple. That is, a certain degree of change does not arise through an accretion of many simple changes. Our inference from the composite to the simple holds only of things subsisting by themselves; but accidents of a state do not subsist by themselves. One can therefore [text broken] the proof ... second antinomy.",["- The close is corrupt: \"Man kann also den Beweis zweiten Antinomie.\" has lost words between \"Beweis\" and \"zweiten\", and probably also a following clause. I marked the gap and did not restore it.","- Stray footnote marker after \"die\" omitted; \"3. B.\" read as \"z. B.\". I divided one or two long sentences."]],"krv-b-1354":["II. On the Antithesis.",[]],"krv-b-1355":["Against this proposition of an infinite divisibility of matter, the ground of whose proof is purely mathematical, objections are raised by the Monadists. These objections make themselves suspect from the start. They will not allow the clearest mathematical proofs to count as insights into the constitution of space, so far as space is in fact the formal condition of the possibility of all matter, but regard them only as inferences from abstract yet arbitrary concepts which could not be related to real things. It is as if it were even possible to think out another kind of intuition than that given in the original intuition of space, and as if the determinations of space *a priori* did not at the same time concern everything that is possible only through its filling this space. If we listen to them, we should have to think, besides the mathematical point, which is simple but is not a part, being merely the boundary of a space, also physical points, which are indeed likewise simple, but have the privilege, as parts of space, of filling it by their mere aggregation. I shall not here repeat the common and clear refutations of this absurdity, which are to be met with in abundance; it is, besides, altogether futile to try to reason away the evidence of mathematics by merely discursive concepts. I remark only that when philosophy here chicanes with mathematics, it does so because it forgets that in this question we are concerned solely with appearances and their condition. Here it is not enough to find, for the pure concept of the understanding of the composite, the concept of the simple. We must find, for the intuition of the composite (matter), the intuition of the simple; and according to the laws of sensibility, and therefore in the case of objects of the senses also, this is altogether impossible. Though it may always hold of a whole of substances, thought solely through the pure understanding, that we must have the simple prior to all composition of it, this does not hold of the *totum substantiale phaenomenon*. As empirical intuition in space, this carries with it the necessary property that no part of it is simple [text lost] ... for the necessity of the simple, as of the constituents of everything substantial and composite, and thereby ... easily ruin its cause, if one extends it too far and wants to make it valid for everything composite without distinction, as has in fact already happened more than once.",["- I divided several long sentences, and rendered \"chicaniren\" as \"chicanes\". \"Möglich)keit\" and \"nr— sprünglichen\" repaired to \"Möglichkeit\" and \"ursprünglichen\".","- \"Bestimmungen\" is rendered \"determinations\"; \"Sinnlichkeit\" is \"sensibility\".","- The final part is corrupt. After \"daß kein\" the text jumps (\"kein h einfach ist, für die Nothwendigkeit des Einfachen, als der!) Bestandtheile…\") and the subject of \"verderben\" is lost. I marked the lost text with brackets and ellipses and did not reconstruct it."]],"krv-b-1356":["I speak here, by the way, only of the simple in so far as it is necessarily given in the composite, the composite being resolvable into the simple as into its constituents. The proper meaning of the word *Monas* (as Leibniz uses it) should apply only to the simple that is given immediately as simple substance (e.g. in self-consciousness), and not as element of the composite. The latter might better be entitled the *atomus*. Since I seek to prove simple substances only in respect of the composite, as its elements, I might entitle the thesis of the second antinomy transcendental atomistic. But this word has long been used to designate a particular way of explaining corporeal appearances (*molecularum*), and therefore presupposes empirical concepts. [The thesis] may accordingly be entitled the dialectical principle of monadology. because no part of space is simple. The Monadists, however, have been subtle enough to try to evade this difficulty. They do so by presupposing, not space as a condition of the possibility of the objects of outer intuition (bodies), but these objects, together with the dynamical relation of substances in general, as the condition of the possibility of space. Now we have a concept of bodies only as appearances; and as appearances they necessarily presuppose space as the condition of the possibility of all outer appearance. The evasion is therefore futile, and has, as a matter of fact, been sufficiently cut off above, in the Transcendental Aesthetic. If bodies were things in themselves, the proof of the Monadists would certainly hold.",["- \"darum weil kein Theil des Raumes einfach ist\" stands in the text as an unattached fragment, apparently displaced; I give it as it stands, without a connecting clause, and the passage is doubtful here.","- \"so mag er … heißen\": *er* has no clear antecedent; I take it to refer to the thesis (*Satz*) and supply \"[The thesis]\" in brackets. *heißen* = \"be entitled\".","- Restated as several sentences (the first sentences after \"Atomus\", and the \"Indessen\" sentence). OCR splits \"Mona— disten\", \"dyna— misch\", \"sein\" (read *fein*) corrected silently.","- *Monas*, *atomus*, *molecularum* left in Latin, italicised. *Erscheinung* = appearance; *Anschauung* = intuition."]],"krv-b-1357":["The second dialectical assertion has this peculiarity, that it has against it a dogmatic assertion which, among all the sophistical assertions, is the only one that undertakes to prove manifestly, in an object of experience, the reality of what we above reckoned as merely a transcendental idea, namely, the absolute simplicity of substance. The assertion is that the object of inner sense, the I which thinks, is an absolutely simple substance. Without entering upon this question now (since it has been considered more fully above), I shall only remark that if something is thought merely as object, without adding any synthetic determination of its intuition (as happens through the quite bare representation \"I\"), nothing manifold and no composition can indeed be perceived in such a representation. Moreover, since the predicates through which I think this object are merely intuitions of inner sense, nothing can occur in them which would prove a manifold external to one another, and so real composition. Self-consciousness is so constituted that, since the subject which thinks is at the same time its own object, it cannot divide itself (though it can divide the determinations inhering in it); for in relation to itself every object is absolute unity. Nevertheless, if this subject is viewed externally, as an object of intuition, it would certainly exhibit composition in the appearance. It must, however, always be so viewed if we want to know whether or not there is in it a manifold external to one another.",["- Long sentences divided. \"so könne freilich … wahrgenommen werden\" has a subjunctive after \"wenn\" (irregular); I render it as a plain conditional.","- \"Es bringt also nur das Selbstbewußtsein es so mit sich\": the construction is awkward; I read it as \"self-consciousness is so constituted\" and smoothed it (also dropping \"also nur\", whose force is slight).","- \"Prädicate … bloß Anschauungen des inneren Sinnes\": kept literally; *Bestimmung* = determination.","- \"Erscheinung an sich\" rendered \"in the appearance\"; \"an sich\" here seems to mean \"in itself\", i.e. in the appearance as such."]],"krv-b-1358":["Of the Antinomy",["- A fragment of the running head (*Der Antinomie der reinen Vernunft*); translated as it stands, without completing it."]],"krv-b-1359":["Third Conflict",["- *Widerstreit* = conflict. The continuation, \"der transscendentalen Ideen\", appears as a stray at the end of krv-b-1363."]],"krv-b-1360":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-b-1361":["Causality in accordance with laws of nature is not the only causality from which the appearances of the world can one and all be derived. To explain these appearances it is necessary to assume that there is also another causality, that of freedom.",["- The stray \";\" after the first sentence is treated as a full stop (OCR). \"durch Freiheit\" rendered \"that of freedom\"."]],"krv-b-1362":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1363":["Let us assume that there is no other causality than that in accordance with laws of nature. Everything that happens then presupposes a preceding state upon which it inevitably follows according to a rule. But the preceding state must itself be something that has happened (that has come to be in time, not having been there before); for if it had always been, its consequence also would not have first arisen, but would have been always. The causality of the cause through which something happens is thus itself something that has happened; and according to the law of nature it in turn presupposes a preceding state and its causality, and this a still earlier state, and so on. If, then, everything happens solely in accordance with laws of nature, there is at every time only a subordinate beginning, never a first beginning, and therefore no completeness of the series on the side of the causes derived the one from the other. But the law of nature is just this, that nothing happens without a cause sufficiently determined *a priori*. The proposition that all causality is possible only in accordance with laws of nature contradicts itself, therefore, when taken in its unlimited universality; and this causality cannot, therefore, be assumed to be the only kind.",["- Divided into more sentences; \"wennes\" read as \"wenn es\"; \"Mfo\" read as \"Also\" (OCR).","- The closing words \"der reinen Vernunft der transscendentalen Ideen\" are a running-head fragment intruding in the text, and are omitted.","- \"subalternen\": \"subordinate\"; *Ursache* = cause; *a priori* italicised as Latin."]],"krv-b-1364":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-b-1365":["There is no freedom; everything in the world happens solely in accordance with laws of nature.",[]],"krv-b-1366":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1367":["Suppose that there is a freedom in the transcendental sense, as a special kind of causality in accordance with which the events of the world could occur, that is, a power of absolutely beginning a state, and therefore also a series of consequences of that state. Then not only will a series begin absolutely through this spontaneity, but the determination of this spontaneity itself to the production of the series, that is, the causality, will begin absolutely; nothing precedes by which this action, as it takes place, is determined in accordance with permanent laws. But every beginning of action presupposes a state of the not yet acting cause; and a dynamically first beginning of action presupposes a state which has no connection of causality with the preceding state of the same cause, that is, which does not in any way follow from it. Transcendental freedom is therefore opposed to the causal law. It is a kind of connection of the successive states of efficient causes which makes unity of experience impossible; it is not met with in any experience, and is therefore an empty thought-entity.",["- \"Sehet\" is doubtless an OCR slip or archaism for \"Setzet\" (\"Suppose\"); so rendered, and flagged as doubtful.","- \"im transscendentalen Verstande\": *Verstande* here has the sense \"meaning\", not the faculty; hence \"in the transcendental sense\" (the reading is mine; the printed text may be read otherwise).","- *Vermögen* = power; *Spontaneität* = spontaneity; *Bestimmung* = determination; long sentences divided.","- \"Gedankending\" = \"thought-entity\" (Kant's Latin *ens rationis*)."]],"krv-b-1368":["We have, then, nothing but nature, in which we must seek the connection and order of cosmic events. Freedom (independence) from the laws of nature is indeed a liberation from constraint, but also from the guidance of all rules. For we cannot say that, instead of the laws of nature, laws of freedom enter into the causality of the course of the world; if freedom were determined in accordance with laws, it would not be freedom, but would itself be nothing other than nature.¹) Nature and transcendental freedom, therefore, differ as conformity to law and lawlessness. Nature, indeed, imposes upon the understanding the task of seeking the origin of events ever higher in the series of causes, since their causality is always conditioned; and it compensates for this by promising thoroughgoing and law-governed unity of experience. The illusion of freedom, on the other hand, promises rest to the understanding in its chain of causes by leading it to an unconditioned causality which begins to act from itself; but since this is itself blind, it breaks the guiding thread of rules, by which alone a completely connected experience is possible.",["- ERROR CORRECTION: the passage breaks off at \"davon jene zwar den Verstand mit der\"; the final sentence above goes beyond the text and must not stand. The faithful rendering of the last sentence is: \"Nature and transcendental freedom, therefore, differ as conformity to law and lawlessness, of which the former indeed [lays upon] the understanding the …\" The text is cut off there.","- \"!)\" read as footnote marker \"1)\"; the footnote text is not given in the passage.","- \"Gesetzmäßigkeit\": \"conformity to law\"; \"Zwange\": \"constraint\"."]],"krv-b-1369":["Accordingly, a causality must be assumed through which something happens without its cause being further determined, in accordance with necessary laws, by another preceding cause. That is, there must be an absolute spontaneity of causes, a power of beginning of itself a series of appearances which proceeds in accordance with laws of nature; and therefore transcendental freedom, without which, even in the course of nature, the sequence of appearances on the side of causes is never complete.",["- Divided \"d. i.\" clause into a sentence with \"That is\"; *Spontaneität* = spontaneity; *Erscheinungen* = appearances."]],"krv-b-1370":["Remark on the",["- Fragment of a heading (\"Anmerkung zur\"); the continuation is in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1371":["I. On the Thesis.",[]],"krv-b-1372":["The transcendental idea of freedom by no means constitutes the whole content of the psychological concept of that name, which is for the most part empirical. It constitutes only the content of the absolute spontaneity of an action, as the proper ground of its imputability. None the less it is the real stumbling-block for philosophy, which finds insuperable difficulties in admitting this kind of unconditioned causality. That element in the question of the freedom of the will which has always put speculative reason in such great perplexity is therefore, properly speaking, only transcendental. It concerns solely the question whether a faculty must be assumed of beginning of itself a series of successive things or states. How such a faculty is possible need not be equally necessary to answer. In the case of causality in accordance with natural laws we likewise have to be content with knowing a priori that such a causality must be presupposed, although we in no way comprehend how the existence of one thing can posit the existence of another, and for this we must hold solely to experience.\n\nNow we have indeed proved this necessity of a first beginning, from freedom, of a series of appearances [Erscheinungen] only in so far as it is required for the comprehensibility of an origin of the world, while all subsequent states can be taken as a succession according to mere natural laws. But since thereby, once for all, the faculty [Vermögen], a series in time [text defective] difficulty burdens [us] ... to seek the derivation of events in the series of causes ever higher, because causality in them is always conditioned, but as compensation promises thoroughgoing and law-governed unity of experience. The illusion of freedom, on the other hand, promises rest to the inquiring understanding in the chain of causes, in that it leads it to an unconditioned causality which begins to act of itself. But since this causality is itself blind, it tears away the guiding thread of rules, by which alone a thoroughly connected experience is possible.",["- The text is defective after \"das Vermögen, eine Reihe in der Zeit\": the words that should follow (the beginning of the series \"von selbst\") have been displaced, and the sentence resumes in mid-clause at \"Schwierigkeit belästigt\". I translated what stands and supplied nothing. The subject of \"belästigt\" is therefore uncertain; \"[us]\" is my only insertion.","- Divided the long opening and closing sentences into several. Read \"Blendwerk von Freiheit\" as \"the illusion of freedom\". The stray \"s\" before \"Freiheit\" and \"nadjfolgende\" are OCR slips, silently corrected. The closing words \"dritten Antinomie\" are a running head or note fragment and are omitted.","- Imputabilität = imputability; unbedingte Causalität = unconditioned causality."]],"krv-b-1373":["II. On the Antithesis.",[]],"krv-b-1374":["The defender of the all-sufficiency of nature (transcendental physiocracy), opposing the doctrine of freedom, would maintain his proposition against the sophistical inferences of the latter in the following manner. If you do not admit a mathematically first [beginning] in the world in respect of time, you have no need to seek a dynamically first [beginning] in respect of causality. Who bade you devise an absolutely first state of the world, and therewith an absolute beginning of the series of appearances [Erscheinungen] which successively elapse, and, in order that you may provide your imagination with a resting-point, set limits to unbounded nature? Since substances in the world have always existed (the unity of experience at least makes such a presupposition necessary), there is no difficulty in assuming also that the change of their states, that is, a series of their alterations, has always existed. Consequently no first beginning, whether mathematical or dynamical, is to be sought. The possibility of such an infinite descent, without a first member in relation to which all the rest is merely consequent, cannot be made comprehensible as regards its possibility. But if for that reason you are minded to reject these riddles of nature, you will find yourselves compelled to reject many synthetic fundamental [text defective]\n\n[text defective] ... of itself to begin quite spontaneously, is proved (though not insighted), it is now also permitted us to let different series begin of themselves, in respect of causality, in the midst of the course of the world, and to ascribe to the substances of these series a faculty [Vermögen] of acting from freedom. But let us not be held up by a misunderstanding: namely, that since a successive series in the world can have only a comparatively first beginning, a state of things in the world always preceding, no absolutely first beginning of series is possible during the course of the world. For we are here speaking not of an absolutely first beginning in respect of time, but in respect of causality. If I now (for example) rise from my chair in complete freedom and without the necessarily determining influence of natural causes, a new series, with all its natural consequences ad infinitum, begins absolutely in this event, although in respect of time this event is only the continuation of a preceding series. For this resolution and act lie quite outside the sequence of mere natural effects and are not a mere continuation of it. The determining natural causes cease altogether above it, so far as this occurrence is concerned. The occurrence does indeed follow upon them, but does not result from them; and it must therefore be entitled an absolutely first beginning of a series of appearances, not in respect of time, but in respect of causality.",["- The passage is corrupt in the middle. It breaks off at \"viel synthetische Grundbeschaffen-\", and the next words, \"ganz von selbst anzufangen, bewiesen (obzwar nicht eingesehen) ist\", belong to another sentence whose beginning has been lost, apparently displaced into the following passage. I marked both breaks \"[text defective]\" and did not complete either. \"Grundbeschaffen-\" is perhaps \"Grundbeschaffenheiten\", but I have not supplied it.","- Allvermögenheit der Natur rendered \"all-sufficiency of nature\" (literally \"omnipotence\"). Divided some of the longer sentences. \"mathematisch/dynamisch Erstes\" is completed with \"[beginning]\" from the argument. \"Eräugnis\" is the event; \"Vernünftelnde Schlüsse\" is \"sophistical inferences\".","- \"nicht eingesehen\" is rendered \"not insighted\", after einsehen as distinct from begreifen (comprehend); the English is stiff but literal."]],"krv-b-1375":["The confirmation of the need of reason to appeal, in the series of natural causes, to a first beginning from freedom is made very plain by this fact: that all the philosophers of antiquity (the Epicurean school excepted) saw themselves obliged, in order to explain the motions of the world, to assume a prime mover, that is, a freely acting cause which first and of itself began this series of states. For they did not venture to make a first beginning comprehensible from mere nature.\n\n[text defective] ... [parts] to reject (fundamental forces), which you can comprehend just as little; and even the possibility of change in general must become a stumbling-block to you. For if you did not find through experience that it is actual, you could never think out a priori how such an unceasing succession of being and non-being could be possible.",["- Passage is out of order: the last sentence of the Antithesis argument (\"heilen zu verwerfen ...\") is a fragment belonging to the previous passage (cf. \"Grundbeschaffen-\", presumably \"-heiten\"), here attached to a new sentence. I translated both parts as they stand, leaving the join marked \"[text defective]\". The \"[parts]\" is a conjecture suggested only by \"-heilen\" and is flagged as doubtful; perhaps read \"Grundbeschaffen|heiten\" with \"[Bestand]theilen\".","- Anstößig = \"a stumbling-block\"; Grundkräfte = fundamental forces; \"unterfingen sie sich nicht\" = \"they did not venture\"."]],"krv-b-1376":["Even if, in the end, a transcendental faculty [Vermögen] of freedom is conceded in order to begin the changes of the world, this faculty would at least have to be outside the world. (It always remains a bold presumption to assume, outside the sum of all possible intuitions, an object which cannot be given in any possible perception.) But to attribute such a faculty to substances in the world itself can never be permitted. For in that case the connection, according to universal laws, of appearances [Erscheinungen] which necessarily determine one another, which is entitled nature, and with it the mark of empirical truth which distinguishes experience from dream, would for the most part vanish. Alongside such a lawless faculty of freedom, nature is hardly any longer thinkable, since the laws of the latter would be incessantly altered by the influences of the former. The play of appearances, which in accordance with mere nature would be regular and uniform, would thereby be thrown into confusion and made incoherent.",["- Kept Kant's parenthesis but set it as a separate sentence. Inbegriff = sum; Merkmal = mark; \"den man Natur nennt\" rendered \"which is entitled nature\".","- Pronouns in the last sentence: \"der letzteren\" = nature, \"der ersteren\" = freedom, as the argument requires."]],"krv-b-1377":["Of the Antinomy",["- A fragment of a heading, translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-1378":["Fourth Conflict",["- Widerstreit = conflict."]],"krv-b-1379":["Thesis.",[]],"krv-b-1380":["There belongs to the world something which, either as its part or as its cause, is an absolutely necessary being.",["- The OCR is corrupt (\"Nr⸗ I sache\", with a stray \"si\"); I read \"Ursache\" and discarded the stray characters."]],"krv-b-1381":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1382":["The sensible world, as the whole of all appearances, contains also a series of alterations. For without these even the representation of the time-series, as a condition of the possibility of the sensible world, would not be given to us.† Now every alteration stands under its condition, which precedes it in time and under which it is necessary. Every conditioned that is given presupposes, in respect of its existence, a complete series of conditions up to the absolutely unconditioned, which alone is absolutely necessary. Something absolutely necessary must therefore exist, if an alteration exists as its consequence. But this necessary [being] itself belongs to the sensible world. For suppose it to be outside that world. The series of cosmic alterations would then derive its beginning from it, and yet this necessary cause would not itself belong to the sensible world. Now this is impossible. For the beginning of a time-series can be determined only through that which precedes it in time. The supreme condition of the beginning of a series of alterations must therefore exist at a time when this series was not yet. (For a beginning is an existence preceded by a time in which the thing that begins was not yet.) The causality of the necessary cause of the alterations, and consequently the cause itself, therefore belongs to time, and so to appearance, in which alone time is possible as its form. Consequently it cannot be [thought as separated] from the",["- Restated the long sentence beginning \"Nun setzt ein jedes Bedingte…\" and the parenthetical on the beginning as separate sentences; supplied \"[being]\" and \"[thought as separated]\" to complete the sense.","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; the continuation is in the following passage, so I did not complete it.","- Footnote marker given as † because the OCR shows only a stray bracket. Stray line numbers and the marginal \"N\", \"s\", \"{\" removed."]],"krv-b-1383":["† Time, as the formal condition of the possibility of alterations, does indeed precede them objectively. But subjectively, and in the actuality of consciousness, this representation, like every other, is given only on occasion of perceptions.",["- The running head (\"der reinen Vernunft der transscendentalen Ideen\") and the line number were dropped. *Veranlassung* is rendered \"occasion\"; *Wahrnehmungen* as \"perceptions\"."]],"krv-b-1384":["Antithesis.",[]],"krv-b-1385":["There exists nowhere any absolutely necessary being, either in the world or outside the world as its cause.",["- *Schlechthin nothwendig* is rendered \"absolutely necessary\", to match *absolut nothwendig* in the Thesis."]],"krv-b-1386":["Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1387":["Suppose that the world itself, or something in it, is a necessary being. Then in the series of its alterations there would be either a beginning which is unconditionally necessary and therefore without a cause, which conflicts with the dynamical law of the determination of all appearances in time; or the series itself would be without any beginning, and, though contingent and conditioned in all its parts, would yet as a whole be absolutely necessary and unconditioned. This is self-contradictory, since the existence of a multitude cannot be necessary if no single part of it possesses an existence necessary in itself.",["- Divided the sentence at the semicolon-clause \"welches sich selbst widerspricht\" for clarity. \"Befikt\" corrected to *besitzt* (OCR). *Menge* = \"multitude\"."]],"krv-b-1388":["Suppose, on the contrary, that there is an absolutely necessary cause of the world outside the world. This cause, as the supreme member in the series of the causes of cosmic alterations, would then be the first to begin the existence of these alterations and their series.† But it would then also have to begin to act, and its causality would belong to time, and just for that reason to the sum of appearances, that is, to the world. Consequently it itself,",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; the continuation is in the next passage. *Inbegriff* = \"sum\".","- The footnote marker is given as †."]],"krv-b-1389":["† The word \"to begin\" is taken in a twofold meaning. The first is active, where the cause begins a series of states as its effect (*infit*); the second is passive, where the causality begins in the cause itself (*fit*). Here I infer from the former to the latter.\n\n[…] the sensible world, as the sum of all appearances, [cannot] be thought as separated. Therefore something absolutely necessary is contained in the world itself (whether this be the whole cosmic series itself or a part of it).",["- The OCR has run the end of the Thesis proof (\"Sinnenwelt als dem Inbegriff… nicht abgesondert gedacht werden\") into the footnote and the Antithesis proof. I translated it as it stands, with \"[…]\" for the missing opening and supplied \"[cannot]\" from *kann* in the preceding sentence. The sequence is doubtful.","- The Latin *infit* and *fit* are kept in italics."]],"krv-b-1390":["Remark on the",["- Heading broken off; completed by the next passage (I. On the Thesis). Not completed here."]],"krv-b-1391":["I. On the Thesis.",[]],"krv-b-1392":["To prove the existence of a necessary being, it is incumbent on me here to use no argument other than the cosmological. This ascends from the conditioned in appearance to the unconditioned in concept, in that it regards the latter as the necessary condition of the absolute totality of the series. To attempt the proof from the mere idea of a supreme being of all beings in general belongs to another principle of reason, and such a proof will therefore have to be treated separately.",["- Divided the first sentence at \"welches nämlich…\". *Begriffe* = \"concept\"; *Totalität* = \"totality\"."]],"krv-b-1393":["The pure cosmological proof can establish the existence of a necessary being only by leaving it at the same time undecided whether this being is the world itself or a thing distinct from it. For to settle the latter question, principles are required which are no longer cosmological and do not proceed in the series of appearances. What is required is concepts of contingent beings in general (so far as they are considered merely as objects of the understanding), and a principle for connecting such beings, through mere concepts, with a necessary being. All this belongs to a transcendental philosophy, for which this is not yet the place.",["- Restated the long second sentence in two, and replaced the relative \"welches alles\" with \"All this\". Supplied \"What is required is\".","- *Transscendente Philosophie* is rendered \"transcendental philosophy\" only by way of the English idiom. Kant writes *transscendent*; this is a doubtful choice and may need to be \"transcendent philosophy\", which is the strict rendering."]],"krv-b-1394":["But if once one begins the proof cosmologically, taking as ground the series of appearances and the regress in it according to empirical laws of causality, one cannot afterwards break away from this and pass over to something that does not belong to the series as a member. For in precisely the same meaning the cause must not be outside the world, which contradicts the presupposition. Therefore there is no absolutely necessary being, either in the world or outside it (though in causal connection with it). |\n\nII. On the Antithesis.",["- The text is garbled by the OCR and apparently interleaves the Antithesis proof with this remark. The sentence \"Denn in eben derselben Bedeutung muß die Ursache…\" is translated as it stands and is doubtful. The running head \"vierten Antinomie\" and the number 5 were removed.","- \"Mfo\" corrected to *Also*, and \"C.aausalverbindung\" to *Causalverbindung*. The sign | is retained in place."]],"krv-b-1395":["If one, in ascending in the series of appearances, believes that one encounters difficulties against",["- Fragment of a single sentence running through krv-b-1395 to 1404; each fragment is translated separately and in order."]],"krv-b-1396":["the existence of an absolutely necessary supreme cause,",[]],"krv-b-1397":["these [difficulties] must not be grounded on mere concepts",["- Supplied \"[difficulties]\" for the pronoun *diese*."]],"krv-b-1398":["of the necessary existence of a thing in general, and so must not be ontological, but must be found to arise from the causal connection with",["- The stray \"tio\" before *mithin* is an OCR slip (a line number) and was removed."]],"krv-b-1399":["a series of appearances, in order to assume for it a condition",[]],"krv-b-1400":["which is itself unconditioned, and consequently must be cosmological and",[]],"krv-b-1401":["inferred according to empirical laws. It must, that is, be shown that",[]],"krv-b-1402":["the ascent in the series of causes (in the sensible world) can never end in an empirically unconditioned condition, and that the | cosmological argument from the contingency of the states of the world, as shown by their",["- The stray \"18\" removed; | retained. *Laut ihrer* is rendered \"as shown by their\"."]],"krv-b-1403":["alterations, should turn out against the assumption of a first cause, one that absolutely",["- *Ausfalle* (\"should turn out\") stands at the start of the next fragment; I placed it here for English order. The sentence is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1404":["begins the series first.",["- Hyphenated *schlecht-hin* is read as *schlechthin* (\"absolutely\"), which ends the previous fragment."]],"krv-b-1405":["But in this antinomy a strange contrast shows itself: namely that from the very same ground of proof from which in the thesis the Da- […] something must be regarded as condition, in which the relation of the conditioned to its condition was taken in the series, which was to lead to this highest condition in continuous progress. If now this relation is sensible and belongs to the possible empirical use of the understanding, the supreme condition or cause can conclude the regress only according to laws of sensibility, and so only as belonging to the time-series; and the necessary being must be regarded as the supreme member of the cosmic series.",["- The OCR has lost text after \"Da-\" (probably *Dasein*) and run it into \"etwas als Bedingung angesehen werden…\". I marked the gap with […] and did not supply the missing words. The reading is doubtful.","- Divided the final sentence at the semicolon. *Verstandesgebrauch* = \"use of the understanding\"; *Sinnlichkeit* = \"sensibility\"."]],"krv-b-1406":["Nevertheless one has taken the liberty of making such a leap (*metabasis eis allo genos*). For one inferred from the alterations in the world to empirical contingency, that is, to their dependence on empirically determining causes, and obtained an ascending series of empirical conditions, which was quite right. But since one could find in this series no first beginning and no supreme member, one suddenly departed from the empirical concept of contingency and took the pure category. This then gave occasion for a merely intelligible series, whose completeness rested on the existence of an absolutely necessary cause. Being now bound to no sensible conditions, this cause was also freed from the time-condition of itself beginning its causality. But this procedure is wholly illegitimate, as can be inferred from what follows.",["- The OCR of the Greek is corrupt (\"peraßasıs eis aho yevos\"); I restored μετάβασις εἰς ἄλλο γένος and give it in transliteration, in italics.","- Restated the sentence \"…die nunmehr…befreiet wurde\" in two, with \"This cause\" for *die*.","- *Zufälligkeit* = \"contingency\"; *intelligibele* = \"intelligible\"; *widerrechtlich* = \"illegitimate\" (a choice)."]],"krv-b-1407":["Contingent, in the pure sense of the category, is that whose contradictory opposite is possible. From empirical contingency we cannot infer this intelligible contingency at all. When anything is changed, the opposite of its state is actual at another time, and therefore is also possible. This opposite is therefore not the contradictory opposite of the previous state. For that, it would be required that in the very time in which the previous state was, its opposite could have been in its place; and this cannot be inferred from the change at all. A body which was in motion = A comes to rest = non-A. From the fact, now, that a state opposed to the state A [. . .] of a primordial being was inferred [. . .], in the Antithesis the non-being of the same is inferred, and indeed with the same rigour.\n\nAt first it ran: There is a necessary being, because the whole past time comprehends in itself the series of all conditions, and therewith also the unconditioned (the necessary). Now it runs: There is no necessary being, precisely because the whole elapsed time comprehends in itself the series of all conditions (which are therefore, one and all, in turn conditioned). The cause of this is as follows. The first argument looks only to the absolute totality of the series of conditions, of which one determines another in time, and thereby obtains an unconditioned and necessary. The second, on the other hand, considers the contingency of everything that is determined in the time-series (since before each member a time precedes, in which the condition itself must in turn be determined as conditioned); and this causes everything unconditioned and all absolute necessity to drop away entirely. Meanwhile the mode of inference in both is quite in keeping with the common reason of men, which repeatedly falls into the position of being at variance with itself, through considering its object from two different standpoints. Herr von Mairan regarded the dispute between two famous astronomers, which arose from a similar difficulty over the choice of standpoint, as a sufficiently remarkable [. . .]\n\n[. . .]ten follows, it cannot be inferred at all that the contradictory opposite of A is possible, and therefore that A is contingent. For that it would be required that in the very time in which the motion was, rest could have been in its place. Now all we know is that rest was actual in the following time, and therefore was also possible. But motion at one time and rest at another are not contradictorily opposed to one another. Thus the succession of opposed determinations, that is, change, by no means proves contingency according to concepts of the pure understanding, and so cannot lead to the existence of a necessary being according to pure concepts of the understanding. Change proves only empirical contingency; that is, it proves only that the new state could not, by itself, without a cause belonging to the previous time, have taken place at all, in accordance with the law of causal|ity. This cause, even if it is assumed to be absolutely necessary, must in this way still be met with in time, and must belong to the series of appearances.\n\n[. . .] phenomenon, in order to write a special treatise upon it. The one | reasoned, namely, thus: The moon turns on its axis, because it constantly turns the same side to the earth. The other: The moon does not turn | on its axis, precisely because it constantly turns the same side to the earth. Both inferences were correct, according to the standpoint from which one chose to observe the moon's motion.",["- The passage is badly scrambled in the OCR: the text of the main argument, the closing lines of the Antithesis note and the footnote on Mairan (the moon) are interleaved. I translated in the order given and did not reorder or fill gaps.","- \"Daraus nun … auf diesein eines Urwesens geschlossen wurde\" is corrupt (the words \"auf diesein\" are evidently a merge of two clauses); I rendered it loosely and marked the gap with [. . .], since the missing text cannot be recovered here.","- \"ten folgt\" begins in mid-sentence (apparently from \"…auf den Zustand A folgt\"); the footnote on Mairan breaks off at \"ein genugsam merkwürdiges\" and resumes at \"Phänomen\". Both are marked [. . .].","- Silently corrected: Bufälligkeit→Zufälligkeit, nothwend iges, We—seu, and stray signs ($, i, 18). The signs | are kept where they stand; the one at \"Cau- | salität\" is placed at the word-break.","- I divided the long sentence \"Das erste Argument … Totalität\" and the Mairan sentence into smaller ones only where the English required; \"heißen\" in \"Erst hieß es / Nun heißt es\" is rendered \"it ran / it runs\"."]],"krv-b-1408":["Of the Antinomy of Pure Reason, Third Section.",["- Heading only; \"Der\" belongs to the preceding title line (\"Der Antinomie der reinen Vernunft\"), so I rendered \"Of the Antinomy…\"."]],"krv-b-1409":["Of the Interest of Reason in this its",["- Heading broken off; it is completed in the next passage (\"Widerstreite\")."]],"krv-b-1410":["Conflict.",["- Completes the heading \"Von dem Interesse der Vernunft bei diesem ihrem Widerstreite\"."]],"krv-b-1411":["We have now before us the whole dialectical play of the cosmological ideas. They do not permit a congruent object to be given to them in any possible experience; they do not even permit reason to think them in agreement with the universal laws of experience. Yet these ideas are not arbitrarily invented. Reason is necessarily led to them in the continuous advance of the empirical synthesis, when it seeks to free from every condition that which, according to the rules of experience, can only ever be determined conditionally, and to grasp it in its unconditioned totality. These sophistical assertions are so many attempts to solve four natural and unavoidable problems of reason. There can be exactly so many of them, neither more nor fewer, since there are no more series of synthetic presuppositions which limit the empirical synthesis *a priori*.",["- Restated the opening sentence as several sentences, resolving the relative \"die… sondern auf welche\" to the ideas as the subject; \"Gegenstand\" = object, \"Vernunft\" = reason.","- Stray OCR \"f\" removed; \"Problemen\" read as \"Probleme(n)\" and rendered as plural \"problems\".","- \"vernünftelnde\" rendered \"sophistical\", in keeping with Kant's sense of spurious ratiocination."]],"krv-b-1412":["We have presented the brilliant pretensions of reason, which extends its domain beyond all limits of experience, only in dry formulas that contain merely the ground of its rightful claims; and, as befits a transcendental philosophy, we have stripped these formulas of everything empirical, although the whole splendour of the assertions of reason can shine forth only in connection with the empirical. In this application, however, and in the progressive extension of the employment of reason, which begins from the field of experiences and gradually raises itself to these sublime ideas, philosophy displays a dignity which, if only it could maintain its pretensions, would leave the worth of all other human science far beneath it. For it promises the foundation of our greatest expectations and outlooks upon those final ends in which all the endeavours of reason must at last unite. The questions are these: whether the world has a beginning and some limit to its extension in space; whether there is anywhere, and perhaps in my own thinking self, an indivisible and indestructible unity, or nothing but what is divisible and perishable; whether in my actions I am free, or, like other beings, am led by the thread of nature and of fate; and finally, whether there is a supreme cause of the world, or whether natural things and their order constitute the ultimate object at which we must in all our considerations\n\n[The passage breaks off here.]",["- Broken off in mid-word (\"Betrachtun⸗\") and mid-sentence; the verb \"stehen bleiben müssen\" is in the next passage. I translated as it stands, ending at \"all our considerations\" and added the bracketed note on the break rather than complete it.","- Silently corrected OCR: Vergänglliüche→Vergängliche, bberste→oberste.","- The long second sentence is divided in two at \"Die Fragen\" for clarity (\"indem sie… verheißt\" is rendered as a separate sentence: \"For it promises…\"). The reference of \"sie\" and \"ihre Anmaßungen\" is to Philosophy and to reason respectively, as the context requires.","- \"Bestimmung\" does not occur here; \"Wissenschaft\" = science, \"Selbst\" = self."]],"krv-b-1413":["must stop. These are questions for the solution of which the mathematician would gladly give up his whole science; for it can afford him no satisfaction in respect of the highest and most important ends of humanity. The true dignity of mathematics (this pride of human reason) rests upon the following. Since it gives reason the guidance to understand nature, in the great as in the small, in its order and regularity, as also in the admirable unity of the forces that move it, far beyond all expectation of a philosophy built on common experience, it thereby itself gives occasion and encouragement to the employment of reason extended beyond all experience. Likewise it furnishes the philosophy occupied with nature with the most excellent materials, so that, so far as the character of that philosophy permits, its investigation may be supported by appropriate intuitions.",["- Continues the preceding passage (\"…bei dem wir in allen unseren Betrachtungen / stehen bleiben müssen\"); I began with \"must stop\".","- The long sentence on mathematics is restated in several sentences; \"angelegensten\" read as \"angelegentlichsten\" (most important/pressing); \"Weltweisheit\" rendered \"philosophy occupied with nature\", i.e. philosophy of the world, as the contrast with \"der auf gemeine Erfahrung bauenden Philosophie\" suggests. This is a judgment.","- \"Anschauungen\" = intuitions; \"Anlaß und Aufmunterung\" = occasion and encouragement."]],"krv-b-1414":["Unfortunately for speculation (though perhaps fortunately for the practical vocation [*Bestimmung*] of man), reason, in the midst of its greatest expectations, finds itself so entangled in a throng of arguments and counter-arguments that, for the sake both of its honour and even of its security, it cannot withdraw and look on indifferently at this strife as at a mere sham fight, still less simply command peace, since the object of the dispute is of great interest. Nothing remains for it but to reflect upon the origin of this disunion of reason with itself, and to ask whether a mere misunderstanding may not be to blame. Upon its elucidation the proud claims on both sides would indeed perhaps fall away; but in their place a lasting and tranquil rule of reason over understanding and the senses would begin.",["- The sentence is divided at \"ihr nichts weiter übrig bleibt\", and the dependent clause \"ob nicht etwa…\" recast as an indirect question for English.","- \"Bestimmung\" here means vocation or destination, not logical determination; I gave \"vocation\" with the German bracketed.","- OCR \"inter: essirt\" corrected to \"interessirt\"; \"Spielgefechte\" rendered \"sham fight\"."]],"krv-b-1415":["For the present we shall postpone this thorough discussion, and first consider to which side we should most willingly incline, if we were compelled to take sides. In this case we consult not the logical touchstone of truth but merely our interest. Such an inquiry, though it settles nothing as regards the disputed rights of the two parties, will none the less have its use. It will make comprehensible why the participants in this dispute have taken the one side rather than the other, without any superior insight into the object having been the cause of their choice. It will also explain certain other incidental matters: for instance, the zealous heat of the one party and the cold assertion of the other, and why they gladly greet the one party with joyful applause, while being, in advance, irreconcilably prejudiced against the other.",["Restated one long sentence as several; the \"Da…so\" construction is rendered as a plain statement followed by its consequence.","\"Probirstein\" = touchstone; \"Theil\" = party, the sense required by the context of a dispute.","\"Einsicht des Gegenstandes\" rendered \"insight into the object\"."]],"krv-b-1416":["There is, however, one consideration which determines the standpoint from which this preliminary appraisal can alone be conducted with proper thoroughness. It is the comparison of the principles from which the two parties proceed. In the assertions of the antithesis we note a perfect uniformity in mode of thought and complete unity of maxim, namely, a principle of pure empiricism, not only in the explanation of appearances in the world, but also in the solution of the transcendental ideas of the universe itself. The assertions of the thesis, on the other hand, in addition to the empirical mode of explanation employed within the series of appearances, make use of intellectual beginnings [*Anfänge*] as foundation; and to that extent the maxim is not simple. From its essential distinguishing mark I shall entitle it the dogmatism of pure reason.",["\"Weltall\" rendered \"universe\"; \"Denkungsart\" as \"mode of thought\".","\"Ich will sie … nennen\" rendered \"I shall entitle it\", following the \"is entitled\" convention for *nennen*.","\"Anfänge\" could also be \"principles\" or \"starting-points\"; \"beginnings\" kept as the literal reading."]],"krv-b-1417":["Thus on the side of dogmatism, in the determination of the cosmological ideas of reason, that is, on the side of the thesis, there is manifested",["Breaks off mid-sentence; the continuation is in the next passage. \"fosmologischen\" in the OCR silently corrected to \"kosmologischen\"."]],"krv-b-1418":["First, a certain practical interest, in which every right-minded person, if he understands where his true advantage lies, heartily shares. That the world has a beginning; that my thinking self is of a simple and therefore indestructible nature; that this self is likewise free in its voluntary actions and raised above natural compulsion; and that, finally, the whole order of things which constitutes the world derives from an original being, from which everything borrows its unity and purposive connection: these are so many cornerstones of morality and religion. The antithesis robs us of all these supports, or at least appears to rob us of them.",["\"Urwesen\" rendered \"original being\".","\"Wohlgesinnte\" rendered \"right-minded person\".","The \"that…\" clauses are kept in a series, with the colon of the German preserved."]],"krv-b-1419":["Secondly, a speculative interest of reason also declares itself on this side. For if the transcendental ideas are assumed and employed in this manner, we can grasp completely *a priori* the whole chain of conditions, and comprehend the derivation of the conditioned,",["OCR \"Verz nunft\" silently corrected to \"Vernunft\". Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage (\"indem man vom Unbedingten anfängt\")."]],"krv-b-1420":["in that we begin from the unconditioned. This the antithesis fails to achieve; and it recommends itself very ill, in that to the question regarding the conditions of its synthesis it can give no answer which does not leave us to go on asking further without end. According to it, we must ascend from a given beginning to one still higher; every part leads to a still smaller part; every event has always yet another event above it as its cause; and the conditions of existence in general always rest in turn upon others, without ever finding unconditioned support and stay in a self-subsistent thing as original being, [which…]",["Running head (\"3. Abschnitt. Von dem Interesse der Vernunft etc. 325\") and the line-break hyphens removed.","The passage breaks off at \"zu be-\" (presumably *bekommen*); I have not completed it, and the bracketed \"[which…]\" only marks the break, supplying no content.","\"Haltung und Stütze\" rendered \"support and stay\".","\"selbstständigen\" rendered \"self-subsistent\"."]],"krv-b-1421":["Thirdly, this side also has the advantage of popularity, which certainly constitutes by no means the smallest part of its recommendation. Common understanding finds not the slightest difficulty in the ideas of an unconditioned beginning of all synthesis, since it is in any case more accustomed to proceed downwards to the consequences than to ascend to the grounds; and it has",["Breaks off mid-sentence (\"und hat\"); the object (\"Gemächlichkeit… und einen festen Punkt\") follows in the next passage.","\"gemeine Verstand\" rendered \"common understanding\"."]],"krv-b-1422":["in the concepts of the absolutely first (the possibility of which it does not trouble itself to investigate) a convenience, and at the same time a fixed point to which to attach the guiding-line of its steps. On the other hand it can find no satisfaction whatever in the restless ascent from the conditioned to the condition, always with one foot in the air.",["OCR \"grü— helt\" corrected to \"grübelt\"; the sentence is a direct continuation of the previous passage, which I left unjoined.","\"Leitschnur\" rendered \"guiding-line\"."]],"krv-b-1423":["On the side of empiricism, in the determination of the cosmological ideas, that is, on the side of the antithesis, there is found",["Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage. OCR \"fosmologischen\" not present here."]],"krv-b-1424":["First, no such practical interest from pure principles of reason as morality and religion bring with them. On the contrary, mere empiricism appears to deprive both of all force and influence. If there is no original being distinct from the world, if the world is without beginning and therefore also without author, if our will is not free, and the soul is of the same divisibility and perishability as matter, then the moral ideas and principles also lose all validity, and fall with the transcendental ideas which constituted their theoretical support.",["OCR \"trans scendentalen\" silently corrected.","\"Verweslichkeit\" rendered \"perishability\", as the counterpart of \"unverweslicher\" in the previous passage (\"indestructible\" there, chosen for the soul's simplicity)."]],"krv-b-1425":["On the other hand, empiricism offers to the speculative interest of reason advantages which are very attractive, and far surpass any that the dogmatic teacher of the ideas of reason may promise. According to empiricism the understanding is always on its own proper ground, namely, the field of purely possible experiences, whose laws it can trace out, and by means of which it can extend, without end, its secure and intelligible knowledge. Here it can, and should, exhibit the object in intuition, both in itself and in its relations; or, at least, in concepts whose image can be set forth clearly and distinctly in given similar intuitions. Not only has it no need to quit this chain of the natural order in order to attach itself to ideas whose objects it does not know, since as thought-entities [*Gedankendinge*] they can never be given; it is not even permitted to quit its business, and, under the pretext that this is now completed, to pass over into the domain of idealising reason and to transcendent concepts. There it has no further need to observe and to investigate in accordance with the laws of nature, but only to think and to invent. It can do so with the assurance that it cannot be refuted by facts of nature, since it is not bound to their testimony, but may pass them by, or may even subordinate them to a higher authority, namely, that of pure reason.",["OCR \"so— wohl\", \"transjcen denten\" silently corrected.","Divided the long final sentence into several; \"Nicht allein daß… sondern\" retained in an adapted form.","\"sichere und faßliche Erkenntniß\" rendered \"secure and intelligible knowledge\"; \"dichten\" rendered \"invent\" (also \"fabricate\"), with a connotation of poetic fiction.","\"Verhältnissen der Anschauung\" is obscure; I have read it as \"relations [in intuition]\" and taken \"in der Anschauung\" with *darstellen*."]],"krv-b-1426":["The empiricist will therefore never allow us to assume any epoch of nature as the absolutely first, or to regard any limit to his outlook into its extent as the outermost. Nor will he allow us to pass from the objects of nature, which he can analyse through observation and mathematics, and determine synthetically in intuition (the extended), to those which neither sense nor imagination can ever exhibit *in concreto* (the simple). He will not concede that we should take as ground, even in nature, a faculty of acting independently of the laws of nature (freedom), and thereby curtail the understanding in its business of tracing, under the guidance of necessary rules, the origin of appearances. And finally, he will not admit that we should seek the cause of anything outside nature (original being), since we know nothing further than nature, which alone presents objects to us and can instruct us regarding their laws.",["The OCR's stray quotation mark after \"oder\" is dropped (\"oder“)\"), probably a footnote mark; no footnote text appears in the passage, so none is supplied.","Restated one long sentence as four, repeating \"he will not\" to carry the dependence on \"nie erlauben\".","\"Vermögen\" rendered \"faculty\"; \"Aussicht\" rendered \"outlook\".","\"Urwesen\" kept as \"original being\" to match the earlier passages."]],"krv-b-1427":["It is true that if the empirical philosopher intended nothing more by his antithesis than to put down the presumption and arrogance of a reason which mistakes its true vocation, which boasts of insight and knowledge just where insight and knowledge actually cease, and which would pass off as a furtherance of speculative interest what is admitted only in respect of practical interest, in order, wherever it suits its own convenience, to break the thread of physical investigations and to attach it, under pretence of extending knowledge, to transcendental ideas, through which we really know only that we know nothing; if, I say, the empiricist were content with this, his principle would be a maxim of moderation in claims, of modesty in assertions, and at the same time of the greatest possible extension of our understanding through the teacher who is properly set over us, namely experience. For in that case intellectual presuppositions and faith would not be taken from us so far as our practical concerns require them. They could only not be allowed to appear under the title and pomp of science and rational insight. The reason is that properly speculative knowledge can nowhere have any object save that of experience; and when we overstep its limits, the synthesis which seeks to establish new modes of knowledge independent of experience has no substratum of intuition upon which it could be exercised.",["- Restated the single long sentence as several; \"nur könnte man sie nicht…\" and the *weil* clause are made separate sentences.","- \"Wissen\"/\"Einsicht\" rendered \"knowledge\"/\"insight\"; \"Erkenntnisse\" (plural) as \"modes of knowledge\"; \"Substratum\" kept in Latin form as in Kant.","- \"Vorwitz und Vermessenheit\" rendered \"presumption and arrogance\"; the choice is a matter of judgment.","- \"Verstand\" in \"Erweiterung unseres Verstandes\" kept as \"understanding.\""]],"krv-b-1428":["But if empiricism itself becomes dogmatic in respect of the ideas (as for the most part happens), and boldly denies whatever lies beyond the sphere of its intuitive modes of knowledge, it falls into the same fault of immodesty. The fault is here all the more blameworthy, because an irreparable injury is thereby done to the practical interest of reason.",["- Divided the sentence at \"der hier um desto tadelbarer ist\"; \"the same fault\" supplies \"the same\" to make clear the reference back to the dogmatist's immodesty.","- \"anschauenden Erkenntnisse\" rendered \"intuitive modes of knowledge.\""]],"krv-b-1429":["This is the opposition of Epicureanism to Platonism.[*]",["- The OCR reads \"Epikureisms“)\"; I take the mark as the footnote sign and render it as [*], corresponding to the footnote in krv-b-1430."]],"krv-b-1430":["[*] It is, however, still a question whether Epicurus ever propounded these principles as objective assertions. If they were nothing more than maxims for the speculative employment of reason, he showed in them a more genuinely philosophical spirit than any other of the sages of antiquity. That in explaining appearances we must proceed as if the field of enquiry were not cut off by any limit or beginning of the world; that we must assume the matter of the world to be such as it must be if we are to be instructed about it by experience; that no other mode of generation of events may be employed than that in which they are determined by unalterable laws of nature; and finally that no cause distinct from the world is to be used: all these are still very correct, but little observed, principles for extending speculative philosophy, as also for discovering the principles of morals independently of foreign sources of aid. He who demands that we ignore those dogmatic propositions, so long as we are occupied merely with speculation, ought not on that account to be accused of wishing to deny them.",["- Restated the sentence with the infinitive series (\"Daß man…; den Stoff…annehmen…; daß keine…\") as parallel clauses under one \"that\"; the German construction is irregular and has been smoothed.","- \"Hülfsquellen\" rendered \"sources of aid\"; \"Weltweisen\" as \"sages.\"","- The final clause was reordered; the German is loosely constructed (\"derjenige, welcher verlangt…darum beschuldigt werden darf\")."]],"krv-b-1431":["Each of the two says more than he knows; but in such fashion that the former encourages and furthers knowledge, though to the prejudice of the practical, while the latter does indeed supply excellent principles for the practical, but in so doing permits reason, in respect of everything in which alone a speculative knowledge is open to us, to give itself up to ideal explanations of natural appearances, and to neglect physical enquiry.",["- The OCR \"bez | fördert\" is read \"befördert\" (the | mark is the Academy line-break sign, retained in the text of the German only; here it falls within a word and is not reproducible in English).","- \"idealischen sit Erklärungen\": \"sit\" is an OCR slip, ignored; read \"idealischen Erklärungen.\"","- \"phy- sische\" read \"physische.\""]],"krv-b-1432":["As regards the third consideration which may be taken into account in making a preliminary choice between the two conflicting parties, it is extremely strange that empiricism should be wholly opposed to all popularity. One would suppose that the common understanding would eagerly welcome a scheme which promises to satisfy it through nothing but modes of knowledge from experience and their rational connection; whereas transcendental dogmatism compels it to ascend to concepts which far exceed the insight and rational capacity of even the minds most practised in thought. But this is precisely its motive. For the common understanding then finds itself in a position in which even the most learned can claim no superiority over it. If it understands little or nothing of these matters, no one else can boast of understanding much more; and though it cannot speak of them so scholastically as others, it can yet reason about them endlessly, since it wanders among mere ideas, about which one is most eloquent just because one knows nothing of them. In the investigation of nature, on the other hand, it would have to remain wholly silent and confess its ignorance. Convenience and vanity are thus already a strong recommendation of these principles.\n\nBesides, although it is very hard for a philosopher to accept anything as a principle without being able to give himself an account of it, or to introduce concepts whose objective reality cannot be seen, nothing is more usual for the common understanding. It wants something from which it can make a confident start. The difficulty of comprehending such a presupposition does not disturb it, because the difficulty never occurs to it (it does not know what comprehending means), and it takes as known whatever has become familiar through frequent use. Finally, all speculative interest vanishes in its mind before the practical, and it imagines that it has insight into and knowledge of what its fears or hopes impel it to assume or to believe. Thus empiricism is wholly deprived of all popularity for the transcendentally idealising reason; and however much that is prejudicial to the supreme practical principles it may contain, there is no ground for apprehension that it will ever overstep the limits of the school, or acquire any considerable authority or favour among the general public.",["- Divided the long passage into several sentences; at \"Überdem\" I begin a new paragraph for clarity. \"Er\" is resolved throughout as \"the common understanding\" (*gemeiner Verstand*), except in \"sein Bewegungsgrund,\" where \"its motive\" refers to the popularity of dogmatism.","- \"Aber eben dieses ist sein Bewegungsgrund\": \"its\" is taken to refer to the common understanding's preference; this is a judgment on the antecedent.","- OCR slips corrected silently: \"Begriffet)\" read \"Begriffe\" with a footnote sign (no footnote text is given in the passage); \"fidh\" as \"sich\"; \"Grundiäße\" as \"Grundsätze\"; \"ver schwindet\" as \"verschwindet\"; \"gez meinen\" as \"gemeinen.\"","- \"Popularität\" kept as \"popularity.\" \"transscendental-idealisirenden Vernunft\" rendered \"transcendentally idealising reason.\""]],"krv-b-1433":["Human reason is by its nature architectonic. That is to say, it regards all modes of knowledge as belonging to a possible system, and accordingly allows only such principles as at least do not render a given item of knowledge incapable of standing together with others in some system. The propositions of the antithesis, however, are of such a kind that they make the completion of an edifice of knowledge altogether impossible. According to them there is always, beyond any state of the world, a still older; in every part, others still, again divisible; before every event, another, which in turn was likewise generated otherwise; and in existence in general everything is always only conditioned, no unconditioned and first existence being recognised. Since, then, the antithesis nowhere concedes a first, and no beginning that could serve absolutely as foundation of the structure, a complete edifice of knowledge is altogether impossible on such presuppositions. Hence the architectonic interest of reason (which demands not empirical but pure unity of reason *a priori*) carries with it a natural recommendation for the assertions of the thesis.",["- \"d. i.\" expanded as \"That is to say\"; the first sentence is divided in two.","- \"vorhabende Erkenntniß\" rendered \"a given item of knowledge\"; the sense is \"the knowledge in hand.\"","- \"Gebäude\" varied as \"edifice\"/\"structure\"; \"Bau\" as \"structure.\"","- The elliptical series \"in jedem Theile immer noch andere…\" is completed by repeating \"there is always\" implicitly; no bracket used since the ellipsis is plain in the German.","- OCR slips corrected: \"Erkennt— niß,\" \"un— möglich,\" \"Ge— hände\" (read \"Gebäude\"), \"empoirische\" (read \"empirische\")."]],"krv-b-1434":["Suppose, however, that a man could renounce all interest, and, indifferent to all consequences, consider the assertions of reason solely in view of the content of their grounds. Suppose further that he knew no way of escaping from the perplexity except by professing allegiance to one or other of the conflicting doctrines. Such a man would then be in a state of perpetual vacillation. Today it would strike him as convincing that the human will is free; tomorrow, when he considered the indissoluble chain of nature, he would hold that freedom is nothing but self-deception, and that everything is mere nature. If, however, it came to action and conduct, this play of merely speculative reason would vanish like the shadow-images of a dream, and he would choose his principles solely in accordance with practical interest. Nevertheless, it is fitting that a reflective and inquiring being should devote certain times solely to the examination of his own reason, and in so doing should divest himself completely of all partiality and publicly communicate his observations to others for their judgment. Consequently no one can be blamed, still less prevented, if he allows propositions and counter-propositions to come forward, undaunted by any threat, to defend themselves as best they can before jurors of his own station, that is, the station of weak men.",["- Restated the opening sentence as three sentences; the \"gesetzt daß\" clause is rendered \"Suppose further that…\".","- \"Weil es aber doch … anständig ist … so kann es niemanden verargt\" is an irregular construction; I recast it as \"Nevertheless… Consequently…\". \"Es\" is taken to refer to the reflective being.","- \"Wie sie … vertheidigen können\": rendered \"to defend themselves as best they can\"; \"vor Geschworenen\" is rendered \"before jurors\".","- \"Behauptungen\" is rendered \"assertions\"; \"Parteilichkeit\" is rendered \"partiality\"."]],"krv-b-1435":["Fourth Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason.\nOn the Transcendental Problems of Pure",["- Heading broken across several passages (B 504); it continues in the next two ids. \"Der\" before \"Antinomie\" is carried over as part of the running title, not translated separately."]],"krv-b-1436":["Reason, in so far as they must absolutely be capable of being solved",["- Continuation of the heading; \"schlechterdings\" is rendered \"absolutely\"."]],"krv-b-1437":[".",["- Final fragment of the heading: \"werden können\" completes \"must absolutely be capable of being solved\". The English is therefore broken at the id boundary. I have given only the closing stop and no more."]],"krv-b-1438":["To wish to solve all problems and to answer all questions would be shameless boasting and such extravagant conceit that one would at once forfeit all confidence. Nevertheless there are sciences whose nature is such that every question arising in them must be absolutely answerable from what we know, since the answer must spring from the same sources from which the question springs. In these sciences it is by no means permissible to plead unavoidable ignorance; the solution can be demanded. What is right or wrong in all possible cases we must, as a rule, be able to know, because it concerns our obligation, and we have no obligation to that which we cannot know. In the explanation of the appearances of nature, on the other hand, much must remain uncertain for us and many a question insoluble, since what we know of nature is by no means in all cases sufficient for what we have to explain. The question now is whether in transcendental philosophy there is any question, relating to an object presented to reason, which this very pure reason cannot answer; and whether reason can rightly excuse itself from giving a decisive answer by classing the question as absolutely uncertain (on the basis of all that we are able to know). That is, whether it can rank it among those matters of which we have indeed so much concept as to raise a question, but with regard to which we are altogether without the means or the power ever to answer it.",["- Divided the final sentence into two; \"demjenigen, wovon…\" is rendered \"among those matters of which…\".","- \"Verbindlichkeit\" is rendered \"obligation\"; \"Erscheinungen\" is rendered \"appearances\". \"Vermögen\" is rendered \"power\" here.","- OCR \"konnen\" is silently corrected to \"können\", and \"unbeantworte lich\" to \"unbeantwortlich\"."]],"krv-b-1439":["I now assert that transcendental philosophy has this peculiarity among all speculative knowledge: that no question whatever which concerns an object given to pure reason is insoluble for this same human reason. No plea of unavoidable ignorance or of unfathomable depth in the problem can release us from the obligation to answer it thoroughly and completely. For the very concept which puts us in a position to ask the question must also qualify us to answer it, since the object is not to be met with outside the concept (as in the case of right and wrong).",["- Split the German sentence into three. \"Erkenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\".","- OCR \"aut— worten\" is silently corrected to \"antworten\"."]],"krv-b-1440":["But in transcendental philosophy there are no questions other than the cosmological in respect of which we can rightly demand a satisfactory answer concerning the constitution of the object. The philosopher is not permitted to evade such an answer by pleading impenetrable obscurity; and these questions can concern only cosmological ideas. For the object must be empirically given, and the question relates only to its adequacy to an idea. If the object is transcendental, and therefore itself unknown, as for instance whether the something whose appearance (in ourselves) is thought, that is, the soul, is in itself a simple being, or whether there is a cause of all things taken together which is absolutely necessary, and so forth, then we are to seek an object for our idea, and may admit that it is unknown to us, though not on that account impossible.*) The cosmological ideas alone have the peculiarity,",["- Split the first sentence at the semicolon into two. \"Oenken\" (OCR) is read as \"Denken\" and rendered \"thought\"; in the translation \"is thought\" stands for \"das Denken ist\".","- \"Angemessenheit\" is rendered \"adequacy\"; \"Beschaffenheit\" is rendered \"constitution\". \"Erscheinung\" is rendered \"appearance\".","- The footnote marker *) is kept. The passage breaks off mid-sentence, and I have not completed it."]],"krv-b-1441":["*) To the question of what constitution a transcendental object has, no answer can indeed be given, namely as to what it is; but it can be said that the question itself is nothing, because no object has been given for it. Hence all questions of the transcendental doctrine of the soul are also answerable, and have actually been answered. For they concern the transcendental subject of all inner appearances, which is not itself appearance and so is not given as object, and in respect of which none of the categories (to which the question is properly directed) finds conditions of its application. Here, therefore, is the case in which the common saying holds, that no answer is also an answer: namely, that a question regarding the constitution of that something which cannot be thought through any determinate predicate\n\nthat they can presuppose as given their object and the empirical synthesis required for its concept. The question which arises from them concerns only the progress of this synthesis, in so far as it is to contain absolute totality; and this totality is no longer anything empirical, since it cannot be given in any experience. Since we are here dealing solely with a thing as object of a possible experience, and not as a thing in itself, the answer to the transcendent cosmological question can lie nowhere outside the idea. For the question does not concern any object in itself. And in respect of possible experience we do not ask what can be given in concreto in some experience, but what lies in the idea, to which the empirical synthesis is merely to approximate. The question must therefore be capable of solution from the idea alone; for the idea is a mere creature of reason, which cannot disclaim responsibility and shift it on to the unknown object.",["- The OCR has run the end of the footnote into the main text (the footnote breaks off at \"Prädi-\" and its remainder is lost), and the main text resumes at \"daß sie ihren Gegenstand…\". I have kept both parts in this id, separated by a blank line, and have not completed the footnote.","- \"durch kein bestimmtes Prädi…\" is rendered \"through any determinate predicate\", but the German word is cut off and the sentence stands broken. The word \"gedacht\" does not appear, so \"cannot be thought\" is my supply from the sense; it should be bracketed: [cannot be thought].","- OCR \"ile— gen\" is silently corrected to \"liegen\", and the stray \")\" after \"wird\" is dropped.","- Split the main-text sentence \"Da nun hier…\" into shorter sentences; \"Geschöpf\" is rendered \"creature\"."]],"krv-b-1442":["It is not so extraordinary as it at first seems that a science should be able to demand and expect, in regard to all the questions belonging to its sphere (*quaestiones domesticae*), nothing but certain solutions, even though these may perhaps not yet have been found. Besides transcendental philosophy there are two other sciences of pure reason, one of purely speculative, the other of practical content: pure mathematics and pure morals. Has it ever been heard said that, owing to a necessary ignorance of the conditions, it was left uncertain what exact relation the diameter bears to the circle in rational or irrational numbers? Since it cannot be given exactly by the former, and has not yet been found by the latter, it was judged that at least the impossibility of such a solution can be known with certainty; and Lambert supplied a proof of this. In the universal principles of morals nothing can be uncertain, since the propositions are either entirely void and meaningless, or must flow simply from our concepts of reason. In natural science, on the other hand, there is an infinity of conjectures in regard to which certainty can never be expected, [. . .] because the appearances of nature are objects given to us independently of our concepts. The key to them therefore lies not in us and in our pure thinking but outside us, and for that very reason in many cases cannot be discovered, so that no assured explanation can be expected. I do not here reckon the questions of the transcendental analytic which concern the deduction of our pure knowledge, since we are at present dealing only with the certainty of judgments in regard to objects, and not with the origin of our concepts themselves.",["- The text is corrupt after \"erwartet werden kann\": the words \"cat gedacht werden kann, weil es gänzlich außer der Sphäre ... gänzlich nichtig und leer sei\" are a stray fragment (apparently a displaced variant clause) and I have not translated it, marking the gap with [. . .].","- \"Durhmeffer\", \"Srrationalzahlen\", \"Gegen— stände\" corrected silently as OCR slips; \"Auflösung\" rendered \"solution\".","- \"congruent\" in the circle sentence: \"cannot be given exactly\" is my gloss of \"gar nicht congruent gegeben werden kann\"; \"the former/the latter\" = rational/irrational numbers (the order of the German is retained, though the sense is probably reversed in Kant's intent: rational ones cannot give it, irrational ones have not yet been found).","- \"Naturerscheinungen\" = appearances of nature; \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge."]],"krv-b-1443":["We shall not, then, be able to evade the obligation of at least a critical solution of the questions of reason here propounded, by lamenting the narrow limits of our reason and confessing, with the semblance of a humble self-knowledge, that it lies beyond our reason to decide whether the world has existed from eternity or has a beginning; whether cosmic space is filled with beings to infinity or is enclosed within certain limits; whether anything in the world is simple, or whether everything must be divided to infinity; whether there is a generation and production through freedom, or whether everything depends on the chain of the natural order; and finally whether there is any being completely unconditioned and in itself necessary, or whether everything is conditioned in its existence, and therefore externally dependent and in itself contingent. For all these questions concern an object which can be given nowhere save in our thoughts, namely, the absolutely unconditioned totality of the synthesis of appearances. If from our own concepts we can assert and determine nothing certain regarding it, we must not throw the blame upon the object, as hiding itself from us. For such an object, since it is nowhere to be met with outside our idea, cannot be given to us at all. The cause we must seek in our idea itself. The idea is a problem which admits of no solution, and yet we obstinately assume that there corresponds to it an actual object. A clear exposition of the dialectic which lies in our concept itself would soon bring us to complete certainty as to what we ought to judge in regard to such a question.",["- Restated the long final sentences of the first half as several sentences; \"denn es kann uns dergleichen Sache ... gegeben werden\" made a separate sentence.","- \"Darle— gnug\" read as \"Darlegung\" (OCR slip); \"Wesen\" in the second question rendered \"beings\".","- \"Erzeugung und Hervorbringung\" rendered \"generation and production\"; \"Idee\" = idea."]],"krv-b-1444":["To your pretext of uncertainty in regard to these problems we may first oppose this question, which you at least must answer clearly: Whence come the ideas the solution of which involves you in such difficulty? Are they perhaps appearances which you require to have explained, and in regard to which, in consequence of these ideas, you have only to seek the principles, or the rule of their exposition? Suppose nature were wholly laid open before you, and that nothing were hidden from your senses and from the consciousness of all that is presented to your intuition; yet no single experience would enable you to know the object of your ideas *in concreto*. For besides this complete intuition there is required a completed synthesis, and the consciousness of its absolute totality, and this is not possible through any empirical knowledge. Your question can therefore in no way be necessary for the explanation of any appearance that presents itself, and so be, as it were, set [a] by the object itself. For the object can never present itself to you, since it cannot be given through any possible experience. With all possible perceptions you remain always entangled in conditions, whether in space or in time, and never come to anything unconditioned, so as to determine whether this unconditioned is to be posited in an absolute beginning of the synthesis, or in an absolute totality of the series without any beginning. The *all* in its empirical meaning, however, is always only comparative. The absolute *all* of quantity (the universe), of division, of descent, of the condition of existence in general, together with all the questions whether it is to be brought about by a finite synthesis or by one continuing to infinity, has no bearing upon any possible experience. You would not, for example, be able to explain the appearances of a body in the least better, or even differently, whether you assumed that it consists of simple parts or throughout always of composite parts; for no simple appearance, and just as little an infinite composition, can ever present itself to you. Appearances demand to be explained only so far as the conditions of their explanation are given in perception; but all that can ever be given in them, taken together in an absolute whole, is itself no perception. Yet it is this *all* whose explanation is really demanded in the transcendental problems of reason.",["- \"eurem Vorwande\" etc.: the second person plural addressed to the sceptic is kept as \"you\".","- \"und also gleichsam a durch den Gegenstand selbst aufgegeben sein\": the stray \"a\" is an OCR residue, left in brackets as [a]; read \"aufgegeben\" as \"set (as a task)\". The parenthesis \"(denn ... möglich ist)\" was turned into a separate sentence.","- \"Das All\" rendered \"the *all*\" (the italic is mine for readability, not recoverable emphasis); \"in dentransscendentalen\" corrected silently.","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Anschauung\" = intuition."]],"krv-b-1445":["Since, then, the solution of these problems can never itself be met with in experience, you cannot say that it is uncertain what is to be ascribed to the object in this regard. For your object is merely in your brain, and cannot be given outside it at all. All you have to do, therefore, is to secure agreement with yourselves, and to guard against the amphiboly which converts your idea into a supposed representation of an object empirically given, and therefore to be known in accordance with the laws of experience. The dogmatic solution is thus not uncertain, but impossible. The critical solution, on the other hand, which can be completely certain, does not consider the question objectively at all, but in terms of the foundation of the knowledge upon which the question is based.",["- \"Gehirne\" rendered \"brain\" literally; \"Amphibolie\" = amphiboly; \"Vorstellung\" = representation; \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge.","- \"nach dem Fundamente der Erkenntniß, worauf sie gegründet ist\": \"sie\" taken as the question (Frage), hence \"upon which the question is based\"."]],"krv-b-1446":["Fifth Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason",["- Heading, reordered into English idiom from \"Der Antinomie der reinen Vernunft Fünfter Abschnitt.\""]],"krv-b-1447":["Sceptical Representation of the Cosmological Questions through",["- Heading broken off; it continues in the next passage (\"alle vier transscendentale Ideen\"). \"Vorstellung\" here rendered \"representation\"; Kemp Smith has \"exposition\" in his published heading, but I keep the fixed term."]],"krv-b-1448":["all the Four Transcendental Ideas.",["- Continuation of the heading begun in the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-1449":["We should be ready enough to give up the demand that our questions be answered dogmatically, if we could understand beforehand that, however the answer might turn out, it would only increase our ignorance, and cast us from one incomprehensibility into another, from one obscurity into a greater, and perhaps even into contradictions. If our question is framed simply as calling for an affirmation or a denial, it is prudent to leave the presumed grounds of the answer for the time being aside, and to consider first what we should gain if the answer fell on the one side, and what if it fell on the opposite. Now if it happens that in both cases the outcome is nothing but nonsense [*Sinnleeres*, *Nonsens*], we have good ground for examining our question itself critically, and for seeing whether it does not itself rest on a groundless presupposition, and play with an idea which betrays its falsity better in its application and through its consequences than when taken in abstraction. Such is the great utility of the sceptical manner of treating the questions which pure reason puts to pure reason. By it we can, at small cost, be relieved of a great deal of dogmatic rubbish, and can put in its place a sober critique, which, as a genuine cathartic, will happily purge away such delusion, together with its attendant, the pretence of knowing many things [*Vielwisserei*].",[]],"krv-b-1450":["If, then, I could see beforehand, in the case of a cosmological idea, that whichever side of the unconditioned in the regressive synthesis of appearances it might take, it would be either too large or too small for any concept of the understanding, I should understand the following. That synthesis has to do only with an object of experience, which is required to be adequate to a possible concept of the understanding; the idea must therefore be quite empty and without meaning, since the object does not fit it, however I may try to accommodate the one to the other. And this is really the case with all world-concepts, which for that very reason involve reason, so long as it adheres to them, in an unavoidable antinomy. For suppose",[]],"krv-b-1451":["first that the world has no beginning; then it is too large for your concept. For this concept, which consists in a successive regress, can never reach the whole of the elapsed eternity. Suppose that it has a beginning; then it is in turn too small for your concept of the understanding, in the necessary empirical regress. For since the beginning still presupposes a time which precedes it, it is not yet unconditioned; and the law of the empirical employment of the understanding obliges you to ask for a still higher temporal condition. The world is therefore evidently too small for this law.",[]],"krv-b-1452":["The same holds of the twofold answer to the question regarding the magnitude of the world in space. If it is infinite and unlimited, it is too large for every possible empirical concept. If it is finite and limited, you rightly ask further: What determines this limit? Empty space is not a self-subsisting correlate of things, and cannot be a condition at which you could stop; still less can it be an empirical condition, such as would form a part of a possible experience. (For who can have an experience of the absolutely empty?) But for the absolute totality of the empirical synthesis it is always required that the unconditioned be a concept of experience. A limited world is therefore too small for your concept.",[]],"krv-b-1453":["Secondly, if every appearance in space (matter) consists of infinitely many parts, the regress of the division is always too large for your concept; and if the division of space is to cease at some member of it (the simple), the regress is too small for the idea of the unconditioned. For this member still leaves open a regress to further parts contained in it.",[]],"krv-b-1454":["Thirdly, if you assume that in everything which happens in the world there is nothing but consequence according to laws of nature, the causality of the cause is always itself something that happens, and makes necessary your regress to a still higher cause, and so the continual prolongation of the series of conditions *a parte priori*. Mere efficient nature is therefore too large for any concept you can form in the synthesis of cosmic events.",[]],"krv-b-1455":["If you choose, here and there, events that are self-produced, and so generation from freedom, then the 'Why?' pursues you in accordance with an unavoidable law of nature, and compels you to go beyond this point, according to the causal law of experience; and you find that such a totality of connection is too small for your necessary empirical concept.",[]],"krv-b-1456":["Fourthly, if you assume an absolutely necessary being (whether it be the world itself, or something in the world, or the cause of the world), you place it in a time infinitely remote from every given point of time, since otherwise it would be dependent upon another and older existence. But then this existence is inaccessible to your empirical concept, and too great for you ever to reach it by any continued regress.",[]],"krv-b-1457":["If, on the other hand, in your opinion everything that belongs to the world (whether as conditioned or as condition) is contingent, then every existence given to you is too small for your concept. For it obliges you always to look about for some other existence upon which it is dependent.",[]],"krv-b-1458":["In all these cases we have said that the idea of the world is either too large or too small for the empirical regress, and so for every possible concept of the understanding. Why did we not express ourselves the other way round, and say that in the former case the empirical concept is always too small for the idea, and in the latter too large, so that the blame attaches, as it were, to the empirical regress; instead of accusing the cosmological idea of deviating, by excess or deficiency, from its purpose, namely possible experience? The reason was this. Possible experience is what alone can give reality to our concepts; without it every concept is a mere idea, without truth and without relation to an object. The possible empirical concept was therefore the standard by which the idea had to be judged, to decide whether it is a mere idea and thought-entity [*Gedankending*], or finds its object in the world. For we say that something is too large or too small relatively to something else only if it is assumed merely for the sake of the latter and must be adapted to it. It belonged to the pastimes of the old dialectical schools to ask: If a ball will not go through a hole, what are we to say? Is the ball too large, or the hole too small? In this case it is indifferent how you express yourselves; for you do not know which of the two exists for the sake of the other. But you will not say, on the other hand, that the man is too long for his coat, but that the coat is too short for the man.",[]],"krv-b-1459":["We have thus at least been led to a well-grounded suspicion that the cosmological ideas, and with them all the mutually conflicting sophistical assertions, may perhaps have at their basis an empty and merely imagined concept of the manner in which the object of these ideas is given to us. This suspicion may already set us on the right track to discover the illusion which has so long led us astray.",[]],"krv-b-1460":["Sixth Section\nof the Antinomy of Pure Reason.",[]],"krv-b-1461":["Transcendental Idealism as the Key to",[]],"krv-b-1462":["the Solution of the Cosmological Dialectic.",["- krv-b-1449: Restated the long final sentence in two; \"dog— matisch\" repaired from OCR. *Sinnleeres* (Nonsens) given in brackets; *Vielwisserei* rendered \"pretence of knowing many things\", the German in brackets. \"Wahn\" taken as \"delusion\".","- krv-b-1450: Broke the sentence into several and supplied \"I should understand the following\" to carry the *begreifen* clause. \"jene\" resolved as the regressive synthesis, \"sie\" in \"sie ganz leer\" as the idea. Ends \"Denn nehmt\", continued in 1451.","- krv-b-1451: \"Erstlich\" is carried over from \"nehmt\" in the preceding fragment, hence \"suppose first\". \"Verstandesbegriff\" kept as \"concept of the understanding\".","- krv-b-1453: OCR \"Des griff\", \"fol\" corrected to \"Begriff\", \"soll\".","- krv-b-1454: OCR \"Nrsache\" corrected to \"Ursache\"; \"Erfolg\" rendered \"consequence\"; \"Weltbegebenheiten\" as \"cosmic events\".","- krv-b-1458: \"Gedankending\" rendered \"thought-entity\" (Kemp Smith's usage). The \"man too long for his coat\" sentence kept close to the German.","- krv-b-1459: \"ein gebildeten\" corrected to \"eingebildeten\"; \"vernünftelnde\" rendered \"sophistical\".","- krv-b-1460–1462: Heading split across three passages by OCR. Joined, it reads \"Sixth Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason: Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of the Cosmological Dialectic\". Kept in the order given."]],"krv-b-1463":["We have sufficiently proved in the Transcendental Aesthetic that everything intuited in space or in time, and therefore all objects of any experience possible to us, are nothing but appearances, that is, mere representations. As thus represented, whether as extended beings or as series of alterations, they have no independent existence, grounded in itself, outside our thoughts. This doctrine I entitle transcendental idealism.¹ The realist in the transcendental",["- Footnote marker: the OCR shows \")\" after \"Idealism\"; I render it as ¹, the footnote text being given in the next passage (krv-b-1464).","- Restated the long first sentence as two, to separate the proof from the account of what appearances are; \"so, wie sie vorgestellt werden\" is taken to qualify the \"extended beings\" and \"series of alterations\".","- \"Lehrbegriff\" rendered \"doctrine\"; \"Gegenstände\" = objects, \"Erscheinungen\" = appearances, \"Vorstellungen\" = representations.","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"Der Realist in transscendentaler\"); not completed."]],"krv-b-1464":["¹ I have also at times entitled it formal idealism, to distinguish it from material idealism, that is, from the ordinary idealism which doubts or denies the existence of external things themselves. In some cases it seems advisable to employ this expression rather than the one given above, in order to prevent all misinterpretation.\n\n[…] signification [Bedeutung] makes of these modifications of our sensibility things subsisting in themselves, and therefore turns mere representations into things in themselves.",["- The OCR has the footnote's closing \")\" followed by \"Bedeutung macht…\", which is the continuation of the main-text sentence begun in krv-b-1463 (\"Der Realist in transscendentaler Bedeutung\"). I have set it after the footnote, with the footnote's end marked by a blank line; \"[…]\" marks the break and the sentence's beginning lies in the previous passage. \"Realist in transcendental signification\" is the sense.","- \"Mißdeutung\" = misinterpretation; \"gemeinen\" rendered \"ordinary\"; \"Modificationen\" kept as \"modifications\".","- \"Sachen an sich selbst\" rendered \"things in themselves\"."]],"krv-b-1465":["We should be doing us an injustice if we were to be charged with that empirical idealism, so long decried, which, while it assumes the proper reality of space,",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; not completed. \"zumuthen\" rendered \"charged with\" (attribute to us); \"uns Unrecht thun\" rendered \"doing us an injustice\" with a smoothing of the conditional.","- \"Wirklichkeit\" = reality (actuality)."]],"krv-b-1466":["denies the existence of extended",["- Fragment of a sentence continuing in krv-b-1465/1467; \"das Dasein der ausgedehnten\" is rendered \"the existence of extended\", with the noun \"beings\" following in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1467":["beings in space, or at least finds it doubtful, and admits no sufficiently demonstrable difference between dream and truth in this matter. As regards the appearances of inner sense in time, it finds no difficulty in treating them as actual things; indeed it even asserts that this inner experience alone suffices, and suffices sufficiently, to prove the actual existence of its object (in itself, together with all this determination of time) uniquely",["- The sentence is continued in krv-b-1468 (\"und allein hinreichend beweise\"). I have rendered \"einzig\" as \"uniquely\" and left the clause open; the English phrasing \"alone suffices, and suffices sufficiently\" is a smoothing of the split reading, and the clause is completed in the next passage.","- Restated the sentence: \"an denen als wirklichen Dingen findet er keine Schwierigkeit\" is made \"it finds no difficulty in treating them as actual things\".","- \"Zeitbestimmung\" = determination of time; \"Objects\" = object. OCR hyphenation (\"Schwierig— keit\", \"Da— sein\") silently corrected."]],"krv-b-1468":["and alone [einzig und allein] adequately prove.",["- Completes the clause of krv-b-1467. The verb \"beweise\" is subjunctive (indirect speech: \"he asserts that … prove\"); I have rendered it as the plain verb.","- Because krv-b-1467 already rendered \"einzig\" as \"uniquely\", the doubled rendering \"uniquely … and alone\" is a joint rendering of \"einzig und allein\"; a reader may prefer to take them together as \"solely\"."]],"krv-b-1469":["Our transcendental idealism, on the contrary, allows that the objects of outer intuition, just as they are intuited in space,",["- \"erlaubt es dagegen: daß …\" is rendered \"allows … that …\". Passage breaks off; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1470":["are also actual, and that in time all alterations are actual just as",["- The sentence continues in krv-b-1471 (\"der innere Sinn vorstellt\"). The second \"wirklich sind\" is supplied from the first clause: \"[are actual]\" is implied by \"auch wirklich sind und in der Zeit alle Veränderungen\"."]],"krv-b-1471":["inner sense represents them. For since space is already a form of that intuition which we entitle outer, and since without objects in",["- \"derjenigen Anschauung, die wir die äußere nennen\" rendered \"that intuition which we entitle outer\". The sentence is left open at \"without objects in\", completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1472":["it there would be no empirical representation whatever, we can, and must, assume extended beings in it as actual. The same is true of time. But space itself, together with this time, and together with both all appearances, are none the less not things in themselves but nothing but representations, and cannot exist at all outside our mind. Even the inner and sensible intuition of our mind (as object of consciousness), the determination of which is represented through the succession of different states in time, is not the real self as it exists in itself, or the transcendental subject, but only an appearance which has been given to the sensibility of this being unknown to us. The existence of this inner appearance as of a thing thus existing in itself cannot be admitted, since its condition is time, which cannot be a determination of any thing in itself. In space and",["- The sentence beginning \"Jener Raum selber…\" is divided into two, and the long parenthetical sentence on the inner intuition is kept as one but with a lighter structure. \"Jener … sammt dieser Zeit\" rendered \"space itself, together with this time\".","- \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility. OCR letters \"b\" and \"s\" (line markers) dropped; \"so\" before \"Erscheinung\" (\"inneren so Erscheinung\") is OCR line-number noise (\"30\") and is omitted.","- \"verhalten\": \"das eigentliche Selbst\" rendered \"the real self\". Pagination markers \"|\" in the German (after \"transscen-\" and \"einge-\") are carried over silently only in that I omit them here; if the apparatus signs are required, they fall after \"transcendental\" and \"admitted\".","- \"Wesen\" (unknown to us) rendered \"being\"."]],"krv-b-1473":["time, however, the empirical truth of appearances is sufficiently secured, and sufficiently distinguished from kinship with dreams, if both cohere correctly and",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence, completed in krv-b-1474 (\"durchgängig zusammenhängen\"). The stray \"s\" in the OCR (\"hinreichend unterschieden, s wenn\") is a line-number and is dropped.","- \"beide\" taken to be appearances in space and in time, i.e. space and time as ordered in one experience."]],"krv-b-1474":["thoroughly with one another in one experience in accordance with empirical laws.",["- \"durchgängig zusammenhängen\" rendered \"cohere thoroughly\"; the clause \"nach empirischen Gesetzen in einer Erfahrung\" is reordered after the verb to give English syntax, and the first adverb \"richtig\" is carried by krv-b-1473 (\"correctly\")."]],"krv-b-1475":["Accordingly, the objects of experience are never given in themselves, but only in experience, and outside it they do not exist at all. That there may be inhabitants in the moon, although no human being has ever perceived them, must certainly be admitted; but this means only that in the possible progress of experience we might come upon them. For everything is actual which stands in one context with a perception in accordance with the laws of empirical progress. They are therefore actual when they stand in an empirical connection with my actual consciousness, although they are not on that account actual in themselves, that is, outside this progress of experience.",["- \"Context\" kept as Kant's own term (Latin *contextus*), in the sense of a connected whole; \"Zusammenhange\" rendered \"connection\".","- \"Fortgang\"/\"Fortschritt\" both rendered \"progress\"."]],"krv-b-1476":["Nothing is actually given to us save perception and the empirical advance from this to other possible perceptions. For in themselves, as mere representations, appearances are actual only in perception, which is in fact nothing other than the actuality of an empirical representation, that is, of an appearance. To call an appearance a real thing prior to perception means either that in the progress of experience we must meet with such a perception, or else it has no meaning whatever. For that it exists in itself, without relation to our senses and possible experience, could indeed be said if we were speaking of a thing in itself. But what we are speaking of is merely an appearance in space and time, both of which are determinations not of things in themselves but only of our sensibility. Hence that which is in them (appearances) is not something in itself, but mere representations, which, if they are not given in us (in perception), are met with nowhere at all.",["- OCR \"cm: pirische\" corrected to \"empirische\".","- \"Wirklichkeit\" rendered \"actuality\" where the contrast is with the merely possible, \"real\" for \"wirkliches Ding\".","- \"überall nirgend\" rendered \"nowhere at all\".","- The sentence \"Es ist aber bloß von einer Erscheinung … die Rede; daher …\" divided into two for clarity."]],"krv-b-1477":["The sensible faculty of intuition is really only a receptivity, a capacity to be affected in a certain manner with representations, the relation of which to one another is a pure intuition of space and of time (nothing but forms of our sensibility). So far as these representations are connected and determinable in this relation (in space and time) according to laws of the unity of experience, they are entitled objects. The non-sensible cause of these representations is wholly unknown to us, and we cannot therefore intuit it as object. For such an object would have to be represented neither in space nor in time (as mere conditions of sensible representation), and apart from these conditions we cannot think any intuition. We may, however, entitle the merely intelligible cause of appearances in general the transcendental object, solely in order that we may have something which corresponds to sensibility regarded as a",["- \"Receptivität\" rendered \"receptivity\"; \"Vermögen\" in \"Anschauungsvermögen\" rendered \"faculty\".","- \"welche\" taken to refer to the representations (Vorstellungen), not to the forms; the sentence on the relation is restated in two.","- \"Gegenstände heißen\" = \"are entitled objects\". Passage breaks off mid-sentence; the clause continues in krv-b-1478."]],"krv-b-1478":["receptivity. To this transcendental object we can ascribe the whole extent and connection of our possible perceptions, and can say that it is given in itself prior to all experience. Appearances, however, are given in conformity with it, not in themselves, but only in this experience, because they are mere representations, which as perceptions signify an actual object only when this perception is connected with all others according to the rules of the unity of experience. Thus we can say that the actual",["- OCR \"Wahrib nehmungen\", \"si sselbst\" and stray line-number letters (\"b\", \"lo\") silently corrected.","- \"ihm gemäß\" rendered \"in conformity with it\" (the transcendental object).","- The sentence ends \"So kann man sagen: die wirklichen\" and continues in krv-b-1479."]],"krv-b-1479":["things of past time are given in the transcendental object of experience; but they are objects for me, and actual in past time, only so far as I represent to myself that a regressive series of possible perceptions (whether under the guidance of history,",["- OCR \"Gegenstande\" for \"Gegenstände\" is a slip: \"transscendentalen Gegenstande\" rendered \"transcendental object\". The clause continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1480":["[This passage is a fragment of the preceding sentence and is translated there; see krv-b-1479.]",["- The German of krv-b-1480 (\"vergangenen Zeit wirklich, so fern als ich mir vorstelle, daß eine regressive Reihe möglicher Wahrnehmungen (es sei am Leitfaden der Geschichte,\") is already incorporated in the English of krv-b-1479 above, since the sentence is divided between them; the translation of 1479 therefore runs over this fragment."]],"krv-b-1481":["or by the footprints of causes and effects), in accordance with empirical laws, in a word, the course of the world, leads to a past series of time as condition of the present time. This series, however, is represented as actual only in the",["- \"mit einem Worte der Weltlauf\" rendered \"in a word, the course of the world\".","- Clause continues in krv-b-1482."]],"krv-b-1482":["connection of a possible experience, and not in itself, so that all events which have elapsed from time immemorial before my own existence mean nothing but the possibility of prolonging the chain of experience from the present perception upwards to the conditions which determine this perception in respect of time.",["- OCR \"Moglichkeit\" corrected to \"Möglichkeit\"; stray \"lo\" dropped.","- \"aufwärts\" rendered \"upwards\", i.e. regressively."]],"krv-b-1483":["If, then, I represent to myself all existing objects of the senses in all time and in all spaces together, I do not place them in both of these prior to experience. This representation is nothing but the thought of a possible experience in its absolute completeness. In it alone are those objects (which are nothing but mere representations) given. To say that they exist prior to all my experience means only that they are to be met with in that part of experience to which I, starting from perception, must first advance",["- OCR \"Bolständigkeit\" corrected to \"Vollständigkeit\"; stray \"5\", \"b\" dropped.","- \"in beide hinein\" rendered \"in both of these\" (time and space).","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"allererst fortschreiten\"); not completed, and \"must\" is supplied from the construction \"zu welchem … fortschreiten [muß]\" — flagged here as supplied: [must]."]],"krv-b-1484":["...must be met with. The cause of the empirical conditions of this advance, and therefore the question at which members, or how far, I may come upon such conditions in the regress, is transcendental, and is therefore necessarily unknown to me. But that is not what concerns us. What concerns us is only | the rule of the advance of experience, in which objects, that is, appearances, are given to me. In the upshot, too, it is quite immaterial whether I say that in the empirical advance in space I can come upon stars a hundred times more remote than the outermost that I see, or whether I say that perhaps such stars are to be met with in cosmic space, even if no human being has ever perceived them or ever will. For even if they were given as things in themselves, without relation to possible experience in general, they would still be nothing for me, and so would not be objects, save in so far as they are contained in the series of the empirical regress. Only in another connection, namely when these same appearances are to be used for the cosmological idea of an absolute whole, and when the question at issue therefore goes beyond the limits of possible experience, is the distinction of the manner in which we take the reality of the objects of the senses here thought of of importance, in order to guard against a deceptive illusion, which must inevitably arise from misinterpretation of our own empirical concepts.",["- The OCR garble \"auf derglei— eben\" is left as it stands in the German; the translation begins mid-sentence, so \"must be met with\" is a fragment, not completed. The reading \"auf dergleichen\" is probable but doubtful.","- \"noths wendig\", \"Mber\" (= Aber), \"Unter: scheidung\", \"unver: weichlich\" are OCR slips, silently corrected.","- \"Erfahrungsbegriffe\" rendered \"empirical concepts\" (literally, concepts of experience).","- \"gedachter Gegenstände der Sinne\" rendered \"the objects of the senses here thought of\"."]],"krv-b-1485":["THE ANTINOMY OF PURE REASON\nSection the Seventh.",["- Heading; the title continues in the next two passages.","- \"Der\" begins the title in the German and is carried here by \"THE\"."]],"krv-b-1486":["Critical Solution of the Cosmological Conflict of",["- Heading fragment; it continues in the next passage (\"Vernunft mit sich selbst\")."]],"krv-b-1487":["Reason with Itself.",["- Completes the heading begun in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-1488":["The whole antinomy of pure reason rests upon the following dialectical argument: If the conditioned is given, the whole series of all its conditions is likewise given; now objects of the senses are given to us as conditioned; therefore, etc. Through this syllogism of reason, the major premiss of which seems so natural and evident, there are introduced, in accordance with the diversity of the conditions (in the synthesis of appearances) in so far as they constitute a series, just as many cosmological ideas. These postulate the absolute totality of such series, and in doing so inevitably set reason at variance with itself. Before we expose what is deceptive in this sophistical argument, we must prepare ourselves for the task by rectifying and determining certain concepts which occur in it.",["- \"Vernunftschluß\" = syllogism of reason; \"vernünftelnden\" = \"sophistical\", suggesting specious reasoning.","- Restated the relative clause \"welche ... versetzen\" as two sentences for clarity."]],"krv-b-1489":["First of all, the following proposition is clear and indubitably certain: if the conditioned is given, a regress in the series of all its conditions is thereby set as a task for us. For this is already involved in the very concept of the conditioned, namely that through it something is referred to a condition, and, if this condition is itself conditioned, to a more remote condition, and so through all the members of the series. This proposition is therefore analytic, and is exalted above all fear of a transcendental criticism. It is a logical postulate of reason, that it should trace, through the understanding, that connection of a concept with its conditions which attaches already to the concept itself, and should continue it as far as possible.",["- \"aufgegeben\" rendered \"set as a task\"; the German plays on *gegeben*/*aufgegeben* (given/set as a task), which is lost in English.","- Divided the German sentence at \"denn\"."]],"krv-b-1490":["Further: if the conditioned and its condition are both things in themselves, then, when the former is given, the regress to the latter is not merely set as a task; the latter is thereby actually already given along with it. And since this holds of all members of the series, the complete series of conditions, and therefore the unconditioned also, is thereby given at the same time, or rather is presupposed in the very fact that the conditioned, which was possible only through that series, is given. Here the synthesis of the conditioned with its condition is a synthesis of the mere understanding, which represents things as they are, without considering whether and how we can come to know them. On the other hand, when I have to deal with appearances, which as mere representations are not given at all unless I attain to knowledge of them (that is, to the appearances themselves, for they are nothing but empirical modes of knowledge), I cannot say in the same sense that if the conditioned is given, all its conditions (as appearances) are likewise given. I cannot therefore in any way infer the absolute totality of the series of them. For appearances are, in apprehension itself, nothing but an empirical synthesis (in space and time), and are therefore given only in this synthesis. Now it does not at all follow that, when the conditioned (in the appearance) is given, the synthesis which constitutes its empirical condition is also thereby given and presupposed. This synthesis first takes place in the regress, and never without it. But what we can certainly say in such a case is that a regress to the conditions, that is, a continued empirical synthesis, is enjoined or set as a task on this side, and that there cannot be any lack of conditions given through this regress.",["- \"Kenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\" in both occurrences, as the sense requires; \"empirische Kenntnisse\" rendered \"empirical modes of knowledge\".","- \"moglich\" is an OCR slip for \"möglich\", silently corrected.","- \"auf dieser Seite\" (\"on this side\") is kept literally; it means the side of the conditioned in appearance.","- Divided the long sentence \"Dagegen ... schließen\" into two."]],"krv-b-1491":["From this it is evident that the major premiss of the cosmological syllogism takes the conditioned in the transcendental meaning of a pure category, while the minor premiss takes it in the empirical meaning of a concept of the understanding applied to mere appearances. Consequently there is found in the argument that dialectical deception which is entitled *sophisma figurae dictionis*. This deception is not, however, contrived; it is a quite natural illusion of common reason. For through common reason we presuppose (in the major premiss) the conditions and their series, as it were without looking into the matter, when something is given as conditioned. This is nothing but the logical demand that we should assume complete premisses for a given conclusion; and in the connection of the conditioned with its condition no order in time is to be met with, the two being presupposed as given together in themselves. Further, it is just as natural (in the minor premiss) to regard appearances as things in themselves, and equally as objects given to the mere understanding, as was done in the major premiss, where I abstracted from all the conditions of intuition under which alone objects can be given. But in doing so we overlooked a noteworthy difference between the concepts. The synthesis of the conditioned with its condition, and the whole series of the latter (in the major premiss), carried with it no limitation through time and no concept of succession. The empirical synthesis, on the other hand, and the series of conditions of the appearance (which is subsumed in the minor premiss), is necessarily successive, and is given only in time, one member after another. Consequently I could not here presuppose the absolute totality of the synthesis and of the series thereby represented, as I could there; for there all the members of the series are given in themselves (without condition of time), whereas here they are possible only through the successive regress, which is given only through our actually carrying it out.",["- Garbled OCR \"von in schränkung\" read as \"von Einschränkung\"; \"fucceffiv\" as \"successiv\"; stray line numbers and \"W\", \"ö\" removed.","- \"gemeinen Vernunft\" rendered \"common reason\".","- Divided the sentence beginning \"Denn durch dieselbe\" at the semicolon, and kept \"*sophisma figurae dictionis*\" in Latin.","- \"Prämissen\" and \"Obersatz/Untersatz\" rendered \"premisses\", \"major/minor premiss\"."]],"krv-b-1492":["After the convicting of such a false step in the argument which is commonly laid at the foundation (of the cosmological assertions), both contending parties may justly be dismissed as persons whose claim rests on no sound title. But their dispute is not thereby ended in the sense that they have been convinced that one or both of them is in the wrong in the matter itself which he asserts (in the conclusion), even if he did not know how to build it on valid grounds of proof. Nothing seems clearer than that, of two persons, one of whom asserts that the world has a beginning, the other that the world has no beginning but has existed from eternity, one must nevertheless be in the right",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in the next passage (\"haben müsse\").","- \"Überweisung\" rendered \"convicting\"; \"Fehltritt\" as \"false step\".","- OCR line numbers and stray letters (\"zo\", \"ss\", \"Wielt\") silently corrected or removed; \"klärer\" read as \"klarer\".","- Restated the final long sentence, keeping its sense; \"[one] must be in the right\" is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1493":[". But if that is so, then, since the clearness is equal on both sides,",["- Continues the sentence from the previous passage (\"einer Recht haben müsse\"); the \"must be in the right\" belongs to the preceding fragment.","- Leading full stop supplied only for the English join; the German passage begins \"haben müsse\", translated at the end of the previous passage."]],"krv-b-1494":["it is nevertheless impossible ever to make out on which side the",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1495":["right lies; and the dispute continues as before, even if the parties",["- Fragment; breaks off mid-clause."]],"krv-b-1496":["[there is] therefore no means left of settling the dispute thoroughly and to the satisfaction of both",["- Fragment; the sentence begins before and breaks off after \"beider\" (both). The supplied \"[there is]\" renders \"übrig\" with \"kein Mittel\", and the phrase \"also\" is rendered \"therefore\".","- The German is an incomplete clause, so no completion is offered."]],"krv-b-1497":["...parts to an end than this: that, since they are able to refute one another so admirably,",["- Fragment, broken off at both ends; translated as it stands, without completion. The sentence continues in krv-b-1498."]],"krv-b-1498":["they should at last be convinced that they are disputing about nothing, and that a certain",["- Fragment; the clause continues in krv-b-1499 (\"daß ... ein gewisser transscendentaler Schein\"). Left unfinished."]],"krv-b-1499":["transcendental illusion has painted before them a reality where",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"wo ...\"); not completed. *Schein* rendered \"illusion\"; *vorgemalt* as \"painted before them\"."]],"krv-b-1500":["The Eleatic Zeno, a subtle dialectician, was already severely censured by Plato",["- None beyond the fact that the sentence continues in krv-b-1501."]],"krv-b-1501":["as a wanton sophist, because, in order to display his art, he sought to prove one and the same proposition by plausible arguments, and then, shortly afterwards, to overthrow it again by others equally strong.",["- The sentence is completed in krv-b-1502 (\"zu beweisen ... umzustürzen suchte\"); I have joined the end here for coherence only by anticipation. See next note.","- Doubtful: strictly, the tail \"einerlei Satz ... umzustürzen suchte\" belongs to krv-b-1502, so it is repeated there."]],"krv-b-1502":["to show his art, one and the same proposition by plausible arguments, and shortly afterwards to overthrow it again by others equally strong, he sought to prove.",["- OCR slip \"bewei— s seu\" read as \"beweisen\"; line-number debris \"s\" removed.","- The passage opens mid-clause (continuing krv-b-1501); the English follows the German order awkwardly because the fragment cannot be made a sentence on its own."]],"krv-b-1503":["He asserted that God (presumably for him nothing but the world) was",["- Parenthesis kept as in the German; \"vermuthlich\" = \"presumably\". Breaks off before the predicates in krv-b-1504."]],"krv-b-1504":["neither finite nor infinite, neither in motion nor at rest, and neither like nor unlike any other thing. Those who judged him on this account thought that he meant to deny outright two propositions that contradict one another, which is absurd. But I do not find",["- Restated: \"Es schien denen, die ihn ... beurteilten, er habe ... ableugnen wollen\" rendered as \"thought that he meant to deny\". OCR \"o\" (line number) dropped.","- Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in krv-b-1505."]],"krv-b-1505":["that this can justly be laid to his charge. The first",["- \"Den ersteren dieser Sätze\" completes in krv-b-1506 (\"the first of these propositions\")."]],"krv-b-1506":["of these propositions I shall shortly examine more closely. As to the others, if by the word \"God\" he understood the universe, he was bound",["- *beleuchten* rendered \"examine\"; \"mußte er allerdings sagen\" continues in krv-b-1507."]],"krv-b-1507":["to say that it is neither permanently present in its place (at rest), nor changes it (moves), since all places are only in the universe, which itself is therefore in no place. If the universe comprehends in itself everything that exists, then to that extent it is also neither like nor unlike any other",["- OCR \"eriftirt\" read as \"existirt\"; line number \"25\" dropped.","- \"so ist es auch so fern\" rendered \"then to that extent it is also\"; sentence completed in krv-b-1508."]],"krv-b-1508":["thing, since there is nothing else outside it with which it could be compared. If two opposed judgments presuppose an inadmissible condition, then, in spite of their conflict (which is yet no real contradiction), both of them fall away, since the condition falls away under which alone each of these propositions was to hold.",["- Split into the sentence-continuation and a new sentence only by the German's own break; the first words complete krv-b-1507.","- *Widerstreit* = \"conflict\", distinguished from *Widerspruch* = \"contradiction\"; stray \"o\" dropped."]],"krv-b-1509":["If anyone said, \"Every body either smells good or does not smell good,\" a third case is possible, namely that it has no smell at all (gives off no scent), and so both conflicting propositions may be false. If I say, \"It is either sweet-smelling or not sweet-smelling\" (*vel suaveolens vel non suaveolens*), the two judgments are opposed contradictorily, and only the first is false; its contradictory opposite, namely \"some bodies are not sweet-smelling\", includes also the bodies that have no smell at all. In the previous opposition (*per disparata*) the contingent condition of the concept of body (smell) remained attached to the conflicting judgment, and was therefore not also cancelled by it; hence the latter was not the contradictory opposite of the former.",["- \"riecht nicht gut\" given as \"does not smell good\"; \"ausdufte\" as \"gives off no scent\".","- Stray \"ab\" and line number \"10\" dropped as OCR debris.","- \"contradictorisch\" rendered \"contradictorily\"; Latin tags kept in italics."]],"krv-b-1510":["If, then, I say, \"The world is, in respect of space, either infinite or not infinite\" (*non est infinitus*), then if the first proposition is false, its contradictory opposite, \"The world is not infinite\", must be true. I should thereby be removing only an infinite world, without positing another, namely the finite. But if it ran, \"The world is either infinite or finite (non-infinite)\", both might be false. For I should then be regarding the world as determined in itself in respect of its magnitude, since in the opposition I do not merely remove the infinity, and with it perhaps the whole of its separate existence, but add to the world, as a thing real in itself, a determination; and this may equally well be false, namely if the world is not given at all as a thing in itself, and therefore not as infinite or as finite in respect of its magnitude. Let me be permitted to entitle this kind of opposition the dialectical, and that of contradiction the analytic opposition. Of two judgments which are dialectically opposed, both may therefore be false, because the one does not merely contradict the other but says something more than is required for contradiction.",["- Line number \"20\" dropped. \"Hieße es\" rendered \"if it ran\"; \"nennen darf\" as \"to entitle\", per the method.","- \"Bestimmung\" = \"determination\"; \"Gegensatz\" = \"opposition\".","- Long final sentences kept whole; the middle one is lightly restructured around \"since\"."]],"krv-b-1511":["If we regard the two propositions, \"The world is infinite in magnitude\" and \"The world is finite in magnitude\", as contradictorily opposed, we assume that the world (the whole series of appearances) is a thing in itself. For it remains, whether I remove the infinite or the finite regress in the series of its appearances. But if I set aside this presupposition, or this transcendental illusion, and deny that it is a thing in itself, the contradictory conflict of the two assertions is changed into one that is merely dialectical. And since the world does not exist in itself at all (independently of the regressive series of my representations), it exists neither as an infinite whole in itself nor as a finite whole in itself. It is to be met with only in the empirical regress of the series of appearances, and not at all by itself. Hence, if this series is always conditioned, it is never given as a whole; and the world is therefore not an unconditioned whole, and does not exist as such a whole, either of infinite or of finite magnitude.",["- OCR corrections: \"fidh\"→\"sich\", \"je\"→\"so\", \"ver wandelt\"→\"verwandelt\", \"Welt!)\" footnote mark dropped (footnote text not supplied), \"Große\"→\"Größe\"; line number \"3\" dropped.","- \"Denn sie bleibt, ich mag ...\" is elliptical; I read \"sie\" as the world's status as a thing in itself, i.e. \"it [the supposition] remains\".","- \"Vorstellungen\" = \"representations\"; \"Erscheinungen\" = \"appearances\"."]],"krv-b-1512":["What has here been said of the first cosmological idea, namely the absolute totality of magnitude in appearance, holds also of all the rest. The series of conditions is to be met with only in the regressive synthesis itself, and not in the appearance in itself, as a thing existing independently and given prior to all regress. Hence I shall also have to say: the multitude of the parts in a given appearance is",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in krv-b-1513 (\"in itself neither finite nor infinite\").","- Supplied nothing; \"an sich\" in the next passage completes \"ist ... an sich\"."]],"krv-b-1513":["in itself neither finite nor infinite, because appearance is nothing existing in itself, and the parts are given first of all through the regress of the decomposing synthesis, and in it; and this regress is never given absolutely as a whole, either as finite or as infinite. The same holds of the series of subordinated causes, or of the conditioned, up to unconditionally necessary existence. This series can never be regarded as in itself, in its totality, either finite or infinite, since, as a series of subordinated representations, it consists only in the dynamical regress, and cannot exist at all prior to this regress, as a series of things in themselves subsisting by themselves.",["- Reading \"sich\" as the end of \"an sich\" begun in krv-b-1512; the opening of this passage is therefore a continuation.","- OCR \"fidh\"→\"sich\". \"Über einander geordneten\" = \"subordinated\" in sense; \"dynamischen\" = \"dynamical\"."]],"krv-b-1514":["Thus the antinomy of pure reason in its cosmological ideas is removed, by showing that it is merely dialectical, a conflict due to an illusion, which arises from our having applied the idea of absolute totality, valid only as a condition of things in themselves, to appearances, which exist only in representation and, when they form a series, in successive regress, and otherwise not at all. But we may also, conversely, draw from this antinomy a true benefit, not dogmatic indeed, but critical and doctrinal: namely, that of proving indirectly the transcendental ideality of appearances, should anyone find the direct proof in the Transcendental Aesthetic insufficient. The proof would consist in this dilemma. If the world is a whole existing in itself, it is either finite or infinite. Now both the former and the latter are false (as the proofs of the antithesis on the one side and of the thesis on the other, given above, show). It is therefore also false that the world (the sum of all appearances) is a whole existing in itself. From this it follows that appearances in general are nothing outside our representations, which is just what we meant by their transcendental ideality. |",["- OCR: \"kos— mologischen\", \"dialek— tisch\", \"Vor stellung\" repaired; \"fidh\" not present; \"Ästhetik\" rendered \"Aesthetic\".","- Parenthesis \"laut der ... andererseits\" restated as \"as the proofs ... show\"; \"einer- und ... andererseits\" rendered \"on the one side ... on the other\".","- Final sign | kept as in the source."]],"krv-b-1515":["This remark is of importance. It shows that the above proofs of the fourfold antinomy are not delusive trickeries but well grounded, on the presupposition, namely, that appearances, or a world of sense which comprehends them all within itself, are things in themselves. The conflict of the propositions drawn from this presupposition reveals, however, that there is a falsity in the presupposition, and so brings us to a discovery of the true constitution of things as objects of the senses. The transcendental dialectic therefore in no way favours scepticism, though it does favour the sceptical method, which can find in it an example of its great utility. This is so when the arguments of reason are allowed to confront one another in complete freedom. Though these arguments do not in the end yield what we were seeking, they will always yield something useful and serviceable for the correction of our judgments.",["The long final sentence is slightly recast: \"die ... Dienliches liefern werden\" is made a separate sentence.","The footnote asterisk before \"Sinnenwelt\" is omitted because no footnote text is given in the passage. \"in dem Voraussetzung\" is read as \"in der Voraussetzung\".","\"Blendwerke\" is rendered \"delusive trickeries\" and \"gründlich\" \"well grounded\"."]],"krv-b-1516":["Eighth Section of the Antinomy of Pure Reason.",["\"Der\" is the opening of the title: \"Of the Antinomy of Pure Reason, Eighth Section\"."]],"krv-b-1517":["Regulative principle of pure reason in respect",["Heading fragment; it continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1518":["of the cosmological ideas.",["Completes the heading begun in the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-1519":["Since through the cosmological principle of totality no maximum of the series of conditions is given in a world of sense regarded as a thing in itself, but can only be set as a task in the regress of that series, the principle of pure reason here in question retains its good validity in this corrected meaning. It does so, indeed, not as an axiom requiring us to think the totality as actual in the object,",["\"ausgegeben\" is read as \"aufgegeben\" (\"set as a task\"), as the sense requires; \"Marimum\" and \"ans\" are corrected silently.","The sentence is broken off and continues in the next passage. \"gedachte\" is rendered \"here in question\"."]],"krv-b-1520":["but as a problem for the understanding, and so for the subject, in order",["Fragment; the sentence continues in the next two passages."]],"krv-b-1521":["that, in conformity with the completeness required in the idea, it may institute the regress in the series of",["\"der Vollständigkeit in der Idee gemäß\" is taken as \"in conformity with the completeness required in the idea\"."]],"krv-b-1522":["conditions for a given conditioned, and may continue it.",["Completes the sentence begun two passages earlier."]],"krv-b-1523":["For in sensibility, that is, in space and time, every condition to which we can attain in the exposition of given appearances",["\"Bedingung\" is restored from the OCR \"Bedin- 5 gnug\". The line number is dropped."]],"krv-b-1524":["is again conditioned; for these appearances are not objects in themselves, in which the absolutely unconditioned might possibly find a place,",["\"diese\" is taken to refer to the appearances (Erscheinungen) of the previous passage."]],"krv-b-1525":["but merely empirical representations, which must always find in intuition the condition",["Fragment; the relative clause is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1526":["which determines them in respect of space or time. The principle of reason is therefore properly only a rule, which",["\"welche sie ... bestimmt\": the subject is the condition found in intuition. The line number is dropped."]],"krv-b-1527":["commands a regress in the series of the conditions of given appearances,",["The German word order is inverted, with the object placed before the verb of the next passage; the sentence continues there."]],"krv-b-1528":["a regress which is never permitted to stop at an absolutely unconditioned.",["\"dem\" is rendered \"a regress which\", to keep the English clear."]],"krv-b-1529":["It is therefore not a principle of the possibility of experience",["\"Erfah⸗rung\" is restored from the line break. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1530":["and of the empirical knowledge of the objects of the senses, and consequently",["\"Erkenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-1531":["not a principle of the understanding; for every experience is enclosed within its limits",["The parenthesis \"(der gegebenen Anschauung gemäß)\" follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1532":["(in accordance with the given intuition); nor is it a constitutive",["\"conftituti-ves\" is corrected to \"constitutive\"."]],"krv-b-1533":["principle of reason, enabling us to extend the concept of the world of sense beyond all possible",["\"enabling us\" is supplied to carry the force of \"den Begriff ... zu erweitern\"."]],"krv-b-1534":["experience; it is, rather, a principle of the greatest possible",["\"liebe\" is read as \"liche\" (mög-liche). \"sondern ein Grundsatz\" is given as \"it is, rather, a principle\"."]],"krv-b-1535":["continuation and extension of experience, in accordance with which no empirical limit must count as an absolute limit. It is thus a principle of reason which, as a rule, postulates what ought to be done by us in the regress,",["Sentence divided at \"also ein Principium\", to ease the construction."]],"krv-b-1536":["and does not anticipate what is given in the object in itself prior to all regress.",["\"fol\" is read as \"soll\"; \"Ne-gressus\" is read as \"Regressus\"."]],"krv-b-1537":["Hence I entitle it a regulative principle",["\"nenne ich es\" is rendered \"I entitle it\" in keeping with the heißen/nennen convention."]],"krv-b-1538":["of reason; whereas the principle of the absolute totality of the series of conditions, as given in the object (in the appearances) in itself, would be a constitutive cosmological principle, the nullity of which",["\"Nich-tigkeit\" is restored across the line break; the relative clause is completed in the following passages."]],"krv-b-1539":["I have wished by this very distinction to indicate, and thereby to prevent",["Fragment: \"ver-hindern\" continues into the next passage."]],"krv-b-1540":["(as otherwise unavoidably happens, through transcendental subreption) our ascribing to an idea which serves merely as a rule",["\"daß man nicht ... beimesse\": the \"nicht\" is pleonastic and left untranslated. The parenthesis is moved ahead of the verb for smoothness."]],"krv-b-1541":["objective reality.",["Completes the sentence begun in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-1542":["In order, then, to determine properly the meaning of this rule of pure reason,",["\"be-stimmen\" is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1543":["we must first observe that it cannot tell us what the object is, but only how the empirical regress is to be instituted in order to arrive at the complete concept of the object. For if the former were the case, it would be a constitutive principle, of which kind none is ever possible from pure reason. We cannot, therefore, have the intention of saying by it that the series of conditions for a given conditioned is in itself finite or infinite. For thereby a mere idea of absolute totality, which is created solely within itself, would be thinking an object that can be given in no experience, since an objective reality independent of empirical synthesis would be conferred on a series of appearances. The idea of reason will therefore prescribe to the regressive synthesis in the series of conditions only a rule, according to which it proceeds from the conditioned, by means of all the conditions subordinated to one another, to the unconditioned, although this is never reached. For the absolutely unconditioned is not met with in experience at all.",["\"zu bestimmen ... so ist zuvörderst zu bemerken\": the opening is joined to the previous passage's \"In order, then, to determine\", so the main clause begins \"we must first observe\".","\"angustellen\" is read as \"anzustellen\"; the stray \"es\" before \"Denn\", the unclosed parenthesis and the line number are dropped.","\"die lediglich in ihr selbst geschaffen ist\" is rendered \"which is created solely within itself\", and \"Synthesis\" is kept as \"synthesis\"."]],"krv-b-1544":["To this end we must first determine exactly the synthesis of a series, so far as it is never complete. Two expressions are commonly employed in this connection, which are supposed to differ in some respect, though no one is able to state properly the ground of the distinction. The mathematicians speak solely of a *progressus in infinitum*. The inquirers into concepts (philosophers) would allow in its place only the | expression *progressus in indefinitum*. I shall not pause to examine the scruple which led them to suggest such a distinction, nor the [good] or fruitless use made of it, but shall try to determine these concepts exactly in relation to my purpose.",["The sign | is carried over as in the source; the stray number 18 is dropped.","\"grten\" is an OCR slip. I read \"guten\" (\"good\"), shown in brackets because it is conjectural.","\"Forscher der Begriffe\" is rendered \"inquirers into concepts\"; \"Bedenklichkeit\" is rendered \"scruple\"."]],"krv-b-1545":["Of a straight line one can rightly say that it can be produced to infinity, and here the distinction between the infinite and the indefinitely distant progress (*progressus in indefinitum*) would be an empty subtlety. For although, when we say \"Produce a line\", it is certainly more correct to add *in indefinitum* than *in infinitum*, since the former means no more than \"produce it as far as you please\", the latter \"you shall never cease to produce it\" (which is not here the intention), yet when only what can be done is in question, the former expression is quite",["The long sentence is kept in one piece with its dashes replaced by commas, to keep the construction visible. \"unbestimmbar weiten\" is rendered \"indefinitely distant\".","The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"ganz ...\")."]],"krv-b-1546":["correct; for you can always make it longer to infinity. And so it is in all cases where we speak only of the progressus, that is, the advance from the condition to the conditioned; this possible advance proceeds to infinity in the series of appearances. From a parental pair you can proceed without end in the descending line of generation, and can quite well think that the line does actually so proceed in the world. For here reason never requires absolute totality of the series, since it does not presuppose such totality as a condition and as given (*datum*),",["The stray numbers 8 and 20 and the OCR \"se\" are dropped. \"fort gehen\" is taken as one word.","The sentence is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1547":["but only as something conditioned, which is merely [capable of being given] (*dabile*) and is added to without end.",["The text reads \"angeblich (dabile)\". I take it to be a corruption of \"angebbar\" and render the sense of *dabile*; the reading is doubtful."]],"krv-b-1548":["Quite otherwise is it with the problem: how far does the regress extend, which ascends from the given conditioned to the conditions in a series; can I say that it is a regress to infinity, or only a regress extending indeterminably far (*in indefinitum*); and whether, accordingly, I can ascend to infinity from the men now living through",["\"Res gressus\" is read as \"Regressus\". \"unbestimmbar weit\" is rendered \"indeterminably far\", in keeping with the previous passages. The sentence is broken off and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1549":["the series of their forefathers, or whether it can only be said that, however far I have gone back, never",["\"ob o nur\" is read as \"oder ob nur\" (OCR slip). \"nie-mals\" is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1550":["is an empirical ground met with for [regarding] the series anywhere",["The passage breaks off at \"für\"; the missing verb phrase is not supplied in the translation, and the bracketed \"[regarding]\" is only a minimal bridge."]],"krv-b-1551":["[…] to be limited, so that I am entitled, and at the same time bound, to seek out for each of the primal ancestors a still further ancestor, though not indeed to presuppose one. I say, therefore: if the whole is given in empirical intuition, the regress in the series of its inner conditions proceeds to infinity. But if only one member of the series is given, from which the regress is first to proceed to absolute totality, then there takes place only a regress of indeterminate extent (*in indefinitum*). Thus of the division of a matter given between its limits (of a body) it must be said that it proceeds to infinity. For this matter is given as a whole, and therefore with all its possible parts, in empirical intuition. Now since the condition of this whole is its part, and the condition of this part is the part of the part, and so on, and since in this regress of decomposition no unconditioned",["- The passage opens mid-sentence; \"obgleich eben nicht vorauszusetzen\" is rendered as it stands, and the OCR stop-and-colon after \"vorauszusetzen\" is treated as a stray mark. Doubtful reading.","- \"So o muß\" corrected silently to \"So muß\"; stray \"s\" before \"und\" dropped as OCR noise.","- The sentence ending \"…unconditioned\" breaks off and is continued in the next passage.","- Regressus kept in English as \"regress\"; \"in indefinitum\" kept in Latin italics."]],"krv-b-1552":["(indivisible) member of this series of conditions is ever met with, there is not only no empirical ground for stopping in the division, but the further members of the division still to be continued are themselves empirically given prior to this further division; that is, the division proceeds to infinity. On the other hand, the series of ancestors of any given man is not given in its absolute totality in any possible experience; yet the regress proceeds from each member of this line of descent to a higher, so that no empirical limit is to be met with which would exhibit a member as absolutely unconditioned. But since, none the less, the members which could supply the condition for this are likewise not already contained in the empirical intuition of the whole prior to the regress, [the following continues]",["- \"Beugung\" is presumably an OCR slip for \"Zeugung\" (generation), hence \"line of descent\"; doubtful reading.","- \"s\" and \"o\" before \"auch\"/\"geht\" are OCR marks, dropped.","- The last sentence breaks off and is completed in the next passage; my bracketed \"[the following continues]\" is an editorial marker only."]],"krv-b-1553":["empirical intuition of the whole prior to the regress, so",["- Fragment continuing the previous sentence; \"liegen\" is already rendered there, so only the closing words appear here. The previous passage's bracketed marker should be disregarded in favour of this fragment."]],"krv-b-1554":["this regress does not proceed to infinity (as in the division of what is given), but to an indeterminable distance in the search for further members additional to those given, | which in turn are always given only as conditioned.",["- The footnote marker \"1\" at the end is retained in the source but no footnote text is present in the passage.","- The sign | is carried over as it stands."]],"krv-b-1555":["In neither of the two cases, whether the *regressus in infinitum* | or the *regressus in indefinitum*, is the series of conditions regarded as given in the object as infinite. What are given are not things in themselves but only appearances, which, as conditions of one another, are given only in the regress of the conditions. [The question is not whether this series of conditions is in itself finite or infinite; for it is nothing in itself.] The question is, rather, how we are to carry out the empirical regress, and how far we are to continue it. And here there is a notable difference in respect of the rule of this advance. If the whole has been empirically given, it is possible to go back to infinity in the | series of its inner conditions. But if the whole is not given, and is first to be given through empirical regress, I can only say that it is possible to proceed to ever higher conditions of the series to infinity. In the former case I could say: there are always more members present and empirically given than I reach through the regress (of decomposition); in the second case, however: I can always go still further in the regress, because no member is empirically given as absolutely unconditioned, and a higher member therefore still remains possible, and the inquiry after it accordingly necessary. There it was necessary to meet with more members of the series; here it is always necessary to inquire after more, because no experience is absolutely limiting. For either you have no perception which absolutely limits your empirical regress, and then you must not regard your regress as completed; or you have such a perception limiting your series, and then it cannot be a part of the series you have traversed (since that which limits must be distinguished from that which is thereby limited), and you must therefore continue your regress also to this condition, and so on.",["- The OCR text is corrupt in the middle (\"nur im Regressus der Bedingungen an sich selbst sei, ob endlich oder unendlich, denn sie ist nichts an sich selbst, sondern:\"). The bracketed sentence is a reconstruction from sense, not a translation of the damaged text; doubtful reading.","- \"fortzug eben\" read as \"fortzugehen\"; \"Rez 2 gressus\" as \"Regressus\"; \"F\", \"f\", \"?\" are OCR noise, dropped. \"als möglich?\" read as \"als möglich\".","- Marginal numerals and the signs | are carried over where they stand."]],"krv-b-1556":["The following section will set these remarks in their proper light by applying them. |",["- None beyond the carried-over sign |."]],"krv-b-1557":["Of the\nAntinomy of Pure Reason,\nSection the Ninth.",["- Heading rendered as it stands; \"Der\" at the start is a fragment of the running arrangement."]],"krv-b-1558":["On the Empirical Employment of the Regulative Principle of Reason in respect of All Cosmological Ideas.",["- Stray quotation mark before \"der Vernunft\" dropped as OCR noise."]],"krv-b-1559":["Since, as we have repeatedly shown, there is no transcendental",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the following passages."]],"krv-b-1560":["employment, either of pure concepts of the understanding or of concepts of reason;",["- \"Verstandes- als Vernunftbegriffen\" rendered \"concepts of the understanding or of concepts of reason\" for clarity."]],"krv-b-1561":["since the absolute totality of the series of conditions in the world of sense rests solely upon a transcendental employment of reason,",["- \"fußt\" rendered \"rests\"."]],"krv-b-1562":["which demands this unconditioned completeness from that which it presupposes as thing in itself, while the world of sense contains nothing of the kind:",["- Sentence restated across fragments; the main clause follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1563":["there can never be any further question of the absolute magnitude",["- \"die Rede … sein\" rendered \"there can be question of\"."]],"krv-b-1564":["of the series in the world of sense, whether they may be limited or in themselves unlimited,",["- Plural \"sie\" refers to the series (Reihen)."]],"krv-b-1565":["but only of how far we are to go back in the empirical regress, in tracing experience back to its conditions, so as, in accordance with",["- Line number \"15\" and the dash in \"Zurück— führung\" dropped as OCR artefacts."]],"krv-b-1566":["the rule of reason, to stop at no answer to its questions other than one adequate to the object.",["- \"auge— messenen\" read as \"angemessenen\"."]],"krv-b-1567":["It is therefore only the validity of the principle of reason, as a",["- Fragment; continues below."]],"krv-b-1568":["rule for the continuation and magnitude of a possible experience, that alone remains to us, once its invalidity as a constitutive principle of appearances in themselves has been sufficiently demonstrated. Moreover, if we can set the former indubitably before our eyes, the conflict of reason with itself is completely brought to an end; for not only is the illusion which set reason at variance with itself removed by critical solution, but in its place the sense in which reason agrees with itself, and the misinterpretation of which alone occasioned the conflict, is disclosed, and a principle otherwise dialectical is converted into a doctrinal one. In fact, if this principle can be confirmed in its subjective meaning, as determining the greatest possible employment of the understanding in experience in a manner adequate to the objects of experience, it is exactly the same as if it determined, like an axiom (which is impossible from pure reason), the objects in themselves *a priori*. For even such an axiom could have no greater influence, in respect of the objects of experience, upon the extension and correction of our knowledge than that of proving itself active in the most extended empirical employment of our understanding.",["- \"Pedeutung\" read as \"Bedeutung\".","- \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"; the stray \"I\" at the end is dropped.","- \"ungezweifelt\" rendered \"indubitably\"; \"jene\" taken to refer to the validity of the principle."]],"krv-b-1569":["I.\nSolution of the Cosmological Idea\nof the",["- Title continues in the next fragments."]],"krv-b-1570":["Totality of the Composition of Appearances",["- \"Erscheinung en\" read as \"Erscheinungen\"."]],"krv-b-1571":["into a Cosmic Whole.",["- \"Weltganzes\" rendered \"cosmic whole\"."]],"krv-b-1572":["Here, as in the other cosmological questions, the ground of the regulative principle of reason is the proposition that in the empirical regress no experience of an absolute limit, and therefore of no condition as one that is empirically absolutely unconditioned, can be met with. The ground of this is that such an experience would have to contain in itself a limitation of appearances by nothing or by the void, upon which the regress, as it is carried on, might come by means of a perception; and this is impossible. |",["- OCR noise (\"us\", \"T\", \"schlecht⸗ hin\", \"1af möglich\") silently corrected to \"schlechthin\" and \"unmöglich\".","- \"Gränze\" rendered \"limit\"; the sign | carried over."]],"krv-b-1573":["This proposition, which says just as much as that in the empirical regress I can only ever arrive at a condition which must itself in turn be regarded as empirically conditioned, contains the rule *in terminis*: that however far I may have advanced in the ascending series, I must always inquire after a higher member of the series, whether or not this becomes known to me through experience.",["- \"in terminis\" kept in Latin italics. Line number \"20\" dropped."]],"krv-b-1574":["Now for the solution of the first cosmological problem nothing further is required than to decide whether, in the regress to the unconditioned magnitude of the cosmic whole (in respect of time and space), this never-limited ascent can be entitled a regress to infinity, or only a regress continued indeterminably (*in indefinitum*).",["- \"Weltganzen\" rendered \"cosmic whole\"; \"heißen\" rendered \"be entitled\". The sentence is completed by the next fragment."]],"krv-b-1575":["to",["- Stray OCR fragment; left as it stands."]],"krv-b-1576":["[the sentence is completed in 1574 above; the text here repeats \"niemals begränzte Aufsteigen … (in indefinitum)\"]",["- Passage is a duplicate of the end of the preceding sentence as the OCR splits it; \"7\" is an OCR mark. The translation of the sentence is given under 1574; doubtful division."]],"krv-b-1577":["The mere general representation of the series of all past states of the world, and likewise of the things which coexist in cosmic space, is itself nothing but a possible empirical regress. I think this regress to myself, though still indeterminately, and through it alone can the concept of such a series of conditions for the given perception arise.”) Now I have the world-whole always only in concept, never (as a whole) in intuition. Hence I cannot infer from its magnitude to the magnitude of the regress, and determine the latter in accordance with the former. On the contrary, I must first form a concept of the magnitude of the world through the magnitude of the empirical regress. Of this regress, however, I never know anything more than that from every given member of the series of conditions I must always advance empirically to a higher (more remote) member. Thereby, then, the magnitude of the whole of appearances is in no way determined absolutely. Consequently it also cannot be said that this regress proceeds to infinity. To say so would be to anticipate the members which the regress has not yet reached, and to represent their number as so great that no empirical synthesis could attain to it; and this would be to determine the magnitude of the world prior to the regress (though only negatively), which is impossible. For the world is not given to me by any intuition (in its totality), and consequently its magnitude also is not given prior to the regress. We can therefore say nothing whatever of the magnitude of the world in itself, not even that a *regressus in infinitum* takes place in it. We can only seek the concept of its magnitude in accordance with the rule which determines the empirical regress in it. This rule says no more than that, however far we may have advanced in the series of empirical conditions, we ought nowhere to assume an absolute limit, but must subordinate every appearance, as conditioned, to another as its condition, and so must advance still further to this other. This is the *regressus in indefinitum*, which, because it determines no magnitude in the object, can be distinguished clearly enough from the regress *in infinitum*.",["- The opening and the stray ”) belong to a footnote of the original; the OCR has run text and footnote together, so I have left the mark where it stands.","- The long final sentences are divided and the \"denn/weil\" chain recast; \"dieses\" (the assertion of an infinite regress) and \"diese\" (the world, not the regress) are resolved by the argument.","- Latin *regressus in infinitum / in indefinitum* retained; \"Weltgröße\" = \"magnitude of the world\"."]],"krv-b-1578":["I cannot therefore say: the world is infinite as regards past time or space. For such a concept of magnitude, as a given infinitude, is empirical, and therefore also, in respect of the world as an object of the senses, absolutely impossible. Nor shall I say: the regress from a given perception—",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage (after the footnote text). Not completed here.","- \"ist empirisch, mithin auch … unmöglich\" is rendered as it stands; the sense is that such a concept would have to be given empirically, which for the sensible world is impossible."]],"krv-b-1579":[") This series of the world can therefore be neither greater nor smaller than the possible empirical regress upon which alone its concept rests. And since this regress can yield neither a determinate infinite, nor, equally, a determinate finite (absolutely bounded), it is clear that we can assume the magnitude of the world to be neither finite nor infinite, because the regress (through which that magnitude is represented) admits of neither. perception to everything that bounds this perception in a series, in space as in past | time, proceeds to infinity; for this presupposes the infinite magnitude of the world. Nor shall I say: it is finite; for the absolute limit is likewise empirically impossible. Accordingly I shall be able to say nothing of the whole object of experience (the world of sense), but only of the rule in accordance with which experience, in conformity with its object, ought to be instituted and continued.",["- The footnote text comes first and the main-text continuation of the preceding passage (\"nehmung an …\") follows it, as in the OCR; I kept that order and the | mark.","- The continuation completes \"Nor shall I say: the regress from a given perception—\" of the preceding passage; I have not joined them.","- \"jene\" (the world-magnitude) supplied in the footnote's last clause by judgment."]],"krv-b-1580":["To the cosmological question, then, regarding the magnitude of the world, the first | and negative answer is: the world has no first beginning in time and no outermost limit in space. 16",["- The \"16\" is most probably the OCR of a footnote mark; left as it stands."]],"krv-b-1581":["For in the opposite case it would be bounded on the one side by empty time, and on the other by empty space. Since, however, as appearance it cannot in itself be either of these (for appearance is not a thing in itself), a perception of the limitation by absolutely empty time or empty space would have to be possible, through which these ends of the world would be given in a possible experience. But such an experience, being completely empty of content, is impossible. Consequently an absolute limit of the world is empirically, and therefore also absolutely, impossible.“)",["- A stray \"1\" (line number) was dropped; the closing “) is the OCR of a footnote mark, left in place.","- \"einer- und … andererseits\" restored from the OCR run-together \"einerund\".","- \"empirisch, mithin auch schlechterdings unmöglich\" is kept as it stands; the sense is \"impossible in experience, hence impossible simpliciter\"."]],"krv-b-1582":["From this there follows at once the affirmative answer: the regress in the series of the appearances of the world, as a determination of the magnitude of the world, proceeds *in indefinitum*. This is as much as to say: the world of sense has no absolute magnitude, but the empirical regress (through which alone it can be given on the side of its conditions) has its rule. The rule is that from every member of the series, as a conditioned member, we must always advance to one still more remote (whether through our own experience, or by the guidance of history, or through the chain of effects and their causes), and must nowhere exempt ourselves from the extension of the possible empirical Ge- |",["- Breaks off at \"Ge-\" (the word \"Gebrauchs\", \"use\", is completed only in the next passage); not completed here.","- \"sich … zu überheben\" appears in the following passage; the infinitive construction is read as dependent on \"Regel … fortzuschreiten und …\" and rendered in the plural \"we\".","- OCR debris (\"2%\", \"2.\", \"f\") dropped; \"Bestimmung\" = \"determination\"."]],"krv-b-1583":[") It will be observed that the proof has here been conducted in a quite different manner from the dogmatic proof above, in the antithesis of the first antinomy. There we had allowed the world of sense, according to the common and dogmatic mode of representation, to count as a thing which was given in itself, in its totality, prior to all regress; and, if it did not occupy all time and all spaces, we had denied it any determinate position whatever in either. Hence the conclusion also was different from the present one: namely, we inferred the actual infinity of the world. use of its understanding, which is indeed the proper and sole business of reason in its principles.",["- Footnote first, then the concluding words of the main sentence begun in the preceding passage (\"…brauchs seines Verstandes nirgend zu überheben\"), as the OCR has it; line number \"30\"/\"35\" dropped.","- \"Vorstellungsart\" = \"mode of representation\"; \"Totalität nach\" rendered \"in its totality\".","- The last clause is rendered \"which is indeed the proper and sole business of reason in its principles.\""]],"krv-b-1584":["A determinate empirical regress, which would proceed without cessation in a certain kind of appearances, is not thereby prescribed. It is not prescribed, for instance, that from a living man we must always ascend in a series of ancestors without expecting a first pair, or that in the series of cosmic bodies we must admit no outermost sun. What is enjoined is only the advance from appearances to appearances, even if these should yield no actual perception (when it is, in degree, too weak for our consciousness to become experience), since they nevertheless belong to possible experience.",["- Divided the sentence at the \"z. B.\" and supplied \"It is not prescribed\" and \"we must admit no\" to carry the construction.","- \"Wahrnehmung\" = \"perception\"; line number \"5\" dropped.","- The parenthesis about degree is retained in place, though its antecedent (\"diese … sie\") is loose; it refers to the appearances."]],"krv-b-1585":["All beginning is in time, and all limit of the extended is in space. Space and time, however, are only in the world of sense. Consequently only appearances in the world are limited conditionally; the world itself, however, is limited neither conditionally nor in an unconditioned manner.",["- \"bedingterweise\" / \"auf unbedingte Art\" rendered \"conditionally\" / \"in an unconditioned manner\"."]],"krv-b-1586":["For this very reason, and since the world can never be given as a whole, and since even the series of conditions for a given conditioned cannot be given as a whole in the form of a world-series, the concept of the magnitude of the world is given only through the regress, and not prior to it in a collective intuition. The regress, however, consists always only in the determining of magnitude, and so yields no determinate concept, and therefore also no concept of a magnitude which would be infinite in respect of a certain measure. It does not proceed, then, to infinity (as if the infinite were given), but to an indeterminate distance, in order to yield a magnitude (of experience) which first becomes actual through this regress.",["- The OCR reads \"giebt also seinen bestimmten Begriff\"; I have read \"keinen\" (the following \"also auch keinen\" requires it), silently corrected.","- Divided the sentence at \"Jener … \" and \"geht also …\"; \"Jener\" = the regress, \"der Welt\" supplied for \"Reihe … als Weltreihe\".","- Possible further corruption in \"im Bestimmen der Größe\" (perhaps \"im Bestimmen\" without an object); rendered as it stands."]],"krv-b-1587":["II.\nSolution of the Cosmological Idea\nof the\nTotality of the Division of a Given Whole\nin Intuition.",["- Section heading; \"Auflösung\" = \"solution\", \"Anschauung\" = \"intuition\"."]],"krv-b-1588":["When I divide a whole which is given in intuition, I proceed from a conditioned to the conditions of its possibility. The division of the parts (*subdivisio* or *decompositio*) is a regress in the series of these conditions. The absolute totality of this series would be given only if the regress could arrive at simple parts. But if all the parts in a continually progressing decomposition are always again divisible, the division, that is, the regress from the conditioned to its conditions, proceeds *in infinitum*. For the conditions (the parts) are contained in the conditioned itself; and since the conditioned is given as a whole in an intuition enclosed within its limits, the conditions are all given along with it. The regress, therefore, may not be entitled merely a regress *in indefinitum*, as was alone permissible in the case of the preceding cosmological idea. There I was to proceed from the conditioned to its conditions, which, being outside it, were not given along with it, but were added to it only in the empirical regress. Nevertheless, it is by no means permissible to say of a whole which is divisible to infinity that it consists of infinitely many parts. For although all the parts are contained in the intuition of the whole, the whole division is not contained in it; the division consists only in the continued decomposition, that is, in the regress itself, which first makes the series actual. Since this regress is infinite, all the members (parts) to which it attains are indeed contained in the given whole as an aggregate. But the whole series of the division is not so contained. That series is successively infinite and never complete, and consequently cannot represent an infinite multitude, nor any taking together of such a multitude in a whole.",[]],"krv-b-1589":["The general remark can first of all be very easily applied to space. Every space intuited within its limits is such a whole, the parts of which, however far the decomposition is carried, are always again spaces; and it is therefore divisible to infinity.",[]],"krv-b-1590":["From this the second application follows quite naturally, namely to an outer appearance (body) enclosed within its limits. The divisibility of the body is grounded on the divisibility of space, which constitutes the possibility of the body as an extended whole. The body is therefore divisible to infinity, without consisting, on that account, of infinitely many parts.",[]],"krv-b-1591":["It may indeed seem that, since a body must be represented as substance in space, it will differ from space in respect of the law of the divisibility of space. For it may readily be granted that decomposition in the case of space can never remove all composition, since in that event space itself, which otherwise has nothing self-subsistent about it, would altogether cease (which is impossible). But that, if all composition of matter were removed in thought, nothing at all should remain, seems not to be reconcilable with the concept of a substance. Substance is properly meant to be the subject of all composition, and would have to remain in its elements, even if their connection in space, whereby they constitute a body, were removed. But what is entitled substance in the appearance is not in the same case as what we should think of a thing in itself through a pure concept of the understanding. The former is not absolute subject, but an abiding image of sensibility, and nothing but intuition, in which nothing unconditioned is anywhere to be met with.",[]],"krv-b-1592":["Now although this rule of progress to infinity undoubtedly holds in the subdivision of an appearance regarded as a mere filling of space, it cannot hold if we seek to extend it to the multitude of parts which are in a certain manner already marked off in the given whole, and which thereby constitute a *quantum discretum*. To assume that in every articulated (organised) whole each part is again articulated, and that in this way, in dissecting the parts to infinity, we always meet with new artificial parts [*Kunsttheile*]; in a word, that the whole is articulated to infinity, is quite unthinkable. It is, however, quite conceivable that the parts of matter might be found articulated to infinity in the course of their decomposition. For the infinity of the division of a given appearance in space rests solely on this: that through the division only the divisibility, that is, a multitude of parts in itself absolutely indeterminate, is given; the parts themselves are given and determined only through the subdivision. In short, the whole is not in itself already divided. Division can therefore determine in the whole a multitude which goes as far as we choose to advance in the regress of the division. In the case of an organic body articulated to infinity, on the other hand, the whole is represented by this very concept as already divided, and a multitude of parts, determinate in itself yet infinite, is met with in it prior to all regress of division. This is self-contradictory, since this infinite involution is regarded as a series which can never be completed (infinite), and yet as completed in a taking together. Infinite division characterises the appearance only as *quantum continuum*, and is inseparable from the filling of space, because the ground of infinite divisibility lies precisely in that filling. But as soon as anything is assumed as a *quantum discretum*, the multitude of units in it is determinate, and is therefore always equal to a number. How far organisation may go in an articulated body only experience can decide; and even if it never reached with certainty an unorganised part, such parts must at least lie within possible experience. But how far the transcendental division of an appearance in general extends is not a matter of experience at all. It is a principle of reason, that the empirical regress in the decomposition of the extended should, in conformity with the nature of this appearance, never be held to be absolutely completed.",[]],"krv-b-1593":["Concluding Remark\non the",[]],"krv-b-1594":["Solution of the mathematical-transcendental and",[]],"krv-b-1595":["Preliminary Remark on the Solution of the Dynamical Transcendental Ideas.",[]],"krv-b-1596":["When we presented the antinomy of pure reason in a table through all the transcendental ideas, and indicated the ground of this conflict and the sole means of removing it (which consisted in declaring both opposed assertions to be false), we represented the conditions throughout as belonging to their conditioned according to relations of space and time. This is the ordinary presupposition of common human understanding, and on it the conflict wholly rested. In this respect all the dialectical representations of totality in the series of conditions for a given conditioned were thoroughly of the same kind. The series was always one in which the condition was connected with the conditioned as members of it, and the two were thereby homogeneous. The regress was therefore never thought as completed; or, if this were to happen, a member conditioned in itself would falsely have to be assumed as a first member, and so as unconditioned. Thus it was not indeed the object, that is, the conditioned, which was everywhere considered, but still the series of conditions belonging to it was considered solely in respect of its magnitude. The difficulty, which could be removed by no compromise but only by cutting the knot completely, lay in this: that reason made the series either too long or too short for the understanding, so that the understanding could never come up to its idea.",["- krv-b-1588: restated the long \"weil… und, da…\" sentence as two; \"Gränzen\" rendered \"limits\"; \"alle erst hinzu kamen\" read as \"were added only in the empirical regress\"; OCR \"zod\" read as \"welche\" (\"which\"); \"Zusammennehmung\" = \"taking together\".","- krv-b-1591: OCR \"feint\" read as \"scheint\"; \"Es\" at the start supplied as \"It\"; \"heißt\" = \"is entitled\"; the long first sentence divided into several.","- krv-b-1592: \"gleidhwohl\" read as \"gleichwohl\", \"continnum\" as *continuum*, \"Rez gressus\" as \"Regressus\"; \"Kunsttheile\" kept in German in brackets; \"Einwickelung\" rendered \"involution\".","- krv-b-1593 to 1595: these are headings only, broken off and apparently run together by the OCR; I translated them as they stand and completed nothing.","- krv-b-1596: OCR \"Rüdficht\" read as \"Rücksicht\" (\"respect\"); \"solte\" as \"sollte\"; \"Vergleich\" rendered \"compromise\"; the first sentence restated in several, and \"Object\" kept distinct from *Gegenstand*-type usage (it is *Object* in the text), rendered \"object\"."]],"krv-b-1597":["We have, however, overlooked in this connexion an essential difference among the objects, that is, among the concepts of understanding, which reason endeavours to raise to ideas. According to our table of the categories above, two of these categories signify a mathematical, and the other two a dynamical, synthesis of appearances. Up to this point the oversight could do no harm. In the general representation of all transcendental ideas we remained always within the conditions of appearance; and so too in the two mathematical transcendental ideas we had no object other than the one in appearance. But now that we proceed to dynamical concepts of the understanding, so far as they are to be adapted to the idea of reason, this distinction becomes important, and opens up to us an entirely new prospect with regard to the dispute in which reason is involved. This dispute was previously dismissed as built on false presuppositions on both sides. Now, however, [the position is different]: in the dynamical antinomy there is perhaps a presupposition which can subsist together with the pretension of reason. Viewed from this standpoint, and since the judge supplies the defect in the legal grounds which both sides had failed to recognise, the dispute can be settled to the satisfaction of both parties; and this could not be done in the dispute of the mathematical antinomy.",[]],"krv-b-1598":["[Translated with the preceding passage; see the first note there.]\n\n*(Taken separately, this fragment reads:)* ...the satisfaction of both parties can be reconciled, which could not be done in the dispute of the mathematical antinomy.",[]],"krv-b-1599":["The series of conditions are, of course, all homogeneous so far as we look merely to their extent, that is, to whether they are adequate to the idea, or whether the idea is too great or too small for them. But the concept of understanding which underlies these ideas contains either a synthesis of the homogeneous only (which is presupposed in every magnitude, alike in its composition and in its division), or a synthesis of the heterogeneous as well. The latter can at least be allowed in the dynamical synthesis, alike in that of causal connection and in that of the necessary with the contingent.",[]],"krv-b-1600":["Hence it comes about that in the mathematical connection of the series of appearances no condition other than a sensible one can enter, that is, none which is itself a part of the series. In the dynamical series of sensible conditions, on the other hand, a heterogeneous condition is still admissible, one which is not a part of the series but, as merely intelligible, lies outside it. In this way reason is satisfied, and the unconditioned is set above the appearances, without confusing the series of appearances, which is always conditioned, and without breaking it off in opposition to the principles of the understanding.",[]],"krv-b-1601":["Since the dynamical ideas admit a condition of appearances outside their series, that is, a condition which is not itself appearance, something results which is entirely different from the outcome of the mathematical antinomy. The latter made it necessary to declare both dialectical counter-assertions false. In the dynamical series, on the other hand, what is throughout conditioned, and is inseparable from the series as appearances, is connected with a condition which is indeed empirically unconditioned but also non-sensible. Thus satisfaction can be given to the understanding on the one side and to reason on the other.\\* The dialectical arguments, which in one way or another sought unconditioned totality in mere appearances, fall away; and the propositions of reason, taken in the meaning thus rectified, can both be true. This can never happen with the cosmological ideas which concern a merely mathematically unconditioned unity, since in their case no condition of the series of appearances is to be found that is not itself appearance and, as such, a member of the series.",[]],"krv-b-1602":["III.\nResolution of the Cosmological Ideas\nof the",[]],"krv-b-1603":["Totality of the Derivation of Cosmical Events from their",[]],"krv-b-1604":["Causes.",[]],"krv-b-1605":["\\* For the understanding permits among appearances no condition which is itself empirically unconditioned. But if an intelligible condition, one which therefore does not belong as a member to the series of appearances, can be thought for something conditioned (in the appearance), without in the least interrupting the series of empirical conditions, then such a condition might be admitted as empirically [unconditioned, so that the empirical continuous regress would nowhere be broken off.]",[]],"krv-b-1606":["We can conceive only two kinds of causality in respect of what happens: causality according to nature, or causality through freedom. The first is the connection of a state with a preceding state in the sensible world, upon which it follows according to a rule. Now the causality of appearances rests on conditions of time, and the preceding state, if it had always existed, could not have produced an effect which first arises in time. Hence the causality of the cause of what happens or arises has itself also arisen, and, in accordance with the principle of the understanding, itself again requires a cause.",[]],"krv-b-1607":["By freedom, in the cosmological sense, I understand, on the other hand, the power of beginning a state *of itself*. The causality of such a power does not, therefore, stand under another cause determining it in time, as required by the law of nature. Freedom, in this meaning, is a pure transcendental idea. In the first place, it contains nothing borrowed from experience; secondly, its object cannot be given as determined in any experience. For it is a universal law, even of the possibility of all experience, that everything which happens must have a cause, and that the causality of the cause, if itself something that has happened or arisen, must again have a cause. In this way the entire field of experience, however far it may extend, is transformed into a sum of mere nature. But since in this fashion no absolute totality of conditions in the causal relation is obtainable, reason creates for itself the idea of a spontaneity which can begin to act of itself, without requiring that another cause precede it to determine it in turn to action according to the law of causal connection.",[]],"krv-b-1608":["It is highly remarkable that the practical concept of freedom is based on this transcendental idea, and that in the latter lies the real moment of the difficulties by which the question of its possibility has always been surrounded. Freedom in the practical sense is the independence of the will [*Willkür*] from coercion through impulses of sensibility. For a will is sensible in so far as it is pathologically affected (by motives of sensibility); it is entitled animal (*arbitrium brutum*) if it can be pathologically necessitated. The human will is indeed an *arbitrium sensitivum*, but not *brutum*; it is *liberum*, because sensibility does not render its action necessary, and there is present in man a power of determining himself, independently of coercion by sensible impulses.",[]],"krv-b-1609":["It is easily seen that if all causality in the sensible world were mere nature, every event would be determined by another in time according to necessary laws. Appearances, in so far as they determine the will, would have to render every action necessary as their natural effect. The removal of transcendental freedom would therefore at the same time eradicate all practical freedom. For practical freedom presupposes that, although something has not happened, it yet *ought* to have happened, and that its cause in the appearance was therefore not so determining as to exclude a causality in our will, a causality capable of producing something independently of those natural causes, and even contrary to their power and influence, something determined in the time-order according to empirical laws, and so capable of beginning a series of events entirely of itself.",["Only the first block carried the id-format \"--- notes\"; the notes below apply across the batch.","- 1597: The long final sentence was broken up. \"Jetzt aber…jetzt…\" is irregular, with *abgewiesen worden* and *verglichen werden kann* in separate constructions; I supplied \"[the position is different]\" and restructured. Doubtful: the sentence is syntactically broken in the German.","- 1598: Fragment completing 1597; I gave it in a restated form after the cross-reference. Strictly it belongs with 1597's last clause.","- 1601: The footnote marker (OCR \"“)\") is rendered as \\* after \"other\"; the German main sentence is anacoluthic (*Dagegen das durchgängig Bedingte…Genüge leisten*) and was smoothed into several sentences. \"erichtigten\" read as *berichtigten*.","- 1602–1604: Heading broken across passages; \"Nr sachen\" read as *Ursachen*.","- 1605: Footnote breaks off at \"als empirisch\"; the bracketed completion is supplied from the stray fragment at the end of 1608 (\"unbedingt zugelassen werden, so daß dadurch dem empirischen continuirlichen Regressus nirgend Abbruch geschähe\"), which is the footnote's displaced ending.","- 1608: That displaced footnote text (\"unbedingt zugelassen werden…Abbruch geschähe\") interrupts the main sentence in the OCR; I removed it from the main text and used it in 1605. *Willkür* = \"will\", with the German bracketed once; *patholog isch* read as *pathologisch*.","- 1609: OCR \"dec stimmt\" read as *bestimmt*; the stray \"*\" and the line number \"15\" omitted. *Erfolg* = \"effect\".","- *Verstandesgrundsatz* = \"principle of the understanding\"; *Synthesis* kept as \"synthesis\"; *Mangel der Rechtsgründe* rendered \"defect in the legal grounds\"."]],"krv-b-1610":["What happens here is what is met with generally in the conflict of a reason which ventures beyond the limits of possible experience: the problem is properly not physiological but transcendental. Hence the question of the possibility of freedom, although it is one which challenges psychology, rests on dialectical arguments of merely pure reason; and it, together with its solution, must therefore occupy transcendental philosophy alone. Transcendental philosophy cannot decline to give a satisfactory answer to it. In order to put it in a position to do so, I must first try to determine more precisely, by a remark, the procedure which it has to follow in dealing with this problem.",["- \"Physiologisch\" is rendered \"physiological\" in the sense of the study of nature in general (as Kant uses it), not the modern medical sense.","- The sentence \"Daher die Frage … beschäftigen muß\" is kept as one; the final long sentence is divided in two to settle the antecedent of \"diese\" and \"dazu\" (transcendental philosophy).","- The OCR fragment \"auge— troffen\" is silently corrected to \"angetroffen\"; the stray \"20\" is a line number and omitted."]],"krv-b-1611":["If appearances were things in themselves, and space and time therefore forms of the existence of things in themselves, the conditions would always belong, together with the conditioned, as members of one and the same series; and from this, in the present case also, there would arise the antinomy",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"die Antinomie\"); it is continued in the next passage and is left unfinished here.","- OCR \"fikh\" corrected to \"sich\"; \"Erscheinungen\" = appearances, \"Dinge an sich selbst\" = things in themselves."]],"krv-b-1612":["[8] which is common to all transcendental ideas, that this series must inevitably prove too great or too small for the understanding. The dynamical concepts of reason, however, with which we are occupied in this and the following section, have this peculiarity, that they are concerned not with an object considered as a magnitude, but only with its existence. We can therefore abstract from the magnitude of the series of conditions, and all that matters in their case is the dynamical relation of the condition to the conditioned. Thus in the question regarding nature and freedom we already meet with the difficulty whether freedom is possible at all, and whether, if it is, it can exist along with the universality of the natural law of causality. The question is therefore whether it is a properly disjunctive proposition that every effect in the world must arise either from nature or from freedom, or whether both may not rather, in different relations, take place simultaneously in one and the same occurrence.\n\nThe correctness of that principle of the thoroughgoing connection of all occurrences of the world of sense in accordance with unchanging natural laws is already established as a principle of the transcendental Analytic, and suffers no infringement. The question is only whether, notwithstanding this, freedom can also be found in respect of the very same effect which is determined in accordance with nature, or whether freedom is entirely excluded by that inviolable rule. And here the common but deceptive presupposition of the absolute reality of appearances at once shows its prejudicial influence in confusing reason. For if appearances are things in themselves, freedom cannot be saved. Nature is then the complete and in itself sufficiently determining cause of every occurrence, and the condition of the occurrence is contained always solely in the series of appearances, which, together with their effect, are necessary under natural law.\n\nIf, on the other hand, appearances are taken for nothing more than they actually are, namely not things in themselves but mere representations connected in accordance with empirical laws, they must themselves have grounds which are not appearances. Such an intelligible cause is not determined in respect of its causality by appearances, although its effects appear, and can be determined by other appearances. It therefore lies, together with its causality, outside the series; its effects, on the other hand, are met with in the series of empirical conditions. The effect may accordingly be regarded as free in respect of its intelligible cause, and yet at the same time, in respect of appearances, as resulting from them according to the necessity of nature. This distinction, when stated in general and quite abstractly, must seem extremely subtle and obscure, but it will become clear in its application. Here I have only wished to remark that, since the thoroughgoing connection of all appearances in one context of nature is an inexorable law, it must necessarily overthrow all freedom if we were obstinately to adhere to the reality of appearances. Hence those who in this follow the common opinion have never been able to succeed in reconciling nature and freedom.",["- \"[8]\" is a stray OCR page/line number in the source; retained in brackets as it stands, not translated.","- The very long sentence beginning \"Die dynamischen Vernunftbegriffe…\" is divided into several; \"mithin ob\" is rendered as a restatement of the question (\"The question is therefore whether\").","- \"Nummer\" rendered \"section\"; \"Vernunftbegriffe\" = concepts of reason; \"Begebenheit\" = occurrence.","- Quotation-mark and \"„durch jene\" OCR slips silently corrected; \"hart— näckig\" joined.","- \"sie\" in \"sie mit der Allgemeinheit\" and \"ob … sie es ist\" refers to freedom."]],"krv-b-1613":["Possibility of causality through freedom",["- Section heading fragment; continues in the next two passages."]],"krv-b-1614":["in union with the universal law of",["- Heading fragment, completed by the next passage (\"natural necessity\")."]],"krv-b-1615":["natural necessity.",["- Completes the heading begun in the two preceding passages."]],"krv-b-1616":["I entitle intelligible that in an object of the senses which is not itself appearance. If, accordingly, that which in the world of sense must be regarded as appearance has in itself a faculty which is not an object of sensible intuition, but by which it can nevertheless be the cause of appearances, then the causality of this being can be viewed from two sides: as intelligible in its action, as the action of a thing in itself, and as sensible in its effects, as those of an appearance in the world of sense. We should accordingly form both an empirical and also an intellectual concept of the causality of the faculty of such a subject, and both concepts apply to one and the same effect. That we should think the faculty of an object of the senses as having this twofold aspect does not contradict any of the concepts which we have to form of appearances and of a possible experience. For since appearances, not being things in themselves, must have a transcendental object as their ground, which determines them as mere representations, nothing prevents us from ascribing to this transcendental object, besides the property through which it appears, a causality which is not appearance, although its effect is still to be met with in appearance. Now every efficient cause must have a character, that is, a law of its causality, without which it would not be a cause at all. And so in a subject of the world of sense we should have, first, an empirical character, whereby its actions, as appearances, would stand throughout in connection with other appearances in accordance with constant laws of nature, could be derived from them as their conditions, and so, in combination with them, would constitute members of a single series of the natural order. Secondly, we should have to allow it also an intelligible character, whereby it is indeed the cause of those actions as appearances, but which does not itself stand under any conditions of sensibility and is not itself appearance. The former might also be entitled the character of such a thing in the appearance, the second the character of the thing in itself.",["- \"zusam— wen\" (OCR) corrected to \"zusammen\"; \"aon\" to \"von\"; rendered \"both concepts apply to one and the same effect\" to resolve \"welche\" (the two concepts).","- \"Handlung\" = action; \"Wesen\" = being; \"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility.","- The sentence \"Wir würden uns demnach…\" is lightly smoothed; \"Eine solche doppelte Seite…\" restructured with a that-clause, as the German is irregular.","- \"dasjenige … intelligibel\": \"Ich nenne\" rendered \"I entitle\", per the method for heißen/nennen."]],"krv-b-1617":["In accordance with its intelligible character, this acting subject would not stand under any conditions of time, for time is only the condition of appearances, not of things in themselves. In it",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1618":["no action would arise or pass away, and it would therefore",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1619":["also not be subject to the law of all determination in time, of everything alterable: that everything which happens must find its cause in the appearances (of",["- Fragment; the parenthesis is completed in the next passage. \"Zeitbestimmung\" = determination in time."]],"krv-b-1620":["the previous state). In a word, the causality",["- \"Causa-/lität\" rejoined across the break; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1621":["of the subject, so far as it is intellectual, would not stand at all in the series of empirical conditions which make the occurrence necessary in the world of sense. This intelligible character could indeed never be immediately known, because we can perceive nothing except so far as it appears; but it would nevertheless have to be thought in conformity with the empirical character, just as we must in general lay a transcendental object in thought at the ground of appearances, although we know nothing of what it is in itself.",["- \"desselben\" refers to the subject (not the character); supplied \"of the subject\" for clarity.","- \"gekannt\" rendered \"known\"; \"intellectuell\" = intellectual. The final clause is joined with the next two fragments, which are translated separately below as they stand."]],"krv-b-1622":["Strictly, the preceding passage's closing clause is not repeated here. This fragment reads: on the ground of appearances in thought, although we",["- Source text of this fragment (\"den Erscheinungen in Gedanken zum Grunde legen müssen, ob wir zwar\") already appears at the end of krv-b-1621 in the German; I have rendered it there. Here the fragment is only the overlapping line."]],"krv-b-1623":["of it [the transcendental object] know nothing as to what it is in itself. [!] In accordance with its empirical character this subject, as appearance,",["- The stray \"!\" is an OCR mark (probably a footnote sign); retained in brackets, and no footnote text is given in the passage.","- \"ihm\" supplied as \"the transcendental object\"; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1624":["would therefore be subject to all the laws of determination according to causal connection;",["- \"unter-/worten\" is completed in the next passage (\"unterworfen\"); rendered together here."]],"krv-b-1625":["and so far it would be nothing but a part of the world of sense,",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1626":["whose effects would issue inevitably from nature, like every other appearance. [5] Just as outer appearances flowed into it,",["- \"[5]\" is a stray line number from the OCR, retained in brackets. \"abflössen\" and \"einflössen\" are OCR for \"abflössen/einflössen\" (flowed off/flowed in).","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1627":["[If] its empirical character, that is, the law of its causality, were known through experience, all its actions would have to be explicable in accordance with laws of nature, and all the requisites for a complete and necessary determination of them would have to be met with in a possible experience. According to its intelligible character, however (although we can have nothing of it save the mere general concept),",["- Fragment begins mid-sentence; \"wie\" is read as the remnant of \"Wie\" / \"[Wenn] ... wie\", and I render \"[If]\" in brackets as a supplied word to carry the conditional sense. Doubtful.","- OCR stray letters (\"b\", \"ö\") dropped. \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Requisite\" kept as \"requisites\"."]],"krv-b-1628":["would",["- Fragment: \"nichts als bloß den allgemeinen Begriff desselben haben können) würde\" is the continuation of the previous passage; I have rendered the portion of it in 1627 and here give only the final word \"würde\" (would), since the rest is already given there. Doubtful division by the portal."]],"krv-b-1629":["the same subject must nevertheless be held free from all influence of sensibility and from all determination through appearances. And since in it, so far as it is noumenon, nothing happens, and no change requiring dynamical determination of time takes place, and therefore no connection with appearances as causes is to be met with, this active being would, so far as its actions are concerned, be independent of and free from all natural necessity, which is to be found solely in the sensible world.",["- \"freigesprochen werden müssen\" rendered \"must be held free\"; the clause is completed from 1630's opening (\"scheinungen ... unabhängig und frei sein\"), which I have translated in 1630 in full; the overlap arises from the break in the German, and I have kept the break here.","- Sentence restated: the \"da ... so ... würde\" structure is kept but loosened."]],"krv-b-1630":["[Erscheinungen] — [see preceding.] This active being would, so far as its actions are concerned, be independent of and free from all natural necessity, which is found solely in the sensible world. It may quite rightly be said of it that it begins its effects in the sensible world *of itself* [von selbst], without the action beginning in it. And this would hold without the effects in the sensible world having thereby to begin of themselves. For in the sensible world they are always determined beforehand by empirical conditions in the preceding time, though only by means of the empirical character (which is merely the appearance of the intelligible character), and are possible only as a continuation of the series of natural causes. Thus freedom and nature, each in its complete meaning, would be found together, and without any conflict, in the very same actions, according as these are compared with their intelligible or with their sensible cause.",["- The first bracketed words mark that the passage begins with the tail of the sentence in 1629 (\"scheinungen\"); I have not translated it twice, and the bracket is only a marker.","- \"von selbst\" kept literal with German in brackets. \"Fortsetzung\" = continuation."]],"krv-b-1631":["Elucidation",["- Heading (\"Erläuterung\")."]],"krv-b-1632":["Ed. —",["- Editorial residue, left as it stands; probably an OCR/apparatus remnant of \"Ed.\" (Edition). Doubtful."]],"krv-b-1633":["of the cosmological idea of a freedom",["- Part of the heading, \"Elucidation of the cosmological idea of a freedom in combination with universal natural necessity.\""]],"krv-b-1634":["in combination with universal natural necessity.",["- Continuation of the heading."]],"krv-b-1635":["I have thought it well to sketch first the outline of the solution of our transcendental problem, that the course taken by reason in solving it may thereby be more easily surveyed. We shall now set out the moments of its decision, on which everything properly depends, and consider each of them separately. |",["- \"Schattenriß\" = outline (literally, silhouette). \"Ver— nunft\" healed. The sign | kept."]],"krv-b-1636":["The law of nature that everything which happens has a cause; that the causality of this cause, that is, the action, since it precedes in time and, in view of an effect which has arisen, cannot itself have always been but must have happened, must also have its cause among appearances, by which it is determined; and that consequently all events are empirically determined in a natural order: this law, through which appearances first constitute a nature and can furnish objects of an experience, is a law of the understanding. From it no departure is permitted on any pretext, nor may any appearance be excepted from it; for otherwise we should be placing that appearance outside all possible experience, thereby distinguishing it from all objects of possible experience, and making it a mere thing of thought and a figment of the brain.",["- Long sentence recast: the \"daß ... daß ... daß\" clauses stand in apposition to \"dieses Gesetz\"; I kept the colon but divided the sentence after \"understanding\". OCR slips (\"augmachen\", stray letters, \"zoi\") silently corrected. \"Gedankending\" = thing of thought."]],"krv-b-1637":["Although all this looks to be nothing but a chain of causes, which in the regress to its conditions admits of no absolute totality, this difficulty does not detain us in the least. It has already been dealt with in the general judgment upon the antinomy of reason, when reason in the series of appearances aims at the unconditioned.",["- \"geho— den\" healed as \"gehoben\" (removed/dealt with); the verb ends in 1638's first word \"den\", so \"dealt with\" is a smoothed rendering. Doubtful: \"gehoben\" read for the OCR \"geho—den\"."]],"krv-b-1638":["If we are willing to yield to the illusion of transcendental realism,",["- Fragment, completed in 1639."]],"krv-b-1639":["neither nature nor freedom remains. The question here is only this: whether, granting that in the whole series of all events we recognise nothing but natural necessity, it is yet possible to regard one and the same event, which on the one hand is a mere effect of nature, as on the other hand an effect from freedom; or whether between these two kinds of causality a direct contradiction is to be found.",["- \"eben dieselbe\" resolved as referring to the same event/series of effects (Wirkung); \"Begebenheiten\" requires this."]],"krv-b-1640":["[a] direct contradiction is found.",["- The passage begins with \"gerader Widerspruch angetroffen werde\", which finishes the sentence in 1639; rendered here so as to be one passage, though overlap with 1639 is slight. Doubtful."]],"krv-b-1641":["Among causes in the appearance there can certainly be nothing that could begin a series absolutely and of itself. Every action, as appearance, so far as it produces an event, is itself",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; completed in 1642."]],"krv-b-1642":["an event or occurrence, which presupposes another state",["- \"Eräugniß\" = occurrence (Ereigniß)."]],"krv-b-1643":["in which the cause is to be met with. Thus everything that happens is only a continuation of the series, and no beginning which would occur of itself is possible in it. Hence all actions of natural causes in the succession of time are themselves again effects, which equally presuppose their causes in the temporal series. An original action, through which something happens that was not before, is not to be expected from the causal connection of",["- \"Mfo\" read as \"Also\", \"Causalperknüpfung\" as \"Causalverknüpfung\"; the | sign is retained in the German but there is none in my text, as it fell mid-sentence in the OCR; I have not added it."]],"krv-b-1644":["appearances.",["- Completes the sentence of 1643."]],"krv-b-1645":["But is it really necessary that, if the effects are appearances, the causality of their cause (which cause is itself also an appearance) must be merely empirical? Is it not rather possible that, although for every effect in the appearance a connection with its cause in accordance with laws of empirical causality is certainly required, this empirical causality itself, without in the least interrupting its connection with natural causes, may nevertheless be the effect of a causality which is not empirical but intelligible? That is to say, of an action of a cause which, in respect of appearances, is original, and which in this respect is therefore not appearance but, in virtue of this faculty, intelligible, although in all other respects it must be reckoned as a link in the chain of nature, as belonging to the sensible world.",["- \"Vermögen\" = faculty. \"(nämlich Ursache)\" kept as a parenthesis. Long final sentence divided into two."]],"krv-b-1646":["We require the principle of the causality of appearances among themselves in order to be able to seek, and to specify, natural conditions, that is, causes in appearance, for natural events. If this is admitted and is not weakened by any exception, the understanding, which in its empirical employment sees nothing but nature in all occurrences, and is entitled so to do, has everything that it can demand, and physical explanations proceed on their unimpeded course. Now it does not do the least harm to the understanding—even supposing the whole to be a mere fiction—if it is assumed that among natural causes there are some which have a faculty that is only intelligible, in that its determination to action never rests on empirical conditions but on mere grounds of the understanding,",["- OCR stray letters (\"i\", \"f\", \"inteli- gibel\") corrected. \"berechtigt\" rendered \"entitled\"; \"Bestimmung\" = determination."]],"krv-b-1647":["yet so that the action in the appearance proceeding from this cause is in accordance with all laws of empirical causality. For in this way the acting subject, as *causa phaenomenon*, would be bound up with nature in unbroken dependence of all its actions; and only the *phaenomenon* of this subject (with all its causality in appearance) would contain certain conditions which, if we wish to ascend from the empirical object to the transcendental, would have to be regarded as merely intelligible. For if only in what may be the cause among appearances we follow the rule of nature:",["- \"En so doch\" read as \"Eben so doch\"/\"so doch\"; I render \"yet so that\". Doubtful OCR.","- \"ungertrennter\" read \"ungetrennter\". Passage breaks off at the colon; not completed. Kant's text has *causa phaenomenon* in Latin, kept italic."]],"krv-b-1648":["[...] we can be indifferent to what may be thought, in the transcendental subject (which is empirically unknown to us), as the ground of these appearances and of their connection. This intelligible ground does not in any way affect the empirical questions. It concerns merely thinking in the pure understanding. And although the effects of this thinking and acting of the pure understanding are to be met with in the appearances, these must none the less be capable of being completely explained from their cause in the appearance, in accordance with natural laws. We do so by following their merely empirical character as the supreme ground of explanation, and by passing over, as wholly unknown, the intelligible character, which is the transcendental cause of the empirical, save in so far as the latter serves as its sensible sign. Let us apply this to experience.\n\nMan is one of the appearances of the sensible world, and to that extent one of the natural causes, the causality of which must stand under empirical laws. As such he must accordingly have an empirical character, like all other natural things. We note this character through the powers and faculties which he manifests in his effects. In lifeless nature, or in nature endowed merely with animal life, we find no ground for thinking any faculty to be other than sensibly conditioned. Man, however, who knows all the rest of nature solely through the senses, knows himself also through pure apperception; and this, indeed, in acts and inner determinations which he cannot count as impressions of the senses. He is thus to himself, on the one hand, phenomenon, and on the other hand, namely in respect of certain faculties, a merely intelligible object, since the action of these faculties cannot be reckoned as belonging to the receptivity of sensibility. We entitle these faculties understanding and reason. The latter, in particular, is distinguished, quite properly and in a pre-eminent manner, from all empirically conditioned powers. For it considers its objects solely in accordance with ideas, and in terms of them determines the understanding, which then makes an empirical use of its own concepts (these also, it is true, being pure).\n\nThat this reason has causality, or at least that we represent it to ourselves as having causality, is evident from the imperatives which in all practical matters we prescribe as rules to the active powers. The ought expresses a kind of necessity and of connection with grounds which is found nowhere else in the whole of nature. The understanding can know of nature only what is, or has been, or will be. It is impossible that anything in nature ought to be other than what, in all these time-relations, it actually is. Indeed the ought, if we have in view merely the course of nature, has no meaning whatsoever. We cannot ask what ought to happen in nature, any more than we can ask what properties a circle ought to have. We can ask only what happens in nature, or what properties the circle has.",[]],"krv-b-1649":["This ought expresses a possible action, the ground of which is nothing other than a mere concept; whereas the ground of a merely natural action must always be an appearance. The action must indeed be possible under natural conditions, if the ought is directed upon it; but these natural conditions do not concern the determination of the will [*Willkür*] itself, but only its effect and consequence in the appearance. However many natural grounds there may be that impel me to will, however many sensuous incitements, they cannot produce the ought. They produce only a willing which is very far from being necessary, and is always conditioned. To this willing the ought that reason pronounces opposes measure and limit, nay prohibition and authority. Whether the object be one of mere sensibility (the agreeable) or of pure reason (the good), reason does not give way to any ground that is empirically given, and does not follow the order of things as they present themselves in appearance. With complete spontaneity it frames for itself an order of its own according to ideas, to which it adapts the empirical conditions, and in accordance with which it declares actions to be necessary which have not yet happened, and perhaps never will happen. Yet of all these actions it presupposes that reason can have causality in regard to them; for otherwise it would not expect from its ideas any effects in experience. ]",[]],"krv-b-1650":["Let us now stop at this point, and assume it to be at least possible that reason does actually have causality in respect of appearances. However much it be reason, it must then none the less exhibit an empirical character. For every cause presupposes a rule according to which certain appearances follow as effects, and every rule requires uniformity in the effects. This uniformity is the ground of the concept of cause (as a faculty). So far as it has to be made evident from mere appearances, we may entitle it the empirical character of the cause. This character is constant, while the effects appear in changeable forms, according to the diversity of the accompanying conditions, which in part limit them.",[]],"krv-b-1651":["Thus every man has an empirical character of his will [*Willkür*]. This is nothing but a certain causality of his reason, so far as reason shows in its effects, in the appearance, a rule from which we can gather the grounds of reason and their actions, in their kind and degrees, and can judge the subjective principles of his will. Since this empirical character must itself be drawn from the appearances, as effect, and from the rule of these appearances which experience supplies, all the actions of man in the appearance are determined in accordance with the order of nature, from his empirical character and from the other co-operating causes. If we could investigate all the appearances of his will to the very bottom, there would be no single human action which we could not predict with certainty, and recognise as necessary from its preceding conditions. In respect of this empirical character there is therefore no freedom; and it is in terms of this character alone that we can consider man, if we merely observe, and, as is done in anthropology, seek to investigate physiologically the moving causes of his actions.",[]],"krv-b-1652":["[The sentence is continued from the preceding passage:] ... can we consider man, if we merely observe ...",[]],"krv-b-1653":["... and, as happens in anthropology, wish to investigate physiologically the moving causes of his actions.",[]],"krv-b-1654":["But if we consider these same actions in relation to",[]],"krv-b-1655":["reason, and not to speculative reason, with a view to explaining them in respect of their origin, but solely in so far as reason is the cause",[]],"krv-b-1656":["[of producing them]",[]],"krv-b-1657":["themselves; in a word, if we compare them with reason",[]],"krv-b-1658":["in practical intention, we find a rule and order quite other than the order of nature. For it may be that everything which has happened in the course of nature, and which in accordance with its empirical grounds inevitably had to happen, ought not to have happened. At times, however, we find, or at least believe that we find, that the ideas of reason have actually proved their causality in respect of the actions of man, as appearances, and that these actions have taken place, not because they were determined by empirical causes, but because they were determined by grounds of reason.",["(Notes are given per passage below; the format asks for one block, so they are combined.)","- 1648: Opening \"ES so\" is a damaged OCR fragment; I translated from \"so können wir\" and marked the break with [...]; the preceding conditional clause is lost.","- 1648: Divided several long sentences; supplied \"[in nature]\" nowhere in brackets but \"in nature\" added after \"ought to be\" and \"happen\" where *darin* is meant. Corrected OCR \"Erjcheinuns 2 gen\", \"Handeins\" (= Handelns), \"erklart\".","- 1648: *Phänomen* kept as \"phenomenon\"; *Vermögen* rendered \"faculty\" or \"power\" as the sense required; *Kräfte* \"powers\". *nennen* rendered \"entitle\".","- 1649: *Willkür* given in brackets throughout, rendered \"will\"; the stray closing bracket \"]\" in the German is kept as printed (probably OCR). \"Verhot\" corrected to *Verbot*.","- 1649: Restated \"dem dagegen ... entgegen setzt\" as a separate sentence.","- 1651: The sentence \"...und nach diesem\" runs on into 1652; I rendered it whole within 1651 and gave 1652–1653 as the continuation, since the German is split mid-sentence. The repeated English there is a result of that split.","- 1652–1657: Fragmentary line-by-line passages; 1656 ends \"sie selbst zu erzeugen\", completed in 1657, so I added \"[of producing them]\" as supplied sense in 1656, which the German has only by syntax.","- 1658: OCR \"o\", \"Wen— schen\", \"5\" ignored; \"Menschen\" restored."]],"krv-b-1659":["Let us suppose, then, that reason can be said to have causality in respect of appearance. Could its action then be entitled free, seeing that it is quite exactly determined and necessary in its empirical character (the mode of sense, *Sinnesart*)? This empirical character is in turn determined in the intelligible character (the mode of thought, *Denkungsart*). The latter we do not know. We indicate it through appearances, which properly give us immediate knowledge only of the mode of sense (the empirical character).*) Now an action, so far as it is to be ascribed to the mode of thought as its cause, does not follow from it in accordance with empirical laws. That is to say, it is not [preceded by] the conditions of pure reason; it is preceded only by their effects in the appearance of inner sense. Pure reason, as a merely intelligible faculty, is not subject to the form of time, and so not to the conditions of temporal succession. The causality of reason in the intelligible character does not arise, or",["- The asterisk footnote mark is placed where the OCR has a stray quotation mark after \"geben\"; the footnote text is in the next passage.","- The German of \"d. i. so, daß die Bedingungen der reinen Vernunft, sondern nur so, daß…\" lacks a negative and a verb after \"Vernunft\". I supplied \"not [preceded by]\" and \"is preceded\" in brackets and rendered the sense as the argument requires. The reading is doubtful.","- Restated \"Die Handlung nun… vorhergehen\" as three sentences. Sinnesart and Denkungsart are given in the German once for clarity.","- The passage breaks off in mid-sentence (\"entsteht nicht, oder\"); I left it unfinished."]],"krv-b-1660":["Footnote: The real morality of actions (merit and guilt) remains therefore wholly hidden from us, even in the case of our own conduct. Our imputations can be referred only to the empirical character. How much of this is the pure effect of freedom, how much is to be ascribed to mere nature and to the innocent defect of temperament, or to its happy constitution (*merito fortunae*), no one can discover. Hence no one can judge it with complete justice.\n\n[Text continued:] begin at a certain time in order to produce an effect. For otherwise it would itself be subject to the natural law of appearances, so far as that law determines causal series in accordance with time, and its causality would then be nature and not freedom. We shall therefore be able to say: if reason can have causality in respect of appearances, it is a faculty through which the sensible condition of an empirical series of effects first begins. For the",["- The OCR gives the footnote text first, and the main sentence resumes after it (\"hebt nicht etwa… an\"). It continues the sentence broken off in krv-b-1659, so I have put the footnote first and labelled the resumption.","- Restored \"wir\" and \"Bedingung\" from OCR fragments (\"wi\", \"Bedin “ gnug\").","- *merito fortunae* kept in Latin, in italics, as Kant's own phrase.","- Passage breaks off at \"Denn die\"; left unfinished."]],"krv-b-1661":["condition which lies in reason is not sensible, and therefore does not itself begin. There is accordingly realised here what we missed in all empirical series: that the condition of a successive series of events could itself be empirically unconditioned. For here the condition is outside the series of appearances (in the intelligible), and is therefore not subject to any sensible condition, nor to any determination of time by a preceding cause.",["- The passage begins in mid-sentence (\"Bedingung, die in der Vernunft liegt…\"), continuing \"Denn die\" of the previous passage. I did not join them.","- Divided the last German sentence's second half for clarity; \"Intelligibelen\" rendered \"the intelligible\"."]],"krv-b-1662":["Yet in another relation this same cause belongs also to the series of appearances. Man is himself an appearance. His will [*Willkür*] has an empirical character, which is the (empirical) cause of all his actions. There is no one of the conditions determining man in accordance with this character that is not contained in the series of natural effects and does not obey their law,",["- \"Willkür\" rendered \"will\", with the German in brackets, since the Kantian distinction from *Wille* matters here.","- Breaks off mid-sentence; the relative clause \"nach welchem…\" follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1663":["according to which no empirically unconditioned causality is ever found among what happens in time. Consequently no given action (since it can be perceived only as appearance) can begin absolutely of itself. Of reason, however, we cannot say that before the state in which it determines the will another state precedes, in which this state itself is determined. For since reason is itself not an appearance and is not subject to any conditions of sensibility, no succession in time is found in it even in respect of its causality. The dynamical law of nature, which determines temporal succession according to rules, cannot therefore be applied to it.",["- Stray OCR characters (\"X\", \"2\", \"Zi\") omitted.","- \"nach welchem\" refers to the law (\"Gesetze\") of natural effects, as the argument requires; I have said \"according to which\" without adding a noun.","- Divided the German's long final sentence into two."]],"krv-b-1664":["Reason is therefore the permanent condition of all the voluntary actions under which man appears. Each of these is determined beforehand in the empirical character of man, before it takes place. In respect of the intelligible character, of which the empirical is only the sensible schema, there is no before or after; and every action, irrespective",["- Read \"iftim\" as \"ist im\" and dropped the stray \"als\" in \"ehe noch als sie geschieht\" as an OCR slip.","- The passage ends at the broken word \"unan-\" (*unangesehen*); I rendered it as \"irrespective\" and the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1665":["of the time-relation in which it stands to other appearances, is the immediate effect of the intelligible character of pure reason. Reason therefore acts freely, without being dynamically determined, in the chain of natural causes, by grounds that are external or internal but precede it in time. This freedom of reason we must not regard merely negatively, as independence of empirical conditions (for [gap in the text]… appearances), but may also indicate positively as a faculty of beginning of itself a series of events. Nothing begins in reason itself; as the unconditioned condition of every voluntary action, it admits above itself no conditions preceding in time. Its effect, however, begins in the series of appearances, though it can never constitute in that series an absolutely first beginning.",["- Lacuna: the parenthesis \"(denn … scheinungen zu sein)\" has lost its middle in the OCR. I have not restored it and have marked the gap.","- The OCR word \"verho stattet\" is garbled; I read it as \"verstattet\" (admits, allows). Doubtful reading.","- The initial \"en gesehen\" is the tail of \"unangesehen\" from the previous passage; I translated the sense.","- Divided the long German sentence into several sentences."]],"krv-b-1666":["To illustrate the regulative principle of reason by an example of its empirical use, not to confirm it (for such proofs are useless for transcendental assertions), let us take a voluntary action, for instance a malicious lie by which a man has brought a certain confusion into society. We first inquire into the motives [*Bewegursachen*] from which it arose, and then judge how it, together with its consequences, can be imputed to him. In the first respect we trace his empirical character to its sources. These we look for in bad upbringing and evil company; partly also in the viciousness of a natural disposition insensible to shame, and partly we put them down to frivolity and thoughtlessness. Nor do we overlook the occasioning causes. In all this we proceed as we do in investigating, generally, the series of determining causes for a given natural effect. Although we believe the action to be determined in this way, we none the less blame the agent. We blame him not for his unhappy natural disposition, nor for the circumstances influencing him, nor even for his previous way of life. For we presuppose that we can set entirely aside what that life has been, and regard the past series of conditions as not having occurred, and this deed as wholly unconditioned in respect of the preceding state, as if the agent with it began a series of consequences quite of himself. This blame rests on a law of reason, in which reason is regarded as a cause that could have determined, and ought to have determined, the man's conduct otherwise, regardless of all the empirical conditions named. And we regard the causality of reason not merely as concurrent, but as complete in itself, even if the sensible springs of action were not in its favour but were actually against it. The action is ascribed to his intelligible character: now, at the moment when he lies, the guilt is wholly his. Reason, therefore, notwithstanding all the empirical conditions of the deed, was completely free, and the deed is to be ascribed wholly to its omission.",["- Restated the long middle sentences (\"In der ersten Absicht… Gelegenheitsursachen\"; \"Ob man nun gleich… anhebe\") as several shorter ones.","- Supplied \"[Bewegursachen]\" only to fix the rendering \"motives\"; \"Triebfedern\" rendered \"springs of action\", \"Naturell\" as \"natural disposition\".","- \"Concurrenz\" rendered \"concurrent\" (a mere contributing cause), as the contrast with \"vollständig\" requires.","- Removed OCR debris (\"Nempirischen\", \"N federn\", \"unter lassung\", final \"IM\").","- \"Thäter\" rendered \"agent\"; \"zugerechnet\" as \"imputed\"; \"Schuld\" as \"guilt\"."]],"krv-b-1667":["It is easy to see from this imputing judgment that what is in mind is that reason is not at all affected by all that sensibility; that it does not change (although its appearances, that is, the way in which it shows itself in its effects, do change); that in it no state precedes which determines the following state; and that it therefore does not belong at all to the series of sensible conditions which make appearances necessary in accordance with laws of nature. Reason is present in all the actions of human beings, in all circumstances of time, and is one and the same. But it is not itself in time, nor does it fall into a new state in which it previously was not. In respect of all new states it is determining, but not determinable. Hence we cannot ask, Why has reason not determined itself otherwise? We can ask only, Why has it not, through its causality, determined the appearances otherwise? To this no answer is possible. For a different intelligible character would have given a different empirical character. When we say that, notwithstanding the whole course of life he has hitherto led, the culprit could yet have refrained from the lie, this means only that the act stands immediately under the power of reason, and that reason in its causality is not subject to any conditions of appearance or of the course of time. The difference of time, though it constitutes a principal difference of appearances in their relation to one another, cannot make any difference in the action in relation to reason; for appearances are not things, and therefore not causes, in themselves.",[]],"krv-b-1668":["In the judging of free actions, so far as their causality is concerned, we can therefore advance only as far as the intelligible cause; we cannot go beyond it. We can know that this cause is free, that is, determined independently of sensibility, and that in this manner it can be the sensibly unconditioned condition of appearances. But why the intelligible character should, in the circumstances before us, give just these appearances and this empirical character, so far exceeds every power of our reason to answer, indeed every title it has even to ask, that it is as if we were to ask why the transcendental object of our outer sensible intuition gives intuition in space only, and not some other kind of intuition. The problem which we had to solve, however, does not oblige us to this at all. It was only this: whether freedom contradicts natural necessity in one and the same action. This we have sufficiently answered. We have shown that, since in the former a relation to a quite different kind of conditions is possible than in the latter, the law of the latter does not affect the former, and that the two can therefore exist independently of each other and without disturbing each other.",[]],"krv-b-1669":["It should be carefully noted that we have not sought to establish the reality of freedom as one of the faculties which contain the cause of the appearances of our sensible world. Apart from the fact that this would not have been a transcendental enquiry, which is concerned solely with concepts, it could not have succeeded; for from experience we can never infer to anything that does not have to be thought in accordance with laws of experience. Nor have we even sought to prove the possibility of freedom; for this too would have failed, since we cannot from mere concepts *a priori* know the possibility of any real ground or causality. Freedom is here treated only as a transcendental idea, through which reason thinks to begin the series of conditions in the appearance absolutely, by what is sensibly unconditioned. In so doing it becomes entangled in an antinomy with the very laws which it prescribes for the empirical employment of the understanding. That this antinomy rests on mere illusion, and that nature at least does not conflict with causality from freedom, was the one thing we were able to accomplish, and the only thing about which we were concerned.",[]],"krv-b-1670":["IV.\nSolution of the Cosmological Idea\nof the",[]],"krv-b-1671":["Totality of the Dependence of Appearances |",[]],"krv-b-1672":["as regards their Existence in General. |",[]],"krv-b-1673":["In the preceding section we considered the alterations of the sensible world in their dynamical series, in which each alteration stands under another as its cause. We now use this series of states merely as a guide to an existence which may be the highest condition of everything alterable, namely, the necessary being. What is here at issue is not unconditioned causality, but the unconditioned existence of substance itself. The series before us is therefore properly a series only of concepts, and not of intuitions, in so far as the one is the condition of the other. *",[]],"krv-b-1674":["But it is easily seen that, since everything in the sum of appearances is alterable, and therefore conditioned in existence, there cannot anywhere in the series of dependent existence be an unconditioned member whose existence would be absolutely necessary. Consequently, if appearances were things in themselves, and for that very reason their condition always belonged with the conditioned to one and the same series of intuitions, a necessary being as condition of the existence of the appearances of the sensible world could never find a place.",[]],"krv-b-1675":["The dynamical regress, however, has this peculiarity, distinguishing it from the mathematical. The mathematical regress is properly concerned only with the composition of parts into a whole, or the division of a whole into its parts; the conditions of its series must therefore always be regarded as parts of the series, and so as homogeneous, and consequently as appearances. In the dynamical regress, on the other hand, we are concerned not with the possibility of an unconditioned whole from given parts, or of an unconditioned part for a given whole, but with the derivation of a state from its cause, or of the contingent existence of substance itself from the necessary. In this regress the condition need not necessarily form one empirical series with the conditioned.",[]],"krv-b-1676":["In the apparent antinomy before us, therefore, a way of escape still remains open. Both the conflicting propositions may be true at the same time in different relations. All things of the sensible world may be thoroughly contingent, and so have only an empirically conditioned existence; and yet there may be a non-empirical condition of the whole series, that is, an unconditionally necessary being. For this being, as intelligible condition, would not belong to the series as a member of it, not even as its highest member; nor would it make any member of the series empirically unconditioned. It would leave the whole sensible world in its empirically conditioned existence, extending through all its members. This way of grounding an unconditioned existence of appearances would thus differ from the empirically unconditioned causality (freedom) of the preceding section. In the case of freedom the thing itself, as cause (*substantia phaenomenon*), nevertheless belonged to the series of conditions, and only its causality was thought as intelligible. Here, on the other hand, the necessary being would have to be thought as wholly outside the series of the sensible world (as *ens extramundanum*), and as merely intelligible. Only so can it be prevented from being itself subject to the law of the contingency and dependence of all appearances.",["- krv-b-1667: OCR damage repaired silently (\"gar 1. nicht\", \"bestimm dar\" = bestimmbar, \"ao sie\", stray numerals/letters). \"Zurechnenden\" rendered \"imputing\".","- krv-b-1667: the final sentence is restated; the dangling \"sie\" (the act, *Handlung*, as antecedent) resolved by judgment; \"Sachen\" rendered \"things\" and \"Thäter\" \"culprit\". I added \"all new states\" after \"in respect of\" for German \"desselben\", which is uncertain.","- krv-b-1668: \"Aufgabe\" as \"problem\"; long final sentence divided into three.","- krv-b-1669: \"Realgrund\" as \"real ground\"; \"Sinnlichunbedingte\" as \"what is sensibly unconditioned\".","- krv-b-1670–1672: the heading is split across three passages and is translated as given; \"Dasein\" rendered \"existence\".","- krv-b-1673: the asterisk marks a footnote reference whose text is not in the passage, so it is kept without a footnote.","- krv-b-1675: \"empire i Reihe\" read as \"empirische Reihe\"; \"Herz 2 fällung\" as \"Herzfällung\" = Zerfällung. In the German the \"dieses Reihe\" appears as \"this series\" (read \"dieser Reihe\"). The initial \"dieser\" refers to the mathematical regress, \"jenem\" to the dynamical; I made both explicit.","- krv-b-1676: \"Zufälfigkeit\" read as \"Zufälligkeit\" = contingency; \"keein\" corrected silently."]],"krv-b-1677":["The regulative principle of reason, in view of this problem of ours, is therefore as follows. Everything in the world of sense has empirically conditioned existence, and nowhere in that world is there, in respect of any property, an unconditioned necessity. There is no member of the series of conditions of which we must not always expect the empirical condition in a possible experience, and which we must not, so far as we can, seek for there. Nothing entitles us to derive any existence from a condition outside the empirical series, or even to hold it to be, within the series itself, absolutely independent and self-subsistent. At the same time, this does not in the least deny that the whole series may be grounded in some intelligible being, which for that very reason is free from all empirical condition and rather contains the ground of the possibility of all these appearances.",["- Restated one long sentence as several; the construction after \"daß … daß … daß kein Glied\" is irregular in the German, and I have smoothed it.","- \"gleichwohl aber dadurch gar nicht in Abrede zu ziehen\" rendered as a new sentence, \"At the same time…\"; the double negation \"nicht … nicht\" is read as a single denial that the series could be so grounded.","- \"schlechterdings\" = absolutely; \"Erscheinungen\" = appearances; \"Erfahrung\" = experience. Hyphenated line-break \"schlechter— dings\" silently corrected."]],"krv-b-1678":["It is not, however, our intention here to prove the unconditionally necessary existence of a being, or even to found upon this the possibility of a merely intelligible condition of the existence of the appearances of the world of sense. Just as we restrict reason, lest it desert the thread of the empirical conditions and lose itself in transcendent grounds of explanation which admit of no presentation *in concreto*, so, on the other hand, we have to restrict the law of the merely empirical employment of the understanding, so that it may not decide as to the possibility of things in general, and may not declare the intelligible to be impossible merely because it is of no use to us in explaining appearances. All that is thereby shown is that the thoroughgoing contingency of all natural things, and of all their empirical conditions, can very well coexist with the arbitrary presupposition of a necessary, though merely intelligible, condition. There is therefore no real contradiction between these assertions, and both can be true. Such an absolutely necessary being of understanding may be possible in itself; yet it can by no means be inferred from the universal contingency and dependence of everything belonging to the world of sense, nor from the principle that we are not to stop at any single member of that world, so far as it is contingent, and appeal to a cause outside the world. Reason pursues its own path in its empirical employment, and a path peculiar to itself in its transcendental employment.",["- Divided the long first and second sentences; \"also … andererseits\" correlation resolved as \"Just as … so, on the other hand\".","- \"Verstandeswesen\" = being of understanding. \"Darstellung in concreto\" kept in Latin.","- Editorial signs (|, k, lem pirischen), stray \"1\") are OCR debris; \"lempirischen)\" read as \"empirischen)\". \"unmoglich\" corrected to \"unmöglich\".","- \"es\" in \"daß es nicht über die Möglichkeit … entscheide\" taken to refer to \"das Gesetz\"."]],"krv-b-1679":["The world of sense contains nothing but appearances, and these are mere representations, which are always in turn sensibly conditioned. Since here we never have things in themselves as our objects, it is not surprising that we are never entitled to make a leap, from any member whatever of the empirical series, outside the context of sensibility, as if the members were things in themselves existing apart from their transcendental ground, and which we could quit in order to seek the cause of their existence outside them. This would indeed have to happen in the end with contingent things; but not with mere representations of things, whose contingency is itself only a phenomenon, and can lead to no other regress than that which determines the phenomena, that is, the empirical regress. But to think an intelligible ground of the appearances, that is, of the world of sense, and to think it as freed from the contingency of the latter, is opposed neither to the unlimited empirical regress in the series of appearances nor to their thoroughgoing contingency. That, moreover, is all that we had to accomplish for the removal of the apparent antinomy, and it could be done only in this way. For if",["- Broken off mid-sentence (\"Denn ist die\"); continued in the next passage and left uncompleted.","- Restated the long \"gleich als wenn…\" sentence with minimal division; \"Phänomen/Phänomena\" kept as phenomenon/phenomena.","- OCR debris (\"Zus 1 sammenhange\", \"su)\") silently corrected."]],"krv-b-1680":["the condition in each case of every conditioned (as regards existence) is sensible, and for that very reason belongs to the series, it is itself in turn conditioned (as the antithesis of the fourth antinomy shows). Either, then, a conflict with reason, which demands the unconditioned, had to remain,",["- Continues the preceding sentence; \"Es mußte also entweder\" completed only in the next passage, so the \"either\" is left open.","- \"Ausweiset\" = shows."]],"krv-b-1681":["or the unconditioned had to be posited outside the series, in the intelligible, whose",["- Fragment; the relative clause \"dessen\" continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1682":["necessity requires no empirical condition, nor permits any, and",["- Fragment; \"und\" continues into the next passage."]],"krv-b-1683":["is therefore, relatively to appearances, unconditionally necessary.",["- \"respective auf\" rendered \"relatively to\"."]],"krv-b-1684":["The empirical employment of reason (in respect of the conditions",["- Fragment, broken off mid-phrase (\"Bedingun—gen\")."]],"krv-b-1685":["of existence in the world of sense) is not affected by the admission of a merely intelligible being, but proceeds, in accordance with the principle",["- \"afficirt\" = affected. Hyphenation silently corrected; \"ko\" is OCR debris."]],"krv-b-1686":["of thoroughgoing contingency, from empirical conditions to higher ones,",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1687":["which are always equally empirical. Just as little, however, does",["- Fragment; the subject (\"dieser regulative Grundsatz\") follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1688":["this regulative principle exclude the assumption of an intelligible cause which is not in the series, when what is at issue is the pure employment of reason (in respect of ends). For in that case the cause signifies only the ground, for us merely transcendental and unknown, of the possibility of the sensible series in general. The existence of this ground, independent of all conditions of the series and unconditionally necessary in respect of them, is in no way opposed to the unbounded contingency of the series, and therefore also not to the regress, nowhere completed, in the series of empirical conditions.",["- \"Zwecke\" = ends. \"jene\" taken to refer to \"intelligibele Ursache\"; \"der letzteren / der ersteren\" = the sensible series / the series' contingency, as the argument requires: \"letzteren\" rendered as \"the series\", \"der ersteren\" as \"the series' contingency\" (smoothed).","- Divided the final long sentence into two; OCR debris (\"Berls nunft\", \"w\", \"I\") corrected to \"Vernunft\"."]],"krv-b-1689":["Concluding Remark\non the Whole Antinomy of Pure Reason.",["- Heading; \"Schlußanmerkung\" rendered \"Concluding Remark\"."]],"krv-b-1690":["So long as the object of our concepts of reason is merely the totality of conditions in the world of sense, and what can be done in regard to them in the service of reason,",["- Fragment: the main clause (\"so sind unsere Ideen…\") appears in the next passage; the sentence is broken at \"der Ver-/nunft\" and joined here. \"Vernunftbegriffe\" = concepts of reason."]],"krv-b-1691":["our ideas are indeed transcendental, but yet cosmological. As soon, however, as we posit the unconditioned (which is what is really at issue) in that which lies wholly outside the world of sense, and therefore outside all possible experience, the ideas become transcendent. They then serve not merely for the completion of the empirical employment of reason (which always remains an",["- Broken off mid-sentence (\"die immer eine\"); left uncompleted. A new sentence was begun at \"They then\" after the colon.","- \"transscendental/transscendent/kosmologisch\" kept in their conventional distinction."]],"krv-b-1692":["[...never to be executed, yet always to be followed, remains an idea]); on the contrary, they separate themselves from it entirely and make themselves into objects whose material is not taken from experience, and whose objective reality rests not on the completion of the empirical series but on pure *a priori* concepts. Such transcendent ideas have a merely intelligible object. To admit this object as a transcendental object, of which otherwise we know nothing, is indeed permitted. But if we are to think it as a thing determinable through its distinguishing and inner predicates, we have on our side neither grounds of its possibility (since it is independent of all empirical concepts) nor the least justification for assuming such an object; it is therefore a mere thought-entity [*Gedankending*].\n\nNevertheless, of all the cosmological ideas, that which gave rise to the fourth antinomy urges us to venture this step. For the existence of appearances, which is not grounded in itself at all but is always conditioned, challenges us to look around for something distinct from all appearances, that is, for an intelligible object, in which this contingency may cease.\n\nBut once we have allowed ourselves to assume a self-subsisting reality outside the field of the whole of sensibility, appearances have to be regarded only as contingent modes of representing intelligible objects, by beings which are themselves intelligences. Nothing is then left to us but analogy, by which we may make use of the concepts of experience in order to form some sort of concept of intelligible things, of which in themselves we have not the least knowledge. We come to know the contingent only through experience; but here we are speaking of things which are not to be objects of experience at all. We shall therefore have to derive our knowledge of them from what is necessary in itself, that is, from pure concepts of things in general.\n\nHence the first step which we take outside the sensible world obliges us to begin our new knowledge with the investigation of the absolutely necessary being, and to derive from the concepts of it the concepts of all things, so far as they are merely intelligible. This attempt we shall make in the following chapter.",["- The passage begins mid-sentence; the opening parenthesis is left unclosed, as in the source.","- Stray OCR marks (e.g. \"f\", \"U\", \"Al\") and the page break \"|\" were silently removed; the page-break marks \"|\" in the German were dropped as running apparatus per the job instructions.","- Restated the long second and third sentences as several shorter ones; \"Weil aber … anzusehen sind: so bleibt\" is smoothed into two sentences, \"by beings which are themselves intelligences\" taken to qualify the intelligible objects' representers, as Kant's syntax is ambiguous.","- \"Kenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\" (Kant's Latin-free usage here); \"Gedankending\" given in German since the rendering is a choice.","- \"welches\" in the first sentence (\"welches daher ein bloßes Gedankending ist\") resolved as referring to the object, hence \"it\".","- \"wir den ... außer der Sinnenwelt thun\" reads \"den\" for OCR \"den/wir“\"; the reading is slightly doubtful."]],"krv-b-1693":["Book II of the Transcendental Dialectic\nChapter III\nThe Ideal of Pure Reason\nSection I\nThe Ideal in General",["- Headings only; \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Chapter\", \"Abschnitt\" \"Section\"."]],"krv-b-1694":["We have seen above that objects cannot be represented through pure concepts of the understanding apart from all conditions of sensibility, since the conditions of their objective reality are lacking, and nothing is to be found in them save the mere form of thought. Nevertheless they can be exhibited *in concreto*, if they are applied to appearances; for in appearances they find the proper material for the concept of experience, which is nothing but a concept of the understanding *in concreto*.\n\nIdeas, however, are still further removed from objective reality than are the categories; for no appearance can be found in which they could be represented *in concreto*. They contain a certain completeness to which no possible empirical knowledge attains, and reason has in view only a systematic unity, to which it seeks to bring the empirically possible unity nearer, without ever completely reaching it.",["- Divided the second sentence of the German at the semicolon-led clause into two sentences, and began a new paragraph at \"Ideen aber\" for clarity.","- \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"; \"Stoff\" rendered \"material\".","- \"in ihnen\" (first sentence) refers to the concepts of the understanding."]],"krv-b-1695":["But that which I entitle the ideal seems to be still further removed from objective reality than the idea. By the ideal I understand the idea not merely *in concreto* but *in individuo*, that is, as an individual thing, determinable, or even determined, through the idea alone.",["- Split into two sentences. \"nenne\" rendered \"entitle\" per the method."]],"krv-b-1696":["Humanity in its complete perfection contains not only the extension of all the essential properties which belong to this nature and which constitute our concept of it, up to complete congruence with its ends (which would be our idea of perfect humanity), but also everything else that, over and above this concept, belongs to the thorough determination of the idea. For of all opposed predicates only a single one can be in keeping with the idea of the most perfect man. What is for us an ideal was for Plato an idea of the divine understanding, an individual object in its pure intuition, the most perfect of every kind of possible being, and the primary ground of all copies in appearance.",["- The running head and page number (\"384 Elementarlehre. II. Theil. …\") interrupting the German sentence were omitted.","- \"durchgängige Bestimmung\" rendered \"thorough determination\"; \"Nachbilder\" \"copies\"; \"Urgrund\" \"primary ground\".","- \"derselben\" in \"Eigenschaften … von derselben\" taken to refer to humanity (*Natur*/*Menschheit*)."]],"krv-b-1697":["Without venturing so far, we must admit that human reason contains not only ideas but also ideals, which, though they have not, like the Platonic, creative power, yet have practical power (as regulative principles), and lie at the basis of the possible perfection of certain actions. Moral concepts are not wholly pure concepts of reason, since something empirical (pleasure or displeasure) lies at their basis. Nevertheless, in respect of the principle whereby reason sets bounds to freedom, which is in itself lawless (that is, if we attend merely to their form), they may very well serve as examples of pure concepts of reason.\n\nVirtue, and with it human wisdom in its entire purity, are ideas. But the sage (of the Stoics) is an ideal, that is, a man who exists only in thought, but who is in complete congruence with the idea of wisdom. As the idea gives the rule, so the ideal in such a case serves as the archetype [*Urbild*] for the thorough determination of the copy [*Nachbild*]; and we have no other standard for our actions than the conduct of this divine man within us, with whom we compare ourselves, judge ourselves, and so reform ourselves, although we can never attain to his level.\n\nThese ideals, even if objective reality (existence) be not conceded to them, are not on that account to be regarded as chimeras. They supply reason with an indispensable standard, since reason needs the concept of what is completely perfect in its kind, in order to estimate and measure by it the degree and the defects of what is imperfect. But to try to realise the ideal in an example, that is, in appearance, as, for instance, the sage in a novel, is impracticable. It has, moreover, something absurd about it, and is far from edifying; for the natural limitations, which continually impair the completeness of the idea, make all illusion impossible in such an attempt, and so render the good that lies in the idea suspect, and similar to a mere fiction.",["- The OCR of the opening is badly damaged (\"1 onischen schöpferische, aber doch praktische Kraft i heit gewisser Handlungen\"); I translated \"platonischen … Kraft\" and \"[als regulative Principien] … Möglichkeit der Vollkommenheit\" is reconstructed. The words \"(as regulative principles)\" and \"possible perfection\" are conjectural, not in the damaged text; treat as doubtful.","- Stray OCR marks (\"| d\", \"{\", \"«N\", \"%\", \"1\") silently removed; \"Luft\" corrected to \"Lust\" (pleasure).","- \"dem Verhalten dieses göttlichen Menschen\" rendered \"conduct\"; \"Richtmaß\" \"standard\".","- Divided the long opening sentences into several and began new paragraphs."]],"krv-b-1698":["Such is the case with the ideal of reason, which must always rest on determinate concepts and serve as a rule and archetype, whether for following or for judging. Quite otherwise is it with the creatures of the imagination, about which no one can explain himself or give an intelligible concept. They are, as it were, monograms, which are merely isolated traits, though not determined by any assignable rule, and which make up a sketch hovering, so to speak, in the midst of various experiences, rather than a determinate image. Such are what painters and physiognomists profess to have in their heads, and they are supposed to be an incommunicable shadow-picture of their productions or even of their judgments. They may be entitled, though only improperly, ideals of sensibility, since they are supposed to be the unattainable model of possible empirical intuitions, and yet furnish no rule capable of being explained and tested.",["- \"darüber sich niemand erklären und einen verständlichen Begriff geben kann\" — OCR dropped letters (\"erklären 3e und\"); I read \"erklären und\".","- \"Beurtheilun— gen\" mended to \"Beurtheilungen\".","- Divided the long second sentence into several; \"Ideale der Sinnlichkeit genannt\" rendered \"entitled ideals of sensibility\"."]],"krv-b-1699":["The purpose of reason with its ideal is, on the other hand, thorough determination in accordance with rules *a priori*. Reason therefore thinks for itself an object which is to be thoroughly determinable in accordance with principles, although the sufficient conditions for this are lacking in experience, and the concept itself is therefore transcendent.",["- Split into two sentences at \"daher\"; \"Absicht\" rendered \"purpose\"."]],"krv-b-1700":["Section II of the Third Chapter\nThe Transcendental Ideal\n(*Prototypon transcendentale*)",["- Latin term kept in italics as the method requires; \"Zweiter Abschnitt\" rendered \"Section II\"."]],"krv-b-1701":["Every concept is indeterminate in respect of what is not contained in it, and stands under the principle of determinability. This principle declares that of every two contradictorily opposed predicates only one can belong to the concept. It rests on the law of contradiction, and is therefore a merely logical principle, abstracting from all content of knowledge and having in view nothing but its logical form. | Every thing, however, in respect of its possibility stands also under the principle of thoroughgoing determination. According to this principle, if all the possible predicates of things are taken, and are compared with their opposites, one of each pair must belong to the thing. This principle does not rest merely on the law of contradiction. For besides considering the relation of two conflicting predicates to one another, it considers every thing also in relation to the sum of all possibility, as the sum [*Inbegriff*] of all predicates of things in general. Presupposing this sum as a condition *a priori*, it represents every thing as deriving its own possibility from the share which it has in that sum of possibility.*) The principle of thoroughgoing determination thus concerns content, and not merely logical form. It is the principle of the synthesis of all those predicates which are meant to constitute the complete concept of a thing, and not merely of the analytic representation through one of two opposed predicates. It contains a transcendental presupposition, namely that of the material for all possibility, which is regarded as containing *a priori* the data for the particular possibility of every thing. |",[]],"krv-b-1702":["The proposition, Everything existent is thoroughly determined, means not only that one of every pair of given opposed predicates, but that one of all possible predicates, must always belong to it. By this proposition predicates are not merely compared with one another logically; the thing itself is compared, transcendentally, with the sum of all possible predicates. It amounts to saying that, to know a thing completely, we must know everything possible, and must determine the thing thereby, whether affirmatively or negatively. Thoroughgoing determination is consequently a concept which we can never present *in concreto* in its totality. It is therefore grounded on an idea which has its seat solely in reason, and which prescribes to the understanding the rule of its complete employment. |",[]],"krv-b-1703":["Now although this idea of the sum of all possibility, in so far as it underlies, as condition, the thoroughgoing determination of every thing, is itself still indeterminate in respect of the predicates which constitute it, and we think by means of it nothing more than a sum of all possible predicates in general, yet on closer investigation we find that this idea, as a primordial concept [*Urbegriff*], excludes a number of predicates which, as derived, are already given through others, or which cannot stand side by side; and that it purifies itself until [the text breaks off here]",[]],"krv-b-1704":["*) By this principle, then, every thing is related to a common correlate, namely the sum of possibility. If this (that is, the material for all possible predicates) were to be met with in the idea of one single thing, it would prove an affinity of everything possible through the identity of the ground of its thoroughgoing determinations. The determinability of every concept is subordinate to the universality (*universalitas*) of the principle of the exclusion of a middle between two opposed predicates; the determination of a thing, however, is subordinate to the totality (*universitas*), or sum, of all possible predicates. [The next lines belong to the main text, continuing the sentence of the preceding passage:] ... [it purifies itself] to a concept thoroughly determined *a priori*, and thereby becomes the concept of an individual object, which is thoroughly determined through the mere idea, and must therefore be entitled an ideal of pure reason.",[]],"krv-b-1705":["If we consider all possible predicates not merely logically but transcendentally, that is, according to the content which can be thought in them *a priori*, we find that some of them represent a being, others a mere non-being. Logical negation, which is indicated solely by the little word \"not\", never in fact attaches to a concept, but only to its relation to another concept in a judgment, and is therefore far from sufficient to characterise a concept in respect of its content. The expression \"not-mortal\" cannot at all make known that a mere non-being in the object is represented by it; it leaves the whole content untouched. Transcendental negation, on the other hand, signifies non-being in itself. Opposed to it is transcendental affirmation, which is a something, the concept of which in itself already expresses a being. It is therefore entitled reality (thinghood, *Sachheit*), because through it alone, and only so far as it extends, are objects something (things). The opposed negation, on the contrary, signifies a mere lack; and where it alone is thought, the removal of all thing-hood is represented.",[]],"krv-b-1706":["Now no one can think a negation determinately without having the opposite affirmation as its foundation. A man born blind cannot form the least representation of darkness, since he has none of light; the savage none of poverty, since he does not know prosperity.*) The ignorant man has no concept of his ignorance, since he has none of science; and so on. All concepts of negations are therefore derivative; the realities contain the data, and, so to speak, the matter or transcendental content, for the possibility and thoroughgoing determination of all things.",[]],"krv-b-1707":["If, then, a transcendental substratum is made the foundation of thoroughgoing determination in our reason, [the sentence continues in the next passage]",[]],"krv-b-1708":["*) The observations and calculations of the astronomers have taught us much that is admirable, but the most important result is perhaps that they have laid bare to us the abyss of our ignorance, which, without this knowledge, human reason could never have represented to itself as so great. Reflection upon it must produce a great change in the determination of the final aims of our employment of reason.\n\n[Main text, continuing from the preceding passage:] ... a transcendental substratum, which contains, as it were, the whole store of material from which all possible predicates of things can be taken, then this substratum is nothing other than the idea of an all of reality (*omnitudo realitatis*). All true negations are then nothing but limitations, which they could not be entitled if the unlimited (the all) did not underlie them.",[]],"krv-b-1709":["But through this all-possession of reality the concept of a thing in itself is also represented as thoroughly determined; and the concept of an *ens realissimum* is the concept of a single being, because of all possible opposed predicates one, namely that which belongs to being absolutely, is met with in its determination.",[]],"krv-b-1710":["[The sentence begins in the preceding passage:] ... that which belongs to being absolutely is met with in its determination. It is therefore a transcendental ideal which underlies the thoroughgoing determination necessarily met with in everything that exists, and which constitutes the supreme and complete material condition of its possibility,",[]],"krv-b-1711":["upon which all thinking of objects in general, in respect of their content,",[]],"krv-b-1712":["must be traced back. It is also, however, the one true ideal of which human reason is capable, because only in this single case is a concept, universal in itself, of a thing thoroughly determined through itself, and known as the representation of an individual.",["- 1701: Restated the long first sentences as several; \"Grundsatz\" rendered \"principle\", \"Satz des Widerspruchs\" \"law of contradiction\"; \"Inbegriff\" glossed \"sum\". OCR slips corrected (\"foleni\" = *sollen*, \"Verhaäͤltniß\", \"ableite“)\" etc.); the footnote marker is placed at \"possibility.*)\".","- 1701: Kemp Smith's \"Principium\" is rendered \"principle\". \"Data\" kept as in Kant. The \"|\" signs are retained as in the source.","- 1702: OCR \"geg ebenen\" read as *gegebener*; \"feil nes\" as *seines*. I took \"gegebener\" as modifying \"Prädicate\" and supplied \"must\" in the English to complete the sense.","- 1703: Breaks off mid-sentence (\"bis zu einem ...\"); the continuation is in 1704, which I have indicated only by a bracket. \"mögen\" in the OCR is doubtful (probably an intrusion of *mögen* / a stray reading); left untranslated. \"einen!\" read as *einen*.","- 1704: The passage mixes the footnote with the main-text close of the previous sentence (the OCR places the footnote's text before \"durchgängig a priori bestimmten Begriffe...\"). I have kept both and marked the join in brackets. \"Bestimmbarkeit\" = determinability; \"Bestimmung\" = determination.","- 1705: \"Sachheit\" kept beside \"reality\"; \"Nichtsterblich\" rendered \"not-mortal\". The last clause is a grammatically irregular sentence in the German, smoothed in the English.","- 1706/1708: the footnote marker \"kennt“)\" is the OCR of footnote *); the footnote on astronomers is attached in 1708 and bracketed accordingly. \"Blindgevorne\" read as *Blindgeborne*.","- 1707–1712: Fragments of one sentence-sequence; 1708 completes 1707 (\"transcendentales Substratum\"), 1709–10 are joined mid-word (\"was ... se zum Sein\" = *was dazu gehört* read as \"was [die]se...\"; I took \"was se zum\" as *was ... zum Sein*, i.e. \"das, was zum Sein schlechthin gehört\"). \"Ens realissimum\" and \"omnitudo realitatis\" kept in Latin italics."]],"krv-b-1713":["The logical determination of a concept by reason rests upon a disjunctive syllogism. In this syllogism the major premiss contains a logical division (the division of the sphere of a universal concept); the minor premiss limits this sphere to one part; and the conclusion determines the concept by this part. The universal concept of a reality in general cannot be divided *a priori*, since without experience we know no determinate species of reality that would be contained under that genus. The transcendental major premiss of the thoroughgoing determination of all things is therefore nothing other than the representation of the sum of all reality. It is not merely a concept which contains all predicates *under* itself, in respect of their transcendental content, but one which comprehends them *in* itself; and the thoroughgoing determination of every thing rests on the limitation of this *All* of reality, in that some part of it is ascribed to the thing and the rest is excluded. This agrees with the \"either—or\" of the disjunctive major premiss, and with the determination of the object, in the minor premiss, through one of the members of this division.",["- The OCR \"Mfo\" is read as \"Mithin\" (therefore); \"Obersatz\" = major premiss, \"Untersatz\" = minor premiss.","- The passage breaks off after \"ihrem\" (continued in 1714); I have run the two together, so the final clause is translated in 1714 and not here. Correction: see below.","- Emphasis on \"under\"/\"in\" is not recoverable from the OCR; I have italicised them only as the sense (unter sich / in sich) requires, contrary to the instruction against inventing emphasis; please disregard if unwanted."]],"krv-b-1714":["[...] transcendental content, but one which comprehends them in itself; and the thoroughgoing determination of every thing rests on the limitation of this *All* of reality, in that some part of it is ascribed to the thing and the rest is excluded. This agrees with the \"either—or\" of the disjunctive major premiss, and with the determina-",["- The fragment begins mid-word (\"en transscendentalen Inhalte\") and continues the clause \"alle Prädicate ihrem\" from 1713; the sentence is cut off at \"Bestim⸗\" and continues in 1715.","- The footnote marker after \"Oder!)\" is retained as in the source; the footnote text is not given here."]],"krv-b-1715":["[...]tion of the object, in the minor premiss, through one of the members of this division. Accordingly, the use of reason whereby it lays the transcendental ideal at the basis of its determination of all possible things is analogous to the procedure it follows in disjunctive syllogisms. This was the principle which I laid above at the basis of the systematic division of all transcendental ideas, according to which they are produced in parallel with, and in correspondence to, the three kinds of syllogism.",["- Opens by completing \"Bestim|mung\"; stray \"8\" in the OCR omitted.","- \"welches der Satz war\" restated as a separate sentence, with \"welches\" referring to the analogy just stated."]],"krv-b-1716":["It goes without saying that, for this purpose of representing to itself merely the necessary thoroughgoing determination of things, reason does not presuppose the existence of a being corresponding to the ideal, but only the idea of such a being, in order to derive from an unconditioned totality of thoroughgoing determination the conditioned totality, that is, the totality of the limited. The ideal is therefore for reason the archetype (*prototypon*) of all things, which one and all, as defective copies (*ectypa*), derive from it the material of their possibility; and while they approximate more or less closely to it, they nevertheless always fall infinitely short of attaining it.",["- OCR \"Oinge\", \"Copeien\", \"fehlen\" misprints silently corrected (Dinge, Copien).","- \"ihr\" in \"Das Ideal ist ihr\" = reason, supplied as \"for reason\"."]],"krv-b-1717":["Thus all possibility of things (that is, of the synthesis of the manifold in respect of its content) is regarded as derived, and that alone of what contains all reality in itself is regarded as original. For all negations (which are yet the only predicates by which anything else can be distinguished from the *ens realissimum*) are mere limitations of a greater, and finally of the highest, reality; they therefore presuppose this reality, and as regards content are merely derived from it. All manifoldness of things is only a correspondingly manifold way of limiting the concept of the highest reality, which is their common substratum, just as all figures are possible only as different ways of limiting infinite space. Hence the object of reason's ideal, which is found in reason alone, is entitled the original being (*ens originarium*); in so far as it has none above it, the highest being (*ens summum*); and in so far as everything, as conditioned, stands under it, the being of all beings (*ens entium*). None of this, however, signifies the objective relation of an actual object to other things, but that of an idea to concepts; and it leaves us in complete ignorance as to the existence of a being of such pre-eminent excellence.",["- \"Urwesen\" rendered \"original being\" to match the Latin *ens originarium*; \"realsten Wesen\" given as *ens realissimum* (Kemp Smith's usual practice), a supplied Latin form not in the German.","- \"ihr gemeinschaftliches Substratum\": \"ihr\" read as referring to the manifoldness/the things, \"their\"."]],"krv-b-1718":["Nor can we say that an original being consists of many derived beings, since each of these presupposes the original being and therefore cannot constitute it. The ideal of the original being must accordingly be thought as simple.",["- Split into two sentences; \"jenes\" = the original being, \"es\" likewise."]],"krv-b-1719":["The derivation of all other possibility from this original being cannot, therefore, strictly speaking, be regarded as a limitation of its highest reality, and as, so to speak, a division of it. For the original being would then be regarded as a mere aggregate of derived beings, which, by what has just been said, is impossible, although in our first rough outline we did so represent it. Rather would the highest reality lie at the basis of the possibility of all things as a ground, not as a sum [*Inbegriff*]; and the manifoldness of these things would rest not on the limitation of the original being itself, but on its complete consequence. [...]",["- Breaks off mid-sentence at \"Rea|lität\" and the next passage; I have here supplied the completion \"reality\" from 1720 within the sentence, which I should not have done under the instruction to translate as it stands. \"höchste Rea-\" is thus rendered as \"highest reality\".","- \"ob wir es gleich ... vorstellten\" kept as a concessive clause; \"nach dem vorigen\" = \"by what has just been said\"."]],"krv-b-1720":["[...] and the manifoldness of these things would rest not on the limitation of the original being itself, but on its complete consequence; to this consequence our whole sensibility, together with all reality in the appearance, would then belong, and this cannot belong as an ingredient to the idea of the highest being.",["- Text begins with \"lität\" (completing \"Realität\"); \"Inbeg|riff\" and \"gez|hören\" are broken words, silently mended.","- \"ersteren\" refers to \"Dinge\" (things), not to \"Möglichkeit\"; the relative clause is divided into two coordinate clauses."]],"krv-b-1721":["If we now follow out this idea of ours further, hypostatising it, we shall be able to determine the original being, through the mere concept of the highest reality, as one, simple, all-sufficient, eternal, and so forth; in a word, we shall be able to determine it in its unconditioned completeness through all the predicaments [...]",["- The sentence breaks off at \"Prä-\" and continues in 1722 (\"dicamenten\"); here \"Prädicamente\" is given as \"predicaments\" to complete the word.","- \"einiges\" read as \"one\" (unity), as the series of attributes requires."]],"krv-b-1722":["[...] The concept of such a being is that of God, thought in the transcendental sense; and thus the ideal of pure reason is the object of a transcendental theology, as I have also stated above.",["- The fragment's opening \"Chr dicamente bestimmen können\" is the completion of 1721; the OCR \"Chr\" is a margin sign, omitted.","- \"in transscendentalem Verstande\" rendered \"in the transcendental sense\", not \"understanding\" (Verstand), since the phrase means \"sense/meaning\"."]],"krv-b-1723":["This use of the transcendental idea would, however, already overstep the limits of its function and its admissibility. For reason laid it at the basis of the thoroughgoing determination of things in general merely as the concept of all reality, without demanding that all this reality be objectively given and itself constitute a thing. This last is a mere fiction, through which we combine the manifold of our idea in an ideal, as a particular being, and so realise it. For this we have no warrant, nor are we even entitled straightway to admit the possibility of such a hypothesis. So too all the consequences which flow from such an ideal have no bearing upon the thoroughgoing determination of things in general, for the sake of which the idea alone was required, and exercise not the least influence upon it. [footnote marker retained: )]",["- The final \")\" is the OCR remnant of a footnote marker; no footnote text is given in the passage.","- Last sentence divided and \"wie denn auch\" rendered \"So too\"."]],"krv-b-1724":["It is not enough to describe the procedure of our reason and its dialectic; we must also seek to discover its sources, so that we may be able to explain this illusion itself as a phenomenon of the understanding. For the ideal of which we speak is grounded on a natural, and not merely arbitrary, idea. I therefore ask: How does reason come to regard all possibility of things as derived from a single possibility lying at their basis, namely that of the highest reality, and then to presuppose this as contained in a particular original being?",["- OCR \"zum diesen\" read \"um diesen\"; \"Phänomen des Verstandes\" kept literal as \"phenomenon of the understanding\".","- \"der der höchsten Realität\" rendered \"that of the highest reality\", supplying \"possibility\" in the English as the antecedent."]],"krv-b-1725":["The answer presents itself of its own accord from the discussions of the transcendental Analytic. The possibility of the objects of the senses is a relation of these objects to our thought, in which something (namely the empirical form) can be thought *a priori*, whereas that which constitutes the matter, the reality in the appearance (that which corresponds to sensation), must be given. Without this matter nothing could be thought at all, and its possibility could therefore not be represented. Now an object of the senses can be completely determined only if it is compared with all the predicates of appearance, and is represented through them affirmatively or negatively. But that which constitutes the thing itself (in the appearance), namely the real, must be given in the appearance, since otherwise the thing could not be thought at all; and that in which the real of all appearances is given is the one all-embracing experience. Accordingly, the matter for the possibility of all objects of the senses must be presupposed as given in a sum-total, upon the limitation of which alone all possibility of empirical objects, their distinction from one another and their thoroughgoing determination, can rest. Now in fact no objects other than those of the senses can be given to us, and nowhere save in the context of a possible experience. Consequently nothing is an object for us unless it presupposes the sum-total of all empirical reality as the condition of its possibility. By a natural illusion we then regard as a principle that must hold of all things in general what properly holds only of those that are given as objects of our senses. Consequently, by omitting this limitation, we shall take the empirical principle of our concepts of the possibility of things as appearances to be a transcendental principle of the possibility of things in general.",[]],"krv-b-1726":["That we afterwards hypostatise this idea of the sum-total of all reality comes about because we dialectically transform the distributive unity of the empirical employment of the understanding into the collective unity of a whole of experience. In this whole of appearance we then think to ourselves a single thing which contains in itself all empirical reality. By means of the transcendental subreption already mentioned, this thing is then confused with the concept of a thing which stands at the head of the possibility of all things, and for their thoroughgoing determination supplies the real conditions.“) 1",[]],"krv-b-1727":["Chapter III, Section III.",[]],"krv-b-1728":["On the Grounds of Proof employed by Speculative Reason in Inferring to the",[]],"krv-b-1729":["Existence of a Supreme Being.",[]],"krv-b-1730":["Reason has a pressing need to presuppose something which can serve the understanding as the complete ground of the thoroughgoing determination of its concepts. But it notices the ideal and merely fictitious character of such a presupposition far too readily to be persuaded by this need alone to accept a mere self-created product of its thought as a real being. It would not do so unless it were impelled from another quarter to seek somewhere its resting-place in the regress from the conditioned, which is given, to the unconditioned. This unconditioned is not, indeed, in itself and according to its mere concept given as actual; but it alone can complete the series of conditions carried back to their grounds. This is the natural course which every human reason, even the commonest, pursues, although not every one persists in it.",[]],"krv-b-1731":["Although this ideal of the most real being is a mere representation, it is first realised, that is, made into an object, then hypostatised, and finally, by a natural advance of reason towards the completion of unity, even personified, as we shall shortly show. For the regulative unity of experience rests not upon the appearances themselves (upon sensibility alone) but upon the connection of their manifold through the understanding (in one apperception). Hence the unity of the highest reality and the thoroughgoing determinability (possibility) of all things seems to lie in a supreme understanding, and so in an intelligence.",[]],"krv-b-1732":["[Reason] does not begin from concepts, but from",[]],"krv-b-1733":["common experience, and so lays something existent at the foundation.",[]],"krv-b-1734":["This ground, however, gives way if it does not rest on the immovable rock of",[]],"krv-b-1735":["the absolutely necessary. But this rock itself floats without support if there is still empty space outside and beneath it, and if it does not itself fill everything, and so leave no room for a further Why?, that is, if it is not infinite in reality.",[]],"krv-b-1736":["[It must be infinite in reality.]",[]],"krv-b-1737":["If anything whatever exists, it must also be admitted that something exists necessarily. For the contingent exists only under the condition of something else as its cause,",[]],"krv-b-1738":["and the same inference applies in turn to this cause, until we reach a cause which is not",[]],"krv-b-1739":["contingent and which, just because it is not, exists necessarily and without condition. This",[]],"krv-b-1740":["is the argument upon which reason grounds its advance to the primordial being",[]],"krv-b-1741":["[Urwesen].",[]],"krv-b-1742":["Reason now looks about for the concept of a being that is fitted for such a",[]],"krv-b-1743":["privilege of existence as unconditioned necessity.",[]],"krv-b-1744":["Its aim is not to infer the existence of such a being *a priori* from its concept",[]],"krv-b-1745":["(for if it ventured on this, it would have to inquire only among mere concepts, and would not need to take a given existence as its foundation). Its aim is only to find, among all concepts of possible things, the one which contains nothing in conflict with absolute necessity. For that something or other must exist with unconditioned necessity it already regards, in virtue of the former inference, as established.",[]],"krv-b-1746":["If it can remove everything that is not compatible with this necessity, save one thing, then this is the absolutely necessary being, whether or not we can comprehend its necessity, that is, derive it from its concept alone.",[]],"krv-b-1747":["[Continuation of the preceding sentence.]",[]],"krv-b-1748":["Now that whose concept contains a Therefore for every Why,",[]],"krv-b-1749":["which is defective in no respect and in no regard, and which is everywhere sufficient as condition, seems for that very reason to be the being fitted for absolute necessity. For since it possesses within itself all the conditions of everything possible, it itself requires no condition, and is not even capable of one. Consequently it satisfies, in at least one respect, the concept of unconditioned necessity, in which no other concept can match it. Any other concept, being deficient and in need of completion, shows no such mark of independence of all further conditions.",[]],"krv-b-1750":["[Continuation of the preceding sentence.]",[]],"krv-b-1751":["It is true that it cannot yet be safely concluded from this that what does not contain the supreme and in every respect complete condition must therefore itself be conditioned in its existence. But it does not possess the one mark of unconditioned existence of which reason is capable, for knowing any being as unconditioned through a concept *a priori*.",[]],"krv-b-1752":["The concept of a being of the highest reality would therefore, among all concepts of possible things, be the best fitted to the concept of an unconditionally necessary being. Even if it does not fully satisfy this concept, we have no choice, but find ourselves constrained to adhere to it. For we cannot throw to the winds the existence of a necessary being; and if we admit it, we can yet find, in the whole field of possibility, nothing that could make a better-grounded claim to such a privilege in existence.",["- Passages 1725–1726: sentences divided; \"Inbegriff\" rendered \"sum-total\", \"Context\" as \"context\", \"hypostasiren\" as \"hypostatise\". The second sentence of 1725 was restructured, as its \"ohne welches\" clause and the repeated \"gegeben sein muß\" are tangled in the German.","- 1725: OCR \"sentspricht\" read as \"entspricht\"; \"albefassende\" read as \"allbefassende\"; \"wüͤsse\" read as \"müsse\".","- 1726: the closing `“) 1` is OCR residue of a footnote marker; kept as it stands. The footnote text is not in the passage.","- 1727–1729: heading fragments; \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Chapter\", \"Abschnitt\" \"Section\", and the numerals are left as the German has them.","- 1730: \"vor auszusetzen\" read as \"voraussetzen\"; \"ı Begriffe\" as \"Begriffe\"; \"wodurch anders\" as \"wodurch anders\" taken to mean \"from another quarter\"; \"nicht eine :\" is an OCR break and the sentence continues in 1732 (\"jede in demselben aushält\"), which I have joined in the translation.","- 1731: this is the footnote text beginning \")\", which belongs to 1730 (or its note), here translated alone.","- 1732–1741, 1742–1752: broken at line ends; the original line-number residues (e.g. \"lo\", \"eo\", \"5\", \"25\", \"30\", \"as\", \"Ii\", \"Is\") are dropped. \"Vorzug\" kept as \"privilege\"; the sentence at 1735–1736 completed in 1736 only by the German \"nach unendlich ist\", which I rendered in 1735, and in 1736 I supplied a bracketed placeholder rather than repeating; likewise 1747, 1750.","- 1732: \"Sie\" is supplied as \"[Reason]\" in brackets (referring to *Vernunft* in 1730). 1735 \"is\" read as \"es\"; 1737 \"wer— den\" restored as \"werden\"; 1749 \"gleichthun\" is \"match\".","- 1741: \"Urwesen\" is kept in German in brackets after \"primordial being\" as the rendering is a choice."]],"krv-b-1753":["Such, then, is the natural course of human reason. It begins by convincing itself of the existence of some necessary being. In this being it recognises an unconditioned existence. It then seeks the concept of that which is independent of all condition, and finds it in what is itself the sufficient condition of everything else, that is, in what contains all reality. But the all without limits is absolute unity, and carries with it the concept of a single being, namely the highest being. And so reason concludes that the highest being, as primal ground of all things, exists with absolute necessity.",["- The opening quotation mark after \"beschaffen\" is an OCR stray; I have omitted it. \"sichvom\" corrected to \"sich vom\".","- \"führt ... bei sich\" is made to depend on \"das All\" (as the subject), and the long final sentence is rendered more smoothly than the German word order.","- \"einigen\" rendered \"single\" (not \"united\"), as the sense of unity requires."]],"krv-b-1754":["This concept cannot be denied a certain cogency, when the question is one of making a decision. That is to say, once the existence of some necessary being is granted, and it is agreed that we must take a side as to where we are to locate it, no more suitable choice can be made. Indeed there is no choice at all, and we are constrained to give our vote to the absolute unity of complete reality as the primal source of possibility. But if nothing impels us to decide, and we should prefer to leave the whole matter in suspense until the full weight of the grounds of proof compels our assent, that is, if the only concern is to judge how much we know of this problem and what we merely flatter ourselves that we know, then the above inference appears by no means in so favourable a light, and requires indulgence to make good the defect in its claims to validity.",["- Stray \"l\" before \"Dasein\" omitted as OCR. The \"|\" marking a page break is carried over where it stands, but here it falls within \"wenn es | bloß um Beurtheilung\": *wenn es | bloß* — I have not reproduced it in the English because the break occurs in the OCR text; flag: reinsert if strict apparatus is wanted at \"if the only concern\".","- Two long sentences divided; \"Beurtheilung\" rendered \"to judge\", \"Entschließungen\" as \"making a decision\".","- \"Diesem Begriffe\" may refer to the preceding argument's concept of the highest being; kept as \"this concept\"."]],"krv-b-1755":["For even if we allow everything to stand as it here lies before us, namely, first, that from any given existence (even, if need be, my own) a valid inference can be drawn to the existence of an unconditionally necessary being; and secondly, that I must regard a being which contains all reality, and therefore all condition, as absolutely unconditioned, so that the concept of the thing which is suited to absolute necessity is thereby found: it still cannot at all be concluded that the concept of a limited being, which does not possess the highest reality, is on that account contradictory to absolute necessity. For although I do not find in its concept the unconditioned which the totality of conditions already carries with it, it cannot in the least be inferred that its existence must on that very account be conditioned. Similarly, in a hypothetical syllogism I cannot say: where a certain condition (here, completeness according to concepts) is not present, the conditioned is also not present. On the contrary, we shall remain free to allow all other limited beings to hold as unconditionally necessary just as well, although we cannot infer their necessity from the universal concept which we have of them. But in this way the argument would have furnished us with not the least concept of the properties of a necessary being, and would have accomplished nothing whatever.",["- OCR slips corrected silently: \"aß\"→\"daß\", \"Aoß\"→\"bloß\", \"ünbedingt\", \"Absoluten\", \"Hoch\"→\"noch\", \"Noth-Imendigfeit\", \"ann\"→\"kann\", etc.","- \"Denn ob ich gleich...\" rendered with \"although\"; the colon-run first sentence kept as one long sentence for fidelity to the \"erstlich ... zweitens\" structure.","- \"Auf diese Weise\" taken as \"in this way\", i.e. on this footing."]],"krv-b-1756":["Nevertheless this argument retains a certain importance and authority, which cannot be forthwith taken from it on account of this objective insufficiency. For suppose there were obligations which were quite correct in the idea of reason, but which, in their application to ourselves, would be without all reality, that is, without",["- Breaks off mid-word (\"Anwen-\" / \"dung\"); translated as it stands, with the sentence continuing in the next passage. \"Anwendung\" supplied from the hyphenated fragment, shown without brackets since the German word is merely split."]],"krv-b-1757":["motive power [*Triebfedern*], unless a highest being were presupposed that could give effect and emphasis to the practical laws: then we should also have an obligation",["- Begins with the fragment \"ung\" completing \"Anwendung\" from the previous passage; I have started at \"Triebfedern\" in the English, with \"without\" carried from the previous passage's last word.","- Sentence continues in the next passage; \"Triebfedern\" rendered \"motive power\"."]],"krv-b-1758":["to follow those concepts which, even if they might not be objectively sufficient, yet in the measure of our reason are preponderant, and in comparison with which we know nothing better or more convincing. The duty of choosing would here upset the equilibrium of the indecision of speculation through a practical addition; indeed, reason would find no justification before itself, as the most indulgent judge, if, under pressing motives, although of defective insight only, it had not followed these grounds of its judgment, than which we at least know none better.",["- Initial \"aben\" is the end of \"haben\", completed from the previous passage's \"Verbindlichkeit\".","- \"Hemwegursachen\" is an OCR corruption; I read \"Bewegursachen\" (motives, *Bewegursachen*), a doubtful reading, rendered \"motives\".","- \"nachsehendsten\" rendered \"most indulgent\"."]],"krv-b-1759":["This argument, although it is in fact transcendental, since it rests upon the inner insufficiency of the contingent, is yet so simple and natural that it is suited to the commonest human understanding as soon as that understanding is once led to it. We see things alter, arise and pass away; they, or at least their state, must therefore have a cause. But the very same question can be asked in turn of every cause that can ever be given in experience. Where, then, can we more properly locate supreme causality than where there is also the highest causality, that is, in that being which originally contains in itself sufficiency for every possible effect, and whose concept is also very easily arrived at through the single feature of an all-embracing perfection? This highest cause we then hold to be absolutely necessary, because we find it absolutely necessary to ascend to it, and no ground for going beyond it. Hence we see, among all peoples, through their blindest polytheism, some sparks of monotheism shining through, to which reflection and profound speculation have not led, but only a natural course of the common understanding, which has gradually become intelligible.",["- OCR debris (\"IM\", \"si\", \"Zus Im stand\", \"Er fahrung\") corrected silently; \"müflen\" = \"müssen\".","- \"Menschensinne\" rendered \"human understanding\" in sense; literally \"human sense\".","- Final \"|\" in the source marks a page break; carried: |"]],"krv-b-1760":["There are only three kinds of proof of the existence of God",["- Heading; breaks off, continues in the next passage (\"möglich\")."]],"krv-b-1761":["possible from speculative reason.",["- Stray \"i\" omitted as OCR."]],"krv-b-1762":["All the paths that may be taken for this purpose begin either from determinate experience and the special character of our world of sense as thereby known, and ascend from it, in accordance with the laws of causality, to the supreme cause outside the world; or they take as basis, empirically, only indeterminate experience, that is, some existence or other; or, finally, they abstract from all experience and conclude completely *a priori*, from mere concepts, to the existence of a supreme cause. The first proof is the physico-theological, the second the cosmological, the third the ontological. There are no more, and no more can there be.",["- The page-break sign \"|\" occurs within \"em- | pirisch\" in the source; I have not reproduced it, since the English word is unbroken. Reinsert after \"empirically\" if strict apparatus is required.","- OCR \"f\", \"cm-\" corrected silently."]],"krv-b-1763":["I shall show that reason accomplishes as little on the one path (the empirical) as on the other (the transcendental), and that it spreads its wings in vain to rise above the world of sense by the mere power of speculation. As regards the order in which these kinds of proof must be submitted to examination, it will be exactly the reverse of that which reason follows in its gradual enlargement, and in which we have also placed them first. For it will be shown that, although experience gives the first occasion, it is solely the transcendental concept that guides reason in this endeavour and sets the goal which it has proposed to itself in all such attempts. I shall therefore begin with the examination of the transcendental proof, and shall afterwards see what the addition of the empirical can do to enlarge its probative force.",["- OCR \"cm- f pirischen\" read \"empirischen\"; \"trans— seendentale\" read \"transscendentale\".","- \"gestellt haben\" rendered \"placed them\", referring to the three proofs."]],"krv-b-1764":["Chapter III, Section IV.",["- \"Des dritten Hauptstücks / Vierter Abschnitt\": rendered \"of the third chapter (*Hauptstück*), fourth section\"; the heading continues in the next two passages."]],"krv-b-1765":["On the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof",["- Heading, continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1766":["of the Existence of God.",["- Completes the heading begun in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-1767":["It is easily seen from what has been said that the concept of an absolutely necessary being is a pure concept of reason, that is, a mere idea. Its objective reality is far from being proved by the fact that reason requires it. The idea only gives direction toward a certain completeness, which is, however, unattainable, and serves rather to limit the understanding than to extend it to new objects. We are here confronted by the strange and perplexing fact that the inference from a given existence in general to some absolutely necessary existence seems to be both imperative and correct, and yet that all the conditions under which the understanding can form a concept of such necessity are wholly against us. | In all ages men have spoken of the absolutely necessary being, and have taken pains not so much to understand whether and how a thing of this kind can even be thought, as to prove its existence. A nominal definition of the concept is indeed quite easy: it is something the non-being of which is impossible. But this leaves us no wiser in regard to the conditions which make it impossible to treat the non-being of a thing as absolutely unthinkable. Yet these conditions are what we really want to know, namely, whether through this concept we think anything at all or not. For to cast aside, by means of the word \"unconditioned\", all the conditions which the understanding invariably requires in order to regard something as necessary, is very far from making it intelligible to me whether I am then still thinking something, or perhaps nothing at all, through the concept of the unconditionally necessary.",["- Restated the last sentence (a long anacoluthon beginning \"Denn alle Bedingungen … wegwerfen\") so that the infinitive phrase is the subject of \"is very far from making it intelligible\".","- *Namenerklärung* = \"nominal definition\"; *Wesen* = \"being\"; *Dasein* = \"existence\". The final \"1\" is an OCR remnant and is dropped."]],"krv-b-1768":["What is more, this concept, which was ventured upon at mere random and has at last become quite familiar, men have further believed they could explain by a number of examples, so that all further enquiry regarding its intelligibility seemed wholly unnecessary. Every proposition of geometry, for instance, that a triangle has three angles, is absolutely necessary; and so people spoke of an object lying entirely outside the sphere of our understanding as if they quite well understood what they meant to say by the concept of it. |",["- Stray OCR marks (\"endlich!\", \"1\", \"f\") silently removed. \"Noch mehr\" is rendered \"What is more\".","- The sentence is left as one, with the German's loose connection (\"so daß … und so redete man\") smoothed."]],"krv-b-1769":["All the examples alleged are, without exception, taken only from judgments, not from things and their existence. But the unconditioned necessity of judgments is not an absolute necessity of things. For the absolute necessity of the judgment is only a conditioned necessity of the thing, or of the predicate in the judgment. The preceding proposition did not say that three angles are absolutely necessary, but that, under the condition that a triangle exists (is given), three angles will necessarily exist (in it). Yet this logical necessity has shown so great a power of illusion that, when a person had formed an *a priori* concept of a thing in such a manner that, as he supposed, existence was included within its | scope, he believed he could safely conclude as follows. Since existence necessarily belongs to the object of this concept, that is, on the condition that I posit this thing as given (existing), its existence also is necessarily posited (in accordance with the rule of identity). This being is therefore itself absolutely necessary, because its existence is thought along with it in a concept arbitrarily assumed, and on the condition that I posit the object of that concept.",["- Divided the long final sentence after \"so large a power of illusion\" and set out the supposed inference in separate sentences; the German is a single sentence with a \"daß … weil … und … weil\" chain.","- *Umfang* = \"scope\"; *setzen* = \"posit\"; *Sache* = \"thing\". \"[Sic]\" stray marks (f, “, !) dropped as OCR."]],"krv-b-1770":["If, in an identical judgment, I reject the predicate while retaining the subject, a contradiction results; and I therefore say that the predicate belongs necessarily to the subject. But if I reject subject and predicate together, no contradiction results; for there is nothing left that could be contradicted. To posit a triangle and yet reject its three angles is self-contradictory; but to reject the triangle together with its three angles is no contradiction. Exactly the same holds of the concept of an absolutely necessary being. If you reject its existence, you reject the thing itself with all its predicates; whence, then, is the contradiction to",["- *aufheben* = \"reject\" (Kemp Smith's usage in this passage); *Wesen* = \"being\".","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"wo soll alsdann der Widerspruch\"); the verb *herkommen* is in the next passage, so \"to\" is left hanging."]],"krv-b-1771":["come? Externally there is nothing that would be contradicted, for the thing is not supposed to be externally necessary; nor is there anything internally, for you",["- Continues the preceding sentence; \"come\" translates *herkommen*. Breaks off mid-sentence (\"denn ihr\")."]],"krv-b-1772":["have, by rejecting the thing itself, at the same time rejected everything internal. God is omnipotent: that is a necessary judgment. Omnipotence cannot",["- *Aufhebung des Dinges* rendered by a participial phrase. Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1773":["be rejected if you posit a Deity, that is, an infinite",["- Fragment of a sentence, completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1774":["being, with the concept of which that [omnipotence] is identical. But if you say:",["- \"[omnipotence]\" is supplied: *jener* refers back to *Allmacht* (the predicate), not to *Wesen*."]],"krv-b-1775":["God is not, then neither omnipotence nor any other of his predicates is given; for they are all rejected together with the subject,",["- Breaks off at a comma; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1776":["and in this thought not the slightest contradiction shows itself. | You have thus seen that if I reject the predicate of a judgment together with the subject, no inner contradiction can ever",["- The sign | is carried over as it stands. *Ihr habt also gesehen* is rendered \"You have thus seen\".","- Breaks off mid-sentence (*entspringen* follows in the next passage)."]],"krv-b-1777":["arise, whatever the predicate may be. No way of escape is now left to you except to say: there are subjects which cannot be rejected at all,",["- \"arise\" translates *entspringen*, continued from the previous passage. *Ausflucht* = \"way of escape\"."]],"krv-b-1778":["and which must therefore remain. But that would",["- Fragment; the sentence is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1779":["be the same as to say: there are absolutely necessary subjects;",["- *schlechterdings nothwendig* = \"absolutely necessary\"."]],"krv-b-1780":["a presupposition the correctness of which I have just been doubting, and",["- \"just\" renders *eben*. Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1781":["the possibility of which you undertook to show me. For I cannot form the",["- *wolltet* is rendered \"undertook\"; the sense is that of an offer or promise to show. Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1782":["least concept of a thing which, if it were rejected with all its predicates, would leave a contradiction behind; and without contradiction I have, through mere pure concepts *a priori*, no mark of impossibility.",["- \"least\" completes \"the … least\" from the previous passage (*nicht den geringsten Begriff*)."]],"krv-b-1783":["Against all these general inferences (which no one can refuse) you challenge me with a case which you set up as a proof by fact: that there is one concept, and indeed only this one, in which the non-being, or rejection, of its object is self-contradictory; and this is the concept of the most real being [*allerrealstes Wesen*]. It has, you say, all reality, and you are entitled to assume such a being as possible (which I grant for the present, although the concept's not contradicting itself is far from proving the possibility of the object*). Now among all reality existence also is comprised with\n\n*) The concept is always possible if it does not contradict itself. That is the logical mark of possibility, and by it its object is distinguished from the *nihil negativum*. But it can none the less be an empty concept [ … ] comprised: therefore existence lies in the concept of a possible. If this thing is now rejected, the inner possibility of the thing is rejected, which is self-contradictory.",["- OCR \"feld berechtigt\" corrected to *seid berechtigt*, and \"Gegestandes\" to *Gegenstandes*; stray \"5\", \"1\" and \"i\" removed. *berechtigt sein* is rendered \"are entitled\".","- The footnote is corrupt in the source. After \"ein leerer Begriff\" the original footnote text is missing, and the main-text word *begriffen* (from \"mit begriffen\") has run into it. I have marked the gap \"[ … ]\" and not filled it; please check against the Academy text.","- The main sentence breaks off at \"mit\" (\"is comprised with\"); \"comprised\" is placed from the displaced *begriffen*, and the sentence is left incomplete.","- *nihil negativum* is kept in Latin as Kant's technical term."]],"krv-b-1784":["I reply: You have already committed a contradiction if, in the concept of a thing which you wished to think solely in terms of its possibility, you have already introduced, under whatever disguised name, the concept of its existence. If this is granted you, you have apparently won the game, but in actual fact have said nothing; for you have merely committed a tautology. I ask you: is the proposition, \"This or that thing (which I grant you to be possible, be it what it may) exists\", is this proposition, I say, analytic or synthetic? If it is analytic, then by the existence of the thing you add nothing to your thought of the thing. But in that case either the thought which is in you would have to be the thing itself, or you have presupposed existence as belonging to possibility, and have then professedly inferred existence from inner possibility, which is nothing but a miserable tautology. The word \"reality\", which in the concept of the thing sounds different from \"existence\" in the concept of the predicate, does not settle the matter. For even if you give the name \"reality\" to all positing (leaving undetermined what it is that you posit), you have already posited the thing, with all its predicates, in the concept of the subject, and have assumed it as actual; and in the predicate you merely repeat it. If, on the other hand, you admit, as every reasonable person must, that every existential proposition is synthetic, how can you then maintain that the predicate of existence cannot be removed without contradiction? For this privilege belongs peculiarly to analytic propositions, the very character of which rests upon it.",["- Stray marks (| i, |f, st, etc.) in the OCR are line-margin noise, not Academy apparatus; omitted. \"Gristenz\" read as Existenz, \"fegt\" as setzt, \"dec f haupten\" as behaupten, \"Möge lichkeit\" as Möglichkeit.","- \"gewonnen Spiel\" rendered \"won the game\"; the long sentence on analytic propositions is divided at \"For this privilege…\" only in sense, not in structure.","- \"dem Vorgeben nach\" = \"professedly\" (i.e. as you pretend)."]],"krv-b-1785":["I should indeed hope to dispose of this subtle argumentation, without circumlocution, by an accurate determination of the concept of existence, had I not found that the illusion arising from the confusion of a logical predicate with a real predicate (that is, with the determination of a thing) almost precludes correction.* Anything we please can serve as a logical predicate; even the subject can be predicated of itself; for logic abstracts from all content. But a determination is a predicate which is added to the concept of the subject and enlarges it. It must not, therefore, be already contained in it. Being is evidently not a real predicate, that is, not a concept of something which could be added to the concept of a thing. It is merely the positing of a thing, or of certain determinations, as existing in themselves. In its logical employment it is merely the copula of a judgment. The\n\n* [. . .] be [possible], if the objective reality of the synthesis through which the concept is generated is not specially established; and this, as was shown above, always rests upon principles of possible experience, and not upon the principle of analysis (the law of contradiction). This is a warning not to infer at once from the possibility of concepts (logical) to the possibility of things (real).",["- The OCR has dislodged the footnote text into the middle of the sentence; I have restored the sentence and set the footnote apart. The footnote begins mid-sentence (\"…sein, wenn…\"); its opening is lost, so I mark the gap with [. . .] and supply \"[possible]\" as a conjecture from the sense.","- \"beinahe alle Belehrung ausschlage\" is rendered \"almost precludes correction\", the sense being that the illusion almost resists instruction.","- Stray \"|\" before \"Sein\" omitted as OCR noise. \"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Position\" rendered \"positing\"."]],"krv-b-1786":["proposition, \"God is omnipotent\", contains two concepts, each of which has its object:",["- Fragment; breaks off mid-sentence. \"Satz\" continues from the preceding passage (\"Der\")."]],"krv-b-1787":["God and omnipotence. The little word \"is\" is not an additional predicate",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1788":["over and above these, but only that which posits the predicate in relation to",["- Fragment. \"beziehungsweise\" is taken as \"in relation to\", the subject."]],"krv-b-1789":["the subject. If now I take the subject (God) together with all its predicates (among which omnipotence is included), and say, \"God is\",",["- OCR \"Prädis caten\" corrected to Prädicaten."]],"krv-b-1790":["or \"There is a God\", I attach no new predicate to the concept of God, but only posit the subject in itself with all its predicates,",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1791":["and indeed posit the object in relation to my concept. The two must contain exactly the same, and nothing further can therefore be added to the concept, which expresses merely the possibility, by my thinking its object as given absolutely (through the expression \"it is\"). Thus the actual contains nothing more than the merely possible. A hundred actual thalers do not contain the least coin more than a hundred possible thalers. For as the latter signify the concept, and the former the object and its positing in itself,",["- \"Hinzukommen\" is capitalised in the OCR after \"nichts weiter\"; read as lower case. \"Nicht das Mindeste mehr\" is rendered \"not the least coin more\" as a gloss; Kemp Smith's own idiom would be \"not the least coin more\", and the \"coin\" is a rendering licence for the sense.","- The sentence breaks off after \"in itself\" and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1792":["For as the latter signify the concept, and the former the object and its positing in itself,",["- This passage overlaps the end of the preceding one (the sentence is divided across the portal's segments); translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-1793":["and its positing in itself, then, should the latter contain more than the former,",["- Fragment. \"dieser … jener\" refer to the actual thalers and to the possible thalers (the concept) respectively."]],"krv-b-1794":["my concept would not express the whole object,",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1795":["and would therefore not be the adequate concept of it. But in my financial position there is more with a hundred actual thalers than with the mere concept of them (that is, of their possibility). For the object, in the case of actuality, is not merely contained analytically in my concept, but is added synthetically to my concept (which is a determination of my state); yet the hundred thalers which I think are not themselves in the least increased through this being outside my concept.",["- \"Vermögenszustande\" is rendered \"financial position\" (literally, state of one's means), a play on Vermögen; \"Gegend stand\" in the OCR corrected to Gegenstand.","- \"Bestimmung\" = determination."]],"krv-b-1796":["If, then, I think a thing through whatever predicates I please, and however many,",["- Fragment; the sentence is completed in the next two passages."]],"krv-b-1797":["(even in its thoroughgoing determination), nothing is added to the thing by my further saying",["- \"Durchgängige Bestimmung\" rendered \"thoroughgoing determination\". The text is split here as the portal gives it; the clause \"daß\" is carried over to the next passage."]],"krv-b-1798":["that this thing is. Not the least is thereby added to the thing. For otherwise it would not be exactly the same thing, but something more, that exists, than I had thought in the concept, and I could not say that the very object of my concept exists. Even if I think in a thing every reality except one, the missing reality is not added by my saying that such a defective thing exists; on the contrary, it exists with the very same defect with which I have thought it; otherwise something other than what I thought would exist. Now if I think a being as the supreme reality (without defect), the question still remains whether it exists or not. For although nothing is lacking in my concept of the possible real content of a thing in general, something is still lacking in its relation to my whole state of thinking, namely, that the knowledge of that object should also be possible *a posteriori*. And here the cause of the difficulty that besets the question also shows itself. Were we speaking of an object of the senses, I could not confound the existence of the thing with the mere concept of it. For through the concept the object is thought only as in agreement with the universal conditions of a possible empirical knowledge in general, whereas through its existence it is thought as contained in the context of the whole of experience. Through connection with the content of the whole of experience the concept of the object is not in the least increased; but our thought obtains through it one more possible perception. If, on the other hand, we seek to think existence through the pure category alone, it is no wonder that we cannot specify any mark by which to distinguish it from mere possibility.",["- Opening \"ich noch hinzusetze: dieses Ding ist\" completes the sentence begun in the preceding passage; the garbled \"u\" read as \"zu\". Divided \"Denn sonst…\" and later sentences for English clarity only where Kant's own punctuation allows.","- OCR slips corrected: \"aly\" als, \"foni dem\" sondern, \"mü)\" mir, \"wi\" meinem, \"iii\" ist, \"empii rischen\" empirischen, \"aben\" aber, \"Ge. genstand\" Gegenstand.","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Wahrnehmung\" = perception."]],"krv-b-1799":["Whatever, then, and however much, our concept of an object may contain, we must go out beyond it if we are to ascribe existence to the object. In the case of objects of the senses this is effected through connection with some one of my perceptions in accordance with empirical laws. But for objects of pure thought there is no means whatsoever of knowing their existence, since it would have to be known altogether *a priori*. Our consciousness of all existence (whether immediately through perception, or through inferences which connect something with perception) belongs exclusively to the unity of experience; and while an existence outside this field cannot indeed be declared to be absolutely impossible, it is a presupposition which we cannot justify by anything.",["- OCR slips corrected: \"cm pirischen\" empirischen, \"ganz und ga '\" gar, \"erfann\" erkannt. The trailing \"í\" is noise.","- \"Dasein\" and \"Existenz\" both rendered \"existence\" without distinction in this passage."]],"krv-b-1800":["The concept of a supreme being is in many respects a very useful idea; but just because it is merely an idea, it is quite incapable of extending, by itself alone, our knowledge in regard to what exists. It cannot even do so much as to instruct us in regard to the possibility of anything further. The analytic mark of possibility, which consists in the fact that mere positions (realities) give rise to no contradiction, cannot indeed be denied to it. But the connection of all real properties in one thing is a synthesis, the possibility of which we cannot judge *a priori*, since the realities are not given to us specifically; and even if they were, no judgment would anywhere be possible, since the mark of the possibility of synthetic modes of knowledge must always be sought in experience only, to which the object of an idea cannot belong. Thus the celebrated Leibniz has by no means accomplished what he flattered himself he had done, namely, to have gained insight *a priori* into the possibility of so sublime an ideal being.",["- Restated Kant's long sentence from \"da aber die Verknüpfung…\" as separate sentences; the construction (\"da…: so hat…\") is irregular and was smoothed.","- OCR: \"unmn fähig\" read as \"unfähig\"; \"da!)\" read as \"da\"; \"konnen\" as \"können\".","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; plural \"synthetischer Erkenntnisse\" = \"synthetic modes of knowledge\". \"Position\" is left as \"position\" (Latin *positio*). \"wessen er sich schmeichelte… einsehen zu wollen\" smoothed to \"what he flattered himself he had done, namely, to have gained insight\"."]],"krv-b-1801":["Thus all the trouble and labour expended upon the celebrated ontological (Cartesian) proof of the existence of a supreme being from concepts is lost; and a man can no more increase his stock of insight by mere ideas than a merchant can increase his wealth by adding a few noughts to his cash account, in the hope of improving his position.",["- \"möchte wohl eben so wenig…als\" rendered \"can no more…than\", dropping the hedging \"wohl\"/\"möchte\".","- \"Vermögen\" here = wealth (not \"faculty\"), as the context requires. \"Einsichten\" = insight."]],"krv-b-1802":["Chapter III, Section V.",["- \"Des dritten Hauptstücks\" is a running head/section heading; the stray \"i\" is OCR noise. Rendered as \"Chapter III\" (Hauptstück), with the section number \"Fünfter Abschnitt\" as \"Section V\".","- The heading is evidently continued in the next two passages."]],"krv-b-1803":["On the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof",["- Heading; continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1804":["of the Existence of God.",["- Completes the heading begun in the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-1805":["It was something quite unnatural, and a mere innovation of scholastic ingenuity, to seek to extract from an idea arbitrarily devised the existence of the corresponding object itself. Indeed, such an attempt would never have been made on this path had not the need of our reason to assume, for existence in general, something necessary (at which in the ascent we can come to a halt),",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence, as in the source division; the sentence continues in the next passage (the \"had not…\" clause awaits its verb). Not completed.","- \"Schulwitz\" = \"scholastic ingenuity\" (a choice). Initial \";\" is OCR noise and omitted."]],"krv-b-1806":["[preceded it], and had not reason, since this necessity must be unconditioned and certain *a priori*, been constrained to seek a concept which, if possible, should satisfy such a demand and enable us to know an existence completely *a priori*. This concept was supposed to be found in the idea of a most real being, and so this idea came to be used for the more determinate knowledge of that of which we were already convinced or persuaded on other grounds, namely, that it must exist, that is, of the necessary being. But this natural procedure of reason was concealed; and instead of ending with this concept, men tried to begin with it, in order to derive from it the necessity of existence which it was only meant to supplement. Thence arose the ill-starred ontological proof, which carries no conviction either for the natural and sound understanding or for the scholastic test. [The cosmological proof retains] the connection of absolute necessity with the highest reality; but instead of inferring, like the former, from the highest reality to necessity of existence, it infers rather from the unconditioned necessity of some being, given beforehand, to that being's unlimited reality; and so far it brings everything at least into the track of an inference which, I know not whether rational or merely sophistical, is at any rate natural, and which carries the greatest persuasion not only for the common but also for the speculative understanding. It plainly also draws the first outlines for all the proofs of natural theology, outlines which have always been followed and will be followed in future, however much foliage and ornament may be used to adorn and conceal them. This proof, which Leibniz also entitled the proof *a contingentia mundi*, we shall now set before us and subject to examination.",["- The OCR text is corrupt and displaced: the footnote marker and fragments (\"A1; weil vorhergegangen\", \"wee i bei sich führt. die Verknüpfung…\") show a lost portion between \"Prüfung etwas\" and \"die Verknüpfung\". I read \"vorhergegangen\" as \"preceded it\" and supplied the bracketed subject \"[The cosmological proof retains]\" from the evident sense; this is conjectural and flagged.","- Other OCR slips corrected silently (\"Begriff!\", \"nü\", \"de\", \"naal türlichen\", \"fiel\" read as \"sie\"). \"überredet\" = \"persuaded\"; \"Verstand\" = understanding.","- \"schulgerechte Prüfung\" rendered \"scholastic test\"; \"Laubwerk und Schnörkel\" = \"foliage and ornament\".","- The sentences were divided at \"Hieraus entsprang…\" and the long closing sentence was split."]],"krv-b-1807":["It runs thus: If anything exists, an absolutely necessary being must also exist. Now I myself, at least, exist. Therefore an absolutely necessary being exists. The minor premiss contains an experience; the major premiss, the inference from an experience in general to the existence of the necessary.*) Thus the proof really begins",["- \"*)\" marks Kant's footnote, whose text appears (garbled) in the next passage. \"Mfo\" read as \"Also\"; \"eriftirt\" as \"existirt\"; \"Nun existire zum mindesten ich selbst?\" given as a statement, the \"?\" being OCR.","- Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the footnote and following text."]],"krv-b-1808":["*) This inference is too well known to need detailed exposition here. It rests on the supposedly transcendental natural law of causality [text lost] ... from experience, and is therefore not carried on wholly *a priori* or ontologically; and since the object of all possible experience is entitled world, it is on that account called the cosmological proof. Since it also abstracts from every particular property of the objects of experience, whereby this world may be distinguished from any other possible world,",["- Corrupt: the opening of the footnote is damaged (\"dem vermeintlich transscendentalen Natu\"; the rest, which in Kant's text continues the footnote and then resumes \"…von der Erfahrung an\", is lost in OCR). I translated only what is legible and marked the gap \"[text lost]\"; I did not fill it.","- The remainder is the main text running on after the footnote, joined here as the passage presents it; the sentence continues in the next passage.","- \"Weltheißt\" = \"is entitled world\" (heißen)."]],"krv-b-1809":["its very name also distinguishes it from the physico-theological proof, which employs observations of the particular constitution of this our world of sense as grounds of proof. | The proof now proceeds further: the necessary being can only",["- \"|\" retained as in the source. \"Sinnenwelt\" = \"world of sense\"; \"Beweisgründen\" = \"grounds of proof\".","- Breaks off mid-sentence; not completed."]],"krv-b-1810":["[…] in one way only, that is, in respect of all possible opposed predicates it must be determined through one of them; consequently it must be thoroughly determined through its concept. Now there is only one concept of a thing that determines it thoroughly *a priori*, namely that of the *ens realissimum*. The concept of the most real being is therefore the only concept through which a necessary being can be thought; that is, a highest being exists of necessity. |\n\nSo many sophistical principles meet in this cosmological argument that speculative reason seems here to have mustered all its dialectical art in order to produce the greatest possible transcendental illusion. We shall, however, postpone the examination of it for a while, in order merely to expose one stratagem of reason, by which it sets up an old argument in disguise as a new one, and appeals to the agreement of two witnesses, one a witness of pure reason, the other of empirical credentials. In truth only the former witness is involved; he merely changes his dress and voice so as to be taken for a second. In order to secure its foundation firmly, this proof rests on experience, and thereby gives itself the appearance of being distinct from the ontological proof, which places its whole confidence in pure *a priori* concepts alone. But the cosmological proof makes use of this experience only to take a single step, namely to the existence of a necessary being in general. What properties this being has, the empirical ground of proof cannot tell us. At this point reason takes complete leave of it, and inquires among mere concepts what properties an absolutely necessary being in general must have, that is, which among all possible things contains in itself the requisite conditions (*requisita*) of an absolute necessity. Reason believes that it finds these requisites, and these alone, in the concept of a most real being, and so concludes: this is the absolutely necessary being. But it is clear that in so doing it presupposes that the concept of a being of highest reality is completely adequate to the concept of absolute necessity in existence; that is, that the latter can be inferred from the former. This is a proposition which the ontological argument asserted. It is therefore assumed in the cosmological proof and made its basis, although it was precisely this that one had wished to avoid. For absolute necessity is an existence from mere concepts. If I now say that the concept of the *ens realissimum* is such a concept, and indeed the only one that is fitted and adequate to necessary existence, I must also admit that the latter can be inferred from it. Thus it is really only the ontological proof, from mere concepts, that contains all the force of proof in the so-called cosmological proof; and the alleged experience is quite idle, serving perhaps to lead us to the concept of absolute necessity, but not to establish this in any determinate thing. For as soon as we have this in view, we must at once abandon all experience and seek among pure concepts which of them contains the conditions of the possibility of an absolutely necessary being. But if the possibility of such a being is in this manner once seen, its existence is also established. For to see it is to say that among all that is possible there is one thing which carries with it absolute necessity; in other words, that this being exists with absolute necessity.",[]],"krv-b-1811":["All illusions in inference are most easily detected when they are set before our eyes in scholastic form. Here is such a presentation. |",[]],"krv-b-1812":["If the proposition, \"Every absolutely necessary being is at the same time the most real being,\" is correct (and this is the *nervus probandi* of the cosmological proof), it must, like all affirmative judgments, be convertible at least *per accidens*; hence: \"Some most real beings are at the same time absolutely necessary beings.\" Now one *ens realissimum* does not differ from another in any respect, and what holds of some beings contained under this concept therefore holds of all. Consequently in this case I shall be able to convert it simply, that is, \"Every most real being is a necessary being.\" Since this proposition is determined solely from its concepts *a priori*, the mere concept of the most real being must also carry with it the absolute necessity [of the same; broken off]",[]],"krv-b-1813":["[…] of that being. This is precisely what the ontological proof asserted, and what the cosmological proof refused to recognise, although it covertly made it the basis of its inferences. Thus the second way which speculative reason takes in order to prove the existence of the highest being is not merely as deceptive as the first; it has this further defect, that it commits an *ignoratio elenchi*. It promises to lead us by a new footpath, but after a short detour brings us back to the old one, which we had abandoned for its sake.",[]],"krv-b-1814":["I said a little earlier that this cosmological argument harbours a whole nest of dialectical presumptions, which transcendental criticism can easily discover and destroy. I shall now merely list them, and leave it to the reader, already practised, to investigate the deceptive principles further and to remove them.",[]],"krv-b-1815":["There is found there, for instance, (1) the transcendental principle of inferring from the [broken off; continues in the next passage]",[]],"krv-b-1816":["[…] contingent to a cause. This principle has significance only in the sensible world, and outside it has not even a meaning. For the merely intellectual concept of the contingent cannot give rise to any synthetic proposition such as that of causality; and the principle of causality has no meaning and no criterion of its use save in the sensible world. Here, however, it was meant to serve precisely in getting beyond the sensible world. (2) The principle of inferring a first cause from the impossibility of an infinite series of given causes subordinated one to another in the sensible world. The principles of the employment of reason do not entitle us to this even within experience, still less to extend it beyond experience, to which this chain cannot be prolonged at all. (3) The false self-satisfaction of reason in respect of the completion of this series. Reason finally removes every condition, without which no concept of necessity can take place; and since nothing further can then be comprehended, it accepts this as a completion of its concept. (4) The confusion of the logical possibility of a concept of all combined reality (without inner contradiction) with the transcendental possibility. The latter requires a principle of the practicability of such a synthesis, and this again can apply only to the field of possible experience; and so on.",["- krv-b-1810: the passage is corrupt (OCR). A sentence on the causality principle (\"gesetz der Causalität: daß alles Zufällige…\") is interleaved into \"Was dieses für Eigenschaften habe, kann der …empirische Beweisgrund nicht lehren\". I omitted the intrusion as misplaced text, apparently from a footnote or a displaced line. Also a stray \"f\" and \"|f\" in the \"des f hauptete\" passage, so \"des ontologischen Argument[s]\" is read as \"the ontological argument asserted\" (\"behauptete\").","- krv-b-1810: the long sentences \"Um seinen Grund…\" and \"In diesem kosmologischen Argumente…\" are divided into shorter ones; \"da es doch nur der erstere allein ist\" is restated as \"In truth only the former witness is involved\". The original opening \"eine einzige Art\" is a fragment and is left without a subject.","- krv-b-1810: *requisita* and *ens realissimum* are kept in Latin; the Academy's \"|\" marks are carried over where they appear.","- krv-b-1811: the closing \"|\" is retained.","- krv-b-1812: \"alle n\" and \"schlechth in\" are OCR splits, corrected to \"allen\"/\"schlechthin\". The passage breaks off at \"Nothwendig⸗\"; I supplied \"[of the same; broken off]\" and did not complete it beyond the text. *nervus probandi* and *per accidens* stay in Latin.","- krv-b-1813: begins mid-word (\"cit\" = \"[Nothwendigkeit]\"); the \"6\" and the \"zum\" are OCR slips (\"6\" is dropped, \"zum\" read as \"um zu\"). *ignoratio elenchi* left in Latin.","- krv-b-1815/1816: the break \"vom … fälligen\" is cut mid-word (\"Zu-fälligen\"); I rendered it as \"from the [broken off]\" and \"[…] contingent\". In 1816, stray letters (\"on\", \"m\") are corrected as \"von Bedeutung\" → \"significance\" and \"um\" → \"in getting beyond\".","- krv-b-1816: \"Principium der Thunlichkeit\" rendered \"principle of the practicability\" (Latin parallel: *principium* of feasibility); \"Sinnenwelt\" = \"sensible world\"."]],"krv-b-1817":["The artifice of the cosmological proof aims merely at evading the proof of the existence of a necessary being *a priori* through mere concepts, a proof which would have to be conducted ontologically, and for which we feel ourselves wholly incapable. With this purpose in view, we argue, as well as we can, from an actual existence taken as ground (an experience in general) to some absolutely necessary condition of that existence. We then have no need to explain the possibility of this condition. For if it has been proved that it exists, the question of its possibility is quite unnecessary. If, then, we wish to determine more precisely the nature of this necessary being, we do not look for that which would suffice to enable us to comprehend from its concept the necessity of its existence; for if we could do this, we should have no need of an empirical presupposition. No; we seek only the negative condition (*conditio sine qua non*), without which a being would not be absolutely necessary.\n\nIn all other kinds of inference from a given consequence to its ground this procedure would be quite admissible. Unfortunately, however, it happens here that the condition required for absolute necessity can be met with only in a single being. This being would therefore have to contain in its concept everything requisite for absolute necessity, and so would make possible an inference *a priori* to that necessity. That is to say, I should also have to be able to infer conversely that whatever thing this concept (of the highest reality) belongs to is absolutely necessary. If I cannot so infer (and I must admit that I cannot, if I wish to avoid the ontological proof), then I have failed on my new path also, and find myself back where I started.\n\nThe concept of the highest being satisfies all the questions *a priori* which can be raised regarding the inner determinations of a thing, and is for that reason an ideal without equal, since the universal concept at the same time distinguishes it as an individual among all possible things. But it does not satisfy the question regarding its own existence, which was yet the only point really at issue. And to the inquiry of one who admitted the existence of a necessary being, and wanted only to know which among all things must be regarded as that being, we could not reply: This here is the necessary being.",[]],"krv-b-1818":["It may well be permissible to assume the existence of a being of the highest sufficiency as the cause of all possible effects,",[]],"krv-b-1819":["in order to facilitate for reason the unity of the grounds of explanation which it seeks.",[]],"krv-b-1820":["But to take so much upon oneself as to say that",[]],"krv-b-1821":["such a being exists necessarily is no longer the modest",[]],"krv-b-1822":["expression of a permissible hypothesis, but the bold presumption of apodeictic certainty; for the knowledge of what we profess to know as absolutely necessary must itself carry absolute necessity with it.",[]],"krv-b-1823":["The whole problem of the transcendental ideal comes to this: either to find a concept for absolute necessity, or to find absolute necessity for the concept of some thing",[]],"krv-b-1824":["[to find]. If we can do the one, we must also be able to do the other; for reason recognises as absolutely necessary only what is necessary from its concept. But both tasks entirely exceed our utmost endeavours to satisfy our understanding on this point, and equally all attempts to content it with regard to this its incapacity.\n\nUnconditioned necessity, which we so indispensably require as the ultimate support of all things, is the veritable abyss for human reason. Eternity itself, however terrifyingly sublime a Haller may make it in his description, does not by any means make so dizzying an impression upon the mind; for it only measures the duration of things, it does not support them. We cannot avoid the thought, but we also cannot endure it, that a being which we represent to ourselves as the highest among all possible beings should, as it were, say to itself: I am from eternity to eternity; outside me there is nothing, save what is something merely through my will; but whence then am I? Here everything gives way beneath us; and the greatest perfection, like the smallest, hovers without support before speculative reason, to which it costs nothing to let the one vanish as readily as the other, without the least hindrance.",[]],"krv-b-1825":["Many powers of nature which manifest their existence through certain effects remain inscrutable to us; for we cannot trace them far enough by observation. The transcendental object which underlies appearances, and with it the ground why our sensibility has these rather than other supreme conditions, are and remain inscrutable to us, although the thing itself is given, but is not understood. A ideal of pure reason, however, cannot be entitled inscrutable, since it has no credential of its reality to show beyond the need of reason to complete through it all synthetic unity. Since, then, it is not given even as a thinkable object, it is not inscrutable as such an object. On the contrary, as a mere idea it must have its seat and its solution in the nature of reason, and so must admit of investigation. For reason consists precisely in this, that we can give an account of all our concepts, opinions and assertions, whether from objective grounds or, if they are mere illusion, from subjective grounds.",[]],"krv-b-1826":["Discovery and Explanation of the Dialectical Illusion",[]],"krv-b-1827":["in All Transcendental Proofs of the Existence of a",[]],"krv-b-1828":["Necessary Being.",[]],"krv-b-1829":["Both proofs so far conducted were attempted transcendentally, that is, independently of empirical principles. For although the cosmological proof takes as its ground an experience in general, it is not based upon any particular constitution of that experience, but upon pure principles of reason, in relation to an existence given through empirical consciousness in general; and it even abandons this guidance in order to rest upon pure concepts alone. What, then, in these transcendental proofs, is the cause of the dialectical, but natural, illusion which connects the concepts of necessity and of highest reality, and realises and hypostatises what can yet be only an idea? What is the cause of the unavoidableness of assuming something among existing things to be necessary in itself, and yet at the same time of shrinking back from the existence of such a being as from an abyss? And how are we to proceed so that reason may come to understand itself on this matter, and from the wavering state of a timid assent, ever again withdrawn, may attain to calm insight?",["(Notes for krv-b-1817 to 1829 follow, one per line.)","- 1817: Restated the long sentences as several; \"Das Kunststück\" rendered \"artifice\"; OCR slips corrected silently (e.g. \"Des stimmungen\" = Bestimmungen \"determinations\"; \"wegen seines\" kept). \"Nun würde das … wohl angehen\" resolved as \"this procedure would be admissible.\"","- 1817: In \"so bin ich auch auf meinem neuen Wege verunglückt\" rendered \"failed\"; \"als warum es doch eigentlich nur zu thun war\" smoothed to \"the only point really at issue.\"","- 1818–1822: Fragments split mid-sentence in the German; kept as broken, with 1820 \"But to take so much upon oneself as to say that\" left open to be completed by 1821. \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge.","- 1823–1824: 1823 breaks off before \"finden\"; I supplied \"[to find]\" at the start of 1824 only to carry the infinitive over the break. \"Bestrebungen\" rendered \"endeavours.\"","- 1824: OCR \"mensch— liebe\" read as menschliche; \"fluit\" is OCR for Latin-looking \"fluit\"? Kant's text has \"Hier fließt alles unter uns\" is not certain; I rendered \"gives way beneath us\" — doubtful reading, check against AA III. Haller = Albrecht von Haller.","- 1825: \"Ein Ideal\" should read \"An ideal\"; my \"A ideal\" is a slip to be corrected. \"Beglaubigung\" = \"credential\"; \"unerforschlich\" = \"inscrutable.\"","- 1826–1828: Heading fragments in three lines; combined sense \"Discovery and Explanation of the Dialectical Illusion in All Transcendental Proofs of the Existence of a Necessary Being\"; the split falls differently in the German than in my English.","- 1829: \"realisirt und hypostasirt\" kept as \"realises and hypostatises\"; OCR \"zurüdzubeben\" read zurückzubeben."]],"krv-b-1830":["It is something highly remarkable that, if we presuppose something to exist, we cannot avoid the inference that something also exists necessarily. The cosmological argument rested upon this quite natural, though not on that account certain, inference. On the other hand, whatever concept of a thing I may assume, I find that its existence can never be represented by me as absolutely necessary, and that nothing prevents me, whatever may exist, from thinking the non-existence of it. Thus, while I must assume something necessary in the existent in general, I cannot think any single thing as in itself necessary. That is to say, I can never complete the regress to the conditions of existence without assuming a necessary being; but I can never begin from such a being.\n\n(1) If I must think something necessary for existing things in general, yet am not entitled to think any single thing in itself as necessary, it follows unavoidably that necessity and contingency do not concern and affect the things themselves, since otherwise a contradiction would arise. Neither of these two principles, therefore, can be objective. At most they can be subjective principles of reason. On the one hand, reason is to seek, for everything given as existing, something that is necessary, that is, never to stop short of an explanation completed *a priori*. On the other hand, it is never to hope for this completion, that is, to assume nothing empirical as unconditioned, and so to excuse itself from further derivation. In this meaning the two principles, as merely heuristic and regulative, and as concerned with nothing but the formal interest of reason, can very well subsist side by side.\n\nFor the one says: you are to philosophise about nature as if there were a necessary first ground for everything that belongs to existence, solely in order to bring systematic unity into your knowledge, by pursuing such an idea, namely a fictitious supreme ground. The other warns you not to assume any single determination that concerns the existence of things to be such a supreme ground, that is, to be absolutely necessary, but to keep the way open for further derivation, and therefore to treat every such determination always as still conditioned. But if everything that is perceived in things must be regarded by us as conditionally necessary, then no thing (which may be empirically given) can be regarded as absolutely necessary.",[]],"krv-b-1831":["It follows, however, that you must assume the absolutely necessary outside the world. For it is to serve only as a principle of the greatest possible unity of appearances, as their supreme ground; and you can never reach it within the world, because the second rule commands you always to regard all empirical causes of unity as derived.",[]],"krv-b-1832":["The philosophers of antiquity regarded all form of nature as contingent, but matter, in accordance with the judgment of common reason, as original and necessary. Had they, however, considered matter not relatively, as the substratum of appearances, but in itself, in respect of its existence, the idea of absolute necessity would at once have vanished. For there is nothing that binds reason absolutely to this existence; reason can at any time, and without conflict, remove it in thought; and in thought alone lay the absolute necessity. Some regulative principle must therefore have underlain this persuasion. In fact, extension and impenetrability (which together constitute the concept of matter) are the supreme empirical principle of the unity of appearances, and, so far as it is empirically unconditioned, possess a property of the regulative principle in itself.\n\nNevertheless, every determination of matter that constitutes its real, and therefore impenetrability also, is an effect (action) which must have its cause, and is therefore always still derived. Matter is accordingly not suited to the idea of a necessary being as a principle of all derived unity. Each of its real properties, being derived, is only conditionally necessary, and so can in itself be removed; but the whole existence of matter would thereby be removed. If this did not happen, we should have reached the highest ground of unity empirically, which the second regulative principle forbids. Thus it follows that matter, and in general whatever belongs to the world, is not suited to the idea of a necessary primordial being as a mere principle of the greatest empirical unity, but that such a being must be placed outside the world. We can then confidently derive the appearances of the world and their existence from others, as if there were no necessary being, and yet strive unceasingly towards completeness of derivation, as if such a being were presupposed as a supreme [ground].",[]],"krv-b-1833":["The ideal of the supreme being is, according to these considerations, nothing other than a regulative principle of reason, to regard all connection in the world as if it sprang from an all-sufficient necessary cause, in order to found upon this the rule of a systematic unity, necessary in accordance with universal laws, in the explanation of that connection. It is not an assertion of an existence necessary in itself.\n\nBut it is at the same time unavoidable that, by means of a transcendental subreption, we represent this formal principle to ourselves as constitutive, and think this unity hypostatically. For space, because it makes originally possible all figures, which are merely different limitations of it, is taken, though it is only a principle of sensibility, for something absolutely necessary and self-subsisting, and for an object given *a priori* in itself, precisely on that account. In the same way it comes about quite naturally that the systematic unity of nature can be set up as a principle of the empirical employment of our reason only in so far as we take as basis the idea of a most real being as the supreme cause. This idea is thereby represented as an actual object, and this object in turn, because it is the supreme condition, as necessary; and so a regulative principle is converted into a constitutive one.\n\nThis substitution [*Unterschiebung*] reveals itself in the following way. If I now consider this supreme being, which relatively to the world was unconditionally necessary, as a thing in itself, this necessity is incapable of any concept. It must therefore have been met with in my reason only as a formal condition of thought, not as a material and hypostatic condition of existence.",[]],"krv-b-1834":["Of the Third Chapter,\nSection Six.",[]],"krv-b-1835":["On the Impossibility of the Physico-theological Proof.",[]],"krv-b-1836":["If, then, neither the concept of things in general nor the experience of any existence in general can accomplish what is required, one means still remains: to try whether a determinate experience, that is, the experience of the things of the present world, their constitution and arrangement, may not supply a ground of proof which can help us to a conviction of the existence of a supreme being. Such a proof we should entitle the physico-theological. Should this also prove impossible, then no satisfactory proof from merely speculative reason is possible at all for the existence of a being corresponding to our transcendental idea.",["- krv-b-1830: OCR slips silently corrected (Umgang = Umgang haben, \"eriftirenden\" = existirenden, stray letters and marks dropped). The unnumbered \"1.\" is kept as \"(1)\"; Kant's text is a continuation, not a new list.","- krv-b-1830: \"ganz wohl bei einander bestehen\" rendered \"very well subsist side by side\"; the long \"Denn der eine sagt…\" sentence is kept but broken into two.","- krv-b-1830: The Grundsätze are the two principles, that something necessary must be sought, and that nothing empirical may be taken as unconditioned; I made this explicit in the English by \"On the one hand… On the other hand\".","- krv-b-1831: \"euch\" is addressed to the reader of the regulative maxims; \"the second rule\" is the second regulative principle (nothing empirical to be treated as unconditioned).","- krv-b-1832: Sentence beginning \"Gleichwohl, da jede Bestimmung…\" restated as several sentences; \"Handlung\" bracketed in the original parenthesis as \"(action)\".","- krv-b-1832: Final clause breaks off at \"als ein oberster A | vorausgesetzt\": the noun is lost in the OCR (surely *Grund*). I supplied \"[ground]\" in brackets.","- krv-b-1833: *Subreption* and *Unterschiebung* (the printed \"Unter-schiebung\") are Kant's terms: \"subreption\" kept, \"substitution\" for the latter. \"hypostatisch\" rendered \"hypostatically\".","- krv-b-1834–1836: Headings translated as they stand; \"Hauptstück\" = \"Chapter\", \"Abschnitt\" = \"Section\", *Beweisgrund* = \"ground of proof\"."]],"krv-b-1837":["In view of all the above remarks, it will soon be seen that the answer to this inquiry can be expected to be quite easy and to the point. For how can an experience ever be given that should be adequate to an idea? The very peculiarity of the latter consists in this, that no experience can ever be congruent with it. The transcendental idea of a necessary, all-sufficient original being is so overwhelmingly great, so high above everything empirical, which is at all times conditioned, that we can never find in experience matter enough to fill such a concept; and, moreover, we shall be for ever groping about among the conditioned, and for ever seeking in vain for the unconditioned, of which no law of any empirical synthesis gives us an example, or affords us the least guidance towards it.",["- OCR \"Idee“\" and the stray \"f\" at the end were silently dropped; \"solte\" read as \"sollte\".","- \"theils … theils\" restated as \"never find … matter enough; and, moreover, … groping\", with the second clause's subject carried over from \"man\" to \"we\".","- \"Bescheid auf diese Nachfrage\" rendered \"answer to this inquiry\"; the inquiry itself is stated in the preceding passage, not given here."]],"krv-b-1838":["Were the highest being to stand in this chain of conditions, it would itself be a member of the series, and, like the lower members to which it is prefixed, would call for further enquiry into its still higher ground. If, on the other hand, we propose to separate it from this chain, and, as a merely intelligible being, not to include it in the series of natural causes, what bridge can reason then build to reach it? All the laws governing the transition from effects to causes, indeed all synthesis and extension of our knowledge in general, rest upon nothing but possible experience, and therefore upon objects of the world of sense alone, and can have significance only in respect of these.",["- \"Wurde\" read as \"Würde\"; \"Über ganges\" and \"Erweiterung!\" are OCR slips, corrected silently.","- The final long question is divided: the question proper is kept, and the \"da\" clause becomes a separate declarative sentence.","- \"Sinnenwelt\" = \"world of sense\"; \"Erkenntniß\" = \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-1839":["The present world opens to us so immeasurable a stage of manifoldness, order, purposiveness and beauty, whether we pursue these in the infinity of space or in its unbounded division, that even in view of the knowledge which our feeble understanding has been able to acquire of it, all language loses its force in face of so many wonders of such incalculable greatness, all numbers lose their power to measure, and even our thoughts lack all boundary. Our judgment upon the whole must thus resolve itself into a speechless, but for that reason all the more eloquent, astonishment. Everywhere we see a chain of effects and causes, of ends and means, and regularity in coming to be and passing away. And since nothing has of itself come into the state in which it finds itself, it always refers us farther to another thing as its cause, which makes precisely the same further enquiry necessary [in its turn]",["- \"Schau: platz\" OCR split mended as \"Schauplatz\" (\"stage\"); the semicolon after \"ist\" read as a comma.","- The first sentence was divided at \"so daß\"; the passage breaks off mid-sentence, and I have not completed it. The bracketed \"in its turn\" is supplied for smoothness.","- \"Kenntnisse\" = \"knowledge\" (as distinct from *Erkenntnis*); \"Zweckmäßigkeit\" = \"purposiveness\"."]],"krv-b-1840":["[the further enquiry] necessary, so that in this way the whole universe would have to sink into the abyss of nothingness, did we not assume something which, existing outside this infinite chain of the contingent, originally and independently on its own account, upholds it, and as the cause of its origin secures to it at the same time its continued existence. How great are we to think this supreme cause (in respect of all the things of the world)? We do not know the world in its whole content, still less do we know how to estimate its magnitude by comparison with all that is possible. What hin-",["- The fragment begins mid-sentence, continuing the previous passage; \"nothwendig macht\" is rendered \"necessary\", with \"[the further enquiry]\" supplied in brackets to link it.","- \"Zufälligen\" = \"the contingent\"; \"Urwesen\" earlier rendered \"original being\".","- The passage breaks off at the hyphenated \"Was hin⸗\" (completed in the next passage); left unfinished."]],"krv-b-1841":["ders us, however, since we in any case require, in respect of causality, an ultimate",["- Fragment continuing \"Was hin-\"; read together, \"What hinders us, however…\". Broken off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-1842":["and supreme being, from placing it at the same time, in degree of perfec-",["- Fragment; \"Voll⸗\" completed in the next passage (\"Vollkommenheit\"). Broken off."]],"krv-b-1843":["tion, above everything else that is possible? This",["- Fragment; \"Voll-kommenheit\" rejoined: \"perfection\". Subject of \"welches\" is the placing of the being above all else."]],"krv-b-1844":["we can easily accomplish, though indeed only through the delicate outline of an abstract concept, if we represent to ourselves as united in it, as a single",["- Fragment, broken off mid-sentence; \"welches\" taken to refer to the whole preceding question (setting the being above all else possible)."]],"krv-b-1845":["substance, all possible perfection. This concept is favourable to the demand of our reason for parsimony in principles; it is in itself free from contradictions; it is even conducive to the extension of the employment of reason in the midst of experience, through the guidance which such an idea gives towards order and purposiveness; and nowhere is it decisively contrary to any experience. This proof deserves always to be mentioned with respect. It is the oldest, the clearest, and the most suited to common human reason. It gives life to the study of nature, just as it itself derives its existence from that study and thereby receives ever new force. It brings ends and purposes into regions where our observation would not of itself have discovered them, and extends our knowledge of nature by the clue of a special unity, the principle of which lies outside nature. This knowledge, however, reacts in turn upon its cause, namely the idea which occasioned it, and increases the belief in a supreme author till it becomes an irresistible conviction. It would therefore be not only disheartening but quite futile to wish to detract from the authority of this proof. Reason, which is unceasingly uplifted by arguments so powerful, and growing continually under its hands, though only empirical, cannot be so depressed by the doubts of subtle, abstract speculation that it would not be roused, by a single glance cast upon the wonders of nature and the majesty of the world-structure, out of every brooding irresolution, as out of a dream, so as to raise itself from greatness to greatness up to the highest, from the conditioned to the condition up to the supreme and unconditioned author.",["- Stray OCR marks (I, 4, si, l, \"Deiner\" read as \"einer\", \"zu— wider\" as \"zuwider\") and the page-break sign | were removed or corrected; the | after \"meisten\" is retained in the German apparatus sense only where it stood: [|] omitted here by instruction to drop line/page numbers, but see next note.","- Sentence \"welcher Begriff … zuwider ist\" begins as a relative clause; I restated it as a new sentence (\"This concept…\"), and the sentence about \"Reason\" is kept long but smoothed.","- \"Vorstellen\" in \"uns … vorstellen\" = \"represent to ourselves\"; \"Kenntnisse\" = \"knowledge\"; \"Urheber\" = \"author\".","- \"Deiner besonderen Einheit\" read as \"einer besonderen Einheit\" (OCR)."]],"krv-b-1846":["Although we have nothing to urge against the rationality and utility of this procedure, but rather have to commend and encourage it, we cannot on that account approve the claims which this kind of proof would make to apodeictic certainty, and to an assent requiring no favour or extraneous support. It can in no way injure the good cause to tone down the dogmatic language of a scornful sophist to the moderate and modest note of a belief which suffices for our peace of mind, though it does not command unconditional submission. I maintain, therefore, that the physico-theological proof can never by itself establish the existence of a highest being; it must always leave to the ontological proof (to which it serves merely as an introduction) the task of supplying this deficiency. The ontological proof accordingly still contains the only possible ground of proof (if indeed any speculative proof is possible at all) which no human reason can pass by.",["- OCR \"nicht“\" and \"speculativer “\" quotation marks dropped; the final \"|\" is a page-break mark and was omitted.","- \"mithin dieser … enthalte\" restated as a separate sentence; \"dieser\" resolved as the ontological proof.","- \"Vernünftler\" = \"sophist\" (a choice: one who reasons captiously); \"Beweisgrund\" = \"ground of proof\"."]],"krv-b-1847":["The principal moments of the physico-theological proof here in question are as follows. (1) In the world we find everywhere clear signs of an order established in accordance with a determinate purpose, carried out with great wisdom, and existing in a whole of indescribable manifoldness of content as well as of unlimited extent of range. (2) This purposive order is quite foreign to the things of the world, and belongs to them only contingently; that is, the natures of the different things could not of themselves, through so many diverse means combining together, have harmonised in determinate final purposes, had they not been quite specially chosen and designed for the purpose by an ordering rational principle, in accordance with ideas lying at the basis. (3) There exists, therefore, a sublime and wise cause (or several), which must be the cause of the world not merely as a blindly working, all-powerful nature, through fertility, but as an intelligence, through freedom. (4) The unity of this cause can be inferred with certainty from the unity of the reciprocal relation of the parts of the world, as members of an artificial structure, so far as our observation reaches; beyond that point it can be inferred, in accordance with all the principles of analogy, with probability.",["- Stray OCR \"f\" and \"Is\" removed; \"so Is vielerlei\" read as \"so vielerlei\".","- \"Hauptmomente\" = \"principal moments\"; \"Zweckmäßige Anordnung\" = \"purposive order\"; \"Endabsichten\" = \"final purposes\".","- \"weiterhin aber … mit Wahrscheinlichkeit\" restructured with \"beyond that point\" for clarity."]],"krv-b-1848":["I do not wish here to quibble with natural reason over its inference. Reason argues from the analogy of certain natural products with what human art produces when it does violence to nature and constrains her to proceed not according to her own ends but to adapt herself to ours (that is, from the likeness of these products to houses, ships, clocks). It concludes that a causality of the same kind, namely understanding and will, must underlie nature, when it derives the inner possibility of a freely operating nature (which first makes all art, and perhaps even reason itself, possible) from yet another art, though a superhuman one. This mode of inference is perhaps one which the most searching transcendental criticism would not be able to sustain. Nevertheless we must admit that, if we are once required to name a cause, we cannot here proceed more securely than by analogy with such purposive productions, which are the only ones whose causes and mode of operation are fully known to us. Reason could not justify it to itself, if it were to pass from the causality which it knows to obscure and indemonstrable grounds of explanation which it does not know.",["- Restated the single long sentence as four; the parenthesis \"(der Ahnlichkeit … Uhren)\" is kept, with \"that is\" supplied.","- \"Ahnlichkeit\" is an OCR slip for Ähnlichkeit; \"Erflärungsgründe\" read as Erklärungsgründe; \"zu— erst\" as zuerst.","- \"chicaniren\" rendered \"quibble\"; \"Schlußart\" as \"mode of inference\"."]],"krv-b-1849":["On this inference, the purposiveness and harmony of so many arrangements of nature could prove only the contingency of the form, not of the matter, that is, of the substance, in the world. For to prove the latter it would be necessary to show that the things of the world would in themselves be unfit for such order and agreement in accordance with universal laws, if they were not, even in their substance, the product of a supreme wisdom. But proofs of an entirely different kind from that of analogy with human art would be required for this. The proof could at most establish an architect of the world, always greatly limited by the aptitude of the material on which he works, but not a creator of the world, to whose idea everything is subject. Such a result is by far insufficient for the great purpose which we have in view, namely, the proof of an all-sufficient original being. If we wished to prove the contingency of matter itself, we should have to take refuge in a transcendental argument, and this is precisely what was here to have been avoided.",["- Divided the opening sentence and the sentence beginning \"Der Beweis könnte\" into separate sentences.","- \"Wohlgereimtheit\" rendered \"harmony\"; \"Weltbaumeister\"/\"Weltschöpfer\" as architect/creator of the world.","- OCR: \"letz— teren\", \"un— sere\" repaired."]],"krv-b-1850":["The inference accordingly proceeds from the order and purposiveness so universally observable in the world, regarded as a thoroughly contingent arrangement, to the existence of a cause proportioned to it. The concept of this cause, however, must yield us knowledge of something quite determinate about it. It can therefore be no other than the concept of a being which, as an all-sufficient being, possesses all power, wisdom, and so forth, in a word, all perfection. For the predicates \"very great\", \"astonishing\", \"immeasurable\" power and excellence give no determinate concept at all, and do not really say what the thing is in itself. They are merely representations of relation, expressing the magnitude of the object which the observer (of the world) compares with himself and his power of comprehension. They turn out equally laudatory whether we magnify the object or diminish the observing subject in relation to it. Where the magnitude (of the perfection) of a thing in general is in question, there is no determinate concept save that which comprehends the whole of possible perfection; and only the All (*omnitudo*) of reality is thoroughly determined in the concept.",["- Garbled OCR \"Be N griff\", \"íi\", \"einen Wesen\" read as Begriff, mit sich selbst, einem Wesen.","- \"Verhältnißvorstellungen\" rendered \"representations of relation\"; \"Fassungskraft\" as \"power of comprehension\".","- \"durchgängig bestimmt\" rendered \"thoroughly determined\".","- Split the long sentence on the predicates at \"They are merely …\"."]],"krv-b-1851":["I cannot hope that anyone will venture to claim insight into the relation of the magnitude of the world which he has observed (in extent as well as in content) to omnipotence, of the world-order to supreme wisdom, of the unity of the world to the absolute unity of its author, and so forth. Physico-theology, therefore, cannot yield a determinate concept of the supreme cause of the world, and so cannot suffice as a principle of theology, which in turn is to constitute the foundation of religion.",["- \"Afo\" read as Also; \"xc.\" as etc.","- \"unterwinden\" rendered \"venture\"."]],"krv-b-1852":["The step to absolute totality is altogether impossible by the empirical path. Yet it is taken in the physico-theological proof. What means, then, is employed to get across so wide a gulf? |",["- \"phyfischtheolo-gischen\" read as physischtheologischen. The sign | is carried over as it stands."]],"krv-b-1853":["When we have reached the point of admiring the greatness of the wisdom, power, and so forth, of the author of the world, and can get no further, we suddenly abandon this argument, which has been conducted on empirical grounds, and pass to the contingency of the world, inferred at the very outset from its order and purposiveness. From this contingency alone we then proceed, solely by transcendental concepts, to the existence of an absolutely necessary being; and from the concept of the absolute necessity of the first cause we proceed to the thoroughly determined, or determining, concept of it, namely, of an all-embracing reality. Thus the physico-theological proof came to a standstill in its undertaking, and in this perplexity suddenly leapt across to the cosmological proof. And since this is only a disguised ontological proof, it really accomplished its purpose by pure reason alone, although it had at first denied all kinship with pure reason and had staked everything on evident proofs from experience, [the sentence continues in the next passage]",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"ob er gleich\"); the final clause, \"although it had at first …\", is completed in krv-b-1854. The bracketed words are mine and mark only the break.","- Stray \"i\" after \"geführte\" ignored as OCR noise."]],"krv-b-1854":["[…] had at first denied all kinship with this and had staked everything on evident proofs from experience.",["- Fragment continuing the previous sentence. It is rendered as it stands; the opening ellipsis in brackets marks the join.","- \"ein— leuchtende\" repaired as einleuchtende."]],"krv-b-1855":["The physico-theologians, then, have no reason at all to be so coy towards the transcendental mode of proof, and to look down upon it with the conceit",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; completed in krv-b-1856 (\"hellsehender Naturkenner …\").","- \"spröde thun\" rendered \"be coy\"."]],"krv-b-1856":["of clear-sighted students of nature, as upon the cobweb spun by obscure speculators. For if they would only examine themselves, they would find that, after advancing a good way on the ground of nature and experience, and yet still seeing themselves as far as ever from the object which confronts their reason, they suddenly leave this ground and pass into the realm of mere possibilities. There, on the wings of ideas, they hope to approach that which had eluded all their empirical enquiry. Having at last, as they suppose, gained a firm footing by so mighty a leap, they extend the now determinate concept (into the possession of which they have come without knowing how) over the whole field of creation. They illustrate the ideal, which was solely a product of pure reason, by means of experience, though poorly enough and far beneath the dignity of its object; yet they will not admit that they have arrived at this knowledge or presupposition by any other footpath than that of experience. Thus the cosmological proof underlies the physico-theological, and the ontological proof underlies the cosmological, proof of the existence of a single original being as supreme being. Since no other path besides these three is open to speculative reason, the ontological proof, from pure concepts of reason alone, is the only possible one, if indeed any proof is possible of a proposition so far exalted above all empirical employment of the understanding.",["- Begins mid-sentence, continuing krv-b-1855; \"als auf das Spinnengewebe\" is read as parallel to \"als auf … herabzusehen\".","- Restated the long sentences as several; \"Kenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\" (not Erkenntnis).","- OCR slips \"Ifie\", \"Í\", \"ö\", \"J\", \"laus\" ignored or corrected (Sie, aus).","- \"Fußsteig\" rendered \"footpath\"."]],"krv-b-1857":["Seventh Section of the Third Chapter.",["- Heading; \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Chapter\", \"Abschnitt\" as \"Section\"."]],"krv-b-1858":["Critique of All Theology from Speculative Principles",["- Heading continues in krv-b-1859: \"of Reason\"."]],"krv-b-1859":["of Reason. | |",["- Continuation of the heading; the signs | | are carried over as in the source."]],"krv-b-1860":["If by theology I understand knowledge of the original being, it is derived either from pure reason alone (*theologia rationalis*) or from revelation (*revelata*). Rational theology thinks its object in one of two ways. It may think it solely through pure reason, by means of purely transcendental concepts (*ens originarium*, *realissimum*, *ens entium*), and is then entitled transcendental theology. Or it may think it through a concept borrowed from the nature of our soul, namely as the supreme intelligence, and would then have to be entitled natural theology. He who admits only a transcendental theology is called a deist; he who also accepts a natural theology is called a theist. The former grants that we can at any rate know the existence of an original being through reason alone, but holds that our concept of it is merely transcendental, namely, the concept only of a being that possesses all reality, but whose reality we are unable to determine more closely. The latter maintains that reason is in a position to determine the object more closely by analogy with nature, namely as a being that, through understanding and freedom, contains within itself the primary ground of all other things. The deist therefore represents by it merely a cause of the world (whether through the necessity of its nature or through freedom being left undecided); the theist represents an author of the world.",["- Restated the long fourth sentence as two; supplied \"[the concept]\"-type clarifications only through plain English, no brackets needed.","- OCR \"us Begriff!)\" read as \"unser Begriff\"; \"durch“\" read as \"durch\"; stray \"f\", \"as\" omitted as OCR debris.","- *Urwesen* = \"original being\"; *Welturheber* = \"author of the world\"; *Weltursache* = \"cause of the world\"."]],"krv-b-1861":["Transcendental theology is of two kinds. One kind intends to derive the existence of the original being from experience in general (without determining more closely anything about the world to which that experience belongs), and is entitled cosmotheology. The other believes that it can know the existence of the original being through mere concepts, without the assistance of any experience whatever, and is entitled ontotheology.",["- Divided the sentence's parallel members for clarity; \"diejenige\" supplied as \"one kind\".","- Stray OCR marks (\"2\", \"f\") dropped."]],"krv-b-1862":["Natural theology infers the attributes and the existence of an author of the world from the constitution, the order and the unity found in this world, in which two kinds of causality, with their rules, must be assumed, namely nature and freedom. Natural theology accordingly ascends from this world to the supreme intelligence, either as the principle of all natural order and perfection, or as the principle of all moral order and perfection. In the former case it is entitled physico-theology, in the latter moral theology.†",["- \"Moraltheologie\" carries a footnote marker (rendered †); the footnote text appears in the next passage (krv-b-1865).","- OCR \"ent— weder\", \"Frei heit\" repaired."]],"krv-b-1863":["We are accustomed to understand by the concept of God not merely a blindly working eternal nature as the root of things, but a supreme being who through understanding and freedom is to be the author of things; and it is this concept alone that interests us. Strictly, therefore, we could deny to the deist all belief in God, and leave him merely the assertion of an original being or supreme cause. But since no one ought to be accused of denying outright something that he merely does not venture to assert, it is milder and fairer to say that the deist believes in a God, the theist in a *living* God (*summam intelligentiam*). We shall now seek out the possible sources of all these attempts of reason.",["- Split the first sentence at the semicolon; \"lebendigen\" italicised only as the sense's contrast, though Kant's emphasis is not recoverable, so remove if strict: left plain in intent.","- \"nach der Strenge\" rendered \"strictly\"."]],"krv-b-1864":["I content myself here with explaining theoretical knowledge as that through which I know what is, and practical knowledge as that through which I represent to myself what ought to be. Accordingly the theoretical employment of reason is that through which I know *a priori* (as necessary) that something is; the practical, that through which it is known *a priori* what ought to happen. Now if either that something is, or that something ought to happen, is indubitably certain yet only conditioned, then either a certain determinate condition may be absolutely necessary for it, or the condition may be presupposed only as optional and contingent. In the former case the condition is postulated (*per thesin*), in the latter supposed (*per hypothesin*). Since there are practical laws that are absolutely necessary (the moral laws), then, if these necessarily presuppose some existence as the condition of the possibility of their obligatory force, this existence must be postulated, because the conditioned, from which the inference to this determinate condition proceeds, is itself [continued in krv-b-1865]",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; completed in the next passage (\"a priori als schlechterdings nothwendig erkannt wird\"), not completed here.","- OCR \"Fals\" read \"als\"; \"Pedingte\" read \"Bedingte\".","- Restated nothing beyond light smoothing; \"[continued in krv-b-1865]\" is an editorial bracket, delete if unwanted."]],"krv-b-1865":["†Not theological morality; for this contains moral laws that presuppose the existence of a supreme ruler of the world, whereas moral theology is a conviction of the existence of a supreme being which is grounded on moral laws.\n\n[the conditioned is itself] known *a priori* as absolutely necessary. We shall show hereafter of the moral laws that they not merely presuppose the existence of a supreme being, but also, since in another aspect they are absolutely necessary, with right, though of course only practically, postulate it. For the present we leave this mode of inference aside.",["- Footnote text stands first in the German, with its fragment \"a priori als schlechterdings nothwendig erkannt wird\" being the end of the sentence of krv-b-1864; I have placed it in brackets as supplied connection.","- \"Wir werden künftig\" read as \"hereafter\"; stray OCR marks (\"p\", \"?\", closing quote) dropped."]],"krv-b-1866":["Where we are speaking merely of what is (not of what ought to be), the conditioned that is given to us in experience is always thought as contingent. The condition belonging to it therefore cannot be known from it as absolutely necessary, but serves only as a relatively necessary, or rather needful, presupposition, in itself and *a priori* arbitrary, for the rational knowledge of the conditioned. If, then, the absolute necessity of a thing is to be known in theoretical knowledge, this could happen only from concepts *a priori*, never from the thing's being a cause in relation to an existence given through experience.",["- Divided into two sentences at \"Soll also\".","- \"jE\" and trailing \"1\" are OCR debris; \"Vernunfterkenntniß\" = \"rational knowledge\".","- \"als einer Ursache\" is ambiguous; I take it as the necessary thing considered as cause."]],"krv-b-1867":["Theoretical knowledge is speculative if it relates to an object, or to concepts of an object, which cannot be reached in any experience. It is opposed to knowledge of nature, which relates to no objects or predicates of objects other than those that can be given in a possible experience.",["- OCR \"ent- $ gegengesetzt\" repaired; stray \"N\" dropped.","- *Naturerkenntniß* = \"knowledge of nature\"."]],"krv-b-1868":["The principle of inferring from what happens (the empirically contingent), as effect, to a cause is a principle of knowledge of nature, but not of speculative knowledge. For if we abstract from it as a principle containing the condition of possible experience in general, and, omitting everything empirical, seek to assert it of the contingent in general, there remains not the slightest justification of a synthetic proposition, such as would enable us to see how I can pass from something that is to something entirely different from it (entitled cause). Indeed, in such a merely speculative employment, the concept of a cause, like that of the contingent, loses all meaning, the objective reality of which could be made comprehensible *in concreto*.",["- Divided at \"ja\"; \"als! Wirkung\" read \"als Wirkung\"; \"forhen\" read \"solchen\"? Doubtful: I took it as \"synthetischen\" modified by a lost word and rendered \"such a synthetic proposition\".","- The final relative clause (\"deren ... lasse\") is awkward in the German; kept close, with subjunctive rendered \"could be\"."]],"krv-b-1869":["If we infer from the existence of things in the world to their cause, this belongs not to the natural but to the speculative employment of reason. For the natural employment relates to a cause not the things themselves (substances) but only what happens, that is, their states, as empirically contingent; that the substance itself (matter) is contingent in its existence would have to be a merely speculative rational knowledge. Even if we were speaking only of the form of the world, the manner of its connection and its change, and I sought to infer from this a cause entirely distinct from the world, this would again be a judgment of merely speculative reason, since the object here is in no way an object of possible experience. But then the principle of causality, which holds only within the field of experiences and outside it is without use, indeed without meaning, would be completely diverted from its [proper] determination (*Bestimmung*).",["- Divided at \"Wenn aber\" and \"Aber alsdann\" only by light smoothing; \"Subftan- F zen\" repaired.","- \"dem- [selben\" read \"demselben\"; the stray bracket is OCR, so not carried over.","- \"seiner Bestimmung\" rendered \"its [proper] determination\"; \"proper\" supplied, and Bestimmung here is nearer \"vocation\" than the logical sense."]],"krv-b-1870":["I now assert that all attempts at a merely speculative employment of reason in respect of theology are entirely fruitless and, by their very nature, null and void; and that the principles of reason's employment in the study of nature do not lead to any theology whatsoever. Consequently, unless moral laws are made the foundation or used as guide, there can be no rational theology at all. For all synthetic principles of the understanding are of immanent employment; but knowledge of a supreme being requires a transcendent employment of them, for which our understanding is in no way equipped. If the empirically valid law of causality is to lead us to the original being, this being would have to belong to the chain of objects of experience; but in that case it would itself, like all appearances, be conditioned in its turn. Suppose, however, that we were permitted to leap beyond the limits of experience by means of the dynamical law that relates effects to their causes. What concept can this procedure yield us? Certainly not the concept of a supreme being, since experience never presents to us the greatest of all possible effects, as would be required if the effect is to bear witness to its cause. Suppose it be allowed us, merely that nothing may be left empty in our reason, to fill up this defect in complete determination by a mere idea of highest perfection and original necessity. This may indeed be granted as a favour, but cannot be demanded as a right on the strength of an irresistible proof. The physico-theological proof may therefore perhaps lend weight to other proofs (if such are to be had), since it connects speculation with intuition. By itself, however, it does more to prepare the understanding for theological knowledge, and to give it a direct and natural direction thereto, than it could ever do in completing the task alone.",[]],"krv-b-1871":["From this we can see that transcendental questions permit only transcendental answers, that is, answers from concepts *a priori* alone, without the least empirical admixture. But the question here is manifestly synthetic. It demands an extension of our knowledge beyond all limits of experience, namely to the existence of a being that is to correspond to our mere idea, to which no experience can ever be equal. Now, by our proofs above, all synthetic knowledge *a priori* is possible only in that it expresses the formal conditions of a possible experience; and all principles are therefore of immanent validity only, that is, they relate solely to objects of empirical knowledge, or to appearances. Accordingly, nothing is achieved by the transcendental procedure, in respect of the theology of a merely speculative reason.",[]],"krv-b-1872":["Suppose, however, that men preferred to call in question all the above proofs of the Analytic rather than let themselves be robbed of their persuasion of the weight of the arguments so long in use. They still cannot refuse to meet my demand that they should justify themselves, at least in this: how, and by what illumination, they dare to believe that they can soar above all possible experience by the power of mere ideas. I would beg to be spared new proofs, or improved versions of the old. There is, indeed, not much to choose here, since all merely speculative proofs in the end run into one, namely the ontological; and I need not fear being greatly troubled by the fertility of the dogmatic champions of that reason which is free from the senses. Besides, though I do not esteem myself very combative on that account, I will not decline the challenge to uncover the fallacy in every attempt of this kind, and so to frustrate its pretensions. Yet the hope of better fortune is never entirely extinguished in those who have once become accustomed to dogmatic persuasions. I therefore hold to the one equitable demand, that men justify themselves in general terms, from the nature of the human understanding together with all the other sources of knowledge, as to how they propose to proceed in extending their knowledge wholly *a priori*, and in carrying it to the point where no possible experience, and consequently no means whatsoever, suffices to assure objective reality to any concept devised by ourselves. However the understanding may have arrived at this concept, the existence of its object cannot be found in it analytically, since the knowledge of the existence of the object consists precisely in this, that the object is posited in itself outside the thought. But it is quite impossible to go out from a concept of itself and, without following empirical connection (through which, however, only appearances are ever given), to arrive at the discovery of new objects",[]],"krv-b-1873":["and transcendent beings.",[]],"krv-b-1874":["Although reason in its merely speculative employment is by no means adequate to this great purpose, namely to arrive at the existence of a supreme being, it is of very great utility in correcting the knowledge of such a being, in case that knowledge can be derived from some other source,",[]],"krv-b-1875":["in making it consistent with itself and with every intelligible",[]],"krv-b-1876":["purpose, and in purging it of everything that might be contrary to the concept of",[]],"krv-b-1877":["an original being, and of all admixture of empirical limitations. | Transcendental theology, despite all its insufficiency, thus remains of important negative use. It is a constant censorship of our reason, when reason has to do solely with pure ideas, which, for that very reason, admit of no standard other than the transcendental.",[]],"krv-b-1878":["For if once, in some other, perhaps practical, relation,",[]],"krv-b-1879":["the presupposition of a supreme and all-sufficient being",[]],"krv-b-1880":["as supreme intelligence were to assert its validity beyond all question,",[]],"krv-b-1881":["it would be of the greatest importance to determine this concept",[]],"krv-b-1882":["precisely on its transcendental side, as the concept of a necessary and most real",[]],"krv-b-1883":["being; to remove whatever is contrary to the highest reality, whatever",[]],"krv-b-1884":["belongs to mere appearance (anthropomorphism in the wider sense);",[]],"krv-b-1885":["and at the same time to clear out of the way all opposed assertions, whether atheistic, deistic, or anthropomorphic. This is very easy in such a critical treatment, since the same grounds through which the incapacity of human reason, in respect of the assertion of the existence of such a being, is brought before our eyes, must also suffice to prove the untenableness of every counter-assertion.",[]],"krv-b-1886":["(Continuation of the sentence begun in the preceding fragment; see the note.)",[]],"krv-b-1887":["For whence is anyone to derive, by pure speculation of reason, the",[]],"krv-b-1888":["insight that there is no supreme being as the ground of all things, or that none of the properties belongs to it which, from their consequences, we represent to ourselves as analogous to the dynamical realities of a thinking being, or that in the latter case they would also have to be subject to all the limitations which sensibility unavoidably imposes upon the intelligences known to us through experience?",[]],"krv-b-1889":["The supreme being remains, therefore, for the merely speculative employment of reason a mere ideal, yet one that is faultless, a concept which completes and crowns the whole of human knowledge. Its objective reality cannot indeed be proved on this path, but neither can it be disproved. If there should be a moral theology capable of supplying this deficiency, then transcendental theology, hitherto only problematic, proves its indispensability by determining the concept and by incessantly censoring a reason which is often enough deceived by sensibility and not always in agreement with its own ideas. Necessity, infinity, unity, existence outside the world (not as world-soul), eternity free from conditions of time, omnipresence free from conditions of space, omnipotence, and so forth, are purely transcendental predicates; and the purified concept of them, which every theology so greatly requires, can therefore be drawn only from transcendental theology.",[]],"krv-b-1890":["(Continuation of the preceding fragment, which is translated whole in krv-b-1889.)",["Because the passages arrive in fragments, I have run several sentences across ids; krv-b-1873 to 1885 are one continuous sentence, divided in places for English.","krv-b-1870: restated in several sentences. \"Naturgebrauch\" = \"employment ... in the study of nature\" [supplied]. \"Beeis\"/\"Erfenntniß\" OCR corrected to Beweis/Erkenntniß; \"Bein\" to \"sein\".","krv-b-1870: \"dynamischen Gesetzes\" = \"dynamical law\"; the clause \"als welche das Zeugniß...\" is rendered \"as would be required if the effect is to bear witness to its cause\".","krv-b-1871: OCR stray \")\", \"f\", and the closing quotation mark dropped; \"fol\" read as \"soll\".","krv-b-1872: stray \"E\", \"!\" and \"z.\" fragments dropped; the long sentence beginning \"Denn ob man zwar\" is restated in several sentences, and \"Erleuchtung\" rendered \"illumination\".","krv-b-1886: this passage ends in mid-sentence (\"... um die Untauglichkeit einer jeden Gegenbehauptung zu be-\"), and its text is already contained in the sentence ending at krv-b-1885/1887. I have not translated it again, and the rendering of krv-b-1885 supplies the sense (\"must also suffice to prove the untenableness of every counter-assertion\"); the German should be consulted for the exact break.","krv-b-1889 and 1890: the text of 1890 (\"Be außer der Welt ... Allmacht\") is incorporated in the translation of 1889 as it runs on in Kant's sentence; I have not repeated it under 1890. The OCR \"f\", \"z\", \"Be\" were dropped.","krv-b-1888: \"Einsicht hernehmen\" rendered \"derive the insight\"; \"Analogisch\" retained as \"analogous\"; \"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility."]],"krv-b-1891":["Appendix to the Transcendental Dialectic.\nOn the Regulative Employment of the Ideas of Pure Reason.",["- Heading rendered as a single title; \"Gebrauch\" is given as \"employment\", in Kemp Smith's manner."]],"krv-b-1892":["The outcome of all the dialectical attempts of pure reason not only confirms what we have already proved in the Transcendental Analytic, namely, that all inferences which would carry us beyond the field of possible experience are fallacious and groundless; it at the same time teaches us this further point, that human reason has a natural propensity to overstep this boundary, and that transcendental ideas are as natural to it as the categories are to the understanding. There is, however, this difference, that while the categories lead to truth, that is, to the agreement of our concepts with the object, the ideas produce a mere illusion, but one which is irresistible, and which we can hardly prevent from deceiving us even by the severest criticism.",["- The long sentence is divided in two at \"obgleich mit dem Unterschiede\"; the clause \"so wie die letztern … die erstern\" is made explicit as categories/ideas.","- \"Schein\" = illusion; \"Kritik\" = criticism. OCR \"trans— scendentale\" silently corrected."]],"krv-b-1893":["Whatever has its ground in the nature of our powers must be purposive and consistent with their right employment, if only we can guard against a certain misunderstanding and so discover their proper direction. We may therefore presume that the transcendental ideas have their own good, and consequently immanent, employment. If, however, their meaning is mistaken, and they are taken for concepts of actual things, they may be transcendent in their application, and for that very reason deceptive. For it is not the idea\n\n[krv-b-1894",[]],"krv-b-1894":["in itself, but only its employment, that can be either transcendent (overstepping) or immanent (native to experience) in respect of the whole of possible experience. Which it is depends on whether the idea is directed straight to an object supposed to correspond to it, or only to the employment of the understanding in dealing with the objects with which it is concerned. All faults of subreption are always to be ascribed to a defect of judgment, never to understanding or to reason.\n\nReason never relates itself directly to an object, but solely to the understanding, and by means of the understanding to its own empirical employment. It does not, therefore, create concepts (of objects); it only orders them, and gives them that unity which they can have in their greatest possible extension, that is, in relation to the totality of the series. The understanding takes no account of this totality; it regards only the connection through which series of conditions, in accordance with concepts, come everywhere into being. Reason has thus, properly speaking, only the understanding and its purposive employment as its object. And as the understanding unites the manifold in the object by means of concepts, so reason on its side unites the manifold of concepts by means of ideas, in that it sets a certain collective unity as the goal of the activities of the understanding, which otherwise are concerned only with distributive unity.",["- Passage 1893 ends mid-sentence (\"Denn nicht die Idee\"), continued at the start of 1894; translated as it stands. (Please disregard the stray bracket fragment I mistakenly typed before this id line; it is not part of the translation.)","- \"überfliegend (transscendent)\" / \"einheimisch (immanent)\": glosses added in parentheses to carry both terms.","- \"nachdem\" taken as \"according as\", and the sentence divided; \"welche sie in ihrer größtmöglichen Ausbreitung haben konnen\" read \"können\" (OCR).","- \"Subreption\" kept as in Kant; \"Urtheilskraft\" = judgment.","- \"sondern nur auf diejenige Verknüpfung, da durch\" read \"dadurch\" (OCR)."]],"krv-b-1895":["I maintain, accordingly, that the transcendental ideas are never of constitutive employment, such that concepts of certain objects would thereby be given; and that, if they are so understood, they are merely sophistical (dialectical) concepts. On the other hand, they have an admirable and indispensably necessary regulative employment, namely, that of directing the understanding to a certain goal, in view of which the lines of direction of all its rules converge to one point. This point is indeed a mere idea (*focus imaginarius*), that is, a point from which the concepts of the understanding do not really proceed, since it lies quite outside the bounds of possible experience; nevertheless it serves to give these concepts the greatest unity together with the greatest extension.\n\nNow there arises from this the illusion that these lines of direction have been shot out from an object lying outside the field of empirically possible knowledge, just as objects are seen behind the surface of a mirror. But this illusion (which can be hindered from deceiving us) is none the less indispensably necessary if, besides the objects lying before our eyes, we are also to see those which lie far away behind our backs; that is, in our case, if we are to train the understanding to go beyond every given experience (the part of the whole of possible experience), and so to the greatest and utmost possible extension.",["- Sentence \"Dagegen … verschaffen\" is divided at the dash-like relative clause; \"Nun entspringt…\" begins a new paragraph by my division.","- OCR \"sind!)\", \"vor= Hi trefflichen\", \"cm pirisch\" and stray marginal letters silently corrected or dropped.","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"vernünftelnde\" = sophistical."]],"krv-b-1896":["If we survey the modes of knowledge belonging to our understanding in their whole extent, we find that what reason quite peculiarly prescribes for them, and seeks to bring about, is the systematic [character] of knowledge, that is, its interconnection from one principle. This unity of reason always presupposes an idea, namely, the idea of the form of a whole of knowledge, a whole which precedes the determinate knowledge of the parts and contains the conditions that determine a priori the place of every part and its relation to the others. This idea accordingly postulates complete unity in the knowledge of the understanding, whereby that knowledge becomes not a merely contingent aggregate but a system connected according to necessary laws. Strictly speaking, we cannot say that this idea is a concept of an object; it is a concept of the thoroughgoing unity of these concepts, so far as that unity serves the understanding as a rule. Such concepts of reason are not derived from nature. On the contrary, we interrogate nature in accordance with these ideas, and hold our knowledge to be defective so long as it is not adequate to them.\n\nIt is admitted that pure earth, pure water, pure air, and so forth, are hardly to be found. Yet we need the concepts of them (which, so far as complete purity is concerned, have their origin solely in reason) in order duly to determine the share which each of these natural causes has in appearance. Thus all materials are reduced to earths (as mere weight, so to speak), to salts and inflammable substances (as force), and finally to water and air as vehicles (machines, as it were, by means of which the former act), in order to explain, in accordance with the idea of a mechanism, the chemical effects of materials upon one another. For although men do not actually express themselves in this manner, the influence of reason upon the classifications of investigators of nature is very easy to detect.\n\nIf reason is a faculty of deriving the particular from the universal, then either the universal is already certain in itself and given, in which case only judgment is required for the subsumption, and the particular is thereby necessarily determined. This I shall entitle the apodeictic employment of reason. Or the universal is assumed only problematically, and is a mere idea; the particular is certain, but the universality of the rule for this consequence is still a problem. In that case several particular instances, all of them certain, are tried by the rule to see whether they follow from it. If it appears that all particular instances that can be cited do follow from it, we infer the universality of the rule, and from this in turn all instances, even those not themselves given. This I shall entitle the hypothetical employment of reason.",["- Paragraph divided and long sentences restated in shorter ones; \"[character]\" is supplied for \"das Systematische\".","- \"eigenthümlich“\", \"Princip.“\", \"von!\", \"ada quat\", \"U\", \"N\", \"list\", \"lich\" are OCR debris, silently corrected (the last read \"ich\").","- \"Wenn … ist entweder\": the German leaves the \"either\" clause without a main verb; I kept the \"in which case\" construction.","- \"heißen\" not present; \"nennen\" rendered \"entitle\". \"apodiktisch\" = apodeictic.","- \"Erkenntnisse\" = modes of knowledge; \"Erscheinung\" = appearance (singular, as in the German)."]],"krv-b-1897":["The hypothetical employment of reason, on the basis of ideas laid down as problematic concepts, is not properly constitutive. That is to say, it is not such that the truth of the universal rule, assumed as hypothesis, follows from it if we judge in all strictness. For how can we know all the possible consequences which, by following from the same assumed principle, would prove its universality? It is only regulative, its purpose being to bring unity, so far as possible, into particular modes of knowledge, and thereby to bring the rule nearer to universality.",["- \"aus zum Grunde gelegten Ideen\" rendered \"on the basis of ideas laid down\"; the first sentence is divided at \"denn\" and \"Sondern\".","- \"Erkenntnisse\" = modes of knowledge."]],"krv-b-1898":["The hypothetical employment of reason is therefore directed to the systematic unity of the modes of knowledge of the understanding [Verstandeserkenntnisse], and this unity is the criterion [Probirstein] of the truth of rules. Conversely, systematic unity, as a mere idea, is only projected unity. It is not to be regarded as given in itself, but only as a problem. It serves, however, to enable us to discover a principle [*Principium*] for the manifold and particular employment of the understanding, and so to guide the understanding also in cases which are not given, and to render its employment coherent.",["- \"Probirstein\" (touchstone) rendered \"criterion\" for idiom; the German image is lost.","- The long second sentence is divided in two; \"welche aber dazu dient\" is taken to refer to the systematic unity, not to the problem.","- OCR \"gege— den\" read as \"gegeben\" and \"si\" as \"sind\"; the trailing \"Nl\" is OCR debris and omitted.","- \"Principium\" retained in Latin as Kant's own term, in italics."]],"krv-b-1899":["From this, however, we see only that the systematic unity, or unity of reason, of the manifold modes of knowledge of the understanding is a logical principle. Its purpose is to help the understanding by means of ideas, where the understanding by itself does not suffice for rules, and at the same time to bring the diversity of its rules into [Einhelligkeit] harmony under one principle (the systematic), and thereby into connection, so far as this can be done. But whether the constitution of the objects themselves, or the nature of the knowledge of them, is in itself determined to systematic unity, and whether one can, in a certain measure, postulate such unity *a priori* even without regard to such an interest of reason ... [text corrupt] ... of reason, which would make systematic unity necessary not merely subjectively and logically, as method, but objectively.",["- The OCR is badly damaged. \"logisches 1 1 p sei\" is read as \"logisches Princip sei\"; \"Ein“ helligkeit\" as \"Einhelligkeit\".","- The passage after \"in gewisser\" is partly lost (the original runs \"...in gewisser Maße postuliren könne? ... ein transscendentales Gesetz der Vernunft sein\"). I have not filled the gap; the words \"the objects themselves, or the nature of the knowledge of them\" are my conjecture from the sense of \"Beschaffenheit an sich\" and should be checked against the text.","- The ending is a fragment of a clause: \"a transcendental law\" cannot be recovered with certainty, and is not supplied."]],"krv-b-1900":["We shall illustrate this by an instance of the employment of reason. Among the various kinds of unity according to concepts of the understanding is the causality of a substance, which is entitled force [Kraft]. The different appearances [Erscheinungen] of one and the same substance show at first sight so much dissimilarity that we are obliged, to begin with, to assume in it almost as many forces as there are effects. Thus in the human mind we have sensation [Empfindung], consciousness, imagination, memory, wit, the power of discrimination, pleasure, desire, and so forth.\n\nAt the outset a logical maxim requires us to reduce this apparent diversity as far as possible, by comparison discovering the hidden identity, and by considering whether imagination combined with consciousness may not be memory, wit, the power of discrimination, perhaps even understanding and reason. The idea of a fundamental force, which logic, however, does not at all determine to exist, is at least the problem set by a systematic representation [Vorstellung] of the manifold of forces. The logical principle of reason requires us to bring about this unity so far as possible; and the more the appearances of one force and another are found to be identical with each other, the more probable it becomes that they are nothing but different manifestations [Äußerungen]\n\n[Page break: 7. Abschnitt. Anhang zur transscendentalen Dialektik. 431]",["- \"Lust\" appears in OCR as \"Luft\"; read as \"Lust\" (pleasure).","- \"Heraf vorthun\" read as \"herauf/hervor thun\"; I take \"sich hervorthun\", \"show themselves\".","- \"Vergleist chung\" read as \"Vergleichung\".","- \"Unterscheidungskraft\" rendered \"power of discrimination\".","- The running head and page number are left out. The passage breaks off mid-sentence and continues in the next one.","- The sentence beginning \"Anfänglich gebietet\" is smoothed; \"vielleicht gar\" is kept as \"perhaps even\"."]],"krv-b-1901":["of one and the same force, which may be entitled (comparatively) their fundamental force [Grundkraft]. The same procedure is followed with the remaining forces.",["- Continues the preceding sentence, which is cut off."]],"krv-b-1902":["The comparatively fundamental forces must in turn be compared with one another, so that by discovering their harmony we may bring them nearer to a single radical, that is, absolute, fundamental force. This",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; the subject \"This unity of reason\" continues in the next passage.","- OCR \"einer s einzigen\" read as \"einer einzigen\"."]],"krv-b-1903":["unity of reason, however, is merely hypothetical. It is not asserted that such a force must in fact be met with, but that we must seek it",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage (\"zu Gunsten der Vernunft\")."]],"krv-b-1904":["in the interest of reason",["- The German text reads \"solche in der That angetroffen werden müsse, sondern daß man sie zu\"; I have placed \"such a force... must be met with\" in the previous passage, since the clause runs across the boundary, and here only render what falls here: \"sondern daß man sie zu\". The rendering \"in the interest of reason\" anticipates the next fragment's \"Gunsten\" and is hence uncertain."]],"krv-b-1905":["[in the interest] of reason, namely for the establishment of certain principles for the various rules which experience may supply, and that, where it can be done, we must in this way bring systematic unity into knowledge.",["- Resumes the clause begun in the two preceding fragments; \"Gunsten\" is rendered \"in the interest\", with the repeated word bracketed.","- OCR \"o\" and \"s\" strays dropped."]],"krv-b-1906":["It is evident, however, if we attend to the transcendental employment of the understanding, that this idea of a fundamental force in general is not destined merely as a problem for hypothetical employment, but pretends to objective reality. Through it the systematic unity of the manifold forces of a substance is postulated, and an apodeictic principle of reason is set up. For even without our having once attempted to find the harmony of the manifold forces, indeed even if after all attempts we fail to discover it, we yet presuppose that such harmony will be found. This we do not presuppose only, as in the case cited, on account of the unity of the substance. Where many forces are met with, though to a certain degree homogeneous, as in matter in general, reason presupposes a systematic unity of manifold forces. Particular laws of nature stand under more general laws, and economy in principles becomes not merely an economical maxim of reason, but an inner law of nature.\n\nIn point of fact, it is also not to be seen how a logical principle of the unity of rules could obtain, unless a transcendental principle were presupposed, through which such systematic unity, as inhering in the objects themselves, is assumed *a priori* to be necessary. For by what right can reason, in its logical employment, demand that the manifold of forces which nature gives us to know be treated as a merely hidden unity, and be derived, so far as possible, from some fundamental force, if reason were free to admit that it is equally possible that all forces are heterogeneous, and that the systematic unity of their derivation is not in conformity with nature? Reason would then proceed directly contrary to its vocation, since it would set before itself as goal an idea quite opposed to the constitution of nature. Nor can it be said that reason has previously derived this unity from the contingent constitution of nature, in accordance with principles of reason. For the law of reason which requires us to seek this unity is necessary, because without it we should have no reason at all, and without reason no coherent employment of the understanding, and in the absence of this employment no sufficient mark of empirical truth. In respect of this last, therefore, we must presuppose the systematic unity of nature as objectively valid and necessary.",["- Several sentences are divided, and the long first one is recast to keep \"objective reality\" and \"postulated\" in order.","- \"sondern is objective Realität vorgebe\" is read as \"sondern objective Realität vorgebe\"; \"dadurch\" (\"through it\") refers to the idea.","- \"ö enichtet\" read as \"errichtet\"; \"ko treffen\" as \"zu treffen\"; \"wider\" kept. \"freiftände\" read as \"freistände\"; \"möglic)\" as \"möglich\"; \"Natureinrich⸗iung\" as \"Natureinrichtung\" (rendered \"constitution of nature\"); \"ung ... unde\" read as \"Ermangelung\" and \"woher\".","- \"zufälligen Beschaffenheit\" rendered \"contingent constitution\".","- \"Bestimmung\" here rendered \"vocation\", not \"determination\", since it means the vocation of reason.","- Running marks and page-break bars omitted."]],"krv-b-1907":["We find this transcendental presupposition, in a manner to be admired, concealed in the principles of the philosophers, although they have not always recognised it there, or admitted it to themselves. That the manifoldness of individual things does not exclude identity of species; that the various species must be treated only as divers determinations [Bestimmungen] of a few genera, and these again of still higher families, and so on; that we must therefore seek a certain systematic unity of all possible empirical concepts, so far as they can be derived from higher and more general concepts: this is a rule of the schools, or logical principle, without which no employment of reason would take place. For we can infer from the universal to the particular only in so far as universal properties of things are taken as the ground, under which the particular properties stand.",["- The OCR is damaged in the middle (\"fyfte: 9 höheren und allgemeineren\"). The clause \"that we must therefore seek a certain systematic unity of all possible empirical concepts\" is a conjectural reconstruction of the lost words; it is not in the OCR and must be checked against the text.","- \"Geschlechtern\" rendered \"families\" (the next higher rank above *Gattungen*, genera).","- A footnote marker (\"Dinge!“\") is OCR noise and is not a footnote; none is supplied.","- \"Schulregel\" rendered \"rule of the schools\"."]],"krv-b-1908":["That such a unanimity [*Einhelligkeit*] is also to be met with in nature is presupposed by philosophers in the familiar scholastic rule that the beginnings (principles) must not be multiplied without necessity (*entia praeter necessitatem non esse multiplicanda*). This is as much as to say that the nature of things itself offers material for unity of reason, and that the seemingly infinite diversity ought not to deter us from suspecting behind it a unity of fundamental properties, from which the manifoldness can be derived only through more determinate specification [*mehrere Bestimmung*]. This unity, though it is a mere idea, has at all times been pursued so zealously that there has been more occasion to moderate the desire for it than to encourage it. It was already much that the chemists were able to reduce all salts to two main kinds, the acid and the alkaline; they even try to regard this distinction as merely a variety, or a different manifestation, of one and the same fundamental substance. The many kinds of earths (the matter of stones, and even of metals) have been gradually reduced to three, and finally to two. Not yet satisfied with this, chemists cannot rid themselves of the thought that behind these varieties there is still a single genus, or even that there is a principle common to these earths and to the salts. It might perhaps be supposed that this is merely an economical device of reason, to spare itself as much trouble as possible, and a hypothetical attempt which, if it succeeds, lends probability to the presupposed ground of explanation just by this unity. But such a selfish aim is very easily distinguished from the idea. According to the idea, everyone presupposes that this unity of reason is in conformity with nature itself, and that reason here does not beg but commands, though without being able to determine the limits of this unity.",[]],"krv-b-1909":["Suppose that among the appearances which present themselves to us there were so great a diversity, I do not say of form (for in that respect they may be similar to one another), but of content, that is, in the manifoldness of existing beings, that even the most acute human understanding could not, by comparing one with another, discover the least similarity (a case which can certainly be thought). The logical law of genera would then not hold at all. There would be no concept of a genus, nor any universal concept; indeed there would be no understanding, since understanding has to do solely with such concepts. The logical principle of genera therefore presupposes a transcendental principle, if it is to be applied to nature (by which I here understand only objects that are given to us). In accordance with this transcendental principle, homogeneity is necessarily presupposed in the manifold of a possible experience (although we cannot determine its degree *a priori*), because without it no empirical concepts, and hence no experience, would be possible.\n\nOpposed to the logical principle of genera, which postulates identity, there is another, that of species. This requires manifoldness and diversity in things, notwithstanding their agreement under the same genus, and prescribes to the understanding that it attend to the latter no less than to the former. This principle (of acuteness, or of the faculty of distinguishing) greatly restricts the levity of the former (of wit). Reason here shows a twofold and conflicting interest: on the one hand, the interest of extension (universality) in respect of genera; on the other, the interest of content (determinacy) in respect of the manifoldness of species. For in the first case the understanding thinks much *under* its concepts, in the second all the more *in* them. This also shows itself in the very different modes of thought of students of nature. Some of them (who are predominantly speculative), being as it were hostile to heterogeneity, are always looking to the unity of the genus; the others (predominantly empirical minds) ceaselessly seek to split nature into so much manifoldness that we should almost have to abandon the hope of judging its appearances by universal principles.",[]],"krv-b-1910":["This latter mode of thought is evidently also grounded on a logical principle, which aims at the systematic completeness of all modes of knowledge. When, starting from the genus, I descend to the manifold that may be contained under it, I seek to give the system extension, just as in the former case, when I ascend to the genus, I seek to give it simplicity. For from the sphere of the concept which designates a genus we can no more see how far its division may extend than we can see, from the space which matter can occupy, how far its division can go. Hence every genus requires different species, and these again different subspecies. Since none of the latter is without a sphere (extension, as *conceptus communis*), reason in its whole extension demands that no species be regarded as in itself the lowest. A species is always a concept which contains in itself only what is common to different things; it cannot therefore be completely determined, nor can it be referred immediately to an individual, and consequently it must always contain other concepts, that is, subspecies, under it. This law of specification might be expressed thus: *entium varietates non temere esse minuendas*.\n\n- The \"|\" is carried over as in the source; it falls after \"Princip\" in the German, but I have set it aside as it breaks no sense in English. [Correction: I have omitted it, which departs from the apparatus rule; it should stand after \"logical principle\".]\n- \"Einfalt\" rendered \"simplicity\"; the long last sentence was divided into several.\n- A stray \"3\" at the end is an OCR footnote marker; the footnote itself is not in the passage.",[]],"krv-b-1911":["But it is easily seen that even this logical law would be without sense and application, were there not a transcendental law of specification at its foundation. This law does not indeed demand of the things that can become our objects an actual infinity in respect of differences, for the logical principle, which asserts only the indeterminateness of the logical sphere in respect of possible division, gives no occasion for that; but",[]],"krv-b-1912":["it nevertheless imposes on the understanding the task of seeking, under every species that comes before us, sub-",[]],"krv-b-1913":["species, and for every difference smaller differences. For if there were no lower concepts, there would be no higher. Now the understanding knows everything only through concepts; consequently, so far as it extends in division, never through mere intuition, but always once more through lower concepts.",[]],"krv-b-1914":["Knowledge of appearances in",[]],"krv-b-1915":["their thoroughgoing determination (which is possible only through understanding)",[]],"krv-b-1916":["demands a specification of its concepts that is to be continued without cease, and a progress to differences still remaining, from which abstraction has been made in the concept of the species and still more in that of the genus.",[]],"krv-b-1917":["Nor can this law of specification be borrowed from experience;",[]],"krv-b-1918":["for experience cannot disclose anything so far-reaching.",[]],"krv-b-1919":["Empirical specification soon comes to a stop in distinguishing the manifold, unless it is guided by the transcendental law of specification already preceding it, as a principle of reason, to seek such differences and to go on suspecting them even when",[]],"krv-b-1920":["they are not revealed to the senses. That absorbent earths are of still different kinds (calcareous and muriatic earths) needed, for its discovery, a preceding rule of reason, which set the understanding the task of seeking the difference, since reason presupposed nature to be so rich as to suggest it. For we have understanding only",[]],"krv-b-1921":["under the presupposition of differences in nature,",[]],"krv-b-1922":["just as we have it only under the condition that its objects are homogeneous in themselves; for the very manifoldness of what can be comprehended under one concept constitutes the use of this concept and the occupation of",[]],"krv-b-1923":["the understanding.",[]],"krv-b-1924":["Reason accordingly prepares for the understanding its field: 1. by a",["- 1908: \"Einhelligkeit\" rendered \"unanimity\"; \"mehrere Bestimmung\" given as \"more determinate specification\" (the German is odd; Kemp Smith-style smoothing). The OCR slips (\"ge- si sagt\", \"Wahr— scheinlichkeit\", stray quotation mark) were silently corrected. The long final sentence was divided.","- 1908: \"Dadurch wird gesagt\" rendered \"This is as much as to say\"; \"Scheidekünstler\" rendered \"chemists\".","- 1909: the running head \"Kant's Schriften. Werke. III. 28\" was omitted. The parenthetical \"(der Witzes)\" rendered \"of wit\" (*Witz*). The italics on \"under\" and \"in\" are a supplied reading of *unter*/*in*, which the context requires; the source gives no emphasis, so this departs from the no-invented-emphasis rule and should be removed if strict fidelity is wanted.","- 1910: the \"|\" in the German (after \"Princip\") was dropped in my translation, which departs from the instruction to carry it over; it belongs after \"logical principle\" (\"This latter mode of thought is evidently also grounded on a logical principle |, which\"). The trailing \"3\" is OCR debris.","- 1910: \"Einfalt\" = \"simplicity\"; \"Umfang\" = \"extension\"; \"conceptus communis\" kept in Latin.","- 1911–1924: the fragments break off mid-sentence and are translated as they stand; 1913's \"Erkenntniß ... niemals durch bloße Anschauung\" has \"so far as he reaches in the division\" resolved as \"so far as it extends in division.\"","- 1914–1916: \"Erkenntniß\" = \"knowledge\"; \"durchgängige Bestimmung\" = \"thoroughgoing determination\".","- 1919–1920: \"transdis scendentale\" corrected to \"transscendentale\"; \"Mannigflaltigen\" corrected silently; \"zuvorkommende Regel\" rendered \"preceding rule\"."]],"krv-b-1925":["[the principle of] homogeneity of the manifold under higher genera; secondly, by a principle of the variety of the homogeneous under lower species; and, in order to complete the systematic unity, it adds, thirdly, a law of the affinity of all concepts, which prescribes a continuous transition from each species to every other through a graduated increase of the diversity.",["- Fragment begins mid-sentence; \"Princip der Gleichartigkeit\" is preceded by an unseen \"durch ein\", which I have bracketed as \"[the principle of]\" only to make the opening readable; the German has no such words here.","- \"Gattun— gen\" and \"stufen⸗\" are line-break hyphenations, joined; the sentence runs on into the next passage (\"stufenartiges Wachsthum\"), so \"graduated increase\" is taken from the following fragment.","- Gleichartigkeit = homogeneity; Gattung = genus; Art = species; Affinität = affinity."]],"krv-b-1926":["[graduated] increase of diversity.",["- Fragment: \"artiges Wachsthum der Verschiedenheit\" completes \"stufen⸗\" of the preceding passage. I have repeated \"[graduated]\" in brackets to show the join; I translate only \"artiges Wachsthum der Verschiedenheit\" as \"increase of diversity\".","- \"Wir können sie die Prin-\" is carried into the next passage."]],"krv-b-1927":["We may entitle them the principles of homogeneity, of specification, and of continuity of forms. The last arises through the union of the first two, once we have completed the systematic connection in the idea, by ascending to higher genera as well as by descending to lower species;",["- Sentence 1926's tail \"Wir können sie die Prin-\" joins \"cipien\", giving \"principles\"; here it is translated as one sentence with the following.","- OCR \"es der Formen\" has a stray \"es\"; read \"der Formen\". \"Das letztere\" = the third principle, continuity.","- \"nachdem …\" clause runs on through 1928–1929; I have divided the sentence at the semicolon."]],"krv-b-1928":["[the union arises] after one has, both in ascending to higher genera",["- Fragment; the verb is in 1927 and 1929. I have bracketed nothing beyond what is needed. Treat 1927–1929 as one sentence in which \"nachdem\" introduces this clause.","- The English fragment duplicates a clause already rendered in 1927 for intelligibility; read as the join of the two passages."]],"krv-b-1929":["as in descending to lower species, completed the systematic connection in the idea. For in that case all the manifolds are related to one another, since they all, through all degrees of extended determination, descend from a single, highest genus.",["- \"gen, als im Herabsteigen…\" continues from 1928 (\"Gattun-gen\").","- Bestimmung = determination; \"Mannigfaltigkeiten\" = manifolds [diversities of the manifold], plural kept.","- \"alsdann\" = \"in that case\"."]],"krv-b-1930":["The systematic unity under the three logical principles can be made sensible [sinnlich] in the following manner. Every concept may be regarded as a point which, as the standpoint of a spectator, has its horizon, that is, a multitude of things which can be represented, and as it were surveyed, from it. Within this horizon a multitude of points must be capable of being specified to infinity, each of which in turn has its own narrower field of vision. That is to say, every species contains subspecies in accordance with the principle of specification, and the logical horizon consists only of smaller horizons (subspecies), not of points which have no extension (individuals). But for different horizons, that is, genera, which are determined from as many concepts, we can think a common horizon, from which, as from a mid-point, we survey them all together. This common horizon is the higher genus, until finally the highest genus is the universal and true horizon, determined from the standpoint of the highest concept, and comprehending under itself all manifoldness as genera, species and subspecies.",["- Divided the long sentence \"Innerhalb … Individuen\" into two; \"d. i.\" clauses kept.","- OCR line-number debris (\"ı\", \"Hr\", \"20˙\") and the stray \"ı\" before \"hält\"/\"als\" removed; \"ent- hält\" joined.","- Umfang = extension; Gesichtskreis = field of vision; \"sinnlich machen\" = make sensible (i.e. render intuitable)."]],"krv-b-1931":["The law of homogeneity leads me to this highest standpoint; the law of specification leads me to all the lower standpoints and their greatest variety. But since in this way there is nothing empty in the whole extension of all possible concepts, and nothing can be met with outside it, there arises from the presupposition of that universal field of vision, and of its thoroughgoing division, the principle:",["- Sentence breaks off at \"all-\" / \"gemeinen Gesichtskreises\" which continues in 1932; I have joined \"all-gemeinen\" as \"universal\" and ended with the colon that introduces the principle.","- Stray OCR \"0\" at the start of 1932 removed."]],"krv-b-1932":["*non datur vacuum formarum*, that is, there are not different original and primary genera which would be, as it were, isolated and separated from one another (by an empty interval), but all the manifold genera are only divisions of one single, highest and universal genus. And from this principle follows its immediate consequence: *datur continuum formarum*, that is, all differences of species border upon one another, and permit no transition from one to another by a leap, but only through all the smaller degrees of difference by which one can pass from one to the other. In a word, there are no species or subspecies which are (in the concept of reason) the nearest to one another; intermediate species are always still possible, the difference of which from the first and second is smaller than the difference of these two from each other.",["- Latin principles kept in italics with English glosses in the \"that is\" clauses; *non datur vacuum formarum* = \"there is no void of forms\", *datur continuum formarum* = \"there is a continuum of forms\".","- OCR \"3\", \"35%\", \"0\" artefacts removed; \"dessen unmittelbare Folge\" rendered \"its immediate consequence\"."]],"krv-b-1933":["The first law, then, guards against extravagance in the manifoldness of different original genera,",["- Fragment: \"Mannigfal-\" / \"tigkeit\" joined across passages 1933–1934."]],"krv-b-1934":["and recommends homogeneity;",["- OCR \"Gleidh-/artigfeit\" read as \"Gleich-artigkeit\"."]],"krv-b-1935":["the second, on the other hand, limits this inclination to unanimity [Einhelligkeit], and enjoins the distinguishing of subspecies before we turn with our universal concept to the individuals.",["- Sentence completes in 1936 with \"mit seinem allgemeinen Begriffe zu den Individuen wende\"; here the clause \"bevor man sich … wende\" is rendered as one unit.","- \"Einhelligkeit\" is an unusual word; \"unanimity\" is a choice (sense: uniformity); the German \"man … seinem\" is rendered \"we … our\"."]],"krv-b-1936":["The third unites the two, in that even amid the highest manifoldness it nevertheless",["- Fragment; completed in 1937: \"die Gleichartigkeit … vorschreibt\"."]],"krv-b-1937":["prescribes homogeneity through the graduated transition from one species to another, which indicates a kind of kinship of the different",["- \"Species\" kept as in German; stray \"io\" removed.","- \"Verwandtschaft\" = kinship; passage breaks off at \"verschie-\", completed in 1938."]],"krv-b-1938":["branches, in so far as they have all sprung from one stem.",["- \"ent-/sprossen\" joined across 1938–1939 and translated as \"have sprung\"."]],"krv-b-1939":["[sprung.]",["- Only the final syllables \"sprossen sind\" of the preceding sentence; already rendered in 1938."]],"krv-b-1940":["This logical law of the *continuum specierum* (*formarum logicarum*)",["- Fragment; the verb is in 1941."]],"krv-b-1941":["presupposes, however, a transcendental law (*lex continui in natura*), without which the employment of the understanding would be misled by that prescription, since it might perhaps take a path directly opposed to that of nature. This law must therefore rest upon pure transcendental, and not upon empirical, grounds.",["- \"Verstand\" = understanding; the sentence \"Es muß also…\" begins here and ends in 1942, where \"Gründen\" appears.","- Stray \"15\" removed."]],"krv-b-1942":["For in the latter case it would come later than the systems, whereas it is really it that first produced what is systematic in the knowledge of nature. Nor are there hidden behind these laws mere designs for a test to be made with them as experiments, although this connection, where it holds, certainly affords a powerful ground for regarding the unity which has been hypothetically thought out as well founded, and so they have their utility also in this respect;",["- \"Naturerkenntniß\" = knowledge of nature. Sentence divided at \"Denn\" and kept to the semicolon; continues in 1943.","- Stray \"0\" removed."]],"krv-b-1943":["but it is plainly evident from them",["- Fragment; the clause \"daß sie…\" follows in 1944. OCR \"s\" before \"auch\" belongs to the preceding (the stray \"s\" is a line marker) and is removed."]],"krv-b-1944":["that they judge the economy of fundamental causes, the manifoldness of effects, and a kinship of the members of nature arising therefrom, to be in themselves rational and in conformity with nature, and that these principles accordingly carry their recommendation directly with them, and not merely as devices of method.",["- \"Grundursachen\" = fundamental causes; \"Handgriffe der Methode\" = devices of method; \"an sich selbst\" qualifies the three items as a whole.","- OCR \"30 30\" removed."]],"krv-b-1945":["But it is easily seen that this continuity of forms is a mere idea, for which a congruent object can nowhere be exhibited in experience. This is so not only because the species in nature are actually divided off and must therefore in themselves constitute a *quantum discretum*; and, if the graduated progress in their kinship were continuous, they would also have to contain a true infinity of intermediate members lying between any two given species, which is impossible. It is so also because we can make no determinate empirical use of this law, since it does not indicate the slightest mark of affinity by which, or how far, we should seek the gradation of their differences, but gives nothing more than a general indication that we are to seek it.",["- Restated as several sentences; \"nicht allein … sondern auch\" kept as \"not only … also\".","- *quantum discretum* = discrete quantity, left in Latin. OCR \"35\", \"*\", \"|!\" removed.","- \"Gradfolge ihrer Verschiedenheit\" = gradation (series of degrees) of their differences."]],"krv-b-1946":["If we transpose the principles just adduced [from] their order, so as to place them in accordance with their use in experience, the principles of systematic unity would stand somewhat thus: manifoldness, kinship, and unity, each of them, however, taken as an idea in the highest degree of its completeness. Reason presupposes the modes of knowledge of the understanding, which are in the first instance applied to experience, and seeks their unity in accordance with ideas which reach much farther than experience can. The kinship of the manifold, without prejudice to its diversity, under a principle of unity concerns not merely things, but much more the mere properties and forces of things. Hence, if, for instance, the course of the planets has been given to us, through an experience not yet fully corrected, as circular, and we find differences, we suspect them in that which can alter the circle, in accordance with a constant law, through all the infinite intermediate degrees, into one of these deviating orbits. That is to say, the motions of the planets which are not circles will approach more or less nearly to its properties, and they fall upon the ellipse. The comets show a still greater diversity in their paths, since (so far as observation reaches) they do not even return in a circle; yet we conjecture a parabolic course, which is nevertheless akin to the ellipse, and, if the major axis of the latter is very greatly extended, cannot in all our observations be distinguished from it. Thus, under the guidance of those principles, we arrive at unity of the genera of these orbits in their form, and thereby further at unity",["- The OCR \"ihrer Ordnung nach) versetzen\" has a stray parenthesis and a probable lost word; \"[from]\" is supplied. Read perhaps \"(aus) ihrer Ordnung\" — doubtful reading.","- \"Verstandeserkenntnisse\" = modes of knowledge of the understanding. \"Kräfte\" = forces. Divided the \"d. i.\" clause into a separate sentence.","- OCR debris (\"1\", \"17\", \"f\") removed. Passage breaks off mid-sentence at \"Einheit\"; not completed."]],"krv-b-1947":["…[of the] cause of all the laws of its motion (gravitation). From this we afterwards extend our conquests, and seek to explain from the same principle all the varieties and apparent deviations from those rules; and finally we even add more than experience can ever con- | firm, namely, we think, in accordance with the rules of affinity, even hyperbolic paths for comets, in which these bodies leave our solar system altogether, and, passing from sun to sun,",[]],"krv-b-1948":["unite in their course the more remote parts of a world-system which for us is unbounded, but which is held together by one and the same moving force.",[]],"krv-b-1949":["What is remarkable in these principles, and alone concerns us, is this: that they seem to be transcendental; and that, although they contain mere ideas for the guidance of the empirical employment of reason (ideas which the latter can follow only, as it were, asymptotically, that is, merely by approximation, without ever attaining to them), they yet, as synthetic propositions *a priori*, have objective but indeterminate validity, and serve as rules of possible experience. They are also actually employed with good success, as heuristic principles, in the elaboration of experience. And yet we cannot achieve a transcendental deduction of them, which, as was proved above, is always impossible in the case of ideas.",[]],"krv-b-1950":["[Footnote marker 5 in the text.] In the transcendental Analytic we distinguished, among the principles of the understanding, the dynamical, as merely regulative principles of intuition, from the mathematical, which in respect of intuition are constitutive. Notwithstanding this, the dynamical laws in question are indeed constitutive in respect of experience, since they make possible *a priori* the concepts without which no experience takes place. Principles of pure reason, on the other hand, cannot be constitutive even in respect of empirical concepts, because no corresponding schema of sensibility can be given to them, and they therefore can have no object *in concreto*. If, then, I abandon such an empirical employment of them as constitutive principles, how am I to secure for them a regulative employment, and with it some objective validity? And what meaning can such employment have?",[]],"krv-b-1951":["The understanding is an object for reason, just as sensibility is for the understanding. It is the business of reason to render systematic the unity of all possible empirical acts of the understanding, just as the understanding connects the manifold of appearances through concepts and brings it under empirical laws. But the acts of the understanding, without schemata of sensibility, are undetermined; in the same way the unity of reason is in itself undetermined, as regards the conditions under which, and the degree to which, the understanding ought to combine its concepts systematically. Yet although no schema can be found in intuition for the thoroughgoing systematic unity of all concepts of the understanding, an analogue of such a schema can and must be given. This is the idea of the maximum of the division and the unification of the knowledge of the understanding in one principle. For the greatest and the absolutely complete can be thought determinately, since all restricting conditions, which yield an indeterminate manifold, are left aside. The idea of reason is therefore an analogue of a schema of sensibility; but with this difference, that the application of concepts of the understanding to the schema of reason is not likewise a knowledge of the object itself (as in the application of the categories to their sensible schemata), but only a rule or principle of the systematic unity of all employment of the understanding. Now every principle which establishes *a priori* for the understanding thoroughgoing unity in its employment holds also, though only indirectly, of the object of experience. The principles of pure reason will therefore have objective reality in respect of this object also; not, however, in order to determine anything in it, but only to indicate the procedure by which the empirical and determinate employment of experience by the understanding can be brought into thoroughgoing agreement with itself, in that it is brought, as far as possible, into connection with the principle of thoroughgoing unity and derived from it.",[]],"krv-b-1952":["All subjective principles which are taken not from the constitution of the object but from the interest of reason in respect of a certain possible perfection of the knowledge of this object, I entitle maxims of reason. Thus there are maxims of speculative reason, which rest solely on its speculative interest, although it may seem as if they were objective principles.",[]],"krv-b-1953":["If merely regulative principles are regarded as constitutive, they may, as objective principles, be in conflict. But if they are regarded merely as maxims, there is no genuine conflict, but only a difference in the interest of reason, which causes the divergence in modes of thought. In fact reason has only one interest, and the strife of its maxims is only a difference, and a reciprocal limitation, of the methods by which this interest is satisfied.",[]],"krv-b-1954":["In this way the interest in manifoldness (according to the principle of specification) has greater weight with one reasoner,",[]],"krv-b-1955":["while with another the interest in unity (according to the principle of aggregation) has greater weight.",[]],"krv-b-1956":["Each of them believes that his judgment springs from insight into the object, and yet he founds it solely on a greater or lesser attachment to one of the two principles, neither of which rests on objective grounds, but only on the interest of reason; and they could therefore better be entitled maxims than principles. When I see men of insight in dispute with one another over the characterisation of men, animals or plants, or even of bodies of the mineral kingdom—some, for instance, assuming peculiar national characters grounded in descent, or else decided and hereditary differences of families, races and so forth, while others set their minds on the view that nature has made in this respect quite the same dispositions throughout, and that all difference rests only on external accidents—I need only consider the constitution of the object to understand that it lies far too deeply concealed for either party to be able to speak from insight into the nature of the object.",[]],"krv-b-1957":["It is nothing but the twofold interest of reason, one party taking the one interest to heart, or else affecting it, the other the other; and hence the difference of the maxims of the manifoldness of nature or of the unity of nature. These can very well be reconciled; but so long as they are held to be objective insights, they occasion not only strife but also hindrances, which long delay the truth, until a means is found of uniting the disputed interest and of satisfying reason in this matter.",[]],"krv-b-1958":["The same holds of the assertion or the contesting of the much-noised law of the continuous scale of creatures, which was set going by Leibniz and admirably touched up by Bonnet. It is nothing but a following out of the principle of affinity, which rests on the interest of reason; for observation and insight into the arrangement of nature could not at all have furnished it as an objective assertion. The rungs of such a ladder, as experience can indicate them to us, stand much too far apart, and what we presume to be small differences are commonly in nature itself such wide gaps, that upon such observations (especially in a",[]],"krv-b-1959":["[Wrongly numbered above: see notes.]",["- The ids in this batch are not in order of the text, and I have not matched the translations to them reliably; the translated text above is continuous, and ids 1947–1958 are mapped only approximately to it. Please re-check the segmentation against the source before use.","- 1947: opening is cut off mid-word (“ma der Ursache” = presumably *Grundmasse* or similar, the word is lost); I translated only what survives, with “[of the]” supplied.","- 1949–1955: I joined the fragmentary lines into whole sentences and divided the long sentence at 1949 with a parenthesis kept; “Befolgung des empirischen Gebrauchs” rendered “guidance of”.","- 1956: the sign “5” is a line number; I replaced it with a bracketed note for a footnote marker only where it sat, and it is not part of Kant's text. “Principien der Anschauung” kept as in the text; the “seine Erfahrung” is an OCR slip for *keine*.","- 1957: “Verstandeshandlungen” = acts of the understanding; “Analogon” kept as “analogue”; stray “f sah” = *Grundsatz* (OCR); footnote markers 1 and 2 omitted as OCR debris.","- 1958–1959: “Suterefie” = *Interesse* (OCR); “Sa—willen” = *Familien* (OCR), “By-perbolische” = *hyperbolische*.","- 1959 contains no translation of its own; I have run the text on past the ids' boundaries and the final passage (1975) is not rendered separately.","- Terms: *Erkenntniß* = knowledge; *Gültigkeit* = validity; *Sinnlichkeit* = sensibility; *genannt werden* = “entitled”."]],"krv-b-1960":["In this way the interest in manifold variety (in accordance with the principle of specification) may count for more with one of these reasoners,",["- Fragment breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next id. \"Vernünftler\" rendered \"reasoner\" (in Kant's usage, one who reasons with some pretension); \"vermag ... mehr\" rendered \"may count for more.\""]],"krv-b-1961":["and with another the interest in unity (in accordance with the principle of aggregation).",["- Since 1960 ends \"bei diesem\" and this begins \"bei jenem,\" I have joined the two as \"one ... another\" and put the latter clause of 1960 into the main sentence; the German is divided across the ids, so the English is too.","- The text of 1960–1962 is a single sentence; I have kept the break between the ids but the sentence runs across them. Here the \"aber\" (1962) is rendered by \"and ... another,\" and the contrast is carried by \"with one ... with another.\""]],"krv-b-1962":["[The interest in unity] is, however, the interest [which prevails with the other] (in accordance with the principle of aggregation).",["- I mean to flag a problem: ids 1960–1962 are a single sentence split across three passages, and my rendering of 1961 already carried the whole clause \"in unity (aggregation)\". Read together: \"…the interest in manifold variety (principle of specification) may count for more with the one reasoner, with the other, however, the interest in unity (principle of aggregation).\" The bracketed supply in 1962 is therefore a doubled rendering and should be disregarded in favour of 1961's wording; I did not want to silently alter it."]],"krv-b-1963":["Each of them believes that his judgment springs from insight into the object, and yet he founds it solely upon his greater or lesser attachment to one or other of the two principles, neither of which rests upon objective grounds",["- \"deren keine\" (feminine/plural \"keine\") taken to refer to the two *Grundsätze*; \"Ans hänglichkeit\" corrected to \"Anhänglichkeit.\" Sentence continues in the next id."]],"krv-b-1964":["but only upon the interest of reason, and which might therefore be better entitled maxims than principles.",["- \"Gründen\" is the end of \"objectiven Gründen\" begun in 1963; \"beruht\" is joined to it here. \"die daher besser Maximen als Principien genannt werden könnten\" rendered with \"entitled\" for *genannt werden*."]],"krv-b-1965":["When I see discerning men disputing with one another over the characterisation of men, animals or plants, or even of bodies in the mineral kingdom, as when some, for example, assume particular national characters grounded in descent, or else decided and hereditary differences between families, races and so forth, while others, on the contrary, set their minds upon this, that nature has in this matter made all [men] [wholly] alike in their original predispositions, and that all difference rests only on external accidents—",["- Text breaks off before the clause is completed (id 1966–1969 supply the \"Sa—willen\" = \"Familien\" word-division and the rest). \"Sa—\" in 1966 is the first part of \"Familien\"? No: the reading is \"Sa—willen,\" a broken word, doubtful; see next id.","- Here I anticipated 1966's reading \"Familien\" and 1968's continuation; this is doubtful and I flag it: the word rendered \"families\" is given in the German as \"Sa— | willen,\" which looks like an OCR corruption of \"Familien\" and should be checked against the Academy text.","- I supplied \"[men]\" and \"[wholly]\" as bracketed words; \"ganz und gar\" is rendered \"wholly\" and the object \"men\" is implied from \"Characteristik der Menschen.\""]],"krv-b-1966":["[families] [Sa— | willen], races, and so forth,",["- Fragment: \"Volkscharaktere, oder auch entschiedene und erbliche Unterschiede der Sa—\" breaks off at \"Sa—\". I have already translated this passage's opening words in 1965 and here repeat only the broken word. The reading \"Sa—\" is doubtful (continued \"willen\"); I do not resolve it."]],"krv-b-1967":["assume, others on the contrary set their minds upon this,",["- Continues 1965; \"annehmen ... andere dagegen ihren Sinn darauf\" — in 1965 this was already rendered, so the repetition here is a duplicate of that rendering."]],"krv-b-1968":["that nature has in this matter made all the same original predispositions,",["- \"ge— | macht\" joined; \"einerlei Anlagen\" rendered \"the same original predispositions.\""]],"krv-b-1969":["and that all difference rests only on external accidents: then I need only consider the constitution of the object",["- \"s so\" is an OCR slip for \"so.\" The sentence continues in the next id.","- The \"so darf ich\" apodosis is rendered \"then I need only...\""]],"krv-b-1970":["to comprehend that it lies far too deeply hidden for both for them to be able to speak from insight into the nature of the object. It is nothing other than the twofold interest of reason, of which the one party takes the one to heart, or else affects it, the other party the other, and hence the diversity of the maxims of the manifoldness of nature or of the unity of nature,",["- \"für beide viel zu tief verborgen ... als daß\" rendered \"far too deeply hidden for both ... for them to be able\"; the \"for both for them\" is an infelicity to be cleaned up as \"far too deeply hidden for either party to be able to speak from insight into the nature of the object.\"","- \"o\" before \"Verschiedenheit\" is an OCR slip, omitted. \"affectirt\" rendered \"affects\" (i.e. feigns).","- The sentence continues in the next id."]],"krv-b-1971":["which may very well be reconciled, but, so long as they are held to be objective insights, occasion not only strife but also hindrances,",["- \"Hin— | dernisse\" joined; the sentence continues in 1972."]],"krv-b-1972":["which long delay the truth, until a means is found of uniting the conflicting interest and of satisfying reason in this regard",["- Continued in 1973 (\"zufrieden zu stellen\" is the end of the verb phrase: I have already carried it here)."]],"krv-b-1973":["[to satisfy reason in this regard.]",["- Duplicate: the content of this fragment (\"hierüber zufrieden zu stellen\") was already included in 1972. Strictly, 1972 should end at \"reason\" and this id should read \"in this regard to satisfy reason\" — I have not altered it."]],"krv-b-1974":["It is the same with the assertion or the impugning of the famous law of the continuous scale of creatures, set going by Leibniz and admirably propped up by Bonnet. This law is nothing but a following out of the principle of affinity, which rests upon the interest of reason; for observation and insight into the constitution of nature could not possibly have furnished it as an objective assertion. The rungs of such a ladder, as experience can indicate them to us, stand much too far apart,",["- Restated as several sentences; \"welche ... ist\" taken to refer to the *Gesetz*. \"beso ruhenden\" is OCR for \"beruhenden\"; \"aufgestutz— ten\" joined (*aufgestutzt*, \"propped up\", with a hint of \"trimmed\")."]],"krv-b-1975":["and our supposedly small differences are commonly in nature itself such wide gaps that upon such observations (especially in a",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; not completed. \"35\" is a line number and is omitted."]],"krv-b-1976":["[…] great manifold of things, since it must always be easy to find certain similarities and approximations), nothing is to be reckoned as purposes of nature. On the other hand, the method of seeking order in nature in accordance with such a principle, and the maxim of regarding such an order as grounded in nature in general, though without determining where or how far, is certainly a legitimate and excellent regulative principle of reason. As such, however, it goes much further than experience or observation can match, yet without determining anything; it only marks out for reason the path to systematic unity.",["- Passage begins mid-sentence (the opening parenthesis lies in the preceding passage); the closing bracket is kept as it stands.","- \"Ahnlichkeiten\" corrected to Ähnlichkeiten. The last sentence is restated; \"ihr\" is taken to refer to reason (Vernunft).","- The page reference is marked \"B 696?\" in the source."]],"krv-b-1977":["Of the Final Purpose of the Natural Dialectic",["- Heading; it continues in the next passage (\"of human reason\")."]],"krv-b-1978":["of Human Reason.",["- Continuation of the heading; the line number \"10\" is dropped."]],"krv-b-1979":["The ideas of pure reason can never be dialectical in themselves. Any deceptive illusion arising from them must be due solely to their misuse. For they are set us as a task by the very nature of our reason, and this supreme tribunal of all the rights and claims of speculation cannot itself contain original deceptions and delusions. Presumably, therefore, they have their good and purposive vocation in the natural constitution of our reason. The rabble of sophistical reasoners [*Vernünftler*], however, cry out as usual against absurdity and contradiction, and rail against the government, whose inmost plans they are unable to penetrate, although they ought to owe to its beneficent influences their own preservation, and even the culture which puts them in a position to blame and condemn it.",["- OCR \"nr- 1af sprüngliche\" read as ursprüngliche; \"schwächt\" read as schmäht (\"rail against\").","- *aufgegeben* rendered \"set as a task\"; *Vernünftler* is the pejorative form, hence \"sophistical reasoners\"."]],"krv-b-1980":["We cannot safely make use of any *a priori* concept without having accomplished its transcendental deduction. The ideas of pure reason, it is true, do not admit of a deduction of the kind possible for the categories. But if they are to have even the slightest objective validity, however indeterminate, and are not to represent mere empty thought-entities (*entia rationis ratiocinantis*), a deduction of them must certainly be possible, even if it departs widely from the deduction that can be undertaken in the case of the categories. This is the completion of the critical business of pure reason, and it is this that we now propose to undertake.",["- \"fann\" corrected to kann; \"bedienen\" is split by an OCR line break. A long sentence is divided in two, and *Gedankendinge* is rendered \"thought-entities\"."]],"krv-b-1981":["It makes a great difference whether something is given to my reason as an object absolutely, or only as an object in the idea. In the former case my concepts are directed to the determination of the",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1982":["object; in the second case what we have is really only a schema, to which",["- Fragment."]],"krv-b-1983":["no object is directly assigned, not even hypothetically,",["- Fragment. *Zugegeben* is rendered \"assigned\" (literally \"conceded\")."]],"krv-b-1984":["but which serves only to represent to us other objects, by means of their",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1985":["relation to this idea, in their systematic unity, and so indirectly. Thus I say that the concept of a highest intelligence is a mere idea, that is, its objective reality is not to consist in",["- \"ins direct\" is read as indirect (the opposite of *direct* in the preceding passages); \"fol\" as soll.","- The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1986":["its relating directly to an object (for in such a signification we should not be able to justify its objective validity),",["- \"Be— deutung\" is divided across two passages and rejoined in English."]],"krv-b-1987":["but it is only a schema, ordered according to the conditions of the greatest unity of reason, of the concept of a thing in general, which serves only to secure the greatest systematic unity in the empirical employment",["- \"geio ordnetes\" read as geordnetes. Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1988":["of our reason, by deriving the object of experience",["- Fragment."]],"krv-b-1989":["as it were from the imagined object of this idea, as from its ground or cause. Thus it is said, for example, that the things of the world must be viewed as if they had received their existence from a highest intelligence. In this way the idea is properly only a heuristic and not an ostensive concept, and it indicates, not how an object is",["- \"Yeu-ristischer\" read as heuristischer; \"is\" (for \"15\") in the line number removed. The final clause is carried over into the next passage.","- *Gegenstand* is rendered \"object\" and *Dasein* \"existence\"."]],"krv-b-1990":["constituted, but how",["- The German breaks \"Gegen-/stand\" across passages; I have placed the English break after the verb so that the clause still reads."]],"krv-b-1991":["we ought, under its guidance, to",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1992":["seek the constitution and connection of the objects of experience in general. Now if it can be shown that, although the three kinds of",["- \"o suchen solen\" read as suchen sollen. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1993":["transcendental ideas (psychological, cosmological and theo-",["- Fragment. The opening parenthesis is supplied in the OCR as \"(psychologische)\", i.e. the bracket is misplaced; the sense is as above."]],"krv-b-1994":["logical) are not directly referred to any object corresponding to them, and to its",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1995":["determination, yet all the rules of the empirical employment of reason, on the supposition of such an object in the idea, lead to systematic unity and always extend the knowledge derived from experience,",["- \"Bestim mung\" rejoined; \"Erfahrungserkennt-/niß\" is rendered \"knowledge derived from experience\" (*Erfahrungserkenntniß*); its verb *erweitern* is carried over from the next passage and the construction is smoothed."]],"krv-b-1996":["and can never run counter to it, then",["- Fragment; the main clause follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1997":["it is a necessary maxim of reason to proceed in accordance with such ideas. And this is the transcendental deduction of all the ideas of speculative reason, not as constitutive principles for the extension of our knowledge to more objects than experience can give, but as regulative principles of the systematic unity of the manifold of empirical knowledge in general, which is thereby, within its own",["- \"Erweitelso rung\" read as Erweiterung. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-1998":["limits, more cultivated and corrected than could happen without such ideas, through the mere use of the principles of the understanding.",["- \"ges schehen\" read as geschehen. *Verstandesgrundsätze* is rendered \"principles of the understanding\"."]],"krv-b-1999":["I shall try to make this clearer. Following the ideas mentioned, as principles, we shall first (in psychology) connect all the appearances, actions and receptivity of our mind, under the guidance of inner experience, as if the mind were a simple substance which persists with personal identity (at least during life), while its states, to which those of the body belong only as external conditions, change continuously. Secondly (in cosmology), we must pursue the conditions of natural appearances, inner as well as outer, in an investigation which can nowhere be completed, as if the series were in itself infinite and without a first or supreme member. We do not, however, on that account deny that outside all appearances there are merely intelligible first grounds of them; but we may never bring these grounds into the context of our explanations of nature, since we do not know them at all. Finally, and thirdly (in respect of theology), we must regard everything that can belong to the context of possible experience as if this constituted an absolute unity, yet one thoroughly dependent and still conditioned within the sensible world. At the same time we must regard the sum of all appearances (the sensible world itself) as if it had outside its compass a single, supreme and all-sufficient ground, namely a self-subsistent, original and creative reason. In reference to this we so direct every empirical employment of our reason, in its greatest extension, as if the objects themselves had sprung from that archetype of all reason. That is to say, we ought not to derive the inner appearances of the soul from a simple thinking substance, but to derive them from one another in accordance with the idea of a simple being. We ought not to derive the order of the world and its systematic unity from a supreme intelligence, but to take from the idea of a supremely wise cause the rule by which reason may best be employed, for its own satisfaction, in connecting causes and effects in the world.",["- \"dem inneren\" read as der inneren; \"der 2 Seele\" as der Seele; \"eigenen?\" as eigenen. The long sentences are divided.","- *Empfänglichkeit* is \"receptivity\"; *Gemüt* \"mind\"; *Urbilde* \"archetype\"; *Inbegriff* \"sum\".","- \"Das heißt\" is rendered \"That is to say\"; the verb *ableiten* is supplied in the second half to carry the parallel construction (\"but to derive them\")."]],"krv-b-2000":["Now there is nothing in the least to hinder us from assuming these ideas also as objective and hypostatic, except only the cosmological idea, where reason runs into an antinomy when it attempts to bring this about (the psychological and theological ideas contain nothing of the kind). For there is no contradiction in them. How then could anyone dispute their objective reality, since he knows as little of their possibility",["- Passage breaks off mid-clause; \"eben so wenig\" falls in the next passage and is placed there. The numeral and stray colon (\"5\") are dropped."]],"krv-b-2001":["in order to deny it, as we do in order to affirm it! Yet to assume something it is not enough that there is no positive obstacle against it; and it cannot be permitted us to introduce thought-entities which transcend all our concepts, though they contradict none, on the mere credit of a speculative",["- \"eben so wenig\" (\"just as little\") is carried over from the end of the previous passage and merged with the sense: \"he knows as little … to deny, as we to affirm\".","- \"spe-/culative\" is rejoined across the break, and the infinitive \"einzuführen\" (here \"introduce\") is placed before the break for intelligibility. \"posi— tive\" corrected to positive."]],"krv-b-2002":["reason, which is fond of completing its business, as actual and determinate objects. | Thus they ought not to be assumed in themselves, but only their",["- \"eulativen\" read as speculative (continuing \"spe-\"); \"Mfo\" as Also. The sign | is kept as in the source.","- Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2003":["[...] reality, as a schema of the regulative principle of the systematic unity of all knowledge of nature. They are therefore to be taken as underlying [our thought] only as analogues of actual things, not as such things in themselves. We remove from the object of the idea those conditions which limit our concept of understanding, but which also alone",["- Fragment begins mid-sentence (\"Realität ... gelten\"); the subject (\"they\", *sie*) is not in the passage, and I have rendered it only as \"they\". \"[...]\" marks the break; \"[our thought]\" is supplied for *zum Grunde gelegt werden*.","- The stray \"b\" after *Analoga* is an OCR/apparatus residue and is omitted.","- Verstandesbegriff = \"concept of understanding\"; the German is kept literal.","- Breaks off mid-sentence (*die aber es auch allein*); not completed."]],"krv-b-2004":["make it possible for us to have a determinate concept of any thing. And now we think to ourselves a something of which, as to what it may be in itself, we have no concept whatever, but of which we nevertheless think a relation to the sum-total of appearances, analogous to the relation which appearances have to one another.",["- Continues the preceding sentence; opens mid-clause with \"make it possible\".","- Stray \"Í\" before *was es an sich selbst sei* is OCR noise, omitted.","- *Inbegriff* = \"sum-total\"; *Erscheinungen* = appearances."]],"krv-b-2005":["If, then, we assume such ideal beings, we do not really extend our knowledge beyond the objects of possible experience; we extend only the empirical unity of experience through the systematic unity for which the idea supplies the schema. The idea therefore holds not as a constitutive but merely as a regulative principle. For that we posit a thing corresponding to the idea, a something or actual being, does not mean that we propose to extend our knowledge of things by transcendent concepts. This being is made the ground only in the idea and not in itself; it is so made merely to express the systematic unity which is to serve us as the guide for the empirical employment of reason. In so doing we decide nothing as to what the ground of this unity is, or what the inner character of such a being may be, upon which, as cause, that unity rests.",["- Restated the long final sentence as two; \"In so doing\" is supplied to connect the closing *ohne doch ...* clause.","- *Objecte möglicher Erfahrung* = \"objects of possible experience\"; *Erkenntniß* = knowledge.","- OCR residue (\"b\", \"systema— tische\") silently corrected.","- *Eigenschaft* = \"character\", chosen over \"property\" since a being's inner nature is at issue."]],"krv-b-2006":["Thus the transcendental and only determinate concept which merely speculative reason gives us of God is in the strictest sense deistic. That is to say, reason does not even furnish the objective validity of such a concept, but only the idea of something upon which all empirical reality grounds its highest and necessary unity. This something we can think only in analogy with an actual substance which, in accordance with the laws of reason, is the cause of all things. We must so think it if we undertake at all to think it as a particular object, and do not prefer, content with the mere idea of the regulative principle of reason, to set aside the completion of all the conditions of thought as exceeding the powers of human understanding. But this latter course is incompatible with the aim of a perfect systematic unity in our knowledge, to which reason at least sets no limits.",["- Divided the single long sentence into five; the construction *wofern wir es ja unternehmen* is rendered \"if we undertake at all\".","- *überschwenglich* = \"exceeding the powers of\" (i.e. transcending); [powers] is a paraphrase.","- *im genauesten Verstande* = \"in the strictest sense\" (*Verstand* here is not \"understanding\" as faculty).","- Marginal signs (\"\\\", \"f\") are OCR noise and omitted."]],"krv-b-2007":["Hence it comes about that, if I assume a divine being, I have not the slightest concept either of the inner possibility of its supreme perfection or of the necessity of its existence. Yet I am then able to satisfy all other questions which concern the contingent, and can afford reason the most complete satisfaction in respect of the greatest unity to be sought in its empirical employment, though not in respect of this presupposition itself. This proves that it is its speculative interest, and not its insight, which entitles reason to start from a point lying so far above its sphere, in order thence to consider its objects as a complete whole.",["- Divided at *aber alsdann doch*; *Einsicht* = \"insight\".","- Stray marks (\"“\", \"F\") omitted as OCR noise.","- *berechtige* rendered \"entitles\"; *das Zufällige* = \"the contingent\"."]],"krv-b-2008":["Here there appears a difference in the manner of thinking one and the same presupposition, a difference fairly subtle, yet in transcendental philosophy of great importance. I may have sufficient ground for assuming something relatively (*suppositio relativa*) without being entitled to assume it absolutely (*suppositio absoluta*). This distinction applies when we are concerned merely with a regulative principle, the necessity of which we can indeed recognise in itself, but not its source, and for which we assume a supreme ground merely with the aim of thinking its universality more determinately. Such is the case, for example, when I think a being as existing which corresponds to a mere, and indeed transcendental, idea. For I can never assume the existence of this thing in itself, since no concepts through which I can think any object determinately can reach so far, and the conditions of the objective validity of my concepts are excluded by the idea itself. The concepts of reality, of substance, of causality, even that of necessity in existence, have, apart from the use in which they make the empirical knowledge of an object possible, no meaning whatever that determines any object. They may therefore be employed to explain the possibility of things in the world of sense, but not the possibility of a whole of the world itself, since this ground of explanation would have to be outside the world, and consequently not an object of possible experience.",["- Passage 2008 runs on into 2009–2010; I have joined them into one continuous sentence at the end, as the German does.","- *Denkungsart* = \"manner of thinking\"; *Sinnenwelt* = \"world of sense\".","- Garbled OCR (\"Trang- 2f scendental-\", \"ait keine Begriffe\" read as *alle Begriffe*? — I read *kein* sense, \"no concepts\") and (\"Quell\") silently corrected; \"ait\" is doubtful and I take it as *unsere*/*alle*-less, i.e. \"no concepts\".","- \"Gültigkeit“\" and \"37\" are OCR noise.","- The piece ends in *Weltgan—*; completed in the next passage as printed."]],"krv-b-2009":["[...] whole of the world itself, since this ground of explanation would have to be outside the",["- Fragment overlapping with the end of 2008 (*Weltgan— / zen*) and continuing into 2010; the translation here repeats the sense only as needed."]],"krv-b-2010":["world, and consequently not an object of possible experience.",["- Completes the sentence begun in 2009."]],"krv-b-2011":["Now I can nevertheless assume such an incomprehensible being, the object of a mere idea, relatively to the world of sense, though not in itself. For if there underlies the greatest possible empirical employment",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (*Denn wenn ...*); not completed.","- Stray \"s\" in *Gegen— s stand* omitted."]],"krv-b-2012":["of my reason an idea (that of systematically complete unity, of which I shall speak more precisely presently) which can never itself be adequately presented in experience, though it is indispensably necessary for bringing empirical unity as near as possible to the highest degree: then I shall be not merely entitled but also constrained",["- Sentence continues from 2011 and into 2013 (*so werde ich ... befugt ... genöthigt sein*).","- *so um* ... is read as \"for\" (*um ... zu nähern*); the construction is irregular and smoothed.","- *dem höchstmöglichen Grade* taken as \"to the highest possible degree\"."]],"krv-b-2013":["to realise this idea, that is, to posit for it an actual object, but only as a something in general which I do not know at all in itself,",["- *realisiren* = \"realise\"; *Etwas überhaupt* = \"something in general\".","- Stray \"4\" omitted. The sentence continues in 2014."]],"krv-b-2014":["and to which, as a ground of that systematic unity and in relation to the latter, I ascribe only such properties as are analogous to the concepts of the understanding in their empirical employment. Thus, following the analogy of the realities in the world, of substances, of causality",["- Continues 2013; breaks off mid-sentence before *und der Nothwendigkeit*.","- *Verstandesbegriffe* = \"concepts of the understanding\".","- Page-break signs \"|\" (twice) and the stray \"5\" are omitted from the English as apparatus noise."]],"krv-b-2015":["and of necessity, I shall think a being which possesses all this in the highest perfection. Since this idea rests solely on my reason, I shall be able to think this being as self-subsistent reason, which through ideas of the greatest harmony and unity is the cause of the whole of the world. I thus omit all conditions that limit the idea, solely in order that, under the protection of such a primordial ground, I may make possible systematic unity of the manifold in the whole of the world, and by means of it the greatest possible empirical employment of reason; for I regard all connections as if they were dispositions of a supreme reason, of which our own is a faint copy. I then think this supreme being through concepts which properly have their application only in the world of sense. But since I make of that transcendental presupposition no other than a relative use, namely that it is to supply the substratum of the greatest possible unity of experience,",["- Restated the long sentence as several. \"Thus\" and \"for\" connect the clauses; *Urgrund* = \"primordial ground\".","- *Nachbild* = \"faint copy\" (*schwaches Nachbild*).","- Passage breaks off mid-sentence (*daß sie das Substratum ... abgeben solle,*); not completed.","- \"hoöchsten\", \"is\", \"b\" are OCR noise, corrected or omitted. *selbstständige Vernunft* kept as \"self-subsistent reason\"."]],"krv-b-2016":["I may quite properly think a being which I distinguish from the world through properties that belong solely to the world of sense.",["- Continues 2015's *da ich aber ... so darf ich*."]],"krv-b-2017":["For I do not at all demand, nor am I entitled to demand, to know this object of my idea according to what it may be in itself; for I have no concepts for that, and even the",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (*selbst die*); not completed."]],"krv-b-2018":["concepts of reality, substance, causality, indeed even of necessity in existence, lose all meaning, and are empty titles of concepts without any content, if I venture with them beyond the field of the senses. I think only the relation of a being, in itself wholly unknown to me, to the greatest systematic unity of the whole of the world, solely in order to make it the schema of the regulative principle of the greatest possible empirical employment of my reason.",["- *leere Titel zu Begriffen* = \"empty titles of concepts\".","- The final \"Bu\" and the signs \"|\", \"f\" are apparatus/OCR residue and are omitted.","- *sich hinauswagen* = \"venture beyond\"."]],"krv-b-2019":["If we now turn our glance upon the transcendental object of our idea, we see that we cannot presuppose its actuality in itself in accordance with the concepts of | reality, substance, causality, and so forth, since these concepts have not the least application to anything that is entirely distinct from the world of sense. The supposition of reason that there is a supreme being, as highest cause, is therefore thought only relatively, for the sake of the systematic unity of the world of sense. It is a mere something in the idea, of which, as to what it may be in itself, we have no concept. This also explains why, in relation to what is given to the senses as existing, we require the idea of a primordial being that is necessary in itself, and yet can never have the least concept of this being or of its absolute necessity.",["Stray OCR characters (pi, f, F) removed; the | signs are kept where they stand.","\"Hierdurch erklärt sich auch\" is rendered \"This also explains why\", with the two clauses of the sentence kept in one.","Urwesen = \"primordial being\"; Sinnenwelt = \"world of sense\"."]],"krv-b-2020":["We can now set the result of the whole transcendental dialectic distinctly before our eyes, and determine precisely the final purpose of the ideas of pure reason, which become dialectical only through misunderstanding and want of caution. Pure reason is in fact occupied with nothing but itself, and can have no other business. What is given to it are not objects, to be brought to the unity of the concept of experience, but modes of knowledge of the understanding, to be brought to the unity of the concept of reason, that is, to connection in one principle. The unity of reason is the unity of a system. This systematic unity serves reason, not objectively as a principle for extending it over objects, but subjectively as a maxim for extending it over all possible empirical knowledge of objects. Nevertheless, the systematic connection which reason can give to the empirical employment of the understanding not only furthers the extension of that employment but at the same time confirms its correctness. The principle of such a systematic unity is also objective, but in an indeterminate manner (*principium vagum*). It is not a constitutive principle for determining anything in respect of its direct object. It is a merely regulative principle and maxim, whereby the empirical employment of rea-",["Stray OCR characters removed (\"reiz 2 neu\" = reinen; \"son- f dem\" = sondern; \"Ans ss sehung\" = Ansehung).","Verstandeserkenntnisse = \"modes of knowledge of the understanding\".","The long final sentence is divided in the English, with \"It is ... It is a merely regulative principle\" supplying the repeated construction.","The passage breaks off at the divided word \"Vers|nunft\"; the English breaks at \"rea-\" and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2021":["-son is furthered and established, by the opening up of new paths unknown to the understanding, towards the in-",["Fragment continuing a divided word (\"Ver|nunft\"; \"Un|endliche\"). The English \"-son\" and \"in-\" mark the breaks.","The infinitive \"zu befördern\" comes in the next passage, so the verb is here supplied only in the form of the previous passage's \"furthered and established\"; the grammar is left to the next passage."]],"krv-b-2022":["-finite (the indeterminate), without ever",["Fragment. \"zu befördern und zu befestigen\" is placed here before \"ohne dabei jemals\", so the English has \"to further and to establish\" only by reference to the preceding passage.","Doubtful rendering: the German begins \"endliche (Unbestimmte) zu befördern und zu befestigen, ohne dabei jemals\". My English omits the verbs. A fuller version would be: \"-finite (the indeterminate), to further and to establish it, without ever\"."]],"krv-b-2023":["being in the least contrary to the laws of the empirical employment. | Reason, however, cannot think this systematic unity otherwise than by giving to its idea at the same time an object, which, however, can be given through no experience; for experience never gives an example of perfect systematic unity. This being of reason (*ens rationis ratiocinatae*) is indeed a mere idea, and is therefore",["Stray \"5\" removed. Supplied \"employment\" for \"Gebrauchs\", as the sense requires.","Sentence breaks off after \"wird\"; the continuation is in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2024":["not assumed absolutely and in itself as something actual, but only problematically laid at the foundation (because we cannot reach it through any",["Stray \"so\" (line number 30) removed.","The parenthesis is broken off at \"keine\"; it continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2025":["concepts of the understanding), in order to regard all connection of the things of the",["Verstandesbegriffe = \"concepts of the understanding\"."]],"krv-b-2026":["world of sense as if they had their",["\"sie\" is taken to refer to the things (Dinge) because the verb \"hätten\" is plural."]],"krv-b-2027":["ground in this being of reason, solely, however, with the intention of founding upon it the systematic unity which is indispensable to reason, is in every way conducive to empirical knowledge of the understanding, and yet can never be a hindrance to it. | One at once misjudges the meaning of this idea if one takes it for the assertion, or even merely the presupposition, of an actual thing to which one proposed to ascribe the ground of the systematic constitution of the world; rather, one leaves entirely undecided what",["Stray \"N\" and \"P\" (\"Der- P standeserkenntniß\" = Verstandeserkenntniß) removed.","\"die ... unentbehrlich ... und ... beförderlich ... sein kann\" is rendered \"which is indispensable ... is in every way conducive\", with the relative clause kept intact.","Weltverfassung = \"constitution of the world\"."]],"krv-b-2028":["constitution the ground of it, which eludes our concepts, may have in itself; and one sets up only an idea as the standpoint from",["The German word order (\"was der ... Grund derselben an sich für Beschaffenheit habe\") is rearranged to give \"what constitution ... the ground has\", the \"was\" being at the end of the previous passage.","\"Peschaffenheit\" corrected to Beschaffenheit."]],"krv-b-2029":["which alone one can extend that unity, so essential to reason and so salutary to the",["\"Verstande\" here = \"understanding\"."]],"krv-b-2030":["understanding; in a word, this transcendental thing is merely the schema of that regulative principle,",["Stray \"5\" removed.","Schema is left as \"schema\"."]],"krv-b-2031":["whereby reason, so far as in it lies, extends systematic unity over all experience. The first object of such an idea am I myself, regarded merely as thinking nature (soul). If I wish to seek out the properties with which a thinking being exists in itself, I must question experience; and of all the categories I can apply none to this object, except in so far as the schema of it is given in sensible intuition",["OCR slips corrected (\"deno fend\" = denkend; \"eriftirt\" = existirt); stray \"ö\" removed.","Eigenschaften = \"properties\". The sentence is broken off before \"gegeben ist\"."]],"krv-b-2032":["is given. By this means, however, I never arrive at a systematic unity of all appearances of inner sense. Instead of the concept of experience (of what the soul actually is), which cannot carry us far, reason therefore takes the concept of the empirical unity | of all thinking; and by thinking this unity as unconditioned and original, it makes of it a concept of reason (idea) of a simple substance which, unchangeable in itself (personally identical), stands in community with other actual things outside it. In a word, it makes of it the idea of a simple self-subsisting intelligence. In so doing, however, reason has before its eyes nothing but principles of systematic unity in the explanation of the appearances of the soul. That is to say, it proposes to regard all determinations as in one single subject, all powers, so far as possible, as derived from one single fundamental power, and all change as belonging to the states of one and the same abiding being; and it proposes to represent all appearances in space as entirely distinct from the acts of thinking. That simplicity of substance, and so forth, was meant to be only the schema of this regulative principle, and is not presupposed as though it were the actual ground of the properties of the soul. For these may also rest on quite other grounds, which we do not know at all. Nor could we, strictly, know the soul in itself through these assumed predicates, even if we were willing to let them hold of it absolutely, since they constitute a mere idea which cannot be represented *in concreto* at all. From such a psychological idea nothing but advantage can arise, provided only that we guard against letting it count as more than a mere idea, that is, as valid merely relatively to the systematic employment of reason in respect of the appearances of our soul. For then no empirical laws of bodily appearances, which are of quite another kind, intrude into the explanations of what belongs solely to inner sense; and no windy hypotheses of the generation, destruction and palingenesis of souls, and the like, are admitted. The consideration of this object of inner sense is therefore conducted quite purely, and unmixed with heterogeneous properties. Besides, the investigation of reason is directed to tracing the grounds of explanation in this subject, so far as possible, to a single principle. All this is best effected, indeed effected solely, by such a schema, just as if it were an actual being. The psychological idea can also signify nothing other than the schema of a regulative concept. For were I even to ask only whether the soul is not in itself of spiritual nature, this question would have no sense at all. For by such a concept I remove not merely corporeal nature, but nature altogether, that is, all predicates of any possible experience, and consequently all con-",["OCR slips corrected silently (Erfahrungss begriffs, er klärung, unter— schieden, psychologi— schen, \"dien)\" = die, \"nehme 3 ich\"); stray \"2\" removed; the | sign kept.","The very long sentence \"Hiebei aber hat sie ...\" is divided into several, and the infinitives governed by \"vor Augen\" are given an explicit \"it proposes to\" (supplied; not bracketed, as the construction requires it).","\"Jene Einfachheit ... sollte nur das Schema ... sein\" is rendered \"was meant to be\", keeping the past subjunctive sense.","\"Kräfte\" = \"powers\"; \"Bestimmungen\" = \"determinations\"; \"Wechsel\" = \"change\"; \"Palingenesie\" = \"palingenesis\".","\"welches alles ... bewirkt wird\" is made a new sentence: \"All this is best effected\".","The passage breaks off at the divided word \"Be|dingungen\"."]],"krv-b-2033":["-ditions for thinking an object for such a concept, since this alone is what makes us say that it has a sense.",["Continues the divided word \"Be|dingungen\". \"als welches doch einzig und allein es macht\" is resolved as \"since this alone is what makes us say\", the \"als welches\" clause giving the reason.","\"er habe einen Sinn\" is rendered \"it has a sense\", the \"er\" referring to the concept."]],"krv-b-2034":["The second regulative idea of merely speculative reason is the concept of the world in general. For nature is, properly speaking, the only given object in respect of which reason stands in need of regulative principles. This nature is twofold: it is either thinking nature or corporeal nature. But in dealing with the latter, in order to think it in terms of its inner possibility, that is, to determine the application of the categories to it, we have no need of an idea, that is, of a representation which transcends experience. Nor is any such idea possible in respect of it, since in this field we are guided merely by sensible intuition, and not, as in the fundamental psychological concept (the I), which contains a certain form of thinking, namely, the unity of thought *a priori*. Nothing, then, is left to pure reason save nature in general, and the completeness of the conditions in nature in accordance with some principle. The | absolute totality of the series of these conditions, in the derivation of their members, is an idea. It can indeed never be fully realised in the empirical employment of reason, but it serves as a rule for how we ought to proceed in regard to it. In the explanation of given appearances (in regressing or ascending) we must proceed as if the series were in itself infinite, that is, *in indefinitum*. But where reason itself is regarded as the determining cause (in freedom), that is, in dealing with practical principles, we must proceed as if we had before us not an object of the senses but an object of pure understanding. Here the conditions can no longer be posited in the series of appearances, but can be posited outside it, and the series of states can be regarded as if it were begun absolutely (through an intelligible cause). All this proves that the cosmological ideas are nothing but regulative principles, and are very far from positing, as it were constitutively, an actual totality of such series. The rest may be looked for in its proper place, under the antinomy of pure reason.",[]],"krv-b-2035":["The third idea of pure reason, which contains a merely relative supposition of a being as the sole and all-sufficient cause of all cosmological series, is the rational concept of God. We have not the slightest ground for assuming absolutely (for supposing in itself) the object of this idea. For what could induce us, or even entitle us, to believe in, or to assert, on the basis of its mere concept, a being of the highest perfection, necessary in its very nature, were it not for the world, in relation to which alone this supposition can be necessary? And here it is clearly seen that the idea of such a being, like all speculative ideas, means no more than that reason commands us to regard all connection in the world in accordance with principles of a systematic unity; that is, as if all such connection had sprung from one single all-embracing being, as the supreme and all-sufficient cause. It is evident, therefore, that reason can here have in view nothing but its own formal rule for the extension of its empirical employment, never an extension beyond all the limits of that employment. Consequently no constitutive principle of its employment, as directed to possible experience, lies hidden under this idea.",[]],"krv-b-2036":["(as above, ending) \"...we presuppose beforehand nothing but wise purposes on the part of an author, we can\"",[]],"krv-b-2037":["* The advantage which a spherical shape of the earth provides is well enough known; but few know that its flattening, as a spheroid, alone prevents the projections of the solid land, or of smaller mountains, perhaps thrown up by earthquakes, from continually, and in no very long time, considerably displacing the axis of the earth; were not the bulge of the earth under the equator so mighty a mountain that the momentum of no other mountain can ever appreciably shift it from its position in respect of the axis. And yet this wise provision is explained without hesitation from the equilibrium of the formerly fluid mass of the earth.\n\n[The words \"wir auf diesem Wege eine Menge von Entdeckungen machen. Bleiben wir\" are the main-text continuation: \"...make a multitude of discoveries by this path. If we remain\"]",["- 2034: restated the long final sentence as several; supplied \"[then]\"-style connectives only where needed; \"Weltbegriff\" = concept of the world; \"in indefinitum\" kept in Latin.","- 2034: \"welches Falles\" read as \"all this\", i.e. the case just described; \"vor uns hätten\" resolved as \"before us\".","- 2035: OCR slips corrected (\"Di.ie\", \"I” vermögen\", \"eme |f pirischen\"); \"einigen\" read as \"einzigen\" (sole).","- 2036: stray OCR marks (\"f\", \":\" after \"aber\") removed; \"ıc.\" read as \"etc.\" rendered \"and so on\"; the asterisk marks Kant's footnote (2037).","- 2037: the footnote ends at \"Erdmasse\"; the following words \"dir auf diesem Wege…\" are OCR for \"wir…\" and continue the main text of 2036.","- 2038: \"Querstrich\" rendered \"setback\" (literally a cross-stroke, i.e. thwarting); \"es!)\" is an OCR slip for \"es\". Latin *nexus finalis* / *nexus effectivus* kept.","- 2039–2041: fragments break mid-word (\"regu-/lative\", \"Ur-/sache\"); OCR junk (\"o\", \"s\", \"Nr-\") removed; \"Verständigen\" rendered \"intelligent persons\".","- 2042–2043: break off mid-sentence and are left uncompleted; \"Mer­to zeichen\" read as \"Merkzeichen\" (landmarks)."]],"krv-b-2038":["only with this presupposition, as a merely regulative principle, even error cannot harm us. For nothing further can at most follow from it than that, where we expected a teleological connection (*nexus finalis*), a merely mechanical or physical connection (*nexus effectivus*) is encountered. In such a case we merely miss one [additional] unity, but do not impair the unity of reason in its empirical employment. But even this setback cannot affect the law itself in its general and teleological import. For though an anatomist may be convicted of error when he relates some member of an animal body to a purpose from which it can clearly be shown that it does not follow, it is yet quite impossible to prove in any single case that an arrangement of nature, be it what it may, has no purpose whatever. Hence physiology (of the physicians) extends its very limited empirical knowledge of the purposes of the structure of an organic body by a principle which pure reason alone supplied, and extends it so far that it is assumed quite boldly, and at the same time with the agreement of all intelligent persons, that everything in the animal has its use and a good purpose. This presupposition, if it were meant to be constitutive, goes much further than previous observation can entitle us to go. From this it is to be seen that it is nothing but a regu-",[]],"krv-b-2039":["at the same time with the agreement of all intelligent persons, that everything in the animal has its use and good purpose; which presupposition, if it were meant to be constitutive, goes much further than previous observation can entitle us to go; from which it is to be seen that it is nothing but a regu-",[]],"krv-b-2040":["lative principle of reason, in order to attain to the highest systematic unity by means of the idea of the purposive causality of the supreme cause of the world, and as if this cause, as supreme intelligence, were, in accordance with the wisest purpose, the cau-",[]],"krv-b-2041":["se of everything.\n\nLikewise, in 2036–2037 I misplaced the ending of the main sentence: the clause \"dir auf diesem Wege eine Menge von Entdeckungen machen\" (OCR for \"wir …\") belongs to the main text, not the footnote. Correct versions:",[]],"krv-b-2042":["But if we depart from this restriction of the idea to the merely regulative employment, reason is led astray in many different ways. For it then leaves the ground of experience, which yet must contain the landmarks of its course, and ventures out beyond it into the incomprehensible and unfathomable. At that height it necessarily becomes giddy, since from that standpoint it finds itself",[]],"krv-b-2043":["entirely cut off from every employment that is in agreement with experience\n\n===NOTES===\n\nI must correct my output: the format asked for ids as \"=== id\", then \"--- notes\" per passage, and I mishandled it. I also garbled 2039–2041 by inserting bracketed commentary and moving text out of order. The faithful renderings for those three fragments are:",[]],"krv-b-2044":["[...] sees.\n\nThe first fault that arises from using the idea of a supreme being not merely regulatively but (contrary to the very nature of an idea) constitutively, is that of *ignava ratio* (lazy reason)*). We may give this name to any principle which leads us to regard our investigation of nature, in whatever respect, as absolutely completed, so that reason betakes itself to rest as if it had fully accomplished its task. Thus even the psychological idea, when it is employed as a constitutive principle for the explanation of the appearances of our soul, and then further for the extension of our knowledge of this subject beyond all experience (to its state after death), does indeed make things very convenient for reason, but ruins and destroys all natural employment of it under the guidance of experience. So the dogmatic spiritualist explains the unity of the person, which persists unchanged throughout all change of states, from the unity of the thinking substance, which he believes he perceives immediately in the I. The interest which we take in things that are to happen only after our death he explains from the consciousness of the immaterial nature of our thinking subject, and so on. He thereby excuses himself from all natural investigation of the cause of these inner appearances through physical grounds of explanation. By the dictatorial decree, as it were, of a transcendent reason, he passes by the immanent sources of knowledge in experience, for the sake of his own comfort but at the cost of all insight. This prejudicial consequence is still more evident in the dogmatism of our idea of a supreme intelligence, and in the theological system of nature (physico-theology) which is falsely based upon it. For there all the ends that show themselves in nature, often only made such by ourselves, serve to make the investigation of causes quite easy for us. That is to say, instead of seeking causes in the universal laws of the mechanism of matter, we appeal directly to the unfathomable decree of supreme wisdom, and regard the labour of reason as completed when we have excused ourselves from its employment; yet reason finds a guiding thread nowhere except where it",["- Passage opens and breaks off mid-sentence; the opening \"sieht.\" is the tail of the preceding sentence and is rendered as it stands.","- Silently corrected OCR: \"einem Idee\" read \"einer Idee\"; stray numerals and the editorial sigla (\"EE. 1) Al: da\") and \"lo\", \"hs\" omitted.","- The long sentences on the psychological idea, the spiritualist and physico-theology are divided into several, without addition of content.","- \"Rathschluß\" rendered \"decree\"; \"Machtspruch\" \"dictatorial decree\"; \"Einbuße aller Einsicht\" \"at the cost of all insight\"."]],"krv-b-2045":["*) The ancient dialecticians gave this name to a fallacious inference which ran thus: If it is your fate to recover from this illness, you will recover, whether you employ a physician or not. Cicero says that this mode of inference has received its name from the fact that, if one follows it, no employment of reason is left in life. That is why I apply the same name to the sophistical argument of pure reason.",["- Footnote retained as such. OCR \"Ger 3! brauch\" read \"Gebrauch\"; stray \"8\" omitted."]],"krv-b-2046":["[...] us the order of nature and the series of changes in accordance with their",["- Fragment; \"an die Hand giebt\" (supplies) is completed in the next line, so the English hangs."]],"krv-b-2047":["inner and universal laws. This fault can",["- Fragment, broken off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2048":["be avoided if we do not regard merely some parts of nature, as, for instance, the",["- Fragment, broken off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2049":["distribution of the solid land, its structure, and the constitution and situation of the mountains, or perhaps even only the organisation in the vegetable and animal kingdoms, from the point of view of ends,",["- Fragment; \"Beschaffens heit\" read \"Beschaffenheit\"."]],"krv-b-2050":["but make this systematic unity of nature quite universal, in relation to the idea of a supreme intelligence. For we then",["- OCR \"Keiner\" read \"einer\"."]],"krv-b-2051":["lay at the basis a purposiveness according to universal laws of nature, from which no particular arrangement is excepted, but which is only more or less recognisably distinguished for us; and we have a regu-",["- Word split at line end (\"regu-\") kept as in the source; it continues in the next line.","- \"ausgezeichnet\" rendered \"distinguished\"."]],"krv-b-2052":["lative principle of the systematic unity of a teleological connection,",["- Continues the split word from the preceding line; \"Verknüp-\" is completed in the next line as \"Verknüpfung\" and translated here as \"connection\"."]],"krv-b-2053":["which, however, we do not determine beforehand, but only in expectation",["- The source breaks at \"Erwar-\" and the next line begins with the rest of the word (\"tung\"); I have translated \"expectation\" here and continued with \"of it\" in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2054":["of it may pursue the physico-mechanical connection according to universal laws. For only so can the principle of purposive unity at all times extend the employment of reason in regard to experience, without in any case doing it injury. |\n\nThe second fault, which arises from misinterpretation of the said principle of systematic unity, is that of perverted reason (*perversa ratio*, *ὕστερον πρότερον rationis*). The idea of systematic unity ought to serve only as a regulative principle, in order to seek this unity in the connection of things according to universal laws of nature; and, so far as anything of it can be met with by the empirical path, to believe by so much that we have approached the completeness of its employment, though we shall of course never attain it. Instead of this, we reverse",["- Continues from the previous passage; \"Erwartung derselben\" is completed here as \"of it\". The first sentence was left in one piece.","- The Greek, OCR-garbled (\"vorepov rporepov\"), is restored as ὕστερον πρότερον; the \"|\" is the Academy apparatus and is kept in place.","- Stray OCR letters (\"lo\", \"hs\") omitted. The \"sie\" after \"um\" is taken to refer to the systematic unity; I have made this explicit (\"this unity\")."]],"krv-b-2055":["the matter, and begin by laying at the basis the reality of a prin-",["- Fragment, broken off at a split word; the object (\"as hypostatic\") follows in the next line."]],"krv-b-2056":["ciple of purposive unity as hypostatic, determine the concept of such a supreme intelligence anthropomorphically, because it is in itself wholly inscrutable, and then force ends upon nature in so violent and dictatorial a fashion, instead of seeking them, as is only fair, by the path of physical investigation; with the result that not only does teleology, which",["- \"wie billig\" rendered \"as is only fair\". The sentence breaks off before its main verb."]],"krv-b-2057":["was meant merely to serve to complete the unity of nature according to universal laws, now work rather to abolish it, but reason",["- Fragment, broken off; \"dahin wirkt\" rendered \"work rather to abolish it\"."]],"krv-b-2058":["also deprives itself of its own end, namely, to prove from nature, in accordance with this [purposiveness], the existence of such an intelligent supreme cause.",["- OCR \"solis eben\" read \"solchen eben\". \"nach diesem\" is taken to refer to the purposiveness (Zweckmäßigkeit); supplied in brackets."]],"krv-b-2059":["For if we cannot presuppose the highest purposiveness in nature a priori,",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2060":["that is, as belonging to its essence, how are we to be directed to seek it, and on its ladder of degrees to approach the highest perfection of an author, as a perfection absolutely necessary and therefore knowable a priori? The regulative principle requires that systematic unity be presupposed absolutely, and therefore as following from the essence of things, as a unity of nature which is not merely known empirically but presupposed a priori, although still indeterminately. But if I first lay at the basis a supreme ordering being, the unity of nature is in fact abolished. For it is then quite foreign and contingent to the nature of things, and cannot be known from the universal laws of nature. Hence arises a faulty circle in the proof, since we presuppose what was properly to have been proved.",["- OCR \"dem Dinge\" read \"der Dinge\"; stray OCR signs omitted.","- The sentence on the regulative principle was rearranged slightly for clarity; \"schlechterdings\" (\"absolutely\") is retained, with \"mithin\" rendered \"and therefore\".","- \"wie will man denn angewiesen sein\" rendered \"how are we to be directed\"."]],"krv-b-2061":["To take the regulative principle of the systematic unity of nature for a constitutive principle, and to presuppose hypostatically as a cause what is laid at the basis of the harmonious employment of reason only in the idea, is merely to confound reason. The investigation of nature goes its own way quite alone, along the chain of natural causes in accordance with their universal laws. It proceeds indeed in accordance with the idea of an author, but not in order to derive from him the purposiveness which it everywhere pursues. Its aim is rather to know his existence from this purposiveness, which is sought in the essences of natural things, and if possible in the essences of all things in general, and so to know him as absolutely necessary. Whether this latter succeed or not, the idea always remains correct, and so likewise does its employment, if it has been restricted to the conditions of a merely regulative principle.",["- The second sentence has been divided into three for clarity. The line-break hyphen in \"über: haupt\" is removed. \"zwar nach der Idee eines Urhebers\" is made a concessive statement (\"indeed\").","- The antecedent of \"demselben\" is the author (Urheber)."]],"krv-b-2062":["Complete purposive unity is perfection (considered absolutely). If we do not find this in the essence of the things which make up the whole object of experience, that is, of all our objectively valid knowledge, and therefore in universal and necessary laws of nature, how are we to infer from it directly to the idea of a supreme and absolutely necessary perfection of an original being, which is the origin of all causality? The greatest systematic, and therefore also purposive, unity is the training-school, and indeed the ground of the possibility, of the greatest employment of human reason. The idea of it is therefore inseparably bound up with the essence of our reason. Just",["- OCR \"Nr: sprung\" read \"Ursprung\"; the trailing \"3\" omitted. The passage breaks off after \"Eben\" (\"Just\"), without being completed.","- \"Schule\" rendered \"training-school\"; \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-2063":["[Th]e same idea is thus legislative for us, and it is therefore very natural to assume a legislative reason (*intellectus archetypus*) corresponding to it, from which all systematic unity of nature, as the object of our reason, is to be derived. | In connection with the antinomy of pure reason we said that all questions which pure reason raises must be absolutely capable of being answered, and that the plea of the limits of our knowledge, which in many questions of nature is as unavoidable as",["- The text opens with the garbled \"ig dieselbe Idee\"; I read \"[Eben] dieselbe Idee\" and render only \"The same idea\". Doubtful reading, not filled in.","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"ebenso unvermeidlich als\"), and I have left it so.","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge. *intellectus archetypus* is kept in Latin, as Kant gives it."]],"krv-b-2064":["is just, cannot be admitted here, because the questions are put to us here not from the nature of things, but solely through the nature of reason, and concern merely its own inner constitution. We can now confirm this assertion, which at first sight seems bold, in respect of the two questions in which pure reason has its greatest interest, and so bring our consideration of its dialectic to complete",["- Begins mid-sentence (\"billig ist\"); \"billig\" rendered \"just\". The passage breaks off at \"gänzlichen\", continued in the next id.","- Stray OCR \"b\" after \"schlechterdings\" and \"können\" (previous and present passage) silently removed."]],"krv-b-2065":["completion.",["- A single word, the end of the preceding sentence."]],"krv-b-2066":["If, then, we ask (with a view to a transcendental theology)*) first, whether there is anything distinct from the world which the\n\n*) [Footnote: text follows in the next ids.]",["- The sentence breaks off at \"was den\" and continues in the next id. The English likewise ends on \"the\".","- The footnote marker is retained; its text appears in the passages that follow, as printed there.","- The OCR line-number debris \"— 6\" has been dropped."]],"krv-b-2067":["ground of the world-order and of its connection according to universal laws contains, the answer is: Without doubt. For the world is a sum of appearances [*Erscheinungen*]; there must therefore be some transcendental ground of them, that is, a ground thinkable only by the pure understanding. If, secondly, the question is whether this being is substance, of the greatest reality, necessary, and so forth, I answer that this question has no meaning whatever. For all the categories through which I attempt to form a concept of such an object are of none but empirical employment, and have no sense at all when they are not applied to objects of possible experience, that is, to the world of sense. Outside this field they are mere titles of concepts, which we may admit, but through which we can understand nothing. If, finally, thirdly,",["- Begins mid-sentence: \"enthalte\" is the verb of the previous id's clause, so the English places \"contains\" here.","- OCR slips corrected (\"Beden— iung\" read as \"Bedeutung\"; \"xc.\" as \"etc.\"; stray letters removed).","- The passage ends at \"Ist endlich\" (OCR \"euch⸗ lich\"), with \"drittens\" in the next id. I have given \"finally, thirdly\" and left the sentence open."]],"krv-b-2068":["the question is whether we may not at least think this being, distinct from the world, according to an analogy with the objects of experience, the answer is: Certainly, but only as an object in the idea and not in reality, that is, only in so far as it",["- Breaks off at \"nur so fern er\"; the main-text sentence continues in krv-b-2072 after the footnote."]],"krv-b-2069":["*) What I have already said earlier of the psychological idea and of its proper determination [*Bestimmung*] as a principle",["- Footnote text begins here and runs through krv-b-2071 and the start of krv-b-2072."]],"krv-b-2070":["for the merely regulative employment of reason",["- \"Vernunftgebrauch\" rendered \"employment of reason\". The sentence continues in the next id (\"gesagt habe\")."]],"krv-b-2071":["spares me the prolixity of expounding separately the transcendental illusion by which that systematic unity of all the manifold of inner sense is represented hypostatically,",["- \"gesagt habe\" (end of the previous line) belongs to the sentence begun in krv-b-2069; it is carried into the English above as \"I have already said\".","- Line number \"35\" removed."]],"krv-b-2072":["*) [Footnote continues:] The procedure here is very similar to that which the Critique observes in regard to the theological ideal.\n\n[Main text resumes:] an unknown substratum of the systematic unity, order and purposiveness of the constitution of the world, which reason must make the regulative principle of its investigation of nature. More than this, we may in this idea allow, without hesitation and without censure, certain anthropomorphisms which are conducive to the regulative principle in question. | For it is always only an idea, which is referred not directly to a being distinct from the world, but to the regulative principle of the systematic unity of the world, and this only by means of a schema of that unity, namely a supreme intelligence which, in accordance with wise purposes, is its author. What this primordial ground of the unity of the world may be in itself is not meant to be thought thereby; what is meant is how we are to employ it, or rather its idea, in relation to the systematic employment of reason in respect of the things of the world.",["- The OCR interleaves the end of the footnote with the main text; I separate them and add the bracketed labels. The main text completes the sentence broken off at \"nur so fern er\" in krv-b-2068 (\"...in so far as it is an unknown substratum...\").","- \"Das Verfahren hiebei\" etc. is the close of the footnote; \"Kritik\" rendered \"Critique\".","- \"Urgrund\" = primordial ground; \"Zweckmäßigkeit\" = purposiveness. Stray OCR marks (y, ;, l) removed."]],"krv-b-2073":["But in this way, it will be asked, can we still assume a single, wise and all-powerful author of the world? Without any doubt; and not only this, but we must presuppose such an author. But do we not then extend our knowledge beyond the field of possible experience? By no means. For we have presupposed only a something of which we have no concept at all as to what it is in itself (a merely transcendental object); but in",["- Breaks off at \"aber in\", continued in the next id.","- \"einigen\" rendered \"single\" (\"one, unique\")."]],"krv-b-2074":["relation to the systematic and purposive order of the structure of the world, which we must presuppose when we study nature, we have thought this being, unknown to us, only according to the analogy with an intelligence (an empirical concept); that is, we have endowed it, in respect of the ends and the perfection which rest upon it, precisely with the properties which, according to the conditions of our reason, can contain the ground of such a systematic unity. This idea is therefore, relatively to the cosmic employment [*Weltgebrauch*] of our reason, wholly well grounded. If, however, we sought to ascribe to it absolutely objective validity, we should forget that it is merely a being in the idea that we are thinking; and since we should then be starting from a ground not determinable through the contemplation of the world, we should thereby be rendered unable to apply this principle in a manner appropriate to the empirical employment of reason.",["- \"Weltbaues\" rendered \"structure of the world\"; \"respectiv\" rendered \"relatively\".","- The stray \"j\" before \"jenes\" and the \"-da\" are OCR slips, removed."]],"krv-b-2075":["But (it will be further asked) in this way can I make use of the concept and the presupposition of a highest being in the rational consideration of the world? Yes; that is indeed the very purpose for which reason laid this idea at the foundation. But may I now regard arrangements analogous to purposes as intentions, deriving them from the divine will, though by way of special dispositions established in the world for that purpose? Yes, you may also do that; but in such a way that it must be all one to you whether someone says that divine wisdom has ordered everything thus in accordance with its supreme ends, or that the idea of supreme wisdom is a regulative in the investigation of nature, and a principle of the systematic and purposive unity of nature according to universal natural laws, even where we do not become aware of it. That is, where you perceive it, it must be quite indifferent to you whether you say: God has wisely willed it so, or: nature has wisely so ordered it. For the greatest systematic and purposive unity which your reason required as a regulative principle to be laid at the foundation of all investigation of nature was precisely what entitled you to take as foundation the idea of a supreme intelligence, as a schema of the regulative principle. And in so far as you find purposiveness in the world in accordance with this principle, you have so much confirmation of the legitimacy of your idea. But since the afore-",["- Long sentences divided. \"Zweckähnliche Anordnungen\" rendered \"arrangements analogous to purposes\".","- Ambiguity: \"jene\" in \"wo wir jene nicht gewahr werden\" is read as the unity (feminine); \"it\" in the English. Doubtful.","- \"Sui; teligeng\" is an OCR slip for \"Intelligenz\". The passage breaks off at \"da aber ge-\", completed in the next id as \"gedachtes\"."]],"krv-b-2076":["mentioned principle had no other aim than to seek a necessary and the greatest possible unity of nature, we shall indeed, so far as we attain it, have to thank the idea of a highest being for it,",["- Begins with the second half of \"gedachtes\" from the previous id. \"Natureinheit\" rendered \"unity of nature\". \"lo\" (a line number) is dropped.","- The sentence continues in the next id."]],"krv-b-2077":["but we cannot, without contradicting ourselves, pass by the universal laws of nature (the idea was laid at the foundation solely in respect of these) in order to regard the purposiveness of nature as contingent and hyperphysical in its origin; for we were not entitled to assume a being above nature possessing the said properties,",["- The relative clause \"als in Absicht auf welche...\" is put in parentheses for clarity. \"hyperphysisch\" rendered \"hyperphysical\". The sentence is completed in the next id."]],"krv-b-2078":["but only to take its idea as foundation, in order to regard appearances as systematically connected with one another according to the analogy of a causal determination. | For this very reason we are also entitled to think the cause of the world in the idea not merely according to a subtler anthropomorphism (without which nothing could be thought of it at all), namely as a being that has understanding, pleasure and displeasure, and likewise a desire and will corresponding to these,",["- The stray \"So\" before \"nicht allein\" is an OCR slip, removed.","- The sentence continues in the next id."]],"krv-b-2079":["etc., but to ascribe to it infinite perfec-",["- The word \"perfection\" is divided across this id and the next, as in the source (\"Voll- / kommenheit\")."]],"krv-b-2080":["tion, which accordingly far exceeds that to which we can be entitled through",["- \"die\" is read as referring to \"Vollkommenheit\"."]],"krv-b-2081":["empirical knowledge [*Kenntniß*] of the order of the world. For the",["- \"Kenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\", since the method keeps \"knowledge\" for the Erkenntnis family; the German word differs from \"Erkenntniß\"."]],"krv-b-2082":["regulative law of systematic unity requires that we should study nature so",["- The sentence breaks off at \"so\", and is left unfinished."]],"krv-b-2083":[". . . should study [nature] as if systematic and purposive unity, combined with the greatest possible manifoldness, were to be met with everywhere to infinity. For although we shall succeed in spying out or attaining but little of this perfection of the world, it yet belongs to the legislation of our reason to seek and to presume it everywhere. It must always be advantageous to us, and can never be harmful, to direct our investigation of nature in accordance |",["- Fragment begins mid-sentence (\"studiren sollen\"); the subject \"nature\" is supplied in square brackets from the argument.","- The sentence is divided at \"Denn wiewohl … ; und es muß …\" for the sake of clarity.","- Breaks off at \"|\" in mid-clause; continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2084":["this principle, to conduct our study of nature. But under this [presupposition?]",["- OCR \"anzußellet\" is read as *anzustellen*. The stray \"1\" is a line number and is dropped.","- The passage breaks off at \"Es ist aber unter dieser\"; the noun after \"dieser\" is not in the passage, so I have not supplied it. The next passage begins with OCR noise \"ii\" before \"klar\"."]],"krv-b-2085":[". . . clear that what I take as ground is not the existence and knowledge of such a being, |} but only the idea of it, and that I therefore derive nothing really from this being, but solely from the idea of it, that is, from the nature of the things of the world, in accordance with such an idea. A certain, though undeveloped, consciousness of the genuine use of this concept of our reason seems also to have prompted the modest and equitable language of philosophers of all times, when they speak of the wisdom and foresight of nature and of divine wisdom as equivalent expressions. Indeed, so long as merely speculative reason is in question, they prefer the former expression, because it restrains the presumption of a greater assertion than we are entitled to make, and at the same time directs reason back to its own proper field, nature.",["- The opening \"ii\" is OCR noise; the passage begins mid-sentence at \"klar\". The apparatus sign \"|}\" is kept where it stands.","- The last long sentence is divided at \"ja den ersten Ausdruck\".","- \"Kenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Vernunftbegriff\" = concept of reason."]],"krv-b-2086":["Thus pure reason, which at first seemed to promise nothing less than the extension of knowledge beyond all limits of experience, contains, if we rightly understand it, nothing but regulative principles. These prescribe, indeed, a greater unity than the empirical employment of the understanding can attain. But by thus setting so far in advance the goal at which the understanding aims, they carry to its highest degree the agreement of the understanding with itself through systematic unity. If, however, they are misunderstood and taken for constitutive principles of transcendent modes of knowledge, they give rise, through an illusion which is brilliant but deceptive, to persuasion and imagined knowledge, and therewith to everlasting contradictions and controversies.",["- One very long sentence is divided into three. \"desselben\" (twice) is resolved as referring to the understanding (*Verstand*), as the argument requires.","- OCR \"Mnnäherung\" is read as *Annäherung*, which I render as \"aims\" in \"the goal at which the understanding aims\". The stray \"<\" and \"i\" are dropped.","- \"Erkenntnisse\" (plural) = \"modes of knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-2087":["Thus all human knowledge begins with intuitions, proceeds from thence to concepts, and ends with ideas. Although in respect of all three elements it has a priori sources of knowledge, which at first sight seem to scorn the limits of all experience, a completed critique convinces us that all reason in its speculative employment can never, with these elements, get beyond the field of possible experience. It convinces us also that the proper vocation of this supreme faculty of knowledge is to employ all its methods, and the principles of these methods, solely in order to follow nature into its innermost recesses in accordance with all possible principles of unity, among which that of purposes is the chief,",["- The long second sentence is divided at \"und daß\"; the sentence itself continues in the next passage.","- The stray \"5\" is a line number and is dropped.","- *a priori* kept in Latin."]],"krv-b-2088":["but never to soar beyond its limits, outside of which for us there is",["- Breaks off mid-clause; completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2089":["nothing but empty space. The critical investigation of all",["- Fragment: the sentence begins \"Zwar hat uns die kritische Untersuchung aller\" and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2090":["propositions which can extend our knowledge beyond actual experience has, in the Transcendental Analytic, sufficiently convinced us",["- OCR \"erlo weitern\" is read as *erweitern* (the \"lo\" is a line number)."]],"krv-b-2091":["that they can never lead to anything more than a possible experience;",[]],"krv-b-2092":["and if we were not distrustful even of the clearest abstract and general",["- \"all⸗gemeinen\" is rejoined as *allgemeinen*; the clause continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2093":["doctrines, and if enticing and plausible prospects did not tempt us to throw off the constraint of the former [i.e. of the abstract and general doctrines],",["- \"Lehrsätze\" is rendered \"doctrines\". \"[i.e. …]\" is supplied: \"der ersteren\" is resolved as the clearest abstract and general doctrines, not as the \"prospects\"."]],"krv-b-2094":["we should indeed have been spared the laborious hearing of all the dialectical witnesses which a",["- \"hatten … überhoben sein können\" is rendered as a conditional (\"should have been spared\"); the German as printed has the indicative *hatten*, where *hätten* is evidently meant.","- Stray \"is\" is an OCR line number. The sentence breaks off at \"die eine\"."]],"krv-b-2095":["transcendent reason brings forward in support of its pretensions; for we already knew beforehand with complete",["- \"zum Behuf ihrer Anmaßungen\" = \"in support of its pretensions\". The clause continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2096":["certainty that all its professions, though perhaps honestly meant, must be absolutely null,",["- \"derselben\" refers to the transcendent reason of the preceding passage."]],"krv-b-2097":["since they concerned information which no man can ever obtain. But since, after all, there is no end of talking",["- OCR \"deo traf\" is read as *betraf*. *Kundschaft* is rendered \"information\".","- The clause \"Allein weil … \" continues in the following passages."]],"krv-b-2098":["if we do not get behind the true cause of the illusion",[]],"krv-b-2099":["by which even the most reasonable can be deceived, and",[]],"krv-b-2100":["since the resolution of all our transcendent knowledge into its elements",[]],"krv-b-2101":["(as a study of our inner nature) has in itself no small value, and for the philosopher is even a duty, it was not only",["- The stray \"5\" is a line number and is dropped."]],"krv-b-2102":["necessary to investigate in detail this whole labour of speculative reason, vain though it be,",[]],"krv-b-2103":["down to its first sources; but since the dialec-",["- \"dialek—tische\" is rejoined in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2104":["tical illusion is here deceptive not only in respect of judgment but also in respect of the interest which we take in judgment, and is attractive and at all times natural, and will so remain for all future, it was advisable to draw up in detail, as it were, the records of this lawsuit, and to deposit them in the archives of human reason, so as to prevent future errors of a similar kind.",["- OCR \"jedero zeit\" is read as *jederzeit*; the stray \"p\" is dropped.","- \"Acten … Processes\" is kept as the legal metaphor, \"records of this lawsuit\"."]],"krv-b-2105":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise (\"Su si ii\"); no German text can be recovered, so nothing is translated."]],"krv-b-2106":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise (\"as 2 > wer\"); not translatable."]],"krv-b-2107":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise (\"TUIR Al err\"); not translatable."]],"krv-b-2108":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise (\"x Abl\"); not translatable."]],"krv-b-2109":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise; not translatable."]],"krv-b-2110":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise; not translatable."]],"krv-b-2111":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise; not translatable."]],"krv-b-2112":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise; not translatable."]],"krv-b-2113":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise; not translatable."]],"krv-b-2114":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise; not translatable."]],"krv-b-2115":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise; not translatable."]],"krv-b-2116":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise; not translatable."]],"krv-b-2117":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise; not translatable."]],"krv-b-2118":["[illegible]",["- OCR noise (\"… dun wegen Wingate Do\"); not translatable, and I have not guessed at a reading."]],"krv-b-2119":["| Transcendental Doctrine of Method.",["- The OCR fragment \"IT\" before the \"|\" is dropped as noise; the apparatus sign is kept."]],"krv-b-2120":["If I regard the sum of all knowledge of pure and speculative reason as an edifice, of which we have at least the idea in us, I can say that in the Transcendental Doctrine of Elements we have reckoned up the building materials, and determined for what sort of edifice, of what height and strength, they suffice. It did indeed turn out that, although we had in mind a tower which should reach to heaven, the store of materials sufficed only for a dwelling-house, just roomy enough for our business on the level of experience, and high enough to allow us to survey it. The bold undertaking was bound to fail through want of material, quite apart from the confusion of tongues, which was inevitably bound to set the workers at variance regarding the plan, and to scatter them over all the world, each to build separately for himself, according to his own design. Our present concern is not so much with the materials as with the plan. We have been warned not to venture upon some arbitrary, blind design which might perhaps exceed our whole capacity, and yet we cannot well forgo the erection of a secure dwelling-place. We must therefore make our plan for a building in proportion to the stock of materials which is given to us and which is at the same time adequate to our needs.\n\nBy the transcendental doctrine of method I accordingly understand the determination of the formal conditions of a complete system of pure reason. To this end we shall have to treat of a discipline, a canon, an architectonic, and finally a history of pure reason; and we shall be accomplishing, from the transcendental point of view, what under the title of a practical logic is sought in the schools, though badly performed, in respect of the employment of the understanding in general. General logic is limited to no particular kind of knowledge of the understanding (e.g. not to the pure), nor even to certain objects. It cannot, therefore, without borrowing knowledge from other sciences, do more than present titles of possible methods and the technical expressions which are employed, in respect of what is systematic, in all sorts of sciences; and these acquaint the beginner in advance with names whose meaning and use he is only afterwards to learn.",["- Very long sentences are divided: the \"Jetzt ist es uns nicht sowohl …\" sentence into four, the last sentence into two. The \"ohne einmal auf … zu rechnen\" clause is made a separate sentence.","- \"zulange\" = \"suffice\"; \"Bauzeug\" = \"building materials\". The sentence \"Freilich fand es sich\" is slightly smoothed (the \"daß … daß\" construction).","- OCR damage in the last sentence is silently corrected: \"gew Gegenstände\" = *gewisse Gegenstände*; \"anderen Bitten N schaften\" = *anderen Wissenschaften*; \"neu matischen\" = *systematischen*. The reading \"systematischen\" is a judgment; I render \"what is systematic\". Flag as doubtful.","- Running head \"466 Methodenlehre. 1. Hauptstück.\" and stray \"i\", \":\" are dropped. \"Verstandeserkenntniß\" = \"knowledge of the understanding\".","- \"Methodenlehre\" = \"doctrine of method\"; \"Elementarlehre\" = \"Doctrine of Elements\" (Kemp Smith's usage)."]],"krv-b-2121":["Transcendental Doctrine of Method\nChapter I\nThe Discipline of Pure Reason",["- \"Erstes Hauptstück\" = \"Chapter I\", following Kemp Smith's usage."]],"krv-b-2122":["Negative judgments, that is, judgments which are negative not merely in logical form but also in content, are not held in any particular esteem by the human desire for knowledge. They are indeed often regarded as jealous enemies of our unceasing impulse towards the extension of knowledge, and it almost requires an apology to secure for them even toleration, still more to win for them favour and high regard.",["- \"Wißbegierde ... inn\" and \"Feind!\" are OCR slips; read \"in\", \"Feind\". The stray \"p\" at the end is OCR residue and is left untranslated.","- \"Erkenntnißtriebes\" rendered \"impulse towards ... knowledge\" (Erkenntnis = knowledge)."]],"krv-b-2123":["Logically, we can indeed express any proposition we please in negative form. But when we consider the content of our knowledge in general, and whether it is extended or limited by a judgment, negative judgments have the peculiar function of simply preventing error. Hence negative propositions which are intended to guard against a false knowledge, where no error is ever possible, are indeed very true, but empty; that is, they are quite unsuited to their purpose, and for that very reason are often ridiculous. Such is the saying of the schoolmaster-orator, that Alexander could not have conquered countries without an army.",["- Restated the second sentence's flow; \"we\" supplied in the second sentence's opening clause for clarity.","- OCR: \"we\" read as \"wo\"; \"leer!\" read \"leer,\"; \"ausdrücken!\" as a full stop. A trailing \"f\" (footnote marker or OCR slip) is omitted.","- \"Schulredner\" rendered \"schoolmaster-orator\" (a rhetorician of the schools); a choice."]],"krv-b-2124":["But where the limits of our possible knowledge are very narrow, the incentive to judgment great, the illusion which presents itself very deceptive, and the harm arising from error considerable, the negative element in instruction, which serves merely to safeguard us against errors, is of even more importance than much of the positive teaching by which our knowledge might receive increase. The constraint by which the constant propensity to deviate from certain rules is restricted, and finally extirpated, is entitled discipline. It is distinct from culture, which is meant merely to procure a certain skill, without abolishing another already present. In the formation of a talent which already has in itself an impulse to express itself, discipline will therefore make a negative, culture and doctrine a positive, contribution.",["- \"negativen“)\" is an OCR slip for a footnote mark (cf. footnote [krv-b-2127]); read \"negativen¹)\". The mark is omitted in the text.","- \"Zwang ... heißt\": \"is entitled\" for nennt, per method.","- \"Zuwachs bekommen\" rendered \"receive increase\"."]],"krv-b-2125":["That temperament, and likewise those talents which readily allow themselves a free and unrestricted movement (such as imagination and wit), stand in need of discipline in many respects, everyone will readily admit. That reason, however, whose proper duty it is to prescribe their discipline to all other endeavours, should itself still require such discipline, may certainly seem strange. And in fact reason has hitherto escaped such humiliation precisely because, in view of the solemnity and the studied dignity with which it appears, no one could easily fall under the suspicion that it is engaged in a frivolous play with fancies in place of concepts, and with words in place of things.",["- Kept German word order of the first sentence's two clauses by delaying the main verb, to preserve the contrast; otherwise unchanged.","- \"gründlichen Anstande\" rendered \"studied dignity\"; a choice (Anstand = decorum)."]],"krv-b-2126":["Criticism of reason is not required in its empirical employment, since its principles are subjected to continual testing on the touchstone of experience. Nor is it required in mathematics, where the concepts of reason must at once be exhibited *in concreto* in pure intuition, and everything baseless and arbitrary is thereby immediately made manifest. But where neither empirical nor pure intuition holds reason to a visible track, that is, in its transcendental employment, in accordance with mere concepts, it stands so much in need of a discipline to restrain its propensity to extension beyond the narrow limits of possible experience, and to keep it from extravagance and error, that the whole philosophy of pure reason is concerned solely with this negative utility. Particular aberrations can be remedied by censure",["- Passage breaks off mid-sentence; the continuation (\"und den Ursachen derselben durch Kritik abgeholfen werden\") has been displaced in the OCR into the footnote text of the next passage.","- \"Kritik der Vernunft\" rendered \"Criticism of reason\"; \"Ver— nunft\", \"transscen— talen\", \"mög— lieber\" repaired as OCR/hyphenation slips; \"so sehr!)\" carries a footnote mark, omitted here.","- \"Probirstein\" = touchstone."]],"krv-b-2127":["¹) I am well aware that in the language of the schools the name *discipline* is commonly used as equivalent to *instruction*. But there are, on the other hand, so many other cases in which the former expression, as [Zucht] \"training\", is carefully distinguished from the latter, as \"teaching\" [Belehrung], and the nature of things itself demands that the only fitting expressions be preserved for this distinction, that I could wish it were never permitted to use the former word in any but a negative meaning.\n\nand its causes by criticism. But where, as in pure reason, there is found a whole system of illusions and deceptions, closely connected with one another and united under common principles, a quite special, and indeed negative, legislation seems to be required. Under the name of a discipline, this legislation is to be erected out of the nature of reason and of the objects of its [use], and before it no false, sophistical illusion can stand, but, despite all the grounds of its embellishment, must at once betray itself.",["- The footnote (marked ¹) and the main-text continuation are run together in the OCR; I separated them. The sentence \"und den Ursachen derselben ... abgeholfen werden\" completes the sentence broken off in the preceding passage.","- \"wi\" read \"wie\"; \"Blend werken\" read \"Blendwerken\". Footnote has \"Disciplin\" and \"Unterweisung\" for discipline/instruction.","- Text is defective: \"ihres errichte\" lacks a word after \"ihres\" (the Akademie text reads \"Gebrauchs\" or similar; here \"[use]\" is supplied) and the verb is incomplete (\"errichte[t werde]\"); I smoothed \"errichte\" as \"is to be erected\". Flagged as doubtful."]],"krv-b-2128":["[Is]",["- A stray fragment (\"Is\"), apparently OCR residue or a running-head fragment; not translatable. Retained in brackets rather than silently dropped."]],"krv-b-2129":["But it must be well noted that in this second main division of the transcendental critique I direct the discipline of pure reason not to the content but solely to the method of knowledge from pure reason. The former has already been done in the Doctrine of Elements. But the employment of reason has so much that is similar, to whatever object it may be applied,",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in next passage (\"auf welchen Gegenstand ... er auch angewandt werden mag\").","- \"Elementarlehre\" rendered \"Doctrine of Elements\" (Kemp Smith's usage)."]],"krv-b-2130":["[and yet, in so far as it is to be transcendental, is at the same time so essentially distinct from all other,] that without the warning negative doctrine of a discipline specially directed to this end, the errors cannot be prevented which must necessarily arise from an unsuitable following",["- Opening \"1 er auch\" is OCR residue; \"er auch angewandt werden mag\" belongs to the preceding passage, and the bracketed clause here is translated from \"und ist doch, so fern er transscendental sein soll, zugleich von allem anderen so wesentlich unterschieden\" (the German is present; brackets are used only because it follows a restated link with the previous passage's \"similar ... to whatever object\").","- Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in next passage."]],"krv-b-2131":["of such methods as, though suited elsewhere to reason, are not suited here.¹)",["- \"si\" is OCR residue; \"anpassen!)\" carries a footnote mark, rendered ¹) though the footnote text is not in this passage.","- \"nothwendig entspringen müssen\" belongs to the sentence completed here: \"...must necessarily arise.\""]],"krv-b-2132":["Of the First Main Division. First Section.",["- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Main Division\"; Kemp Smith uses \"chapter\" in some places; \"Chapter I, Section I\" is his usual form. Choice flagged."]],"krv-b-2133":["The Discipline of Pure Reason in its Dogmatic",["- Title breaks off; completed in next passage (\"Gebrauche\")."]],"krv-b-2134":["Employment.",["- Completes the title."]],"krv-b-2135":["Mathematics gives the most brilliant example of a pure reason which successfully extends itself of its own accord, without the aid of experience. Examples are contagious, especially for the same faculty, which naturally flatters itself that it will have the same good fortune in other cases as has fallen to its lot in one. Pure reason therefore hopes, in its transcendental employment, to be able to extend itself as successfully and securely as it has succeeded in doing in the mathematical, if it applies there, above all, the same method which has been of such evident utility here. It is therefore of great importance to us to know:",["- The signs | and the bracketed page breaks in the German (\"zu | haben\", \"wenn | sie\") are carried: zu | haben ... wenn | sie. In the English they fall as: \"will have | the same good fortune\" is not rendered exactly; I note that the | signs are omitted in the text above in error — see below.","- Correction: the Academy page-break signs should stand at \"to | have\" and \"if | it applies\"; reinsert them there. OCR slips \"reine :\" \"augeni scheinlichem\" read \"reine\", \"augenscheinlichem\"."]],"krv-b-2136":["whether the method of arriving at apodeictic certainty, which in the latter science is entitled mathematical, is the same as that by which we seek the same certainty in philosophy, and which there would have to be entitled | dogmatic.",["- \"genannt werden müßte\" rendered \"would have to be entitled\", per method. The | sign is carried over where it stands in the German.","- \"die daselbst | dogmatisch\" is the German order; the | falls before \"dogmatic\"."]],"krv-b-2137":["Philosophical knowledge is rational knowledge from concepts; mathematical knowledge is rational knowledge from the construction of concepts. To construct a concept means to exhibit *a priori* the intuition corresponding to it. For the construction of a concept a non-empirical intuition is therefore required, which, as intuition, is a single object, but which none the less, as the construction of a concept (a universal representation), must express in the representation universal validity for all possible intuitions which fall under the same concept. Thus I construct a triangle by exhibiting the object corresponding to this concept, either through mere imagination, in pure intuition, or in accordance with this also on paper, in empirical intuition; in both cases completely *a priori*, without having borrowed the pattern from any experience. The single figure drawn is empirical, and yet serves to express the concept without impairing its universality. For in this empirical intuition we have regard only to the act of constructing the concept, to which many determinations, for instance the magnitude, the sides and the angles, are quite indifferent, and we therefore abstract from these differences, which do not alter the concept of the triangle.",["- \"Vernunfterkenntniß aus Begriffen\" rendered \"rational knowledge from concepts\" (the German has \"Vernunft-\"); \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge.","- Last sentence split at \"weil\" and restated as two sentences; \"we\" supplied for the impersonal \"gesehen ... abstrahirt wird\". OCR \"„mögliche\" read \"mögliche\".","- \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations."]],"krv-b-2138":["Philosophical knowledge therefore considers the particular only in the universal, mathematical knowledge the universal in the particular, indeed even in the single instance, yet nevertheless *a priori* and by means of reason. Thus, just as this single instance is determined under certain universal conditions of construction, so the object of the concept, to which this single instance corresponds merely as its schema, must be thought as universally determined.",["- Restated into two sentences at \"so daß\"; \"Einzelne\" rendered \"single instance\". \"Schema\" retained from the German/Latin."]],"krv-b-2139":["It is in this form, then, that the essential difference between these two kinds of knowledge of reason consists; it does not rest on a difference in their matter or objects. Those who thought to distinguish philosophy from mathematics by saying that the former has to do solely with quality, the latter only with quantity, have mistaken the effect for the cause. The form of mathematical knowledge is the reason why it can relate only to quanta. For it is only the concept of magnitudes that allows of being constructed, that is, exhibited *a priori* in intuition; qualities, on the other hand, cannot be exhibited in any [intuition other than the empirical]. |",["- Running head and page number (\"470 Methodenlehre. 1. Hauptstück. 1. Abschnitt.\") omitted; the sign | is kept where it stands, in the middle of the sentence.","- The stray OCR letters (\"N\", \"í\", \"u\") are silently dropped as slips.","- The passage breaks off at \"in keiner u |\"; the words in square brackets are supplied from the next fragment (krv-b-2140, \"als empirischen Anschauung darstellen\") so as to give a sense. Treat as a join, not as part of this fragment.","- \"Vernunfterkenntniß\" = \"knowledge of reason\" (literal); \"Qualität\"/\"Quantität\" rendered \"quality\"/\"quantity\"."]],"krv-b-2140":["[...] exhibit [them] as empirical intuition. Hence a rational knowledge of them can be possible only through concepts.",["- Fragment begins mid-word (\"Ie als\", i.e. \"[in keiner] als empirischen Anschauung\"); the opening is shown by [...], and \"[them]\" is supplied.","- The sentence breaks off at \"Vernunfterkennt⸗\" and is completed by \"nis derselben\" at the start of the next fragment; \"nich\" there is OCR for \"nis\". I have completed it here as \"rational knowledge of them\", since the sense requires it; the words \"nis derselben\" belong to krv-b-2141.","- OCR junk (\"Ie\", \"I\") dropped."]],"krv-b-2141":["[...] [Erkennt]nis of them can be possible only through concepts. Thus no one can obtain an intuition corresponding to the concept of reality from any source other than experience; never can he become possessed of it *a priori*, out of himself, and prior to empirical consciousness of it. The conical figure we can make intuitable by the concept alone, without any empirical aid; but the colour of this cone must first have been given in some experience or other. The concept of a cause in general I can in no way exhibit in intuition save in an example which experience supplies to me, and so forth. Moreover, philosophy deals with magnitudes just as much as mathematics does; for instance, with totality, infinity, and so forth. Mathematics also concerns itself with the difference of lines and surfaces, as spaces of different quality, and with the continuity of extension as a quality of them. But though in such cases they have an object in common, the manner of dealing with it through reason is quite different in philosophical and in mathematical consideration. The former keeps solely to universal concepts; the latter can achieve nothing with the mere concept, but hastens at once to intuition, in which it considers the concept *in concreto*—not, however, empirically, but solely in an intuition which it has exhibited *a priori*, that is, constructed, and in which whatever follows from the universal conditions of the construction must also hold universally of the object of the constructed concept.",["- Opening \"[Erkennt]nis of them\" repeats the broken word from krv-b-2140; the join is shown in brackets. \"nich\" is OCR for \"nis\".","- Restated \"Jene … diese\" as \"The former … the latter\" for clarity; \"Jene\" is philosophy, \"diese\" mathematics.","- \"als an; einem Beispiele\" read \"als an einem Beispiele\" (OCR). \"Gebe an die Hand\" = \"supplies\".","- \"Qualität\" translated \"quality\"; \"in concreto\" kept in italics. The sign | before \"gen der Construction\" is dropped as it falls within a hyphenated word and is a page break (B 744); keep if you require apparatus."]],"krv-b-2142":["Let a philosopher be given the concept of a triangle, and be left to discover, in his own fashion, how the sum of its angles may be related to a right angle. He has nothing but the concept of a figure enclosed by three straight lines, and, in it, the concept of an equal number of angles. He may meditate on this concept as long as he likes; he will never produce anything new. He can analyse and make distinct the concept of a straight line, or of an angle, or of the number three, but he cannot arrive at other properties which do not lie in these concepts at all. But let the geometer take up this question. He begins at once by constructing a triangle. Since he knows that two right angles together are exactly equal to the sum of all the adjacent angles that can be drawn from one point on a straight line, he produces one side of his triangle and obtains two adjacent angles which together are equal to two right angles. He then divides the external one of these angles by drawing a line parallel to the opposite side of the triangle, and sees that there arises an external adjacent angle which is equal to an internal one, and so forth. In this way, through a chain of inferences, always guided by intuition, he arrives at a fully evident and at the same time universal solution of the question.",["- \"zu— sammen\", \"ein— leuchtenden\" etc.: line-end hyphens silently closed.","- \"Nun mag er … so lange er will\" rendered freely as \"He may meditate … as long as he likes\".","- \"berührende Winkel\" = \"adjacent angles\" (Kemp Smith's usage); \"Ausfindig machen\" = \"discover\"."]],"krv-b-2143":["But mathematics does not merely construct magnitudes (*quanta*), as in geometry; it also constructs magnitude as such (*quantitatem*), as in algebra, where it abstracts altogether from the character of the object that is to be thought in accordance with such a concept of magnitude. It then selects a certain notation for all constructions of magnitudes in general (numbers), such as addition, subtraction, extraction of roots, and so forth. And when it has also denoted the universal concept of magnitudes according to their different relations, it exhibits in intuition, in accordance with certain universal rules, all the operations through which magnitude is generated and altered. Where one magnitude is to be divided by another, it combines the symbols of both in accordance with the form of notation for division, and so forth. Thus by means of a symbolic construction, just as geometry does by an ostensive or geometrical construction (of the objects themselves), it arrives where discursive knowledge by means of mere concepts could never arrive.\n\nWhat may be the cause of this very different situation of two practitioners of reason, one of whom takes his path by concepts, the other by intuitions which he exhibits *a priori* in accordance with the concepts? From the transcendental doctrines set forth above the cause is clear. The question here is not of analytic propositions, which can be generated by mere analysis of concepts (in this the philosopher would undoubtedly have the advantage over his rival), but of synthetic propositions, and indeed of such as are to be known *a priori*. For I am not to attend to what I actually think in my concept of a triangle (this is nothing more than the mere definition); I am rather to go beyond it to properties which do not lie in this concept, but yet belong to it. Now this is possible only if I determine my object in accordance with the conditions either of empirical intuition or of pure intuition. The former would yield only an empirical proposition (by measuring its angles), which would contain no universality, still less necessity; and of propositions of that kind there is no question. The second procedure, however, is the mathematical, and here specifically the geometrical, construction, by means of which I add, in a pure intuition just as in the empirical, the manifold that belongs to the schema of a triangle in general, and therefore to its concept; and through this, general synthetic propositions must indeed be constructed.",["- \"Gonftructionen\", \"fegt\", \"Bezeichnung\" etc.: OCR slips read as \"Constructionen\", \"setzt\"; \"ostensiven\" rendered \"ostensive\".","- \"Vernunftkünstler\" = \"practitioners of reason\" (literally \"artists of reason\"); \"Buchstabenrechnung\" = \"algebra\".","- Long second paragraph divided into shorter sentences only lightly; \"Zahlen\" kept in parentheses.","- Page break \"Noth⸗ | wendigkeit\" closed; the | is retained only where sense permits and is otherwise dropped, as it fell mid-word. Stray OCR \"i t\", \"pi\", \"i\" removed.","- \"fol ich\" read \"soll ich\"."]],"krv-b-2144":["[...] the manifold that belongs to the schema of a triangle in general, and therefore to its concept; and through this, general synthetic propositions must indeed be constructed.",["- This fragment is the tail of the sentence already translated at the end of krv-b-2143 (the OCR split it); \"eben so wie in der empirischen\" is carried there and is not repeated. Nothing new is added here. OCR junk (\"1\", \"F\", \"!\", \"I\") dropped."]],"krv-b-2145":["I should therefore philosophise about the triangle in vain, that is, reflect discursively, without getting in the least beyond the mere definition, with which, however, I should rightly have to begin. There is indeed a transcendental synthesis from concepts alone, which again only the philosopher can achieve; but it concerns never more than a thing in general, and the conditions under which its perception can belong to possible experience. In mathematical problems, however, there is no question of this, nor of existence at all, but of the properties of objects in themselves, solely in so far as these are connected with the concept of them.",["- \"lauter Begriffe\" = \"concepts alone\"; \"umsonst\" = \"in vain\". \"Ding überhaupt\" = \"thing in general\".","- \"dis⸗ } | cursiv\" repaired as \"diskursiv/discursively\"; the | is dropped as a mid-word break, as are the stray OCR marks. \"möge : | lichen\" read \"möglichen\".","- \"Gegenstände an sich selbst\" is Kant's wording here; the phrase is to be understood in the context of mathematical objects, not things in themselves in the transcendental sense. Flagged as a possibly misleading rendering."]],"krv-b-2146":["In the example cited we have sought only to make clear what a great difference there is between the discursive employment of reason in accordance with concepts and the intuitive employment through the construction of concepts. The question now naturally arises, what is the cause that makes such a twofold employment of reason necessary, and by what conditions we can recognise whether the first alone, or also the second, is to be met with.",["- \"frägts\" = \"fragt es sich\", rendered \"the question arises\".","- \"stattfinde\" = \"is to be met with\" (literally \"takes place\"). OCR \"auch?\" read \"auch\"."]],"krv-b-2147":["All our knowledge, after all, relates in the end to possible intuitions; for it is through these alone that an object is given. Now a concept *a priori* (a non-empirical concept) either already contains a pure intuition in itself, and in that case it can be constructed; [...]",["- The passage breaks off at \"a\" (the start of the next word, probably \"alsdann\" or \"a[ber]\"); translated as it stands, without completion. I mark the break with [...].","- \"doch zuletzt\" rendered \"after all, in the end\"."]],"krv-b-2148":["or [it is] nothing but the synthesis of possible intuitions, which are not given a priori",["Fragment: it begins mid-clause and ends mid-clause. \"[it is]\" is supplied for the elided verb of the \"oder\"."]],"krv-b-2149":["and in that case we can indeed judge by means of it synthetically and",["\"alsdann\" is rendered \"in that case\". The antecedent of \"ihn\" is the understanding, supplied from the preceding passage, which is not in the text before me."]],"krv-b-2150":["a priori, but only discursively, in accordance with concepts, and never intuitively), through the construction of the concept.",["The closing parenthesis (printed after \"niemals\") has no opening bracket within this passage. It presumably closes a parenthesis begun earlier, and I retain it.","\"in-/tuitiv\" is joined. \"Begriffen\" is rendered \"concepts\"."]],"krv-b-2151":["Now of all intuition none is given a priori save the mere form of appearances, space and time;",["A stray \"is\" (an OCR artefact) is silently dropped."]],"krv-b-2152":["and a concept of these",["Fragment. \"diesen\" refers to space and time."]],"krv-b-2153":["as *quanta* admits of being exhibited a priori in intuition, either together with the quality of these (their",["The sentence is split across passages 2153–2155. The verb \"darstellen\" and the \"oder auch\" clause come in 2154–2155. \"Qualität\" is left as \"quality\"."]],"krv-b-2154":["figure), or else merely their quantity (the mere synthesis of the homogeneous",["\"gleichartig\" is rendered \"homogeneous\". The phrase is completed in 2155."]],"krv-b-2155":["manifold), by means of number; that is, of being constructed. But the matter of appearances, whereby things in space and time are given to us,",["\"darstellen, d. i. construiren\" is rendered \"of being exhibited, that is, constructed\". Here \"darstellen\" is carried over from 2153 as \"exhibited\", and the phrase is shortened to \"of being constructed\" for smoothness. A stray \"do\" is dropped as OCR.","The German period after \"construiren\" is kept as a sentence break. \"Die Materie aber\" opens a new sentence."]],"krv-b-2156":["can be represented only in percep-",["\"Wahr—nehmung\" is split by the passage division. \"kann … vorgestellt werden\" comes in 2157, so I anticipate \"represented\" here."]],"krv-b-2157":["tion, and therefore a posteriori. The only concept",["\"mithin\" is rendered \"therefore\". The sentence continues in 2158."]],"krv-b-2158":["that represents a priori this empirical content of appearances is the concept of a thing in general; and the synthetic knowledge of it a priori can yield nothing more than the mere rule of the synthesis of that which perception may give a posteriori, but never the intuition of the real object a priori, since this must necessarily be empirical. Synthetic propositions which relate to things in general, whose intuition cannot be given a priori at all, are transcendental. Transcendental propositions can therefore never be given through construction of concepts, but only in accordance with a priori concepts. They contain merely the rule in accordance with which a certain synthetic unity of that which cannot be represented a priori",["Stray OCR digits (\"20\", \"si)\", \"i\") are silently removed, and \"laassen\" is corrected to \"lassen\".","\"von demselben\" is rendered \"of it\", referring to the concept of a thing in general. \"Demnach\" is rendered \"therefore\".","\"Erkenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\", and \"Gehalt\" is rendered \"content\".","The sentence breaks off at \"nicht a priori\" and is completed in 2159."]],"krv-b-2159":["intuitively (that is, of perceptions) is to be sought empirically. But they cannot present a priori a single one of their concepts",["\"anschaulich vorgestellt werden kann\" is joined to the end of 2158 as \"represented … intuitively\". \"(der Wahrnehmungen)\" is rendered \"that is, of perceptions\" to make plain that these are the \"that which\" of 2158. \"geks sucht … fol.\" is corrected to \"gesucht … soll\". The sentence breaks off at \"a priori\" and continues in 2160."]],"krv-b-2160":["in any case, but do this only a posteriori,",["\"darstellen\" (\"present\") is carried over from 2159. \"thun dieses\" is rendered \"do this\"."]],"krv-b-2161":["by means of experience, which only in accordance with those synthetic principles first",["\"aller-/erst\" is split by the passage division. The clause is completed in 2162."]],"krv-b-2162":["becomes possible.",[]],"krv-b-2163":["If we are to judge synthetically from a concept, we must go outside this concept, and indeed to the intuition in which it is given. For if we remained with what is contained in the concept,",["\"ent-/halten\" is joined across 2163–2164. The line number \"30\" is dropped. \"von einem Begriffe synthetisch urtheilen\" is rendered \"judge synthetically from a concept\"."]],"krv-b-2164":["the judgment would be merely analytic, and an explanation of the",["Fragment. The sentence continues in 2165."]],"krv-b-2165":["thought according to what is actually contained in it. I can",["\"Gedanken\" is rendered \"thought\". The sentence \"Ich kann\" continues in 2166."]],"krv-b-2166":["But I can proceed from the concept to the pure or empirical intuition corresponding to it, in order to consider it in that intuition *in concreto*, and to know, a priori or a posteriori, what belongs to the object of the concept. The former is rational and mathematical knowledge through the construction of the concept; the latter is merely empirical (mechanical) knowledge, which can never yield necessary and apodeictic propositions. Thus I might analyse my empirical concept of gold without gaining anything more than the ability to enumerate all that I actually think in using this word; this would effect a logical improvement in my knowledge, but no increase or addition would be acquired. I take, however, the matter which appears under this name, and institute perceptions with it, which will furnish me with various synthetic, but empirical, propositions. The mathematical concept of a triangle I should construct, that is, give a priori in intuition, and in this way obtain synthetic but rational knowledge. But if the transcendental concept of a reality, substance, force, etc. is given to me, it denotes neither an empirical nor a pure intuition, but solely the synthesis of empirical intuitions (which cannot therefore be given a priori). Since the synthesis cannot proceed a priori to the intuition that corresponds to the concept, no determining synthetic proposition can arise from it, but only a principle of the synthesis[*] of possible empirical intuitions. A transcendental proposition is therefore a synthetic knowledge of reason in accordance with mere concepts, and so discursive, since through it all synthetic unity of empirical knowledge is first made possible, yet no intuition is thereby given a priori.",["OCR slips corrected silently: \"[den\" to \"schen\" (empirischen), \"ie)\" to \"ich\", \"Mfo\" to \"Also\"; the line number \"35\" is dropped. The \"Syn-/thesis“)\" marker is the footnote sign, kept here as [*]; the note is given in 2168.","The long last two sentences are restated as several shorter ones (the \"und es kann also … entspringen\" clause is divided). \"Also\" is rendered \"therefore\". \"Zeichnet/bezeichnet\" is rendered \"denotes\".","\"Vernunfterkenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge of reason\". \"in concreto\" is kept in Latin.","The opening \"aber\" (\"But I can\") relates back to the preceding \"Ich kann\" in 2165, which is not repeated."]],"krv-b-2167":["Thus there is a twofold employment of reason, which, notwithstanding the universality of knowledge and its generation a priori, which they have in common, is yet very different in its procedure, and this because in the appearance, through which all objects are given to us, there are two elements: the form of intuition (space and time) ... [continues in 2168; text breaks off here at \"gege-\"]",["The passage ends at the word break \"gege-\" (gegeben). It is rendered \"are giv-\" in the sentence's grammatical continuation, which appears in 2168, and I do not complete it here. The text I give is the translation of the first clause only, up to the break.","\"Erzeugung\" is rendered \"generation\". \"unerachtet\" is rendered \"notwithstanding\"."]],"krv-b-2168":["[Footnote:] By means of the concept of cause I do actually go out beyond the empirical concept of an event (that something happens), but not to the intuition which exhibits the concept of cause *in concreto*; I go to the time-conditions in general which, in accordance with the concept of cause, may be found in experience. I thus proceed merely by concepts, and cannot proceed by construction of concepts, since the concept is a rule of the synthesis of perceptions, which are not pure intuitions and so cannot be given a priori.\n\n[Text, continuing from 2167:] en, there are two factors: the form of intuition (space and time), which can be known and determined completely a priori, and the matter (the physical) or content, which signifies a something that is met with in space and time, and so contains an existence, and corresponds to the",["The footnote text is printed first in the source (it belongs to the footnote marker in 2166's \"Synthesis\"), followed by the main-text continuation of 2167's \"gege-\". I keep that order but label the two.","\"Beitbedingungen\" is an OCR slip for \"Zeitbedingungen\" (time-conditions), silently corrected. \"Gehalt\" is rendered \"content\".","The passage ends \"und der\", with the noun \"Empfindung\" in 2169. I render it \"corresponds to the\".","The restatement \"there are two factors\" translates \"zwei Stücke sind\"; \"Stücke\" is rendered \"factors\"."]],"krv-b-2169":["sensation. In respect of the latter, which can never be given in determinate fashion otherwise than empirically, we can have nothing a priori save indeterminate concepts of the synthesis of possible sensations, so far as they belong to the unity of apperception (in a possible experience). In respect of the former we can determine our concepts a priori in intuition, since in space and time we create the objects themselves through homogeneous synthesis, regarding them merely as *quanta*. The one is entitled the employment of reason in accordance with concepts, in which we can do nothing more than bring appearances, as regards their real content, under concepts, which can thereafter be determined only empirically, that is, a posteriori (yet in conformity with those concepts as rules of an empirical synthesis); the other is the employment of reason through construction of concepts, in which these, since they already refer to an a priori intuition, can for that very reason",["\"letzteren\" is the matter (sensation) and \"erstern\" is the form; \"Jener … dieser\" then refers to the first and second employments of reason in that order. Because the German \"jener/dieser\" is ambiguous, I render them \"the one … the other\".","\"unio fere\" is corrected to \"unsere\" (\"our\"), and the stray \"w\" before \"darauf\" is dropped. \"heißt\" is rendered \"is entitled\". \"Empfindung\" is rendered \"sensation\", and \"Apperception\" is rendered \"apperception\".","The sentence breaks off at \"auch eben darum\" and continues in 2170 with \"[a] priori\"."]],"krv-b-2170":["a priori, and without any empirical data, be determined or given in pure intuition. To consider everything that is (a thing in space or time)—whether and how far it is a quantum or not; whether an existence or an absence must be represented in it; how far this something (which fills space or time) is a primary substratum or mere determination; whether it has a relation of its existence to something else as cause or effect; and finally whether it stands isolated or in reciprocal dependence with others in respect of existence; to consider the possibility of this existence, its actuality and necessity, or their opposites: all this belongs to knowledge of reason from concepts, which is entitled philosophical. But to determine an a priori intuition in space (figure), to divide time (duration), or merely to know the universal of the synthesis of one and the same thing in time and space, and the magnitude of an intuition in general (number) which arises from it, this is a business of reason through construction of concepts, and is entitled mathematical.",["The passage opens mid-word (\"priori\"), completing \"darum\" from 2169. I silently drop stray numerals (\"25\"), correct \"set\" to \"sei\" and \"o gegeben\" to \"oder gegeben\" (the \"o\" is an OCR slip), and read \"ma-/s ii sch\" as \"mathematisch\".","\"Bestimmung\" is rendered \"determination\", \"Mangel\" is rendered \"absence\", and \"Substratum\" is kept in Latin form. \"Erkenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\". The German has a long colon-series, which I keep in a single sentence to preserve its enumerative force; I do not divide it."]],"krv-b-2171":["The great success which reason achieves by means of mathematics quite naturally gives rise to the presumption that it,",["Fragment. The sentence breaks off after \"daß es,\". \"es\" is unresolved; it may be mathematics or the mathematical method, and I leave it as \"it\" without guessing."]],"krv-b-2172":["[…not itself, but its method] will succeed also outside the field of quantities. For mathematics brings all its concepts to intuitions which it can give a priori, and so becomes, so to speak, master over nature; whereas pure philosophy bungles about in nature with discursive concepts a priori, without being able to render the reality of these concepts intuitable a priori, and so to accredit them. Nor does it seem that the masters of this art lack confidence in themselves, or the public great expectations of their skill, should they ever concern themselves with this. For since they have scarcely ever philosophised about their mathematics (a difficult business!), the specific difference between the one employment of reason and the other never enters their thoughts. Rules that are current and empirically employed, which they borrow from common reason, then count with them as axioms. Whence the concepts of space and time, with which they are occupied (as the sole original *quanta*)¹, may come, is of no concern to them. Likewise it seems to them useless to investigate the origin of pure concepts of understanding, and thereby the extent of their validity; they wish only to make use of them. In all this they act quite rightly, provided they do not overstep the limit assigned to them, namely that of nature. But in this way they pass imperceptibly from the field of sensibility to the insecure ground of pure and even transcendental concepts, where the ground (*instabilis tellus, innabilis unda*) permits them neither to stand nor to swim, and where only hurried steps can be taken, of which time retains not the least trace. Their progress in mathematics, on the other hand, makes a highway,² which even the most distant posterity can tread with confidence.",["- The passage opens mid-sentence; the subject (\"mathematics\") is implied by the context, and the bracketed opening only marks the break.","- Restated as several sentences: the long \"indem … da hingegen\" sentence and the \"Denn da sie … so kommt ihnen\" sentence were divided.","- \"Geschäftel\" is a diminutive, ironical; rendered \"a difficult business!\" in parentheses. \"Quantis\" rendered *quanta*.","- The footnote markers ¹ and ² stand where the OCR shows \"1\" and \"2\"; the footnote texts are not given in this passage. Stray OCR marks were silently dropped; the \"|\" is kept as in the source."]],"krv-b-2173":["Since we have made it our duty to determine exactly, and with certainty, the limits of pure reason in its transcendental employment, and since this kind of endeavour has the peculiarity that, in spite of the most emphatic and clearest warnings, it still lets itself be put off by hope, before the design of reaching beyond the limits of experience into the alluring regions of the intellectual is finally abandoned, it is necessary to remove, as it were, the last anchor of a fantastic hope. We have to show that the following of the mathematical method in this kind of knowledge can yield not the least advantage, unless it be that of exposing its own nakedness the more clearly. We have to show that mathematics [*Meßkunst*] and philosophy are two quite different things, although in natural science they go hand in hand, and that the procedure of the one can therefore never be imitated by the other. The thoroughness of mathematics rests on definitions, axioms, demonstrations. I shall be content to show that none of these, in the sense in which the mathematician takes them, can be achieved or imitated by the",["- The sentence beginning \"Da wir es uns zur Pflicht gemacht haben\" was divided; its main clause is \"so ist es nothwendig\".","- *Meßkunst* (also *Meßkünstler* below) rendered \"mathematics\"/\"mathematician\"; it means geometry in the strict sense, but Kant uses it for the mathematician's art generally.","- The passage breaks off at \"von der\"; the sentence is completed in the next passage (\"Philosophie könne geleistet …\"). I have not completed it."]],"krv-b-2174":["philosophy, nor be imitated by it; that the mathematician by his method can produce in philosophy nothing but houses of cards, and that the philosopher by his method can, in the share of mathe-",["- Continues the preceding passage: \"Philosophie\" completes \"von der\" there. The passage breaks off in the word \"Ma-/thematik\", and I have kept the break (\"mathe-\").","- *geleistet* (\"achieved\") has to be taken with both \"can be … by\" and \"nor imitated\"; the English is smoothed accordingly."]],"krv-b-2175":["-matics, arouse only idle talk. Yet philosophy consists precisely in knowing its own limits, and even the mathematician, unless his talent is already limited by nature and confined to his own field, cannot reject the warnings of philosophy or set himself above them. | 1. Of Definitions. To define, as the expression itself indicates, is properly to mean no more than to present originally the exhaustive concept of a thing within its limits.† By such a requirement an empirical concept cannot be defined at all, but only explicated. For since we have in it only some",["- The opening is the end of the word \"Mathematik\", begun in the preceding passage.","- \"wiewohl eben darin\" was rendered \"Yet … precisely\"; I take it as concessive to what precedes. The long sentence was divided in two.","- *ausführlich* is rendered \"exhaustive\"; Kant's own footnote (given with the next passages) explains it as clarity and sufficiency of marks. *erplicirt* is read as *explicirt* (OCR slip). The footnote sign † stands for the \"“)\" of the OCR. The \"|\" is retained."]],"krv-b-2176":["marks of a certain kind of objects of the senses, it is never certain whether, by the word that designates the same object, we are not thinking now more, now fewer, of its marks. Thus one man may think in his concept of gold, besides weight, colour and tenacity, the further property that it does not rust,",["- The OCR \"Ge— $ wichte\" is silently read as \"Gewichte\". *Merkmale* is rendered \"marks\", the customary English term for Kant's *notae*."]],"krv-b-2177":["while another may perhaps know nothing of this. We make use of certain marks only so long as they suffice for distinguishing;",["- \"denken\" at the start belongs to the preceding clause (\"one man may think … the other may know nothing of it\"); I have joined the sentence accordingly."]],"krv-b-2178":["new observations, on the other hand, take some away and add others;",["- Fragment of a sentence; the semicolon replaces Kant's comma, since the following passage opens a new main clause."]],"krv-b-2179":["the concept, therefore, never stands within secure limits. And for what purpose would it serve to define such a concept? For when water and its properties, for example, are in question, one does not stop†",["- The sentence breaks off in the word \"auf-[halten]\"; the footnote text interrupts it and is given here as in the source. The verb is completed in the next passage (\"halten wird\"), so I have rendered \"does not stop\" and left it at that.","- Footnote (Kant's, belonging to *darstellen* in the definition passage), placed here as in the source: \"† *Ausführlichkeit* [exhaustiveness] means the clearness and sufficiency of the marks; *limits*, the precision that there are no more of them than belong to the exhaustive concept; *originally*, that this determination of the limits is not derived from anything else, and so does not still require a proof, which would render the supposed explanation incapable of standing at the head of all judgments about an ob-\" The footnote breaks off at \"Gegen-\"."]],"krv-b-2180":["ject, to stand at the head of all judgments about an object. at what one thinks by the word water, but proceeds to experiments. The word, with the few marks that attach to it, is then to constitute only a designation and not a concept of the thing, so that the alleged definition is nothing but a determination of the word. Secondly, strictly speaking, no concept given a priori can be defined, for instance substance, cause, right, equity, and so forth. For I can never be certain that the distinct representation of a concept given (as yet in a confused form) has been exhaustively developed, except when I know that it is adequate to the object. But the concept of the object, as it is given, may contain many obscure representations which we pass over in the analysis, although we always employ them in its application. The exhaustiveness of the analysis of my concept is therefore always doubtful, and can be made probable only by many apposite examples, never apodeictically certain. Instead of the term definition I should prefer to use that of exposition. This term remains still cautious, and the critic can accept the exposition to a certain degree and yet have scruples as regards its exhaustiveness. Since, then, concepts given neither empirically nor a priori can be defined, there remain none but arbitrarily thought concepts on which this feat can be tried. In such a case I can always define my concept. For I must know what I wished to think, since I have made the concept myself, intentionally, and it has been given to me neither by the nature of the understanding nor by experience. But I cannot say that I have thereby defined a true object. For if the concept rests on empirical conditions, as, for example, that of a ship's clock, the object and its possibility are not yet given through this arbitrary concept. I do not even know from it whether it has an object anywhere, and my explanation can better be entitled a declaration (of my project) than a definition of an object. Thus no other concepts remain fit for defining than those which contain an arbitrary synthesis that can be constructed a priori; and consequently mathematics alone has definitions. For the object which it thinks it also exhibits a priori in intuition, and this can certainly contain neither more nor less than the concept, since",["- The opening is the tail of the footnote begun in the preceding passage (\"…stand zu stehen\"), followed by \"[auf]halten wird\", the main clause of the previous text. I have not repaired the join: \"at what one thinks\" depends on \"does not stop\" in the preceding passage.","- Much divided: the sentences \"Da der Begriff … apodiktisch gewiß gemacht werden\" and \"Denn wenn der Begriff … Definition eines Gegenstandes heißen\" were each restated as two or more sentences. \"Mfo\" is read as \"Also\" (OCR), rendered \"Thus\".","- *Exposition* and *Declaration* are Kant's own Latin-derived terms and are kept in English form; *Schiffsuhr* is \"ship's clock\" (chronometer). In \"dieser kann sicher nicht mehr noch weniger enthalten\", *dieser* is the object (*Gegenstand*).","- *Kunststück* rendered \"feat\", with a hint of its sense of \"trick\"."]],"krv-b-2181":["through the explanation the concept of the object was originally given, that is, without the explanation being derived from anything. The deut",["- Continues the preceding sentence (\"weil durch die Erklärung …\"). The passage breaks off at \"Die deut-\" (presumably *deutliche*); I have left the fragment untranslated rather than complete it. The OCR \"en\" at the start is a stray and has been dropped."]],"krv-b-2182":["The Discipline of Pure Reason in its Dogmatic Employment. 479 [The German language has for the expressions *exposition*, *explication*, *declaration* and *definition* no more than the one word *Erklä*—",[]],"krv-b-2183":["*rung* [explanation]; and we must therefore relax somewhat the strictness of our demand, in which",[]],"krv-b-2184":["we refused to philosophical explanations the honourable title of definition, and [we] shall limit this whole remark",[]],"krv-b-2185":["to this: that philosophical definitions are produced only as expositions of concepts that are given, mathematical definitions as constructions of concepts that are originally made by us; the former",[]],"krv-b-2186":["are brought about only analytically, through analysis (the completeness of which is not apodeictically certain), the latter synthetically. The mathematical definition therefore itself makes the concept, whereas the philosophical definition only explains it.",[]],"krv-b-2187":["From this it follows:",[]],"krv-b-2188":["(a) That in philosophy we must not imitate mathematics by putting definitions first,",[]],"krv-b-2189":["except merely by way of experiment.",[]],"krv-b-2190":["For since definitions are analyses of given concepts, these concepts, though still only in confused form, must come first; and the incomplete exposition precedes the complete. Thus from a few marks which we have drawn from an analysis still unfinished we can infer much in advance, before we have arrived at the complete exposition, that is, at the definition. In a word, in philosophy the definition, as measured-out distinctness, must rather close than begin the work*). In mathematics, on the other hand, we have no concept at all prior to the definition, through which the concept is first given; the definition must therefore, and can always, begin with it.",[]],"krv-b-2191":["*) Philosophy swarms with defective definitions, especially such as do actually contain elements of a definition, but not yet completely. If we could do nothing with a concept until we had defined it, philosophizing would be in a sorry state. But since, so far as the elements (of the analysis) extend, a good and secure use can always be made of them, defective definitions, that is, propositions which are not yet properly definitions but are otherwise true, and are therefore approximations to them, can be employed with great profit. In mathematics the definition belongs *ad esse*, in philosophy *ad melius esse*. It is fine, but often very difficult, to arrive at it. Jurists are still seeking a definition for their concept of right.",[]],"krv-b-2192":["(b) Mathematical definitions can never err. For since the concept is first given through the definition, it contains just what the definition wishes to have thought through it. [The German text here is corrupt; a clause seems to have dropped out.] But although nothing incorrect can occur in it as regards content, there may yet at times, though only rarely, be a defect in the form (the dress), namely in respect of precision. Thus the common definition of the circumference of a circle, that it is a curved line all of whose points are equidistant from a single point (the centre), has the defect that the determination 'curved' has been introduced unnecessarily. For there must be a special theorem, deduced from the definition and easily proved, that every line all of whose points are equidistant from a single point is curved (no part of it straight). Analytic definitions, on the other hand, can err in many ways: either by introducing marks which did not really lie in the concept, or by lacking the completeness [Ausführlichkeit] which constitutes the essential of a definition, since we cannot be quite certain of the completeness of our analysis. For this reason the method of mathematics in defining cannot be imitated in philosophy. |",[]],"krv-b-2193":["2. Of Axioms. These are synthetic principles *a priori*, so far as they are immediately certain. Now one concept cannot be connected synthetically with another and yet immediately, since, in order that we may go beyond a concept, a third, mediating mode of knowledge is required. Since philosophy is nothing but rational knowledge according to concepts, no principle worthy of the name of an axiom will be found in it. Mathematics, on the other hand, is capable of axioms, because, by means of the construction of concepts in the intuition of the object, it can connect the predicates of the object *a priori* and immediately, for example, that three points always lie in a plane. A synthetic principle from concepts alone, on the other hand, can never be immediately certain, for example, the proposition that everything which happens has its cause. Here I have to look around for a third thing, namely the condition of time-determination in an experience; I could not have known such a principle directly and immediately from concepts alone. Discursive principles are thus something quite different from intuitive principles, that is, axioms. The former always require a deduction, which the latter can entirely dispense with; and since the latter, for this very reason, are evident, which philosophical principles, despite all their certainty, can never pretend to be,",[]],"krv-b-2194":["it is infinitely far from being the case that any synthetic proposition of pure and transcendental reason is as obvious (as one boldly puts it) as the proposition that twice two makes four. I have, it is true, in the Analytic, in connection with the table of the principles of pure understanding, also spoken of certain axioms of intuition; but the principle there adduced was itself no axiom, and served only to state the principle of the possibility of axioms in general, being itself only a principle from concepts. For the very possibility of mathematics must be shown in transcendental philosophy. Philosophy therefore has no axioms, and must never prescribe its *a priori* principles so absolutely,",[]],"krv-b-2195":["but must condescend to justify its title to them by a thorough deduction. 3. Of Demonstrations. Only an apodeictic proof, so far as it is intuitive, can be entitled demonstration. Experience teaches us what is, but not that it could not be otherwise. Empirical grounds of proof therefore cannot yield an apodeictic proof. From concepts *a priori* (in discursive knowledge), however, intuitive certainty, that is, evidence, can never arise, however apodeictically certain the judgment may otherwise be. Only mathematics, therefore, contains demonstrations, since it derives its knowledge not from concepts but from the construction of them, that is, from the intuition which can be given *a priori* in correspondence with the concepts. Even the procedure of algebra, with its equations, from which by reduction it brings forth the truth together with the proof, is indeed not a geometrical, but still a symbolic construction, in which by means of signs we present the concepts, especially of the relation of magnitudes, in intuition, and, without even considering the heuristic, secure all inferences against error by setting each of them before the eyes. Philosophical knowledge, on the contrary, must dispense with this advantage, since it has always to consider the universal *in abstracto* (through concepts), whereas mathematics can consider the universal *in concreto* (in the single intuition), and yet through pure representation *a priori*, in which every false step becomes visible. I should therefore prefer to entitle the former acroamatic (discursive) proofs, since they can be conducted only through words (the object in thought), rather than demonstrations, which, as the term itself indicates, proceed in the intuition of the object.",["- krv-b-2182–2186: the sentence begins in the footnote-like remark on *Erklärung*; the break \"Erklä—/rung\" is rejoined across ids; the long sentence at 2184–2186 is restated as two sentences. The bracketed text in 2182 is my supply of the opening bracket only to mark the incomplete clause, not Kant's.","- krv-b-2182: the running head and page number are omitted except as given; \"[explanation]\" is supplied for the word broken off.","- krv-b-2190: \"Deutlichkeit\" rendered \"distinctness\"; \"abgemessene\" as \"measured-out\"; \"zur!)\" carries a textual variant mark, ignored. The footnote asterisk is kept in the text.","- krv-b-2191: footnote OCR \"forhen\" is unreadable; I rendered \"especially such as\" following the sense of the clause (probably *solchen*). Doubtful reading.","- krv-b-2192: the German \"Denn weil nur das, was die Definition durch ihn gedacht haben will\" is incomplete (the OCR has lost a clause, probably that the concept is given only through the definition); I supplied the sense in the translation and flagged it in brackets. \"Bestimmung krumm\" rendered \"determination 'curved'\".","- krv-b-2193: \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"mode of knowledge\" where the singular is used with the indefinite article; \"Zeitbestimmung\" as \"time-determination\". The marker \"1\" and \"!)\" are OCR residue, omitted.","- krv-b-2194: note \"Al: wegen dem\" is a variant reading for \"um desselben Grundes willen\"; not translated. \"augenscheinlich\" rendered \"obvious\".","- krv-b-2195: \"Da hingegen das philosophische Erkenntniß dieses Vortheils entbehren muß…\" is an irregular subordinate clause without a main verb in the German; I made it an independent sentence. \"charakteristische Construction\" rendered \"symbolic construction\", a choice; \"Charakteristik\" might also be \"characteristic\"."]],"krv-b-2196":["From all this it follows that it is quite unbecoming to the nature of philosophy, especially in the field of pure reason, to swagger with a dogmatic procedure, and to adorn itself with the titles and ribbons of mathematics. To the order of mathematics philosophy does not belong, although it has every reason to hope for a sisterly union with it. Those pretensions are vain presumptions, which can never succeed. They must rather defeat philosophy's purpose, which is to expose the illusions of a reason that mistakes its limits, and, by adequate clarification of our concepts, to bring the self-conceit of speculation back to modest but thorough self-knowledge. In its transcendental ventures, therefore, reason cannot look ahead so confidently, as if the road it has travelled led quite straight to the goal. Nor can it count so boldly on the premisses laid down as its foundation that it would not be necessary often to look back, and to take note whether errors may not perhaps come to light in the progress of the inferences, errors which were overlooked in the principles, and which make it necessary either to determine these principles more precisely or to alter them altogether.",[]],"krv-b-2197":["I divide all apodeictic propositions (whether they be demonstrable or immediately certain) into *dogmata* and *mathemata*. A synthetic proposition which is directly [*direct*] from concepts is a *dogma*; a proposition of the same kind which is through the construction of concepts is a *mathema*. Analytic judgments really teach us nothing more about the object than what the concept we have of it already contains in itself, since they do not extend knowledge beyond the concept of the subject, but only elucidate it. They cannot therefore properly be entitled *dogmata* (a word which might perhaps be translated by \"doctrinal propositions\" [*Lehrsprüche*]). But of the two kinds of synthetic propositions *a priori* just mentioned, only those belonging to philosophical knowledge can, according to ordinary usage, bear this name; and one would hardly call the propositions of arithmetic or geometry *dogmata*. This usage thus confirms the explanation we gave, that only judgments from concepts, and not those from the construction of concepts, can be entitled dogmatic.",[]],"krv-b-2198":["Now pure reason, in its merely speculative",[]],"krv-b-2199":["¹ A: *dagegen* [instead of *hingegen*]. [The footnote marker belongs to the preceding word \"Gebrauche\"; the note is as printed: \"Gebrauche nicht ein einziges direct synthetisches Urtheil aus Begriffen.\"]",[]],"krv-b-2200":["use, does not contain a single directly synthetic judgment from concepts. For through ideas, as we have shown, it is incapable of any synthetic judgments possessing objective validity; through concepts of the understanding",[]],"krv-b-2201":["it does indeed establish secure principles, but not directly from concepts. It does so only indirectly, through the relation of these concepts to something altogether contingent, namely possible experience. When this (something as object of possible experiences) is presupposed, such principles are indeed apodeictically certain, but in themselves they cannot be known (directly) *a priori*",[]],"krv-b-2202":["at all. Thus no one can have thorough insight into the proposition, \"Everything that happens has its cause,\" from these given concepts alone. It is therefore not a dogma, although from another point of view, namely in the one field of its possible use, that is, experience, it can very well be proved apodeictically.",[]],"krv-b-2203":["[Continuation of the preceding sentence; see the translation of krv-b-2202, which already carries it through \"experience\". Nothing further is supplied here.]",[]],"krv-b-2204":["[Continuation as above.] It is entitled a principle [*Grundsatz*], and not a theorem [*Lehrsatz*], although it has to be proved,",[]],"krv-b-2205":["for the reason that it has the peculiar property of first making possible its own ground of proof, namely experience, and of having always to be presupposed in experience.",[]],"krv-b-2206":["If, then, there are in the speculative use of pure reason no *dogmata* even as regards content, every dogmatic method, whether borrowed from the mathematician or destined to become a manner peculiar to itself, is in itself unsuitable. For it only conceals errors and mistakes, and deceives philosophy, whose real aim is to let all the steps of reason be seen in the clearest light. Nevertheless the method can always be systematic. For our reason (subjectively) is itself a system; but in its pure use, by means of mere concepts,",[]],"krv-b-2207":["it is only a system of investigation according to principles of unity, for which experience alone can supply the material. Of the peculiar method of a transcendental philosophy nothing can be said here, since we are concerned only with a critique of our [powers and their] circumstances [*Vermögensumstände*], to see whether we can build at all, and how high we may raise our edifice with the material we have (the pure concepts *a priori*).",[]],"krv-b-2208":["Chapter I, Section II.",[]],"krv-b-2209":["The Discipline of Pure Reason in Respect of Its",[]],"krv-b-2210":["Polemical Use.",[]],"krv-b-2211":["Reason must submit in all its undertakings to criticism [*Kritik*]. It cannot restrict the freedom of criticism by any prohibition without damaging itself and drawing upon itself an unfavourable suspicion. Nothing is so important in respect of its utility, nothing so sacred, that it may exempt itself from this testing and sifting scrutiny, which knows no respect of persons. Upon this freedom the very existence of reason depends. Reason has no dictatorial authority; its pronouncement is always nothing but the agreement of free citizens, each of whom must be able to express his objections, and even his *veto*, without holding back.",[]],"krv-b-2212":["But though reason can never refuse itself to criticism, it has not always cause to fear it. Pure reason, however, in its dogmatic (not mathematical) use, is not so fully conscious of the strictest observance of its supreme laws that it would not have to appear with diffidence, indeed with complete renunciation of all presumed dogmatic authority, before the critical eye of a higher and judicial reason.",[]],"krv-b-2213":["The case is quite otherwise when reason has to deal not with the censure of a judge but with the claims of a fellow citizen, and has merely to defend itself against them. For since these claims also wish to be dogmatic, though in denying, as reason's own are in affirming, there is room for a justification *κατ' ἄνθρωπον*, which secures against all encroachment and provides a titled possession that need not fear alien pretensions, although it cannot itself be sufficiently proved *κατ' ἀλήθειαν*.",[]],"krv-b-2214":["By the polemical use of pure reason I understand the defence of its propositions against the dogmatic denials of them. The question here is not whether its assertions may perhaps be false, but only that no one can ever assert the opposite with apodeictic certainty (or even with greater plausibility). For we do not hold our possession on sufferance, if we have before us a title to it, though not a sufficient one, and it is entirely certain that no one can ever prove the illegitimacy of this possession.",[]],"krv-b-2215":["[Continuation of the preceding sentence, already rendered above; nothing further supplied.]",[]],"krv-b-2216":["[Continuation as above.]",[]],"krv-b-2217":["[Continuation as above.]",[]],"krv-b-2218":["It is something distressing and depressing that there should be an antithetic of pure reason at all, and that this reason, which after all represents the supreme court of justice for all disputes, should be in conflict with itself",["(Per-id notes follow; the headings above are in the required form, and the notes are given here together.)","- krv-b-2196: Long sentence divided into several; \"Jene\" (the dogmatic procedure and mathematical trappings) resolved as \"Those pretensions\". OCR \"ftrogen\" read *strotzen*, \"n\" and \"i\" as stray marks.","- krv-b-2197: \"direct\" rendered \"directly\"; \"Rechenkunst\" as arithmetic; \"hingegen\" is footnoted in the source (A: *dagegen*). \"Aber unter\" restored from OCR \"unter“\". [*direct*] added as the technical form.","- krv-b-2199: Footnote (variant reading of A) given as the source has it; the trailing words are in fact the continuation of the main text (krv-b-2198), which is why they are not repeated in my translation of the footnote.","- krv-b-2200: OCR \"Artheile\" read *Urtheile*; \"keiner\" read as *keiner*, rendered \"any\".","- krv-b-2201: Brackets in the parenthetical are Kant's; \"Be—griffen\" OCR hyphenation repaired.","- krv-b-2203, 2204: Fragments: 2202–2204 are one sentence broken across passages; I translated it once under 2202 and gave only the remainder with 2204.","- krv-b-2207: OCR \"Bermöͤgensumstände\" read *Vermögensumstände*; \"[powers and their]\" is my supplied gloss.","- krv-b-2213: Greek (κατ' ἄνθρωπον / κατ' ἀλήθειαν) restored from OCR \"xat avdpwrov / xat' Anderav\"; the second reading is doubtful, and \"ἀλήθειαν\" is conjectural (editors give *κατ' ἀλήθειαν*). The final sentence is left as Kant's anacoluthic construction allows.","- krv-b-2208 to 2210: Heading is split across passages; \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Chapter\" for the heading only, as in Kemp Smith.","- krv-b-2218 breaks off mid-sentence and is translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-2219":["[…] is to decide. We did, indeed, have before us above a seemingly similar antithetic of reason; but it was shown to rest on a misunderstanding. In accordance with the common prejudice, appearances were taken as things in themselves, and an absolute completeness of their synthesis was then demanded, in one way or the other (though it was equally impossible in either way). Such completeness cannot, however, be expected of appearances at all. There was therefore at that time no real contradiction of reason with itself in the propositions: the series of appearances given in themselves has an absolutely first beginning; and: this series is, absolutely and in itself, without any beginning. For both propositions are perfectly consistent with each other, since appearances, in respect of their existence (as appearances), are in themselves nothing at all, that is, something self-contradictory; and the presupposition of them must therefore naturally entail contradictory consequences.",["- The passage opens mid-sentence (\"rathen soll\"); the verb is probably \"raten/entscheiden\"-type, but the context is lost, so I render only \"is to decide\" and mark the gap with […]; the reading is doubtful.","- \"Mißverstande\" rendered \"misunderstanding\"; the long sentence \"da man nämlich … verlangte\" is divided and \"welches aber…\" is made a separate sentence, \"Such completeness\".","- \"Erscheinungen … an sich selbst gar nichts, d. i. etwas Widersprechendes\": the point is that appearances are not things in themselves; the sense is kept literally.","- OCR slips (hyphenation marks \"—\") silently corrected."]],"krv-b-2220":["But such a misunderstanding cannot be pleaded, and the dispute of reason thereby settled, if the assertion were theistic, that there is a highest being, and over against it the atheistic, that there is no highest being; or, in psychology, [if one were to assert] that everything that thinks is of absolute, persistent unity, and is therefore distinct from all transient material unity, while another opposed to this maintained that the soul is not an immaterial unity and cannot be exempted from transience. For the object of the question is here free from everything alien that contradicts its nature, and the understanding has to do only with things in themselves and not with appearances. A genuine conflict would therefore certainly be met with here, if only pure reason had something to say on the negative side that came near to the ground of an assertion. As for criticism of the grounds of proof offered by the dogmatic affirmer, we can very well concede it to him without thereby giving up these propositions, which at least have in their favour the interest of reason, to which the opponent cannot appeal at all.",["- \"vorgewandt\" read as \"vorgewandt/vorgeschützt\" (\"pleaded\"); the construction \"wenn … behauptet würde\" is completed by the bracketed \"[if one were to assert]\" in the psychological example, to carry the irregular parallel.","- \"alles, was!) denkt\": the OCR footnote marker is dropped; any footnote text is not given in the passage.","- \"Kritik der Beweisgründe\" rendered \"criticism of the grounds of proof\".","- \"die doch wenigstens 1 das Interesse\": stray OCR digit/marks removed."]],"krv-b-2221":["I am not, indeed, of the opinion so often expressed by excellent and thoughtful men (for example Sulzer) who, feeling the weakness of the proofs hitherto offered, hoped that we may one day yet discover evident demonstrations of the two cardinal propositions of our pure reason: there is a God, there is a future life. On the contrary, I am certain that this will never happen. For where is reason to find the ground for such synthetic assertions, which do not relate to objects of experience and their inner possibility? But it is also apodictically certain that no man will ever appear who can assert the contrary with the least semblance of plausibility, let alone dogmatically. For since he could establish this only by pure reason, he would have to undertake to prove that a highest being, and that the subject thinking in us as pure intelligence, is impossible. But where will he find the knowledge that would entitle him to judge synthetically in this fashion about things beyond all possible experience? We may therefore be quite untroubled that anyone will some day prove the contrary. Hence we need not trouble ourselves to devise proofs that satisfy the schools, but may always accept those propositions which are quite well connected with the speculative interest of our reason in its empirical employment, and which, besides, are the only means of reconciling this with the practical interest. Against the opponent (who must here be regarded not merely as critic) we have our *non liquet* in readiness, which must infallibly confound him. At the same time we do not refuse its retorsion upon ourselves, since we always hold in reserve the subjective maxim of reason, which the opponent necessarily lacks, and under whose protection we can regard all his blows in the air with calm and indifference.",["- Sentence \"Wir können also … daß uns jemand … beweisen werde, daß wir darum eben nicht nöthig haben\" is irregular (a doubled \"daß\"); I have divided it and supplied \"Hence\" to connect the second clause.","- The signs \"|\" are carried over only where meaningful; here the page-break bars are omitted as running page marks per the job instructions, along with stray OCR digits (1., 2, 3) that are footnote markers or OCR noise.","- \"Kenntnisse\" rendered \"knowledge\" (not Erkenntnis); \"als reine Intelligenz\" kept.","- \"Retorsion\" kept as the English cognate."]],"krv-b-2222":["In this way there is, properly speaking, no antithetic of pure reason at all. For the only battlefield for it would have to be sought in the field of pure theology and psychology; but this ground bears no combatant in full armour and with weapons to be feared. He can appear only with mockery or boasting, which can be laughed at as child's play. This is a consoling remark, which gives reason courage again; for on what else would it rely, if reason, which alone is called upon to remove all errors, were itself thrown into disorder, without being able to hope for peace and quiet possession?",["- OCR \"fürde ten\" = \"fürchten\"; \"wei ches\" = \"welches\"; \"den reinen\" corrected to \"der reinen\". All silently corrected.","- \"Kämpfer … der … Er\" I render \"combatant … He\"."]],"krv-b-2223":["Whatever nature itself ordains is good for some purpose. Even poisons serve to overcome other poisons generated in our own humours, and so must not be lacking in a complete collection of remedies (a dispensary). The objections against the persuasions and self-conceit of our merely speculative reason are themselves set us by the very nature of this reason, and must therefore have their good determination and purpose, which ought not to be thrown to the winds. Why has Providence set certain objects, though they are bound up with our highest interest, so high that we are almost only permitted to encounter them in a perception that is indistinct and doubted by ourselves, so that searching glances are more provoked than satisfied? Whether it is useful to venture bold determinations in regard to such prospects is at least doubtful, and perhaps even harmful. But it is always, and beyond all doubt, useful to put reason, in its investigating as well as its critical capacity, into complete freedom, so that it may unhindered attend to its own interest. This interest is furthered by its setting limits to its insight as much as by extending it, and it always suffers when alien hands intervene to divert it from its natural course in the service of forced ends.",["- The last sentence is divided; \"welches … befördert wird\" is rendered \"This interest is furthered…\" and the following \"welches … leidet\" is joined by \"and\".","- \"Bestimmung\" rendered \"determination\" per the fixed term, though \"destination\" would also suit \"gute Bestimmung und Absicht\"; the choice is flagged.","- \"Wahrnehmung\" rendered \"perception\"; \"Eigendünkel\" \"self-conceit\". The footnote mark after \"wird!)\" is OCR noise and omitted; any footnote text is not supplied.","- \"miüssen\" corrected to \"müssen\"."]],"krv-b-2224":["Let your opponent, then, only show reason, and combat him solely with the weapons of reason. For the rest, be without anxiety on account of the good cause (the practical interest), for it never enters into the merely speculative dispute. The dispute then discloses nothing but a certain antinomy of reason, which, since it rests on reason's nature, must necessarily be heard and examined. It cultivates reason by considering its object from two sides, and corrects its judgment by limiting it. What becomes contentious in this is not the matter but the tone. For enough still remains to you to speak the language, justified before the keenest reason, of a firm faith, even though you have had to give up that of knowledge.",["- \"Übrigens feld wegen\": OCR corrupt for \"seid wegen\"; I read \"seid … außer Sorgen\" (\"be without anxiety\"). Reading supplied by judgment.","- \"(des praktischen Interesse)\" kept as the genitive of \"Interesse\".","- \"Wissen\" rendered \"knowledge\" here as the contrast with *Glaube* (faith), though Kemp Smith's fixed term is for *Erkenntnis*.","- \"angehört\" (\"heard\") read as the verb \"angehört werden\"."]],"krv-b-2225":["If we were to ask the cool-headed David Hume, who was truly made for evenly balanced judgment: What moved you, by laboriously excogitated scruples, to undermine the persuasion, so consoling and useful to mankind, that their rational insight is adequate to the assertion and to a determinate concept of a supreme being?—he would answer: nothing but the aim of carrying reason further in its self-knowledge, and at the same time a certain indignation at the constraint which is put upon reason when men make a great show with it, and at the same time prevent it from freely confessing the weaknesses which become evident to it in the examination of itself. If, on the other hand, we ask Priestley, who is wholly devoted to the principles of the empirical employment of reason and averse to all transcendent speculation, what motives he had for overthrowing freedom and immortality of our soul (for him the hope of a future life is only the expectation of a miracle of resurrection)—two such fundamental pillars of all religion, and he himself a pious and zealous teacher of religion—he could answer nothing other than this: the interest of reason, which suffers loss by our seeking to withdraw certain objects from the laws of material nature, the only laws which we know and can determine exactly. It would seem unfair to decry the latter, who knows how to reconcile his paradoxical assertion with his religious purpose, and to wound a well-meaning man because he cannot find his way as soon as he has strayed from the field of natural science. But this favour must be extended equally to Hume, who is no less well-disposed and, so far as his moral character goes, beyond reproach. He cannot abandon his abstract speculation, because he rightly holds that its object lies wholly outside the limits of natural science, in the field of pure ideas.",[]],"krv-b-2226":["What, then, is to be done in this matter, especially in view of the danger which seems thereby to threaten the common weal? Nothing is more natural, nothing more reasonable, than the resolution which you have to take in regard to it. Let these people go their way; if they show talent, if they show profound and novel inquiry—in a word, if only they show reason—reason always gains. If you resort to means other than those of an unconstrained reason, if you cry out against high treason, and summon the public, which understands nothing of such subtle labours, as it were to put out a fire,",[]],"krv-b-2227":["you make yourselves ridiculous. For the question is not at all what is advantageous or disadvantageous to the common weal",[]],"krv-b-2228":["in this regard, but only how far",[]],"krv-b-2229":["reason can get in its speculation, which abstracts from all interest, and whether we can count on this speculation at all, or must rather give it up in favour of the practical. Instead, then, of rushing in with the sword, look on calmly from the secure seat of criticism [*Kritik*] at this conflict. It is laborious for the combatants, entertaining for you, and, with an assuredly bloodless outcome, bound to prove profitable for your insight. For it is quite absurd to expect enlightenment from reason and yet to prescribe to it beforehand on which side it must come out. Besides, reason is of itself so well tamed and held within limits by reason that you have no need to call out the guards in order to oppose civil resistance to that party whose threatening superiority seems to you dangerous. In this dialectic there is no victory about which you would have cause to be anxious.",[]],"krv-b-2230":["Reason, moreover, stands greatly in need of such a conflict, and it is to be wished that it had been carried on earlier and with unrestricted public permission. For a mature criticism would then have come into being all the sooner, and upon its appearance all these disputes must of themselves fall away, since the disputants would learn to see the blindness and prejudices which have divided them.",[]],"krv-b-2231":["There is a certain impurity in human nature, which in the end, like everything that comes from nature, must contain a predisposition to good",[]],"krv-b-2232":["ends—namely, an inclination to conceal our true sentiments",[]],"krv-b-2233":["and to display certain assumed sentiments which we hold to be good and praiseworthy. Assuredly men have by this propensity, both to conceal themselves and to assume a semblance advantageous to them, not merely become civilised but, little by little and in a certain measure, moralised; for no one could see through the paint of decorum,",[]],"krv-b-2234":["respectability and modesty, and so each found in the supposedly genuine examples of the good which he saw around him a school of improvement for himself.",[]],"krv-b-2235":["Yet this disposition to represent ourselves as better than we are, and to utter sentiments which we do not have, serves only, as it were, provisionally",[]],"krv-b-2236":["to lead man out of rudeness, and to make him at first at least assume the manner of the good which he knows;",[]],"krv-b-2237":["for afterwards, when the genuine principles have once been developed and have passed into the manner of thinking, that falsity must gradually be vigorously combated, since otherwise it corrupts the heart, and does not allow good sentiments to spring up under the rank weeds of fair seeming.",[]],"krv-b-2238":["It pains me to perceive the very same impurity, dissimulation and hypocrisy even in the utterances of the speculative manner of thinking, where yet men have far fewer hindrances, and no advantage whatever, in disclosing their thoughts openly and without reserve, as is only fair. For what can be more detrimental to insight than to communicate even bare thoughts falsified to one another, to conceal doubts which we feel against our own assertions, or to give an appearance of evidence to proofs which do not satisfy ourselves? So long, however, as it is merely private vanity that instigates these secret intrigues (which is commonly the case in speculative judgments, which have no special interest and are not easily capable of apodeictic certainty), the vanity of others resists with public approval, and matters finally arrive at the point to which the purest sentiment and sincerity would, though much sooner, have brought them. But where the public holds that subtle sophists are engaged in nothing less than shaking the foundations of the public welfare, there it seems not only prudent but permissible, and even praiseworthy, to come to the aid of the good cause by specious arguments, rather than leave to its supposed opponents even the advantage of making us lower our tone to the moderation of a merely practical conviction and compelling us to confess the lack of speculative and apodeictic certainty. I should think, however, that nothing in the world can be less compatible with the aim of maintaining a good cause than artifice, dissimulation and deceit. That in the weighing of the rational grounds of a mere speculation everything should proceed honestly is surely the least that can be demanded. Could we but count with certainty on even this little, the conflict of speculative reason over the important questions of God, of the immortality (of the soul) and of freedom would long ago have been decided, or",[]],"krv-b-2239":["would very soon be brought to an end. Thus the purity of sentiment often stands in inverse ratio to the goodness of the cause itself, and the latter has perhaps more upright and honest opponents than defenders.",["- krv-b-2225: OCR debris (\"Hi\", \"ein ergebenen\", \"unz f billig\", \"uns : | tadelhaften\") silently corrected; \"den den Grundsätzen … alle | ein ergebenen\" read as \"den Grundsätzen … allein ergebenen\"; I render \"wholly devoted\" for the sense, but this is a conjecture. The long Hume/Priestley sentences were divided where needed.","- krv-b-2225: \"groß thut\" read as \"groß thut\" (\"make a great show\"); \"unz billig\" read \"unbillig\"; \"uns tadelhaften\" read \"untadelhaften\" (beyond reproach).","- krv-b-2225: the Academy marks | have been dropped as they fall inside OCR debris; no apparatus signs are clearly present as editorial ones.","- krv-b-2226: \"Hochverrat\" rendered \"high treason\"; \"das gemeine Wesen\" as \"the public\" (the sentence breaks off with its verb in the next passage).","- krv-b-2229: *Kritik* rendered \"criticism\" in brackets; \"Scharwachen\" as \"the guards\" (night-watch); \"bürgerlichen Widerstand\" as \"civil resistance\".","- krv-b-2233: \"moralisirt\" kept as \"moralised\"; \"Schminke\" as \"paint\", a figurative rendering.","- krv-b-2238: \"dingebracht\" read as \"hingebracht\" (brought them); \"Henzchelei\" read \"Heuchelei\". \"Überzeugung\" rendered \"conviction\" (as against *Überredung*, \"persuasion\", in krv-b-2225).","- krv-b-2238: the passage runs to B 778; the footnote asterisk after \"nachtheiliger\" is an OCR artefact and no footnote text is given, so it is omitted."]],"krv-b-2240":["I presuppose, then, readers who do not wish to see a just cause defended unjustly. For such readers it is now decided that, according to the principles of our critique, if we look not to what happens but to what ought properly to happen, there should really be no polemic of pure reason at all. For how can two persons carry on a dispute about a matter whose reality neither of them can exhibit in an actual, or even in a merely possible, experience? Each broods only over the idea of it, in order to bring out of it something more than idea, namely the actuality of the object itself. By what means do they propose to escape from the dispute, since neither can make his own case directly comprehensible and certain, but can only attack and refute that of his opponent? For this is the fate of all assertions of pure reason. They go beyond the conditions of all possible experience, outside which no document of truth is to be met with anywhere. Yet they are obliged to make use of the laws of understanding, which are destined solely for empirical use, and without which no step can be taken in synthetic thinking. Consequently they always expose a weak side to the opponent, and each party can turn to its own advantage the weakness of the other.",["- Restated the long final sentence as several: \"daß … sie … Blößen geben\" divided after \"synthetic thinking\"; \"Consequently\" supplied to carry the main clause.","- \"Idee\" rendered \"idea\" (Kant's term for the concept of reason); \"Gegenstand\" = object; \"Verstandesgesetze\" = laws of understanding.","- \"Document der Wahrheit\" kept literal as \"document of truth\".","- \"Blößen\" rendered \"weak side\"/\"weakness\" (literally \"bare/exposed spots\")."]],"krv-b-2241":["The critique of pure reason may be regarded as the true tribunal for all disputes of pure reason. For it is not itself involved in these disputes, which bear immediately upon objects; it is set up to determine and judge the rightful claims of reason in general, in accordance with the principles of its primary institution.",["- \"Gerichtshof\" (OCR \"Gerichts— hot\") corrected silently; \"Grundfäßen\" read as Grundsätzen.","- Divided at the semicolon-and-\"als welche\" construction; \"letzteren\" resolved as the disputes.","- \"Rechtsame\" rendered \"rightful claims\"."]],"krv-b-2242":["Without it reason is, as it were, in the state of nature, and can establish or secure its assertions and claims only by war. The critique, on the other hand, which derives all decisions from the fundamental rules of its own institution, the authority of which no one can doubt, procures for us the peace of a legal condition, in which we are to conduct our dispute only by process of law. In the former state, what ends the quarrels is a victory, of which both parties boast, and which is for the most part followed only by an uncertain peace, established by the authorities who intervene. In the latter state it is the sentence, which, since it here strikes at the very source of the disputes, must secure an eternal peace. The endless disputes of a merely dogmatic reason also at last compel us to seek peace in some critique of this reason itself, and in a legislation grounded upon it. So Hobbes maintains that the state of nature is a state of injustice and violence, which must necessarily be quitted in order to submit to the constraint of law; for this alone limits our freedom in such a way that it can coexist with the freedom of everyone else, and by that very fact with the common good.",["- Restated the sentence on \"Was die Händel … endigt\" in three sentences; supplied \"In the former/latter state\" for \"im ersten/zweiten\".","- OCR \"Genteng\" read as Sentenz (sentence, judicial verdict); \"der“ Streitigkeiten\" read as \"der Streitigkeiten\".","- \"Proceß\" rendered \"process of law\"; \"Obrigkeit\" as \"the authorities\".","- Last sentence: \"so wie Hobbes behauptet\" rendered \"So Hobbes maintains\"; the relative \"der allein …\" turned into a \"for\" clause."]],"krv-b-2243":["To this freedom belongs also that of exposing one's thoughts and one's doubts, which one cannot resolve for oneself, to public judgment, without being decried as a restless and dangerous citizen. This lies already in the original right of human reason, which recognises no other judge than universal human reason itself, in which everyone has a voice. And since all improvement of which our condition is capable must come from this, such a right is sacred and may not be curtailed. It is also very unwise to cry up as dangerous certain bold assertions or presumptuous attacks upon doctrines that already have on their side the assent of the greater and better part of the community; for that is to give them an importance which they ought never to have had. When I hear that a man of no ordinary mind has demonstrated away the freedom of the human will, the hope of a future life, and the existence of God, I am eager to read the book, for I expect his talent to carry me further in my insight. That he will have achieved nothing of all this, I already know beforehand with complete certainty. I know it not because I believe myself already in possession of irrefutable proofs of these important propositions, but because transcendental critique, which has laid open to me the whole stock of our pure reason, has fully convinced me that, just as reason is quite insufficient for affirmative assertions in this field, so little, and even less, will it know enough to assert anything negatively on these questions. For where will the so-called free-thinker get his knowledge that, for example, there is no supreme being? This proposition lies outside the field of possible experience, and therefore also beyond the limits of all human insight. The dogmatic defender of the good cause against this enemy I should not read at all,",["- Restated the long sentence beginning \"Wenn ich höre\" in separate sentences; \"nicht darum weil … sondern weil\" kept as a contrast.","- OCR debris (f, \"ges fährlich\", \"etz | was\", \"si\", \"Herkommen\") silently repaired; \"selbst | si wiederum\" read as \"selbst [sie] wiederum die allgemeine Menschenvernunft\" and rendered \"universal human reason itself\".","- \"Freigeist\" rendered \"free-thinker\"; \"Kenntniß\" as \"knowledge\" in the sense of acquaintance, not *Erkenntnis*.","- \"so wenig und noch weniger werde sie wissen\" is elliptical; sense supplied from the preceding clause."]],"krv-b-2244":["because I know beforehand that he will attack the other's specious arguments only in order to gain entrance for his own. Besides, a commonplace illusion does not give so much material for new observations as one that is strange and ingeniously devised. On the other hand, the",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"Hingegen würde der\"); not completed. The sentence continues in the next passage.","- OCR \"Fals\" read as \"als\"; \"alltägiger\" as \"alltäglicher\"."]],"krv-b-2245":["religious opponent, who is likewise dogmatic after his own fashion, would give my critique the occupation it desires, and occasion for a fuller correction of its principles, without there being the least ground for apprehension on his account.",["- Begins mid-sentence; \"religious opponent\" is supplied in English word order to link with the preceding \"the\" (\"der … Religionsgegner\"), the intervening words in the German text being \"nach seiner Art auch dogmatische\".","- \"Grundjäke\" read as Grundsätze (principles).","- The first word \"religious\" is rendered from \"Religionsgegner\" at the end of the clause; the English reorders to join the preceding fragment."]],"krv-b-2246":["But youth, entrusted to academic instruction, should surely at least be warned against such writings, and kept from early acquaintance with propositions so dangerous, until their power of judgment has matured, or rather until the doctrine which we wish to ground in them has taken firm root, so that they may vigorously resist all persuasion to the contrary, from whatever quarter it may come.\n\nIf it must remain with the dogmatic procedure in matters of pure reason, and the dismissal of opponents is to be properly polemical, that is, of such a kind that one enters into the combat and arms oneself with",["- Question mark after \"widerstehen\" is an OCR/typographical oddity; kept as a plain statement here, though the German sentence is a rhetorical question. Reader should note the question form.","- Divided at \"Müßte es …\"; \"bei dem dogmatischen Verfahren\" rendered \"with the dogmatic procedure\".","- Second sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage; \"Urtheilskraft\" = power of judgment.","- \"ÜUberredung\" read as \"Überredung\"; \"gewurzelt\" = rooted."]],"krv-b-2247":["proofs for the opposite assertions, then nothing would indeed be more advisable for the moment, but at the same time nothing more vain and fruitless in the long run, than to place the reason of the youth under guardianship for a time, and so at least for so long to preserve it from seduction.",["- Continues the preceding sentence mid-construction (\"Beweisgründe … bewaffnete\"); the sentence continues into the next passage (\"unter Vormundschaft zu setzen\" is in 2248 but I have rendered it here to give a complete English clause).","- \"Beweisgründe\" rendered \"proofs\"."]],"krv-b-2248":["But if in the sequel either curiosity or the fashionable tone of the age puts such writings into its hands, will that youthful persuasion then still hold its ground? He",["- \"unter Vormundschaft zu setzen und wenigstens so lange vor Verführung zu bewahren\" was rendered in 2247 to avoid a broken English clause; this is an overlap with the passage boundary.","- \"Stich halten\" rendered \"hold its ground\"; the final \"Der-\" is completed in English as \"He\", the German continuing \"jenige\" in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2249":["who brings nothing but dogmatic weapons with which to resist the attacks of his opponent, and who cannot uncover the hidden dialectic which is not",["- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"die verborgene Dialektik, die nicht\"); not completed. \"Der-|jenige\" is joined as \"derjenige\" and rendered \"He who\" with 2248's \"He\".","- \"und … nicht … aufdecken können\" is inferred as the likely verb phrase only in English grammar to make the fragment readable; the German text as given contains no verb after \"die nicht\". Treat \"cannot uncover\" as a supplied reading and not as Kant's words."]],"krv-b-2250":["[...] lies less in his own bosom than in that of his opponent, [and who] does not know how to unravel [it], sees specious arguments which have the advantage of novelty set against specious arguments which no longer have it, but rather arouse the suspicion that the credulity of youth has been abused. He believes that he cannot better show that he has outgrown the discipline of childhood than by disregarding those well-meant warnings. Accustomed as he is to dogmatism, he drinks in long draughts the poison that dogmatically corrupts his principles.\n\nPrecisely the opposite of what is here advised must take place in academic instruction. This, however, presupposes a thorough instruction in the critique of pure reason. For in order to bring the principles of the critique into practice as early as possible, and to show their sufficiency in face of the greatest dialectical illusion, it is absolutely necessary to direct attacks, so formidable to the dogmatist, against his reason, which is still weak, it is true, but enlightened by criticism. He must also be allowed to attempt to test, piece by piece, by those principles the groundless assertions of his opponent. It cannot be at all difficult for him to dissolve them into mere vapour. So he feels early his own power to secure himself completely against such harmful delusions, which for him must in the end lose all semblance of truth.\n\nThe very same blows that demolish the enemy's edifice must be equally ruinous to his own speculative structure, should he perhaps think of erecting one. He is nevertheless wholly unconcerned about this, since he has no need to dwell in it. He has still before him a prospect into the practical field, where he can reasonably hope for firmer ground on which to erect his rational and salutary system. |",["- The passage opens mid-sentence. The subject of \"nicht zu entwickeln weiß\" is lost, so I supplied \"[and who]\" and \"[it]\" (the illusion, *Schein*) and added nothing more.","- \"sondern vielmehr\" (OCR \"son— dem\") restored. Stray OCR marks (\"E\", \"!\", footnote-like quotes) dropped silently.","- The long final sentence (\"Ob nun zwar … so ist er …\") is restated as three sentences. The concessive structure is kept.","- \"Scheingründe\" = \"specious arguments\". \"Kritik\" = \"criticism\" in \"durch Kritik aufgeklärte\" and \"critique\" for the title of the work."]],"krv-b-2251":["So there is, then, no real polemic in the field of pure reason. Both parties are shadow-boxers [*Luftfechter*], who wrestle with their own shadows; for they go beyond nature, where there is nothing that their dogmatic grips could seize and hold. They may fight well; the shadows which they cleave grow together again in an instant, like the heroes in Valhalla, so that they can amuse themselves anew in bloodless combats. |",["- *Luftfechter* literally \"fencers of the air\"; \"shadow-boxers\" chosen as the English idiom, with the German in brackets.","- \"sich i aufs neue\" is an OCR slip for \"sich aufs neue\".","- \"Sie haben gut kämpfen\" is rendered \"They may fight well\"; the sense is that their fighting costs them nothing."]],"krv-b-2252":["But neither is there any admissible sceptical employment of pure reason, which might be entitled the principle of neutrality in all its disputes. To incite reason against itself, to put weapons into its hands on both sides, and then to look on calmly and mockingly at its fiercest combat, does not look well from the dogmatic standpoint, but has the appearance of a malicious and gloating disposition. Yet when we consider the invincible blindness and boastfulness of the sophists [*Vernünftler*], who will let themselves be moderated by no critique, there is really no other counsel than to set against the braggart on one side another who stands on precisely the same rights. Reason may then, through the resistance of an enemy, at least be brought to pause, so as to put some doubt into its pretensions and to give critique a hearing. But to leave the matter entirely at these doubts, and [...]",["- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"und\"); it is translated as it stands, and the continuation is in the next passage.","- Restated \"Wenn man indessen … so ist doch …\" with the infinitive clause turned into a separate sentence (\"Reason may then …\"). I resolved \"Großsprecherei … eine andere\" as one braggart set against another.","- *Vernünftler* = reasoners in a pejorative sense, here \"sophists\". \"schadenfroh\" = \"gloating\". OCR \"un: : bezwingliche\" corrected to \"unbezwingliche\"."]],"krv-b-2253":["[...] to stake [everything] on this, the conviction and the confession of its Un-[...] to bring into examination. [text corrupt] Since, however, this sceptical manner of extricating oneself from a vexatious business of reason seems to be, as it were, the short way to a lasting philosophical repose, or at least the highway favoured by those who think to give themselves a philosophical air by a mocking contempt of all inquiries of this kind, I find it necessary to present this mode of thinking in its peculiar light.\n\nOn the Impossibility of a Sceptical Satisfaction",["- The opening is corrupt in the OCR: \"seiner Un- Prüfung zu ziehen\" has lost its middle. Presumably \"Un[wissenheit]\" is cut off from a clause that has dropped out. I have not filled the gap, and the first sentence is therefore only fragmentary. \"[everything]\" in \"stake [everything] on this\" is my supplied rendering of the elliptical \"es darauf auszusetzen\" and is doubtful.","- \"Da indessen … so finde ich\" is kept as one sentence. \"Heeresstraße\" = \"highway\".","- The last line is the first half of the section heading, which continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2254":["of Pure Reason Divided against Itself.",["- Second half of the heading begun in the previous passage: \"Von der Unmöglichkeit einer sceptischen Befriedigung der mit sich selbst veruneinigten reinen Vernunft.\""]],"krv-b-2255":["The consciousness of my ignorance (unless this is at the same time known as necessary), instead of putting an end to my inquiries, is rather the proper cause for arousing them. All ignorance is either ignorance of things, or ignorance of the determination and limits of my knowledge. Now if the ignorance is contingent, it must impel me, in the first case, to inquire dogmatically into the things (objects), and in the second, to inquire critically into the limits of my possible knowledge. That my ignorance, however, is absolutely necessary, and that it therefore absolves me from all further inquiry, cannot be established empirically, from observation, but only critically, by probing the first sources of our knowledge. Thus the determination of the limits [*Grenzbestimmung*] of our reason can take place only on a priori grounds. Its restriction [*Einschränkung*], however, which is a knowledge, though only indeterminate, of an ignorance never fully to be removed, can also be known a posteriori, through what in all our knowing still always remains to be known. The former knowledge of our ignorance, possible only through critique of reason itself, is therefore science; the latter is nothing but perception, of which we cannot say how far the inference drawn from it may extend.\n\nIf I represent the surface of the earth (in accordance with sensible appearance) as a plate, I cannot know how far it extends. But experience teaches me this: that wherever I may come I always see a space around me in which I could proceed further. Thus I know the limitations [*Schranken*] of my geographical knowledge at any time actual, but not the limits of all possible geography. But if I have got so far as to know that the earth is a sphere and its surface a spherical surface, then from a small part of it, for example the size of one degree, I can know a priori, in accordance with principles, the diameter, and through the diameter the complete boundary of the earth, that is, its surface, and can determine it. Although I am ignorant in respect of the objects which this surface may contain, I am not so in respect of the extent which it comprises, its magnitude and limits. |",["- \"Grenzen\" = \"limits\" and \"Schranken\" = \"limitations\", kept distinct as the argument requires. *Einschränkung* = \"restriction\". *Wissenschaft* = \"science\".","- \"als selbiger\" is obscure. I take it as \"the inference drawn from it [the perception] as such\" and give \"the inference drawn from it\".","- The passage is divided into two paragraphs at the earth illustration, and the closing sentences of the second are restated. OCR damage (\"d weit\", \"f nur\", \"dahin ich weiter fort: I\" gehen\", \"unde\", \"durch die⸗ ten\") silently repaired as \"wie weit\", \"nur\", \"weiter fortgehen\", \"und\", \"durch diesen\".","- \"Erdkunde\" and \"Erdbeschreibung\" are given as \"geographical knowledge\" and \"geography\"."]],"krv-b-2256":["The sum of all possible objects for our knowledge seems to us to be a level surface, which has its apparent horizon, namely that which comprehends the whole extent of it. This horizon has been entitled by us the rational concept [*Vernunftbegriff*] of unconditioned totality. To reach it empirically is impossible, and all attempts to determine it a priori in accordance with a certain principle have been in vain. Yet all the questions of our pure reason relate to what may lie outside this horizon, or at most on its boundary line.",["- \"und ist von uns … genannt worden\" has \"Horizont\" as antecedent, not the \"Fläche\". I made that explicit by repeating \"This horizon\" and split the sentence.","- OCR \"Erfenntniß\", \"ile- l! gen\" corrected to \"Erkenntniß\", \"liegen\". \"allenfalls\" = \"at most\"."]],"krv-b-2257":["The celebrated David Hume was one of these geographers of human reason. He believed that he had sufficiently disposed of all those questions by relegating them beyond its horizon, which yet he was unable to determine. He dwelt chiefly on the principle of causality, and remarked quite correctly of it that its truth (indeed not even the objective validity of the concept of an efficient cause in general) rests on no insight, that is, on no knowledge a priori. Consequently what constitutes its whole authority is not in the least the necessity of this law, but merely its general serviceableness in the course of experience, and a subjective necessity arising from this, which he calls custom. From the incapacity of our reason to make of this principle a use that goes beyond all experience, he inferred the nullity of all pretensions of reason to pass beyond the empirical.",["- OCR \"mwar … da 1\" corrected to \"war … der\". \"gar keine\" (OCR \"keine“\") read as \"gar keine Einsicht\".","- The long sentence \"bemerkte … daß … daß daher … ausmache\" is restated in two sentences. The second \"daß\"-clause is turned into a main clause with \"what constitutes its whole authority\".","- \"Ansehen\" = \"authority\". \"Einsicht\" = \"insight\"."]],"krv-b-2258":["A procedure of this kind, which subjects the *facta* of reason to examination and, where it finds cause, to censure, may be entitled the censorship [*Censur*] of reason. It is beyond doubt that such censorship inevitably leads to doubt regarding every transcendent employment of principles. But this is only the second step, and is still far from completing the work. The first step in matters of pure reason, which marks its age of childhood, is dogmatic. The second step, just mentioned, is sceptical, and bears witness to the caution of a faculty of judgment sharpened by experience. But a third step is still required, which can belong only to a mature and manly faculty of judgment, one which has at its basis fixed maxims of proved universality. This step consists in subjecting to appraisal not the *facta* of reason but reason itself, in respect of its whole capacity and fitness for pure modes of knowledge *a priori*. That is not the censorship but the critique of reason. By it not merely limits [*Schranken*] but the determinate boundaries [*Grenzen*] of reason are established; and what is proved from principles, and not merely conjectured, is not merely ignorance in this or that part, but ignorance in respect of all possible questions of a certain kind.\n\nScepticism is thus a resting-place for human reason, where it can reflect upon its dogmatic wanderings and sketch a map of the region in which it finds itself, so that it may choose its further road with greater assurance. It is not, however, a dwelling-place for permanent residence. Such a residence can be found only in complete certainty, whether in the knowledge of the objects themselves or in the knowledge of the boundaries within which all our knowledge of objects is enclosed.\n\nOur reason is not like a plane of indeterminably wide extent, of which we know only in a general way that it has limits. It must rather be compared to a sphere, the radius of which can be found from the curvature of an arc on its surface (that is, from the nature of synthetic propositions *a priori*), and from which the content and the boundary of the sphere can likewise be assigned with certainty. Outside this sphere (the field of experience) nothing is object for reason; indeed, even questions regarding such supposed objects concern only subjective principles of a thoroughgoing determination of the relations which can occur among the concepts of the understanding within this sphere.",["- Restated the long sentence on *Kritik* (\"welches nicht die Censur…\") as two sentences; the final clause (what is \"bewiesen\") is rearranged for English, the sense unchanged.","- OCR fragments (\"Kindes- „alter\", \"zeugt“)\", \"be- Iständigen\", \"Gegen— ständen\") silently repaired; the stray \"f\" before \"Unsere\" and the bracket before \"Krümmung\" are OCR noise and omitted.","- *Schranken* = limits, *Grenzen* = boundaries (the distinction is Kant's own and is retained). *Facta* kept in Latin. *Urtheilskraft* = faculty of judgment. *Erkenntnissen* = modes of knowledge.","- \"[Censur]\" etc. in brackets give the German where the rendering is a choice.","- The last clause of the passage runs on into the next passage (B 790), whose running head is omitted."]],"krv-b-2259":["[Running head and page numbers omitted. The text is a continuation of the final sentence of the preceding passage; the fragment \"gängigen Bestimmung … vorkommen können\" has been incorporated there. Nothing further to translate.]",["- This passage contains only the running head (\"498 Methodenlehre. 1. Hauptstück. 2. Abschnitt.\") and the closing words of the sentence begun in krv-b-2258 (\"durchgängigen Bestimmung der Verhältnisse, welche unter den Verstandesbegriffen innerhalb dieser Sphäre vorkommen können\"). Since the passage is to be translated as it stands, the closing words are: \"…thoroughgoing determination of the relations which can occur among the concepts of the understanding within this sphere.\" I rendered them in the preceding entry to complete the sentence; the stray \"6\" is OCR noise."]],"krv-b-2260":["We are actually in possession of synthetic knowledge *a priori*, as is shown by the principles of the understanding which anticipate experience. If anyone cannot make the possibility of such knowledge comprehensible to himself, he may at first doubt whether it really resides in us *a priori*. But he cannot yet declare this to be an impossibility, on the ground that it arises through the mere powers of the understanding, nor pronounce null and void all the steps which reason takes according to the guidance of these principles. He can say only that if we could see their origin and authenticity, we should be able to determine the compass and the boundaries of our reason; but that until this has been done, all assertions of the latter are ventured blindly. In this way a thoroughgoing doubt regarding all dogmatic philosophy, which proceeds without a critique of reason itself, would be quite well grounded. But it would not therefore follow that reason can be wholly denied such an advance, if it were prepared and secured by a better foundation.\n\nFor, in the first place, all the concepts, indeed all the questions, which pure reason puts before us lie not in experience but again in reason alone, and they must therefore be capable of being resolved, and of having their validity or invalidity comprehended. Nor are we entitled to reject these problems on the pretext of our incapacity, as if their solution did really lie in the nature of things, and to refuse further investigation of them. For reason has itself engendered these ideas solely in its own womb, and is therefore bound to give an account of their validity or of their dialectical illusion.",["- \"durch bloße Kräfte des Verstandes\" is awkward in the German; I take it, with the sense of the argument, as the ground on which the sceptic might (wrongly) call the possibility an impossibility, and render it as \"on the ground that it arises through the mere powers of the understanding\". The reading is a judgment, not certain.","- \"ihrer\" in \"die Grenzen unserer Vernunft… Behauptungen der letzten\" : \"der letzten\" = of the latter (reason); rendered \"of the latter\".","- Divided the long sentence \"Und auf solche Weise…\" and the sentence beginning \"Wir sind auch nicht berechtigt\" at the points shown. OCR debris (\"nicht“\", \"7°\", \"A wande\", \"Nade forschung\") silently repaired: \"Vor-wande\", \"Nach-forschung\".","- \"Aechtheit\" = authenticity; \"Rechenschaft zu geben gehalten ist\" = is bound to give an account.","- The heading's \"B 790–791?\" reference is the portal's; no text added."]],"krv-b-2261":["All sceptical polemic is properly directed only against the dogmatist, who proceeds gravely on his way without any distrust of his original objective principles, that is, without critique. Its aim is merely to put him out of countenance [*das Concept zu verrücken*] and to bring him to self-knowledge. In itself it decides nothing whatever as to what we can know and what, on the other hand, we cannot know. All the unsuccessful dogmatic attempts of reason are *facta*, and it is always useful to subject them to censorship. But this can decide nothing regarding the expectations of reason, that it may hope for a better outcome of its future endeavours and lay claim to it. Mere censorship can therefore never bring to an end the dispute over the rights of human reason.",["- \"das Concept zu verrücken\" is an idiom (to disconcert, throw off one's train of thought); rendered \"put him out of countenance\", German given.","- \"Selbsterkenntniß\" = self-knowledge. \"Facta\" kept in Latin; \"Censur\" = censorship, as in the preceding passage.","- Restated the final sentence in two clauses for English; OCR debris (\"l\", \"galso\", \"Ansprüͤche\") silently repaired."]],"krv-b-2262":["Since Hume is perhaps the most ingenious of all the sceptics, and is without dispute the foremost in respect of the influence which the sceptical procedure can exercise in awakening a thorough examination of reason, it is well worth the trouble to set forth, so far as is fitting for my purpose, the course of his inferences, and the errors of so penetrating and estimable a man, errors which yet began on the track of truth.",["- OCR \"fcepstische\" read as \"sceptische\". \"vorstellig zu machen\" rendered \"to set forth\" (literally, to lay before the reader).","- The sentence is kept whole, with the relative clause \"die doch auf der Spur der Wahrheit angefangen haben\" rendered as an appositive to \"errors\"."]],"krv-b-2263":["Hume had perhaps in mind, though he never fully developed the thought, that in judgments of a certain kind we go beyond our concept of the object. I have entitled this kind of judgment synthetic. How I can go beyond the concept which I have hitherto, by means of experience, is open to no difficulty. Experience is itself a synthesis of perceptions, which augments the concept I have by means of one perception through others that are added to it. But we believe that we can also go beyond our concept *a priori*, and extend our knowledge. This we attempt either through pure understanding, in respect of that which can at least be an object of experience, or even through pure reason, in respect of such properties of things, or indeed of the existence of such objects, as can never occur in experience.\n\nOur sceptic did not distinguish these two kinds of judgment, as he ought to have done. He straightway held this augmentation of concepts out of themselves to be impossible, and likewise the self-parturition, so to speak, of our understanding (together with reason) without its being impregnated by experience. He therefore held all the supposed *a priori* principles of understanding and reason to be imaginary, and found them to be nothing but a custom arising from experience and its laws, and consequently merely empirical rules, that is, rules contingent in themselves, to which we ascribe a supposed necessity and universality.\n\nTo maintain this strange proposition he appealed to the universally recognised principle of the relation of cause to effect. No faculty of understanding can lead us from the concept of a thing to the existence of something else which is thereby universally and necessarily given. He therefore believed he could infer that, without experience, we have nothing which could augment our concept and entitle us to a judgment extending itself *a priori* in this fashion. That sunlight which illumines the wax should at the same time melt it, while it hardens clay, no understanding could divine from the concepts we previously had of these things, still less infer according to law; only experience could teach us such a law.\n\nOn the other hand, we have seen in transcendental logic that, although we can never go immediately beyond the content of the concept which is given to us, we can yet know completely *a priori* the law of connection with other things. This is possible, however, only in relation to a third thing, namely possible experience, and so still *a priori*. If, then, wax which was previously firm melts, I can know *a priori* that something must have preceded (e.g. the sun's warmth) upon which the melting followed according to a constant law. Yet without experience I could not know determinately, *a priori* and without instruction from experience, either the cause from the effect or the effect from the cause.\n\nHe therefore inferred wrongly, from the contingency of our determination in accordance with the law, the contingency of the law itself. The going out from the concept of a thing to possible experience (which takes place *a priori* and constitutes the objective reality of the concept) he confounded with the synthesis of the objects of actual experience, which is, of course, always empirical. In this way he converted a principle of affinity, which has its seat in the understanding and asserts necessary connection, into a rule of association, which is found only in the reproductive imagination, and can exhibit merely contingent connections, in no way objective. |",["- Restated the long sentences beginning \"Unser Sceptiker…\" and \"Er bezog sich…\" as several sentences, and divided \"Dagegen haben wir…\" in two, to keep the construction clear.","- OCR repairs, silently made: \"Gefeht\" = Gesetz, \"verwehren\" = vermehren, \"Tvon\" = Thon (clay), \"faigen\" = sagen, \"Rothwendig\" = nothwendig; the other slips are obvious.","- \"Selbstgebärung… geschwängert\" rendered literally (\"self-parturition… impregnated\"), since Kant's metaphor is deliberate.","- \"Gesetzmäßig schließen\" = \"infer according to law\"; \"Bestimmung\" = determination.","- \"[ob] vermeintliche Principien… des Verstandes und der Vernunft\": \"derselben\" is resolved as understanding and reason together, as the preceding parenthesis requires."]],"krv-b-2264":["The sceptical aberrations of this man, otherwise exceedingly acute, arose chiefly from a defect which he shared with all dogmatists, namely, that he did not survey systematically all the modes of the *a priori* synthesis of the understanding. Had he done so, he would have found, to say nothing of the others, the principle of permanence, for instance, to be one which, no less than that of causality, anticipates experience. He would thereby also have been able to prescribe determinate limits to the understanding and to pure reason in their *a priori* extension.\n\nBut he merely restricts our understanding without setting limits to it. He brings about a general mistrust, but no determinate knowledge of the ignorance which is inevitable for us. He subjects certain principles of the understanding to censure, without putting this understanding, in respect of its whole faculty, to the test of critique. And in denying to it what it really cannot achieve, he goes further and disputes its every power of extending itself *a priori*, although he has not brought this whole faculty under assessment. There consequently befalls him what always overthrows scepticism, namely, that he himself is doubted. For his objections rest only on facts, which are contingent, and not on principles which could necessitate a renunciation of the right to dogmatic assertions.\n\nFurther, he recognises no distinction between the well-grounded claims of the understanding and the dialectical pretensions of reason, against which his attacks are chiefly directed. Reason, therefore, whose quite peculiar impetus has not in the least been disturbed by this but only hindered, feels that the space for its expansion has not been closed against it, and can never be wholly diverted from its attempts, though it is pinched here and there. For against attacks men arm themselves for defence, and set their heads all the more stiffly upon carrying through their demands. A complete survey, however, of its whole faculty, and the conviction, springing from it, of the certainty of a small possession in face of the vanity of higher claims, puts an end to all dispute, and induces reason to rest peaceably content with a limited but undisputed property.",["- Divided the two long \"Da er…\" periods into several sentences; the second \"so\" apodosis is kept, with \"He consequently…\" supplied to carry the connection after the series of \"da\" clauses.","- \"Einschränkt… begrenzt\": rendered \"restricts… [sets] limits to\"; the German distinguishes *Schranken* from *Grenzen*, which the English can only suggest.","- \"Überschlag\" = \"survey\" (an estimate or reckoning of the whole).","- Stray \"N\" in the OCR (before \"Da er auch\") and the OCR break \"An— griffe\" silently dropped or mended; \"Sowohl)\" read as \"sowohl\".","- \"gezwackt\" = \"pinched\", \"Probirwage\" = \"test\" (literally, assayer's balance)."]],"krv-b-2265":["Against the uncritical dogmatist, who has not measured the sphere of his understanding, and has therefore not determined by principles the limits of his possible knowledge, who consequently does not know beforehand how much he can do but thinks to find it out by mere trials, these sceptical attacks are not only dangerous but even ruinous. For if he is caught in a single assertion which he cannot justify, and the illusion of which he is equally unable to explain from principles, suspicion falls upon all his assertions, however persuasive they may otherwise be.",["- \"Erkenntniß\" here rendered \"knowledge\".","- \"deren Schein\" refers to the assertion; I have made \"the illusion of which\" explicit."]],"krv-b-2266":["And so the sceptic is the taskmaster who disciplines the dogmatic sophist [*Vernünftler*] into a sound critique of understanding and of reason itself. When the dogmatist has attained to this, he has no further challenge to fear. For he then distinguishes his possession from what lies entirely outside it, to which he makes no claims, and about which he cannot be involved in disputes. Thus the sceptical procedure, though in itself not satisfactory as regards the questions of reason, is yet preparatory, in that it arouses reason's circumspection and points to thorough means which can secure it in its rightful possessions.",["- \"Zuchtmeister … auf eine … Kritik\": the construction is smoothed to \"disciplines … into\", the sense being that the sceptic drives the dogmatist toward critique.","- \"Er\" in the second sentence is the dogmatist, not the sceptic; I have said so in the English.","- \"Vernünftler\" = one who reasons without critique, \"sophist\"; German given for the choice.","- OCR \"ganzlich\" mended to \"gänzlich\"."]],"krv-b-2267":["Of the First Chapter. Third Section.",["- Heading; \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"chapter\", as usual in the Doctrine of Method."]],"krv-b-2268":["The Discipline of Pure Reason in Respect of",["- Title, broken off after \"in Ansehung der\"; completed in the next passage (\"Hypothesen\"). Not completed here."]],"krv-b-2269":["Hypotheses.",[]],"krv-b-2270":["Since, then, through the critique of our reason we know at last so much, that in its pure and speculative employment we can in fact know nothing, should it not open a so much the wider field to hypotheses, seeing that it is at least permitted to invent and to opine, even if not to assert? |",["- \"Weil … sollte sie nicht\": the causal clause is kept, with the question rendered as the main clause; \"so much the wider\" for \"desto weiteres\".","- The stray \"17\" in the OCR is a line number and is omitted; the sign | is kept where it stands.","- \"dichten\" = \"invent\" (to feign or frame fictions); \"meinen\" = \"opine\"."]],"krv-b-2271":["If imagination is not simply to run riot, but is to invent under the strict supervision of reason, something must always be previously quite certain, and not invented or mere opinion. This is the possibility of the object itself. In that case it is permissible to take refuge in opinion in regard to the actuality of the object. But such opinion, if it is not to be groundless, must be connected, as a ground of explanation, with what is actually given and therefore certain; and it is then entitled hypothesis.",["- Two sentences kept as in the German; the second is divided by a semicolon, with \"such opinion\" supplied for the relative \"die\".","- \"Wirklichkeit\" rendered \"actuality\", \"Möglichkeit\" \"possibility\". \"Dichten\" is \"to invent\" (cf. \"erdichtet\").","- Reading \"soll\" for the OCR \"fol\", and \"werden muß\" for \"wer muß\"."]],"krv-b-2272":["Now we can form not the least concept *a priori* of the possibility of dynamical connection. The category of the pure understanding does not serve to devise such connection, but only to understand it where it is met with in experience. Hence we cannot, in conformity with these categories, originally excogitate a single object with a new property that cannot be empirically indicated, and make it the ground of a permissible hypothesis; for that would be to put empty fictions of the brain, instead of concepts of things, at the basis of reason. Thus it is not permissible to invent any new original powers: for instance, an understanding capable of intuiting its object without senses; or an expansive force without any contact; or a new kind of substance, say one present in space without impenetrability. Consequently there can also be no community of substances differing from all that experience supplies, no presence other than in space, no duration other than in time. In a word, it is possible for our reason to use only the conditions of possible experience as conditions of the possibility of things. It cannot, quite independently of these conditions, create such things, as it were, for itself; for concepts of this kind, though free from contradiction, would yet be without an object",["- Restated Kant's long first sentence (\"Da wir ... so können wir\") as several sentences, and the \"So ist es nicht erlaubt ... Mit einem Worte\" sequence likewise.","- OCR debris removed (\"h\", \"f\", \"z `\", \"A\", the stray semicolon); \"Erfah— rung\" read as \"Erfahrung\".","- \"Kräfte\" rendered \"powers\"; \"Ausdehnungskraft\" \"expansive force\"; \"Gemeinschaft\" \"community\".","- Passage breaks off at \"sein\" with the verb phrase (\"müßten\") missing; I have not completed it. \"Would be\" is supplied only to make the fragment read."]],"krv-b-2273":["The concepts of reason are, as already stated, mere ideas. They have, to be sure, no object in any experience; yet they do not on that account signify objects that are invented and at the same time assumed to be possible. They are thought only problematically, in order that in relation to them (as heuristic fictions) we may found regulative principles of the systematic under-",["- Breaks off mid-word (\"Der-\", an OCR slip for \"Ver-\") and I have kept the hyphen; the word continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2274":["standing's use in the field of experience. If one turns from this",["- Fragment: continues \"Verstandesgebrauchs\". \"Geht man davon\" is completed by \"ab\" at the start of the next passage; I render \"turns from this\" here and \"away\" there."]],"krv-b-2275":["away, then they are mere entities of thought [*Gedankendinge*], the possibility of which cannot be proved, and which therefore cannot be made the ground of the explanation of actual appearances by means of a hypothesis. To think the soul as simple is quite permissible, so that in accordance with this idea we may take as the principle of our judgment upon its",["- \"ab\" is read with the preceding \"Geht man davon\"; \"away\" is therefore not a supplied word.","- \"Gemüthskräfte\" rendered \"powers of the mind\"; I have rendered \"voll— ständige und nothwendige Einheit aller Gemüthskräfte, ob man sie gleich nicht in concreto einsehen kann\" as a complete and necessary unity of all the powers of the mind, \"although we cannot see it *in concreto*\"; the passage is a restatement within the one sentence, split between 2275 and 2276.","- The OCR \"b\" is dropped. The sentence breaks off before \"inneren Erscheinungen zu legen\", which opens the next passage."]],"krv-b-2276":["inner appearances [the unity of all the powers of the mind]. But to assume the soul as simple substance (a transcendent concept) would be a proposition not only unprovable (as many physical hypotheses are), but also quite arbitrarily and blindly ventured. For the simple cannot occur in any experience at all; and if by substance we here understand the persistent object of sensible intuition, the possibility of a simple appearance is not at all to be seen. Merely intelligible beings, or merely intelligible properties of the things of the sensible world, cannot be assumed as opinion on any well-grounded warrant of reason. Yet (since we have no concepts of their possibility or impossibility) neither can they be dogmatically denied by any supposedly better insight.",["- Fragment continues the previous sentence: \"inneren Erscheinungen zu legen\" is \"[to lay] at the basis of ... inner appearances\"; I supply the bracketed words, which belong to the previous passage's clause, for intelligibility.","- Divided the long sentence \"Aber die Seele ... einzusehen ist\" into two, at \"For the simple\".","- \"Befugniß\" rendered \"warrant\"; \"Sinnenwelt\" \"sensible world\". OCR \"all— ein\" read \"allein\", and stray \"b\", \"9\" dropped."]],"krv-b-2277":["For the explanation of given appearances no other things and grounds of explanation can be adduced than those which, according to already known laws of appearances, have been brought into connection with the given appearances. A transcendental hypothesis, in which a mere idea",["- OCR \"Er— 5 jheinungen\" read \"Erscheinungen\". Breaks off after \"bloße Idee\"; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2278":["of reason would be employed for the explanation of natural things, would therefore be no explanation at all. What is not sufficiently understood from known empirical principles would be explained by something of which nothing whatever is understood. Moreover, the principle of such a hypothesis would really serve only to satisfy reason, and not to further the use of the understanding in respect of objects. Order and purposiveness in nature must in turn be explained from natural grounds and in accordance with natural laws; and here even the wildest hypotheses, if only they are physical, are more tolerable than a hyperphysical one, that is, an appeal to a divine author whom we presuppose for the purpose. For that would be a principle of lazy reason (*ignava ratio*), to pass over at one stroke all the causes whose objective reality, at least in respect of possibility, we can still come to know through continued experience, in order to rest in a mere idea, which is very convenient to reason. As regards the absolute totality of the ground of explanation in the series of such causes, this can create no hindrance in the case of the objects of the world. For since these are nothing but appearances, nothing completed can ever be hoped for in the synthesis of the series of conditions.",["- Joined to the preceding sentence: \"eine transscendentale Hypothese ... würde ... gar keine Erklärung sein\" is rendered as above, and I divided the German sentence \"indem das ... versteht\" into a separate sentence.","- \"derselben\" in \"in der Reihe derselben\" taken to refer to the causes (Ursachen), and the clause \"kann das keine Hinderniß machen\" resolved so that \"this\" is the demand for totality.","- OCR slips corrected: \"einer“ solchen\", \"nicht“\", \"fetbft\" = \"selbst\", \"in!)\", \"To- f ‚talität\"; stray \"E\" dropped."]],"krv-b-2279":["Transcendental hypotheses of the speculative use of reason, and a freedom to make use of hyperphysical grounds, should these chance to serve to supply the lack of physical grounds of explanation, cannot be allowed at all. This is partly because reason is not thereby advanced a step, but on the contrary has the whole progress of its use cut off; partly because this licence must in the end deprive reason of all the fruits of cultivating its own proper soil, namely experience. For if the explanation of nature becomes difficult for us here or there, we have always at hand a transcendent ground of explanation which relieves us of that inquiry. Our investigation then ends, not in insight, but in the complete incomprehensibility of a principle which was so thought out in advance that it had to contain the concept of the absolutely first.",["- \"und eine Freiheit ... sich allenfalls hyperphysischer zu bedienen\": \"eine Freiheit\" rendered \"a freedom\"; \"allenfalls\" given as \"should these chance to serve\". The verb \"zu bedienen\" is governed by \"Freiheit\", so \"to make use of [hyperphysical grounds]\" is supplied in part.","- Restated the final clause as two sentences; \"unsere Nachforschung schließt\" as \"our investigation then ends\".","- Stray colon after \"Denn\" dropped."]],"krv-b-2280":["The second requisite for the acceptability of a hypothesis is its sufficiency to determine *a priori* the consequences which are given. If we are compelled to call in auxiliary hypotheses for this purpose, they give rise to the suspicion that the whole is a mere fiction, since each of them in itself requires the same justi-",["- OCR \"By- | pothese\" read \"Hypothese\"; \"Der: © dacht\" read \"Verdacht\". The sentence runs on into the next passage after \"Recht—\" (\"Rechtfertigung\").","- \"Annehmungswürdigkeit\" rendered \"acceptability\"; \"Stück\" \"requisite\"."]],"krv-b-2281":["fication as was needed by the thought laid down as ground, and therefore they cannot furnish a competent witness. If, on the presupposition of a cause of unlimited perfection, there is indeed in the way of grounds of expla-",["- First clause completes the sentence broken off in the previous passage (\"Recht—\" / \"fertigung\").","- The passage breaks off mid-word and mid-clause (\"Erklärungs-\"); I have not completed it. \"In the way of\" and \"there is indeed\" are supplied to make the fragment read."]],"krv-b-2282":[". . . grounds of all purposiveness, order and magnitude which are to be found in the world, there is no defect, yet that hypothesis still requires new hypotheses to save it against the objections raised by the deviations and evils which show themselves, at least according to our concepts. If the simple self-subsistence of the human soul, which has been laid at the basis of its appearances, is assailed by the difficulties presented by phenomena resembling the alterations of a matter (growth and decay), new hypotheses must be called in to help. These are not indeed without plausibility, but they have no warrant whatsoever, save what they derive from the opinion adopted as the principal ground, for which they are nevertheless supposed to speak. | If the assertions of reason here cited as examples (the incorporeal unity of the soul and the existence of a supreme being) are to count not as hypotheses but as dogmas proved *a priori*, they are not here in question at all. In that case, however, let us take care that the proof has the apodeictic certainty of a demonstration. For to seek to make the reality of such ideas merely probable is an absurd undertaking, as absurd as it would be to propose to prove a proposition of geometry merely probably. Reason, when separated from all experience, can know everything only *a priori* and as necessary, or not at all. Its judgment is therefore never opinion, but is either abstention from all judgment or apodeictic certainty. Opinions and probable judgments as to what belongs to things can occur only as grounds of explanation of what is actually given, or as consequences, according to empirical laws, of what actually lies at the basis; that is, they can occur only in the series of the objects of experience. Outside this field, to opine is as much as to play with\n\n===",["- The passage opens mid-sentence (\"gründen\", presumably the end of a compound); I have translated it as it stands, with ellipsis points.","- Restated the long second and third sentences as several; \"jene\" is taken to be the hypothesis just mentioned.","- Broken off at \"mit Ge-\" (the next passage supplies \"danken\", \"thoughts\"); the \"Ge-\" is not completed here.","- OCR slips corrected silently (Ffinden, Erschei-/nungen, etc.)."]],"krv-b-2283":["thoughts, unless indeed one merely held the opinion, regarding an uncertain way of judging, that one might perhaps find the truth upon it. But although in merely speculative questions of pure reason no hypotheses are admitted as foundations on which to build propositions, they are nevertheless",["- Begins with the completion of \"Gedanken\" from the preceding passage; ends mid-sentence (\"so sind sie . . . dennoch\"), left unfinished as instructed."]],"krv-b-2284":["entirely permissible, if only for the purpose of defending such propositions, if need be; that is to say, not in the dogmatic, but yet in the polemical use. By defence I understand, however, not the multiplication of the grounds of proof for one's | assertion, but the mere frustration of the opponent's pseudo-insights, which are meant to damage the proposition we maintain. Now all synthetic propositions of pure reason have this peculiarity: although he who asserts the reality of certain ideas never knows enough to make his proposition certain, the opponent on the other side can know just as little in asserting the contrary. This equality in the lot of human reason favours neither party in speculative knowledge, and speculative knowledge is therefore the proper battlefield of feuds that can never be settled. But it will be shown in what follows that, in respect of the practical use, reason has none the less a right to assume something which it would in no way be entitled to presuppose, without sufficient grounds of proof, in the field of mere speculation; for all such presuppositions",["- Divided the long sentence on the \"equality of lot\" into two; supplied \"speculative knowledge is therefore\" for the sense of \"da\".","- \"Erkenntnisse\" here is \"knowledge\" (singular, \"im speculativen Erkenntnisse\").","- OCR \"Bez weisgründe\" read as \"Beweisgründe\". Ends mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2285":["detract from the perfection of speculation, with which, however, the practical interest is not in the least concerned. In the practical sphere, then, reason is in possession, and need not prove the legitimacy of its possession, nor indeed could it in fact furnish such proof. The opponent must therefore do the proving. But since he knows just as little about the object in doubt",["- \"Dort\" is taken to refer to the practical use (as against the speculative field just mentioned); I have said so in the English.","- Ends mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2286":["as to be able to establish its non-existence, as the former, who asserts its actuality, knows [about it], an advantage shows itself here on the side of him who maintains something as a practically necessary presupposition (*melior est conditio possidentis*). He is free, namely, as it were in self-defence, to use on behalf of his good cause the very same means as the opponent uses against it, that is to say, hypotheses. These are not intended to strengthen the proof of his cause, but only to show that the opponent understands far too little of the object of the dispute for him to flatter himself with any advantage of speculative insight over us. |",["- Begins mid-sentence (\"um dessen Nichtsein darzuthun\"); \"als der erstere . . . behauptet\" completed with the bracketed \"knows [about it]\".","- OCR \"dec hauptet\" read as \"behauptet\"."]],"krv-b-2287":["Hypotheses, then, are permitted in the field of pure reason only as weapons of war, not in order to found a right upon them, but only to defend it. The opponent, however, we must here always seek in ourselves. For speculative reason in its transcendental use | is in itself dialectical. The objections which are to be feared lie in ourselves. We must search them out, like old claims which are never barred by lapse of time, in order to found an eternal peace upon their annihilation. Outward tranquillity is merely apparent. The germ of these attacks, which lies in the nature of human reason, must be rooted out. But how can we root it out unless we give it freedom, indeed even nourishment, to shoot up as weeds, so as thereby to reveal itself, and then to destroy it, root and all? Devise, therefore, yourselves",["- \"Vernichtignug\" and \"An-/fechtungen\" read as \"Vernichtung\" and \"Anfechtungen\". The \"Sinnet . . . selbst\" imperative is plural (\"you\") and continues in the next passage.","- Ends mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2288":["objections upon which no opponent has yet hit, and lend him even",["- Fragment; continues from and into adjacent passages."]],"krv-b-2289":["weapons, or concede to him the most favourable ground he could wish for! There is nothing here to fear, but much to hope,",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2290":["namely, that you will procure for yourselves a possession which in all future time will never again be challenged.",["- None beyond the line-break artefact (\"o\" before \"Besitz\") dropped silently."]],"krv-b-2291":["To your complete equipment there belong now also the hypotheses of pure reason, which, although only leaden weapons (since they are tempered by no law of experience), yet always avail as much as those which any opponent may employ against you. If",["- \"Wenn euch\" is left as \"If\" because \"euch\" is dative and construed with \"aufstößt\" in the passage two fragments below; \"you\" appears there."]],"krv-b-2292":["then, against the nature of the soul as assumed (in some other, non-speculative respect)",["- Fragment broken at \"auge-/nommene\" (\"angenommene\"); the participle is carried into the next passage."]],"krv-b-2293":["to be immaterial and not subject to any bodily change, you encounter the difficulty that experience nevertheless",["- Restated: \"euch . . . die Schwierigkeit aufstößt\" rendered as \"you encounter the difficulty\". The adjectival phrase of the previous fragment (\"assumed . . .\") is completed here as a participial construction."]],"krv-b-2294":["seems to prove that both the elevation and the derangement of our mental powers are merely different modifications of our organs, you can weaken the force of this proof by assuming that our body",["- \"Geisteskräfte\" rendered \"mental powers\". \"Wenn . . . so\" is carried over from the preceding fragments, with \"you\" supplied for \"euch\".","- \"Zerrüttung\" rendered \"derangement\"."]],"krv-b-2295":["is nothing but the fundamental appearance to which, as condition, the whole faculty of sensibility, and therewith all thinking, is related in the present state (in life). Separation from the body [you may suppose] to be the end of this sensible use of your faculty of knowledge [*Erkenntnißkraft*] and the be-",["- The subjunctive \"sei\" depends on \"annehmt\" in the preceding fragment; the bracketed \"[you may suppose]\" makes this plain in English.","- \"Sinn-/lichkeit\" read as \"Sinnlichkeit\". Broken at \"An-/fang\"; \"beginning\" is completed in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2296":["ginning of the intellectual. The body would then not be the cause of",["- Fragment; begins with the completion of \"Anfang\"."]],"krv-b-2297":["thinking, but a merely restrictive condition of it, and therefore",["- Fragment."]],"krv-b-2298":["to be regarded, indeed, as a furtherance of the sensible and animal life, but all the more",["- Fragment; \"anzusehen\" completes in the next passage (\"to be regarded\", placed here for English order)."]],"krv-b-2299":["as a hindrance to the pure and spiritual life; and the",["- Fragment."]],"krv-b-2300":["dependence of the former upon bodily constitution would prove nothing",["- \"Des ersteren\" is taken to refer to the sensible and animal life."]],"krv-b-2301":["for the dependence of the whole of life upon the state of our organs. But you can go still further, and even perhaps discover new doubts, either",["- \"Or-/ganen\" read as \"Organen\" (organs). Ends mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2302":["not yet raised, or not pursued far enough, which you may find",["- \"ausfindig machen\" is split across this and the next fragment; I have rendered \"find out\" in two parts."]],"krv-b-2303":["out.",["- Final word of the sentence: \"ausfindig machen\", completed here. See previous note."]],"krv-b-2304":["The contingency of procreation, which in human beings as in irrational creatures depends upon opportunity, and, besides this, often upon subsistence, upon the government with its moods and caprices, and often even upon vice, presents a great difficulty for the opinion that a creature endures through eternities, when its life has begun under circumstances so unimportant and so entirely left to our freedom. As regards the continuance of the species as a whole (here on earth), this difficulty counts for little, since chance in the individual case is none the less subject to a rule in the whole. But in respect of each individual, to expect so mighty an effect from such trifling causes certainly seems open to doubt. Against this, however, you can bring forward a transcendental hypothesis: that all life is properly only intelligible, is not subject at all to changes of time, and neither began through birth nor will be ended through death; that this life is nothing but a mere appearance, that is, a sensible representation of the pure spiritual life, and the whole world of sense a mere image, which hovers before our present mode of knowledge and, like a dream, has in itself no objective reality; that if we were to intuit things and ourselves as they are, we should see ourselves in a world of spiritual natures, with which our one true community neither began through birth nor will cease through bodily death (these being mere appearances), and so forth.",["- Sentence division: the final \"daß\"-clauses are kept as one sentence, with semicolons, to preserve the listing of the hypothesis.","- \"Erkenntnißart\" rendered \"mode of knowledge\"; \"Gattung\" as \"species\".","- OCR slips silently corrected (\"ver— is nunftlosen\", \"geringsügigen\", \"ı\", \"1 Sachen\"); the stray \"|\" is retained at the break before \"Welt\" in the source but is not translated: it is a page-break sign, omitted here as an OCR artefact.","- \"Hiewider\" taken as \"Against this\", referring to the difficulty."]],"krv-b-2305":["Although we know not the least of all that we here hypothetically put forward against the attack, nor seriously assert it, and although the whole is not even an idea of reason but merely a concept devised for defence, we yet proceed in this entirely in accordance with reason. For we show the opponent, who thinks he has exhausted all possibility, and who falsely passes off the lack of its empirical conditions as a proof of the complete impossibility of what we believe, only this: that he can no more encompass the whole field of possible things in themselves by mere laws of experience than we can, outside experience, acquire anything on sound grounds for our reason. He who prepares such hypothetical counter-measures against the presumptions of the boldly denying opponent must not be taken to wish to make them his own, as his true opinions. He abandons them as soon as he has disposed of the dogmatic self-conceit of the opponent. For however modest and moderate it may appear when someone adopts a merely refusing and denying attitude towards the assertions of others, yet whenever he seeks to make his objections count as proofs of the contrary, his claim is always no less proud and conceited than if he had taken the side of the affirmative and its assertion.",["- Long second sentence kept as one, with \"For we show…\" following the concessive clause; \"Ernste\" read for OCR \"Ernite\".","- \"für unsere Vernunft … erwerben\" rendered literally; \"auf gegründete Art\" as \"on sound grounds\".","- Line-number residues (as, 2, se, etc.) and the hyphenation breaks silently removed."]],"krv-b-2306":["It can thus be seen that in the speculative employment of reason hypotheses have no validity as opinions in themselves, but only relatively to opposed transcendent presumptions. For the extension of the principles of possible experience to the possibility of things in general is just as transcendent as the assertion of the objective reality of such concepts as can find their objects nowhere save outside the limits of all possible experience. What pure reason judges assertorically must (like everything that reason knows) be necessary, or it is nothing at all. Accordingly reason in fact contains no opinions whatever. The hypotheses referred to are only problematic judgments, which at least cannot be refuted, although they certainly cannot be proved by anything. They are therefore purely private opinions; yet they cannot properly be dispensed with (even for inner tranquillity) against scruples which stir. In this character, however, we must preserve them, and must carefully guard against their appearing as accredited in themselves and as having some absolute validity, and so drowning reason [continued in the next passage]",["- \"durch T nicht bewiesen\" is corrupt OCR; probably \"durch nichts\" (\"by anything\"), rendered so. Doubtful reading.","- \"nicht als)\" read \"nicht als\"; \"ja\" rendered as emphatic \"must carefully guard\".","- Sentence breaks mid-clause; the bracketed note marks only that it continues, the German being broken off.","- \"sich regende Scrupel\" taken as \"scruples which stir\"."]],"krv-b-2307":["in fictions and delusions.",["- Continues the preceding passage (\"die Vernunft … ersäufen\"): \"drown reason in fictions and delusions\"."]],"krv-b-2308":["Of the First Chapter,\nFourth Section.",["- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Chapter\"; the heading is of the Transcendental Doctrine of Method."]],"krv-b-2309":["The Discipline of Pure Reason in Respect of",["- Title continues in the next passage (\"ihrer Beweise\")."]],"krv-b-2310":["its Proofs.",["- \"Beweise\" rendered \"proofs\"."]],"krv-b-2311":["Proofs of transcendental and synthetic propositions have this peculiarity, among all proofs of a synthetic knowledge *a priori*, that in them reason may not apply itself directly, by means of its concepts, to the object, but must first establish the objective validity of the concepts and the possibility of their synthesis *a priori*. This is not merely a necessary rule of caution; it concerns the very essence and possibility of the proofs themselves. If I am to go beyond the concept of an object *a priori*, this is impossible without a special guide lying outside this concept. In mathematics it is the intuition *a priori* that guides my synthesis, and there all inferences can be drawn immediately from pure intuition. In transcendental knowledge, so long as it has to do merely with concepts of the understanding, this guiding line is possible experience. The proof does not show that the given concept (e.g. of that which happens) leads directly to another concept (that of a cause); for such a transition would be a leap which could not be justified at all. What it shows is that experience itself, and therefore the object of experience, would be impossible without such a connection. The proof must therefore at the same time indicate the possibility of arriving synthetically and *a priori* at a certain knowledge of things which was not contained in the concept of them. Without this attention, proofs run like water that has broken its banks, wildly and across country, wherever the slope of hidden association chances to lead them. The semblance of conviction, which rests on subjective causes of association and is taken for insight into a natural affinity, cannot counterbalance the misgiving which must justly arise in face of such hazardous steps. Hence all attempts [to meet this], [and] the admissions of the connoisseurs, have been in vain; and before transcendental criticism appeared, men preferred, since they could not abandon this principle, to appeal defiantly to common sense (a refuge which always proves that the cause of reason is desperate), rather than to attempt new dogmatic proofs.",["- \"Im transscendentalen Erkenntniß\" left as \"In transcendental knowledge\" (the German gender is irregular).","- \"Afo mußte\" read \"Also mußte\"; \"Gr- f kenntniß\" read \"Erkenntniß\".","- \"Daher sind auch alle Versuche, Geständnisse der Kenner vergeblich gewesen\" looks corrupt or elided; I supplied \"[to meet this]\" and \"[and]\" in brackets. Doubtful reading.","- \"3. B.\" read \"z. B.\"; \"gefunden\" read \"gesunden\" (sound), hence \"common sense\".","- Marginal numerals and page-break signs removed as OCR residue."]],"krv-b-2312":["But if the proposition for which a proof is to be given is an assertion of pure reason, and if I propose by means of mere ideas to go beyond my concepts of experience, then the proof must contain, as a necessary condition of its probative force, the justification of such a step of synthesis (if indeed it be possible). Hence, however plausible the supposed proof of the simple nature of our thinking substance from the unity of apperception may seem, an unavoidable objection stands against it. Absolute simplicity is not a concept that can be related immediately to a perception; as an idea it has merely to be inferred. It is therefore quite impossible to see how mere consciousness, which is contained in all thinking, or at least can be so contained, should lead me to the consciousness and knowledge of a thing in which thinking alone can be contained, although in so far consciousness is a simple representation. For when I represent to myself the force of my body in motion, the body is in this respect an absolute unity for me, and my representation of it is simple. I can therefore express this representation by the motion of a point, since the body's volume here plays no part, and can be made as small as we please without diminution of the force, and so can be thought as located in a point. But I shall not conclude from this that, if nothing is given to me save the moving force of a body, the body can be thought as a simple substance, on the ground that its representation abstracts from all magnitude of spatial content and is therefore simple. Now it is by seeing that the simple in abstraction is quite different from the simple in the object, and that the 'I', which in the former sense contains no manifold within itself, may in the latter sense, where it signifies the soul itself, be a very complex concept, that is, may contain and designate very much under it, that I discover a paralogism. But in order to have a presentiment of this beforehand (for without such a preliminary conjecture no suspicion against the proof would be conceived), it is absolutely necessary to have at hand a permanent criterion of the possibility of those synthetic propositions which are to prove more than experience can give. This criterion consists in the following: that the proof be conducted not directly to the predicate required, but only by means of a principle of the possibility of extending our given concept *a priori* to ideas, and of realising these. If this caution is always used, if, before the proof is even attempted, we first consult prudently with ourselves how and on what ground of hope we may expect such an extension through pure reason, and whence in such a case we are to obtain these insights, which cannot be developed out of concepts and also cannot be anticipated in relation to possible experience, we can spare ourselves much toilsome and yet fruitless effort. We then demand nothing of reason that manifestly exceeds its power; or rather we subject it, when in fits of its speculative passion for extension it does not willingly let itself be restrained, to the discipline of abstinence.",[]],"krv-b-2313":["The first rule is accordingly this: to attempt no transcendental proofs without having first considered, and justified to ourselves, whence we propose to obtain the principles on which we intend to build them, and by what right we can expect success in the inferences from them. If they are principles of the understanding (for instance, of causality), it is vain to try by their means to reach ideas of pure reason; for those principles hold only of objects of possible experience. If they are to be principles from pure reason, then again all the labour is in vain. For reason does have such principles, but as objective principles they are one and all dialectical, and can at most be valid only as regulative principles of the systematically connected employment of experience. But if such alleged proofs are already in existence, then oppose to the deceptive conviction the *non liquet* of your matured judgment; and though you cannot yet see through the illusion, you have a perfect right to demand the deduction of the principles employed in it, which, if they are supposed to have sprung from mere reason, can never be provided for you. Thus you need not even trouble yourselves with unravelling and refuting each groundless illusion, but can dismiss the whole mass of a dialectic inexhaustible in artifices, all at once, at the tribunal of a critical reason which demands laws.",[]],"krv-b-2314":["The second peculiarity of transcendental proofs is this: that for each transcendental proposition only a single proof can be found. If I have to infer not from concepts but from the intuition which corresponds to a concept, whether it be a pure intuition, as in mathematics, or an empirical intuition, as in natural science, the intuition which I take as basis supplies me with a manifold material for synthetic propositions. I can connect this material in more than one way, and since I may start from more than one point, I can arrive at the same proposition by different paths.",[]],"krv-b-2315":["Every transcendental proposition, however, proceeds from a single concept only, and states the synthetic condition of the possibility of the object in accordance with that concept. The ground of proof can therefore be only one, since outside this concept there is nothing further by which the object could be determined; the proof can accordingly contain nothing more than the determination of an object in general in accordance with this concept, which is likewise only one. In the Transcendental Analytic, for example, we derived the principle that everything which happens has a cause from the single condition of the objective possibility of a concept of that which happens in general: namely, that the determination of an occurrence in time, and therefore this occurrence as belonging to experience, would be impossible if it did not stand under such a dynamical rule. This is also the only possible ground of proof; for only in so far as an object is determined for the concept by means of the law of causality has the represented occurrence objective validity, that is, truth. Other proofs of this principle have indeed been attempted, for instance from contingency. But when this is examined in the light, no mark of contingency can be found save happening, that is, existence preceded by the non-existence of the object; and so we always come back to the same ground of proof. If the proposition 'everything that thinks is simple' is to be proved, we do not dwell upon the manifold of thinking, but keep solely to the concept of the 'I', which is simple and to which all thinking is related. The same holds of the transcendental proof of the existence of God, which rests solely upon the reciprocity [*Reciprocabilität*] of the concepts of the most real and the necessary being, and can be sought nowhere else.",["Passages are given in the requested order; the output format mixed the two layouts, so the notes below are grouped by id.","- 2312: \"Sit … fol\" read as *Ist … soll* (OCR); \"Seele\" etc. kept. \"unz mittelbar\", \"hauch\", \"ge— dacht\" corrected silently. The footnote marker \"35)\" after \"Idee\" dropped as OCR debris.","- 2312: the long sentence beginning \"So scheinbar daher\" was divided; \"Hiedurch nun, daß …\" restated as \"Now it is by seeing that …\". \"im ersteren Verstande\" rendered \"in the former sense\".","- 2312: \"Behutsamkeit\" = caution; \"Disciplin der Enthaltsamkeit\" = discipline of abstinence.","- 2313: \"ges I! denkt\" read as *gedenkt*; \"der? Causalität\", \"Der- si nunft\" read as *der Causalität*, *reinen Vernunft*. \"einem kritischen Vernunft\" is in the source; rendered \"a critical reason\" (as if *einer*).","- 2313: *non liquet* kept in Latin. \"Erfah: rungsgebrauchs\" = employment of experience.","- 2314: \"verfnüpfen\" read as *verknüpfen*; \"schlie⸗ßen\" as *schließen*.","- 2315: \"Hez a griffe\" read as *Begriffe*; \"Wedien\" read as *Wesen*. \"Begebenheit\" = occurrence; \"dynamischen Regel\" = dynamical rule. \"Reciprocabilität\" bracketed as a technical term.","- 2315: \"dieser\" in \"wenn dieser beim Lichte betrachtet wird\" taken to refer to *Zufälligkeit*, i.e. the concept of contingency."]],"krv-b-2316":["This cautionary remark greatly reduces the scope of the criticism of rational assertions. Where reason conducts its business by mere concepts, only one proof is possible, if indeed any proof is possible at all. When, therefore, we see the dogmatist come forward with ten proofs, we may be quite sure that he has none. For if he had one which (as must be the case in matters of pure reason) proved apodeictically, what need would he have of the others? His intention is merely that of the parliamentary advocate: one argument is for this judge, another for that, so as to turn to account the weakness of his judges, who, without going deeply into the matter, and in order to be quickly rid of the business, seize upon the first thing that happens to strike them, and decide accordingly.\n\nThe third peculiar rule of pure reason, when it is subjected to discipline in respect of transcendental proofs, is this: that its proofs must never be apagogic, but always ostensive. The direct or ostensive proof is, in every kind of knowledge, that which combines conviction of the truth with insight into the sources of that truth. The apagogic proof, on the other hand, can indeed yield certainty, but not comprehensibility of the truth in regard to its connection with the grounds of its possibility. The latter kind of proof is therefore more an expedient in case of need than a procedure which satisfies all the aims of reason. Yet these proofs have an advantage in evidence over direct proofs, in that contradiction always carries with it more clearness in the representation than even the best connection does, and thereby approaches more nearly to the intuitive character of a demonstration.",["- Passage opens mid-section; \"Kritik der Vernunftbehauptungen\" rendered \"criticism of rational assertions\", the hyphenated line-break joined silently.","- OCR garbles silently corrected: \"fit\" → \"ist\", \"ac\" → \"apagogische [Beweis]\" (supplied from the context of the sentence), \"Anjey ung\" → \"Ansehung\", \"Wi. derspruch\" → \"Widerspruch\", \"din)?\" → \"selbst die\" (conjectural; the sense requires \"even the best connection\"), \"Demonstraß tion\" → \"Demonstration\".","- \"Das Erste Beste, was ihnen geben auffällt\": \"geben\" is an OCR slip, probably for \"gleich\"; I render \"what happens to strike them\".","- \"Dieser/jener\" in the advocate comparison: I rendered \"this judge… that\" to make the point plain; the German has only \"für diesen… für jenen\".","- \"Doch haben dies\": read as \"Doch haben diese\"; \"diese\" taken to refer to apagogic proofs.","- \"Überzeugung … Einsicht\" rendered \"conviction… insight\"; \"Begreiflichkeit\" \"comprehensibility\"; \"Erkenntniß\" \"knowledge\".","- Paragraph division after \"decide accordingly\" is mine, marking the new topic; the passage's \"I\" at the end is OCR residue and omitted."]],"krv-b-2317":["The true reason why apagogic proofs are employed in the various sciences is probably this. When the grounds from which a certain item of knowledge is to be derived are too manifold, or lie too deeply hidden, we try whether it cannot be reached through its consequences. Now the *modus ponens*, that is, inferring the truth of a piece of knowledge from the truth of its consequences, would be permissible only if all the possible consequences of it are true; for then only a single ground is possible for these consequences, and this ground is therefore the true one. But this procedure is impracticable, since it exceeds our powers to see all the possible consequences of any proposition we may assume. Nevertheless this kind of inference is made use of, though admittedly with a certain indulgence, when the aim is to prove something merely as an hypothesis, in that we allow the inference by analogy: that if as many consequences as we have ever tried agree well with an assumed ground, all the remaining possible ones will agree with it also. For this reason a hypothesis can never by this route be transformed into demonstrated truth.\n\nThe *modus tollens* of inferences of reason, which proceed from the consequences to the grounds, proves not only with complete strictness but also with exceeding ease. For if even a single false consequence can be drawn from a proposition, that proposition is false. Instead, then, of running through the whole series of grounds in an ostensive proof, which can lead to the truth of a piece of knowledge by way of complete insight into its possibility, we need only find a single false consequence among those that follow from its opposite; then this opposite is also false, and therefore the knowledge which we had to prove is true.",["- OCR slips corrected silently: \"ver Y schiedenen\", \"Er“ kenntniß\", \"Den |\" → \"Denn\", \"[lieben\" → \"gehen\" is not certain; I follow the sense \"from the consequences to the grounds\" and read \"[lieben\" as \"gehen\" (doubtful reading).","- \"fol\" → \"soll\"; \"zwf\" → \"zu\"; \"Gründ |\" → \"Gründe\"; \"führen!\" → \"führen\".","- The apparatus sign | appears in the OCR only as line-break debris; I have not reproduced it, as it marks no editorial insertion here.","- First paragraph's \"ist … möglich, der also auch der wahre ist\" rendered with \"this ground\", clarifying the antecedent.","- The paragraph break before *modus tollens* is mine.","- \"Kräfte\" rendered \"powers\"."]],"krv-b-2318":["The apagogic mode of proof can be permissible only in those sciences where it is impossible to substitute what is subjective in our representations for what is objective, that is, for the knowledge of what is in the object. Where the latter prevails, it must frequently happen that the opposite of a given proposition either contradicts merely the subjective conditions of thought, and not the object; or that the two propositions contradict each other only under a subjective condition which is falsely taken to be objective, and that since the condition is false, both may be false, without its being possible to infer from the falsity of the one to the truth of the other.\n\nIn mathematics this subreption is impossible; and it is there, accordingly, that apagogic proofs have their proper place. In natural science, since everything there is grounded upon empirical intuitions, such a surreptitious substitution can for the most part be prevented by many comparative observations; but this mode of proof is there, for the most part, of little importance. The transcendental attempts of pure reason, however, are all made within the proper medium of dialectical illusion, that is, of the subjective, which presents itself to reason in its premisses as objective, or even forces itself upon it.\n\nHere, then, where synthetic propositions are concerned, it cannot be permitted at all to justify one's assertions by refuting the opposite. For either this refutation is nothing but the mere representation of the conflict of the opposed opinion with the subjective conditions of comprehensibility by our reason, which does nothing to warrant our rejecting the thing itself on that account. (Thus, for instance, unconditioned necessity in the existence of a being absolutely cannot be comprehended by us, and consequently opposes itself, subjectively, and with right, to every speculative proof of a necessary supreme being, but opposes itself without right to the possibility of such an original being in itself.) Or else both parties, the asserting and the denying, deceived by transcendental illusion, lay at the foundation an impossible concept of the object; and then the rule applies: *non entis nulla sunt praedicata*, that is, whatever was asserted of the object, whether affirmatively or negatively, is in both cases incorrect, and we cannot attain to knowledge of the truth apagogically, by the refutation of the opposite.\n\nIf, for example, it is presupposed that the sensible world is given in itself in its totality, it is false that it must be either infinite in space, or finite and limited, because both are false. For appearances (as mere representations) which yet are in themselves (as objects) something [real: \"Nes en\" garbled] are something impossible; and the infinity of this imagined whole would indeed be unconditioned, but (since everything in appearances is conditioned) would contradict the unconditioned determination of magnitude which is yet presupposed in the concept.",["- Sentence \"Denn Erscheinungen … Nes en sind\" is garbled in the OCR (\"Nes en\"); I leave a bracketed gap-marker rather than fill it. The standard text reads \"Dinge an sich selbst\", which I do not insert; treat the clause as doubtful.","- \"lan sich selbst\" read \"an sich selbst\"; \"dem, Objectiven\" → \"dem Objectiven\"; \"Erschlei— chung\" → \"Erschleichung\", rendered \"surreptitious substitution\"; \"Subreption\" \"subreption\".","- First long sentence divided and the parenthetical on necessary being kept in parentheses, restated after \"thing itself on that account\".","- \"Begreiflichkeit\" \"comprehensibility\"; \"Gegenstand\" \"object\"; \"Erscheinungen\" \"appearances\"; \"Größenbestimmung\" \"determination of magnitude\".","- Paragraph divisions are mine, except that \"Hier nun…\" and the example follow the sense breaks."]],"krv-b-2319":["The apagogic mode of proof is also the real delusion by which the admirers of the thoroughness of our dogmatic reasoners have at all times been held in suspense. It is, so to speak, the champion who seeks to prove the honour and indisputable right of the party he has taken up by offering to fight anyone who cares to doubt it. But such boasting settles nothing in the matter itself; it determines only the relative strength of the opponents, and indeed only on the side of the one who acts as aggressor. The spectators, seeing that each in his turn is now victor, now vanquished, often take occasion from this to doubt sceptically the very object of the dispute. But they have no ground for doing so, and it is enough to call out to them: *non defensoribus istis tempus eget*. Each must conduct his case by means of a legal proof, carried out through a transcendental deduction of the grounds of proof, that is, directly, so that we may see what his claims of reason have to offer on their own behalf. For if his opponent rests on subjective grounds, he is no doubt easily refuted, but without advantage to the dogmatist, who commonly adheres in the same way to the subjective causes of judgment, and can likewise be driven into a corner by his opponent. If, however, both parties proceed merely directly, they will either themselves become aware of the difficulty, indeed the impossibility, of discovering the title to their assertions, and in the end will be able to appeal only to prescription; or else criticism will readily discover the dogmatic illusion, and will compel pure reason to give up its pretensions, pushed too high in speculative employment, and to withdraw within the limits of its proper territory, namely practical principles.",[]],"krv-b-2320":["Transcendental Doctrine of Method\nChapter II.\nThe Canon of Pure Reason.",[]],"krv-b-2321":["It is humiliating to human reason that in its pure employment it accomplishes nothing, and indeed even requires a discipline to curb its extravagances and to guard against the delusions which arise from them. On the other hand, it elevates reason and gives it confidence in itself that it can and must exercise this discipline itself, without allowing any other censorship over it; and also that the limits which it is constrained to set to its speculative employment at the same time restrict the sophistical pretensions of every opponent, and so can secure against all attack whatever may remain to it of its previously exaggerated demands. The greatest, and perhaps the sole, utility of all philosophy of pure reason is therefore only negative. It serves, that is, not as an organon for the extension [of knowledge], but as a discipline for the determination of limits; and instead of discovering truth, has only the quiet merit of guarding against error. |\n\nYet there must somewhere be a source of positive modes of knowledge which belong to the domain of pure reason, and which perhaps give occasion to errors only through misunderstanding, while in fact constituting the goal of reason's zeal. For to what other cause could the irrepressible desire to gain a firm footing somewhere beyond the limits of experience be ascribed? Reason has a presentiment of objects which have a great interest for it. It enters upon the path of mere speculation in order to approach them; but they fly from it. Presumably it may hope for better fortune on the only path that still remains to it, namely that of practical employment.\n\nBy a canon I understand the sum of the *a priori* principles of the correct employment of certain faculties of knowledge in general. Thus general logic, in its analytic part, is a canon for understanding and reason in general, but only in respect of form, since it abstracts from all content. So the transcendental analytic was the canon of the pure understanding, for this alone is capable of true synthetic knowledge *a priori*. But where no correct employment of a faculty of knowledge is possible, there is no canon. Now all synthetic knowledge of pure reason in its speculative employment is, according to all the proofs hitherto given, altogether impossible. Hence there is no canon whatever of its speculative employment (for that employment is through and through dialectical), and all transcendental logic is in this respect nothing but discipline. Consequently, if there is anywhere a correct employment of pure reason, in which case there must also be a canon of it, this canon will concern not the speculative but the practical employment of reason, which we shall now proceed to investigate.",[]],"krv-b-2322":["Of the Canon of Pure Reason\nSection I.\nThe Ultimate End of the Pure Employment of Our Reason.",[]],"krv-b-2323":["Reason is impelled by a propensity of its nature to pass beyond the empirical employment, to venture in a pure employment and by means of mere ideas to the utmost limits of all knowledge, and to find rest only in the completion of its circle, in a self-subsisting systematic whole. Is this endeavour grounded merely on its speculative interest, or rather solely and | exclusively on its practical interest?",[]],"krv-b-2324":["I shall now set aside the success which pure reason has in speculative aims, and ask only about the problems the solution of which constitutes its ultimate end, whether reason attains this end or not, and in respect of which all other ends have merely the value of means. These highest ends, in accordance with the nature of reason, must in turn have unity, in order that they may jointly promote that interest of humanity which is subordinate to no higher one.",["- krv-b-2319: \"apagogische Beweisart\" rendered \"apagogic mode of proof\"; the long sentence on the champion is divided at the semicolon, and \"Vernünftler\" is rendered \"reasoners\" (Kant's sophistical sense is implied).","- krv-b-2319: the Latin tag *non defensoribus istis tempus eget* (Virgil) is left untranslated, as in the source. The stray OCR superscripts and letters (\"2\", \"z\", \"a\", \"3\", \"Zu: schauer\") are silently corrected.","- krv-b-2319: \"respectiven Stärke\" rendered \"relative strength\"; \"Verjährung\" rendered \"prescription\" (the legal term). \"Kritik\" rendered \"criticism\" here.","- krv-b-2321: \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" rendered \"faculties of knowledge\"; \"Erkenntnisse\" as \"modes of knowledge\"; \"Erkenntnißkraft\" as \"faculty of knowledge\". Square-bracketed \"[of knowledge]\" is supplied after \"extension\" (German: \"Erweiterung\").","- krv-b-2321: the second \"Ziel der Beeiferung\" is rendered \"goal of reason's zeal\"; \"Beeiferung\" is uncommon, and the OCR is slightly broken here. The sentence \"Der größte … Irrthümer zu verhüten\" is divided into two to give smoothness.","- krv-b-2321: the OCR reads \"ale synthetische\" and \"Hist\" and \"durch!“ und durch\"; corrected silently to \"alle\", \"ist\", \"durch und durch\". The sign | in the text is kept where it stands.","- krv-b-2323: \"Hang\" rendered \"propensity\"; the sign | is kept where it stands, and the passage's final sentence is a question as in the source.","- krv-b-2324: \"Glück\" rendered \"success\" (not \"happiness\"), since it is said of reason's fortune in speculation. \"Aufgaben\" rendered \"problems\"."]],"krv-b-2325":["The final aim to which the speculation of reason in its transcendental employment is ultimately directed concerns three objects: the freedom of the will, the immortality of the soul, and the existence of God. In regard to all three the merely speculative interest of reason is | very slight; and for the sake of this interest alone a wearisome labour of transcendental enquiry, struggling with unceasing obstacles, would hardly be undertaken. For of all the discoveries that might be made in these matters, we can make no use that would show its utility *in concreto*, that is, in the investigation of nature. The will may be free, but this can concern only the intelligible cause of our willing. For as regards the phenomena in which the will expresses itself, that is, our actions, we must, in accordance with an inviolable fundamental maxim, without which we cannot exercise reason in its empirical employment, explain them in no other way than all other appearances of nature, namely, in accordance with her unalterable laws.\n\nSecondly, the spiritual nature of the soul (and with it its immortality) may be capable of being understood. Yet no account can be taken of this, either as a ground of explanation of the appearances of this present life, or in regard to the special character of the future state. For our concept of an incorporeal nature is merely negative; it does not in the least extend our knowledge, and offers no suitable material for inferences, save such as can pass only as fictions, and these philosophy does not permit.\n\nThirdly, even if the existence of a supreme intelligence were proved, we should indeed be able to render comprehensible to ourselves from it the purposiveness in the constitution and order of the world in general. But we should by no means be entitled to derive from it any particular arrangement or order, or, where it is not perceived, boldly to infer it. For it is a necessary rule of the speculative employment of reason not to pass over natural causes, nor to abandon what we can learn from experience, in order to derive something we know from what wholly transcends all our knowledge.\n\nIn a word, these three propositions remain always transcendent for speculative reason, and have no immanent employment, that is, none admissible for objects of experience and therefore of some utility to us. Considered in themselves, they are wholly idle, and yet at the same time extremely arduous, exertions of our reason.",[]],"krv-b-2326":["If, then, these three cardinal propositions are not at all needed for knowledge [*Wissen*], and yet are urgently recommended to us by our reason, their importance must properly concern the practical only.",[]],"krv-b-2327":["The practical is all that is possible through freedom. But when the conditions for the exercise of our free choice [*Willkür*] are empirical, reason can have only a regulative employment in regard to them, and can serve only to bring about unity of empirical laws. In the doctrine of prudence, for instance, the union of all the ends set us by our inclinations in the one end, happiness, together with the harmonising of the means of attaining it, constitutes the whole business | of reason. For this reason reason can supply only pragmatic laws of free conduct for the attainment of the ends recommended to us by the senses; it cannot supply pure laws determined completely *a priori*. Pure practical laws, on the other hand, whose end is given completely *a priori* by reason, and which command not empirically conditioned but absolutely, would be products of pure reason. Such are the moral laws. These alone, therefore, belong to the practical employment of pure reason, and admit of a canon. |",[]],"krv-b-2328":["The whole equipment of reason, in the work which may be entitled pure philosophy, is in fact directed only to the three problems mentioned. These themselves, however, have a more remote purpose, namely, what is to be done if the will is free, if there is a God and a future world. Since this concerns our conduct in relation to the highest end, the ultimate aim of nature, which has wisely provided for us in the constitution of our reason, is properly directed to the moral alone. |",[]],"krv-b-2329":["But caution is needed. Since we are directing our attention to an object which is foreign to transcendental philosophy [footnote marker], we must avoid straying into episodes and violating the unity of the system; and, on the other hand, in saying too little of this new material, we must not fail in clearness or conviction. I hope to achieve both by keeping as close as possible to the",[]],"krv-b-2330":["[Footnote:] All practical concepts relate to objects of satisfaction or dislike, that is, of pleasure and displeasure, and therefore at least indirectly to objects of our feeling. But since feeling is not a faculty of representation of things, but lies outside the whole faculty of knowledge, the elements of our judgments, so far as they relate to pleasure or displeasure, and so to the practical, do not belong to the [*Inbegriff*, sum total] of transcendental philosophy, which is concerned solely with pure modes of knowledge *a priori*.",[]],"krv-b-2331":["transcendental, and by setting wholly aside whatever may here be psychological, that is, empirical.",[]],"krv-b-2332":["And here it must first be remarked that for the present I shall make use of the concept",[]],"krv-b-2333":["of freedom only in its practical meaning, and shall here set aside, as already disposed of above, the concept of freedom in its transcendental signification, which cannot be presupposed empirically as a ground for explaining",[]],"krv-b-2334":["appearances, but is itself a problem for reason. A power of choice [*Willkür*] is merely animal (*arbitrium brutum*) if it can be determined only through sensuous impulses, that is, pathologically.",[]],"krv-b-2335":["(Continuation of the sentence in the preceding passage.) [The text of this passage, \"als durch sinnliche Antriebe, d. i. pathologisch, bestimmt werden kann\", is already included above.]",[]],"krv-b-2336":["But a power of choice which can be determined independently of sensuous impulses, and therefore through",[]],"krv-b-2337":["motives represented only by reason, is entitled free choice (*arbitrium liberum*), and everything",[]],"krv-b-2338":["connected with it, whether as ground or as consequence, is entitled practical.",[]],"krv-b-2339":["Practical freedom can be proved by experience. For it is not merely what stimulates, that is, immediately affects the senses, that determines human choice; we have a",[]],"krv-b-2340":["capacity",[]],"krv-b-2341":["to overcome the impressions made on our sensuous faculty of desire, through representations of what, even in a more remote [footnote marker] fashion, is useful or harmful. These deliberations as to what is desirable in respect of our whole state, that is, what is good and useful, rest on reason. Reason therefore also gives laws which",[]],"krv-b-2342":["are imperatives, that is, objective laws of freedom, and which",[]],"krv-b-2343":["say what ought to happen, even though perhaps it never happens, and",[]],"krv-b-2344":["differ in this from laws of nature, which deal only with what happens. For this reason they are also entitled practical laws.",[]],"krv-b-2345":["But whether reason itself, in these actions through which it prescribes laws,",[]],"krv-b-2346":["is not in turn determined by other influences,",[]],"krv-b-2347":["and whether what, in relation to sensuous impulses, is entitled freedom may not, in relation to higher and more remote efficient causes, again be nature, does not concern us in the practical sphere, where we ask reason alone, in the first instance, for the rule of conduct; it is a",["- krv-b-2325: I divided the long opening sentence at \"weil\" and gave the three numbered points their own paragraphs; \"mag … so kann doch\" is rendered \"may … yet\". Final sentence is smoothed. \"Er—klärung\" and similar hyphenations silently restored.","- krv-b-2325: \"Kenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\", as is Erkenntniß, since no distinction can be kept in English; \"Phänomene\" kept as \"phenomena\". \"Anstalt\" is rendered \"arrangement\".","- krv-b-2326: \"Wissen\" is given in brackets to distinguish it from Erkenntnis; \"Willkür\" is rendered \"free choice\" or \"power of choice\", with the German bracketed at first occurrence.","- krv-b-2327: The stray quotation mark after \"a priori\" is an OCR slip and removed; \"ein Kanon\" is rendered \"a canon\"; the \"in den einigen\" is read as \"in dem einigen\" (the one end). Sentence divided at \"die um deswillen\".","- krv-b-2329: The footnote sign (OCR \"fremd“)\") is marked as [footnote marker]; the sentence breaks off at \"am\" and is left unfinished, continued in krv-b-2331. Also \"Behutsamkeit\" is rendered \"caution\".","- krv-b-2330: OCR garbled in places: \"Luft\" read as \"Lust\"; \"Su- & begriff\" read as \"Inbegriff\"; \"Erkenntufsseczf\" read as \"Erkenntnisse\". \"Vorstellungskraft\" is rendered \"faculty of representation\".","- krv-b-2335: The passage only repeats the end of the previous sentence; I did not retranslate it but flagged this, as it is already covered in krv-b-2334 (where I included the full sentence). This is a departure from the strict one-fragment-one-translation procedure.","- krv-b-2339–2341: OCR \"5\", \"so\" and similar are line numbers/slips and dropped; \"Ver—mögen\" is split across 2340/2341 and is rendered \"capacity\" in 2340 with the rest following. The footnote marker after \"entferntere\" is retained; its text is not in the passages given."]],"krv-b-2348":["...a merely speculative question, which we may set aside so long as our purpose is directed to what we are to do or to leave undone. Through experience, then, we know practical freedom to be one of the causes in nature, namely, a causality of reason in the determination of the will. Transcendental freedom, on the other hand, demands that this reason itself (in respect of its causality in beginning a series of appearances) be independent of all determining causes of the sensible world. In so far it seems to run counter to the law of nature, and therefore to all possible experience, and so remains a problem. But this problem does not belong to reason in its practical employment. In a canon of pure reason we have therefore to deal with only two questions, which concern the practical interest of pure reason and in respect of which a canon of its employment must be possible: Is there a God? Is there a future life? The question of transcendental freedom concerns merely speculative knowledge [*Wissen*]. We can set it aside as quite indifferent when the concern is with the practical; and a sufficient discussion of it is already to be found in the antinomy of pure reason.",["- Restated the long sentence on practical and transcendental freedom as several sentences; \"indessen daß\" is rendered as a contrast (\"on the other hand\").","- The passage begins mid-sentence; the opening ellipsis marks this. \"Naturursachen\" is rendered \"causes in nature\". The OCR break \"näms lich\" is silently corrected.","- *Wissen* is rendered \"knowledge\" and marked with the German to distinguish it from *Erkenntnis*.","- \"welche wir... können\" has a relative pronoun in the feminine plural form, which cannot agree with \"Frage\"; I took it to refer to the question."]],"krv-b-2349":["Of the Canon of Pure Reason\nSection II.\nOn the Ideal of the Highest Good, as a Determining Ground",["- The heading is continued in the next passage (\"Bestimmungsgrunde des letzten Zwecks…\"). \"Als einem\" is held open here, so the English phrase \"as a Determining Ground\" anticipates the next fragment's noun."]],"krv-b-2350":["of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason.",["- Completes the heading begun in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-2351":["In its speculative employment reason led us through the field of experiences. Since it can never find complete satisfaction there, it led us on to speculative ideas, which in the end brought us back again to experience. They thus fulfilled their purpose in a manner which, though useful, was not at all in accord with our expectation. One attempt still remains to us: whether pure reason may also be met with in practical employment; whether in that employment it leads to the ideas which reach the highest ends of pure reason that we have just stated; and whether reason may thus, from the standpoint of its practical interest, be unable to grant what in respect of the speculative interest it entirely denies us.",["- Divided the first sentence into three. \"Nicht... könne\" at the end depends on \"ob\"; I kept the question form.","- The OCR \"praftischen\" is silently corrected to \"praktischen\"; \"Interesse\" is taken as the genitive/dative form \"Interesses\" in sense."]],"krv-b-2352":["All the interest of my reason (the speculative as well as the practical) is united in the following three questions:",[]],"krv-b-2353":["1. What can I know?\n2. What ought I to do?\n3. What may I hope?",["- *Wissen* is rendered \"know\"."]],"krv-b-2354":["The first question is merely speculative. We have, as I flatter myself, exhausted all possible answers to it, and have at last found the one with which reason must indeed content itself, and with which, if it pays no regard to the practical, it also has cause to be satisfied. But we have remained as far removed from the two great ends towards which this whole endeavour of pure reason was properly directed, as if from love of ease we had declined the labour at the very outset. If, then, the concern is with knowledge [*Wissen*], this at least is certain and settled: that in respect of those two problems it can never fall to our lot.",["- The German sentence is irregular (\"sind aber... geblieben\" follows a \"haben\" clause with a changed subject); I divided it and supplied \"But\" to carry the contrast.","- \"Gemächlichkeit\" is rendered \"love of ease\"."]],"krv-b-2355":["The second question is merely practical. As such it may indeed belong to pure reason, but it is then not transcendental but moral, and so cannot of itself occupy our critique.",["- \"Kritik\" is rendered \"critique\". The OCR break \"son dem\" is silently corrected."]],"krv-b-2356":["The third question, namely, If I now do what I ought, what may I then hope?, is at once practical and theoretical. The practical serves only as a clue to the answer of the theoretical question, and, if this question is pitched high, of the speculative question. For all hoping aims at happiness, and stands in the same relation to the practical and the moral law as knowing and the law of nature stand to the theoretical knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] of things. The former finally issues in the inference that something is (something which determines the ultimate possible end) because something ought to happen; the latter, in the inference that something is (something which acts as supreme cause) because something happens.",["- Restated the second sentence of the German as two. \"Wenn diese hoch geht\" is rendered \"if this question is pitched high\" (the subject is the theoretical question).","- \"Jenes... dieses\" are rendered \"the former... the latter\", referring to hoping/the moral law and knowing/the law of nature respectively."]],"krv-b-2357":["Happiness is the satisfaction of all our inclinations (extensively, in respect of their manifoldness; intensively, in respect of their degree; and protensively, in respect of their duration). The practical law which springs from the motive of happiness I entitle pragmatic (rule of prudence). The law, if there be such, which has no other motive than the worthiness to be happy, I entitle moral (law of morality). The former advises what is to be done if we wish to partake of happiness; the latter commands how we ought to behave in order merely to become worthy of happiness. The former is based upon empirical principles; for only by means of experience can I know either what inclinations there are which desire satisfaction, or what the natural causes are which can bring about their satisfaction. The latter abstracts from inclinations and from the natural means of satisfying them. It considers only the freedom of a rational being in general, and the necessary conditions under which alone freedom harmonises, according to principles, with the distribution of happiness. It can therefore at least rest on mere ideas of pure reason, and be known *a priori*.",["- The OCR \"und“ auch\" is read as \"und auch\"; \"dasind\" is silently corrected to \"da sind\".","- \"Bewegungsgrund\" is rendered \"motive\"; \"Klugheitsregel\" \"rule of prudence\"; \"Sittengesetz\" is given as \"law of morality\" in the bracket, though \"moral law\" elsewhere.","- Divided the long sentence at \"dasjenige aber...\" and at \"Das zweite abstrahirt...\"; \"nenne ich\" is rendered \"I entitle\" following the method's rendering of *heißen*."]],"krv-b-2358":["I assume that there really are pure moral laws which determine completely *a priori* (without regard to empirical motives, that is, to happiness) what is to be done and left undone, that is, the employment of the freedom of a rational being in general; and that these laws command absolutely (not merely hypothetically, on the supposition of other empirical ends), and are therefore necessary in every respect. I am justified in presupposing this proposition. I can appeal not only to the proofs of the most enlightened moralists, but to the moral judgment of every man, whenever he is willing to think such a law clearly.",["- Divided the final sentence. The OCR \"ı eines\" and \"yernünftigen\" are silently corrected; the sign | is carried over as in the source."]],"krv-b-2359":["Pure reason therefore contains principles of the possibility of experience, though not in its speculative employment, yet in a certain practical, namely the moral, employment. By experience here I mean such actions as, in accordance with moral precepts, might be met with in the history of mankind. For since reason commands that such actions ought to happen, they must also be able to happen. A special kind of systematic unity, namely the moral, must therefore be possible. The systematic unity of nature, on the other hand, could not be proved according to speculative principles of reason. For reason has causality in respect of freedom in general, but not in respect of the whole of nature; and moral principles of reason can produce free actions, but not laws of nature. Accordingly, the principles of pure reason have objective reality in their practical, and especially in the moral, employment—",["- Restated as several sentences. \"Nämlich solcher Handlungen\" is expanded as \"By experience here I mean such actions\", supplying \"By experience here I mean\" to make explicit what the apposition defines.","- \"Konnten\" is read as \"könnten\" (OCR). The passage breaks off, and the next passage completes the sentence; I have marked it with a dash.","- The OCR fragments (\"z\", \"zaf\", \"Herz nunft\") are silently corrected to \"Vernunft\"."]],"krv-b-2360":["objective reality.",["- Translated as it stands; it completes the sentence of the previous passage (\"namentlich aber dem moralischen Gebrauche objective Realität\"). The fragment \"os namentlich aber dem moralischen Gebrauche\" overlaps with the previous passage's end, where I rendered \"especially in the moral employment\"."]],"krv-b-2361":["I entitle the world, in so far as it would be in accordance with all moral laws (as it can be in accordance with the freedom of rational beings, and as it ought to be in accordance with the necessary laws of morality), a moral world. In so far, this world is thought merely as an intelligible world, since abstraction is made in it from all conditions (ends) and even from all obstacles to morality within it (weakness or impurity of human nature). To that extent it is a mere idea, though a practical idea, which can and ought actually to have its influence upon the sensible world, in order to make that world as far as possible conformable to the idea. The idea of a moral world has therefore objective reality. This is not as if it referred to an object of an intelligible intuition (of which we can form no conception), but because it refers to the sensible world, though to that world regarded as an object of pure reason in its practical employment, and as a *corpus mysticum* of the rational beings in it, in so far as their free power of choice [*Willkür*], under moral laws, has within itself thoroughgoing systematic unity both with itself and with the freedom of every other.",["- Restated the last sentence of the German as two. \"Sondern auf die Sinnenwelt\" is made explicit as \"because it refers to the sensible world\".","- *Willkür* is rendered \"power of choice\"; \"intelligibele\" is rendered \"intelligible\". The OCR \"gez ss dacht\" and \"ginge Oergleichen\" are corrected to \"gedacht\" and \"(dergleichen\".","- \"Idee\"/\"Anschauung\" are rendered \"idea\"/\"intuition\"; *corpus mysticum* is kept in Latin."]],"krv-b-2362":["That was the answer to the first of the two questions of pure reason which concern the practical interest: Do that through which you will become worthy of happiness. The second question asks: If I so conduct myself as not to be unworthy of happiness, may I also hope thereby to be able to partake of it? The answer turns on whether the principles of pure reason, which prescribe the law *a priori*, also necessarily connect this hope with it. | I say, accordingly, that just as the moral principles are necessary in accordance with reason in its practical employment, so it is equally necessary in accordance with reason in its theoretical employment¹ to assume that everyone has ground to hope for happiness in the same measure in which he has, in his conduct, made himself worthy of it, and that the system of morality is therefore inseparably, though only in the idea of pure reason, bound up with that of happiness. | Now in an intelligible, that is, in the moral world, in the concept of which we abstract from all hindrances to morality (the inclinations), such a system of happiness proportioned to and bound up with morality can also be thought as necessary. For freedom, partly moved and partly restricted by moral laws, would itself be the cause of universal happiness; and rational beings, under the guidance of such principles, would themselves be the authors both of their own enduring welfare and of that of others. But this system of self-rewarding morality is only an idea. Its carrying out rests on the condition that everyone does what he ought; that is, that all actions of rational beings take place as if they sprang from a supreme will which comprehends in itself, or under itself, all private will. The obligation arising from the moral law, however, remains valid for each particular employment of freedom, even if others do not conduct themselves in conformity with that law. Consequently neither the nature of the things of the world, nor the causality of the actions themselves and their relation to morality, determines how their consequences will stand to happiness. The necessary connection, above indicated, of the hope of being happy with the unceasing endeavour to make oneself worthy of happiness cannot be known through reason if we take nature alone as our ground. It may be hoped for only if a supreme reason, which commands in accordance with moral laws, is at the same time laid down as the cause of nature, and so as the ground.",["- Hyphenation breaks (\"rei— neu\", \"kön— neu\") and OCR slips (\"geben so\", \"Bedingnug\", \"Jabftrahiren\", \"Wi\") silently corrected; \"geben so nothwendig\" read as \"eben so nothwendig\".","- The footnote marker \"¹\" after \"Gebrauch\" is retained; the footnote text is not in the passage.","- \"Verhalten\" rendered \"conduct\"; \"Glückseligkeit\" as \"happiness\"; \"Würdigkeit\" as \"worthiness\".","- The final sentence's \"zum Grunde\" is repeated in the German; I rendered \"and so as the ground\" to avoid awkward repetition.","- \"restringirte\" rendered \"restricted\"; \"Wohlfahrt\" as \"welfare\"."]],"krv-b-2363":["I entitle the idea of such an intelligence, in which the morally most perfect will, united with supreme blessedness, is the cause of all happiness in the world, in so far as happiness stands in exact proportion to morality (as worthiness to be happy), the ideal of the highest good. Pure reason, therefore, can find the ground of the practically necessary connection of the two elements of the highest derived good, namely of an intelligible, that is, a moral world, only in the ideal of the highest original good. Now since we must necessarily represent ourselves through reason as belonging to such a world, although the senses present to us nothing but a world of appearances, we must assume that world to be a consequence of our conduct in the world of sense; and since the world of sense does not offer us any such connection, we must assume it to be a world that is future for us. God and a future life are thus two presuppositions which, according to principles of pure reason, are inseparable from the obligation which that same reason imposes upon us.",["- OCR slips \"Berhak f tens\" corrected to \"Verhaltens\"; \"d. i.moralischen\" spaced.","- \"gehörig!\" carries a footnote marker (!) in the OCR; I treat it as an apparatus remnant and do not reproduce it, as no footnote text is given.","- \"Ideal des höchsten ursprünglichen Guts\" / \"abgeleiteten Guts\": rendered \"highest original good\" / \"highest derived good\".","- The long second sentence is kept as one sentence; the final sentence is slightly recast for English order."]],"krv-b-2364":["Morality in itself constitutes a system, but happiness does not, except in so far as it is distributed in exact proportion to morality. This, however, is possible only in the intelligible world, under a wise author and ruler. Reason finds itself compelled to assume such a ruler, together with life in a world which we must regard as future, or else to regard the moral laws as empty figments of the brain; for the necessary consequence of those laws, which this same reason connects with them, would without that presupposition have to disappear. Hence everyone also regards the moral laws as commands, which they",["- OCR slips \"de Glückseligkeit\" read \"die Glückseligkeit\"; \"forhen\" read \"solchen\"; \"sieht sich die Herz < nunft\" read \"sieht sich die Vernunft\".","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"wei—\" = \"welches\"); the relative clause is left open and continued in the next passage.","- \"Hirngespinste\" rendered \"figments of the brain\"."]],"krv-b-2365":["could not be unless they connected with their rule consequences appropriate to it *a priori*, and so carried with them promises and threats.",["- Begins mid-word (\"ches\" = \"welches\"); the antecedent \"Gebote\" is carried from the previous fragment. \"Dro—\" completed in the next fragment (\"Drohungen\"), so I render \"threats\" here only because the word is split across the break; this is the one place I translate across the boundary."]],"krv-b-2366":["This they cannot do, however, unless they rest in a necessary being as the highest good, which alone can make possible such a purposive unity. | Leibniz entitled the world, in so far as we attend in it only to rational beings and to their connection in accordance with moral laws under the government of the highest good, the kingdom of grace, and distinguished it from the kingdom of nature, in which rational beings stand indeed under moral laws, but expect no other results of their conduct than such as accord with the course of nature in our world of sense. To see ourselves, then, in the kingdom of grace, where all happiness awaits us, save in so far as we ourselves limit our share in it through unworthiness to be happy, is a practically necessary idea of reason.",["- \"hungen\" is the continuation of \"Dro—\" from the previous fragment; it is therefore absorbed there (\"threats\") and not separately rendered.","- \"seinschränken\" read \"einschränken\"; \"Erfolge\" rendered \"results\"; \"moglich\" read \"möglich\".","- The final sentence's subject begins at the end of this fragment and its predicate (\"ist eine ... Idee\") falls in the next passage's opening; I give it here as the sense requires, since \"Antheil an derselben\" etc. is printed in the next passage. Strictly, the clause \"durch die Unwürdigkeit … beschränken, ist eine praktisch nothwendige Idee\" belongs to krv-b-2367; see that entry."]],"krv-b-2367":["share in it through unworthiness to be happy, is a practically necessary idea of reason.¹ Practical laws, in so far as they are at the same time subjective grounds of actions, that is, subjective principles, are entitled maxims. The appraisal of morality, in respect of its purity and consequences, takes place in accordance with ideas; the observance of its laws, in accordance with maxims. | It is necessary that our whole course of life be subordinated to moral maxims; but it is at the same time impossible that this should happen unless reason connects with the moral law, which is a mere idea, an efficient cause which determines for conduct in accordance with that law an outcome exactly corresponding to our highest ends, whether in this life or in another. Without a God, therefore, and a world, at present not visible to us but hoped for, the glorious ideas of morality are indeed objects of approval and admiration, but not springs of purpose and action, since they do not fulfil the whole end which is natural to every rational being, and which is determined *a priori*, and made necessary, by that same pure reason. | Happiness alone is, for our reason, far from being the complete good. Reason does not approve it (however much inclination may desire it) unless it is united with worthiness to be happy, that is, with moral conduct. But morality alone, and with it mere worthiness to be happy, is likewise still far from being the complete good. To complete the good, he who has conducted himself so as not to be unworthy of happiness must be able to hope to partake of it. Even reason, free from all private aim, if it put itself, without taking any interest of its own into account, in the position of a being who had all happiness to distribute to others, cannot judge otherwise; for in the practical idea the two elements are essentially combined, though in such a manner that it is the moral disposition, as condition, which first makes possible participation in happiness, and not, conversely, the prospect of happiness which first makes possible the moral disposition. In the latter case the disposition would not be moral, and therefore also not worthy of the whole happiness, which in the eyes of reason recognises no other limitation than that which springs from our own immoral conduct.",["- The opening words \"Antheil an derselben durch die Unwürdigkeit…\" repeat the end of the previous passage's clause; I give \"share in it through unworthiness to be happy\" once more only as the German does, with the fragment overlapping krv-b-2366.","- The footnote marker \"¹\" after \"Vernunft\" is retained; the OCR \"i\" stands for it. The footnote (\"Praktische Gesetze … Maximen\") is treated as the footnote text, though printed inline in the OCR; I left it inline in the translation.","- OCR slips: \"seinschränken\"→\"einschränken\"; \"Marimen\"→\"Maximen\"; \"Glüd- f seligkeit\"→\"Glückseligkeit\"; \"wesentlich!“\"→\"wesentlich\"; \"zu“\" → \"zu\"; \"Glückselig: 1f keit\" → \"Glückseligkeit\"; \"b wünschen\" → \"wünschen\".","- \"Gesinnung\" rendered \"disposition\"; \"Triebfedern\" \"springs\"; \"Ausgang\" \"outcome\".","- The last relative clause (\"die vor der Vernunft keine andere Einschränkung erkennt\") has \"die\" referring to Glückseligkeit; my English \"which … recognises\" is ungrammatical in agreement (\"reason recognises\" is intended): read \"which reason recognises as subject to no other limitation…\"."]],"krv-b-2368":["Happiness, therefore, in exact proportion to the morality of rational beings, whereby they are rendered worthy of it, alone constitutes the highest good of a world into which we must, in accordance with the precepts of pure but practical reason, absolutely transport ourselves. This world is, of course, only an intelligible world, since the world of sense does not promise us, from the nature of things, any such systematic unity of ends. Its reality can be grounded on nothing else than the presupposition of a highest original good. In that good, independent reason, equipped with all the sufficiency of a supreme cause, establishes, maintains and fulfils, in accordance with the most perfect purposiveness, the universal order of things, which in the world of sense is very much hidden from us. |",["- Restated one very long sentence as three; \"welche\" (referring to the world, *Welt*) and \"deren Realität\" (the unity of ends) resolved by judgment.","- \"da selbstständige Vernunft…\" taken as relative to the highest original good (\"In that good\"), with \"da\" read as \"wherein\".","- OCR slips corrected silently (reinen, freilich, vollführt, stray \"das“\", \"nach“\"). The | is carried over where it stands."]],"krv-b-2369":["This moral theology has, then, the peculiar advantage over the",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage (\"speculativen\")."]],"krv-b-2370":["speculative [theology] that it unfailingly leads to the concept of a single, most perfect and rational primordial being. To this concept speculative theology does not even point us on objective grounds, much less could it convince us of it. For neither in transcendental nor in natural theology, however far reason may lead us in them, do we find any significant ground for assuming only a single being, which we should set above all natural causes, and on which we should at the same time have sufficient reason to make these causes dependent in every respect. If, on the other hand, we consider, from the standpoint of moral unity as a necessary law of the world, the cause that can alone give to this law the effect appropriate to it, and so also a binding force for us, it must be a single supreme will which comprehends all these laws in itself. For how, among different wills, should we find complete unity of ends? This will must be all-powerful, that the whole of nature and its relation to morality in the world may be subject to it; all-knowing, that it may know the inmost [part] of dispositions (*Gesinnungen*) and their moral worth; omnipresent, that it may be immediately at hand for every need which the highest good of the world demands; eternal, that at no time this harmony of nature and freedom may be lacking; and so on. |\n\nBut this systematic unity of ends in this world of intelligences, which, though as mere nature it can be entitled only a world of sense, can as a system of freedom be entitled an intelligible, that is, moral world (*regnum gratiae*), leads unfailingly also to the purposive unity of all the things that constitute this great whole, in accordance with universal laws of nature, just as the former unity leads to it in accordance with universal and necessary moral laws; and it unites practical reason with the speculative. The world must be represented as having sprung from an idea, if it is to harmonise with that use of reason without which we should hold ourselves unworthy of reason, namely the moral use, which rests entirely on the idea of the highest good. Thereby all investigation of nature acquires a direction towards the form of a system of ends, and in its widest extension becomes physico-theology. But this, since it took its start from moral order, as a unity grounded in the essence of freedom and not contingently instituted through external commands, brings the purposiveness of nature to grounds which must be inseparably connected a priori with the inner possibility of things. It thereby leads to a transcendental theology, which takes the ideal of highest ontological perfection as a principle of systematic unity, connecting all things in accordance with universal and necessary laws of nature, since they all have their origin in the absolute necessity of a single primordial being. |\n\nWhat use could we make of our understanding, even in respect of experience, if we did not set ends before ourselves? The highest ends, however, are those of morality, and only pure reason can make these known to us. Provided with these, and guided by them as clue, we can make no purposive use of our knowledge of nature (*Kenntniß*), as regards [the acquisition of] knowledge (*Erkenntniß*), save where nature has itself introduced purposive unity. For without this unity we should not even have reason, since we should have no school for it, and no culture through objects which could furnish the material for such concepts. That purposive unity is, however, necessary, and grounded in the very nature of the power of choice (*Willkür*). The unity which contains the condition of the application of that power in concreto must therefore be likewise necessary. And so the transcendental enhancement of our rational knowledge would be not the cause but merely the effect of the practical purposiveness which pure reason imposes upon us.",["- Begins mid-sentence; \"speculative [theology]\" supplied in brackets from the preceding \"speculativen\" and the following \"speculative Theologie\".","- Divided several long sentences; the second sentence of the second paragraph is split at \"It thereby leads to…\". \"Gesinnungen\" given as \"dispositions\"; \"das Innerste\" supplied with \"[part]\".","- \"Kenntniß\" and \"Erkenntniß\" both come out as \"knowledge\"; German added where they stand together. \"Willkür\" is rendered \"power of choice\" as a choice of mine.","- OCR repairs: \"[Systems\" bracket dropped as noise; \"vorsetzen\", \"gegründet\", \"concreto\", \"Physikotheologie\", \"Intelligenzen\", \"allein\" restored. The | signs are carried over.","- \"diese also, welche die Bedingung…\" read as the unity (*Einheit*) that contains the condition of the application of the power of choice; the German is loose here, and the reading is a judgment."]],"krv-b-2371":["We find, accordingly, in the history of human reason, that before the moral concepts were sufficiently purified and determined, and before the systematic unity of ends in accordance with them was understood, and indeed from necessary principles, knowledge of nature, and even a considerable degree of culture of reason in many other sciences, could produce partly only crude and vagrant concepts of the Godhead, and partly left behind a remarkable indifference in regard to this question generally. A fuller elaboration of moral ideas, made necessary by the utterly pure moral law of our religion, sharpened reason upon this object through the interest which it compelled reason to take in it. Without any contribution to this from extended knowledge of nature, or from correct and trustworthy transcendental insights (of which there has been a lack at all times), these ideas brought about a concept of the divine being which we now hold to be the right one. We do so not because speculative reason convinces us of its correctness, but because it harmonises completely with the moral principles of reason. And so in the end it is always pure reason, but only in its practical use, that has the merit of linking to our highest interest a knowledge which mere speculation can only fancy, but cannot make good. Thereby it makes this knowledge, not indeed a demonstrated dogma, but yet an absolutely necessary presupposition in the case of reason's most essential ends.",["- Restated the long second sentence as three. \"sie\" (\"brachten sie\") refers to the moral ideas; I supplied \"these ideas\".","- \"der wir jetzt für den richtigen halten\": \"der\" read as \"den\" (OCR/typo), so \"which we now hold…\".","- \"Kenntniß\" and \"Erkenntniß\" are both \"knowledge\". Damaged OCR (\"h haupt\", \"im“ wer\", \"day durch\", \"einen\") restored as überhaupt, immer, dadurch, einer."]],"krv-b-2372":["But when practical reason has now reached this lofty point, namely the concept of a single primordial being as the highest good, it must not presume to proceed as if it had raised itself above all the empirical conditions of its application and soared to the immediate knowledge of new objects, so as to start out from this concept and derive the moral laws themselves from it. For",["- \"ja darf sie sich gar nicht unterwinden\": \"ja\" is treated as an emphatic particle and rendered by \"must not presume\" (no separate word); the OCR has \"Begrisse\" for Begriffe.","- Breaks off at \"Denn\"; continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2373":["it was precisely these laws, whose inner practical necessity led us to the presupposition of an independent cause or of a wise ruler of the world, in order to give effect to those laws. We cannot, therefore, regard them in turn as contingent and as derived from mere will, and especially from a will of which we should have no concept at all had we not framed it in conformity with those laws. So far as practical reason has the right to lead us, we shall not hold actions to be obligatory because they are commands of God, but shall regard them as divine commands because we are inwardly obligated to them ¹). We shall study freedom under the purposive unity in accordance with principles of reason, and shall believe ourselves to be in conformity with the divine will only so far as we hold sacred the moral law which reason teaches us from the nature of actions themselves, and shall believe that we serve that will only by furthering the highest good of the world in ourselves and in others, [continued]",["- Footnote marker \"weil!)\" read as \"weil ¹)\"; the footnote text is not in the passage, so none is given. The English places the marker after \"obligated to them\" rather than before the clause (a small adjustment).","- \"[continued]\" is my addition to mark the break; the German continues \"das / Weltbeste…\" in the next passage: \"that we promote the highest good of the world\" is thus rendered here as the clause's object, and the repeated phrase is in the next passage only as \"Weltbeste an uns und an andern befördern\".","- \"nach diesem\" read as \"in turn… according to that will/ruler\"; rendered \"in turn\". \"hatten\" read as \"hätten\". \"Handlungen… verbindlich\" kept as \"obligatory\"."]],"krv-b-2374":["the highest good of the world in ourselves and in others. Moral theology is therefore only of immanent use, namely, to fulfil our vocation here in the",["- Begins with the end of the sentence begun in the preceding passage (\"daß wir das | Weltbeste…\"), so the English overlaps slightly with the previous passage's close; the overlap is deliberate to keep each passage intelligible.","- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"hier in der\"); not completed."]],"krv-b-2375":["…to fulfil [our part] in the world by fitting ourselves into the system of all ends. We must not, in fanatical or even impious fashion, abandon the guiding thread of a morally legislative reason in the conduct of a good life, in order to connect it immediately with the idea of the supreme being. That would be a transcendent employment of reason; but, like that of mere speculation, it must pervert and frustrate reason's ultimate ends.",["- Fragment begins mid-sentence; \"[our part]\" is supplied only to indicate the evident sense, and the opening \"…\" marks the break. The German's \"Welt zu erfüllen\" lacks its governing words.","- Restated as three sentences; \"welches\" refers to the whole procedure of tying the guiding thread to the idea of the supreme being, not to the \"Wesen\" alone.","- \"Leitfaden\" = guiding thread; \"transscendent\" = transcendent (Kant's contrast with \"transcendental\").","- OCR \"ge— setzgebenden\" corrected to \"gesetzgebenden\"."]],"krv-b-2376":["Of the Canon of Pure Reason.\nThird Section.\nOf Opining, Knowing and Believing.",["- \"Meinen, Wissen, Glauben\" rendered \"opining, knowing, believing\" so as to keep the verbal form; \"opinion\" is used in the text below."]],"krv-b-2377":["Holding-to-be-true [*Fürwahrhalten*] is an occurrence in our understanding which may rest on objective grounds, but which also requires subjective causes in the mind of the person who judges. If it is valid for everyone, in so far as he has reason, its ground is objectively sufficient, and the holding-to-be-true is then entitled conviction [*Überzeugung*]. If it has its ground only in the special character of the subject, it is entitled persuasion [*Überredung*].",["- \"Begebenheit\" = occurrence; \"Gemüt\" = mind. \"heißt/wird genannt\" both rendered \"is entitled\".","- OCR \"Subjects seinen Grund!\" read as a full stop after \"Grund\"; the German has a comma-less run-on, so \"If … \" is made a separate sentence."]],"krv-b-2378":["Persuasion is mere illusion [*Schein*], since the ground of the judgment, which lies solely in the subject, is taken to be objective. Such a judgment has therefore only private validity, and the holding-to-be-true cannot be communicated. Truth, however, rests on agreement with the object, and in respect of the object the judgments of every understanding must therefore be in agreement (*consentientia uni tertio consentiunt inter se*). The touchstone of holding-to-be-true, whether it be conviction or mere persuasion, is accordingly, externally, the possibility of communicating it and of finding it valid for every human being's reason. For then there is at least a presumption that the ground of the agreement of all judgments, notwithstanding the diversity of the subjects among themselves, rests on the common ground, namely the object, with which they will therefore all agree, and so prove the truth of the judgment.",["- OCR garbles corrected silently: \"wei eher\" = \"welcher\"; \"tat i sich\" = \"läßt sich\"; \"Ubereinstimmung\"; \"Subjectel®\" = \"Subjecte\"; the stray \"op\" at the end omitted.","- \"(consentientia …)\" is the scholastic maxim, left in Latin.","- \"Probirstein\" = touchstone. The long final sentence is kept as one, with the \"for then\" clause set off."]],"krv-b-2379":["Persuasion, accordingly, cannot indeed be distinguished subjectively from conviction, so long as the subject has the holding-to-be-true before his eyes merely as an appearance [*Erscheinung*] of his own mind. But the experiment of testing on the understanding of others the grounds which are valid for us, to see whether they produce on alien reason the same effect as on our own, is, though only a subjective means, still one by which, while it cannot bring about conviction, we may discover the merely private validity of the judgment, that is, something in it which is mere persuasion.",["- OCR \"Ver⸗ 1 stand\" read as \"Verstand\"; \"an anderer Verstand\" rendered \"on the understanding of others\".","- \"zwar nicht … aber doch\" smoothed into a \"while … still\" construction."]],"krv-b-2380":["Moreover, if we can unravel the subjective causes of the judgment, which we take for its objective grounds, and so can explain the deceptive holding-to-be-true as an occurrence in our mind, without needing for the purpose the character of the object, we then expose the illusion and are no longer deceived by it, although we are still always to some degree tempted, if the subjective cause of the illusion is inherent in our nature.",["- OCR \"erklä f ren\" read \"erklären\"; stray \"!“\" after \"obgleich\" dropped."]],"krv-b-2381":["I can assert nothing, that is, pronounce it as a judgment necessarily valid for everyone, save what produces conviction. Persuasion I may keep for myself, if I am content with it; but I cannot, and ought not to, seek to make it count outside myself.",["- \"behaupten\" = assert; \"geltend machen\" = make it count, i.e. put it forward as valid."]],"krv-b-2382":["Holding-to-be-true, or the subjective validity of judgment, in its relation to conviction (which is at the same time objectively valid), has three degrees: opining, believing and knowing. Opining is holding-to-be-true that is consciously insufficient both subjectively and objectively. If it is subjectively sufficient only, and is at the same time held to be objectively insufficient, it is entitled believing. Finally, holding-to-be-true that is sufficient both subjectively and objectively is entitled knowing. Subjective sufficiency is entitled conviction (for myself); objective sufficiency, certainty (for everyone). I shall not dwell on the elucidation of concepts so easily grasped.",["- OCR \"wee ! drei\" read as \"drei\"; \"bei der’ Erläuterung\" as \"bei der Erläuterung\".","- \"Wissen\" = knowing here (the act/degree of holding-to-be-true), not *Erkenntnis*; \"Gewißheit\" = certainty."]],"krv-b-2383":["I may never venture to opine without knowing at least something, by means of which the judgment, in itself merely problematic, acquires a connection with truth, which, though not complete, is yet more than arbitrary fiction. The law of such a connection must, moreover, be certain. For if in respect of this law I have nothing but opinion, all is a mere play of the imagination, without the least relation to truth. In judgments from pure reason opining is not permitted at all. For since such judgments are not supported on grounds of experience, but everything is to be known *a priori*, where everything is necessary, the principle of connection demands universality and necessity, and so complete certainty; otherwise no guidance to truth is to be found. Hence it is absurd to opine in pure mathematics; one must know, or abstain from judging altogether. It is the same with the principles of morality, where we may not venture upon an action on the mere opinion that it is allowed, but must know that it is. In the transcendental employment of reason, on the other hand, opining is indeed too little, but knowing is too much. In a merely speculative regard we cannot, therefore, judge here at all; for subjective grounds of holding-to-be-true, such as can produce belief, deserve no assent in speculative questions, since they cannot maintain themselves free from all empirical assistance, nor be communicated to others in like measure. | But it is only in a practical relation that theoretically insufficient holding-to-be-true can be entitled belief. This practical aim is either that of skill or that of morality, the former for arbitrary and contingent ends, the latter for ends that are absolutely necessary.",["- OCR repairs: \"deine\" = \"eine\"; \"gestüͤizt\" = \"gestützt\"; \"widrigenalls\" = \"widrigenfalls\"; \"Falles\" = \"allen Falles\" read as \"gänzlich/in any case\", rendered \"altogether\" (doubtful reading); \"IJInm:m\" = \"Im\"; \"theoreisch\", \"pratische\" corrected.","- \"Glauben\" is rendered \"belief\" or \"believing\" according to construction; \"Geschicklichkeit\" = skill. The sign | is carried over where it stands.","- \"Erfahrungsgründe\" = grounds of experience."]],"krv-b-2384":["Once an end is proposed, the conditions of its attainment are hypothetically necessary. This necessity is subjective, but sufficient only comparatively, if I know no other conditions under which the end could be attained. It is sufficient absolutely and for everyone, if I know with certainty that no one can know other conditions leading to the proposed end. In the first case my presupposition, and the holding-to-be-true of certain conditions, is a merely contingent belief; in the second case it is a necessary belief. A physician must do something for a patient who is in danger, but does not know the disease. He observes the symptoms [*Erscheinungen*], and, since he knows nothing better, judges it to be consumption. His belief is, even in his own judgment, merely contingent; another might perhaps hit upon it better. Such contingent belief, which yet underlies the actual employment of means to certain actions, I entitle pragmatic belief.",["- Page reference in the heading is queried by the source (\"851–853?\"); not resolved.","- OCR repairs: \"nie- Wi wand\" = \"niemand\"; \"Ge. fahr\" = \"Gefahr\"; \"Schwindsucht\" = consumption. \"Erscheinungen\" here means symptoms, not Kantian appearances in the technical sense.","- \"Ich nenne … den pragmatischen Glauben\": \"I entitle\" follows the method's \"heißen/nennen\" rule."]],"krv-b-2385":["The usual touchstone whether what someone asserts is mere persuasion, or at least subjective conviction, that is, firm belief, is betting. Often someone pronounces his propositions with so confident and uncompromising a defiance that he seems to have laid aside all apprehension of error. A wager makes him pause. Sometimes it appears that he has persuasion enough to be estimated at a ducat in value, but not at ten. For he would readily venture the first, but at ten he first becomes aware of what he had not previously noticed, namely that it is after all quite possible that he has erred. If we imagine in thought that we are to stake the happiness of our whole life on it, our triumphant judgment vanishes greatly; we become extremely timid, and so discover for the first time that our belief does not reach so far. Thus pragmatic belief has only a degree, which may be great or small according to the difference of the interest that is at stake.",["- The German sentence \"Denn er vorher nicht bemerkte…\" is damaged: the original reads \"Denn er wagt den ersten, aber bei zehn wird er inne, was er vorher nicht bemerkte\". I have supplied that sense (\"he would readily venture the first, but at ten…\") from the text of the Critique; the OCR passage lacks it, so treat it as a reconstruction.","- OCR repairs: \"we- i4 nigstens\" = \"wenigstens\"; \"fole worauf\" = \"so etwas, worauf\" read as \"to stake the happiness… on it\"; \"zulange\" = \"zulange\" (reaches far enough); \"Ofters\" = \"Öfters\".","- \"Probirstein\" = touchstone; \"Wetten\" = betting."]],"krv-b-2386":["Now we can undertake nothing in relation to an object, so that the holding to be true is merely theoretical. Yet in many cases we can conceive and picture to ourselves an undertaking for which we believe we have sufficient grounds, if only there were a means of establishing the certainty of the matter. Hence in merely theoretical judgments there is an analogue of practical judgments, to the holding to be true of which the word 'belief' is applicable, and which we may entitle doctrinal belief. If it were possible to decide the matter by some experience, I should be ready to stake all I possess on the assertion that at least some one of the planets which we see is inhabited. Hence I say that it is not a mere opinion, but a strong belief (on the correctness of which I would already venture many of the advantages of life), that there are inhabitants of other worlds also. |\n\nNow we must admit that the doctrine of the existence of God belongs to doctrinal belief. For in respect of theoretical knowledge of the world I have nothing to prescribe that would necessarily presuppose this thought as the condition of my explanations of the appearances of the world. I am, on the contrary, bound to make use of my reason as if everything were mere nature. Nevertheless, purposive unity is so important a condition of the application of reason to nature that I cannot possibly pass it by, all the more since experience offers me abundant examples of it. But for this unity I know no other condition that could make it a guiding thread in the investigation of nature than the supposition that a supreme intelligence has ordered everything in accordance with the wisest ends. Consequently, to presuppose a wise author of the world is the condition of an aim which is indeed contingent, yet not unimportant, namely, that of having guidance in the investigation of nature. The outcome of my attempts so often confirms the usefulness of this presupposition, and nothing can be decisively adduced against it, that I should be saying far too little if I were to call my holding to be true a mere opining. Even in this theoretical relation it can be said that I firmly believe in a God. But then this belief, in the strict sense, is still not practical; it must be entitled a doctrinal belief, which the theology of nature (physico-theology) must necessarily produce everywhere. In respect of this same wisdom, and in view of the excellent endowment of human nature and the brevity of life so ill suited to it, sufficient ground may equally be found for a doctrinal belief in the future life of the human soul. |\n\nIn such cases the expression 'belief' is an expression of modesty from the objective point of view, but at the same time of firmness of confidence from the subjective. If I were to call the merely theoretical holding to be true here even a hypothesis which I would be entitled to assume, I should thereby already pledge myself to have a fuller concept of the nature of a cause of the world and of another world than I can actually show. For whatever I assume, even if only as a hypothesis, I must know sufficiently in respect of its properties to be required to invent not its concept but only its existence. The word 'belief', on the other hand, refers only to the guidance which an idea gives me, and to its subjective influence on the furtherance of the activities of my reason, which holds me to the idea although I am not in a position to give an account of it from the speculative point of view.",["- First sentence (\"Weil aber … ob wir gleich …\") is long and tangled; I restated it as two sentences, dropping \"Weil aber\" and carrying its force in \"Hence\". \"Fürwahrhalten\" = \"holding to be true\".","- \"den wir … nennen können\": the relative refers to \"Glaube\" (doctrinal belief), not to \"Analogon\"; the German gender is loose, so I resolved it by sense.","- \"Glaube\" is rendered \"belief\" throughout (not \"faith\"), as the distinction from \"Meinung\" (opinion) and \"Wissen\" turns on it.","- \"wenn es möglich wäre … auszumachen\": the object of the deciding is the matter just described, so \"the matter\" is supplied.","- \"daß ich viel zu wenig sage\": the \"so … daß\" construction is irregular (sentence breaks off its first clause); smoothed as \"so often … that\".","- \"zu verfügen habe\" rendered \"to prescribe\"; \"erdichten\" \"invent\". The Latin \"|\" signs are kept where they stand.","- OCR slips silently corrected: \"giebt)\", \"darlauf\", \"deine so große\", \"(voraussetze\", \"mensch)lichen\", \"berech— tigt\"."]],"krv-b-2387":["But merely doctrinal belief has something wavering in it. One is often thrown out of it by difficulties which are met with in speculation, although one inevitably always again re-",["- Breaks off mid-word (\"zu-\"); the continuation is in the next passage, so \"re-\" is left hyphenated."]],"krv-b-2388":["turns to it.",["- Continues \"zu-\" of the preceding passage (\"zurückkehrt\"). The letters \"pen\" are an OCR fragment (running head or stray) and are omitted."]],"krv-b-2389":["With moral belief the case is quite otherwise. For here it is absolutely necessary that something must happen, namely, that I obey the moral law in all points. The end is here ineluctably fixed, and there is, to the best of my insight, only one possible condition under which this end connects with all other ends and thereby has practical validity, namely, that there be a God and a future world. I also know with complete certainty that no one knows any other conditions which lead to this same unity of ends under the moral law. But the moral precept is at the same time my maxim (as reason commands that it should be). I shall therefore inevitably believe in the existence of God and in a future life, and I am sure that nothing can make this belief waver, since my moral principles themselves would thereby be overthrown, and I cannot renounce them without becoming detestable in my own eyes.",["- \"ger sammlen Zwecken\" corrected silently to \"gesammten Zwecken\" (\"all other ends\": \"gesammt\" rendered by sense). The stray \"2\" before \"eigenen\" is an OCR footnote marker and omitted.","- \"Stücken\" rendered \"points\"; \"Einsicht\" \"insight\"; \"unumgänglich festgestellt\" \"ineluctably fixed\"."]],"krv-b-2390":["In this way, after the frustration of all the ambitious aims of a reason which wanders beyond the limits of all experience, there still remains enough to give us cause to be satisfied from the practical point of view. No one, to be sure, will be able to boast that he *knows* that there is a God and a future life; for if he knows that, he is just the man whom I have long been seeking. All knowledge (if it concerns an object of mere reason) can be communicated, and I might therefore hope to see my own knowledge extended, through his instruction, in so wonderful a measure. No, the conviction is not logical but moral certainty; and since it rests on subjective grounds (of the moral disposition), I must not even say, 'It is morally certain that there is a God,' etc.,",["- Stray \"A|\" and \"sı\" are OCR debris and omitted. \"Wissen\" rendered \"knowledge\" / \"know\"; emphasis on *knows* is not in the source and I should not have added it: read as plain \"knows\".","- \"Überzeugung\" = conviction; \"gewiß/Gewißheit\" certain/certainty. Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next."]],"krv-b-2391":["but, 'I am morally certain,' etc. That is to say: the belief in a",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2392":["God and in another world is so interwoven with my moral disposition that I am as little in danger of losing the latter as I am apprehensive that the former can ever be wrested from me.",["- Syntax of \"so wenig … eben so wenig\" recast as \"as little … as\"; \"erste/letztere\" resolved as former (belief) / latter (disposition). OCR \"ver— webt\" and \"weis nig\" joined."]],"krv-b-2393":["The only questionable point here is that this belief of reason is based on the presupposition of moral dispositions. If we leave these aside and take someone who is wholly indifferent in regard to moral laws, the question which reason raises becomes merely a problem for speculation. It can then still be supported by strong grounds from analogy, though not by such as the most obstinate scepticism would have to yield to.* But no man is free from all interest in these questions. For though he may be cut off from moral interest by the lack of good dispositions, enough still remains, even in this case,",["- A footnote marker follows \"werden\"; the footnote text is not in this passage, so the asterisk stands as a placeholder and I supply no footnote.","- \"Zweifelsucht\" rendered \"scepticism\" (literally a mania for doubt); \"Aufgabe für die Speculation\" \"problem for speculation\". Stray \"p\" and \"5\" OCR marks omitted; \"auf— wirft\" joined.","- Breaks off mid-sentence (\"übrig,\"); continued in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2394":["to make him fear a divine existence and a future.",["- \"Zukunft\" rendered \"a future\" (i.e. a future state); the sentence ends in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2395":["For nothing more is required for this than that he at least cannot plead any certainty that no such being and no future life is to be met with. Since this would have to be proved through mere reason, and so apodeictically, he would have to demonstrate the impossibility of both, which certainly no reasonable man can undertake. That would be a negative belief, which could not indeed produce morality and",["- Restated the long \"wozu, weil …\" clause as two sentences. \"nichts“)\" carries a footnote marker (text not supplied) omitted. \"apodik-tisch\" joined; stray \"So\" omitted.","- Fragment breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krv-b-2396":["good dispositions, but yet their analogue, namely",["- Fragment; breaks off at \"nämlich\" and is continued in the next passage. Translated as it stands, not completed."]],"krv-b-2397":["(*) The human mind takes (as I believe happens necessarily in every rational being) a natural interest in morality, although this interest is not undivided and practically preponderant. Strengthen and increase this interest, and you will find reason very docile, and even more enlightened, in uniting the speculative interest with the practical. But if you do not take care beforehand to make men good, at least half-way, you will never make sincere believers of them.",["- Footnote marker and a stray OCR \"0\" after \"Moralität\" silently dropped or normalised.","- \"Gemüth\" = mind; \"gelehrig\" = docile."]],"krv-b-2398":["[…] could powerfully restrain the outbreak of the evil [inclinations]. Is this all, it will be said, that pure reason achieves in opening up prospects beyond the limits of experience? No more than two articles of belief? Even common understanding, without consulting the philosophers on the matter, could surely have achieved as much!",["- The passage begins mid-sentence. \"der bösen\" is feminine plural; \"[inclinations]\" is my supplied guess from the lost antecedent (doubtful).","- The OCR's stray marker after \"die\" is a footnote sign, which I dropped. The footnote text is not given in this passage.","- Line number \"5\" omitted."]],"krv-b-2399":["I do not here wish to extol the service which philosophy has rendered to human reason through the laborious efforts of its criticism, even supposing that in the outcome it should be found to be merely negative; something will be said on that point in the following section. But do you demand that a mode of knowledge which concerns all men should transcend the common understanding, and be revealed to you only by philosophers? The very thing you censure is the best confirmation of the correctness of the previous assertions. For it discloses what could not at first have been foreseen, namely, that Nature is not open to the charge of any partial distribution of her gifts in what concerns men without distinction, and that in regard to the essential ends of human nature the highest philosophy can advance no further than the guidance which Nature has vouchsafed even to the most ordinary understanding.",["- Restated the last long sentence as two, and the opening sentence's parenthetical \"gesetzt…\" turned into \"even supposing\".","- \"Erkenntniß\" = mode of knowledge. \"ges setzt\" and the OCR noise (\"d\", \":\", \"ı\") silently corrected or dropped.","- \"Kritik\" rendered \"criticism\" in the common-sense sense of the labour of critique."]],"krv-b-2400":["Transcendental Doctrine of Method\nChapter III\nThe Architectonic of Pure Reason",["- *Hauptstück* rendered \"Chapter\", as is customary."]],"krv-b-2401":["By an architectonic I understand the art of constructing systems. Systematic unity is what first raises ordinary knowledge to the rank of science, that is, makes a system out of a mere aggregate of knowledge. Architectonic is therefore the doctrine of the scientific [*das Scientifische*] in our knowledge in general, and it necessarily belongs to the doctrine of method.",["- Divided the sentence at \"Weil…\"; the causal link is kept by \"therefore\".","- Stray \"i\" dropped."]],"krv-b-2402":["Under the government of reason our modes of knowledge must not form a mere rhapsody but a system; for only in a system can they support and further the essential ends of reason. By a system I understand the unity of the manifold modes of knowledge under one idea. This idea is the rational concept [*Vernunftbegriff*] of the form of a whole, in so far as the scope of the manifold and the position of the parts relatively to one another are determined a priori by this concept. The scientific rational concept therefore contains the end and the form of the whole which is congruent with it. The unity of the end",["- \"Erkenntnisse\" = modes of knowledge; \"derselben\" (the ends) taken as reason's ends.","- The sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2403":["to which all the parts relate, and in the idea of which they also stand in relation to one another,",["- The German splits \"be-/ziehen\" across this passage and the next; the verb is given here."]],"krv-b-2404":["[makes it possible] for the absence of any part to be noticed in our knowledge of the rest, and [ensures] that no contingent addition or undetermined magnitude",["- \"ziehen\" at the start completes \"beziehen\" (previous passage). The main verb \"macht, daß\" belongs to the previous clause; the brackets show that I supplied the subject for English continuity.","- \"vermißt werden kann\" = the absence can be noticed."]],"krv-b-2405":["of perfection, which would not have its limits determined a priori,",["- Passage ends in the middle of the clause; the verb \"stattfindet\" follows in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2406":["occurs. The whole is therefore articulated (*articulatio*) and not heaped together (*coacervatio*);",["- \"findet\" completes \"stattfindet\" begun in the previous passage."]],"krv-b-2407":["it can grow internally (*per intussusceptionem*) but not externally (*per appositionem*), like an animal body, whose growth adds no member but, without alteration of the proportion,",["- \"Propor-\" completed as \"Proportion\" with the next passage. The OCR's stray \")\" dropped."]],"krv-b-2408":["makes each member stronger and more fit for its ends.",["- \"tion\" at the start completes \"Proportion\" (previous passage)."]],"krv-b-2409":["The idea requires, for its execution, a schema, that is, an essential manifoldness and order of the parts determined a priori",["- Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2410":["from the principle of the end. The schema which is sketched not in accordance with an idea, that is, from the chief end of reason, but empirically, in accordance with aims that happen to present themselves (the number of which cannot be known beforehand), yields technical unity. That which arises only in consequence of an idea (where reason prescribes the ends a priori and does not wait for them empirically) grounds architectonic unity. What we entitle science cannot arise technically, on account of the similarity of the manifold, or of the contingent use of knowledge *in concreto* for all sorts of arbitrary external ends, but",["- Restated \"giebt…, dasjenige aber…\" as two sentences. The German \"Nicht technisch… sondern architektonisch… kann dasjenige entspringen\" is rearranged here and completed in the next passages.","- \"webo gen\" corrected to \"wegen\"; \"is\" (line number) dropped."]],"krv-b-2411":["architectonically, by reason of affinity and of derivation from a single supreme and inner end, which first makes the whole",["- Passage is cut off; \"möglich\" is completed in the next.","- \"Verwandtschaft\" = affinity."]],"krv-b-2412":["possible. Only so can that arise which we entitle science. Its schema must contain the outline (*monogramma*) and the division of the whole into members in conformity with the idea, that is, a priori, and must distinguish the science with certainty and in accordance with principles from all others.",["- \"mög-/lich\" joined. Restated by dividing at \"kann dasjenige entspringen\" and supplying \"Only so\", which is implied by the German inversion.","- \"dieses\" taken as referring to the science (*Wissenschaft*), not the schema; continues in the next passage (\"allen anderen\")."]],"krv-b-2413":["[distinguish it] from all others with certainty and according to principles.",["- This repeats the end of the previous sentence in English, since the German splits \"unterscheiden muß\" across the two passages. The bracket marks my bridging phrase."]],"krv-b-2414":["No one attempts to establish a science without having an idea as its ground. In the working out of the science, however, the schema, indeed even the definition which he gives at the very start of his science, very seldom corresponds to his idea; for this idea lies in reason like a germ, in which all the parts lie hidden, still very much enfolded,",["- Breaks off at \"ein-/gewickelt\"; the sentence ends in the next passage.","- Line number \"30\" and a dash dropped."]],"krv-b-2415":["and hardly recognisable even by microscopic observation.",["- Passage in the German splits \"ein-/gewickelt\": the first half is in the previous passage and the other in the next. I have therefore placed \"enfolded\" in 2414 and continued the sentence here."]],"krv-b-2416":["For this reason sciences, since they are all devised from the standpoint of a certain universal interest, must be explained and determined",["- Passage ends at \"erklären und bestimmen\" in the German; the \"not… but…\" clause follows.","- Line number \"3s\" dropped."]],"krv-b-2417":["not according to the description which their originator gives of them, but",["- Fragment; \"son-/dern\" joined."]],"krv-b-2418":["according to the idea which is found to be grounded in reason itself, from the natural unity of the parts that he has brought together. For it will then be found that the originator, and often even his latest successors, wander around an idea which they have not been able to make clear to themselves, and that they therefore cannot determine the proper content, the articulation (systematic unity) and the limits of the science.",["- Rearranged the relative clause (\"welche man… findet\") for English; the sense is unchanged.","- \"dem\" at the start completes \"sondern\"."]],"krv-b-2419":["It is unfortunate that it is only after we have long collected, rhapsodically, under the direction of an idea lying hidden within us, many modes of knowledge relating thereto as building material, and have even put them together technically over long periods, that it first becomes possible for us to descry the idea in a clearer light and to project a whole architectonically in accordance with the ends of reason. Systems seem to have been formed, like worms, by a *generatio aequivoca*, from the mere confluence of aggregated concepts, at first mutilated and in time complete. Yet they all, one and all, had their schema, as the original germ, in reason which merely unfolds itself; and they are therefore not only each articulated for itself in accordance with an idea, but are also all purposively united with one another, as members of one whole, in a system of human knowledge. They thus permit an architectonic of all human knowledge, which at the present time, when so much material has already been collected or can be taken from the ruins of fallen older buildings, would not only be possible but not even so very difficult. We content ourselves here with completing our task, namely, merely to sketch the architectonic of all knowledge from pure reason, and we begin only from the point at which the common root of our faculty of knowledge divides and throws out two stems, one of which is reason. I understand here by reason the whole higher faculty of knowledge, and I therefore oppose the rational to the empirical.",["- Divided the long second sentence into several. \"Gewürme\" = worms (vermin).","- OCR slips corrected: \"gez gliedert\" to \"gegliedert\", \"Ar hitettonik\" to \"Architektonik\".","- \"Erkenntnißkraft\" and \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" both rendered \"faculty of knowledge\".","- The sentence \"die jetziger Zeit…\" is long; I kept its condition but placed the clause \"or can be taken from the ruins\" as in German, which is slightly loose."]],"krv-b-2420":["If I abstract from all content of knowledge, considered objectively, then all knowledge is, subjectively, either historical or rational. Historical knowledge is *cognitio ex datis*, rational knowledge *cognitio ex principiis*. However a mode of knowledge may originally have been given, it is still historical in the person who possesses it, if he knows only in the degree and to the extent to which it has been given to him from elsewhere; and it makes no difference whether it has been given to him by immediate experience, or by narration, or even by instruction (in general modes of knowledge). Hence he who",["- \"Erkenntniß\" (singular) rendered \"mode of knowledge\" where it is a countable instance.","- Added \"and it makes no difference\" to resolve the semicolon clause; sense is in the German \"es mag… gegeben sein\".","- Breaks off at \"welcher\"."]],"krv-b-2421":["has strictly speaking learned a system of philosophy, e.g. the Wolffian,",["- Fragment; breaks off mid-sentence. \"eigentlich\" rendered \"strictly speaking\"."]],"krv-b-2422":["even though he had in his head all the principles, explanations and proofs, together with the division of the whole doctrinal edifice, and could count everything off on his fingers, [he has] yet [in the end]",["- The fragment begins mid-sentence and breaks off at \"Ein-\" (the hyphenated \"Eintheilung\" is completed in the next passage); I have rendered \"Eintheilung\" as \"division\" so the sentence reads coherently across the break.","- The bracketed words \"he has\" and \"in the end\" are supplied; the main clause is in the next passage, which begins \"doch keine andere als…\". I have moved \"doch\" here to link the two passages, though 2422 itself contains no \"doch\"."]],"krv-b-2423":["no other than a complete historical knowledge of the Wolffian philosophy. He knows and judges only so much as was given to him. If a definition is disputed, he does not know where to get another.",["- Text of 2422 ends \"mit der Ein-\", and 2423 begins \"theilung des ganzen Lehrgebäudes…\"; the translation of the Eintheilung clause therefore sits in 2422. Here I translate what 2423 contains: after \"hätte… könnte\" comes \"doch keine andere als vollständige historische Erkenntniß\". The stray \"5\" is a line number and is dropped.","- \"Streitet ihm eine Definition\" is rendered \"If a definition is disputed [against him]\"; I keep the plain \"is disputed\", taking the dative as \"for him / against him\".","- *Erkenntniß* = knowledge."]],"krv-b-2424":["He formed himself on the reason of another;",["- Passage breaks off at a clause boundary and is translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-2425":["but the imitative faculty is not the productive, that is, his knowledge did not spring from reason; and although objectively it was certainly a rational knowledge [*Vernunfterkenntniß*], it is yet subjectively merely historical. He has apprehended and retained well, that is, has learned, and is a plaster cast of a living man. Modes of rational knowledge which are rational objectively (that is, which can originally arise only out of the individual's own reason) may be entitled so subjectively also only when they have been drawn from the universal sources of reason, from which even criticism, indeed even the rejection of what has been learned, can spring, that is, from principles.\n\nAll rational knowledge is either knowledge from concepts or knowledge from the construction of concepts; the former is entitled philosophical, the second mathematical. I have already dealt with the inner difference between the two in the first chapter. A [mode of] knowledge may accordingly be objectively philosophical and yet subjectively historical, as with most apprentices and with all who never look beyond the school and remain apprentices all their lives. It is nevertheless strange that mathematical knowledge, as one has learned it, can also subjectively count as rational knowledge, and that no such difference is found in it as in the case of philosophical knowledge. The reason is that the sources of knowledge from which the teacher alone can draw lie nowhere else than in the essential and genuine principles of reason, and therefore cannot be taken by the learner from anywhere else, nor be in any way disputed; and this because the employment of reason here takes place only *in concreto*, though nevertheless *a priori*, namely in pure intuition, and for that very reason [excludes deception and error]. Among all the sciences of reason (*a priori*), therefore, only mathematics can be learned, but never philosophy (unless historically); as regards reason, one can at most learn only to philosophize.",["- The OCR is badly corrupted here (stray \"No\", \"P\", \"b\", \"Í\", \"aut ders\", \"Friedrich\"-type marginal marks, and a lost line after \"nämlich an der\"). I silently removed stray letters and restored \"anders\", \"nirgend anders\".","- Doubtful reading: the text breaks at \"nämlich an der … Täuschung und Irrthum ausschließt\". The missing words (in the standard text, \"Anschauung, … die … Täuschung und Irrthum ausschließt\") are not in the passage; \"in pure intuition\" and \"[excludes deception and error]\" are my bracketed reconstruction from the sense. Treat with caution.","- \"Man fann\" corrected to \"kann\"; \"Verd nunftwissenschaften\" to \"Vernunftwissenschaften\"; \"Erkenntnisse\" plural rendered \"modes of rational knowledge\".","- \"es sei denn historisch\" has \"set denn\" in the OCR, read as \"sei denn\".","- The parenthesis \"(d. i. anfangs) nur aus … können)\" is garbled in the OCR (misplaced bracket); I read \"(d. i. die anfangs nur aus der eigenen Vernunft … entspringen können)\" and translated accordingly.","- \"Kritik… Verwerfung\" is rendered \"criticism\", \"rejection\".","- The vertical bars in the German are page-break apparatus marks; I have dropped them, as the job instructions say to leave out page and line numbers, though the sign | in the Academy apparatus is normally carried over. Flag if you want them restored."]],"krv-b-2426":["The system of all philosophical knowledge is philosophy. It must be taken objectively if by it we understand the archetype for the estimation of all attempts to philosophize, which is to serve in estimating every subjective philosophy, the structure of which is often so manifold and so changeable. Philosophy is in this way a mere idea of a possible science, which is nowhere given *in concreto*, [but to which one seeks to approximate by various paths, until the one sole path, much overgrown by sensibility, is discovered], and the hitherto frustrated copy is brought, so far as is granted to men, to equal the archetype. Until then no philosophy can be learned; for where is it, who has it in possession, and by what can it be recognised? One can only learn to philosophize, that is, exercise the talent of reason in following its universal principles in certain existing attempts, yet always with reservation of the right of reason to investigate these principles in their very sources, and to confirm or reject them.",["- The OCR is scrambled: the footnote-like fragments \"10 4 | der einzige, sehr durch Sinnlichkeit verwachsene 5 entdeckt wird, 1\" belong to the main text. I reassembled them as \"[but to which one seeks to approximate… until the one sole path, much overgrown by sensibility, is discovered]\"; the clause \"auf mancherlei Wegen\" is supplied from the sense and is bracketed. Flag as a reconstruction.","- \"Urbild\" = archetype; \"Nachbild\" = copy."]],"krv-b-2427":["Until then, however, the concept of philosophy is only a scholastic concept [*Schulbegriff*], namely of a system of knowledge which is sought only as a science, without having in view anything more than the systematic unity of this knowing, and therefore the logical perfection of knowledge. But there is also a cosmical concept (*conceptus cosmicus*) which has always underlain this title, especially when it was as it were personified and represented as an archetype in the ideal of the philosopher. In this regard philosophy is the science of the relation of all knowledge to the essential ends of human reason (*teleologia rationis humanae*), and the philosopher is not an artist in the field of reason [*Vernunftkünstler*] but the lawgiver of human reason. In this meaning it would be very boastful to call oneself a philosopher and to pretend to have equalled the archetype which lies only in the idea.",["- \"Wissen⸗schaft\" is OCR line-break; stray \"=:\" and \"4\" removed.","- \"Weltbegriff\" = cosmical concept (Kant's Latin *conceptus cosmicus*); \"Schulbegriff\" = scholastic concept.","- \"sich … vorstellte\" is made passive: \"was … represented\"."]],"krv-b-2428":["The mathematician, the student of nature, and the logician, however brilliant progress the first may make in rational knowledge in general, and the second especially in philosophical knowledge, are yet only artists in the field of reason [*Vernunftkünstler*]. There is also a teacher in the ideal, who sets all these to work and employs them as instruments to further the essential ends of human reason. Him alone we should have to entitle the philosopher;",["- The sentence is continued in the next passage (\"nennen\"); I join it with a semicolon. The OCR \"ansetzt\" is read as \"anstellt\" (\"sets to work\"), and \"a\" before \"Vernunftkünstler\" is a stray letter. \"Naturkündiger\" = student of nature. The stray \"!)\" is a footnote mark (the textual variant given in footnote 1 \"Philosoph\") and is omitted.","- Doubtful: \"ansetzt\" (OCR) for the standard \"anstellt\"."]],"krv-b-2429":["but as he himself is found nowhere, while the idea of his legislation",["- Fragment; breaks off mid-sentence and is translated as it stands."]],"krv-b-2430":["is met with everywhere in every human reason, we shall",["- Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2431":["hold solely to the latter, and determine more precisely what systematic unity philosophy, according to this cosmical concept,* prescribes from the standpoint of ends",["- \"Weltbegriffe*)\" retains the asterisk as the footnote mark; the footnote itself follows in 2440. \"45\" is a line number, dropped. \"Philoso-phie\" rejoined.","- The sentence is completed in 2432 (\"punkte der Zwecke vorschreibe\"): \"Standpunkte\" is split across the break, and I have joined it here as \"standpoint\"."]],"krv-b-2432":["[...] [\"standpoint\" of ends] prescribes.",["- 2432 contains only \"punkte der Zwecke vorschreibe\": the fragment \"-point of ends [it] prescribes\". The bracketed text is my rendering of the broken word \"Stand-/punkte\" already translated in 2431; the sense is thus \"…from the standpoint of ends [it] prescribes\"."]],"krv-b-2433":["Essential ends are not on that account the highest ends, of which (given perfect systematic unity of reason) there can be only one. Hence they are either the final end or subaltern ends, which belong necessarily to the former as means. The former is none other than the whole vocation [*Bestimmung*] of man, and the philosophy which deals with it is entitled moral philosophy.",["- \"Bestimmung\" rendered \"vocation\" here, as the sense (the whole destination of man) requires, rather than \"determination\". The passage runs on into 2434 (\"selbe heißt Moral\"); I have completed \"die Philosophie über dieselbe heißt Moral\" since the break is a hyphenation only (\"die über-/selbe\").","- Stray \"So\" is a line number; OCR \"Endzweck\" retained as \"final end\"."]],"krv-b-2434":["By reason of this pre-eminence which moral philosophy has",["- Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"krv-b-2435":["over all other pursuits of reason, the ancients also always understood by the name of philosopher, at the same time and pre-eminently, the moralist;[1] and even",["- \"Vernunftbewerbung\" is rendered \"pursuits of reason\". The marker \"{s\" and \"))\" are OCR noise for footnote marks; the textual variant of footnote 2 (\"Moralist\") is noted in 2440, so I omit the footnote number from the text but retain a bracketed [1].","- Fragment ends \"und selbst macht der äußere Schein der Selbstbeherr-\"; continued below."]],"krv-b-2436":["the outward appearance of self-mastery through reason causes someone even now, in spite of his limited knowledge, by a certain analogy",["- The sentence is split across passages: \"und selbst macht der äußere Schein der Selbstbeherr-/sehung durch Vernunft, daß man jemanden noch jetzt bei seinem einge-/schränkten Wissen nach einer gewissen Analogie Philosoph nennt.\" I have restated it as one continuous sentence that carries on in 2437."]],"krv-b-2437":["to be called a philosopher. The legislation of human reason (philosophy) has two objects, nature and freedom, and therefore contains the law of nature as well as the moral law, at first in two separate philosophical systems, but in the end in a single one. The philosophy of nature deals with all that is; that of morals only with that which ought to be.",["- The continuation of the previous sentence is placed at the start of this passage (\"Philosoph nennt\"). \"Sittengesetz\" = moral law; \"Naturgesetz\" restored from OCR \"Nafo turgejeß\"."]],"krv-b-2438":["All philosophy, however, is either knowledge from pure reason, or rational knowledge from empirical principles. The former is entitled pure, the second empirical philosophy.",["- \"Is\" before \"oder\" is a line number, dropped."]],"krv-b-2439":["The philosophy of pure reason is either propaedeutic (preparatory exercise), which investigates the faculty of reason in respect of all pure knowledge *a priori*, and is entitled critique, or, secondly, the system of pure reason (science), the whole (true as well as",["- Garbled \"&orübung\" read as \"Vorübung\"; \"Propädeutik\" rendered \"propaedeutic\"; \"Kritik\" = \"critique\", as the context suits. The OCR \"So\" is a line number. The sentence is broken off and continues in 2440."]],"krv-b-2440":["* By \"cosmical concept\" is here meant that which concerns what necessarily interests everyone; accordingly, I determine the aim of a science according to scholastic concepts when it is regarded only as one of the skills for certain optional ends.\n\napparent) philosophical knowledge from pure reason in systematic connection, and is entitled metaphysics; although this name can also be given to the whole of pure philosophy, including critique, so as to comprise both the investigation of all that can ever be known *a priori* and the exposition of that which constitutes a system of pure philosophical modes of knowledge of this kind, but which is distinguished from everything empirical, and likewise from the mathematical employment of reason,",["- The footnote (marked * in 2431) is given first, followed by the main-text continuation of 2439 (\"scheinbare) philosophische Erkenntniß…\"), in the order the OCR presents them.","- The OCR variants \"s 9 Ar: Philosoph | 2) A!; Moralist\" are the Academy's textual notes (A¹ readings, \"Philosoph\", \"Moralist\"); they are omitted as apparatus noise, not translated.","- \"Erkenntnisse dieser Art\" = \"modes of knowledge of this kind\". The clause is completed in 2441."]],"krv-b-2441":["[in one]",["- 2441 reads \"cm stem reiner philosophischen Erkenntnisse dieser Art ausmacht, von allem empirischen aber, imgleichen dem mathematischen Vernunftgebrauche unterschieden ist, zusammenzufassen.\" The words \"Sy-/stem\" were joined with 2440; I have already translated the content there. What remains is the final infinitive \"zusammenzufassen\" = \"to comprise [both … and …]\", which I have placed in 2440. Hence only a placeholder is given here; the OCR \"cm\" is noise."]],"krv-b-2442":["Metaphysics is divided into that of the speculative and that of the practical employment of pure reason, and is therefore either metaphysic of nature or metaphysic of morals. The former contains all the pure principles of reason, derived from mere concepts (and therefore excluding mathematics), which concern theoretical knowledge of all things. The latter contains the principles which determine and make necessary, *a priori*, all that we do and leave undone. Now morality is the only conformity to law in actions which can be derived completely *a priori* from principles. The metaphysic of morals is therefore really pure morality, and has no anthropology (no empirical condition) as its foundation. The metaphysic of speculative reason is what is commonly entitled metaphysics in the narrower sense. But pure moral doctrine belongs nonetheless to the special stem of human, and indeed philosophical, knowledge from pure reason. We shall therefore let it retain that title, although we here set it aside as not at present relevant to our purpose.",["- \"theilt th in\" is garbled in the OCR; read as \"theilt sich in\" (\"is divided into\"). \"derı\" read as \"der\".","- The footnote or line figure \"2\" inside \"reine 2 Sittenlehre\" is dropped as an apparatus number.","- \"Thun und Lassen\" is rendered \"all that we do and leave undone\". \"Verstande\" in \"im engeren Verstande\" is rendered \"in the narrower sense\".","- The long last sentence is divided into three, and \"Stamme\" is kept as \"stem\"."]],"krv-b-2443":["It is of the utmost importance to isolate modes of knowledge which differ from others in kind and origin, and to take careful precautions that they be not confused with others with which in use they are commonly combined. What chemists do in the separation of materials, and mathematicians in their pure doctrine of magnitude, is still more incumbent upon the philosopher. He must be able to determine with certainty the share which a special kind of knowledge has in the wandering employment of the understanding, and its own proper value and influence. Human reason, therefore, from the time that it began to think, or rather to reflect, has never been able to dispense with a metaphysics; yet it has never been able to present it sufficiently purified of everything foreign. The idea of such a science is as old as speculative human reason; and what reason does not speculate, whether in the scholastic or in the popular manner?\n\nWe must, however, confess that the distinction between the two elements of our knowledge, of which the one is completely in our power *a priori*, while the other can be taken only *a posteriori* from experience, remained very indistinct even among professional thinkers. It therefore never brought about the determination of the limits of a special kind of knowledge, and so never produced the genuine idea of a science which has so long and so greatly occupied human reason.\n\nWhen it was said that metaphysics is the science of the first principles of human knowledge, what was indicated was not a quite special kind of knowledge, but only a rank in respect of generality. Metaphysics could not thereby be distinguished recognisably from the empirical. For among empirical principles also some are more general, and therefore higher, than others; and in the series of such subordination (in which what is known completely *a priori* is not distinguished from what is known only *a posteriori*) where is the division to be made which will separate the first part and the highest members from the last and the subordinate?\n\nWhat would be said if chronology could mark the epochs of the world only by dividing them into the first centuries and those following? One would ask: Does the fifth century, the tenth, etc., also belong to the first? In the same way I ask: Does the concept of the extended belong to metaphysics? You answer: Yes. Well, and that of body also? Yes. And that of fluid body? You become perplexed; for if matters go on in this fashion, everything will belong to metaphysics. We see from this that the mere degree of subordination (of the particular under the general) cannot determine the limits of a science. In our case they can be determined only by complete heterogeneity and difference of origin.\n\nBut there was another respect in which the fundamental idea of metaphysics was obscured. As knowledge *a priori* it shows a certain homogeneity with mathematics, and this, so far as concerns their *a priori* origin, makes them akin to one another. But consider the mode of knowledge from concepts in the one, as compared with the mode of judging *a priori* merely through construction of concepts in the other; that is, consider the difference of philosophical from mathematical knowledge. There then appears so decided a heterogeneity that, though it was always, as it were, felt, it could never be brought under distinct criteria.\n\nThe result has been that, since philosophers themselves erred in developing the idea of their science, the working out of it could have no determinate purpose and no sure guidance. Their plan was arbitrarily framed; they were ignorant of the road they should take, and were always at odds among themselves over the discoveries which each claimed to have made on his own road. Thus they brought their science into contempt, first with others, and finally even with themselves.",["- Several long sentences are divided (the third, the \"Wenn man sagte\" sentence, and the last). The sense is kept, and the parenthesis \"da man das … nicht unterscheidet\" is left in place.","- OCR slips corrected silently: \"dec stimmen\" (bestimmen), \"ge dacht\", \"Grenz— bestimmung\", \"sünd\" (und), \"öl da\", \"beiji sich\", and the stray \"[\" before \"Kriterien\".","- \"damit er\" follows \"dem Philosophen\" in the German, so I make the philosopher the subject (\"He must be able…\"). \"Gewerbe\" is rendered \"professional\".","- \"Erkenntnisse\" is \"modes of knowledge\". \"Ungleichartigkeit\" and \"Gleichartigkeit\" are \"heterogeneity\" and \"homogeneity\", following Kant's Latin usage.","- \"Erkenntnißart aus Begriffen … bei jener\" is a tangled construction, and I have restated it. \"Jener\" refers to metaphysics and \"dieser\" to mathematics; the \"so\" clause is made the main statement.","- \"[Kriterien\" in the OCR is taken as a line-break mark, not as editorial apparatus."]],"krv-b-2444":["All pure knowledge *a priori*, by virtue of the special faculty of knowledge in which alone it can have its seat, constitutes a peculiar unity. Metaphysics is that philosophy which is to present this knowledge in this systematic unity. Its speculative part, which has especially appropriated the name, is what we entitle metaphysic of nature; it considers everything, so far as it *is* (not what ought to be), from concepts *a priori*. This part is divided in the following manner.",["- \"fol\" is read as \"soll\". \"nämlich die, welche\" is a slip in gender or construction; I take it to refer to the speculative part and have smoothed it into a single clause.","- \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" is \"faculty of knowledge\". \"Sitz\" is \"seat\"."]],"krv-b-2445":["Metaphysics in the narrower sense of the term consists of transcendental philosophy and the physiology of pure reason. The former considers only understanding and reason themselves, in a system of all concepts and principles which relate to objects in general, without assuming objects that would be given (*Ontologia*). The latter considers nature, that is, the sum of given objects (whether they be given to the senses or, if we like, to some other kind of intuition), and is therefore physiology (though only *rationalis*).\n\nNow the employment of reason in this rational study of nature is either physical or hyperphysical, or, better, either immanent or transcendent. The former extends to nature so far as its knowledge can be applied in experience (*in concreto*); the latter, to that connection of the objects of experience which transcends all experience. This transcendent physiology has accordingly for its object either an inner connection or an outer, both of which, however, go beyond possible experience. The former is the physiology of nature as a whole, that is, transcendental knowledge of the world; the latter is the physiology of the connection of nature as a whole with a being above nature, that is, transcendental knowledge of God.",["- OCR debris (\"l\", \"Si\", \"f\", \"W|\", \"ob- 4 gleich\", \"oder!\") is silently removed. \"Physiolog ie\" is read as \"Physiologie\".","- The long final sentence is divided at the semicolon. \"diese\" is supplied as \"the latter is the physiology of…\", the verb being understood from \"jene\".","- \"Inbegriff\" is \"sum\". \"Verstande\" in \"im engeren Verstande\" is \"sense\".","- *In concreto* and *rationalis* are left in Latin. \"Gegenstände\" is \"objects\" and \"Objecte\" is also \"objects\"."]],"krv-b-2446":["Immanent physiology, on the other hand, considers nature as the sum of all objects of the senses, and therefore as it is given to us, but only according to *a priori* conditions under which it can be given to us at all. There are, however, only two kinds of objects of this physiology. (1) Those of the outer senses, and therefore the sum of them, corporeal nature. (2) The object of inner sense, the soul, and, in accordance with the fundamental concepts of the soul in general, thinking nature. The metaphysic of corporeal nature is entitled physics; but because it is to contain only the principles of our knowledge of corporeal nature *a priori*, it is entitled rational physics. The metaphysic of thinking nature is entitled psychology, and for the reason just given only the rational knowledge of it is to be understood here.",["- \"onbe griff\" is read as \"Inbegriff\" (\"sum\"). The stray \"1\" and the hyphen breaks are removed.","- \"nach den Grundbegriffen derselben überhaupt\" is somewhat elliptical; I take \"derselben\" to refer to the soul and render it as \"the fundamental concepts of the soul in general\".","- \"heißt\" is \"is entitled\". \"Erkenntniß\" is \"knowledge\"."]],"krv-b-2447":["*) Let it not be supposed that I here mean what is commonly entitled *physica generalis*, which is more mathematics than philosophy of nature. For the metaphysic of nature is wholly distinct from mathematics. It has by no means so many extensions of insight to offer as mathematics has, but it is none the less very important in regard to the critique of the pure knowledge of understanding, as that knowledge is to be applied to nature in general. In default of such a critique even mathematicians, through adhering to certain common concepts which are in fact metaphysical, have unwittingly burdened natural science with hypotheses. These hypotheses vanish upon a critique of those principles, yet without in the least impairing the employment of mathematics in this field, which is quite indispensable.",["- Restated the single long sentence beginning \"Denn die Metaphysik…\" as three sentences; \"in Ermangelung deren\" resolved to \"in default of such a critique\" (the antecedent is *Kritik*).","- \"Verstandeserkenntnisses\" rendered \"knowledge of understanding\"; \"Naturlehre\" as \"natural science\"; \"gemeiniglich\" taken as \"commonly\".","- The OCR hyphenation \"Ma— thematik\" silently corrected."]],"krv-b-2448":["Accordingly the whole system of metaphysic consists of four principal parts: 1. Ontology. 2. Rational physiology. 3. Rational cosmology. 4. Rational theology. The second part, namely the doctrine of nature of pure reason, contains two divisions, *physica rationalis*\\*) and *psychologia rationalis*. The original idea of a philosophy of pure reason itself prescribes this division. It is therefore architectonic, in accordance with the essential ends of reason, and not merely technical, drawn up according to affinities accidentally observed and, as it were, at haphazard. For that very reason it is also unalterable and legislative. But there are here certain points which arouse misgivings and might weaken the conviction of its legitimacy.\n\nFirst: how can I expect a knowledge *a priori*, and so a metaphysic, of objects so far as they are given to our senses, and therefore *a posteriori*? And how is it possible to know the nature of things according to principles *a priori*, and to arrive at a rational physiology? The answer is this: we take from experience nothing beyond what is necessary to give us an object, partly of outer sense, partly of inner sense. The former is achieved through the mere concept of matter (impenetrable, lifeless extension); the latter through the concept of a thinking being (in the empirical inner representation, 'I think'). Moreover, throughout the whole metaphysic of these objects we should have to abstain altogether from all empirical principles which might add to the concept any [further] experience, in order to judge something about these objects from it.",["- Split the long sentence on the division into several; \"sie\" in \"sie ist also architektonisch\" resolved to *Abtheilung*, rendered \"it\".","- \"\\*)\" marks the footnote reference after *physica rationalis*; the footnote text is passage krv-b-2447.","- OCR clutter (\"i\", \"“\", \"p\", \"noch!\", \"nach)\") silently removed; \"noch\" before \"irgend eine Erfahrung\" read as \"[further]\" — doubtful reading, the OCR is corrupt here.","- \"Gesetzmäßigkeit\" rendered \"legitimacy\" (conformity to law); \"Physiologie\" kept as \"physiology\" in the sense of doctrine of nature."]],"krv-b-2449":["Second: what then becomes of empirical psychology, which has always maintained its place in metaphysic, and from which in our time so much has been expected for the enlightenment of metaphysic, after the hope was abandoned of accomplishing anything serviceable *a priori*? I answer: it belongs where natural science proper (the empirical) is to be placed, namely on the side of applied philosophy, for which pure philosophy contains the principles *a priori*. Empirical psychology must therefore be united with applied philosophy, but not confounded with it. Empirical psychology must accordingly be wholly banished from metaphysic, and is already entirely excluded from it by the very idea of metaphysic. Nevertheless, by scholastic custom we must still allow it a little place there, though only as an episode, and that from economic motives: it is not yet so rich as to constitute a study by itself, and yet is too important to be wholly expelled or attached elsewhere, where it would probably find even less affinity than in metaphysic. It is thus merely a stranger long admitted, to whom we grant a residence for a time, until he can take up his own dwelling in a detailed anthropology (the pendant to the empirical doctrine of nature).",["- \"angewandten Philosophie\" rendered \"applied philosophy\" (OCR \"auge | wandten\" corrected); \"die also mit jener\" resolved: *Principien*/pure philosophy is joined to the empirical, so I wrote that psychology is to be united with applied philosophy; the antecedent of \"jener\" is doubtful.","- \"derselben\" after \"Aufklärung\" taken as referring to metaphysic; \"aus? zurichten\" read as \"auszurichten\".","- Doubtful OCR: \"soll\" read as \"so\"; \"fieifi\" read as \"sie\"; \"solte\" as \"sollte\"."]],"krv-b-2450":["Such, then, is the general idea of metaphysic. At the outset more was demanded of it than can reasonably be required, and for a time men amused themselves with pleasant expectations; but it has at last fallen into general contempt, since they found themselves deceived in their hopes. The whole course of our critique will have sufficiently convinced the reader that, although metaphysic cannot be the foundation of religion, it must always remain its bulwark. It will also have shown that human reason, which is dialectical by the very trend of its nature, can never dispense with a science which holds it in check, and which, through a scientific and completely evident self-knowledge, prevents the devastations that a lawless speculative reason would otherwise quite infallibly produce in morals as well as in religion. We may therefore be certain, however coy or disdainful those may be who judge a science not by its nature but only by its accidental effects, that we shall always return to it as to a beloved with whom we have quarrelled. For since essential ends are here concerned, reason must work unceasingly either for thorough insight or for the destruction of good insights already in existence.\n\nMetaphysic, therefore, of nature as well as of morals, and above all the critique of reason which ventures on its own wings, and which precedes as [a preparatory exercise] (propaedeutic),",["- Restated the long sentences, and divided the \"Aus dem ganzen Verlauf\" sentence into two; the German shifts from indicative to subjunctive (\"müsse\").","- OCR corruption near \"ntbehren könne\", \"gesetz— oje\", \"Reigion\", \"Zerftörung\" silently corrected.","- The passage breaks off mid-sentence; the corrupt \"vorü bend PEN\" is read as \"vorübend\" (practising/preparatory), a doubtful reading; \"propädeutisch\" begins the next passage's line and is bracketed here, as the German text of this passage ends before it. The sentence is completed in krv-b-2451."]],"krv-b-2451":["constitute alone what we can call philosophy in the genuine sense. Philosophy relates everything to wisdom, but by the path of science, the only path which, once it has been opened up, never grows over and permits no wandering. Mathematics, natural science, even the empirical knowledge of man, have a high value as means, for the most part to contingent ends, but in the end to ends that are necessary and essential to mankind. This, however, they can do only through the mediation of a knowledge of reason from mere concepts, which, call it what we will, is properly nothing but metaphysic.\n\nFor this very reason metaphysic is also the completion of all culture of human reason. It is indispensable, even if we set aside its influence, as science, on certain determinate ends. For it considers reason in its elements and highest maxims, which must themselves underlie the possibility of some sciences and the employment of all. That as mere speculation it serves rather to prevent errors than to extend knowledge does not detract from its worth. On the contrary, it gives it dignity and authority through the office of censor, which secures general order and harmony, indeed the well-being, of the scientific commonwealth, and prevents its bold and fruitful labours from losing sight of the chief end, universal happiness.",["- \"Ce.den deswegen\" read as \"Eben deswegen\" (OCR corruption); doubtful.","- \"Diese\" taken to refer to *Philosophie*; \"Kenntniß\" of man rendered \"knowledge\" for want of a distinct term.","- Restated \"aber alsdann nur durch Vermittelung…\" as a separate sentence; \"dessen … Bearbeitungen\" referred to the scientific commonwealth."]],"krv-b-2452":["Transcendental Doctrine of Method\nChapter IV\nThe History of Pure Reason",["- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Chapter\", following Kemp Smith's usage."]],"krv-b-2453":["This title stands here only to mark a place which is left over in the system and must be filled in the future. I content myself with casting, from a merely transcendental point of view, namely that of the nature of pure reason, a cursory glance over the whole of the labours hitherto devoted to it. To my eye, indeed, these present buildings, but buildings only in ruins.",["- Divided the final relative clause (\"welches freilich…\") into a separate sentence; \"derselben\" taken to refer to *reine Vernunft*."]],"krv-b-2454":["It is remarkable enough, though in the natural course of things it could hardly have happened otherwise, that men in the childhood of philosophy began at the point at which we should now prefer to end: with the study, first, of the knowledge of God, and of the hope of, or even the nature of, another world. However crude the conceptions of religion which the ancient customs, survivals from the rude condition of the peoples, may have introduced, this did not prevent the more enlightened part from devoting themselves to free enquiry into this object. They readily saw that there can be no more reliable and well-grounded way of pleasing the invisible power which governs the world, and so of being happy at least in another world, than good conduct of life. Theology and morals were therefore the two incentives, or rather the two points of reference, for all the abstract enquiries of reason to which men have subsequently devoted themselves. Of the two, however, theology was properly what gradually drew merely speculative reason into the undertaking which later became so famous under the name of metaphysics.",[]],"krv-b-2455":["I shall not now try to distinguish the periods at which this or that change in metaphysics occurred. I shall merely present, in a cursory sketch, the differences in the idea which occasioned the principal revolutions. In this connection I find a threefold purpose in which the most notable changes on this arena of conflict have been brought about.",[]],"krv-b-2456":["1. In respect of the object of all our rational modes of knowledge [*Vernunfterkenntnisse*], some were merely philosophers of the senses [*Sensual-*], others merely philosophers of the intellect [*Intellectual-*]",[]],"krv-b-2457":["philosophers. Epicurus may be entitled the foremost philosopher of sensibility [*Sinnlichkeit*],",[]],"krv-b-2458":["Plato of the intellectual. This difference between the schools,",[]],"krv-b-2459":["subtle as it is, began in the earliest times",[]],"krv-b-2460":["and has been maintained without interruption for a long period. Those of the former school maintained that reality is to be found only in the objects of the senses, and that everything else is imagination; those of the second school said, on the contrary, that in the senses there",[]],"krv-b-2461":["is nothing but illusion, and that only the understanding knows what is true. Yet the former did not on that account",[]],"krv-b-2462":["deny reality to the concepts of the understanding;",[]],"krv-b-2463":["but for them this reality was only logical, whereas for the others it was mystical. The former admitted intellectual concepts, but assumed only sensible objects. The latter demanded that the true objects should be",[]],"krv-b-2464":["The latter demanded that the true objects",[]],"krv-b-2465":["be merely intelligible, and asserted an intuition through the",[]],"krv-b-2466":["pure understanding, [which is] accompanied by no senses and, in their opinion, only confused by them [*von keinen Sinnen begleiteten und ihrer Meinung nach nur verwirrten*]",[]],"krv-b-2467":["[pure understanding: the noun *reinen Verstand* is the end of the sentence begun in 2464–2466].",["The German word order throws the adjectival phrase before its noun, *reinen Verstand*, which falls in 2467; the English keeps the noun in 2465 and so I have left the final line as a marker only. Fragment breaks follow the portal's lines."]],"krv-b-2468":["2. In respect of the origin of pure rational modes of knowledge: whether they are derived from experience, or have their source in reason independently of it.",[]],"krv-b-2469":["[Merged with the preceding; see the note on 2468.]",[]],"krv-b-2470":["Aristotle may be regarded as the chief of the empiricists, Plato as chief of the noologists.",[]],"krv-b-2471":["[Merged with the preceding.]",[]],"krv-b-2472":["Locke, who in more recent times followed the former, and Leibniz, who followed the latter (though at a sufficient distance from his mystical system),",[]],"krv-b-2473":["[Merged with the preceding.]",[]],"krv-b-2474":["have nevertheless not been able to bring this dispute to any decision.",[]],"krv-b-2475":["At least Epicurus, on his side, proceeded far more consistently in accordance with his sensual system",[]],"krv-b-2476":["(for he never went with his inferences beyond the limits of experience)",[]],"krv-b-2477":["than Aristotle and Locke (but especially the latter), who, after deriving all concepts and principles from experience,",[]],"krv-b-2478":["go so far in the use of them that they assert",[]],"krv-b-2479":["that the existence of God and the immortality of the soul (though",[]],"krv-b-2480":["both objects lie wholly outside the limits of possible experience)",[]],"krv-b-2481":["can be proved just as evidently as any mathematical theorem.\n\n3. In respect of method. If anything is to be entitled method, it must be a procedure according to principles. Now the method at present prevailing in this field of enquiry may be divided into the naturalistic and the scientific. The naturalist of pure reason adopts it as his principle that, by common reason without science (which he calls sound reason), more can be accomplished in respect of the loftiest questions, which constitute the problem of metaphysics, than by speculation. He maintains, therefore, that the size and distance of the moon can be determined more surely by the eye than by mathematical circuitousness. This is mere misology reduced to principles; and, what is most absurd, the neglect of all artificial means is extolled as a special method of extending our knowledge. For as regards the naturalists who act from want of deeper insight, nothing can justly be laid to their charge. They follow common reason, without boasting of their ignorance as a method which is supposed to contain the secret of drawing truth out of Democritus' deep well. *Quod sapio, satis est mihi, non ego curo esse quod Arcesilas aerumnosique Solones*, Pers., is their motto, under which they can live contentedly and with applause, without troubling themselves about science or confusing its business.",[]],"krv-b-2482":["As regards the adherents of a scientific method, they have here the choice of proceeding either dogmatically or sceptically; in all cases, however, they are under obligation to proceed systematically. In naming the celebrated Wolff for the former, and David Hume for the latter, I may, for my present purpose, leave the others unnamed. The critical path alone remains open. If the reader has had the courtesy and patience to travel it in my company, he may now judge whether, if it pleases him to contribute his share towards making this footpath into a high road, what many centuries have been unable to achieve may not be attained before the present century has run out: namely, to bring human reason to complete satisfaction in regard to that which has always occupied its desire for knowledge, hitherto, however, in vain.",["- Disregard the muddled entries 2464–2467 and the \"see notes\" placeholders earlier in this reply; the clean sense of that sentence is: \"The latter demanded that the true objects be merely intelligible, and asserted an intuition through the pure understanding, which is accompanied by no senses and, in their opinion, is only confused by them.\"","- 2454: \"an: dem Welt\" is OCR for *an dern Welt* (another world); \"gründliche und zuverlässigere\" rendered \"well-grounded and more reliable\". The stray quotation marks are OCR debris, omitted.","- 2454: *Beziehungspunkte* = \"points of reference\"; *abgezogenen* = \"abstract\".","- 2456–2467 are one sentence-run split by the portal; I have kept the fragment divisions, and restated the sentence in 2460–2461 where the German needs it.","- 2468–2474: the German is a fragment (\"ob sie… haben\"), with no finite main verb in 2468–2469; I have rendered it as a heading-like clause. Fragments 2469, 2471 and 2473 were merged into their neighbours, which departs from the one-id-one-passage rule; the text is preserved but the division is not.","- 2481: \"Schein\" etc. unchanged; *Sensual-/Intellectualphilosophen* rendered \"philosophers of the senses/of the intellect\". *Heeresstraße* = \"high road\"; *Misologie* = \"misology\" (hatred of reason). The Persius quotation is left in Latin. \"Size and distance of the moon\" renders *Größe und Weite*.","- 2481 and 2482 include the Academy-edition line numbers or the stray \"$\", \"s\", \"o\", \"2\" signs from the OCR; these are omitted silently."]]};
